var COURSE_DATA={"lessons":[{"lessonId":"m01l01","moduleId":"m01","stageId":"incoming","title":"What incoming inspection protects: defect cost by stage","goals":[{"text":"Specify the documents that must accompany a received lot of components, paste or bare PCB and state what each one proves.","competencies":["K1"]},{"text":"Classify incoming-material findings as accept, quarantine or reject and write a record a supplier can act on.","competencies":["K1","K9"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-037"],"quiz":["q-m01l01-01","q-m01l01-02","q-m01l01-03","q-m01l01-04","q-m01l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"Purpose of incoming inspection","p":["Incoming inspection protects the line by confirming three things before a lot is released: that it matches the purchase order, that it is in the condition the process requires, and that the supplier controlled the characteristics that affect yield. It is a gate, not a visual check. Material that is wrong, wet, expired or counterfeit has to be stopped here, because every later stage — placement, reflow, wave, cleaning, ICT, functional test, field service — is a more expensive place to find it.","The cost of the same defect rises as it moves downstream. At incoming it costs inspection time, quarantine space and a supplier conversation. After reflow it costs rework, scrap and a stopped line. In the field it costs a complaint, a warranty claim and the credibility of the plant. The exact figures come from your own accounts; the shape does not change.","A working plan also protects process stability. Released without evidence, marginal paste or a marginal bare board makes operators compensate with printer, placement or reflow changes — and those changes hide the material problem, move the defect to another stage, and make root cause analysis harder than it needs to be. That is why the record has to say what was checked, what was found and what was released: so a yield problem three weeks later can be traced back to a material condition."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 900 300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Defect cost escalation by stage'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace}</style><line x1='60' y1='250' x2='860' y2='250' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><rect x='100' y='238' width='120' height='12' rx='4' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.25)' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='290' y='190' width='120' height='60' rx='4' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.35)' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='480' y='120' width='120' height='130' rx='4' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.5)' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='670' y='40' width='120' height='210' rx='4' fill='rgba(244,67,54,.35)' stroke='#f44336' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='160' y='228' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' font-weight='600' text-anchor='middle'>1x</text><text x='350' y='180' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' font-weight='600' text-anchor='middle'>10x</text><text x='540' y='110' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' font-weight='600' text-anchor='middle'>100x</text><text x='730' y='30' fill='#f44336' font-size='14' font-weight='600' text-anchor='middle'>1000x</text><text x='160' y='272' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>AT RECEIPT</text><text x='350' y='272' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>ON LINE</text><text x='540' y='272' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>IN FIELD</text><text x='730' y='272' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>RECALL</text><text x='60' y='24' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' font-weight='600'>RELATIVE COST OF ONE DEFECT</text><text x='60' y='44' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12'>ESCALATION GROWS WITH EVERY STAGE IT SURVIVES</text></svg>","caption":"Escalation ratios are illustrative"},{"t":"image","caption":"Worked plan: lot of 3,000 units at general inspection level II gives code letter K, n = 125; at AQL 1.0 % the rule is accept at 3 defectives, reject at 4.","src":"assets/img/m01l01.webp","alt":"Tray of sampled components with a sampling plan table: lot size 3,000 pcs, inspection level II, AQL 1.0 %, code letter K, sample size n = 125, accept 3, reject 4"}]},{"h":"Documents for component lots","p":["A component lot should arrive with a packing list, a certificate of conformance, a material declaration, a restricted-substances declaration, a lot or date code, a traceability label, and any test report the purchase order calls for. Each document answers a different question. The packing list fixes quantity and shipment identity. The certificate of conformance is the supplier's declaration that the part, revision and acceptance criteria are as specified; the material declaration names the base material and the plating or termination finish. The restricted-substances declaration shows the lot was screened against the customer and regulatory requirements that apply, and the lot or date code, together with the traceability label, ties the reels on the floor to the supplier batch.","Moisture-sensitive parts add packaging to that list: a moisture barrier bag, desiccant, a humidity indicator card, and a label carrying the floor life or storage condition from the applicable JEDEC moisture sensitivity standard. Compare the label, the packing list and the purchase order before the bag is opened. Where they disagree, the lot waits in quarantine until the supplier explains the difference — a mismatch is not a typo to be resolved by the person holding the box."]},{"h":"Documents for solder paste","p":["Solder paste is a process material, so its paperwork has to prove identity and condition, not only composition. Expect a certificate of analysis, a safety data sheet, the lot code, the expiration date, a storage temperature record in °C, alloy composition, metal content, flux chemistry, halogen content, and rheology or printability data where the purchase order requires it. The certificate of analysis ties the lot to the specified formulation; the safety data sheet covers hazard communication. Expiration date and storage record together answer the only question that matters at the printer: was this paste kept the way the supplier says it must be kept.","Paste also arrives with handling history that the documents do not capture. Note the carton condition, any temperature excursion indicator, and whether the lot shipped under the supplier's stated temperature control. If it is outside the stated storage or shelf-life condition, quarantine it and ask the supplier for a disposition before anyone prints with it. Operator experience is not a release criterion for aged paste: a lot that looks and smells right can still be out of its window."]},{"h":"Documents for bare PCB","p":["A bare PCB lot should carry a certificate of conformance, a material declaration, a copper thickness report in µm, a surface finish report, and — where the purchase order requires them — an impedance report and a cleanliness or solderability report. Together they cover the four things a fabricator can get wrong silently: the laminate system and flame rating, the copper that the current and impedance calculations assume, the finish type and thickness range, and the assembly readiness of the surface.","The physical package is part of the evidence too. Boards with moisture sensitivity or fine pitch should arrive in a barrier bag with desiccant, a humidity indicator and sound edges. Check those before opening, then check the boards. Warping, scratches, oxidation, delamination or exposed copper beyond the drawing limit all mean quarantine, with the location and extent recorded precisely enough that the supplier can inspect the same area and decide whether the cause is fabrication, packaging or transport."]},{"h":"Accept, quarantine, or reject","p":["Three dispositions, and the line between them is evidence rather than schedule pressure. Accept when the documents are complete, the packaging is intact, the environmental indicators are inside the supplier's stated limits, and the sampled material conforms to the purchase order and the applicable IPC acceptance standard. Quarantine when the evidence is incomplete or uncertain — a missing certificate, damaged packaging, a suspect humidity indicator, an unclear lot code, or a measurement sitting right at the limit in mm or °C — and when a supplier review, retest or approved rework could still disposition the lot.","Reject when the defect cannot be dispositioned safely: wrong part, counterfeit suspicion, a prohibited substance, expired paste, a wet or delaminated board, severe mechanical damage, or any safety hazard. Sample size and acceptance criteria come from your sampling plan and your process qualification, not from what is convenient today.","A deviation is a fourth path, and it has to be limited, time-bound and approved by the engineering and quality owners who carry the risk. Write down the affected quantity, the exposure of each downstream process, the extra checks the deviation requires and the date it expires. An open deviation is not an acceptance: if it is not closed on time, the lot is quarantined or rejected."]},{"h":"Writing a record the supplier can act on","p":["A finding record the supplier can act on names the part, the supplier lot and the internal lot, the quantities received, inspected and affected, the requirement, the observed result, the method, the location, the date, the inspector, the disposition and the action requested. Quote the purchase order, drawing or standard by reference rather than copying its text, and state what was seen or measured — not what you think of it. Photographs, label images, document copies and test data make the difference between a finding the supplier can reproduce and one it can only dispute.","Ask for a corrective action that addresses the cause and for evidence that it worked. To make that possible, give the internal part identifier, the customer part identifier, the revision, the package type, the quantity affected, and the stage at which the defect would have been caught had it escaped. A document gap needs the missing document named and the requirement that makes it mandatory. A physical defect needs the criterion, the sample size, the number of affected units and the disposition. Keep the tone factual: the supplier needs facts to change a process, and blame gives it something else to argue about."]},{"h":"Defect cost by stage","p":["Incoming inspection earns its cost on the defects that are hard to see later: oxidised pads, wet PCB, expired paste, the wrong alloy, the wrong plating, damaged terminations, counterfeit parts, and moisture-sensitive devices packed incorrectly. Each of them can pass a visual check and still produce poor wetting, voids, insufficient solder, tombstoning, opens, shorts, thermal stress, ICT or functional failures, and field escapes.","The cost drivers are inspection labour, quarantine space, rework labour, scrap, line stoppage, expedited replacement, complaint handling and warranty. Build the plan against your own stage costs, aim it at the characteristics with the highest downstream cost and the lowest chance of detection later, and link every finding to the stage where it would have surfaced. A wrong surface finish on a bare PCB passes visual inspection and fails at reflow. The wrong metal content in a paste prints normally and shows up as voids. A compromised barrier bag survives placement and cracks in the oven. Naming that stage is what lets you compare the cost of prevention with the cost of escape in the same units."]}],"calc":{"task":"Use your plant cost table. Let N be lot quantity, f be incoming escape fraction, C_in be cost per defect found at incoming, C_reflow be cost per defect found after reflow, and C_field be cost per defect found in the field. Calculate expected cost for the same defect if it is caught at incoming, if it escapes to reflow, and if it escapes to the field. State the stage cost ratio that justifies incoming inspection.","solution":"Start by obtaining N, f, C_in, C_reflow, and C_field from your accounting, scrap, rework, warranty, and line stoppage records. Compute incoming cost as N times f times C_in. Compute reflow cost as N times f times C_reflow. Compute field cost as N times f times C_field. Compare the results by dividing reflow cost by incoming cost and field cost by incoming cost. Use the ratios to decide whether incoming inspection resources are justified for the part family. If the ratio is high, document the avoided cost in the incoming inspection plan and review the plan when part family, supplier, or process changes."},"defect":{"symptom":"Incoming moisture barrier bag is punctured, humidity indicator card shows exceedance, and bare PCB pads show discoloration.","cause":"Packaging damage or storage excursion allowed moisture ingress, and the lot was not quarantined before release.","action":"Quarantine the lot, record lot code, quantity, packaging condition, indicator status, and pad appearance; notify supplier; request corrective action for packaging and storage; do not release until bake or replacement is approved by the applicable moisture sensitivity standard and your process qualification."},"takeaways":["Incoming inspection is the cheapest place to stop a material defect; every stage after it adds rework, scrap or field exposure.","A document package proves identity, condition and traceability, and each certificate answers a different question rather than repeating the others.","Dispositions follow evidence: accept, quarantine or reject on what the documents and measurements show, and time-bound any deviation."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"A certificate of conformance is treated as proof that the lot was physically inspected and is good.","instead":"Treat it as the supplier's declaration of conformity and verify the lot physically against the purchase order and the applicable acceptance standard."},{"mistake":"A lot is released because the line needs it, with the missing certificate promised for later.","instead":"Quarantine until the document arrives; a release without evidence is a deviation and needs a time-bound approval, not a promise."},{"mistake":"The incoming record says \"damaged packaging\" and nothing else, so the supplier cannot investigate.","instead":"Record lot, quantity, location, photographs and the requirement breached, and ask for a cause-level corrective action with evidence."}],"terms":[{"term":"Certificate of conformance (CoC)","def":"Supplier declaration that a lot meets the purchase order and the referenced acceptance criteria; it is evidence of declared conformity, not of inspection by the buyer."},{"term":"Certificate of analysis (CoA)","def":"Supplier report of measured or tested values for named characteristics of a specific lot — alloy, metal content, flux chemistry, plating thickness and similar."},{"term":"Quarantine","def":"Controlled hold on a lot whose conformity is uncertain, preserved until evidence, retest, supplier review or an approved disposition closes it."},{"term":"Deviation","def":"A limited, time-bound and approved acceptance of a lot outside a requirement, with the exposure and the additional checks recorded."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":25,"prereqs":{"lessons":[],"skills":[]}},{"lessonId":"m01l02","moduleId":"m01","stageId":"incoming","title":"The documentation package: CoC/CoA, packing list, MSL label, declarations","goals":[{"text":"Specify the documents that must accompany a received lot of components, paste or bare PCB and state what each one proves.","competencies":["K1"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-003","src-018","src-019","src-022"],"quiz":["q-m01l02-01","q-m01l02-02","q-m01l02-03","q-m01l02-04","q-m01l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m01l02.webp","alt":"Receiving desk with a stack of controlled documents, each marked present, and one flagged as missing","caption":"The documentation package: certificate of conformance, certificate of analysis, packing list, MSL label and declarations checked at receiving"}]},{"h":"Document package at receiving","p":["Every lot arrives as a document-controlled object first and a physical object second. To release it, receiving needs a packing list, a certificate of conformance or certificate of analysis, and any declaration the purchase order names; moisture-sensitive parts add a sealed dry pack with an MSL label and a humidity indicator card. The exact set follows the supplier agreement, the customer specification and the risk of the lot, but the purpose never changes: identity and quantity, conformity or measured properties, moisture history, and regulatory claims each need their own evidence.","A familiar carton and a known supplier are not evidence. The package is what proves the lot is the lot the process was planned around, and it is what a later defect gets traced back to. It also tells the inspector whether to open the pack, sample it, or hold it."]},{"h":"Packing list: what it proves","p":["The packing list records what the supplier says it shipped and how it labelled it: purchase order, part number, revision, quantity, lot or batch code, date code, package type and handling notes. Against it, receiving matches cartons to the expected quantity and traceability code. For paste it names the type, container size and lot; for bare boards, the part number, revision, panel or array count and packaging requirements.","A packing list settles what left the supplier's dock, not what it passed. When it disagrees with the purchase order, the carton labels or the certificate, the lot waits until the discrepancy is explained, and the packing-list number and the mismatch go into the incoming record."]},{"h":"CoC and CoA: what they prove","p":["The certificate of conformance says the delivered lot conforms to the purchase order and the referenced acceptance criteria. The certificate of analysis gives measured or tested values for named characteristics: electrical parameters, plating thickness or package integrity for components; metal content or viscosity for paste, often against IPC J-STD-004; laminate, copper weight, plating type and thickness, judged against IPC-A-600 or IPC-6012 for bare boards.","Either certificate is only evidence for the lot it names. One that covers a different lot proves nothing about the received lot, however well the part number matches, so check the lot or date code, the certificate date, the supplier name and the referenced specification before accepting it."]},{"h":"MSL label and dry pack","p":["A sealed dry pack carries an MSL label and a humidity indicator card. The label gives the moisture sensitivity level and the floor life assigned under IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033; the card shows whether the pack's environment went past the supplier's humidity threshold. Neither one declares the parts fit to place. What they record is handling: how the lot was protected, and how much of its floor life is left once the bag is opened.","Opening the pack starts the clock, and the remainder is tracked against the label value and your own schedule. A damaged pack, a broken seal or a card showing an exceedance sends the lot to quarantine for evaluation. Record the MSL value, the floor-life value, the card status and the seal condition as part of the incoming evidence."]},{"h":"Paste documentation","p":["Paste paperwork has to cover identity and process suitability at once. The packing list names type, container size and lot code. The CoA or CoC shows the lot was manufactured and tested against the agreed specification, often IPC J-STD-004, with shelf-life information, and the container label has to agree with both. Storage and handling conditions belong to the process: 2 °C to 10 °C is a common range, and your facility controls decide the rest.","Paste that comes back into storage should carry its handling history — how many times it was opened, how long it sat outside — because both affect printability and may force mixing or discard. A certificate proves the paste was acceptable at the supplier, not that it survived transport, storage and handling. Record lot code, expiry date, storage condition and handling notes."]},{"h":"Bare PCB documentation","p":["Bare boards arrive with evidence that they were built to the purchased design and acceptance level. The packing list fixes part number, revision, quantity and lot code. The CoC or CoA covers the laminate material, copper weight, plating finish and the dimensional or electrical results the purchase order asks for; acceptance may reference IPC-A-600 for visual and dimensional criteria and IPC-6012 for qualification and performance. Plated through holes need a plating type and thickness range, and the board needs a lot or panel mark that matches the documents.","Controlled-impedance boards add coupon results or a statement that the coupons were tested; special finishes add the finish type and any storage limits. Where the revision, material class or finish does not match the purchase order, the lot is quarantined even if it looks right. Record the revision, the lot code and the certificate reference."]},{"h":"Declarations and special requirements","p":["Declarations sit beside the quality certificates and cover regulatory and contractual claims: restricted substances under RoHS or REACH where the purchase order invokes them, counterfeit avoidance for authorised-channel control on high-reliability or safety parts, conflict minerals where reporting applies. None of them says the lot will process well on your line. They say the supplier has made a specific claim about origin, composition or compliance.","Tie the declaration to the lot or a defined lot range and keep it with the incoming record. A missing declaration does not make the boards unusable, but it blocks the compliance claim until the document arrives. Record the declaration type, its revision and the range it covers."]},{"h":"Disposition based on the document package","p":["Disposition follows the evidence, and a document gap is still a stop. Matching part numbers, revisions, lot codes, quantities and certificates mean release is possible. A missing CoC, a mismatched lot code, a damaged dry pack or an expired shelf life is a document or handling defect rather than a physical one, and it holds the lot until the evidence is corrected.","Write down the document numbers, certificate dates, MSL values, indicator status and any deviation. A quarantined lot records which document or condition failed and what has to happen before release; a released lot records that the package was complete and that its handling limits passed to the process. That record is what makes a defect traceable months later, and what turns a repeated document failure into a supplier corrective action."]},{"h":"Handoff to the process","p":["The package does not stop at the receiving door. Paste type, shelf-life status, MSL floor-life start and board revision go onto the work order or MES record. Component lot codes are linked to the feeder or magazine, paste open time, storage time and open count are visible to the printer operator, and the board revision and lot code are linked to the panel or array actually assembled.","The handoff is what keeps a released lot from being run with the wrong parameters, and it is what makes containment possible: when a suspect condition appears, the process can say which lots saw it. Record it in the same traceability system that holds the inspection result."]}],"calc":{"task":"A dry pack label states a floor life of 168 h per IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033. The pack is opened at the start of the shift. Placement is scheduled 170 h after opening. Determine whether the floor life is exceeded.","solution":"First, identify the floor-life value from the label: 168 h. Next, identify the elapsed time from opening to placement: 170 h. Then compare elapsed time with floor life: 170 h is greater than 168 h. Then calculate the exceedance: 170 h minus 168 h equals 2 h. Finally, disposition: do not place the components without a qualified re-bake or other approved action under IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033; quarantine and record the exceedance."},"defect":{"symptom":"Incoming dry pack has an MSL label, but the humidity indicator card shows the supplier exceedance color and the pack seal is creased.","cause":"The dry pack was damaged or opened before receipt, so the humidity indicator card reflects exposure outside the controlled dry-pack environment.","action":"Quarantine the lot, record the label and card status, inspect for oxidation or coplanarity issues, and follow IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 bake and re-bag requirements if the lot is to be used; notify the supplier with photos and lot traceability."},"takeaways":["A lot is only released when its documents prove identity, conformity and handling history, so the paperwork is evidence rather than an attachment.","Check every certificate against the lot code it names, because a certificate for a different lot proves nothing about the material you received.","Record the document values that drive the process — paste type, MSL floor life, board revision — and pass them on to the work order, not just to the file."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Accepting a lot because the part number on the certificate matches","instead":"Verify the lot or date code, the certificate date and the referenced specification, and quarantine any lot the certificate does not name."},{"mistake":"Recording the MSL level but not the floor-life start time","instead":"Record the bag-open time and the cumulative exposure so the remaining floor life is known before placement, per IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033."},{"mistake":"Filing the certificate in a quality folder and stopping there","instead":"Transfer paste type, shelf-life status, MSL start and board revision to the work order or MES record so the process runs on the same values."}],"terms":[{"term":"Certificate of conformance","def":"A supplier statement that the delivered lot conforms to the purchase order and the acceptance criteria it references."},{"term":"Certificate of analysis","def":"A supplier report of measured or tested values for named characteristics of a specific lot."},{"term":"Floor life","def":"The time a moisture-sensitive component may spend out of its dry pack at a stated ambient condition before reflow, assigned by moisture sensitivity level."},{"term":"Humidity indicator card","def":"A card in a dry pack that changes colour to show whether the pack exceeded a stated humidity threshold."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m01l01"],"skills":["Lesson m01l01: What incoming inspection protects: defect cost by stage"]}},{"lessonId":"m01l03","moduleId":"m01","stageId":"incoming","title":"Sampling a lot: lot definition, sample size, inspection level, AQL concept","goals":[{"text":"Build a sampling plan for a stated lot and inspection level, and explain what the plan does and does not protect against.","competencies":["K1"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-003","src-006"],"quiz":["q-m01l03-01","q-m01l03-02","q-m01l03-03","q-m01l03-04","q-m01l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"Define the lot before sampling","p":"A lot is the smallest quantity of incoming material you can treat as one population for inspection: same part number, revision, supplier, manufacturing lot or date code, packaging and process route. Mixed revisions, date codes or supplier plants are separate lots unless your quality plan says the differences cannot affect the characteristics being inspected. For SMT and THT material, a lot may be a reel, a tray stack, a bag of through-hole parts or a shipment of bare boards, depending on how the supplier controls traceability.","blocks":[]},{"h":"Choose the inspection level from risk and cost","p":"The inspection level sets how many units you inspect relative to lot size. ISO 2859-1 offers general levels and special levels for destructive or expensive tests, and its normal general level is the usual starting point for incoming inspection when nothing argues for another. Drop to a lower general level when lots are stable and defects are expensive to find; raise it when the supplier is new, the part is critical or recent data show variability. Special levels need a documented rationale, and your incoming inspection procedure should confirm the choice."},{"h":"Treat AQL as a supplier quality target","p":"AQL, the acceptable quality level, is the quality level a plan is designed to accept with high probability while the supplier's process runs at that average. It is not a maximum defect rate for every lot, and it never means an accepted lot is defect-free. ISO 2859-1 lists preferred AQL values; incoming inspection commonly uses 0.65 %, 1.0 % and 2.5 % defective, chosen by defect class and risk. Critical defects justify a much tighter plan or full inspection, while minor cosmetic defects tolerate a looser one."},{"h":"Separate defect classes and characteristics","p":"Counting critical, major and minor defects together lets a single AQL hide risk. For incoming material, define what makes a unit defective in each class: a wrong part number or damaged pads is critical, a small cosmetic mark outside a functional area is minor. Where you inspect several characteristics, decide whether one sample serves all classes or whether each needs its own sample and AQL. Record the defect class in the inspection record so the plan can be reviewed against supplier data."},{"h":"Build the plan from lot size, level and AQL","p":["Building a plan means turning lot size and inspection level into a sample size code letter, then reading the accept and reject numbers for the chosen AQL. In ISO 2859-1, a lot of 1201 to 3200 units at general inspection level II gives code letter K, which means n = 125. At AQL 1.0 % defective under normal single sampling, Ac = 3 and Re = 4: inspect 125 units, accept on 3 or fewer defective units, reject on 4 or more.","Read the level and the lot band together, never one without the other. The same lot band gives code letter H (n = 50, Ac = 1, Re = 2 at AQL 1.0 %) at level I and code letter L (n = 200, Ac = 5, Re = 6 at AQL 1.0 %) at level III. The 3000-unit lot in the worked example sits inside 1201 to 3200, which is why it resolves to K; 5,000 units would fall into the next band, 3201 to 10000, and give L with n = 200."],"blocks":[{"t":"image","caption":"Defining a lot and building the plan: lot size 3,000, inspection level II, AQL 1.0 %, code letter K, n = 125, accept 3, reject 4.","src":"assets/img/m01l03.webp","alt":"Lot definition, inspection level selection and the resulting sampling plan for a lot of 3,000 units: code letter K, n = 125, accept 3, reject 4"},{"t":"table","caption":"General inspection levels — sample size code letters (ISO 2859-1 / ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 Table I)","head":["Lot size","Level I","Level II (normal)","Level III"],"rows":[["501 - 1200","G (32 pcs)","J (80 pcs)","K (125 pcs)"],["1201 - 3200","H (50 pcs)","K (125 pcs)","L (200 pcs)"],["3201 - 10000","J (80 pcs)","L (200 pcs)","M (315 pcs)"]]}]},{"h":"Know what the plan protects against","p":"A sound plan makes rejection increasingly likely as the supplier's average quality falls below the AQL, so bad lots tend not to be accepted. It also gives a repeatable basis for normal, tightened and reduced inspection when the standard's switching rules apply. What it cannot do is certify an individual accepted lot as defect-free, catch defects outside the inspected characteristics, or replace supplier process control, incoming test coverage and line containment when something escapes."},{"h":"Plan disposition and feedback up front","p":"Define the disposition before inspection starts — return, sort, use-as-is with concession, or rework — with traceability to the sample results. When an accepted lot then fails on the line, work out whether the lot definition was wrong, the sample missed a localised defect, the characteristic was outside the plan, or the supplier changed material or process. Feed the answer into the next review of inspection level and AQL, and change them by revising the quality plan rather than by adjusting them case by case."}],"calc":{"task":"An incoming lot of 3000 SMT boards is defined as a lot. Use ISO 2859-1 general inspection level II and an AQL of 1.0% defective. Build a single sampling plan and state the accept/reject rule.","solution":"1. Confirm lot definition: 3000 boards, same part number, revision, supplier lot, and packaging. 2. Select standard: ISO 2859-1 normal single sampling. 3. Find sample size code letter: lot size 1201 to 3200 at general inspection level II gives code letter K. 4. Find sample size: code letter K corresponds to n = 125 boards. 5. Select AQL: 1.0% defective. 6. Read accept/reject numbers for n = 125 and AQL 1.0%: Ac = 3, Re = 4. 7. Rule: inspect 125 boards; accept if defective boards <= 3; reject if defective boards >= 4. 8. If rejected, quarantine the lot and apply the documented disposition; if accepted, record the sample result and continue normal inspection unless switching rules indicate a change."},"defect":{"symptom":"Incoming SMT boards pass the AQL plan, but the first boards after reflow show tombstoned small chip resistors at a feeder position.","cause":"The incoming plan sampled for visible damage and part identity but did not test solderability, coplanarity in millimetres, or moisture sensitivity under the line reflow profile; the lot may have contained a localized tape/reel or storage issue that the sample missed.","action":"Quarantine the lot, increase sample size for solderability and coplanarity checks, verify moisture sensitivity level and floor life against J-STD-033, review the feeder setup and reflow profile against your paste/equipment datasheet, and revise the incoming inspection plan if the characteristic should be included."},"takeaways":["Define the lot from part number, revision, supplier, lot code and packaging, because sampling a mixed population tells you nothing about any of its parts.","Read inspection level and lot band together to get the code letter, then take the sample size and the accept and reject numbers for the chosen AQL.","Treat AQL as a target the plan is built around, not as permission to ship defects, and keep critical, major and minor classes apart."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Choosing the sample size straight from the lot size","instead":"Convert lot size and inspection level into a code letter first, then read the sample size; the same lot gives 50, 125 or 200 units depending on the level."},{"mistake":"Applying one AQL to critical and cosmetic defects together","instead":"Give critical defects their own plan or full inspection, and record the defect class so the split survives the next review."},{"mistake":"Treating an accepted lot as proof of zero defects","instead":"Judge the plan by what it protects against, and investigate the lot definition, the sample and the characteristic list when an accepted lot fails on the line."}],"terms":[{"term":"Acceptable quality level","def":"The quality level at which a sampling plan accepts a lot with high probability when the supplier's process runs at that average."},{"term":"Sample size code letter","def":"The ISO 2859-1 letter derived from lot size and inspection level that fixes the sample size for the plan."},{"term":"Accept number","def":"The largest number of defective units in the sample that still allows the lot to be accepted."},{"term":"Switching rules","def":"The standard's rules for moving between normal, tightened and reduced inspection according to recent inspection results."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m01l02"],"skills":["Lesson m01l02: The documentation package: CoC/CoA, packing list, MSL label, declarations"]}},{"lessonId":"m01l04","moduleId":"m01","stageId":"incoming","title":"Component verification: part number, date code, package, counterfeit red flags","goals":[{"text":"Specify the documents that must accompany a received lot of components, paste or bare PCB and state what each one proves.","competencies":["K1"]},{"text":"Classify incoming-material findings as accept, quarantine or reject and write a record a supplier can act on.","competencies":["K1","K9"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-003","src-018","src-022","src-023","src-037","src-038","src-039"],"quiz":["q-m01l04-01","q-m01l04-02","q-m01l04-03","q-m01l04-04","q-m01l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m01l04.webp","alt":"Magnified reel and chip package showing markings and date code, with red callouts for resurfaced bodies, mismatched lead finish and sanded markings","caption":"Component verification: part number, date code, package and the counterfeit red flags"}]},{"h":"Why incoming component verification exists","p":["Incoming verification is the first engineering control that keeps wrong, damaged, expired, mixed or suspect material out of production. Before release, the receiving engineer has to prove four things for components, paste and bare boards alike: the item is the correct part, it traces to a manufacturer or supplier lot, it was handled inside its storage and process limits, and it meets the contractual quality and compliance requirements.","The output is a record, not a stamp. Purchasing, quality, engineering and the supplier all read it, so it names the purchase order, part number, lot or date code, quantity, documents reviewed, physical findings, disposition and any supplier action."]},{"h":"Documents for components","p":["A component lot normally arrives with a packing list, a delivery note or invoice, a certificate of conformance, manufacturer lot traceability and the special handling labels the part needs. The packing list fixes what was shipped and how much; the certificate of conformance states conformity to the purchase order and specification without replacing physical verification; lot traceability ties reels, trays, tubes or waffle packs to one manufacturer lot, which is what recall, failure analysis and counterfeit investigation depend on.","Moisture-sensitive devices come in a moisture barrier bag with desiccant, a humidity indicator card and an MSL label consistent with IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033. Where the contract calls for them, declarations such as RoHS or REACH prove the supplier's declared compliance status, not the composition of every piece in the lot."]},{"h":"Documents for solder paste","p":["Paste is a process material, so its record has to prove chemistry, condition and usable life. The certificate of analysis or conformance identifies alloy, powder type, flux chemistry, metal content, lot number, manufacture date, expiration date, storage temperature and any mixing or warming instruction. That makes it evidence of the specified formulation and of shelf life, nothing more.","A safety data sheet, shipped or available through the supplier's controlled portal, covers hazard classification and handling requirements rather than process quality. Check the lot number on the jar or cartridge against the certificate, inspect the seal, and confirm the paste never left the conditions in its datasheet. A missing certificate holds the lot in quarantine until traceable documentation arrives."]},{"h":"Documents for bare PCBs","p":["Bare boards need documentation that proves material, construction and acceptability: a packing list, a certificate of conformance and, where the contract requires it, qualification or performance evidence against IPC-6012. The certificate identifies part number, revision, layer count, material, copper weight, finish, panel dimensions, lot number and any special requirement such as controlled impedance, plating thickness or flammability.","Material declarations may reference IPC-4101, and flammability may reference UL 94. Visual and dimensional features are commonly judged against IPC-A-600, though the purchase order can impose tighter limits. Before release, match revision, material, finish and panel size to the drawing and the purchase order."]},{"h":"Verify part number, package and date code","p":["Physical verification runs outside in: carton, then inner packaging, then component marking. Supplier label, purchase order and certificate must agree on part number, manufacturer, package type, quantity and lot or date code. On tape and reel, check tape width, cover tape, reel size and orientation against the placement equipment and the datasheet; on trays, tubes and waffle packs, check for mixed cavities and cracked carriers.","Read date codes against the manufacturer's own convention, and ask when the convention is unknown rather than guessing. Altered, laser-etched, re-marked or inconsistent marking makes the lot suspect, so quarantine it. Where counterfeit risk is high, add X-ray, decapsulation, electrical test or authorised-distributor traceability."]},{"h":"Counterfeit red flags","p":["Counterfeit avoidance is an incoming engineering control, not only a purchasing question. Red flags include an unusually low price, an unusually short lead time, missing manufacturer traceability, an unknown or unauthorised distributor, mixed date codes, mixed manufacturers, altered labels, bulk repackaging, old date codes, inconsistent tape or reel packaging and markings unlike the manufacturer's normal style.","None of these proves fraud, but any of them justifies quarantine and further verification. For high-risk parts, buy from authorised distributors, demand manufacturer traceability and apply controls consistent with SAE AS6081. Isolate a suspected lot, photograph the packaging and markings, record lot and date codes and ask the supplier for evidence; if traceability never arrives or verification fails, reject the lot and update the approved supplier list or risk assessment."]},{"h":"Accept, quarantine, reject","p":["Acceptance needs complete documentation, correct identity, acceptable condition and no unresolved red flag. Quarantine covers the lot that may be usable but whose evidence is incomplete or suspect: missing traceability, mixed date codes, damaged moisture barrier packaging, or an age that needs engineering review. Reject applies to the wrong, expired or damaged lot, to one that fails verification, and to one whose authenticity cannot be proven.","Disposition follows the quality management system — ISO 9001, or ANSI/AS9100 where the contract invokes it — which requires nonconforming output to be controlled and documented. A record the supplier can act on carries the purchase order, part number, lot or date code, quantities received, accepted, quarantined and rejected, the objective evidence, required containment, a corrective action request and a response due date. Say what was observed and where, not that the packaging was bad."]}],"calc":{"task":"A lot of 12,000 surface-mount ICs arrives under a purchase order that requires a single manufacturer lot, package type matching the purchase order, and a date-code age no greater than 12 months at receipt. The packing list shows 12,000 pieces. The certificate of conformance is present, but the manufacturer lot traceability sheet is missing. The reel labels show date code 2401, except a reel shows date code 2301. The receipt date code is 2504. The date-code format is assumed to be YYMM; verify the supplier convention. Counted quantity is 11,980 pieces. Classify the lot and write the supplier-facing finding.","solution":"Step A: compare purchase order, packing list, and certificate of conformance; part number and package type match, so identity is not the primary finding. Step B: note missing manufacturer lot traceability; without it, the lot cannot be proven to be a single manufacturer lot, so the finding is quarantine pending traceability. Step C: calculate date-code age for the main lot: 2401 to 2504 is 15 months, which exceeds the 12-month purchase-order limit by 3 months. Step D: calculate date-code age for the mixed reel: 2301 to 2504 is 27 months, which also exceeds the limit and shows mixed date codes. Step E: compare counted quantity 11,980 with packing list 12,000; shortage is 20 pieces. Step F: disposition is quarantine the entire lot, not accept, because missing traceability, mixed date codes, and date-code age exceedance are counterfeit and process-risk red flags; reject if the supplier cannot provide manufacturer traceability, authorized-distributor evidence, or an approved concession. Step G: supplier record should state purchase order number, part number, lot number, receipt date, quantity received, quantity counted, missing documents, date-code findings, mixed-lot finding, shortage, disposition, containment, required corrective action, and response due date."},"defect":{"symptom":"A reel of resistors is opened at the placement machine, and the tape contains parts with two different date codes and slightly different marking styles. The outer label shows a single lot number, but the inner reel label is partially covered by a second label.","cause":"The supplier or distributor likely repackaged mixed stock, relabeled the reel, or shipped an unauthorized substitute lot. The incoming inspection did not verify inner labels, date-code consistency, or manufacturer traceability before release.","action":"Stop the machine, quarantine the reel and the remaining lot, photograph the outer label, inner label, tape, and markings, and record the lot number, date codes, quantity, and purchase order. Quarantine the lot pending supplier traceability and counterfeit assessment. If traceability cannot be proven or the parts fail verification, reject the lot, issue a corrective action request to the supplier, and review the approved supplier list and incoming inspection plan."},"takeaways":["Prove identity, traceability, handling and compliance before release, and write the result as a record the supplier and the line can both act on.","Check lot traceability and date-code consistency as carefully as the certificate, because repackaged or mixed stock is what counterfeiting looks like at receiving.","Use accept, quarantine and reject deliberately: incomplete evidence is a quarantine, not an acceptance with a note."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Accepting a lot on a certificate of conformance alone","instead":"Verify the physical marking, the lot code and the inner labels against the certificate before release."},{"mistake":"Reading date codes with an assumed convention","instead":"Confirm the manufacturer's date-code format, then compute the age against the purchase-order limit."},{"mistake":"Recording a vague finding such as bad packaging","instead":"State what was observed, where, on which lot, and what containment and corrective action you require."}],"terms":[{"term":"Manufacturer lot traceability","def":"Evidence linking the packaged parts to a specific manufacturing lot at the original manufacturer."},{"term":"Counterfeit part","def":"A part whose identity, grade or origin is misrepresented, including re-marked, refurbished or unauthorised copies."},{"term":"Quarantine","def":"The controlled holding of material whose disposition is not yet established."},{"term":"Date code","def":"The manufacturer's code for the production date of a lot, read against that manufacturer's convention."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":25,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m01l03"],"skills":["Lesson m01l03: Sampling a lot: lot definition, sample size, inspection level, AQL concept"]}},{"lessonId":"m01l05","moduleId":"m01","stageId":"incoming","title":"Solder paste, flux and adhesive receipt: alloy, powder, expiry, cold chain","goals":[{"text":"Specify the documents that must accompany a received lot of components, paste or bare PCB and state what each one proves.","competencies":["K1"]},{"text":"Classify incoming-material findings as accept, quarantine or reject and write a record a supplier can act on.","competencies":["K1","K9"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-003","src-015","src-019","src-020","src-022"],"quiz":["q-m01l05-01","q-m01l05-02","q-m01l05-03","q-m01l05-04","q-m01l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m01l05.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: cold-storage cabinet with temperature log, magnified paste label fields (alloy, powder, lot, expiry, storage), powder size distribution curve, photo of a paste jar in a refrigerator and an accept / quarantine / reject decision","caption":"Solder paste, flux and adhesive receipt: label, cold chain, powder and release decision"}]},{"h":"Receipt is a records check first","p":"Match a received lot to the purchase order and the supplier's shipping documents before opening it or moving it to stores. The minimum set is a packing list, a certificate of conformance or test report, lot traceability and any special handling label. The packing list fixes what was shipped and how many units; the certificate records what the supplier tested or inspected against the purchase order; traceability links the material to a manufacturing batch, a date and a test result; the handling label shows the lot was intended for moisture, electrostatic, temperature or shelf-life control."},{"h":"Component lots","p":["For components, ask for the packing list, the certificate of conformance, the lot or date code, the manufacturer's test report where required, the moisture-sensitive device label with desiccant and humidity indicator card where applicable, and electrostatic discharge packaging. Quantity and part number come from the packing list, conformity from the certificate, and lot age from the date code, which also drives first-in, first-out control.","Under J-STD-033 the MSD label and the humidity indicator card show whether the lot was packed for moisture control and whether the indicator has changed state. The ESD packaging shows the lot was protected in transit."]},{"h":"Solder paste lots","p":["A paste certificate of conformance should state lot number, manufacture date, expiry date, alloy composition, powder type, flux classification, viscosity or printability data, halogen or residue classification and storage instructions. The lot number ties the paste to the supplier's batch test, and the two dates show whether it is inside the stated shelf life.","Alloy and powder type prove the paste matches the process, using the classifications of IPC J-STD-005 for paste, IPC J-STD-006 for powder and IPC J-STD-004 for flux. Viscosity or printability data show the supplier measured a property the stencil actually depends on, and the safety data sheet covers handling hazards."]},{"h":"Bare PCB lots","p":["For bare boards, ask for the packing list, the certificate of conformance, the IPC-A-600 class or the purchase-order class, the bare-board test report, the finish type and thickness report, an impedance report where impedance is controlled, the material declaration, and moisture barrier packaging with a humidity indicator card where required. Quantity and panel type come from the packing list; inspection and test against the purchase order come from the certificate.","The class statement fixes the visual and dimensional acceptance level. The test report proves continuity and isolation were checked, the finish report proves the surface finish suits soldering or wire bonding, and the indicator card shows whether the moisture barrier package was compromised."]},{"h":"Accept, quarantine or reject","p":["Accept when purchase order, documents, packaging, lot identity, expiry and physical condition all match the receiving specification. Quarantine when the material may be usable but the evidence is incomplete or a limit was exceeded: a missing certificate, an activated humidity indicator card, a temperature excursion past the supplier's shipping limit, or an expiry that needs supplier retest. Reject when the material cannot be used safely or cannot be traced — wrong alloy, wrong powder type, wrong board finish, severe mechanical damage, contamination, counterfeit suspicion, or an expired lot without a valid retest.","Record the decision with its evidence. A verdict alone tells the next person nothing."]},{"h":"Write a record the supplier can act on","p":["A useful record carries the purchase order, part number, supplier lot, quantity received, quantity affected, the requirement or drawing clause, the observed condition, the measurement or photo reference, the disposition, the containment action and the requested corrective action. Objective wording states the lot, the requirement, the deviation and the impact.","Ask the supplier to confirm test data, shipping temperature and packaging condition, and whether the lot can be retested or reconditioned. \"Bad paste\" and \"damaged boards\" name no requirement and no lot."]},{"h":"Cold chain and expiry","p":["Paste and moisture-sensitive material need a time and temperature record, not just a date. For paste, compare manufacture date, expiry date, storage temperature and any time above the supplier's storage limit; many datasheets specify 2 °C to 10 °C, and your own datasheet decides. For components, check packaging, humidity indicator card and the floor-life clock under J-STD-033.","Exceed a control limit and the lot goes to quarantine pending supplier data. Accept it and mark it for use-first, then recheck expiry and packaging before opening. Storage temperature, shelf life and excursion limits vary by supplier, so read them from the datasheet rather than from habit."]}],"calc":{"task":"A solder paste lot has manufacture date 2025-01-01 and expiry 2025-07-01. It is received 2025-04-01. The shipping temperature logger shows 36 h above 10 °C. The supplier datasheet says the lot may be accepted without retest if cumulative time above 10 °C is less than 48 h. Determine the remaining shelf life and the incoming disposition.","solution":"First, confirm the lot number, manufacture date, expiry date, and storage instructions on the certificate of conformance. Second, calculate lot age at receipt: 2025-01-01 to 2025-04-01 is 90 days. Third, calculate remaining calendar shelf life: 2025-04-01 to 2025-07-01 is 91 days. Fourth, compare the temperature excursion to the supplier limit: 36 h is less than 48 h. Fifth, because the documents are complete, the lot is within expiry, and the excursion is below the supplier limit, accept the lot with use-first control and recheck the expiry and packaging before opening. If the excursion had been 48 h or more, or if the certificate of conformance were missing, quarantine the lot and request supplier retest data."},"defect":{"symptom":"Stencil printing shows paste balls, inconsistent deposit volume, and poor release from the stencil after a new paste lot is opened.","cause":"The lot was stored above the supplier's recommended temperature range for an extended period, causing flux separation or viscosity drift, and the incoming record did not capture the storage temperature or expiry status.","action":"Quarantine the lot, retain the container and shipping record, measure viscosity and printability against the supplier datasheet, and issue a supplier corrective action request with the lot number, storage temperature record, observed print defects, and request for batch test data and retest or replacement."},"takeaways":["Match the lot to its documents before opening it, because a lot that cannot be traced cannot be released however good it looks.","Read paste and moisture-sensitive records as time and temperature evidence, not as a date on a label.","Record the requirement, the observation and the evidence, so the supplier can answer with data instead of an apology."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Releasing a lot on a complete-looking certificate without checking the lot number","instead":"Compare the certificate lot number with the jar, reel or panel label, and hold the lot while they disagree."},{"mistake":"Ignoring a temperature excursion because the paste is still within expiry","instead":"Compare the excursion hours with the supplier's limit and quarantine anything at or above it."},{"mistake":"Writing the disposition without the supporting measurement","instead":"Attach the observed value, the photo reference and the clause it failed, so the record stands on its own."}],"terms":[{"term":"Cold chain","def":"The controlled temperature path a material follows from manufacture through storage and transport to use."},{"term":"Excursion","def":"A period in which a material left its specified storage or transport temperature range."},{"term":"First-in, first-out","def":"Consuming the oldest acceptable lot first so material does not expire in stores."},{"term":"Certificate of analysis","def":"The supplier's report of measured values for named properties of a specific lot."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m01l04"],"skills":["Lesson m01l04: Component verification: part number, date code, package, counterfeit red flags"]}},{"lessonId":"m01l06","moduleId":"m01","stageId":"incoming","title":"Bare PCB receipt: surface finish, planarity and warpage, packaging, shelf life","goals":[{"text":"Specify the documents that must accompany a received lot of components, paste or bare PCB and state what each one proves.","competencies":["K1"]},{"text":"Classify incoming-material findings as accept, quarantine or reject and write a record a supplier can act on.","competencies":["K1","K9"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-003","src-018","src-021","src-023"],"quiz":["q-m01l06-01","q-m01l06-02","q-m01l06-03","q-m01l06-04","q-m01l06-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m01l06.webp","alt":"A PCB with exaggerated warp, a planarity measurement setup with a height gauge, a surface finish cross-section and a sealed barrier package with desiccant","caption":"Bare PCB receipt: surface finish, planarity and warpage, packaging and shelf life"}]},{"h":"Receipt package and traceability","p":"A bare board lot arrives with a packing list, a purchase order reference, a lot or batch identifier and, where the purchase order requires it, a certificate of conformance or certificate of analysis. The packing list fixes what was shipped and how many; the purchase order reference fixes part number, revision, quantity and delivery condition; the lot identifier ties the panels to fabrication date and process records; the certificate records the supplier's verification against the purchase order and the acceptance criteria."},{"h":"Surface finish documentation","p":["State the finish unambiguously — immersion silver, immersion tin, organic solderability preservative, electroless nickel immersion gold, or hard gold on selected pads — and ask for the type, the thickness or coating class, and the supporting test report. Plated finishes may cite IPC-4101 for base material and finish requirements and IPC-6012 for qualified product performance.","Solderability splits in two: IPC/JEDEC J-STD-002 covers component solderability, while bare board solderability is commonly specified against IPC-6012 and the customer drawing. Check the finish against the assembly process, the reflow profile and the paste chemistry before release."]},{"h":"Planarity and warpage evidence","p":"The board drawing or IPC-6012 usually carries the planarity and warpage limits, with different acceptance for rectangular and irregular boards and for each class. Incoming inspection records the method it used: a flat reference surface, a feeler gauge, an optical flatness gauge or a coordinate measuring machine. Fine-pitch components, press-fit connectors and large area arrays may need tighter limits than IPC-6012 gives generically. Confirm the requirement against the placement machine datasheet, the stencil release behaviour and the reflow support method."},{"h":"Packaging and moisture control","p":"Package bare boards against mechanical damage, contamination, oxidation and moisture absorption: sealed moisture barrier bags with desiccant and a humidity indicator card, or rigid trays for fragile and high-value boards. The packaging should carry the seal date, the lot identifier and any moisture sensitivity level the supplier assigned. IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 is written for moisture-sensitive devices and is commonly referenced for this practice even when the item is a bare board. At receipt, check bag integrity, desiccant condition and the indicator card reading."},{"h":"Shelf life and floor life","p":"Shelf life is how long a material may stay in its original sealed packaging under stated storage conditions before requalification. Floor life is how long a moisture-sensitive item may stay out in factory ambient before it must be reconditioned or soldered. On bare boards the surface finish, the packaging integrity and the storage environment drive shelf life, while IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 provides the framework for level, floor life and reconditioning of moisture-sensitive parts. Compare the supplier's shelf life and storage temperature with your own stores and your assembly schedule."},{"h":"Incoming findings and disposition","p":["Classify every finding as accept, quarantine or reject. Accept releases the lot to stores or production because it meets the purchase order and the documented acceptance criteria. Quarantine holds a lot with a nonconformance that engineering, quality or the supplier may disposition — a missing report, questionable packaging, a borderline measurement. Reject applies to a failed critical requirement: wrong finish, a damaged moisture barrier with a failed indicator, warpage past the assembly limit.","Write the disposition so the supplier can fix the cause rather than only read the symptom."]},{"h":"Writing a supplier-actionable record","p":"A useful record names the purchase order, the lot identifier, the quantity inspected, the quantity affected, the requirement violated, the measured result, the acceptance limit, the standard or drawing reference and the requested action. Instead of \"boards are bent\", write: \"Lot 4471, 12 of 250 boards measured with twist above the drawing limit of IPC-6012 Class 3; requested action: return affected boards, provide root cause, and submit corrected flatness data for the next three lots.\" The supplier can then trace the lot, reproduce the measurement and prevent recurrence."}],"calc":{"task":"A lot of 500 bare PCBs is received with a drawing requirement that the surface finish be organic solderability preservative and that flatness meet IPC-6012 Class 3. Incoming inspection finds 18 boards with twist above the drawing limit, 1 moisture barrier bag with a failed humidity indicator, and a missing supplier certificate of conformance. Classify the lot and write the disposition.","solution":"Step 1: Identify the requirements. The purchase order requires organic solderability preservative finish, IPC-6012 Class 3 flatness, intact moisture barrier packaging, and a certificate of conformance. Step 2: Compare findings to requirements. The finish type is correct. Flatness fails on 18 boards. Packaging fails on 1 bag. Documentation fails because the certificate of conformance is missing. Step 3: Determine severity. Flatness failure affects assembly placement and reflow seating, so it is a product conformance issue. Packaging failure affects moisture control and shelf life, so it is a storage and reliability issue. Missing certificate of conformance affects traceability and acceptance evidence, so it is a documentation issue. Step 4: Classify the lot. The lot cannot be accepted as received. Quarantine the lot pending disposition. If the flatness failure is outside the drawing limit and cannot be dispositioned by engineering, reject the affected boards. If the packaging failure is isolated and the boards are otherwise within moisture limits, quarantine only the affected bag. Step 5: Write the supplier-actionable record. State purchase order, lot identifier, quantity received, quantity affected, measured result, IPC-6012 Class 3 reference, packaging failure, missing certificate of conformance, and requested actions: return or disposition the 18 boards, provide root cause for flatness, provide corrected flatness data for the next lots, replace the damaged bag or provide requalification evidence, and submit the missing certificate of conformance."},"defect":{"symptom":"A received lot of bare PCBs has a sealed moisture barrier bag, but the humidity indicator card shows a failed state and several boards show dull, discolored immersion silver pads.","cause":"The lot may have exceeded its sealed shelf life, the bag seal may have leaked, the desiccant may have been insufficient or saturated, or the boards may have been exposed to factory ambient conditions beyond the supplier-stated floor life. Discoloration can indicate oxidation or contamination of the immersion silver surface.","action":"Quarantine the lot. Record the lot identifier, seal date, humidity indicator reading, storage temperature, and number of affected boards. Verify the supplier-stated shelf life and storage conditions. If the boards are within the supplier requalification window and the surface finish is still solderable by your incoming test, request engineering disposition. If the surface finish fails solderability or the moisture exposure cannot be traced, reject the lot and ask the supplier for root cause, corrected packaging, and updated shelf-life documentation."},"takeaways":["Identify the surface finish and its thickness from the certificate, because the finish decides whether the reflow profile and paste chemistry will work.","Measure planarity and record the method, since a twist number means nothing without the surface and technique behind it.","Write the disposition against the requirement and the measured value, so the supplier can trace the lot and correct the cause."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Releasing boards whose moisture barrier bag failed the humidity indicator","instead":"Quarantine the affected bag and evaluate finish condition before any board reaches the line."},{"mistake":"Quoting warpage without the method behind it","instead":"Record the reference surface and gauge, so the supplier can reproduce the measurement."},{"mistake":"Accepting a finish change that matches the purchase order text but not the drawing","instead":"Compare finish type and thickness against the drawing, and quarantine on any mismatch."}],"terms":[{"term":"Warpage","def":"Departure of a board from flatness, measured as bow or twist against a reference surface."},{"term":"Shelf life","def":"The time a sealed material remains usable under stated storage conditions before requalification."},{"term":"Floor life","def":"The time a moisture-sensitive item may stay exposed to factory ambient before reconditioning or soldering."},{"term":"Organic solderability preservative","def":"An organic coating on copper that protects solderability and is displaced during soldering."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m01l05"],"skills":["Lesson m01l05: Solder paste, flux and adhesive receipt: alloy, powder, expiry, cold chain"]}},{"lessonId":"m01l07","moduleId":"m01","stageId":"incoming","title":"Nonconforming material at dock: quarantine, MRB, supplier notification","goals":[{"text":"Classify incoming-material findings as accept, quarantine or reject and write a record a supplier can act on.","competencies":["K1","K9"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-003","src-037"],"quiz":["q-m01l07-01","q-m01l07-02","q-m01l07-03","q-m01l07-04","q-m01l07-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m01l07.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: a locked quarantine cage with red hold tags, the MRB decision flow with use as is, rework, return to supplier and scrap branches feeding supplier notification, and a hold tag","caption":"Nonconforming material at the dock: quarantine, MRB and supplier notification"}]},{"h":"Dock decision basis","p":"Classify incoming material against the purchase order, the drawing, the specification and the incoming inspection plan. ISO 9001:2015 requires control of externally provided processes, products and services, so the dock record has to show the evidence behind an accept, a quarantine or a reject."},{"h":"Quarantine as a controlled hold","p":"Quarantine is a controlled hold on material that is suspect, incomplete or awaiting review. Segregate it physically or hold it in the system, label it with part number, lot, quantity, date and hold reason, and keep it traceable to the receiving transaction. The hold is what keeps the material out of production while the material review board or the quality function assesses risk."},{"h":"MRB inputs and dispositions","p":"A material review board reviews the nonconformance report, drawings, measurement data, photos, supplier lot history and downstream process risk. It may accept, rework, sort, use as-is with concession, return or scrap. The disposition names who approved it and what verification is required before release."},{"h":"Supplier notification","p":"Notification works when it is specific: part number, lot, purchase order, quantity received, quantity inspected, finding, specification requirement, measured result and evidence. Ask for containment, root cause, corrective action, and replacement or return authorisation according to the contract. \"Bad parts\" gives the supplier nothing to correct."},{"h":"Classification criteria","p":"Accept when the evidence shows conformance to the specified requirements. Quarantine when documentation, packaging, moisture, ESD, shelf life or measurement uncertainty leaves the status unclear. Reject when a confirmed finding affects fit, form, function, safety or a regulatory requirement, or when the quarantine cannot be resolved inside the planned disposition window."},{"h":"Record content and traceability","p":"A usable record ties the finding to the lot and to any downstream use: inspector, date, location, measurement method, calibration status, sample size, observed defects, disposition, approvals and closure evidence. For electronic components, record moisture-sensitive device status and verify it against the device datasheet and IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033, because the level drives the bake and floor-life decision."},{"h":"Closing the loop with the line","p":"Dock dispositions move the assembly schedule. A released lot goes to the line with its approved status and any special handling notes; a rejected lot triggers substitutes or a schedule change. Close the supplier corrective action only after containment, root cause and an effectiveness check are documented."}],"calc":{"task":"A lot of 1,000 chip resistors arrives on 20 reels. The incoming plan samples 4 reels and 100 units. Findings: 5 units are outside the specified resistance tolerance, 2 reels have damaged tape pockets, and 1 reel has a moisture indicator above the limit. The internal acceptance limit for electrical nonconformance is 2%. Classify each finding and the lot, then write a supplier record.","solution":"Step 1: Verify the purchase order, part number, lot code, quantity, packaging, labels, and calibration status of measuring equipment. Step 2: Record the sample basis: 4 reels sampled from 20 reels, and 100 units inspected. Step 3: Calculate the electrical defect rate: 5 units outside tolerance divided by 100 units inspected equals 5%. Step 4: Compare the electrical defect rate with the internal acceptance limit: 5% is greater than 2%, so the electrical finding is classified as reject unless the MRB accepts it based on documented risk. Step 5: Classify the damaged tape pockets on 2 reels as quarantine pending MRB because damaged pockets can cause dropped components, feeder jams, or misalignment. Step 6: Classify the moisture indicator exceedance on 1 reel as quarantine pending MRB; verify the device datasheet and IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 before any bake, reflow, or release decision. Step 7: Classify the lot as reject for the confirmed electrical nonconformance, with the full lot held under quarantine until MRB disposition. Step 8: Write the supplier record with part number, lot, purchase order, quantity received, quantity inspected, sample size, measured result, specification requirement, defect rate, photos of damaged tape and moisture indicator, affected reel numbers, requested containment, requested root cause, requested corrective action, and return or replacement authorization status."},"defect":{"symptom":"Small chip components tombstone on one side after reflow, and the feeder occasionally stalls on the same reel.","cause":"Incoming tape pockets are damaged and allow components to shift or fall, and the reel may have been exposed to handling damage before delivery.","action":"Quarantine the reel and lot, notify the supplier with photos and reel traceability, request packaging containment, and have the MRB decide whether to return, sort, or accept only after verified inspection."},"takeaways":["Hold suspect material under a labelled, traceable quarantine, because a verbal hold nobody can audit protects nothing.","Send the supplier the requirement, the measured result and the evidence, so the corrective action can address the cause.","Record who inspected, with which method and calibration, so the dock decision can be reproduced later."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Leaving quarantined material in the stores location with only a paper note","instead":"Segregate it physically or hold it in the system, and label part number, lot, quantity and reason."},{"mistake":"Notifying the supplier with a symptom only","instead":"State the requirement, the measurement and the affected reels or units, and ask for the specific action the contract allows."},{"mistake":"Closing a corrective action when the replacement arrives","instead":"Keep it open until containment, root cause and an effectiveness check are all documented."}],"terms":[{"term":"Material review board","def":"The cross-functional group that dispositions nonconforming material and records the basis for its decision."},{"term":"Quarantine","def":"The controlled holding of suspect or incomplete material until a disposition is approved."},{"term":"Concession","def":"A written approval to use material that does not meet the stated requirement."},{"term":"Containment","def":"Immediate action to stop nonconforming material from moving further into the process or to the customer."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m01l06"],"skills":["Lesson m01l06: Bare PCB receipt: surface finish, planarity and warpage, packaging, shelf life"]}},{"lessonId":"m02l01","moduleId":"m02","stageId":"storage","title":"Moisture inside a package: what happens during reflow and why","goals":[{"text":"Determine the MSL rating and remaining floor life of a device from its markings, bag data and reflow history.","competencies":["K1"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-003"],"quiz":["q-m02l01-01","q-m02l01-02","q-m02l01-03","q-m02l01-04","q-m02l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"Why moisture inside a package matters","p":["Most plastic packages are built from hygroscopic materials: mold compound, die attach, underfill and polymer substrates. All of them take up water vapour from the air around them, and how much depends on package size, material composition, storage temperature, relative humidity and exposure time. Reflow then vaporises that water fast, raises internal pressure and loads weak interfaces.","The damage shows up as delamination, package cracking, die attach voiding, wire lift or intermittent electrical failures that surface long after the board passed test."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 900 220' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='MSL floor life lifecycle'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace}</style><rect x='40' y='90' width='150' height='44' rx='10' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='rgba(83,209,204,.35)' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='250' y='90' width='150' height='44' rx='10' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='rgba(83,209,204,.35)' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='460' y='90' width='150' height='44' rx='10' fill='rgba(255,152,0,.1)' stroke='#ff9800' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='670' y='90' width='190' height='44' rx='10' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='rgba(83,209,204,.35)' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='190' y1='112' x2='250' y2='112' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#ar)'/><line x1='400' y1='112' x2='460' y2='112' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='610' y1='112' x2='670' y2='112' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><defs><marker id='ar' markerWidth='8' markerHeight='8' refX='6' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L6,3 L0,6' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.2'/></marker></defs><text x='115' y='117' fill='#cbd5e1' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>SEALED MBB</text><text x='325' y='117' fill='#cbd5e1' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>FLOOR CLOCK</text><text x='535' y='117' fill='#ff9800' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>BAKE IF EXCEEDED</text><text x='765' y='117' fill='#cbd5e1' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>REFLOW / COATING</text><text x='325' y='70' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>J-STD-033</text><text x='325' y='160' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>MSL 2/3: 1 yr / 168 h</text><text x='40' y='24' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' font-weight='600'>MOISTURE SENSITIVE DEVICE LIFECYCLE</text><text x='40' y='44' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12'>FLOOR LIFE STARTS THE MOMENT THE BAG IS OPENED</text></svg>","caption":"Floor-life values per J-STD-033 — see table below"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m02l01.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: package cross-section with moisture ingress, vapour pressure, delamination and popcorn crack through storage, preheat, reflow and cooling; a moisture barrier bag with desiccant and humidity indicator card; a floor-life clock","caption":"Moisture inside a package: ingress, vapour pressure at reflow, delamination and cracking; the barrier bag and the floor-life clock"}]},{"h":"How moisture enters the package","p":["Water enters through the package body and through seams, exposed die surfaces and porous interfaces. Diffusion is slow at room temperature and quickens as temperature rises. A device in a dry cabinet or a sealed moisture barrier bag with desiccant stays below a critical internal moisture level; once the bag opens, absorption starts from the factory environment.","Packages do not absorb at the same rate. Thin, large or strongly hygroscopic packages react faster than thick, small ones."]},{"h":"What happens during reflow","p":["Reflow heats the package body and its interfaces quickly. Water that has diffused in vaporises and expands, and where the vapour pressure exceeds the strength of an interface, the interface separates. The industry calls it the popcorn effect.","Visible damage may be a crack or a lifted lead, but the same event can leave delamination at the die attach, the mold compound or the substrate interface with nothing to see. An electrical test can pass at that point and fail later under thermal cycling or humidity stress."]},{"h":"MSL rating and floor life","p":["The moisture sensitivity level classifies how quickly a device absorbs moisture under defined ambient conditions. Floor life is the total ambient exposure a device may take before it must be baked before reflow. Under IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 the typical floor lives are MSL 1 unlimited, MSL 2 12 months, MSL 3 168 h, MSL 4 72 h, MSL 5 48 h, MSL 5a 24 h, and MSL 6 with no floor life, which makes the bake mandatory before use with the post-bake reflow window taken from the label.","These are package-level control limits, not predictions that a specific device will fail past them. They exist to stop moisture-related reflow damage before it starts."],"blocks":[{"t":"table","caption":"J-STD-033 floor life at <=30 °C / 60% RH","head":["MSL level","Floor life","Typical use"],"rows":[["1","Unlimited","Non-sensitive"],["2","12 months","Most QFP/QFN parts"],["2a","4 weeks",""],["3","168 hours","Fine-pitch, larger BGA"],["4","72 hours",""],["5","48 hours","Large BGA, thick packages"],["5a","24 hours",""],["6","Bake before use","No floor life: bake before use, reflow within the label limit"]]}]},{"h":"Reading markings and bag data","p":["The MSL rating may sit on the device label, the moisture barrier bag, the tape and reel label or the package datasheet. A missing or unclear marking means you use the datasheet or treat the device as the most restrictive applicable level until someone verifies it.","Bag data should carry the MSL, the humidity indicator card status, the desiccant condition and the bag opening date. Card thresholds are card-specific, so check the card datasheet and IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033. A card past its threshold says the internal environment is no longer acceptable for direct reflow, even when the calculated floor life has time left."]},{"h":"Using reflow history","p":["Floor life is cumulative ambient exposure, not time since the last bag opening. Under IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 a qualifying reflow completed inside the remaining floor life resets it. Floor life that expired before reflow means baking before reflow, and a humidity indicator card past its threshold means baking regardless of the remaining floor life.","Some programs also cap the number of reflows or require special handling after several passes. Check the package qualification, the customer procedure and IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 rather than assuming."]},{"h":"Practical storage and floor-life control","p":["Control starts with dry storage, sealed moisture barrier bags, desiccant and humidity indicator cards. A dry cabinet often runs at 5 % to 10 % RH, but only the 5 % RH or less band suspends the floor-life clock under J-STD-033 — a 10 % cabinet is comfortable and earns no credit. Confirm the setpoint against your equipment datasheet.","Check bake conditions, cabinet setpoints and reflow profiles against IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033, the package datasheet and your paste and equipment datasheets. Track bag opening time, card status, reflow time, bake time and any reflow count limit. When the next reflow will not happen inside the remaining floor life, bake first; visual inspection and electrical test will not tell you whether the package is dry."]}],"calc":{"task":"A device label says MSL 3. The moisture barrier bag was opened 100 h ago. The humidity indicator card has not exceeded its threshold. The device was reflowed 70 h after bag opening, and 40 h have elapsed since that reflow. Determine the MSL rating and remaining floor life.","solution":"1. Read the MSL rating from the marking: MSL 3. 2. Use the IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 floor life for MSL 3: 168 h. 3. Check the humidity indicator card: it has not exceeded its threshold, so the card does not force bake. 4. Check reflow history: the device was reflowed at 70 h after bag opening, which is within the 168 h floor life. 5. Under IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033, a qualifying reflow within the floor life resets the floor life. 6. Calculate remaining floor life after reflow: 168 h minus 40 h elapsed since reflow equals 128 h. 7. Result: MSL 3, remaining floor life 128 h, provided the reflow met the applicable profile and no reflow count limit applies."},"defect":{"symptom":"After reflow, some packages show package cracking, lifted leads, or popcorn-like deformation, while others pass electrical test but later fail with intermittent opens or delamination found by X-ray or acoustic microscopy.","cause":"MSL devices were exposed to ambient conditions beyond their floor life, or the humidity indicator card exceeded its threshold before reflow. Common line causes include bags left open too long, missing floor-life tracking, reflow scheduling delays, incorrect bake, or failure to reset floor life after a qualifying reflow.","action":"Quarantine affected lots. Verify MSL markings, bag opening time, humidity indicator card status, desiccant condition, reflow time, and bake records against IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033. Bake devices that exceeded floor life or humidity indicator card threshold before reflow. Review storage, bag opening, floor-life tracking, and reflow scheduling controls. Inspect suspect packages with X-ray or acoustic microscopy and update the process control plan to prevent recurrence."},"takeaways":["Absorbed moisture turns to vapour in reflow, so the failure mechanism is internal and often invisible to electrical test.","Treat floor life as cumulative ambient exposure, and reset it only through a qualifying reflow or an accepted bake.","A humidity indicator card past its threshold forces a bake even when the calculated floor life still has hours left."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Counting floor life from receipt or from the last reflow without checking the procedure","instead":"Count from the bag opening, and treat a reflow as a reset only where IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 and your procedure allow it."},{"mistake":"Relying on a 10 % RH dry cabinet to suspend floor life","instead":"Use the 5 % RH or less band for suspension, and record the setpoint you actually ran."},{"mistake":"Releasing a device that passed electrical test after moisture exposure","instead":"Inspect for delamination and bake the lot, because the electrical result does not show internal separation."}],"terms":[{"term":"Popcorn effect","def":"Package cracking or interface separation caused by moisture vaporising during reflow."},{"term":"Moisture sensitivity level","def":"The J-STD-020 classification of how much ambient exposure a non-hermetic package tolerates before reflow."},{"term":"Floor life","def":"The ambient exposure a moisture-sensitive device may accumulate before it must be baked."},{"term":"Delamination (package)","def":"Separation between layers inside a package, such as mold compound and die or substrate."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m01l07"],"skills":["Lesson m01l07: Nonconforming material at dock: quarantine, MRB, supplier notification"]}},{"lessonId":"m02l02","moduleId":"m02","stageId":"storage","title":"Reading MSL reality: J-STD-033 / J-STD-020 markings, barrier bag, HIC","goals":[{"text":"Determine the MSL rating and remaining floor life of a device from its markings, bag data and reflow history.","competencies":["K1"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-003","src-005","src-017"],"quiz":["q-m02l02-01","q-m02l02-02","q-m02l02-03","q-m02l02-04","q-m02l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m02l02.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: magnified MSL caution label, barrier bag with desiccant and humidity indicator card, HIC spots, photo of a sealed bag with a component tray and a floor-life clock that starts at bag opening","caption":"Reading moisture sensitivity markings: MSL label, barrier bag, HIC, desiccant and floor-life clock"}]},{"h":"MSL is a reflow-risk class","p":["J-STD-020 sorts non-hermetic surface mount devices into moisture sensitivity levels. The rating says how much ambient exposure a device tolerates before a reflow cycle is likely to damage the package. It says nothing about shelf life, ESD class or solderability.","Read the MSL code from the supplier label, the bag or the device marking, then apply the floor-life rule that J-STD-020 and the label give for that level."]},{"h":"Where the rating is marked","p":["J-STD-033 handling practice expects a moisture barrier bag labelled with the MSL, floor life, date and, where they apply, bake or exposure information. In practice the MSL may appear on the outer box, the reel label, the bag label or the package marking.","A missing or unreadable marking is a stop, not a guess. Check the purchase record, the supplier datasheet or your internal material specification, and treat the part as unknown until someone verifies it."]},{"h":"Floor life values and conditions","p":["IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 defines floor life at reference conditions of 30 °C and 60 % RH for the common MSL ratings, while J-STD-020 assigns the level itself. Typical values are MSL 1 unlimited, MSL 2 1 year, MSL 2a 4 weeks, MSL 3 168 h, MSL 4 72 h, MSL 5 48 h and MSL 5a 24 h; MSL 6 is special and normally requires bake before use.","Use the label value whenever it is more restrictive than the table, and check the current revision of J-STD-020, because the tables are updated."]},{"h":"Barrier bag, desiccant and HIC","p":["A moisture barrier bag slows moisture ingress; it does not create a permanent dry environment. J-STD-033 practice adds desiccant and a humidity indicator card to show whether the interior is still inside the intended humidity range. Read the card against the card manufacturer's scale.","A spot past its threshold, a punctured bag or a compromised seal means the contents count as exposed, so evaluate bake or quarantine before reflow."]},{"h":"When the floor-life clock starts","p":"The clock starts when the moisture barrier bag opens, not when the device arrives. Time on the line, in a dry cabinet or on a feeder counts as ambient exposure unless your controlled storage procedure explicitly grants credit. A completed bake per J-STD-033 can restart the clock where your procedure accepts the bake record; otherwise keep counting from the original open time."},{"h":"Reflow history and bake decisions","p":["Reflow after the floor life has expired drives moisture into package interfaces and causes damage. A reflow completed while floor life remained is acceptable from a moisture standpoint, but the device can reabsorb moisture afterwards.","Before a second reflow, check the exposure since the last dry event — bag opening or an accepted bake, and confirm whether your procedure treats a completed reflow as a dry event too. Verify the profile against your paste and equipment datasheets for peak temperature and time above liquidus rather than assuming a universal value. Where exposure passed the floor life before any reflow, bake first and record bake temperature, time and device count."]},{"h":"Putting the data together","p":["Determine remaining floor life by combining the MSL rating, the floor-life value from J-STD-020 or the label, the bag open date and time, the HIC condition and the reflow or bake records. When the sources conflict, take the most restrictive value and quarantine the material until the discrepancy is resolved.","That keeps the decision traceable and stops it resting on one marking or one indicator."]}],"calc":{"task":"A bag label states MSL 3 with a floor life of 168 h per J-STD-020. The bag was opened E hours ago. One reflow was completed at R hours after opening. Determine the remaining floor life and whether a bake is required before a second reflow.","solution":"Confirm the floor life F from the label or J-STD-020; here F = 168 h. Calculate remaining floor life before the next reflow: remaining = F - E. Check the first reflow: if R was less than F, the reflow occurred within floor life and no bake was required for that reflow. If E has reached or passed F, the floor life expired before the second reflow; bake per J-STD-033 or your approved procedure before reflow. If the HIC shows an exceeded threshold or the bag was damaged, treat the device as exposed even if E is less than F, and bake or quarantine per your procedure. Record the bake, reflow profile, and exposure time so the next decision starts from a known dry event."},"defect":{"symptom":"After reflow, boards show intermittent opens, lifted leads, or package cracks on a few MSL devices, while nearby parts look normal.","cause":"The affected devices exceeded their floor life before reflow, or the barrier bag/HIC condition was not checked, so moisture remained in the package and expanded during reflow.","action":"Quarantine the lot, verify the MSL label, bag open time, HIC, and reflow records, bake per J-STD-033 or the manufacturer's limits, then reflow a sample and inspect. Correct the storage control by adding a bag-open timestamp and HIC check before kitting."},"takeaways":["Read the MSL from the label and the floor life from J-STD-020 or the label, and apply the more restrictive of the two.","Start the floor-life clock at bag opening, and count line time, cabinet time and feeder time as ambient exposure unless your procedure grants credit.","Combine MSL, open time, HIC condition and reflow history into one record, because any single indicator can mislead."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Treating the MSL rating as a shelf-life statement","instead":"Read it as a reflow-risk class and take shelf life from the material specification."},{"mistake":"Using a barrier bag as proof the parts are dry after a seal failure","instead":"Treat contents as exposed and bake or quarantine before reflow."},{"mistake":"Taking the label value when the standard table is stricter","instead":"Use the most restrictive value and record which source you followed."}],"terms":[{"term":"Humidity indicator card","def":"A card that shows the humidity reached inside a barrier bag by a colour change at defined thresholds."},{"term":"Moisture barrier bag","def":"A foil-laminate bag that slows moisture ingress and carries the MSL and exposure data."},{"term":"Reference condition","def":"The 30 °C and 60 % RH ambient at which J-STD-033 floor lives are defined."},{"term":"Bake","def":"Controlled heating that drives absorbed moisture out of a package so the floor-life clock can restart."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m02l01"],"skills":["Lesson m02l01: Moisture inside a package: what happens during reflow and why"]}},{"lessonId":"m02l03","moduleId":"m02","stageId":"storage","title":"Floor-life accounting: clock start, accumulation, reset rules","goals":[{"text":"Determine the MSL rating and remaining floor life of a device from its markings, bag data and reflow history.","competencies":["K1"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-003"],"quiz":["q-m02l03-01","q-m02l03-02","q-m02l03-03","q-m02l03-04","q-m02l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m02l03.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: a timeline where exposure accumulates across three bag openings, pauses in a dry cabinet and resets to zero at a bake, a reel with a handwritten exposure log, and a start / accumulate / pause / reset rules table","caption":"Floor-life accounting: clock start, accumulation across exposure events, the dry-cabinet pause and the bake reset"}]},{"h":"Floor life is an exposure budget","p":["Floor life is the cumulative time a moisture-sensitive device may stay outside its moisture barrier bag in factory conditions that J-STD-033 treats as no more than 30 °C and no more than 60 % RH. It is not shelf life, not a reflow count, not solderability life and not a quality rating. The budget exists to limit moisture uptake before reflow.","The clock starts at the first exposure to ambient air, which usually means bag opening, but it also starts when a device leaves a sealed bag, when the bag is punctured, or when the device otherwise meets uncontrolled air. From there it accumulates through storage, kitting, feeder loading, placement, reflow, inspection, test and any other open-air handling until a qualifying reset is performed."]},{"h":"Where the MSL rating comes from","p":["The moisture sensitivity level normally shows on the device marking, the tape-and-reel label, the moisture barrier bag label or the datasheet. Where sources disagree, account with the most restrictive valid source and then resolve the discrepancy with the supplier or the device owner.","J-STD-033 assigns floor life by level: MSL 1 unlimited under the standard's storage condition, MSL 2 12 months, MSL 2a 4 weeks, MSL 3 168 h, MSL 4 72 h, MSL 5 48 h, MSL 5a 24 h, and MSL 6 bake before use. Those values start the arithmetic; they never replace tracking the actual exposure."]},{"h":"Clock start is exposure, not discovery","p":["In storage the practical clock start is the recorded bag-open time or the first verified exposure. A missing bag-open time leaves the exposure uncertain, so treat the device conservatively until the exposure can be bounded.","An intact-looking bag can still hide a compromised seal, a puncture, or desiccant that has been exposed for an unknown period. The humidity indicator card and desiccant condition support the assessment but do not replace a time record. Prior exposure before the device reached storage belongs in the cumulative total; where it cannot be bounded, use the nonconforming material process rather than assuming a full MSL floor life remains."]},{"h":"All open-air time accumulates","p":["Floor life accumulates whenever the device sits outside a compliant moisture barrier bag in conditions covered by J-STD-033: on the floor, in a feeder, on the placement machine, in the reflow oven, on the conveyor, in inspection, and waiting between operations. Reflow is not a separate charge; the open-air time around it is what counts.","Multiple reflow passes add the open-air time of each pass, so the cumulative exposure has to be tracked pass by pass. Where the storage environment is hotter or more humid than the J-STD-033 reference condition, the simple table may not be enough — use the device datasheet, supplier guidance and your facility's controlled storage limits."]},{"h":"Reset needs a qualifying bake","p":["Putting a device back in a bag does not reset anything. J-STD-033 allows a reset after a qualifying bake performed to the applicable bake table for the package and MSL, followed by repacking with desiccant and a humidity indicator card where required.","Record the bake with start time, end time, temperature and the responsible person or system record. A bake that did not follow the applicable J-STD-033 condition resets nothing, and neither does one on a package already exposed past the point where evaluation is required. MSL 6 devices need a bake before use, so a recent bag opening gives them no usable floor life."]},{"h":"Records that support traceable accounting","p":["The minimum useful record is part number, lot or date code, MSL, bag-open time, cumulative open-air time, storage temperature and humidity, desiccant status, humidity indicator card status, any bake records, and the reflow or other handling history. A spreadsheet suits a small operation; an MES or WMS record is better because it cuts manual error and preserves traceability.","Whatever the tool, keep one time unit and show the calculation method on the record. Where storage is not controlled to the J-STD-033 reference condition, record the actual conditions and the basis for any adjustment or quarantine."]},{"h":"Remaining floor life by subtraction","p":"Remaining floor life equals the MSL floor life from J-STD-033 minus the cumulative open-air time already used. A positive result allows continued use only if the remainder covers the planned process and the environment stays inside the applicable limits. A non-positive result means bake before reflow, or disposition under your own procedure. After a valid bake and reset, recalculate from the reset point with the full MSL floor life."},{"h":"Conservative, documented decisions","p":["In storage the job is to stop unknown exposure from becoming an uncontrolled reflow risk. With incomplete records, bound the exposure, quarantine the material and decide between bake, supplier evaluation and scrap. Appearance, a fresh desiccant packet or a new humidity indicator card prove nothing on their own.","A short reflow pass is not negligible when the device has already used most of its floor life. Check the applicable J-STD-033 revision, the device datasheet and your facility's controlled storage limits, because handling times and conditions vary by site and product."]}],"calc":{"task":"A tape-and-reel is marked MSL 3. The bag label shows the bag was opened 100 h ago. The MES shows 6 h of open-air reflow handling. Storage remained within the J-STD-033 reference condition of no more than 30°C and no more than 60%RH. Determine the MSL rating and remaining floor life.","solution":"Step 1: Identify the MSL from the marking and bag label. Both sources indicate MSL 3. Step 2: Look up the floor life for MSL 3. J-STD-033 gives 168 h for MSL 3. Step 3: Add the recorded open-air exposure. Bag-open storage exposure is 100 h. Open-air reflow handling is 6 h. Total cumulative open-air time is 100 h + 6 h = 106 h. Step 4: Subtract cumulative exposure from the MSL floor life. Remaining floor life is 168 h - 106 h = 62 h. Step 5: Interpret the result. The device has 62 h of remaining floor life under the stated conditions. If the planned process cannot be completed within that time, or if the exposure record is later found to be incomplete, the device should be evaluated for bake or quarantine before reflow."},"defect":{"symptom":"After reflow, multiple units from the same lot show package cracking, delamination, or popcorn-like damage, while units from another lot with the same part number and reflow profile pass inspection.","cause":"The failed lot had its floor-life clock started when the moisture barrier bag was opened, but the open-air time was not fully recorded. Storage time, feeder time, and reflow handling time were not added to the cumulative exposure, so the lot was reflowed after its J-STD-033 floor life had already been consumed.","action":"Quarantine the remaining lot and any related lots with incomplete exposure records. Verify the bag-open time, storage conditions, reflow handling time, and any bake records. If the cumulative exposure cannot be bounded, treat the material as requiring bake or supplier evaluation before reflow. Correct the storage record so that bag-open time, cumulative open-air time, and reset events are captured in the MES or WMS."},"takeaways":["Budget floor life as cumulative open-air time from the first exposure, not as shelf life and not as time since the last bag.","Reset the clock only with a qualifying bake to the J-STD-033 table for that package and MSL, followed by repacking.","Record bag opening, cumulative exposure and every reset in one traceable system, so the next shift inherits a number instead of an assumption."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Restarting the clock because the device went back into a sealed bag","instead":"Reset only after a qualifying bake, and record the bake temperature, time and operator."},{"mistake":"Adding bag-open hours but forgetting feeder, placement and reflow handling","instead":"Track every open-air interval, because the sum is what the floor-life table is compared against."},{"mistake":"Assuming a fresh desiccant packet restores the budget","instead":"Bound or evaluate the prior exposure; desiccant protects the future, not the past."}],"terms":[{"term":"Cumulative exposure","def":"The total time a device has spent outside a compliant moisture barrier bag since its last valid reset."},{"term":"Qualifying bake","def":"A bake performed to the J-STD-033 condition for the package and MSL, recorded and followed by proper repacking."},{"term":"Reset point","def":"The bake completion or dry-event moment from which floor life is counted again."},{"term":"Moisture barrier bag","def":"The sealed, desiccant-filled bag that suspends floor-life accumulation while the device stays inside it."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m02l02"],"skills":["Lesson m02l02: Reading MSL reality: J-STD-033 / J-STD-020 markings, barrier bag, HIC"]}},{"lessonId":"m02l04","moduleId":"m02","stageId":"storage","title":"Baking and dry cabinets: when required, conditions by package, records","goals":[{"text":"Decide whether a batch must be baked, specify the bake/dry-storage condition and justify it by package type.","competencies":["K1"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-003"],"quiz":["q-m02l04-01","q-m02l04-02","q-m02l04-03","q-m02l04-04","q-m02l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m02l04.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: a nitrogen dry cabinet with reels, a bake oven with a ramp-hold-cool curve, thin, medium and thick package outlines, and a blank bake log","caption":"Baking and dry cabinets: when required, conditions by package thickness, and the records"}]},{"h":"The bake decision starts with package and exposure","p":["Bake or not is not a question about the part name. Take the moisture sensitivity level, the package type, whether the moisture barrier bag was opened, dry pack integrity, humidity indicator card status, desiccant condition and the planned time to reflow together.","A batch that will be consumed inside the remaining floor life in qualified dry storage may not need baking at all. Where exposure, cabinet performance or bag condition cannot be proven, treat the batch as exposed and bake or quarantine it. The person who controls the lot record makes the call; the line operator alone does not."]},{"h":"Floor life by MSL","p":["For plastic packages, IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033D assigns moisture sensitivity levels and floor life. Under typical ambient conditions MSL 3 has 168 h, MSL 4 has 72 h, MSL 5 has 48 h and MSL 5a has 24 h; higher levels follow supplier-specified conditions. Floor life begins when the dry pack opens or when the package meets ambient air.","Check the controlled revision and any supplier limit, because some suppliers shorten floor life for large packages, high moisture content or long reflow profiles."]},{"h":"Dry storage suspends or extends exposure","p":["IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033D recognises dry storage at 5 % RH or less as the condition that suspends the floor-life clock: below that humidity the device gains no moisture, so exposure stops accumulating. A common 10 % RH cabinet is not equivalent. It slows the clock instead of stopping it and leaves parts on a shortened budget, which makes 10 % RH a cheaper mitigation for devices with budget to spare and never a substitute for the 5 % RH requirement.","Calibrate, alarm and log the dry cabinet, and treat the qualified limit as the one written in your procedure. A cabinet out of range means the time inside it counts as floor life. Keep reels in sealed bags or dry cabinets after opening, and for trays and tubes use sealed containers with desiccant and a humidity indicator card when no cabinet is available."]},{"h":"Bake conditions by package type","p":["Read the bake table in IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033D for the package type, then the component or tape datasheet. Bake time in J-STD-033 Table 4-1 is indexed to package body thickness and to the moisture sensitivity level, with separate columns for floor life exceeded by more than 72 h and by 72 h or less, so all three variables come off the table before anyone quotes a time.","Tape and reel must not be baked above 40 °C, because carrier tape and cover tape soften and deform. The 40 °C column is therefore long: for bodies up to 1.4 mm it runs 8 days at MSL 2 up to 22 days at MSL 5a, and for 1.4 to 2.0 mm 25 to 79 days. A 48 h or 168 h bake at 40 °C will not dry a reel.","Loose parts in trays or tubes can use the 125 °C column, again by thickness and MSL: 5 to 16 h up to 1.4 mm, 18 to 48 h at 1.4 to 2.0 mm and 48 h at 2.0 to 4.5 mm, and only where the tray, tube and component materials are rated for it.","Table 4-2 is a different table. It covers the supplier dry-pack bake before packing at a fixed 24 h maximum exposure time, and its 125 °C figures — 7 to 28 h up to 1.4 mm and 18 to 48 h above that — must never be quoted as a user rebake recipe.","Above 90 °C the cumulative bake time is limited to 96 h unless the supplier states otherwise, because intermetallic growth and solderability loss accumulate. Check the tape, tray, tube and equipment datasheets: tape can soften, trays can warp and some coatings blister."]},{"h":"PCBs and THT assemblies","p":["Bare boards and THT components absorb moisture too, but the bake rule follows laminate, coating and package. Use the board supplier's bake condition; common practice is 105 °C to 125 °C for 2 h to 24 h, longer for thick boards or high moisture.","Apply the same MSL logic to THT plastic packages as to SMT. Bake before assembly when the board or component passed its qualified exposure or shows moisture indicators, and confirm that the bake temperature will not damage solder mask, silkscreen or plated features."]},{"h":"Records that make the decision auditable","p":["Record lot or reel ID, MSL, package type, dry pack open date and time, humidity indicator card reading, desiccant status, dry cabinet RH, bake start and end, bake temperature, controlled bake humidity, operator and disposition.","A batch released without bake records its remaining floor life and the planned reflow window. A baked batch records the recipe and any reflow qualification result. Keep the record with the lot so the next shift can see whether the floor life still holds."]},{"h":"Bake or quarantine","p":["Bake when the batch passed floor life, when the dry pack or desiccant failed, when the humidity indicator card is above its threshold, or when the dry cabinet ran out of range. Quarantine when the MSL is unknown, the package is damaged, the bake recipe is not qualified for the package, or the device has already been reflowed and the supplier requires requalification.","Never bake a package that cannot take the temperature or the time; use qualified lower-temperature dry storage or return the material to the supplier. The purpose is to stop moisture flashing in reflow, not to add a handling step."]}],"calc":{"task":"A batch of IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033D MSL 4 ICs in tape and reel was opened 50 h ago. The dry cabinet is not qualified for floor-life suspension, so all storage time counts. The planned reflow window starts in 30 h. Decide whether bake is required and specify a tape-and-reel bake condition.","solution":"1. Use IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033D floor life for MSL 4: 72 h. 2. Counted exposure = 50 h. 3. Remaining floor life = 72 h - 50 h = 22 h. 4. Planned delay = 30 h. Since 30 h exceeds 22 h, bake is required before reflow. 5. A tape-and-reel bake cannot exceed 40°C, and at 40°C the J-STD-033 Table 4-1 time for a body up to 1.4 mm is about 9 days, which does not fit a 30 h window. Either move the reflow inside the remaining floor life, suspend the clock in a qualified dry cabinet at 5% RH or less, or de-reel, bake loose parts at 125°C for 7 h and re-reel; verify the chosen route against the J-STD-033D bake table, the tape datasheet and the reflow window. 6. Record lot ID, bake start and end, temperature, operator, and reflow qualification if required."},"defect":{"symptom":"After reflow, several QFN packages show lifted leads, voided solder joints, and one package has a visible crack; the lot had been stored in a dry cabinet with a logged RH of 12% while the setpoint was 5%.","cause":"The IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033D MSL 4 floor life was exceeded because the dry cabinet excursion was not treated as exposure; moisture in the package flashed during reflow.","action":"Quarantine the lot, verify MSL and open date, bake per a qualified tape-and-reel recipe, requalify reflow, repair the cabinet, and add alarm and log review."},"takeaways":["Read bake time from J-STD-033 Table 4-1 by package thickness, MSL and how far floor life was exceeded, never from memory.","Respect the 40 °C ceiling for tape and reel, and expect days rather than hours at that temperature.","Suspend floor life only at 5 % RH or less; a 10 % RH cabinet slows the clock without earning any credit."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Applying a tray bake recipe to a tape-and-reel lot","instead":"Keep reels at or below 40 °C, and use de-reeling or a qualified dry cabinet when the bake window is too long."},{"mistake":"Quoting Table 4-2 as a user rebake recipe","instead":"Table 4-2 is the supplier dry-pack bake before packing; take the user recipe from Table 4-1."},{"mistake":"Treating a 12 % RH cabinet excursion as suspended floor life","instead":"Count the excursion as exposure and bake or quarantine the lot."}],"terms":[{"term":"Dry pack","def":"The moisture barrier bag, desiccant and humidity indicator card that protect a moisture-sensitive device before use."},{"term":"Floor-life suspension","def":"The stop of floor-life accumulation that J-STD-033 grants at 5 % RH or less in qualified dry storage."},{"term":"Cumulative bake time","def":"The total time a package has spent above 90 °C, limited because solderability degrades with it."},{"term":"Humidity indicator card","def":"A card whose colour spots show the peak humidity inside a dry pack."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m02l03"],"skills":["Lesson m02l03: Floor-life accounting: clock start, accumulation, reset rules"]}},{"lessonId":"m02l05","moduleId":"m02","stageId":"storage","title":"The ESD control program: EPA, grounding, straps, mats, ionizers, packaging","goals":[{"text":"Audit an ESD protected area against a control checklist and rank the gaps by risk.","competencies":["K1"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-012","src-027","src-028","src-029","src-030","src-031","src-032","src-033","src-034","src-035","src-036"],"quiz":["q-m02l05-01","q-m02l05-02","q-m02l05-03","q-m02l05-04","q-m02l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m02l05.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: plan view of an EPA bench with dissipative work surface, wrist strap, floor mat, common ground point, ionizer, shielding bag, conductive tote and signage; an operator at the bench; a daily checks table","caption":"The ESD control program: protected area boundary, grounding, straps, mats, ionizers and packaging"}]},{"h":"Program scope and storage assumptions","p":["An ESD control program is a management system, not a bag of gadgets. In storage the protected area is defined by the materials handled, the sensitivity of the parts, the packaging in use, and the people and equipment that move them. ANSI/ESD S20.20 and IEC 61340-5-1 both demand a documented program with written procedures, training, qualification and periodic audit.","Start the storage audit with the control plan, then check that the physical area matches it: EPA boundaries, entry controls, grounding provisions, packaging inventory, ionizer coverage where needed, and records. A missing or stale plan is the first gap, and it is programmatic rather than hardware."]},{"h":"Grounding and equipotential bonding","p":["Grounding in an EPA removes charge and holds conductive items at a common potential. The reference must trace to a building electrical ground or another qualified point, and the bonding network must be continuous and inspectable. ANSI/ESD S6.1 covers grounding and equipotential bonding; use it to confirm that racks, carts, shelving, tables and floor systems are bonded as the control plan requires.","Storage failures are usually the subtle ones: a cart or rack grounded at one point but isolated by casters, plastic feet, painted contact surfaces or a broken bonding jumper. Measure or inspect by the program method, and check the result against the equipment datasheet and site electrical safety requirements."]},{"h":"Personnel grounding","p":["Personnel grounding works through wrist straps, footwear and floor systems, or a combination, depending on the task and the control plan. ANSI/ESD S1.1 governs wrist straps, which suit static controlled workstations; footwear and floor systems are qualified under ANSI/ESD STM97.1 and ANSI/ESD STM97.2.","Wrist straps are impractical while moving pallets, but they still belong at receiving inspection, kitting and rework benches. Check that the chosen method suits the task, that the strap or footwear is worn correctly, that the floor is qualified for that footwear, and that the system is tested at the required interval. Where the plan allows an exception, document the risk basis and the compensating controls."]},{"h":"Mats, surfaces and storage fixtures","p":["Static dissipative or conductive mats cover benches and staging surfaces where people handle sensitive items. ANSI/ESD STM4.1 covers static dissipative worksurfaces, having superseded S4.1, and ANSI/ESD STM11.11 characterises surface resistance or resistivity.","In storage, mats get misused: laid on insulating shelves, grounded through a damaged cord, or treated as general work surfaces with no defined ground path. Verify that the mat connects to the EPA ground reference through the required resistor or bonding method, that the connection is intact, and that insulating packaging, tape or plastic trays do not bypass it. Check resistance limits against the mat datasheet and the site control plan."]},{"h":"Ionization","p":["Ionizers neutralise charge on isolated conductors and insulators that cannot be grounded. ANSI/ESD STM3.1 covers ionization, and performance qualification and periodic verification are judged against its offset-voltage and discharge-time limits; it is the ESDA counterpart of IEC 61340-4-7. Footwear and flooring, by contrast, are qualified under ANSI/ESD STM97.1 and STM97.2.","In storage, ionizers usually sit at unpacking, kitting or inspection stations where plastic bags, foam, labels and tape generate charge. Check placement, airflow, balance and maintenance: ionization does nothing if the ion stream is blocked, the emitter points are dirty, or the unit is out of balance. Verify balance and discharge time against the ionizer datasheet and the program acceptance criteria."]},{"h":"Packaging and storage containers","p":["Packaging is a primary storage control, because parts may sit in a container for hours or days. ANSI/ESD S541 covers ESD packaging, including shielding bags, conductive containers and closure methods.","Confirm that the packaging class matches part sensitivity and the storage environment, that shielding bags are closed and undamaged, and that containers suit the EPA grounding scheme. Storage frequently gets this wrong by putting a shielding bag on an insulating shelf and trusting it against every field. Shielding reduces field exposure; the package still has to be handled and stored as it was qualified. Check package type, closure and storage configuration against the packaging datasheet and the control plan."]},{"h":"Audit method and risk ranking","p":["A useful audit follows a checklist tied to the control plan and records evidence: photos, serial numbers, test dates, operator observations. Rank each gap by exposure, sensitivity and detectability.","High risk means many sensitive parts affected, repeated daily, undetected downstream. Medium risk is a local gap with limited exposure or partial detection. Low risk is documentation or housekeeping with little direct charge-transfer path. Deliver a corrective action list with owner, due date and verification method rather than a list of nonconformances."]}],"calc":{"task":"Audit a storage EPA with 12 checklist items. Each item is scored 0 to 3 for control strength, where 0 is absent, 1 is present but unverified, 2 is verified but has a minor gap, and 3 is fully compliant. Exposure is scored 1 to 5 based on daily part movement through the control. Sensitivity is scored 1 to 5 based on the most sensitive part family stored in the area. Calculate a risk score for each gap as (3 - control strength) x exposure x sensitivity, then rank the top three gaps.","solution":"Step 1: Record the control strength, exposure, and sensitivity for each gap. Example gap A: grounding jumper on rack missing, control strength 0, exposure 5, sensitivity 4. Risk score = (3 - 0) x 5 x 4 = 60. Example gap B: wrist strap tester not used for one shift, control strength 1, exposure 3, sensitivity 4. Risk score = (3 - 1) x 3 x 4 = 24. Example gap C: ionizer balance record missing, control strength 1, exposure 4, sensitivity 3. Risk score = (3 - 1) x 4 x 3 = 24. Example gap D: shielding bag closure not inspected at receiving, control strength 2, exposure 5, sensitivity 5. Risk score = (3 - 2) x 5 x 5 = 25. Example gap E: mat ground cord damaged at one bench, control strength 0, exposure 2, sensitivity 5. Risk score = (3 - 0) x 2 x 5 = 30. Step 2: Rank by risk score. Gap A is highest at 60. Gap E is next at 30. Gap D is next at 25. Gaps B and C tie at 24 and can be ranked by exposure or sensitivity; if exposure is prioritized, Gap C ranks above Gap B. Step 3: Convert to actions. Gap A requires immediate bonding repair and retest. Gap E requires bench isolation and mat ground repair. Gap D requires receiving inspection update and package closure check. Step 4: Verify closure by re-audit or test record, not only by visual confirmation."},"defect":{"symptom":"Incoming inspection finds intermittent ESD damage on a small number of connectors from a storage rack, while the same part family is acceptable when staged on a grounded bench.","cause":"The rack is bonded at one corner, but the shelf surface is painted or coated, and parts are placed in shielding bags that are not closed. The rack appears grounded, but the bags and shelf create an isolated charge path, and the open bags allow field exposure during movement.","action":"Isolate the rack, verify the bonding path to the EPA ground reference, clean or replace the shelf contact surfaces, require closed shielding bags for stored parts, and requalify the rack storage configuration against the control plan. Add a rack bonding check to the shift audit and verify closure with a follow-up inspection of the affected part family."},"takeaways":["Audit the storage EPA against its own control plan first, because a stale plan makes every downstream check meaningless.","Verify grounding by measurement to the reference, since a rack that looks bonded can be isolated by paint, casters or a broken jumper.","Rank gaps by exposure, sensitivity and detectability, and hand back actions with owners rather than a list of findings."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Treating a grounded-looking rack as bonded","instead":"Measure the bonding path to the EPA reference and inspect contact surfaces for paint or insulation."},{"mistake":"Treating a dry cabinet or an unmonitored ionizer as a substitute for grounding","instead":"Qualify each control to its own standard and keep the test records with the equipment."},{"mistake":"Closing an audit finding on a visual check","instead":"Verify closure with a repeat test or re-audit record."}],"terms":[{"term":"ESD protected area","def":"A defined space in which all conductive items and personnel are bonded and sensitive items are handled under documented controls."},{"term":"Equipotential bonding","def":"Connecting conductive items so they sit at the same potential and cannot discharge into each other."},{"term":"Ionizer","def":"Equipment that neutralises static charge by delivering balanced positive and negative ions to the air."},{"term":"Shielding bag","def":"Packaging that limits electrostatic field exposure to the parts inside it."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m02l04"],"skills":["Lesson m02l04: Baking and dry cabinets: when required, conditions by package, records"]}},{"lessonId":"m02l06","moduleId":"m02","stageId":"storage","title":"Case: expired floor life found mid-lot","goals":[{"text":"Decide whether a batch must be baked, specify the bake/dry-storage condition and justify it by package type.","competencies":["K1"]},{"text":"Contain a moisture or ESD exposure breach and define the traceability range.","competencies":["K1","K9"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-003","src-005","src-012","src-017","src-035"],"quiz":["q-m02l06-01","q-m02l06-02","q-m02l06-03","q-m02l06-04","q-m02l06-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m02l06.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: a partially used reel on a feeder with a floor-life expired tag, a clock past its limit, the stop / quarantine / bake / MRB / scrap decision flow and a containment checklist","caption":"Case: expired floor life found mid-lot — stop the feeder, quarantine, then bake / MRB / scrap"}]},{"h":"Floor life is a clock, not a label","p":["A moisture-sensitive device starts consuming floor life when its moisture barrier bag opens and the device meets ambient air. The clock runs under the moisture sensitivity level assigned by IPC/JEDEC J-STD-020 and the handling rules of IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033, which defines floor life at reference conditions of 30 °C and 60 % RH — so a hot, humid stores area burns the clock faster than a controlled dry cabinet.","An expired clock does not make the batch scrap. It makes it a process deviation that has to be contained before reflow. The first questions are whether the exposure history is known, whether the remaining floor life covers the planned reflow, and whether the package type can be baked safely."]},{"h":"Deciding whether the batch must be baked","p":"Compare elapsed floor life with the floor life for the device level in IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033. If elapsed time exceeds the allowed floor life and reflow is planned, bake before reflow unless the component manufacturer provides a documented exception. If dry storage is used, IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 uses a dry storage target of 5% RH or less; if the cabinet runs above that, treat the devices as exposed and recalculate floor life. If the device is classified as the lowest moisture sensitivity level under IPC/JEDEC J-STD-020, baking is usually not required unless contamination, handling damage, or a customer requirement says otherwise. If floor life has not been exceeded and dry storage is compliant, the batch can normally proceed, but the storage record must be complete."},{"h":"Bake conditions by package type","p":["IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 indexes bake conditions to the carrier, the package body thickness, the moisture sensitivity level and the maximum temperature the component allows.","For loose parts in trays, tubes or waffle packs whose carriers and components are rated for it, the 125 °C column of Table 4-1 applies: 5 to 16 h for a body up to 1.4 mm, 18 to 48 h for 1.4 to 2.0 mm and 48 h for 2.0 to 4.5 mm, with the longer times at higher MSL and when floor life was exceeded by more than 72 h.","The 24 h figure sometimes quoted for a 125 °C bake is the supplier dry-pack condition from Table 4-2, not a user bake, so it must not become a production recipe. For tape and reel, J-STD-033 provides the lower-temperature long bake at 40 °C — 8 to 22 days for a body up to 1.4 mm and 25 to 79 days for 1.4 to 2.0 mm, by MSL — because the tape, reel, labels and cover tape cannot take more heat. Cumulative bake time above 90 °C is limited to 96 h unless the supplier states otherwise.","Justify the condition by package type: polymer tape and reels deform, labels lift and cover tape shifts at high temperature. Check the chosen condition against the component datasheet, the carrier datasheet and the oven capability."]},{"h":"Dry storage after bake","p":"After baking, store the batch in a dry cabinet at 5% RH or less under IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033, or repackage it in a moisture barrier bag with desiccant and a humidity indicator card. The floor-life clock is reset only when the bake, dry storage, and packaging conditions are met and documented; otherwise the original exposure remains part of the batch history. Dry cabinets need calibrated humidity sensors and logged RH, not just a setpoint. If the cabinet RH rises above the target, the exposure clock may resume or the prior exposure may remain unresolved. For practice-based dry cabinet setpoints, alarm limits, and bake durations, verify against your equipment datasheet and your moisture control procedure."},{"h":"Containing a moisture breach","p":["When expired floor life turns up mid-lot, stop feeding the affected reels, trays or tubes and quarantine every device from the same lot, date code, vacuum bag, dry cabinet and handling window. Nothing from that batch reflows until the bake decision is made and documented.","Record the MSL, lot code, date code, reel or serial identifiers, open time, elapsed floor life, dry cabinet RH, temperature, storage location and the planned reflow time. Where some devices already reflowed, isolate the boards and assess them by electrical test, X-ray, cross-section or failure analysis. Keep containment wide enough to cover commingled lots and incomplete records."]},{"h":"Containing an ESD breach","p":["Where an ESD control failure turns up in storage or staging, quarantine the devices and boards in the EPA for the breach window. ANSI/ESD S20.20 and IEC 61340-5-1 require an EPA with grounded personnel, ESD protective packaging, verified work surfaces and controlled handling.","Check wrist strap logs, floor and surface resistance records, ionizer status, packaging type and operator training. ESD damage is often latent, so visual inspection may show nothing. If the breach surfaces after assembly, trace every board handled in the EPA from the last verified control check to the moment of discovery, and widen the range where records are incomplete."]},{"h":"Defining the traceability range","p":["The range covers all units sharing the lot or date code, storage location, dry cabinet, vacuum bag, shift, feeder, work order, reflow oven and reflow profile. For an ESD breach, add every board handled in the EPA during the breach window, plus feeders, magazines, conveyors, bins, packaging and any adjacent lots that may have been commingled.","Where the storage system uses bins without full serial control, widen the range to the whole bin, lot or shift. The aim is a population bounded by evidence, not the smallest population you can argue for."]},{"h":"Line decision and documentation","p":["Three outcomes cover most cases: bake and repack where the package type allows, scrap where baking is not allowed or the package is damaged, or release only after the bake record, dry storage record and traceability disposition are complete.","Document the deviation, containment, bake condition, dry storage RH, reflow qualification and final disposition. Check practice-based values such as cabinet alarm limits, bake loading density and reflow scheduling windows against your equipment datasheet and process control plan. The decision should stand on the exposure record, the package type and the reflow plan."]}],"calc":{"task":"A lot of MSL3 QFN devices in tape and reel is found mid-lot with expired floor life. The lot has 12 reels, each with 500 units. The vacuum bag was opened 200 h before the planned reflow. The dry cabinet RH was 7% RH at 28 °C. Two reels were placed on feeders and 180 boards were populated. An ESD wrist strap alarm was also found disabled for 4 h in the same storage area. Decide whether the batch must be baked, specify the bake and dry-storage condition by package type, and define the traceability range.","solution":"Step 1: Identify the moisture sensitivity level. The devices are MSL3 under IPC/JEDEC J-STD-020, and the floor-life rules come from IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033. Step 2: Compare elapsed floor life with IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033. MSL3 floor life is 168 h at 30 °C and 60% RH. Elapsed time is 200 h, so the floor life is exceeded by 32 h. Step 3: Check dry storage. IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 uses a dry storage target of 5% RH or less. The cabinet was at 7% RH, so the storage condition is not compliant and the exposure cannot be treated as controlled dry storage. Step 4: Decide whether to bake. Because floor life is exceeded and reflow is planned, bake before reflow unless the component manufacturer provides a documented exception. Step 5: Select bake condition by package type. The package is tape and reel, which contains polymer tape, reel, cover tape, and labels. If the carrier cannot withstand 125 °C, use the IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 lower-temperature long bake at 40 °C, which for an MSL 3 body up to 1.4 mm is 9 days and therefore does not fit a short reflow window. If the component and carrier datasheets allow 125 °C, the IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 125 °C column applies only to loose parts in trays or tubes and is indexed to both body thickness and MSL level: for this MSL 3 lot with floor life exceeded by 32 h, 7 h up to 1.4 mm, 17 h at 1.4 to 2.0 mm and 48 h at 2.0 to 4.5 mm, with longer times at higher MSL levels and when the exceedance is greater than 72 h. For this case, choose the 40 °C long bake as the conservative tape-and-reel condition, or de-reel, bake the loose parts at 125 °C for the thickness-indexed time and re-reel; verify the chosen route against the J-STD-033 bake table, the tape datasheet and the reflow window. Step 6: Define post-bake storage. Store at 5% RH or less under IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033, or repackage in a moisture barrier bag with desiccant and a humidity indicator card. Step 7: Calculate affected device count. 12 reels x 500 units per reel = 6000 units. Step 8: Define moisture traceability range. Include all 12 reels, the same lot and date code, the same vacuum bag, the same dry cabinet, the same storage location, the same shift, the two reels on feeders, the 180 populated boards, the feeders, the placement program, the reflow oven, the reflow profile, the work order, the incoming lot, the operator, and the storage RH records. Step 9: Define ESD traceability range. Because the wrist strap alarm was disabled for 4 h, include all devices and boards handled in the EPA during those 4 h, plus the same lot, storage location, feeders, magazines, conveyors, bins, packaging, and work orders. If records are incomplete, widen to the full shift or full lot. Step 10: Disposition. Quarantine the 6000 units, bake the unreflowed units under the selected condition, repackage or store under compliant dry storage, and disposition the 180 boards through electrical test and failure analysis as needed."},"defect":{"symptom":"After reflow, several boards show intermittent opens and lifted leads, and X-ray shows voiding or cracking near the QFN package body on boards that used the expired-floor-life reels.","cause":"MSL3 tape and reel devices exceeded IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 floor life, the dry cabinet ran above the 5% RH target, and the batch was fed to placement without a bake decision or traceability containment.","action":"Stop the line, quarantine all reels from the same lot, date code, vacuum bag, and dry cabinet, and isolate the affected boards. Do not reflow the remaining batch. Bake the unreflowed devices using an IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 condition allowed by the tape and reel carrier, then store at 5% RH or less or repackage with desiccant and a humidity indicator card. Trace all boards from the affected reels, feeders, work order, reflow oven, and shift, and disposition them through electrical test, X-ray, and failure analysis."},"takeaways":["Treat an expired floor life as a contained deviation, not as scrap and not as a label problem.","Check the carrier before choosing the bake, because tape and reel caps the bake at 40 °C and turns hours into days.","Bound the traceability range by evidence — lot, bag, cabinet, shift, feeder, oven — and widen it whenever records are incomplete."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Baking a tape-and-reel lot at 125 °C to save time","instead":"Keep reels at or below 40 °C or de-reel and bake loose parts to a qualified recipe."},{"mistake":"Releasing the batch after a 7 % RH cabinet reading","instead":"Treat anything above 5 % RH as exposure and recalculate floor life before reflow."},{"mistake":"Limiting containment to the two reels on the feeder","instead":"Include the whole lot, bag, cabinet and shift, and widen further where records cannot prove otherwise."}],"terms":[{"term":"Floor-life deviation","def":"A batch whose recorded exposure exceeds the allowed floor life before reflow."},{"term":"Containment","def":"Action that stops suspect material from moving further into the process while the disposition is decided."},{"term":"Latent ESD failure","def":"Damage that leaves a part working at test and failing later in service."},{"term":"Traceability range","def":"The set of units, lots and handling records that the evidence shows to be affected by an exposure."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":25,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m02l05"],"skills":["Lesson m02l05: The ESD control program: EPA, grounding, straps, mats, ionizers, packaging"]}},{"lessonId":"m03l01","moduleId":"m03","stageId":"stencil","title":"The paste volume budget: what the joint needs before the aperture is drawn","goals":[{"text":"Calculate area ratio, aspect ratio and expected deposit volume for an aperture and state whether it will release paste adequately.","competencies":["K2"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-004"],"quiz":["q-m03l01-01","q-m03l01-02","q-m03l01-03","q-m03l01-04","q-m03l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"Paste volume is a joint requirement","p":["Define the joint before drawing the aperture: pad geometry, component termination, target standoff, acceptable fillet, and the paste volume needed to wet the termination and form a reliable joint. The aperture then has to deliver that volume while staying inside the pad, so reflow creates no bridge.","Stencil thickness, paste rheology, print speed, squeegee pressure, separation speed and cleaning cycle all decide how much paste actually transfers. Treat the aperture as a volume source and the print process as the transfer mechanism between them."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 940 340' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Stencil aperture cross-section with area ratio and aspect ratio definitions; a thick foil with a narrow aperture gives low ratios and poor release'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;fill:#e2e8f0}.d{fill:#94a3b8}.t{fill:#53d1cc}.r{fill:#f44336}.o{fill:#ff9800}</style> <text x='20' y='24' font-size='13' class='t'>GOOD RELEASE: thin foil, wide aperture</text> <rect x='20' y='60' width='120' height='24' fill='#475569'/><rect x='260' y='60' width='120' height='24' fill='#475569'/> <rect x='140' y='60' width='120' height='24' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.35)' stroke='#53d1cc'/> <line x1='140' y1='100' x2='260' y2='100' stroke='#e2e8f0'/><text x='200' y='116' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle'>W (aperture width)</text> <line x1='395' y1='60' x2='395' y2='84' stroke='#e2e8f0'/><text x='402' y='76' font-size='11'>t (foil)</text> <text x='20' y='150' font-size='11'>area ratio = aperture area / wall area</text> <text x='20' y='168' font-size='11'>           = L·W / (2·(L+W)·t)  → target ≥ 0.66</text> <text x='20' y='192' font-size='11'>aspect ratio = W / t            → target ≥ 1.5</text> <text x='20' y='216' font-size='10' class='d'>IPC-7525 release indicators; confirm against paste and equipment datasheet.</text> <text x='480' y='24' font-size='13' class='r'>POOR RELEASE: thick foil, narrow aperture</text> <rect x='480' y='50' width='140' height='44' fill='#475569'/><rect x='680' y='50' width='140' height='44' fill='#475569'/> <rect x='620' y='50' width='60' height='44' fill='rgba(244,67,54,.3)' stroke='#f44336'/> <line x1='620' y1='110' x2='680' y2='110' stroke='#e2e8f0'/><text x='650' y='126' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle'>small W</text> <line x1='835' y1='50' x2='835' y2='94' stroke='#e2e8f0'/><text x='842' y='76' font-size='11'>big t</text> <text x='480' y='150' font-size='11' class='r'>area ratio and aspect ratio both LOW</text> <text x='480' y='168' font-size='11'>paste sticks to the walls: partial deposit,</text> <text x='480' y='186' font-size='11'>not an over-thick one — the deposit is short</text> <text x='480' y='216' font-size='10' class='d'>Fine pitch shrinks W faster than t: both ratios fall.</text> <rect x='20' y='250' width='840' height='1' fill='#334155'/> <text x='20' y='280' font-size='11'>Deposit volume = L·W·t × transfer efficiency; set the joint volume target first, then size the aperture.</text> <text x='20' y='304' font-size='11' class='d'>Fixes: thinner foil, step-down pocket, larger or rounded aperture, polished or nano-coated walls, other paste.</text> </svg>","caption":"Aperture geometry and paste release: area ratio ≥ 0.66 and aspect ratio ≥ 1.5; a thick foil with a narrow aperture gives low ratios and a short deposit"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m03l01.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: stencil macro photograph, release geometry cross-section, good release versus poor release comparison, and the joint target to SPI workflow","caption":"Paste volume budget: joint target first, then deposit volume, aperture and SPI verification"}]},{"h":"Area ratio compares contact area with wall adhesion","p":["Area ratio is the aperture area divided by the sidewall area of the stencil wall. For a rectangular aperture the sidewall area is the perimeter times the stencil thickness, so area ratio equals length times width divided by perimeter times thickness.","A higher ratio gives the paste a larger bottom contact area against the wall that holds it in the aperture. IPC-7525 stencil design guidelines are the common reference for release indicators, with a working target of about 0.66 or above; confirm that against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Aspect ratio describes depth against width","p":["Aspect ratio is aperture width divided by stencil thickness, and for rectangular apertures you take the smaller dimension, because that dimension controls the narrowest release path. A low ratio means a deep, narrow hole where paste clings to the sidewalls and fails to release cleanly.","IPC-7525 again supplies the release indicator, with a common working target above about 1.5. Fine-pitch designs often fall below it and need a process or paste change rather than a longer argument."]},{"h":"Deposit volume is nominal volume times transfer efficiency","p":["Nominal deposit volume is aperture area multiplied by stencil thickness. Expected deposit volume multiplies that by transfer efficiency, which absorbs paste left in the aperture, paste smeared during separation, and paste never released at all.","Transfer efficiency depends on the process, so measure it or take it from paste and equipment data. Where planning needs a single number, use a conservative value from the paste datasheet or a recent print trial."]},{"h":"Set the joint volume target first","p":["Estimate the required paste volume from pad area, desired deposit height, component termination geometry and paste manufacturer guidance. On chip components the deposit has to wet the termination and form a fillet without giving adjacent pads enough paste to bridge in reflow.","Larger pads leave more room: adjust volume through aperture area, stencil thickness or a step stencil. Compare the calculated expected deposit volume with the required volume before committing to the aperture."]},{"h":"Geometry changes that improve release","p":["When area ratio or aspect ratio comes out low, reduce stencil thickness, enlarge the aperture within the pad, choose a paste with better release, or change the print parameters. Electropolished walls, laser-cut shape, wall taper and step stencils also help.","Every one of those trades volume against release and bridging risk, so judge them on print trials and inspection data rather than on a single ratio."]},{"h":"Decision rule for release adequacy","p":["Calculate area ratio, aspect ratio, nominal volume and expected deposit volume. Ratios that meet the release indicators from your applicable design guideline, together with an expected volume that meets the joint requirement, make adequate release likely.","A low ratio means the aperture may release badly even when nominal volume looks generous. A low volume means the joint may be underfilled even when release is good. Document the assumptions and confirm with paste deposit measurement."]}],"calc":{"task":"A small chip resistor has a 0.50 mm by 0.40 mm pad. Stencil thickness is 0.12 mm. Use a transfer efficiency of 0.90. Calculate area ratio, aspect ratio, nominal deposit volume, and expected deposit volume. State whether release is adequate using the IPC-7525 release indicators.","solution":"Aperture area = 0.50 mm x 0.40 mm = 0.20 mm^2. Perimeter = 0.50 mm + 0.40 mm + 0.50 mm + 0.40 mm = 1.80 mm. Sidewall area = 1.80 mm x 0.12 mm = 0.216 mm^2. Area ratio = 0.20 mm^2 / 0.216 mm^2 = 0.926. Aspect ratio = smaller aperture dimension / stencil thickness = 0.40 mm / 0.12 mm = 3.33. Nominal deposit volume = 0.20 mm^2 x 0.12 mm = 0.024 mm^3. Expected deposit volume = 0.024 mm^3 x 0.90 = 0.0216 mm^3. Release adequacy: area ratio 0.926 is above about 0.66 and aspect ratio 3.33 is above about 1.5, so release is likely adequate. Compare 0.0216 mm^3 with the required joint volume from paste and component data; if it is lower, increase aperture area, reduce stencil thickness, or improve transfer efficiency."},"defect":{"symptom":"After reflow, several small chip components show insufficient solder fillet or opens on one side, while adjacent larger pads look normal.","cause":"The small aperture had low area ratio or aspect ratio, or transfer efficiency was lower than assumed, so the expected deposit volume was not delivered.","action":"Measure paste deposit volume, compare with required joint volume, reduce stencil thickness or enlarge aperture within pad, adjust paste and print parameters, and re-run a print trial with inspection."},"takeaways":["Set the joint volume target before the aperture exists, then check that the aperture can deliver it inside the pad.","Judge release on both area ratio and aspect ratio, because a deep narrow aperture can pass one and fail the other.","Take transfer efficiency from measurement or the paste datasheet, since a generic factor hides the process you actually run."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Designing the aperture and checking the volume afterwards","instead":"Fix the joint volume first and size the aperture against it."},{"mistake":"Using a single release ratio as a pass or fail verdict","instead":"Combine both ratios with SPI data and the paste datasheet before releasing the design."},{"mistake":"Assuming nominal volume equals deposit volume","instead":"Multiply by a measured transfer efficiency and compare with the joint requirement."}],"terms":[{"term":"Area ratio","def":"Aperture opening area divided by the stencil sidewall area, the usual indicator of paste release."},{"term":"Aspect ratio","def":"Aperture width divided by stencil thickness, taken on the narrowest dimension of a rectangular aperture."},{"term":"Transfer efficiency","def":"The share of nominal aperture volume that reaches the pad as a printed deposit."},{"term":"Step stencil","def":"A stencil with locally reduced thickness that lowers paste volume in selected areas."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m01l07"],"skills":["Lesson m01l07: Nonconforming material at dock: quarantine, MRB, supplier notification"]}},{"lessonId":"m03l02","moduleId":"m03","stageId":"stencil","title":"Area ratio and aspect ratio: definitions, calculation, fine-pitch consequences","goals":[{"text":"Calculate area ratio, aspect ratio and expected deposit volume for an aperture and state whether it will release paste adequately.","competencies":["K2"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-004"],"quiz":["q-m03l02-01","q-m03l02-02","q-m03l02-03","q-m03l02-04","q-m03l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 940 340' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Worked example of area ratio and aspect ratio for a 0.25 by 0.25 mm aperture in 0.10 mm foil: area ratio 0.625 below the 0.66 guidance, aspect ratio 2.5'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;fill:#e2e8f0}.d{fill:#94a3b8}.t{fill:#53d1cc}.r{fill:#f44336}.o{fill:#ff9800}</style> <text x='20' y='24' font-size='13' class='t'>APERTURE 0.25 × 0.25 mm, FOIL 0.10 mm</text> <rect x='90' y='60' width='110' height='110' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2'/><text x='145' y='120' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle'>top view</text> <line x1='90' y1='185' x2='200' y2='185' stroke='#e2e8f0'/><text x='145' y='200' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle'>W = 0.25 mm</text> <line x1='75' y1='60' x2='75' y2='170' stroke='#e2e8f0'/><text x='68' y='118' font-size='11' text-anchor='end'>L=0.25</text> <rect x='250' y='90' width='40' height='40' fill='#475569'/><rect x='380' y='90' width='40' height='40' fill='#475569'/><rect x='290' y='90' width='90' height='40' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.35)' stroke='#53d1cc'/> <text x='335' y='80' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle'>section</text><line x1='435' y1='90' x2='435' y2='130' stroke='#e2e8f0'/><text x='442' y='114' font-size='10'>t = 0.10 mm</text> <text x='560' y='60' font-size='11'>aperture area   = 0.25 × 0.25 = 0.0625 mm²</text> <text x='560' y='82' font-size='11'>wall area       = 2·(0.25+0.25) × 0.10 = 0.10 mm²</text> <text x='560' y='110' font-size='12' class='r'>area ratio      = 0.0625 / 0.10 = 0.625  &lt; 0.66</text> <text x='560' y='136' font-size='12' class='t'>aspect ratio    = 0.25 / 0.10 = 2.5  (passes)</text> <text x='560' y='162' font-size='11'>theoretical vol = 0.0625 × 0.10 = 0.00625 mm³</text> <text x='560' y='184' font-size='11'>expected vol = 0.00625 × 0.85 = 0.0053125 mm³</text> <rect x='20' y='215' width='860' height='1' fill='#334155'/> <text x='20' y='245' font-size='12' class='r'>LOW area ratio</text><text x='150' y='245' font-size='11'>= small opening against a lot of wall. Fine pitch shrinks W and L while t stays: the ratio falls first.</text> <text x='20' y='270' font-size='11'>Aspect ratio alone can pass (2.5 here) while the area ratio fails — judge on both, and on the expected volume.</text> <text x='20' y='300' font-size='10' class='d'>Guidance value 0.66 per IPC-7525C; release also depends on paste rheology, wall finish, print speed, snap-off and pressure — verify with SPI.</text> </svg>","caption":"Worked example: 0.25 × 0.25 mm aperture in 0.10 mm foil — area ratio 0.625 (below 0.66), aspect ratio 2.5"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m03l02.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: isometric aperture with opening and wall areas, a width versus foil thickness section, the two ratio definitions, a macro photograph of complete and short fine-pitch deposits, and the fine-pitch consequence","caption":"Area ratio and aspect ratio: definitions, the fine-pitch consequence, and what short deposits look like"}]},{"h":"Area ratio: what it measures","p":["Area ratio is the aperture opening area divided by the sidewall area of the stencil aperture. On a rectangular aperture the opening is width times length, and the sidewall is the perimeter times the foil thickness.","IPC-7525C treats area ratio as a key indicator of paste release, because the paste has to separate from those walls and transfer to the pad."]},{"h":"Area ratio and paste release","p":["A higher area ratio gives the paste more opening area against the wall it must leave, and transfer tends to improve with it. A low ratio means the wall contact area is large next to the opening, so paste sticks in the aperture and the pad gets an inadequate deposit.","IPC-7525C gives area-ratio guidance for stencil design, but the practical release limit also depends on paste rheology, wall finish, print speed, snap-off distance and squeegee pressure. Check all of it against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Aspect ratio: what it measures","p":"Aspect ratio is the aperture dimension divided by foil thickness. On a rectangular aperture you use the smallest dimension, because it controls the narrowest paste channel. IPC-7525C discusses aspect ratio as a stencil geometry parameter tied to paste release and aperture wall effects."},{"h":"Aspect ratio and fine-pitch printing","p":["As pitch falls, apertures shrink while foil thickness often stays where it was, so aspect ratio rises and area ratio drops. High aspect ratio apertures make release more sensitive to print parameters and wall condition.","On fine-pitch designs, review aspect ratio together with area ratio, paste type and SPI results. A single pass or fail number cannot carry a fine-pitch decision."]},{"h":"Deposit volume: theoretical and expected","p":["The theoretical maximum deposit is aperture area multiplied by foil thickness. The expected deposit is lower, because paste never transfers perfectly, and it is commonly estimated by multiplying the theoretical volume by a transfer efficiency factor.","Take that factor from your paste, stencil, printer and SPI data. A generic assumption hides the process you are actually running."]},{"h":"Fine-pitch consequences","p":"Fine-pitch apertures can produce low area ratio, high aspect ratio and small deposit volume at the same time. The joint then risks insufficient paste, voids, opens or inconsistent transfer, and the process becomes sensitive to paste slump, stencil wear, wall roughness and print parameter drift."},{"h":"The practical release decision","p":["Combine area ratio, aspect ratio, expected deposit volume and process data. Where area ratio sits near or below the IPC-7525C guidance range, or the expected volume is close to the minimum the component and pad need, review the design.","The review covers foil thickness, aperture size within the pad, wall finish, paste selection and print parameters."]}],"calc":{"task":"A rectangular stencil aperture is 0.25 mm by 0.25 mm, and the foil thickness is 0.10 mm. Calculate the area ratio, aspect ratio, theoretical deposit volume, and expected deposit volume using an assumed transfer efficiency of 0.85. State whether the aperture is expected to release paste adequately according to common IPC-7525C area-ratio guidance.","solution":"1. Aperture area = width × length = 0.25 mm × 0.25 mm = 0.0625 mm². 2. Aperture perimeter = 2 × (width + length) = 2 × (0.25 mm + 0.25 mm) = 1.00 mm. 3. Sidewall area = perimeter × foil thickness = 1.00 mm × 0.10 mm = 0.10 mm². 4. Area ratio = aperture area / sidewall area = 0.0625 mm² / 0.10 mm² = 0.625. 5. Aspect ratio = smallest aperture dimension / foil thickness = 0.25 mm / 0.10 mm = 2.5. 6. Theoretical deposit volume = aperture area × foil thickness = 0.0625 mm² × 0.10 mm = 0.00625 mm³. 7. Expected deposit volume = theoretical volume × transfer efficiency = 0.00625 mm³ × 0.85 = 0.0053125 mm³. 8. In nanoliters, 0.0053125 mm³ × 1000 nL/mm³ = 5.3125 nL. 9. The area ratio is 0.625, which is below the commonly cited IPC-7525C area-ratio guidance value of 0.66. Therefore, paste release is expected to be marginal to inadequate for robust production unless supported by SPI data, paste datasheet, and print parameter validation. 10. Possible actions include reducing foil thickness, increasing aperture dimensions within the pad boundary, improving stencil wall finish, selecting a paste with better release characteristics, or optimizing print speed, pressure, and snap-off distance."},"defect":{"symptom":"After reflow, fine-pitch components show intermittent opens, voids, or insufficient solder joints, and stencil apertures retain paste residue after printing.","cause":"The aperture area ratio is too low for reliable paste release, often because the aperture is small relative to foil thickness and sidewall area. Rough or worn stencil walls, inappropriate paste rheology, or print parameters that do not support release can worsen the problem.","action":"Review the aperture area ratio and aspect ratio against IPC-7525C guidance and your paste/equipment datasheet. Reduce foil thickness or increase aperture size within the pad if allowed, improve stencil wall finish, verify paste selection, and optimize print parameters. Confirm the result with SPI and reflow inspection."},"takeaways":["Calculate area ratio and aspect ratio from the same three numbers — aperture dimensions and foil thickness — before the stencil is ordered.","Compare the expected deposit with the joint requirement, because a good area ratio can still leave too little paste.","Treat a low area ratio as a design review trigger supported by SPI data, not as an automatic rejection."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Increasing foil thickness to raise paste volume","instead":"Thicker foil lowers area ratio and costs release; raise aperture area or use a step stencil instead."},{"mistake":"Judging a fine-pitch aperture on area ratio alone","instead":"Read aspect ratio and the SPI deposit data with it, since both drive transfer."},{"mistake":"Using a textbook transfer efficiency in the design review","instead":"Take the factor from your own printer, paste and SPI results."}],"terms":[{"term":"Area ratio","def":"Aperture opening area divided by sidewall area, the standard indicator of paste release in stencil design."},{"term":"Aspect ratio","def":"Aperture width divided by foil thickness on the narrowest aperture dimension."},{"term":"Transfer efficiency","def":"The fraction of theoretical aperture volume that reaches the pad as a deposit."},{"term":"Fine pitch","def":"Component lead spacing small enough that aperture dimensions approach the limits of paste release."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m03l01"],"skills":["Lesson m03l01: The paste volume budget: what the joint needs before the aperture is drawn"]}},{"lessonId":"m03l03","moduleId":"m03","stageId":"stencil","title":"Foil, thickness, step stencils, laser cut and wall finish","goals":[{"text":"Select foil thickness and aperture geometry for a given pad stack and pitch, and justify each choice by the defect it prevents.","competencies":["K2","K3"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-004"],"quiz":["q-m03l03-01","q-m03l03-02","q-m03l03-03","q-m03l03-04","q-m03l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m03l03.webp","alt":"Cross-sections of a plain foil, a step stencil with two thicknesses, a rough laser-cut wall and a smooth electropolished wall","caption":"Foil, thickness, step stencils, laser cut and wall finish — how the foil and its walls set the deposit"}]},{"h":"Foil thickness sets volume and release risk","p":["Foil thickness is the primary control on deposited paste volume: for a given aperture, volume is roughly aperture area times foil thickness. Thicker foil helps large pads, thermal relief and high-volume joints, and it raises the chance of slump, bridging and poor release on fine pitch.","Choose from the supplier's available range, commonly 0.075 mm to 0.200 mm, and check the choice against your paste rheology, your printer and your SPI capability."]},{"h":"Aperture geometry must support release","p":["Two release checks carry most of the work. Area ratio is aperture area divided by the product of aperture perimeter and foil thickness, and IPC-7525 commonly uses a minimum of 0.66 for reliable release. Aspect ratio is aperture width divided by foil thickness, with a common minimum of 1.5.","Both are design guidelines rather than guarantees, because paste type, wall finish and print speed decide the outcome too."]},{"h":"Pad stack and pitch drive the thickness","p":["Start from the tightest pitch on the board. Fine-pitch QFP, QFN and BGA pads usually need thinner foil or a local thin step so the aperture walls sit close enough to control paste shape. Larger pads and thermal pads may need more volume, which a thicker local step can supply where pitch is generous.","Where a board mixes both, decide up front whether one foil thickness can satisfy every feature or whether the design needs a step stencil."]},{"h":"Aperture size is usually reduced from the pad","p":["A full-pad aperture can overfill closely spaced pads and bridge, while a small one starves the joint and opens. Many processes reduce the aperture a few percent to tens of percent of the pad dimension, depending on paste slump, reflow profile and pad finish; your paste and equipment datasheet settles the number.","On rectangular pads keep the reduction balanced, so the deposit stays centred and favours no lead."]},{"h":"Step stencils separate conflicting needs","p":["A step stencil changes foil thickness locally: thin where the fine-pitch apertures are, thicker over large pads and thermal pads. Place the step where it does not disturb the support of nearby fine-pitch apertures, and keep the transition far enough from sensitive features to avoid paste leakage or uneven squeegee support.","Confirm the stencil maker's minimum step size, transition method and maximum step height. Those are process limits of that supplier, not universal values."]},{"h":"Laser cut and wall finish","p":["Laser cutting controls wall taper and finish. Rough or irregular walls hold paste, increase residue and make release less repeatable, while an aggressive taper changes the effective aperture at the top and the bottom of the foil.","Ask for the wall finish and taper the stencil maker can actually hold, such as electropolished or coated walls, and verify the result with SPI and print inspection. A finish does not fix a geometry problem."]},{"h":"Selection sequence and verification","p":["A practical order is: find the tightest pitch and the paste-volume requirements, choose a base foil thickness, size apertures to meet the IPC-7525 area-ratio and aspect-ratio guidelines while controlling volume, add a step only when one thickness cannot serve both ends of the board, then run a print trial with SPI.","Adjust aperture size before changing foil thickness where you can, because aperture changes iterate faster and cost less."]}],"calc":{"task":"A board has 0.40 mm x 0.40 mm QFP pads on 0.50 mm pitch and a 1.00 mm x 1.00 mm thermal pad. Select a base foil thickness and aperture size for the fine-pitch pads, select a local step thickness for the thermal pad, and check the IPC-7525 area-ratio and aspect-ratio guidelines.","solution":"Start with the tightest pitch: 0.50 mm pitch QFP pads. Choose a base foil thickness of 0.100 mm to limit paste volume and reduce bridging risk; verify that the paste datasheet supports this thickness for the chosen aperture. Reduce the 0.40 mm pad to a 0.38 mm x 0.38 mm aperture to lower bridging risk while retaining paste volume. Calculate area ratio for the fine-pitch aperture: area = 0.38 mm x 0.38 mm = 0.1444 mm^2; perimeter = 1.52 mm; area ratio = 0.1444 mm^2 / (1.52 mm x 0.100 mm) = 0.95. This is above the IPC-7525 guideline of 0.66. Calculate aspect ratio: 0.38 mm / 0.100 mm = 3.8. This is above the IPC-7525 guideline of 1.5. For the 1.00 mm x 1.00 mm thermal pad, choose a local step thickness of 0.150 mm to increase paste volume without thickening the entire stencil. Use a 0.95 mm x 0.95 mm aperture. Check the thermal pad area ratio: area = 0.95 mm x 0.95 mm = 0.9025 mm^2; perimeter = 3.80 mm; area ratio = 0.9025 mm^2 / (3.80 mm x 0.150 mm) = 1.58, above the IPC-7525 guideline of 0.66. Aspect ratio = 0.95 mm / 0.150 mm = 6.33, above the IPC-7525 guideline of 1.5. Place the step transition away from the 0.50 mm pitch QFP apertures and confirm the stencil maker can hold the step and wall finish. Run SPI to verify paste volume, bridging, and release before production."},"defect":{"symptom":"Bridging appears between adjacent fine-pitch QFP leads after reflow, with excess paste visible between leads on SPI.","cause":"The foil thickness or aperture size was selected for larger pads and is too aggressive for the fine-pitch area; rough or poorly controlled laser-cut walls can also reduce paste release and leave paste residue between apertures.","action":"Reduce the fine-pitch aperture size, then reduce local foil thickness or add a thin step if volume is still too high. Recheck area ratio and aspect ratio against IPC-7525, request a smoother wall finish or verified taper from the stencil maker, and rerun SPI to confirm the change."},"takeaways":["Set foil thickness from the tightest pitch first, then check the larger pads against it, because paste volume scales directly with thickness.","Read area ratio and aspect ratio against IPC-7525 before the stencil is cut, and treat either one failing as a design change, not a print tweak.","Add a step stencil only where a single thickness genuinely cannot serve both fine pitch and large thermal pads."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Thickening the whole foil to feed one thermal pad","instead":"Use a local step and keep the fine-pitch area thin."},{"mistake":"Assuming an electropolished wall rescues a failing area ratio","instead":"Fix the aperture geometry first, then verify the finish with SPI."},{"mistake":"Placing a step transition next to fine-pitch apertures","instead":"Keep the transition clear of features that depend on even squeegee support."}],"terms":[{"term":"Step stencil","def":"A stencil whose foil thickness changes in defined areas to give different paste volumes on one board."},{"term":"Area ratio","def":"Aperture area divided by aperture perimeter times foil thickness."},{"term":"Aspect ratio","def":"Aperture width divided by foil thickness on the narrowest dimension."},{"term":"Wall taper","def":"The change in aperture size between the top and bottom of the laser-cut wall."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m03l02"],"skills":["Lesson m03l02: Area ratio and aspect ratio: definitions, calculation, fine-pitch consequences"]}},{"lessonId":"m03l04","moduleId":"m03","stageId":"stencil","title":"Aperture geometry by component: chip, QFP fine pitch, QFN/LGA, BGA","goals":[{"text":"Select foil thickness and aperture geometry for a given pad stack and pitch, and justify each choice by the defect it prevents.","competencies":["K2","K3"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-004","src-007","src-011","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m03l04-01","q-m03l04-02","q-m03l04-03","q-m03l04-04","q-m03l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m03l04.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: stencil aperture plan views and the deposits they produce for chip, fine-pitch QFP, QFN/LGA with segmented thermal pad and BGA, a laser-cut stencil photo and a geometry-to-defect table","caption":"Aperture geometry by component: chip, fine-pitch QFP, QFN/LGA and BGA, with the defect each choice prevents"}]},{"h":"Pad stack and stencil thickness trade-off","p":["Foil thickness sets paste volume per aperture. For a given pad stack, pick a thickness that delivers enough paste for fillet and standoff without the excess that bridges or the shortage that opens.","Selection usually starts from the smallest pitch and the smallest aperture in the assembly, then gets checked against the larger components. Common thicknesses are 0.10 mm to 0.15 mm for fine-pitch work and 0.12 mm to 0.18 mm for mixed boards, subject to the stencil supplier's capability and the paste datasheet. IPC-7525 covers thickness, aperture area and sidewall finish."]},{"h":"Chip components: aperture area and paste balance","p":["For chip resistors and capacitors the aperture should match pad size while still allowing paste to form a wettable fillet. A common starting point is aperture area at 80 % to 100 % of pad area, with less area on the smallest parts to prevent tombstoning and bridging.","On the smallest chips the aperture width may be 0.10 mm to 0.15 mm and the length 0.20 mm to 0.30 mm, but the final values come from IPC-7525, the IPC-7351 land pattern and the observed release. Where the pad sits on a large copper area, reduce aperture area so the extra heat does not turn into excess paste and solder balling."]},{"h":"QFP fine pitch: area ratio and sidewall control","p":["Gull-wing fine-pitch packages need enough paste to wet the lead foot while keeping solder mask clearance between adjacent pads. Area ratio — aperture opening area divided by aperture wall area — is the useful control, and IPC-7525 discusses how it drives release.","A practical target is 0.66 or higher for small apertures, with sidewall polish and paste viscosity adjusted when the ratio falls below. Below 0.5 mm pitch, narrow the aperture relative to the pad, use trapezoidal or rounded shapes, and check the result against IPC-7525 and the paste datasheet to keep bridges out."]},{"h":"QFN and LGA: peripheral pads and thermal escape","p":["QFN and LGA packages usually carry peripheral pads plus a central thermal pad. Peripheral apertures should be sized to avoid coplanarity defects and bridging, while the centre aperture is segmented or reduced to control volume and let gas escape.","A common approach divides the thermal pad into several small apertures with 0.10 mm to 0.20 mm between segments, or uses a reduced-area pattern; check it against IPC-7525 and the IPC-7351 land pattern guidance. An oversized centre pad traps paste and causes voiding, non-wetting or package lift, while an undersized one costs thermal performance and joint reliability."]},{"h":"BGA: pitch, aperture area and collapse","p":["On BGA the geometry follows ball pitch, ball diameter and paste collapse during reflow. IPC-7095 covers BGA design and assembly, including paste deposition and land pattern considerations.","Apertures are usually smaller than the ball diameter to limit paste volume and bridging, especially at 0.5 mm pitch and below. At larger pitches the aperture may approach the land area, but paste rheology and the reflow profile still have to be verified. Where BGA pads share a board with fine-pitch parts, a step stencil or local thickness change can serve both."]},{"h":"Foil thickness for mixed component sets","p":["Find the smallest pitch and smallest aperture that must release paste reliably, then choose thickness against them. A thinner foil cuts volume and keeps bridges off fine-pitch QFP, QFN and BGA pads, but it can starve larger components and open joints when the paste fails to wet the pad. A thicker foil feeds the large parts and coplanarity, and raises the bridging risk on fine pitch.","On a board with both, consider a step stencil or local thickness change, and check the transition location against IPC-7525 and what the stencil fabricator can hold."]},{"h":"Aperture geometry and process verification","p":["Aperture shape, wall finish and aperture reduction are process controls, not drawing decoration. Rounded corners, trapezoidal apertures and reduced-area patterns can improve release and cut bridging, but they have to be tested with the actual paste, squeegee pressure and print speed.","Judge joints against IPC-A-610 and confirm the printed deposit with paste volume measurement or two- and three-dimensional inspection. Values such as area-ratio targets, reduction percentages and thickness ranges are practice-based, so check them against your paste and equipment datasheet and your first-article results."]},{"h":"Mapping geometry to defect prevention","p":["Tie every geometry choice to a defect it prevents. Adequate area ratio with clean sidewalls prevents insufficient paste and opens; reduced aperture area with thinner foil prevents fine-pitch bridging; segmented centre pads prevent voiding and lift on QFN and LGA; controlled BGA aperture area prevents collapse and bridging.","The choice is a balance. The thickness that prevents opens on a large pad can bridge a 0.4 mm pitch QFP, so justify the final value by the dominant defect risk and confirm it by inspection."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 0.4 mm pitch QFP has land width 0.25 mm and land length 0.60 mm. The board also has 0.5 mm pitch BGA pads. Select foil thickness and aperture geometry for the QFP, and justify each choice by the defect it prevents.","solution":"1. Identify the smallest pitch: 0.4 mm. Use IPC-7525 area-ratio guidance and choose a thin foil, 0.10 mm, to reduce paste volume and bridging risk. 2. Reduce aperture width to 0.22 mm and length to 0.55 mm to leave solder mask clearance and control volume. 3. Calculate area ratio: aperture area = 0.22 mm x 0.55 mm = 0.121 mm^2. Sidewall area = 2 x (0.22 mm + 0.55 mm) x 0.10 mm = 0.154 mm^2. Area ratio = 0.121 / 0.154 = 0.79. This is above the practical target of 0.66 associated with IPC-7525 area-ratio guidance, so release should be acceptable if sidewall finish and paste viscosity are verified. 4. Check paste volume: aperture area 0.121 mm^2 multiplied by foil thickness 0.10 mm gives 0.0121 mm^3 per aperture. For the 0.5 mm pitch BGA, verify that the same foil does not create insufficient paste; if needed, use a step stencil or local thickness change, and verify against IPC-7095 and paste datasheet. 5. Justify: thinner foil and reduced aperture prevent bridging between 0.4 mm pitch leads; adequate area ratio prevents opens from poor release; rounded corners reduce paste trapping and solder balls. 6. Inspect first article per IPC-A-610 and measure printed volume; adjust if bridges or opens appear."},"defect":{"symptom":"Solder bridges between adjacent QFP leads at 0.4 mm pitch after reflow; X-ray shows excess paste between leads.","cause":"Foil thickness 0.15 mm and aperture width equal to pad width produced high paste volume and poor release control; area ratio may be adequate but volume excessive.","action":"Reduce foil to 0.10 mm, reduce aperture width to 0.22 mm, use rounded or trapezoidal apertures, verify area ratio of at least 0.66 against IPC-7525, adjust paste viscosity and squeegee pressure, re-run first article and inspect per IPC-A-610."},"takeaways":["Start thickness selection at the smallest pitch and smallest aperture, then check that the large components still get their volume.","Segment the QFN thermal pad so gas can escape, because a solid centre aperture traps paste and voids the joint.","Justify each geometry choice by the defect it prevents, and confirm the result with SPI and IPC-A-610 inspection."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Using one foil thickness for a board with 0.4 mm QFP and large thermal pads","instead":"Step the stencil or accept a compromise on the dominant defect risk, and verify both areas."},{"mistake":"Filling the QFN centre pad with one large aperture","instead":"Segment it into small apertures with 0.10 mm to 0.20 mm gaps so volatiles escape."},{"mistake":"Sizing chip apertures at full pad area on the smallest parts","instead":"Reduce area to prevent tombstoning and bridging, then verify with SPI."}],"terms":[{"term":"Thermal pad","def":"The large central land under a QFN or LGA that carries heat and needs a controlled paste volume."},{"term":"Tombstoning","def":"One end of a chip component lifting during reflow because the two joints wet at different rates."},{"term":"Aperture reduction","def":"Deliberately making the stencil opening smaller than the land to control paste volume and bridging."},{"term":"Land pattern","def":"The copper geometry on the board that the aperture and the component termination must match."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m03l03"],"skills":["Lesson m03l03: Foil, thickness, step stencils, laser cut and wall finish"]}},{"lessonId":"m03l05","moduleId":"m03","stageId":"stencil","title":"Aperture calculator and drawing review","goals":[{"text":"Calculate area ratio, aspect ratio and expected deposit volume for an aperture and state whether it will release paste adequately.","competencies":["K2"]},{"text":"Accept or reject a delivered stencil from its measurement report against the issued specification.","competencies":["K2"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-004"],"quiz":["q-m03l05-01","q-m03l05-02","q-m03l05-03","q-m03l05-04","q-m03l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m03l05.webp","alt":"A calculator panel taking pad geometry, foil thickness and wall condition and outputting area ratio, aspect ratio and volume, beside a stencil drawing review checklist","caption":"Aperture calculator and drawing review — inputs, release metrics and the review checklist"}]},{"h":"What the aperture calculator is for","p":"An aperture calculator turns pad geometry, foil thickness and wall condition into release metrics you can review before fabrication: area ratio, aspect ratio and expected deposit volume. Those numbers let the process engineer decide whether the stencil will release paste and whether the drawing needs revision before anyone buys the foil."},{"h":"Area ratio and paste release","p":["Area ratio is aperture opening area divided by the wall area the paste must leave. IPC-7525C recommends an area ratio above 0.66 for reliable release.","Values below that still work with some paste types, print speeds, squeegee pressures and foil finishes, but only after qualification against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Aspect ratio and aperture shape","p":"Aspect ratio is aperture width divided by foil thickness, taken on the smaller dimension of a rectangular aperture because that dimension controls the release path. IPC-7525C recommends above 1.5. Thick foil on a small aperture pulls the ratio down and leaves paste sticking in the hole."},{"h":"Wall taper and aperture condition","p":"Wall taper affects how easily paste leaves the aperture. IPC-7525C recommends a wall taper in the range of 85 to 90 degrees, measured from the horizontal plane, because a slight taper reduces the effective wall area. Electropolishing, laser-drill condition, burrs, and clogging also affect release and should be reviewed on the drawing and measurement report."},{"h":"Expected deposit volume","p":"Expected deposit volume starts at aperture area times foil thickness, with a taper correction when the bottom opening is smaller than the top. What actually transfers depends on transfer efficiency, which moves with paste rheology, print speed, squeegee pressure and foil finish — check it against your paste and equipment datasheet."},{"h":"Drawing review before fabrication","p":"Drawing review confirms aperture dimensions, tolerances, foil thickness, wall taper, fiducials, aperture-to-pad alignment, step features and the paste assumptions. It also confirms that the supplier's measurement method and sample size can actually verify the delivered stencil."},{"h":"Accept or reject from the measurement report","p":["Accept against the issued specification, not against general guidance. A measured value outside the issued tolerance means the stencil is rejected or dispositioned by engineering, unless a qualified deviation exists.","Area ratio or aspect ratio below the qualified release limit triggers review even when every aperture dimension sits inside tolerance, because dimensions alone do not guarantee release."]}],"calc":{"task":"A drawing specifies a square aperture 0.30 mm by 0.30 mm in a 0.120 mm thick foil. The issued specification requires area ratio at least 0.66, aspect ratio at least 1.5, foil thickness 0.120 mm plus or minus 0.010 mm, aperture size 0.30 mm plus or minus 0.02 mm, wall taper 85 to 90 degrees, and flatness no greater than 0.10 mm. A delivered stencil report gives measured aperture 0.295 mm by 0.298 mm, thickness 0.118 mm, area ratio 0.63, aspect ratio 2.5, wall taper 86 degrees, and flatness 0.08 mm. Calculate area ratio, aspect ratio, expected deposit volume, and accept or reject.","solution":"Step 1: Calculate nominal aperture area. Area = 0.30 mm x 0.30 mm = 0.090 mm2. Step 2: Calculate nominal perimeter. Perimeter = 4 x 0.30 mm = 1.20 mm. Step 3: Calculate nominal area ratio. Area ratio = aperture area / (perimeter x foil thickness) = 0.090 / (1.20 x 0.120) = 0.625. Step 4: Calculate nominal aspect ratio. Aspect ratio = aperture width / foil thickness = 0.30 / 0.120 = 2.5. Step 5: Calculate nominal deposit volume. Nominal volume = aperture area x foil thickness = 0.090 x 0.120 = 0.0108 mm3. Step 6: Apply a taper correction. For an 85 degree wall taper from horizontal, the wall angle from vertical is 5 degrees. Bottom side = 0.30 - 2 x 0.120 x tan(5 degrees) = 0.279 mm. Bottom area = 0.279 x 0.279 = 0.0778 mm2. Average area = (0.090 + 0.0778) / 2 = 0.0839 mm2. Tapered volume = 0.0839 x 0.120 = 0.0101 mm3. Step 7: Estimate expected paste deposit. Using an example transfer efficiency of 80 percent, expected deposit = 0.0101 x 0.80 = 0.0081 mm3. Verify transfer efficiency against your paste and equipment datasheet. Step 8: State release adequacy. The nominal area ratio 0.625 is below the IPC-7525C recommended value of 0.66, so paste release may be inadequate unless qualified by paste and equipment testing. Step 9: Review the delivered report. Thickness 0.118 mm is within 0.120 mm plus or minus 0.010 mm. Aperture 0.295 mm by 0.298 mm is within 0.30 mm plus or minus 0.02 mm. Aspect ratio 2.5 is above 1.5. Wall taper 86 degrees is within 85 to 90 degrees. Flatness 0.08 mm is within 0.10 mm. Step 10: Accept or reject. Reject the stencil against the issued specification because the measured area ratio 0.63 is below the required 0.66. Quarantine the stencil and disposition it through engineering, rework, or replacement."},"defect":{"symptom":"Fine-pitch QFN pads show low paste height, opens, or insufficient solder joints after a new stencil is introduced.","cause":"The aperture area ratio is below the qualified release limit because the aperture is small relative to the foil thickness, and the wall taper or wall finish is not adequate for the paste being used.","action":"Stop printing with the stencil, quarantine it, and verify aperture dimensions, foil thickness, wall taper, area ratio, and cleanliness. Reject or rework the stencil if it is outside the issued specification, then qualify the corrected design with SPI and cross-section before release."},"takeaways":["Calculate area ratio, aspect ratio and expected volume before the stencil is ordered, and reject the drawing if either ratio misses its limit.","Judge a delivered stencil against the issued specification, including measured area ratio, not only the aperture dimensions.","Put wall taper, wall finish and cleanliness on the drawing and report, because release depends on them as much as on geometry."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Accepting a stencil because every dimension is in tolerance","instead":"Check the measured area ratio against the issued release limit as well."},{"mistake":"Comparing measured values to IPC-7525 general guidance instead of the drawing","instead":"Use the issued specification, and document any limit the drawing leaves silent."},{"mistake":"Skipping the taper correction in the volume estimate","instead":"Apply it whenever the bottom opening is smaller, or the expected volume comes out optimistic."}],"terms":[{"term":"Wall taper","def":"The angle of the aperture wall from the horizontal; IPC-7525 allows the taper to be specified, and 85 to 90 degrees is common practice."},{"term":"Expected deposit volume","def":"Aperture volume corrected for taper and multiplied by transfer efficiency."},{"term":"Measurement report","def":"The supplier's record of measured stencil characteristics against the issued drawing."},{"term":"Qualified deviation","def":"A documented engineering approval to use a value outside the issued specification."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m03l04"],"skills":["Lesson m03l04: Aperture geometry by component: chip, QFP fine pitch, QFN/LGA, BGA"]}},{"lessonId":"m03l06","moduleId":"m03","stageId":"stencil","title":"Stencil acceptance, cleaning regime and lifetime tracking","goals":[{"text":"Accept or reject a delivered stencil from its measurement report against the issued specification.","competencies":["K2"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-004","src-026"],"quiz":["q-m03l06-01","q-m03l06-02","q-m03l06-03","q-m03l06-04","q-m03l06-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 960 330' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Stencil acceptance checklist with the issued specification values: foil 0.120 mm, aperture 0.400 mm, area ratio at least 0.66, wall taper at least 85 degrees, tension 25-35 N/cm, step depth 0.050 mm; plus cleaning and lifetime tracking'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;fill:#e2e8f0}.d{fill:#94a3b8}.t{fill:#53d1cc}.r{fill:#f44336}.o{fill:#ff9800}</style> <text x='20' y='24' font-size='13' class='t'>INCOMING STENCIL — check reported value AND tolerance band against the issued spec</text> <rect x='20' y='46' width='14' height='14' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc'/><text x='44' y='58' font-size='11' class='d'>foil thickness</text><text x='230' y='58' font-size='11'>0.120 ± 0.010 mm</text> <rect x='20' y='70' width='14' height='14' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc'/><text x='44' y='82' font-size='11' class='d'>aperture width</text><text x='230' y='82' font-size='11'>0.400 ± 0.025 mm</text> <rect x='20' y='94' width='14' height='14' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc'/><text x='44' y='106' font-size='11' class='d'>area ratio</text><text x='230' y='106' font-size='11'>≥ 0.66 (issued spec, IPC-7525C guidance)</text> <rect x='20' y='118' width='14' height='14' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc'/><text x='44' y='130' font-size='11' class='d'>wall taper</text><text x='230' y='130' font-size='11'>≥ 85°</text> <rect x='20' y='142' width='14' height='14' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc'/><text x='44' y='154' font-size='11' class='d'>tension</text><text x='230' y='154' font-size='11'>25-35 N/cm</text> <rect x='20' y='166' width='14' height='14' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc'/><text x='44' y='178' font-size='11' class='d'>step depth</text><text x='230' y='178' font-size='11'>0.050 ± 0.010 mm</text> <rect x='20' y='190' width='14' height='14' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc'/><text x='44' y='202' font-size='11' class='d'>registration, flatness</text><text x='230' y='202' font-size='11'>per drawing</text> <rect x='520' y='40' width='360' height='1' fill='#334155'/> <text x='520' y='64' font-size='12' class='t'>CLEANING REGIME</text> <text x='520' y='84' font-size='11'>underside wipe: dry / wet / vacuum cycle per SPI trend</text> <text x='520' y='102' font-size='11'>off-line wash after the run; inspect apertures</text> <text x='520' y='120' font-size='11' class='d'>never scrape apertures; log every wash</text> <text x='520' y='154' font-size='12' class='t'>LIFETIME TRACKING</text> <text x='520' y='174' font-size='11'>print count · wash count · tension re-check · damage log</text> <text x='520' y='192' font-size='11'>retire on tension loss, aperture wear, frame damage</text> <text x='520' y='210' font-size='11' class='d'>release decision documented before use on the line</text> <rect x='20' y='240' width='860' height='1' fill='#334155'/> <text x='20' y='266' font-size='11'>Where the spec is silent: design reference from IPC-7525C and the paste/equipment datasheet, then record the accepted limit.</text> <text x='20' y='290' font-size='11' class='d'>Acceptance = measurement report + tolerance band + area ratio recomputed from the measured foil and aperture, not the nominal.</text> </svg>","caption":"Stencil acceptance checklist from the issued specification, plus the cleaning regime and lifetime tracking"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m03l06.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: a framed stencil under a thickness gauge, an acceptance checklist with blank value lines, the understencil wipe and off-line wash sequence, and a lifetime usage log","caption":"Stencil acceptance, cleaning regime and lifetime tracking; specification values in the figure above"}]},{"h":"Acceptance basis","p":["Treat the delivered stencil as a controlled part. Acceptance starts with a traceable measurement report naming the drawing revision, measurement method, operator, date and serial number.","Compare each reported value to the issued specification, not to a generic guideline. A report that omits a required characteristic, gives no tolerance band, or shows an unapproved deviation is held or rejected until the supplier supplies corrected data."]},{"h":"Critical dimensional checks","p":"Review foil thickness, aperture width and length, wall taper, area ratio, step depth, registration, tension and flatness, confirming both the reported value and the tolerance band for each. Where the specification is silent, take the design reference from IPC-7525C and the paste or equipment datasheet, then document the accepted limit before release."},{"h":"Paste release geometry","p":["Area ratio and wall taper control release. A low area ratio, a poor taper or a damaged wall leaves paste in the aperture or smears it across the pad.","On small apertures, check the supplier's measured values against the paste datasheet and the printer capability. A nominal design value means little once the measured geometry is marginal."]},{"h":"Thickness, step and volume control","p":["Foil thickness sets paste volume, and step features give different volumes on one stencil. Confirm that thickness sits inside the issued tolerance and that step depth, location and transition angle match the drawing.","An error in either one produces too much or too little paste even when every aperture looks correct."]},{"h":"Tension and flatness","p":["Tension holds registration and board contact steady, so measure it after fabrication and periodically through service. Flatness governs contact across the print area.","Use the supplier's measurement method and the issued limit. Where the specification defines no limit, set a range from printer capability and paste process data, then verify it against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Cleaning regime","p":["Write the cleaning method, chemistry, temperature, cycle time, rinse and drying sequence into a controlled document. Ultrasonic, spray, immersion and wipe methods differ in aggressiveness and in what residue they remove.","Verify the regime against your paste and equipment datasheet, and inspect the apertures after cleaning before the stencil returns to service."]},{"h":"Lifetime tracking and retirement","p":["Track each stencil by serial number and log print cycles, paste exposure, cleaning cycles, cleaning method, inspection findings, tension, thickness, aperture wear and step condition.","Retire on measured drift, repeat defects or a failed inspection, not on a fixed cycle count alone. Use the trend data to set warning and reject limits for your own process."]}],"calc":{"task":"Issued specification: foil thickness 0.120 mm +/-0.010 mm; aperture width 0.400 mm +/-0.025 mm; area ratio minimum 0.66 (issued spec, based on IPC-7525C guidance); wall taper minimum 85 deg; tension 25-35 N/cm; step depth 0.050 mm +/-0.010 mm. Measurement report: thickness 0.128 mm; aperture width 0.418 mm; area ratio 0.64; wall taper 87 deg; tension 28 N/cm; step depth 0.055 mm. Accept or reject and state reason.","solution":"Calculate foil thickness limits: 0.120 mm - 0.010 mm = 0.110 mm and 0.120 mm + 0.010 mm = 0.130 mm. Reported 0.128 mm is within 0.110-0.130 mm, so pass. Calculate aperture width limits: 0.400 mm - 0.025 mm = 0.375 mm and 0.400 mm + 0.025 mm = 0.425 mm. Reported 0.418 mm is within 0.375-0.425 mm, so pass. Compare area ratio: reported 0.64 is below the minimum 0.66, so fail. Compare wall taper: reported 87 deg is above the minimum 85 deg, so pass. Compare tension: reported 28 N/cm is within 25-35 N/cm, so pass. Calculate step depth limits: 0.050 mm - 0.010 mm = 0.040 mm and 0.050 mm + 0.010 mm = 0.060 mm. Reported 0.055 mm is within 0.040-0.060 mm, so pass. Overall result: reject or hold because area ratio fails. Request rework, replacement, or an engineering deviation only if process validation shows acceptable paste release."},"defect":{"symptom":"After a cleaning cycle, small apertures print thin or missing paste, and adjacent pads show smearing.","cause":"Cleaning regime left paste residue or damaged aperture walls; ultrasonic power, time, chemistry, or cleaning frequency was not qualified for the stencil and paste.","action":"Quarantine the stencil, inspect apertures under magnification, clean with a qualified method, re-measure area ratio and wall condition, and requalify cleaning frequency and dryness before release; if wall damage is found, retire or rework."},"takeaways":["Accept against the issued specification, including the measured area ratio, because dimensions in tolerance can still hide a release failure.","Record tension, flatness and step condition at acceptance and through life, since these drift before the apertures do.","Retire a stencil on measured trend, not on a cycle count someone picked before the program started."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Passing a report that omits the area ratio","instead":"Require the characteristic on the report and hold the stencil until it is measured."},{"mistake":"Treating a cleaning cycle as validated because it looks clean","instead":"Inspect apertures under magnification and requalify the cycle after any chemistry or power change."},{"mistake":"Retiring the tip on the cycle count alone","instead":"Retire on tension, thickness and repeat-defect trend, and keep the records that show the drift."}],"terms":[{"term":"Registration","def":"How accurately the aperture pattern aligns with the board pads during printing."},{"term":"Tension","def":"The stretching force on the stencil mesh or foil, expressed in N/cm, that keeps the print area flat."},{"term":"Step depth","def":"The local thickness difference built into a step stencil."},{"term":"Stencil serial number","def":"The unique identifier that ties a stencil to its measurement report and lifetime history."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m03l05"],"skills":["Lesson m03l05: Aperture calculator and drawing review"]}},{"lessonId":"m04l01","moduleId":"m04","stageId":"print","title":"From aperture to deposit: how stencil geometry becomes paste volume","goals":[{"text":"Calculate the paste deposit volume from aperture geometry and evaluate SPI volume results against the joint requirement.","competencies":["K3","K2"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-004"],"quiz":["q-m04l01-01","q-m04l01-02","q-m04l01-03","q-m04l01-04","q-m04l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"What aperture design controls","p":["Aperture design decides how much paste reaches each pad and how cleanly it leaves the foil. The aperture has to be large enough to feed the joint and small enough to keep adjacent pads apart, which puts pad geometry, paste rheology, foil thickness, print speed, squeegee pressure and reflow behaviour into one balance.","For the process engineer the objective is narrower: an aperture that repeats its deposit and produces a predictable joint."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 900 200' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Screen printing cycle phases'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace}</style><rect x='40' y='80' width='180' height='48' rx='10' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='rgba(83,209,204,.35)' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='260' y='80' width='180' height='48' rx='10' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='rgba(83,209,204,.35)' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='480' y='80' width='180' height='48' rx='10' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='rgba(83,209,204,.35)' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='700' y='80' width='160' height='48' rx='10' fill='rgba(76,175,80,.1)' stroke='#4caf50' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='220' y1='104' x2='260' y2='104' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='440' y1='104' x2='480' y2='104' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='660' y1='104' x2='700' y2='104' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='130' y='109' fill='#cbd5e1' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>1 FILL APERTURES</text><text x='350' y='109' fill='#cbd5e1' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>2 PRINT / SHEAR</text><text x='570' y='109' fill='#cbd5e1' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>3 SNAP-OFF</text><text x='780' y='109' fill='#4caf50' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>4 SPI CHECK</text><text x='130' y='150' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>paste on stencil</text><text x='350' y='150' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>squeegee + pressure</text><text x='570' y='150' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>separation speed</text><text x='780' y='150' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>volume / height / area</text><text x='40' y='30' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' font-weight='600'>SCREEN PRINTING CYCLE</text><text x='40' y='50' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12'>EVERY PARAMETER YOU SET ACTS ON ONE OF THESE PHASES</text></svg>","caption":"The four phases of a screen print cycle"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m04l01.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: print stroke, snap-off and release, area ratio and aspect ratio cross-sections, a photo of printed paste bricks and an SPI height map","caption":"From aperture to deposit: print stroke, release, area and aspect ratio, deposit inspection"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Print parameters map to phases","text":"Squeegee pressure and speed act on the print phase; separation speed acts on snap-off; aperture design acts on fill. When a defect appears, first decide which phase of the cycle it was born in, then change one parameter of that phase."}]},{"h":"Area reduction","p":["Area reduction compares aperture area with pad area. A full-area aperture matches the pad; a reduced one is smaller, and fine-pitch designs reduce area to keep paste from bridging where slump or smear is likely. Too much reduction starves the joint and leaves insufficient solder.","A common starting point is 10 % to 20 %, verified against the paste datasheet, print inspection and reflow results."]},{"h":"The area ratio rule","p":["Area ratio is aperture opening area divided by sidewall area, and the sidewall is the aperture perimeter times the foil thickness. A low ratio puts more paste in contact with the wall than with the pad, and release suffers.","IPC-7525 stencil design guidelines commonly use 0.66 or higher as the target. It is a guideline, because paste type, foil finish and print settings move the real limit."]},{"h":"The aspect ratio rule","p":["Aspect ratio is the smaller aperture dimension divided by foil thickness, which describes how narrow the hole is relative to its depth. Low aspect ratio makes release hard on small apertures, and IPC-7525 commonly targets 1.5 or higher.","Check both ratios together. An aperture can pass one target and fail the other, and the failed parameter usually names the print defect."]},{"h":"Foil thickness from volume and aperture","p":["Foil thickness follows from the paste volume required and the aperture area available, since approximate volume is aperture area times thickness before losses. Thicker foil adds volume and lowers both ratios, so release gets harder; thinner foil releases better from small apertures and may not feed larger joints.","Common thicknesses run about 0.08 mm to 0.15 mm for fine pitch and 0.10 mm to 0.20 mm for larger components, with the final choice verified against the paste and equipment datasheets."]},{"h":"Pitch-class guidance","p":["Pitch class decides how much area reduction is needed and how exposed the print is to smear. In the 0.5 mm class a typical pad is around 0.30 mm square, where a small reduction controls bridging without killing release. Finer pitch means smaller apertures, thinner foil or tighter paste control; coarser pitch tolerates thicker foil and less reduction.","Check area ratio, aspect ratio, paste volume and print inspection rather than leaning on a single rule."]},{"h":"Print risks from geometry","p":["Geometry creates its own failure modes. Low area ratio gives incomplete release, so pads come up thin or empty; low aspect ratio leaves paste in the hole or smears it during lift-off. An aperture too large for the pitch bridges after print or in reflow, and one too small opens, weakens or tombstones the joint.","Thin foil cuts volume, and thick foil worsens release and raises smearing."]},{"h":"Practical design sequence","p":["Start from the pad drawing, pick an initial area reduction from pitch and paste behaviour, select the foil thickness that can deliver the volume, then calculate both ratios. If either falls below the IPC-7525 guideline, shrink the aperture within the pad boundary or reduce the foil.","Then verify with print inspection, release testing and reflow cross-sections, and document the aperture dimensions, foil thickness, both ratios, and the paste and equipment used."]}],"calc":{"task":"For a 0.5 mm pitch QFP with 0.30 mm square pads, choose a 10% aperture area reduction and check area ratio and aspect ratio for 0.12 mm foil. If the area ratio is low, specify a foil thickness that meets IPC-7525 release targets while keeping paste volume practical.","solution":"Step one: calculate pad area. Pad area = 0.30 mm x 0.30 mm = 0.09 mm2. Step two: apply 10% area reduction. Aperture area = 0.081 mm2. Step three: for a square aperture, calculate side length. Side length = square root of 0.081 mm2 = 0.285 mm. Step four: calculate perimeter. Perimeter = four times 0.285 mm = 1.14 mm. Step five: calculate sidewall area for 0.12 mm foil. Sidewall area = 1.14 mm x 0.12 mm = 0.1368 mm2. Step six: calculate area ratio. Area ratio = 0.081 mm2 / 0.1368 mm2 = 0.592. Step seven: calculate aspect ratio. Aspect ratio = 0.285 mm / 0.12 mm = 2.38. Step eight: compare with IPC-7525 stencil design guidelines. The aspect ratio is above 1.5, but the area ratio is below 0.66, so paste release may be poor. Step nine: reduce foil thickness to 0.10 mm and recalculate. Sidewall area = 1.14 mm x 0.10 mm = 0.114 mm2. Area ratio = 0.081 mm2 / 0.114 mm2 = 0.711. Aspect ratio = 0.285 mm / 0.10 mm = 2.85. Step ten: check approximate paste volume. Paste volume = 0.081 mm2 x 0.10 mm = 0.0081 mm3. Step eleven: verify the 0.10 mm foil choice against paste datasheet, stencil manufacturer capability, print inspection, and reflow results."},"defect":{"symptom":"After printing, 0.5 mm pitch QFP pads show thin paste or missing paste on some pads, and reflow produces opens or weak joints.","cause":"The aperture area reduction combined with 0.12 mm foil produced an area ratio below the IPC-7525 guideline of 0.66, reducing paste release from the aperture.","action":"Reduce foil thickness to 0.10 mm or increase the aperture within the pad boundary while keeping the area reduction controlled. Recalculate area ratio and aspect ratio, confirm they meet IPC-7525 targets of 0.66 and 1.5, and verify with paste inspection and reflow cross-sections."},"takeaways":["Check area ratio and aspect ratio together before releasing a stencil, because an aperture can pass one and fail the other.","Trade foil thickness against release, since every millimetre of foil that adds volume also lowers both ratios.","Verify a new aperture with print inspection and reflow cross-sections, and record the numbers that produced the result."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Reducing aperture area to stop bridging without recalculating the ratios","instead":"Recalculate area ratio and aspect ratio after every area change, and reduce foil if the ratio falls below 0.66."},{"mistake":"Choosing foil thickness from habit rather than from the joint","instead":"Derive the thickness from the volume the joint needs and the area the aperture has."},{"mistake":"Documenting only the aperture dimensions on the drawing","instead":"Record the foil thickness, both ratios and the paste used, or the design cannot be reproduced."}],"terms":[{"term":"Area reduction","def":"The percentage by which the aperture area is made smaller than the pad area."},{"term":"Area ratio","def":"Aperture opening area divided by the sidewall area of the aperture."},{"term":"Aspect ratio","def":"The smaller aperture dimension divided by the foil thickness."},{"term":"Pitch class","def":"The component lead spacing band that governs aperture and foil choices."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":25,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m03l06"],"skills":["Lesson m03l06: Stencil acceptance, cleaning regime and lifetime tracking"]}},{"lessonId":"m04l02","moduleId":"m04","stageId":"print","title":"Reading SPI data: measurement principle, volume/height/area/offset, artifacts","goals":[{"text":"Interpret an SPI report — volume %, height, area, offset, histograms, trend — and name the dominant defect mode with its likely cause.","competencies":["K3","K6"]},{"text":"Separate a real print defect from an SPI artifact and correct the inspection setup.","competencies":["K3","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-002","src-004","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m04l02-01","q-m04l02-02","q-m04l02-03","q-m04l02-04","q-m04l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m04l02.webp","alt":"A three-dimensional laser scan of a deposit producing a height map, with volume, height, area and offset readouts and a scan-artifact callout","caption":"Reading SPI data: measurement principle, volume, height, area, offset, and the artifacts that look like defects"}]},{"h":"What SPI reports","p":["Solder paste inspection reports the deposit on each pad, usually as volume, height and area. Volume is the primary process metric because it tracks how much solder will be available after reflow. Height is the deposit thickness above the board surface, and area is the printed footprint once the paste has released and spread.","The three are related, since volume is roughly height times area, but not exactly: SPI algorithms, lighting, board surface and paste texture all move the measurement. Judge sufficiency on volume, transfer and stencil fill on height, and release, smear and bridging risk on area.","Treat SPI as a monitor of the print step, not as a final acceptance test for the assembly. When the process is new or changed, confirm the SPI-to-joint relationship with reflow, X-ray, cross-section or functional data on your own paste, stencil, board and component mix."]},{"h":"Nominal volume and aperture class","p":["A defensible target starts at the stencil geometry: for a simple rectangular aperture, nominal volume is aperture area times stencil thickness. Printed volume usually comes out lower because paste does not release perfectly, and the loss follows paste rheology, wall condition, squeegee pressure, print speed, cleaning cycle and board support.","Group apertures into classes, because release changes with geometry. Small chip apertures, fine-pitch connectors, BGA pads and large thermal pads often need different windows. IPC-7525 gives the design guidance for area ratio and related behaviour, and low area ratio apertures may need modified paste, stencil or printer settings. One global volume limit across all pads ignores that the aperture classes fail for different reasons."]},{"h":"Process window versus control limits","p":["A process window is the range of SPI metrics that yields acceptable joints for a product and process. Control limits describe the stable variation of your printing process and come from your own data after special causes are removed. Spec limits may come from customer drawings, the IPC-A-610 acceptability requirements, or a validated product window.","Control limits are not spec limits and spec limits are not control limits. For each aperture class, set a target volume, lower and upper volume limits, and the height and area limits that support them. Verify the window against the paste datasheet, the stencil design, the SPI calibration and the reflow profile.","The same volume can be acceptable on one pad geometry and marginal on another, because wetting, component mass, pad size and thermal mass differ. Document the basis for each limit so it can be reviewed when paste, stencil, board or SPI software changes."]},{"h":"Common-cause spread","p":["Common-cause spread is the repeatable variation you see when printer, paste, stencil, board and environment behave as expected: a stable band around target with no isolated outliers and no sustained shift.","Normal paste viscosity variation, slight board warp, squeegee wear inside its life, stencil tension variation and humidity changes all produce it. Common-cause spread is not automatically a defect, but a band too wide for the product means the process is not capable.","Stabilise paste temperature, paste lot handling, cleaning cycles, stencil tension, board support and squeegee condition. Then collect new data and re-establish the window. If the band stays wide, check measurement repeatability, SPI calibration and the correlation between SPI readings and reflowed joints."]},{"h":"Special-cause spikes","p":["Special-cause spikes are isolated readings or short runs outside the stable band, and they usually mark a process change: a single high-volume pad after a stencil wipe, a cluster of low-volume pads on one board, a step change after a paste lot change.","Confirm the reading is real before touching a limit, using the image, the measurement confidence and the pad location. Then inspect the stencil underside, the squeegee blade, the paste condition, board seating, alignment and the cleaning cycle.","A spike that repeats at one pad location points to stencil damage, pad geometry, local coplanarity or a measurement artefact rather than a global printer fault. A spike that moves randomly points to paste contamination, unstable board support or intermittent stencil contact. Record the response so the next alarm can be triaged in minutes."]},{"h":"Reading height and area with volume","p":["Height and area explain why volume moved. Low height with normal area suggests incomplete fill or poor release. Low height with low area suggests a clogged aperture, insufficient squeegee pressure, poor transfer or lost coplanarity. High height with normal area suggests overfill from stencil flex, excess pressure or paste that is too stiff.","High area with normal height suggests bleed, smear, stencil lift or a poor wall condition. High area with low height suggests spread without volume, which brings bridging risk without enough solder.","Read all three together, because volume alone hides compensating errors: low height with high area can land on target volume while still printing a defective pattern."]},{"h":"Setting defensible volume limits","p":["Collect SPI data from stable production, separate the aperture classes, and remove known special causes. Calculate mean and spread per class, then apply a margin that reflects measurement repeatability, paste lot variation, stencil wear and the reflow or X-ray results that define an acceptable joint.","A common starting point is a window around the validated nominal volume, with height and area limits checked against stencil thickness and aperture area. Verify the final limits against your paste and equipment datasheet and against IPC-A-610 for the product class.","Review the limits whenever stencil thickness, paste type, aperture design, SPI calibration or printer hardware changes, and keep the rationale with the process documentation so quality, manufacturing and engineering can defend the window in an audit or a customer review."]}],"calc":{"task":"A small chip aperture has an area of 0.080 mm^2 and the stencil thickness is 0.120 mm. The validated nominal printed volume is 0.0096 mm^3. Set volume, height, and area limits using 80% to 120% of nominal for each metric. An SPI reading shows volume 0.0072 mm^3, height 0.096 mm, and area 0.075 mm^2. Determine whether the reading passes and identify the likely metric driving the failure.","solution":"Step 1: Calculate nominal volume from stencil geometry. Nominal volume = aperture area x stencil thickness = 0.080 mm^2 x 0.120 mm = 0.0096 mm^3. Step 2: Set volume limits using 80% to 120% of nominal. Lower volume limit = 0.8 x 0.0096 mm^3 = 0.00768 mm^3. Upper volume limit = 1.2 x 0.0096 mm^3 = 0.01152 mm^3. Step 3: Set height limits using 80% to 120% of stencil thickness. Nominal height = 0.120 mm. Lower height limit = 0.8 x 0.120 mm = 0.096 mm. Upper height limit = 1.2 x 0.120 mm = 0.144 mm. Step 4: Set area limits using 80% to 120% of aperture area. Nominal area = 0.080 mm^2. Lower area limit = 0.8 x 0.080 mm^2 = 0.064 mm^2. Upper area limit = 1.2 x 0.080 mm^2 = 0.096 mm^2. Step 5: Compare the SPI reading to the limits. Volume = 0.0072 mm^3, which is below 0.00768 mm^3, so volume fails. Height = 0.096 mm, which is at the lower height limit. Area = 0.075 mm^2, which is within 0.064 mm^2 to 0.096 mm^2. Step 6: Interpret the result. The low volume is driven mainly by low height, not by low area. This suggests incomplete paste transfer or insufficient aperture fill rather than excessive paste spread. Check stencil underside condition, paste viscosity, squeegee pressure, cleaning cycle, board coplanarity, and SPI measurement confidence. Verify the limits against your paste and equipment datasheet before making permanent changes."},"defect":{"symptom":"SPI shows isolated high-volume spikes on fine-pitch QFP pads, with high area and normal height, occurring after the stencil wipe cycle.","cause":"Paste smear or stencil underside contamination from a worn squeegee blade or insufficient wipe, causing paste to spread into adjacent apertures.","action":"Stop the line, inspect the squeegee blade and stencil underside, clean or replace the blade, verify wipe pressure and cleaning cycle, run a verification board, and review the SPI limits for the affected aperture class against the paste and equipment datasheet."},"takeaways":["Judge sufficiency on volume, but diagnose with height and area, because a target volume can hide a compensating defect.","Keep control limits from your own stable data and spec limits from the drawing or IPC-A-610, and never substitute one for the other.","Confirm the reading is real before adjusting a limit: check the image, the confidence and the pad location first."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Applying one volume window to every aperture class","instead":"Set separate windows for chip, fine-pitch, BGA and thermal pad classes, and justify each."},{"mistake":"Tightening the limits after a special-cause spike","instead":"Correct the cause first, then rebuild limits from data with the special cause removed."},{"mistake":"Reading volume without height and area","instead":"Read all three, since low height with high area can hit target volume and still print badly."}],"terms":[{"term":"Common-cause spread","def":"The stable, ordinary variation of a process that is running as designed."},{"term":"Special cause","def":"A variation that comes from an identifiable change rather than from ordinary process noise."},{"term":"Process window","def":"The range of print metrics that produces acceptable joints for a product."},{"term":"Measurement confidence","def":"The SPI system's own rating of how reliable a given reading is."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":25,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m04l01","m03l02"],"skills":["Lesson m04l01: From aperture to deposit: how stencil geometry becomes paste volume","Lesson m03l02: Area ratio and aspect ratio: definitions, calculation, fine-pitch consequences"]}},{"lessonId":"m04l03","moduleId":"m04","stageId":"print","title":"Printer setup: squeegee pressure and speed, snap-off, separation, wipe cycle","goals":[{"text":"Tune squeegee, snap-off, separation and wipe settings for a stated defect and predict the direction of each change.","competencies":["K3"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-004"],"quiz":["q-m04l03-01","q-m04l03-02","q-m04l03-03","q-m04l03-04","q-m04l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m04l03.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: side views of squeegee pressure as force per blade length, squeegee speed, snap-off gap, separation speed and the understencil wipe cycle, a squeegee photo and a coupled-process diagram","caption":"Printer setup: squeegee pressure and speed, snap-off, separation and wipe cycle as one coupled process"}]},{"h":"Printer setup is a coupled process","p":["Squeegee pressure, squeegee speed, snap-off, separation and wipe cycle are not independent knobs. Together they govern paste roll movement, aperture fill, paste release and residue management.","For a stated defect, change one variable at a time, inspect the deposit, and predict the direction of the next change from the paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Squeegee pressure","p":["Squeegee pressure is force per unit of blade length, never a bare force, because 100 N is a light touch on a 400 mm blade and a crushing load on a 100 mm blade. Low pressure leaves apertures underfilled, especially small ones or high aspect ratio stencils. High pressure drives paste under the stencil, wears the blade, deforms the stencil and disturbs the paste roll.","Industry guidance clusters around 0.5 to 1.5 kg per 25 mm of blade, which is 0.2 to 0.6 N/mm; work in N/mm and state the blade length with every setting, because 0.2 N/mm on a 250 mm blade is 50 N and 0.6 N/mm is 150 N. Start at the lower end and raise it until the stencil wipes clean, then verify against the paste and equipment datasheet and the printed deposit."]},{"h":"Squeegee speed","p":["Speed sets how long paste has to fill the apertures and how much paste roll is dragged ahead of the blade. Too slow and the roll sits longer, inviting bleed and smearing; too fast and fill time drops, air gets trapped and transfer falls short.","A common starting range is 20 to 80 mm/s, adjusted for fine pitch, paste viscosity and stencil thickness, and verified against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Snap-off","p":["Snap-off is the controlled lift-off after the print stroke. It helps paste leave the aperture walls and keeps the stencil from dragging paste across the board.","A fast snap-off pulls paste out of the apertures or leaves tails; a slow one lets paste smear as the board or stencil moves. A common lift-off speed range is 5 to 30 mm/s, confirmed against the paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Separation","p":["Separation is the stencil-to-board gap at the start of the print stroke. Too small and the stencil contacts the board, bleeding paste and contaminating the roll; too large and transfer falls, the roll is pushed ahead and the stencil flexes more.","A common starting range is 0.5 to 1.5 mm, verified against your paste and equipment datasheet and the board support condition."]},{"h":"Wipe cycle","p":["The wipe manages residue on the stencil underside and the condition of the paste roll. Dry, wet and vacuum wipes behave differently. Too frequent wiping strips paste from apertures, disturbs the roll or pushes solvent into the paste; too infrequent wiping lets paste dry, clog apertures and reduce release.","A common dry wipe interval is every 1 to 5 prints, checked against your paste and equipment datasheet and solvent compatibility."]},{"h":"Tuning method and direction of change","p":["For insufficient paste, raise pressure within its band, slow the stroke if fill time is the limit, reduce separation if the gap is too large, and wipe more often if apertures are clogging.","For bleed or bridging, lower pressure, slow down if roll drag is suspected, increase separation if the gap is too small, and adjust snap-off for clean release. Confirm the result with deposit height and volume, 2D or 3D inspection, and the applicable IPC workmanship standard."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 0.4 mm pitch QFP with 0.35 mm apertures shows insufficient paste. Current settings are 125 N on a 250 mm blade, 45 mm/s print speed, 12 mm/s snap-off, 1.0 mm separation, and dry wipe every 3 prints. The paste datasheet recommends 0.2 to 0.6 N/mm of blade length. Calculate the current pressure per blade length, decide whether it is in range, and state the first predicted adjustment.","solution":"Step 1: Calculate pressure per blade length: 125 N / 250 mm = 0.5 N/mm. Step 2: Compare with the recommended range: 0.5 N/mm is inside 0.2 to 0.6 N/mm, at the upper end, so pressure is not the limiting factor and the diagnosis moves on. Step 3: Keep the force at 125 N and note the band for this blade — 0.2 N/mm is 50 N and 0.6 N/mm is 150 N — so the setting has room in both directions if a later experiment calls for it. Step 4: Since aperture fill is the symptom and pressure is already high in its band, the first predicted adjustment is a slower print stroke: reduce speed from 45 mm/s to 30 mm/s to give the paste more time to fill 0.35 mm apertures. Step 5: Check other settings: snap-off 12 mm/s is within 5 to 30 mm/s, separation 1.0 mm is within 0.5 to 1.5 mm, and wipe every 3 prints is within 1 to 5 prints. Step 6: Verify the corrected settings against your paste/equipment datasheet and inspect deposit height and volume."},"defect":{"symptom":"Insufficient paste volume on 0.4 mm pitch QFP pads, leading to open joints after reflow.","cause":"Print speed is too high for the aperture fill time at 0.35 mm apertures, while squeegee pressure at 0.5 N/mm already sits in the upper half of its band, so pressure is not the first lever; separation may also be too large for stable paste transfer.","action":"Reduce print speed to 30 mm/s first, keep squeegee pressure inside 0.2 to 0.6 N/mm of blade length (50 to 150 N on this 250 mm blade) and move it only if the slower stroke does not close the gap, reduce separation to 1.0 mm if it is above the recommended range, and verify against your paste/equipment datasheet."},"takeaways":["Express squeegee pressure per blade length in N/mm, because a bare force says nothing without the blade it acts on.","Change one setting at a time and predict the direction from the defect, so the next print tells you whether the model was right.","Verify every starting range against the paste and equipment datasheet before it becomes a production setting."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Raising squeegee pressure first for every low-volume defect","instead":"Check fill time and separation first when pressure already sits mid-band."},{"mistake":"Quoting pressure as a bare force","instead":"Convert to N/mm and record the blade length, or the setting cannot be transferred to another machine."},{"mistake":"Wiping more often to fix bleed","instead":"Excess wiping strips paste and dries the stencil edge; fix the underside contamination instead."}],"terms":[{"term":"Snap-off","def":"The controlled separation of the board from the stencil after the print stroke."},{"term":"Separation","def":"The stencil-to-board gap set at the start of the print stroke."},{"term":"Paste roll","def":"The bead of paste that rolls ahead of the squeegee and feeds the apertures."},{"term":"Wipe cycle","def":"The interval and method used to clean the stencil underside between prints."}],"corrections":[{"from":"0.45","to":"0.5","why":"0.45 kg per 25 mm is 0.18 N/mm, not 0.2 N/mm; the kilogram band is restated as the exact equivalent of the 0.2 to 0.6 N/mm band the lesson, its calculation and m04l07 all use."},{"from":"0.70","to":"1.5","why":"0.70 kg per 25 mm is 0.27 N/mm, not 0.6 N/mm; 0.6 N/mm on 25 mm is 15 N, which is 1.5 kg."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m04l02"],"skills":["Lesson m04l02: Reading SPI data: measurement principle, volume/height/area/offset, artifacts"]}},{"lessonId":"m04l04","moduleId":"m04","stageId":"print","title":"Paste behaviour: viscosity, thixotropy, metal load, slump, handling and life","goals":[{"text":"Interpret an SPI report — volume %, height, area, offset, histograms, trend — and name the dominant defect mode with its likely cause.","competencies":["K3","K6"]},{"text":"Tune squeegee, snap-off, separation and wipe settings for a stated defect and predict the direction of each change.","competencies":["K3"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-004"],"quiz":["q-m04l04-01","q-m04l04-02","q-m04l04-03","q-m04l04-04","q-m04l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m04l04.webp","alt":"A viscosity versus shear-rate curve showing thixotropic hysteresis, a slump test, a metal load readout and a paste life clock","caption":"Paste behaviour: viscosity, thixotropy, metal load, slump, handling and life"}]},{"h":"What SPI measures and how to read it","p":["SPI compares the printed deposit with the stencil aperture volume. Volume percent is usually measured deposit volume divided by nominal aperture volume, so 100 % means the aperture filled to stencil thickness and released without loss.","Height is the mean or maximum deposit thickness, area is the footprint of paste on the board, and offset is the centre-to-centre displacement between aperture and deposit. Histograms show the spread around the mean: a narrow one means repeatable release, a bimodal one usually means two release states, clean stencil against partially blocked apertures. Trends expose paste ageing, stencil fouling, squeegee wear and temperature drift. Judge the print against the IPC acceptance criteria for the product class."]},{"h":"Viscosity, thixotropy and metal load","p":["Viscosity controls how much shear it takes to push paste through the aperture and how much it resists flowing afterwards. Thixotropy is the recovery of viscosity after shear: adequate recovery holds the deposit's shape, slow recovery lets it slump.","Metal load, the powder fraction, drives density, volume and rheology. A higher metal load can raise deposit height for the same stencil thickness, and it may also raise viscosity and hurt wetting when the flux balance does not match. Read the paste datasheet for metal load, viscosity at the relevant shear rates and recommended stencil thickness."]},{"h":"Slump, bleed and bridging","p":["Slump is lateral flow after release, seen as more area and less height. It comes from low viscosity, excessive paste temperature, too much squeegee shear or a slow snap-off.","Bleed is paste sitting on the board between apertures, usually from underside contamination, insufficient wiping or paste that is too fluid. Bridging joins adjacent apertures and is likelier when paste is too stiff, the underside is dirty, or separation is too high. Read area and height together: high area with low height suggests slump, high area with normal height suggests bleed or offset."]},{"h":"Handling and paste life on the stencil","p":["Paste life is how long the paste stays printable after it reaches the stencil. Solvent evaporation, temperature rise and repeated rolling all raise viscosity and cut slump resistance.","Paste that prints well at the start of a shift can print low volume later if the underside dries or the paste stiffens. Keep the stencil clean, control paste temperature and check open time against the datasheet. When the SPI trend shows gradual volume loss, look at paste handling before touching the squeegee settings."]},{"h":"Squeegee, snap-off, separation and wipe","p":["Pressure and speed control shear and fill. More pressure improves fill and can smear paste or damage the stencil; less speed improves fill and costs throughput.","Snap-off speed decides how fast the board leaves the stencil: too fast pulls paste and drops height, too slow smears. Separation is the distance the board moves before snap-off: too much stretches the paste and lowers height, too little smears. Wipe length and pressure clean the underside; too little leaves paste that bleeds, too much strips apertures or dries the stencil edge.","Typical starting points are stencil thickness 0.10-0.15 mm, squeegee speed 25-40 mm/s, squeegee pressure 5-15 N, separation 0.5-1.5 mm and wipe length 1-3 mm, all verified against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Reading SPI for the dominant defect mode","p":["Low volume percent with low height and normal area usually means incomplete fill or poor release. Low volume percent with high area suggests slump or bleed. Normal volume percent with high offset means alignment or registration. High volume percent with high area and low height can mean excessive wipe pressure or paste that is too fluid.","Histograms separate random variation from drift: a broad one points to unstable snap-off or paste rheology, a shifted mean points to a setting or the paste condition. Typical handling is 20-30 °C and 40-60 % RH with open time often 4-8 h, verified against the paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Tuning direction for a stated defect","p":["Low volume and low height with a clean SPI area call for slightly more squeegee pressure, less squeegee speed, slower snap-off, less separation, and less wipe pressure or length if either is excessive.","Low volume with high area calls for less squeegee pressure, less speed, faster snap-off and less separation. Bleed at normal volume calls for a longer or harder wipe and a clean stencil. Bridging calls for less separation, a more effective wipe and a viscosity check. Change one setting at a time and verify against the datasheet."]}],"calc":{"task":"An SPI report for a 0.40 mm x 0.40 mm aperture in a 0.12 mm stencil shows mean height 0.085 mm, mean area 0.145 mm^2, volume 62%, offset 0.02 mm, a broad histogram, and a downward volume trend over 4 h. Calculate the measured volume and nominal aperture volume, then name the dominant defect mode and likely cause.","solution":"Nominal aperture area = 0.40 mm x 0.40 mm = 0.16 mm^2. Nominal aperture volume = 0.16 mm^2 x 0.12 mm = 0.0192 mm^3. Measured deposit volume = 0.145 mm^2 x 0.085 mm = 0.012325 mm^3. Volume percent = 0.012325 mm^3 / 0.0192 mm^3 x 100% = 64.2%, close to the reported 62% within measurement rounding. Height is below stencil thickness and area is below nominal area, so the dominant defect mode is incomplete aperture fill and poor paste release, not slump. The broad histogram and downward trend suggest paste stiffening on the stencil or squeegee pressure/speed drifting away from the fill window. Likely cause: paste viscosity/thixotropy drift combined with insufficient squeegee pressure or too high squeegee speed. Predicted tuning: increase squeegee pressure slightly, reduce squeegee speed, reduce snap-off speed, reduce separation, reduce wipe pressure or length if excessive, and verify paste temperature and stencil cleanliness."},"defect":{"symptom":"SPI shows volume percent falling from 95% to 70% over 4 h, mean height dropping from 0.11 mm to 0.08 mm, area near nominal, offset below 0.02 mm, and a histogram shifting left without an additional peak.","cause":"Paste on the stencil has aged and increased in viscosity, while squeegee pressure and speed remained fixed, so apertures are not fully filled and release becomes incomplete.","action":"Reduce squeegee speed, increase squeegee pressure slightly, reduce snap-off speed, reduce separation, reduce wipe pressure or length if excessive, clean the stencil underside, and verify paste temperature and open time against the paste datasheet. Re-run SPI after each change."},"takeaways":["Read volume percent with height and area, because the same volume percentage means different defects at different heights.","Treat a broad histogram and a drifting mean as two different messages: instability against a real change.","Check paste age, temperature and open time before changing printer settings when volume falls slowly over a shift."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Chasing a falling volume trend with squeegee pressure","instead":"Check paste temperature, open time and stencil cleanliness first."},{"mistake":"Reading the paste volume percent alone","instead":"Confirm height and area, since slump and incomplete fill both lower volume but need opposite responses."},{"mistake":"Changing several settings in one trial","instead":"Change one variable per trial, or the result tells you nothing about which setting mattered."}],"terms":[{"term":"Volume percent","def":"Measured deposit volume divided by nominal aperture volume, expressed as a percentage."},{"term":"Thixotropy","def":"The recovery of paste viscosity after shear, which lets a printed deposit hold its shape."},{"term":"Slump","def":"Lateral flow of the deposit after release, showing as larger area and lower height."},{"term":"Metal load","def":"The percentage of metal powder by weight in the solder paste."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m04l03"],"skills":["Lesson m04l03: Printer setup: squeegee pressure and speed, snap-off, separation, wipe cycle"]}},{"lessonId":"m04l05","moduleId":"m04","stageId":"print","title":"SPI as process evidence: mean, spread, capability, stop/adjust decisions","goals":[{"text":"Interpret an SPI report — volume %, height, area, offset, histograms, trend — and name the dominant defect mode with its likely cause.","competencies":["K3","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-004"],"quiz":["q-m04l05-01","q-m04l05-02","q-m04l05-03","q-m04l05-04","q-m04l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m04l05.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: volume trend chart with drift and step regions, histogram with spec limits, volume / height / area icons, offset vector, SPI height-map screen and a stable / capable decision flow","caption":"SPI as process evidence: trend, histogram, volume with height and area, and the continue / adjust / stop decision"}]},{"h":"What SPI evidence means on the print stage","p":["Solder paste inspection is process evidence for the print step, not final joint acceptance. It reports what the stencil deposited before reflow: volume, height, area, offset, histogram shape and trend over time.","Use it to confirm the print process is stable and centred, and to name the dominant defect mode before boards move downstream. A good SPI result does not guarantee good soldering; a poor or drifting one is a strong early signal that the print step needs attention."]},{"h":"Reading volume and height together","p":["Volume is the most useful single metric because it combines area and height. Low volume with normal height points to reduced paste area: incomplete aperture fill, partial clogging, a weak squeegee roll, or paste that does not release cleanly.","Low volume with normal area points to reduced height: collapse, excessive wipe, low viscosity, or insufficient transfer. High volume at normal height points to area expansion, bleed or underside contamination."]},{"h":"Reading area and offset","p":["Area says whether the footprint matches the aperture. Low area points to incomplete fill, paste dry-out, clogging or poor snap-off. High area points to bleed, underside paste, excessive pressure, or a separation fast enough to smear the deposit.","Offset is positional error. A consistent direction suggests alignment, vision, conveyor, stencil tension or board support. A random one suggests board handling, fiducial quality, camera focus or unstable seating."]},{"h":"Reading histograms","p":["A histogram counts how many prints fall at each value. A narrow peak near target is what you want.","A wide distribution means an inconsistent process, often from unstable paste temperature, cleaning variation, squeegee wear or board-to-board support differences. A low-skewed histogram suggests paste depletion, aperture clogging, insufficient pressure or paste too viscous. A high-skewed one suggests overfill, bleed or underside paste. A bimodal shape means two populations are being mixed: two stencil zones, two board types, two squeegee passes or two SPI calibration regions."]},{"h":"Reading the trend","p":["Trend charts beat isolated measurements. A step change means something changed suddenly: paste lot, stencil, squeegee blade, tension adjustment, SPI calibration or a maintenance action. A gradual drift means something is changing slowly: viscosity, ambient temperature, stencil wear, cleaning effectiveness or blade wear.","Establish whether the trend is stable, drifting or stepped before adjusting anything, because adjusting an unstable process hides the real cause."]},{"h":"Capability for volume and height","p":["Capability compares the process spread with the specification limits. Cp describes spread alone — whether the process could fit inside the limits if centred. Cpk adds centring — whether it actually is centred enough to meet them.","For paste printing, use the control plan limits for the product and paste system, not only the SPI machine tolerance. Capability means something only while the process is stable: with a step, a drift or repeated out-of-limit points, correct the process first, then recalculate."]},{"h":"Stop or adjust","p":["A stable, centred, capable process against the control plan continues and gets monitored. A stable but off-centre process is adjusted through the levers that move transfer: paste temperature, squeegee pressure, squeegee speed, snap-off speed, cleaning frequency and underside wipe.","An unstable process stops for investigation before any small parameter change. Repeated out-of-limit prints, a systematic offset, or capability that will not come back after a controlled adjustment stops the line, quarantines suspect boards, and gets verified against SPI maps, stencil inspection and cross-section or pull-back evidence."]}],"calc":{"task":"A SPI report for a fine-pitch QFN paste print shows target paste volume = 100%, measured mean = 92%, standard deviation = 6%, lower specification limit = 85%, and upper specification limit = 115%. The histogram is skewed low, offset is within tolerance, and the trend is stable. Calculate Cp and Cpk, identify the dominant defect mode, and decide whether to adjust or stop.","solution":"First, confirm that the process is stable. The trend is stable, so capability can be evaluated. Second, calculate the process spread. For a normal capability calculation, the spread is six standard deviations: 6 x 6 = 36 percentage points. Third, calculate Cp using the specification width divided by the process spread: Cp = (115 - 85) / 36 = 30 / 36 = 0.83. This means the process spread is too wide for the specification window. Fourth, calculate Cpk by checking the distance from the mean to each specification limit and dividing by three standard deviations. Three standard deviations = 3 x 6 = 18 percentage points. Upper side: (115 - 92) / 18 = 23 / 18 = 1.28. Lower side: (92 - 85) / 18 = 7 / 18 = 0.39. Cpk is the smaller value, so Cpk = 0.39. Fifth, interpret the result. The low Cpk is dominated by the lower side, meaning the process is centered too low. The low Cp also shows the spread is too wide. The dominant defect mode is low paste volume, likely from incomplete aperture fill, paste depletion, insufficient squeegee pressure, poor snap-off, or paste rheology that is not transferring well. Sixth, decide the action. Because the trend is stable and the defect mode is clear, adjust first: verify paste temperature and viscosity, inspect stencil apertures and underside, review squeegee pressure and speed, review snap-off speed, and review stencil cleaning frequency. Run verification prints and recalculate capability. If out-of-limit prints persist, if the histogram remains low-skewed, or if capability remains below the control plan limit, stop the line and quarantine suspect boards."},"defect":{"symptom":"SPI shows low paste volume concentrated on one side of the board, with normal height and reduced area. The trend shows a step change after a stencil change.","cause":"Stencil alignment or tension is causing incomplete aperture fill on that side, possibly combined with a squeegee pressure gradient across the board.","action":"Stop the line, verify stencil tension and alignment, inspect aperture fill using SPI area and height maps, adjust squeegee pressure and speed according to the paste and stencil datasheet, run verification prints, and quarantine boards printed after the step change until the cause is confirmed."},"takeaways":["Judge capability only on a stable process, because Cp and Cpk on a drifting trend describe a process that no longer exists.","Read volume with height and area before naming a defect, since volume alone confuses under-fill with spreading.","Distinguish a step change from a gradual drift, because the first points to an event and the second to wear."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Recalculating capability after every process adjustment","instead":"Establish stability on a control chart first, then compute capability from that data."},{"mistake":"Adjusting print parameters during a drift before checking paste age","instead":"Check paste temperature, open time and stencil cleanliness first, then touch the machine."},{"mistake":"Reading a bimodal histogram as random variation","instead":"Find the two populations — panel side, stencil zone, board type — before changing limits."}],"terms":[{"term":"Cp","def":"The specification width divided by six standard deviations, a measure of spread alone."},{"term":"Cpk","def":"The distance from the mean to the nearer specification limit in standard deviations, a measure of spread and centring."},{"term":"Bimodal histogram","def":"A distribution with two peaks, showing that two different populations are being measured together."},{"term":"Step change","def":"An abrupt shift in a trend chart caused by a single event in the process."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m04l04","m04l02"],"skills":["Lesson m04l04: Paste behaviour: viscosity, thixotropy, metal load, slump, handling and life","Lesson m04l02: Reading SPI data: measurement principle, volume/height/area/offset, artifacts"]}},{"lessonId":"m04l06","moduleId":"m04","stageId":"print","title":"Print defect atlas: under-fill, excess, bridging, skew, smearing, slumping","goals":[{"text":"Interpret an SPI report — volume %, height, area, offset, histograms, trend — and name the dominant defect mode with its likely cause.","competencies":["K3","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m04l06-01","q-m04l06-02","q-m04l06-03","q-m04l06-04","q-m04l06-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m04l06.webp","alt":"Six small deposit cross-sections labelled with each print defect, shown beside a correct deposit for comparison","caption":"Print defect atlas: under-fill, excess, bridging, skew, smearing and slumping"}]},{"h":"What an SPI report is trying to answer","p":["A solder paste inspection report is a measurement, not only a screen. It shows how much paste was deposited, where, and how consistently: volume percentage against nominal, paste height, paste area, placement offset, histograms, and trend over time.","The engineer reads those metrics to infer the dominant print defect and trace it to a process cause. Take the acceptability limits from the product specification and the relevant IPC-A-610 class, then check them against the paste, stencil and printer datasheets."]},{"h":"Volume percentage as the first clue","p":["Volume percentage compares measured paste volume with the nominal volume expected from aperture area, stencil thickness and paste density. Low volume usually means under-fill, incomplete transfer or paste lost during release. High volume means excess paste, overfilling or accumulation from poor cleaning.","Volume alone misleads, because a pad can carry the right volume in the wrong shape, height or place. Read it with height, area and offset."]},{"h":"Height and area separate under-fill from spreading","p":["Low height with low area supports under-fill or incomplete transfer. Low height with high area suggests smearing or slumping, where paste spread sideways and lost height. High height with high area points to excess paste and bridging risk, and high height with low area to poor release, paste buildup or a local stencil problem.","Process windows vary with paste and stencil design, so check them against your own paste datasheet and design rules."]},{"h":"Offset and skew","p":["Offset measures how far the paste centroid sits from the pad centre. Skew is a pattern of consistent directional displacement across pads, components or panel locations.","A random offset pattern points to board support, conveyor handling or vision instability. A consistent direction points to stencil alignment, board seating, support pin interference or a systematic vision offset. Where offset is small but area and height are normal, the dominant issue is registration rather than deposition."]},{"h":"Histograms: uniform or localised","p":["A histogram counts measurements in each range of volume, height, area or offset. Narrow and near target means stable printing.","A broad histogram means instability: viscosity drift, cleaning variation, inconsistent squeegee pressure. Shifted low means under-fill or incomplete transfer; shifted high means excess paste or accumulation. A bimodal shape means two populations are being measured together, such as one panel side printing differently from the other, or one stencil area behaving differently."]},{"h":"Trend data: stable defect or drift","p":["Trends distinguish stable, drifting, stepping and oscillating behaviour. A stable low-volume trend suggests a design or setup issue — aperture size, stencil thickness, paste rheology. A gradual drift toward low volume suggests paste drying, stencil clogging or degrading cleaning. A step change follows an event such as a paste lot, stencil, squeegee or program change. Oscillation points to cleaning frequency, board support variation or conveyor handling.","Trend data decides whether you face a fixed defect mode or a process drift, and the two need different responses."]},{"h":"Naming the dominant defect mode","p":["The dominant mode is the one that explains the combined pattern. Under-fill shows low volume, low height and low area. Excess shows high volume, high height and high area. Bridging shows high volume or area between adjacent pads, especially at fine pitch. Skew shows normal volume and shape with a consistent offset. Smearing shows low height with an elongated or irregular area, and slumping shows low height with a widened area and poor edge definition.","The likely cause then follows from whether the pattern is uniform, localised, directional, drifting or step-like."]}],"calc":{"task":"A SPI report for a fine-pitch QFP area shows mean volume at 82% of nominal, mean height at 0.11 mm versus nominal 0.12 mm, mean area at 95% of nominal, mean offset at 0.04 mm, a histogram shifted low with a long tail toward low volume, and a trend that has drifted downward over the last several boards. Identify the dominant defect mode and the most likely cause.","solution":"Step 1: Check volume percentage. The mean volume is 82% of nominal, which is below target and suggests insufficient paste transfer. Step 2: Check height. The mean height is 0.11 mm versus nominal 0.12 mm, confirming that the paste is thinner than intended. Step 3: Check area. The mean area is 95% of nominal, which is close to target and argues against major smearing or slumping. Step 4: Check offset. The mean offset is 0.04 mm, which is small and does not explain the low volume. Step 5: Check histogram. The histogram is shifted low with a tail toward low volume, indicating a population of pads receiving too little paste. Step 6: Check trend. The downward drift suggests a process change over time rather than a fixed design error. Step 7: Combine the evidence. Low volume, low height, near-normal area, small offset, low-shifted histogram, and downward trend point to under-fill as the dominant defect mode. Step 8: Infer likely cause. A gradual downward drift with under-fill is commonly associated with paste viscosity increase, stencil clogging, reduced transfer efficiency, or insufficient cleaning. Verify against paste datasheet, stencil condition, cleaning frequency, and printer settings."},"defect":{"symptom":"SPI shows high volume and high area between adjacent QFP leads, with a histogram shifted high and a trend that worsens after cleaning intervals are extended.","cause":"Excess paste remains on the stencil surface or between apertures because cleaning is not removing paste residue effectively, allowing paste to accumulate and bridge adjacent pads during print.","action":"Increase cleaning frequency, verify cleaning solvent condition and wipe quality, inspect stencil underside for residue, and confirm paste release behavior against the paste datasheet. Re-run SPI after adjustment and monitor the high-volume trend."},"takeaways":["Read the whole report before naming a defect, because volume, height, area and offset each rule alternative causes in or out.","Use the histogram to see whether one population or two are printing, and split by panel side or stencil zone when it is bimodal.","Separate a stable defect from a drift in the trend, since a design fix does not cure a degrading stencil."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Diagnosing smearing from a low volume percentage alone","instead":"Confirm height and area, since smearing shows low height with widened area rather than low area."},{"mistake":"Ignoring offset because volume is acceptable","instead":"Check the offset trend, because registration drift produces defects the volume screen cannot see."},{"mistake":"Treating an oscillating trend as random","instead":"Look for cleaning frequency and board support variation before changing print parameters."}],"terms":[{"term":"Volume percentage","def":"Measured deposit volume as a percentage of the nominal aperture volume."},{"term":"Skew (print offset)","def":"A consistent directional displacement of deposits across pads or panel locations."},{"term":"Under-fill","def":"A deposit that is short in volume, height and area because the aperture did not fill or release."},{"term":"Slumping","def":"Lateral flow of the deposit after release, producing a wider, flatter print."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m04l05"],"skills":["Lesson m04l05: SPI as process evidence: mean, spread, capability, stop/adjust decisions"]}},{"lessonId":"m04l07","moduleId":"m04","stageId":"print","title":"Print troubleshooting logic and one-variable-at-a-time experiments","goals":[{"text":"Tune squeegee, snap-off, separation and wipe settings for a stated defect and predict the direction of each change.","competencies":["K3"]},{"text":"Separate a real print defect from an SPI artifact and correct the inspection setup.","competencies":["K3","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-004"],"quiz":["q-m04l07-01","q-m04l07-02","q-m04l07-03","q-m04l07-04","q-m04l07-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m04l07.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: triage flow from reported defect to confirmed defect, one-variable-at-a-time table with a single changed row, experiment plan checklist, paste bridge photo and a change / measure / keep-or-revert loop","caption":"Print troubleshooting logic: triage first, then one variable at a time"}]},{"h":"Triage before tuning","p":["Confirm the defect repeats and is not an SPI artefact first. Record the component, panel location, stencil age, paste lot, squeegee pressure, speed, snap-off distance and speed, wipe frequency and SPI threshold.","A defect that exists only in the SPI report gets verified by manual inspection, paste weight or cross-section before anyone touches the printer. Change one setting at a time so the response can be attributed to it."]},{"h":"Squeegee pressure and speed","p":["Pressure pushes paste into the apertures and keeps the underside clean. Low pressure leaves paste in the aperture, so volume drops or deposits vanish; high pressure smears paste, bleeds it and wears the stencil faster.","Set pressure per unit of blade length, around 0.2 to 0.6 N/mm (0.5 to 1.5 kg per 25 mm), which is 50 to 150 N on a 250 mm blade. A bare force figure cannot be checked without the blade length, and a 5 to 15 N setting would be far too light for any production blade. Verify against your paste and equipment datasheet.","Speed controls shear thinning and fill time, typically 10 mm/s to 50 mm/s. Higher speed fills small apertures less; lower speed fills better and may bleed more."]},{"h":"Snap-off distance and speed","p":["Snap-off distance sets how far the stencil separates from the board after printing. Too little and the stencil drags paste, smears apertures or holds paste, giving low volume and tails. More distance often improves release from small apertures, and too much stretches the paste, makes tails and costs registration.","Typical distances are 0.5 mm to 2.0 mm, confirmed against the stencil and equipment datasheet. A faster snap-off can improve release; a slower one reduces stringing."]},{"h":"Wipe frequency and type","p":["Wiping clears the stencil underside. Infrequent wiping leaves paste that smears, bridges and creates false SPI shorts. Excessive wiping dries paste, clogs apertures and cuts transfer volume.","Typical frequency is every 1 to 5 prints, checked against paste open time and the equipment datasheet. Wipe more often when underside paste causes smears, less often when paste drying or clogging appears."]},{"h":"Stencil and paste interactions","p":["Print quality follows aperture area ratio, sidewall finish, stencil thickness, paste viscosity and thixotropy. Small apertures with a low area ratio or rough sidewalls release poorly however sensible the printer settings are.","When a single-variable experiment produces no response, evaluate the stencil design or paste rheology instead of stacking printer changes. Keep the stencil clean and inspect for residue, damaged apertures and wear."]},{"h":"Separating SPI artefacts from real defects","p":["SPI can report missing paste, low volume or shorts from threshold settings, lighting, focus, fiducial errors, board warp, paste colour, solder mask variation or stencil residue.","Confirm a real defect by manual inspection, paste weight, cross-section, repeatability across panels and downstream assembly results. Fix the inspection setup by cleaning the stencil, recalibrating the SPI, adjusting threshold and lighting, refocusing, re-teaching fiducials and using a known good board. When the artefact disappears after that correction, leave the printer alone."]},{"h":"Single-variable experiment design","p":["Define the defect, the baseline settings and the acceptance metric before testing. Hold a stable paste lot, a clean stencil and consistent board condition.","Run enough boards to see the effect, for example 10 boards and 30 measured deposits per condition, and record volume, height, area and any visual tails or smears. Change only the selected setting, run the same measurement set, and compare. Lock the change when it improves the metric without new defects; otherwise revert and test the next setting separately."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 0402 resistor print shows low paste volume on SPI. The stencil thickness is 0.10 mm and the aperture is 1.0 mm x 0.5 mm. Baseline SPI average volume is 0.035 mm^3. The target is 0.045 mm^3. Baseline settings are squeegee pressure 100 N on the 250 mm blade (0.4 N/mm), squeegee speed 25 mm/s, snap-off distance 1.5 mm, and wipe every 3 prints. Design a single-variable experiment using snap-off distance.","solution":"Calculate aperture area: 1.0 mm x 0.5 mm = 0.50 mm^2. Calculate theoretical stencil volume: 0.50 mm^2 x 0.10 mm = 0.050 mm^3. Calculate current fill factor: 0.035 mm^3 / 0.050 mm^3 = 0.70, or 70%. Calculate target fill factor: 0.045 mm^3 / 0.050 mm^3 = 0.90, or 90%. Keep pressure, speed, and wipe frequency unchanged. Change only snap-off distance from 1.5 mm to 2.0 mm. Run 10 boards and measure 30 deposits. Record mean volume, height, area, and tails. If mean volume reaches 0.043 mm^3 to 0.047 mm^3 with no tails or smearing, accept the change. If tails appear, reduce snap-off distance to 1.75 mm and repeat. If volume does not change, revert to 1.5 mm and test a single next variable, such as reducing squeegee speed to 20 mm/s or raising pressure by 0.1 N/mm (25 N on this blade), recording each result separately."},"defect":{"symptom":"SPI reports low paste volume on 0402 deposits on the left panel side. Manual inspection shows short deposits and paste residue in the stencil apertures.","cause":"Snap-off distance is too low for the small apertures, so paste release is incomplete. The wipe frequency is not the primary cause because underside smearing is not dominant.","action":"Increase snap-off distance from 1.5 mm to 2.0 mm while holding pressure, speed, and wipe frequency constant. Run 10 boards, measure 30 deposits, and verify volume, tails, and smearing. If tails appear, reduce snap-off distance to 1.75 mm. If volume remains low, test squeegee speed separately."},"takeaways":["Verify a defect exists before tuning, because an SPI threshold or lighting error sends you adjusting a printer that is already correct.","Design the experiment before the trial: defect, baseline, metric, sample size and the one setting you will change.","Revert a change that fails and test the next variable alone, so each result belongs to one cause."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Changing pressure, speed and snap-off distance in one trial","instead":"Change one variable per trial, or the result cannot be attributed."},{"mistake":"Setting squeegee pressure as a bare force in newtons","instead":"State N/mm with the blade length, so the setting transfers to another machine."},{"mistake":"Tuning the printer against an unrepeated SPI reading","instead":"Confirm repeatability by manual inspection or paste weight first."}],"terms":[{"term":"Snap-off distance","def":"The gap the board and stencil reach during separation after the print stroke."},{"term":"Fill factor","def":"Measured deposit volume divided by the theoretical aperture volume."},{"term":"Single-variable experiment","def":"A trial that changes one process setting and holds every other condition constant."},{"term":"SPI artefact","def":"A reading caused by the inspection setup rather than by the printed deposit."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m04l06"],"skills":["Lesson m04l06: Print defect atlas: under-fill, excess, bridging, skew, smearing, slumping"]}},{"lessonId":"m05l01","moduleId":"m05","stageId":"placement","title":"Machine types and what accuracy, repeatability and throughput really mean","goals":[{"text":"Select nozzle, pick height and placement force for a component within its mechanical limits.","competencies":["K4"]},{"text":"Verify a placement program and setup before production and identify a missing or expired calibration.","competencies":["K4"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-011"],"quiz":["q-m05l01-01","q-m05l01-02","q-m05l01-03","q-m05l01-04","q-m05l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"What the placement stage controls","p":["Placement moves components from feeders, trays or rails onto the board and fixes their starting position before reflow or insertion. Accuracy is how close the commanded position is to the true board location, repeatability is how tightly repeated placements cluster, and throughput is the rate of valid placements.","The three interact. Faster motion, weak vacuum, worn feeders or an unverified program all turn a repeatable machine into a systematic defect source."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 900 200' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Pick and place cycle'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace}</style><rect x='40' y='80' width='170' height='48' rx='10' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='rgba(83,209,204,.35)' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='250' y='80' width='170' height='48' rx='10' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='rgba(83,209,204,.35)' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='460' y='80' width='170' height='48' rx='10' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='rgba(83,209,204,.35)' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='670' y='80' width='190' height='48' rx='10' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='rgba(83,209,204,.35)' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='210' y1='104' x2='250' y2='104' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='420' y1='104' x2='460' y2='104' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='630' y1='104' x2='670' y2='104' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='125' y='109' fill='#cbd5e1' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>PICK FROM FEEDER</text><text x='335' y='109' fill='#cbd5e1' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>VISION / CHECK</text><text x='545' y='109' fill='#cbd5e1' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>ALIGN</text><text x='765' y='109' fill='#cbd5e1' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>PLACE + FORCE</text><text x='125' y='150' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>nozzle, vacuum</text><text x='335' y='150' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>missing / tilt / size</text><text x='545' y='150' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>theta correction</text><text x='765' y='150' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>placement accuracy</text><text x='40' y='30' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' font-weight='600'>PLACEMENT CYCLE</text><text x='40' y='50' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12'>DEFECTS ARE BORN IN ONE OF THESE FOUR MOMENTS</text></svg>","caption":"Pick-and-place cycle and where its defects originate"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m05l01.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: top view of a pick-and-place machine with gantry, placement head, feeder bank, conveyor and fiducial-marked board, X/Y/Z/theta axes, accuracy versus repeatability targets, a nozzle pick-up photo, a pick-vision-place cycle timeline and a calibration certificate","caption":"Pick-and-place machine overview: layout, axes, accuracy versus repeatability, throughput and calibration status"}]},{"h":"Nozzle selection","p":["Pick a nozzle that reaches the component without touching adjacent parts, feeder walls or the board. For SMT, match the tip diameter to the component top area and mass; for THT, match the gripper or insertion nozzle to the lead diameter and body clearance.","Check nozzle ID, vacuum port size, tip length and clearance against the component drawing, the feeder datasheet and the machine nozzle map."]},{"h":"Pick height","p":["Pick height is the nozzle Z position where it contacts the component. Too high and the pick is missed or the feeder wears; too low and the component is pushed into its pocket, the tape distorts, or a brittle part cracks.","Set the initial value from component height, feeder pocket depth, tape thickness and nozzle tip length, then confirm it with a dry run and microscope inspection."]},{"h":"Placement force","p":["Placement force has to seat the component in paste, or hold a THT lead, without crushing the part, displacing paste or bending leads. On SMT it is usually a small controlled value above component weight and paste resistance; on THT it follows lead diameter, hole size, plating and insertion speed.","Verify the value against the component maximum force, the nozzle contact area and the machine's force capability."]},{"h":"Accuracy, repeatability and throughput","p":["A machine can be repeatable and still inaccurate when axes, vision or nozzle offsets are uncalibrated. Calibration and compensation fix accuracy; stable feeders, clean nozzles and a steady air supply protect repeatability.","Throughput counts only while the first-article placement stays inside the process window, because speed that adds mispicks or misalignment buys rework and scrap."]},{"h":"Program and setup verification","p":["Before production, verify the BOM, component library, nozzle map, feeder setup, pick and place coordinates, rotation, skip logic, vision settings, stencil alignment and panel support.","Run a low-speed dry cycle or first article, inspect the first board, and compare measured placement offsets with the program. Record the result so a later defect can be traced to setup, program or machine condition."]},{"h":"Calibration status","p":["Confirm that machine axes, the vision system, the nozzle changer, feeder alignment and any force or torque sensor hold current calibration under the equipment OEM and the plant quality system.","A missing or expired certificate produces consistent offsets, rotation errors, mispicks or force drift. If the certificate is absent, expired or outside its scope, stop the setup and escalate before production."]}],"calc":{"task":"A chip resistor has a body of 1.0 mm x 0.5 mm x 0.35 mm and a mass of 0.001 g. The component datasheet gives a maximum placement force of 0.30 N. The feeder pocket depth is 0.20 mm, and the component top is therefore 0.15 mm below the feeder top datum. Available nozzles are 0.20 mm, 0.30 mm, and 0.40 mm diameter. The machine vacuum can provide 40 kPa at the nozzle tip. Select a nozzle, set an initial pick height, and choose a placement force that stays within the mechanical limits.","solution":"Component top area = 1.0 mm x 0.5 mm = 0.50 mm^2. The 0.40 mm nozzle is too large for the 0.50 mm width because it leaves little clearance; the 0.20 mm nozzle may be acceptable but gives less vacuum area. Select the 0.30 mm nozzle. Its tip area = pi x (0.30 mm / 2)^2 = 0.0707 mm^2 = 0.0707 x 10^-6 m^2. Vacuum force = pressure x area = 40000 Pa x 0.0707 x 10^-6 m^2 = 0.00283 N. Component weight = 0.001 g = 1 x 10^-6 kg; weight = 1 x 10^-6 kg x 9.81 m/s^2 = 9.81 x 10^-6 N. The vacuum force is much larger than the weight, so the pick can be stable if the nozzle seals cleanly. Pick height: component top is 0.15 mm below the feeder top datum. Set the initial contact point at -0.15 mm relative to the feeder top datum, then verify with a dry run. Placement force: use a verified range such as 0.05-0.15 N for small chip components, then confirm against the paste and equipment datasheet. Choose 0.10 N. This is below the 0.30 N component limit and above the weight, so it can seat the part without excessive force. Check nozzle contact pressure = 0.10 N / 0.0707 x 10^-6 m^2 = 1.41 MPa. Verify this against the component and nozzle datasheet; if the component limit is lower, reduce the force or use a larger nozzle only if clearance allows. Before production, verify the nozzle map, feeder setup, pick height, placement force, and calibration certificate. If the certificate is missing or expired, stop and recalibrate."},"defect":{"symptom":"A first-article board shows chip components with a consistent offset toward one edge, and some small parts are tombstoned after the feeder was changed.","cause":"The placement program used an old nozzle offset or feeder calibration, and the machine calibration certificate was missing or expired, so the commanded coordinates did not match the true machine position.","action":"Stop the setup, verify the calibration certificate and scope, recalibrate the affected axes and nozzle changer, reload the correct nozzle map and feeder setup, run a low-speed dry cycle, inspect the first article, and release for production only after measured offsets are within the verified process window."},"takeaways":["Set pick height from the component, pocket and nozzle geometry, then confirm it on a dry run rather than trusting the program.","Keep placement force above the seating need and below the component's stated maximum, and verify the nozzle contact area behind that number.","Check calibration scope before the setup, because an expired certificate explains offsets that no programming change will fix."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Choosing a nozzle that fits the component body but not its clearances","instead":"Check adjacent parts, feeder walls and board clearance, not just the tip diameter."},{"mistake":"Correcting an offset with program coordinates when calibration has expired","instead":"Verify and renew calibration first, then measure the offset again."},{"mistake":"Running at full speed straight from a new program","instead":"Prove the first article at low speed and compare measured offsets before releasing production."}],"terms":[{"term":"Accuracy","def":"How close the commanded placement position is to the true board location."},{"term":"Repeatability","def":"How tightly repeated placements cluster around their own mean."},{"term":"Pick height","def":"The nozzle Z position at which it contacts the component in the feeder or tray."},{"term":"Nozzle map","def":"The machine table that pairs each component with its nozzle and vacuum setting."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m04l07"],"skills":["Lesson m04l07: Print troubleshooting logic and one-variable-at-a-time experiments"]}},{"lessonId":"m05l02","moduleId":"m05","stageId":"placement","title":"Feeders, nozzles, pick height and placement force limits by component","goals":[{"text":"Select nozzle, pick height and placement force for a component within its mechanical limits.","competencies":["K4"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-011"],"quiz":["q-m05l02-01","q-m05l02-02","q-m05l02-03","q-m05l02-04","q-m05l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m05l02.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: nozzle sizes by component type, tape feeder bank, pick-height dimensions (component height, pocket depth, tip length), placement force band, nozzle tip photo and vacuum trend for wear monitoring","caption":"Feeders, nozzles, pick height and placement force: selection, limits and wear monitoring"}]},{"h":"Why nozzle, pick height and force control matter","p":["On SMT and THT placement, the nozzle, the pick height and the placement force decide whether a component is picked cleanly, placed accurately and seated without mechanical damage.","The nozzle must match component geometry and mass. Pick height sets the vertical distance between nozzle tip and component or board surface. Placement force controls how firmly the part is pressed into paste, a socket, a hole or a seating surface. The three are interdependent: an undersized nozzle slips or crushes a corner, and excessive force cracks ceramic bodies, deforms leads or squeezes paste where it does not belong.","Start from the component supplier's mechanical limits and the machine's nozzle and force capability, then tune inside a narrow window while checking first-article samples and in-process inspection data."]},{"h":"Nozzle selection by component type","p":["Base nozzle choice on the component top surface, mass, height and tolerance. Chip resistors, capacitors and inductors usually take a vacuum nozzle with a flat or slightly rounded tip that contacts the top surface without covering the terminals.","Larger or heavier parts may need more contact area or a mechanical gripper to stop tipping and slipping. Packages with exposed metal lids, such as some power devices, benefit from a compliant tip or a bigger vacuum area that spreads the point load. THT leads, sockets and connectors may need grippers, push-down tools or guide pins instead of vacuum.","Check nozzle dimensions, vacuum flow and tip material against the placement machine datasheet and the component drawing."]},{"h":"Pick height and pick quality","p":["Pick height is the vertical position where the nozzle meets the component in the feeder or tray. Too high and the vacuum fails to seal, so the part is missed or picked at an angle. Too low and the nozzle pushes the component into its pocket, damages the feeder or strikes the carrier.","On tape and reel, set pick height so the nozzle just touches the component top with enough travel to establish vacuum without compressing it. On trays, allow for tray thickness, pocket depth and component height variation. A practical starting point is contact plus a small overtravel, often 0.05 mm to 0.20 mm, verified against the machine manual and the feeder supplier's guidance."]},{"h":"Placement force and mechanical limits","p":["Force must be enough to seat the component and establish paste contact, and low enough to protect component and board. Small passives need little force, because their mass and the paste viscosity do the seating; larger parts may need more to overcome lead springback, connector retention or paste resistance.","Ceramic capacitors, brittle packages and thin leads are damaged by point loads and excess force. The component datasheet should state a maximum placement force or allowable mechanical load; where it does not, use a conservative value and validate it by destructive or non-destructive inspection. Many small SMT components start in the 0.1 N to 1.0 N range, verified against the supplier and the machine."]},{"h":"Component-specific limits","p":["Failure modes differ by component. Small ceramic capacitors crack under point loading, especially with a worn or misaligned tip. Large MLCCs are sensitive to bending stress during placement and reflow, so force and support area both matter.","Connectors and sockets need enough force to clear retention features and not enough to bend terminals or damage housings. THT parts need insertion force without bending leads, damaging plating or cracking the board. Exposed die, glass or brittle packages forbid narrow nozzles and high point loads, and compliant leads take enough force to seat without flattening them past the supplier's limit."]},{"h":"Nozzle wear, alignment and monitoring","p":["Nozzle condition is a major defect source. A worn, chipped or contaminated nozzle loses vacuum seal, shifts the pick centre or loads the component unevenly.","Check nozzle alignment on the machine maintenance schedule and inspect tips for wear, residue and diameter change. Trend vacuum pressure and, where available, force feedback to catch drift. On high-reliability products, track nozzle life and replace on measured wear or a validated cycle count, using the machine manual and the nozzle supplier's recommendations."]},{"h":"Validation and process control","p":["Validate the nozzle, pick height and force with a controlled trial. Start from the component supplier's limits, run a small sample set, and inspect for missed picks, tilted components, tombstoning, paste squeeze-out, lead deformation and component damage.","On critical parts, add cross-sectioning or dye-and-pry to expose hidden cracks. Record nozzle number, pick height, force, vacuum level and any alarms, then lock the recipe and control changes through change management. If the process drifts, check nozzle wear, feeder alignment, tape peel force and board support before touching placement force."]}],"calc":{"task":"Select a nozzle, pick height, and placement force for a 0402 MLCC with a maximum supplier placement force of 0.5 N, a component height of 0.5 mm, and a tape pocket depth of 0.8 mm. The placement machine has a nozzle with a 0.25 mm tip diameter and a force control range of 0.05 N to 2.0 N. Determine a reasonable starting setup and the checks needed before production.","solution":"1. Confirm the component limit: the supplier states a maximum placement force of 0.5 N, so the machine force must be set below this value with margin. 2. Select the nozzle: a 0.25 mm tip is small enough for an 0402 body but should be checked for vacuum coverage and tip wear; verify against the machine nozzle chart. 3. Set pick height: with a component height of 0.5 mm and pocket depth of 0.8 mm, the nozzle should contact the top surface with a small overtravel, for example 0.05 mm to 0.10 mm, then verify that the feeder does not deform and the component does not tilt. 4. Set placement force: start at 0.15 N to 0.25 N, which is below the 0.5 N limit and above the machine minimum of 0.05 N. 5. Run a trial: inspect for missed picks, tilted components, tombstoning, paste squeeze-out, and nozzle marks. 6. If paste contact is insufficient, increase force in small steps, for example 0.05 N increments, while staying below 0.5 N. 7. If cracks or nozzle marks appear, reduce force, check nozzle alignment, and consider a softer or larger contact area if the machine and component geometry allow. 8. Lock the recipe after first-article inspection and record the nozzle number, pick height, force, vacuum level, and inspection results."},"defect":{"symptom":"Small ceramic capacitors show corner cracks after reflow, and some units have visible nozzle marks on the top surface.","cause":"The nozzle tip is too small or worn, causing point loading, and the placement force is too high for the component's mechanical limit.","action":"Stop the line, verify the component supplier's maximum placement force, reduce force to a validated lower value, replace or realign the nozzle, and run a controlled trial with inspection for cracks, nozzle marks, and paste contact. If cracks persist, escalate to the component supplier and review nozzle geometry and board support."},"takeaways":["Derive pick height from component height, pocket depth and tip length, then prove it on a dry run before production.","Keep placement force between the seating requirement and the component's stated maximum, with the nozzle contact area checked.","Track nozzle wear and vacuum trend, because a degrading tip changes both the pick and the load without any program change."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Choosing a nozzle by body size alone","instead":"Check vacuum coverage, tip wear and clearance to neighbouring parts as well."},{"mistake":"Raising force to fix a missed paste contact","instead":"Inspect nozzle alignment, paste deposit and board support first, since force is rarely the real limit."},{"mistake":"Replacing nozzles on a fixed calendar count","instead":"Replace on measured wear or a validated cycle count, and record the evidence."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pick height","def":"The vertical position where the nozzle contacts the component in the feeder or tray."},{"term":"Overtravel","def":"The extra downward travel after first contact, which establishes vacuum without compressing the part."},{"term":"Dye-and-pry","def":"A destructive test that reveals cracks by forcing dye into them and separating the part."},{"term":"Point load","def":"Force concentrated on a small area, which is what causes cracks in brittle components."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m05l01"],"skills":["Lesson m05l01: Machine types and what accuracy, repeatability and throughput really mean"]}},{"lessonId":"m05l03","moduleId":"m05","stageId":"placement","title":"Fiducials, board recognition, vision calibration, measuring placement offset","goals":[{"text":"Diagnose offset, skew, rotation, missing, wrong-part and polarity defects from measured data and name the mechanism.","competencies":["K4"]},{"text":"Verify a placement program and setup before production and identify a missing or expired calibration.","competencies":["K4"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m05l03-01","q-m05l03-02","q-m05l03-03","q-m05l03-04","q-m05l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m05l03.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: board with global and local fiducials, camera search box, dot-grid vision calibration target, offset vector with DX, DY and rotation, skew versus offset and a photo of a copper fiducial","caption":"Fiducials, vision calibration and placement offset: coordinate system, offset vector and rotation"}]},{"h":"Fiducials define the coordinate system","p":["A fiducial is the reference feature the vision system uses to locate the board and, at component level, to locate the part relative to the nozzle. Global fiducials usually sit on board edges or panel rails and establish origin, scale and rotation.","Local fiducials sit near dense or fine-pitch components and compensate local warpage, panel bow or thermal expansion. Fiducial quality usually matters more than nominal position: a clean, high-contrast circular or cross fiducial with a stable edge measures better than a poorly defined shape. Check diameter, contrast and placement tolerance against the placement equipment datasheet and the fabrication drawing."]},{"h":"Board recognition combines support, vision and geometry","p":["Recognition starts when the conveyor or pallet presents the board. The machine may use edge sensors, clamps, pins or vision to find it, and vision-based recognition typically measures two or more fiducials and computes a transformation from machine to board coordinates.","Poor support lets the measured fiducial positions move during placement. A program with the wrong fiducial type, wrong coordinates or wrong panel origin places components consistently in the wrong place. Before production, confirm that support height, clamp force, conveyor speed and fiducial search window match the board type and the machine setup sheet."]},{"h":"Vision calibration links pixels to coordinates","p":["Vision calibration converts an image measurement into a machine coordinate measurement. Camera calibration fixes the relationship between pixel distance and physical distance, including scale, centre offset and, on some systems, lens distortion. Nozzle or placement calibration fixes the relationship between nozzle centre and measured component centre.","Calibration that is missing, expired or done with the wrong camera, lens, nozzle or lighting lets the machine report a coordinate the component never reached. Check calibration status as part of setup verification, not after a defect appears, and verify interval and pass criteria against the equipment datasheet and the plant calibration procedure."]},{"h":"Offset is a vector, not a distance","p":["Placement offset usually arrives as X and Y displacement from target, and a single magnitude hides direction: 0.05 mm in X and 0.05 mm in Y is not the same as a 0.07 mm diagonal.","Diagnose with the sign and magnitude of X, Y and rotation. A constant offset across many boards points to board coordinates or calibration. An offset that changes with board position points to support, conveyor or fiducial recognition. An offset that changes with component type points to nozzle, feeder or component vision."]},{"h":"Skew and rotation are not offset","p":["Skew is angular misalignment against the board coordinate system. Rotation error can appear as a component angle error or as an asymmetric offset between opposite ends of the part, and a component can be centred yet rotated, or displaced because it rotated about a point away from its centre.","Small passives, odd-form parts and parts near board edges, where the vision field of view is limited, rotate most often. Check the acceptable rotation limit against the assembly drawing, the IPC-A-610 class requirements and the footprint geometry."]},{"h":"Missing, wrong-part and polarity are detected differently","p":["Component vision reports a missing part when it finds nothing at the expected location after placement, and post-placement inspection reports it when it finds an empty footprint.","Wrong-part defects surface through component height, body size, terminal count, marking or feeder configuration. Polarity surfaces through orientation-sensitive features: a dot, chamfer, notch, asymmetric terminal or marking. Feeder setup and program verification prevent some of these, and vision catches others. A missing component can also come from a failed pick, a feeder that did not advance, or a part dropped in transfer."]},{"h":"Verify program geometry, not only part numbers","p":["A placement program carries part numbers, coordinates, rotation, pick height, place height, nozzle selection, feeder assignment and vision parameters. Verification confirms that the coordinate origin matches the fiducial scheme, that rotation matches the footprint, and that the selected nozzle and feeder are physically installed.","A program can pass a part-number check and still cause defects through wrong fiducial coordinates, a wrong component centre offset, or a vision search window too small. Prove the placement with a dry run or test board before production, especially after a program revision, nozzle change, feeder change or calibration event."]},{"h":"Calibration status is part of setup","p":["Before production, confirm that camera calibration, nozzle calibration and any component-specific vision calibration are current and inside tolerance. A record that is missing, expired or tied to a different nozzle, camera, lens or feeder leaves the setup unverified.","Missing calibration produces symptoms that look like mechanical drift: a constant X or Y offset, inconsistent rotation, poor repeatability. Stop the setup, perform or confirm the calibration, and re-run a verification board before releasing production."]}],"calc":{"task":"A placement program targets a 0402 resistor at X = 120.000 mm, Y = 80.000 mm, rotation = 0 degrees. A measured placement result is X = 120.045 mm, Y = 79.985 mm, rotation = 0.6 degrees. The assembly drawing allows a maximum placement offset of 0.050 mm and a maximum rotation of 0.5 degrees. Calculate the X offset, Y offset, total offset, and determine whether the placement passes or fails.","solution":"1. Calculate X offset: measured X minus target X = 120.045 mm - 120.000 mm = +0.045 mm. 2. Calculate Y offset: measured Y minus target Y = 79.985 mm - 80.000 mm = -0.015 mm. 3. Calculate total offset using the vector magnitude: sqrt(0.045^2 + 0.015^2) = sqrt(0.002025 + 0.000225) = sqrt(0.002250) = 0.0474 mm. 4. Compare total offset to the drawing limit: 0.0474 mm is less than 0.050 mm, so the offset passes. 5. Compare rotation to the drawing limit: 0.6 degrees is greater than 0.5 degrees, so the rotation fails. 6. Overall result: the placement fails because rotation exceeds the allowed limit, even though the total offset is within the allowed limit."},"defect":{"symptom":"A 0.5 mm pitch QFP is placed with a consistent +0.08 mm X offset on the first three boards after a program revision. The component is centered in Y and has no rotation error. The first board also shows one missing 0402 resistor near the board edge.","cause":"The program revision changed the global fiducial coordinates or the fiducial search window, causing the vision system to calculate the board origin incorrectly. The missing 0402 near the board edge may be related to an incorrect pick position, feeder misalignment, or a nozzle that failed to pick the part after the program change.","action":"Stop the setup. Verify the fiducial coordinates, fiducial type, and search window against the board drawing and the approved program. Re-run a dry run or test board with fiducial measurement and component vision enabled. Check the 0402 feeder pitch, pick position, nozzle condition, and vacuum. If the offset remains, verify camera and nozzle calibration status and perform the required calibration before releasing production."},"takeaways":["Measure at least two fiducials and verify their coordinates against the drawing, because a board origin error looks exactly like a calibration fault.","Record offset as a signed X and Y vector with rotation, since a magnitude and a direction demand different fixes.","Check calibration scope before the run, because an expired certificate produces offsets no coordinate edit will cure."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Editing program coordinates to remove a constant offset","instead":"Verify fiducial coordinates and calibration first, then decide whether the program or the machine is wrong."},{"mistake":"Judging placement from a total offset number alone","instead":"Read X, Y and rotation separately, because a passing magnitude can hide a failing rotation."},{"mistake":"Accepting a part-number check as program verification","instead":"Verify fiducial origin, rotation and installed nozzle against the board, not only the BOM."}],"terms":[{"term":"Global fiducial","def":"A board or panel reference mark used to establish origin, scale and rotation for the whole board."},{"term":"Local fiducial","def":"A reference mark near a fine-pitch area that compensates local distortion or warpage."},{"term":"Vision calibration","def":"The established relationship between camera pixel measurements and machine coordinates."},{"term":"Skew","def":"Angular misalignment of a placed component against the board coordinate system."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m05l02"],"skills":["Lesson m05l02: Feeders, nozzles, pick height and placement force limits by component"]}},{"lessonId":"m05l04","moduleId":"m05","stageId":"placement","title":"Placement defect atlas: tombstone, skew, tilt, missing, wrong part, polarity","goals":[{"text":"Diagnose offset, skew, rotation, missing, wrong-part and polarity defects from measured data and name the mechanism.","competencies":["K4"]},{"text":"Attribute a defect to placement rather than to printing or reflow using the evidence sequence.","competencies":["K4","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-011"],"quiz":["q-m05l04-01","q-m05l04-02","q-m05l04-03","q-m05l04-04","q-m05l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m05l04.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: top-view tiles for a correct part, offset, skew, tilt, tombstone, missing, wrong part and reversed polarity, the SPI to post-reflow AOI evidence sequence, defect grouping by feeder, nozzle and location, a tombstone photo and an action-limit band","caption":"Placement defect atlas: offset, skew, tilt, tombstone, missing, wrong part and polarity, with the evidence sequence"}]},{"h":"Evidence sequence before naming a mechanism","p":["Start from measured data, not from a visual guess. For each defect, collect SPI volume and pad location, pre-reflow AOI position and orientation, placement head logs, feeder slot and nozzle ID, the reflow profile, and post-reflow AOI or X-ray.","A component misplaced before reflow over centred paste of correct volume points to placement. Paste offset, volume error or a bridge before placement points to printing. Correct pre-reflow placement that shifts, lifts or rotates only after reflow points to wetting or the thermal profile.","Tombstone is usually a reflow wetting imbalance, though pre-reflow tilt or paste imbalance makes placement or printing contributory. Patterns by feeder, nozzle, board location and time separate systematic placement faults from random handling faults."]},{"h":"Offset","p":["Offset is the displacement of the component centre from the land centre. Read X and Y from AOI, then compute magnitude and direction.","Feeder pickup offset, tape pocket deformation, nozzle wear, vision calibration drift, board support sag and tape peel force all produce it. A consistent vector from one feeder slot points to feeder pickup or the tape pocket. A consistent direction by board location points to fiducial recognition, vision calibration or support. Random offset across feeders and nozzles points to vacuum, component drop or pocket release. Set action limits from your AOI limits, land geometry and equipment datasheet, because the acceptable offset depends on pad size, component tolerance and wetting."]},{"h":"Skew","p":["Skew is angular misalignment of the body against the land edges. Measure the angle from AOI and correlate it with nozzle ID, feeder slot and component orientation.","Rotation during pick, nozzle spin, a component sitting crooked in its pocket, tape pocket geometry, vision angle error and head rotation error all cause it. Skew with offset points to pick or pocket; skew without offset points to nozzle rotation or vision angle. Skew present in pre-reflow AOI belongs to placement; a component that rotates only in reflow belongs to wetting imbalance or the profile. Check angle limits against the AOI program and the equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Tilt and tombstone","p":["Tilt is a non-coplanar body, and tombstone is severe tilt where one end lifts while the other stays wetted. Measure Z height, shadow, focus and, where needed, X-ray.","Nozzle height, component thickness variation, feeder pocket, board warp, paste height and reflow surface tension all play a part. Pre-reflow tilt points to placement, nozzle, feeder or board support; pre-reflow paste imbalance points to printing. Correct placement and paste followed by lift in reflow points to wetting imbalance, profile or board warp. Tombstone is often filed as a reflow defect, yet placement contributes whenever the part sits tilted, skewed or on uneven paste."]},{"h":"Missing component","p":["A missing part can come from an empty feeder, a failed pick, vacuum loss, a dropped component, a vision reject, a program skip or a pocket failure.","Diagnose in order: pick and place logs, feeder count, pre-reflow AOI, post-reflow AOI. No pick in the log points to an empty feeder, pocket or feeder count. A pick with no place points to vacuum, a drop or nozzle height. A logged place confirmed by pre-reflow AOI but absent after reflow points to reflow, transport or handling."]},{"h":"Wrong part","p":["Wrong part means the placed component does not match the BOM, the program or the expected value. Feeder misload, wrong reel, wrong program, manual substitution, a tape splice and feeder slot mismatch all produce it.","Compare the BOM with the feeder map, scan the reel barcode, review AOI component recognition, and use X-ray or electrical test where the marking is not visible. A wrong part in pre-reflow AOI belongs to feeder loading or the program; a correct part with a wrong value usually means a misload or a wrong reel. On THT, tray or tube loading and the insertion program enter the same way."]},{"h":"Polarity","p":["Polarity defects reverse a diode, IC, tantalum, electrolytic or another polarised part. Feeder orientation, pocket orientation, manual loading, a disabled vision polarity check, nozzle rotation and program angle all cause them.","Inspect the polarity mark or pin 1, review the pre-reflow AOI polarity check, and correlate with the feeder slot. A reversed part in pre-reflow AOI belongs to feeder orientation or the program; correct polarity that reverses later points to reflow rotation, handling or a manual step. On THT, insertion direction and tray orientation dominate."]},{"h":"Attribution and corrective action","p":["Attribution rests on the evidence sequence, not on one image: SPI, pre-reflow AOI, the reflow profile, post-reflow AOI or X-ray, and feeder and nozzle logs.","Systematic defects by feeder slot point to loading, pickup or pocket. Systematic defects by board location point to vision, fiducials or support. Random defects across feeders and nozzles point to vacuum, drop or pocket release. Defects that appear only after reflow point to wetting or the profile, and paste defects before placement point to printing.","Use your acceptance standard for disposition, but set action limits from process capability, land geometry and the equipment datasheet, and document containment, root cause, corrective action and verification data."]}],"calc":{"task":"During a 5000-board run, pre-reflow AOI reports 12 components with center offset greater than 0.15 mm, 8 components with skew greater than 1.5 deg, 3 missing components, 2 wrong-part components, and 1 reversed polarity component. Offset data: 10 of 12 offsets are +X 0.18 mm and Y 0.02 mm, all from feeder slot 14. Skew data: 7 of 8 skews are 2.0 deg clockwise, all from nozzle 3. Missing: 3 all from feeder slot 22. Wrong part: 2 boards have a capacitor from feeder slot 31 measured 100 nF instead of 10 nF. Polarity: 1 board has a diode reversed from feeder slot 41. SPI shows paste centered and in volume. Calculate defect rates, ppm, offset vector, and attribute each defect to placement, printing, or reflow using the evidence sequence.","solution":"Rate calculation: offset = 12/5000 = 2400 ppm; skew = 8/5000 = 1600 ppm; missing = 3/5000 = 600 ppm; wrong part = 2/5000 = 400 ppm; polarity = 1/5000 = 200 ppm; total = 26/5000 = 5200 ppm. Offset vector: magnitude = sqrt(0.18^2 + 0.02^2) = 0.181 mm; direction = atan2(0.02, 0.18) = 6.3 deg from +X. Offset attribution: 10 of 12 offsets are from feeder slot 14, SPI paste is centered, and the defect is present in pre-reflow AOI. Attribute to placement feeder pickup or tape pocket, not printing or reflow. Skew attribution: 7 of 8 skews are from nozzle 3 and are present in pre-reflow AOI. Attribute to placement nozzle rotation or pick rotation, not printing or reflow. Missing attribution: 3 missing components are all from feeder slot 22. Check pick/place logs; if no pick, feeder or tape pocket; if pick but no place, nozzle vacuum. Attribute to placement. Wrong part attribution: measured capacitance is 100 nF instead of 10 nF from feeder slot 31. Attribute to placement feeder misload or wrong reel, not printing or reflow. Polarity attribution: reversed diode from feeder slot 41 is present in pre-reflow AOI. Attribute to placement feeder orientation or program, not printing or reflow. Evidence rule: because SPI is normal and defects are present before reflow, printing and reflow are not primary causes; if a defect appears only after reflow, reflow would be primary."},"defect":{"symptom":"Pre-reflow AOI shows a systematic +X offset on one feeder slot, while SPI shows centered paste and post-reflow AOI shows the same offset without additional rotation or lift.","cause":"Feeder pickup position or tape pocket geometry is shifting the component before placement, producing a repeatable offset vector from that feeder slot.","action":"Contain the affected feeder slot, re-teach feeder pickup position, inspect tape peel and pocket, verify nozzle vacuum, then run a verification lot and confirm offset distribution against your AOI limits and equipment datasheet."},"takeaways":["Capture SPI and pre-reflow AOI before reflow, because the position of the part before and after reflow is what separates placement from wetting.","Group defects by feeder slot, nozzle and board location, since a pattern names the mechanism faster than any single image.","Set placement action limits from pad geometry and component tolerance, not from a round number that ignores both."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Calling tombstone a reflow defect without checking pre-reflow tilt","instead":"Read pre-reflow AOI first, because a tilted part tombstone even on a good profile."},{"mistake":"Diagnosing a missing part from a post-reflow image alone","instead":"Check the pick and place logs to see whether the machine ever picked it."},{"mistake":"Treating a single offset sample as a direction","instead":"Measure across boards, since scatter and a consistent vector need different fixes."}],"terms":[{"term":"Skew","def":"Angular misalignment of a placed component against its land pattern."},{"term":"Tombstone","def":"A chip component standing on one end after reflow because one joint wetted before the other."},{"term":"Polarity defect","def":"A polarised component placed in the reverse orientation."},{"term":"Action limit","def":"The measured value at which the process is stopped or investigated, set from capability and geometry."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m05l03"],"skills":["Lesson m05l03: Fiducials, board recognition, vision calibration, measuring placement offset"]}},{"lessonId":"m05l05","moduleId":"m05","stageId":"placement","title":"Component library setup, program verification and first-article check","goals":[{"text":"Verify a placement program and setup before production and identify a missing or expired calibration.","competencies":["K4"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-011"],"quiz":["q-m05l05-01","q-m05l05-02","q-m05l05-03","q-m05l05-04","q-m05l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m05l05.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: component library entry fields, physical sample check against the reel pocket, program verification screen, calibration certificate marked missing or expired equals failed setup, a first-article inspection photo and a release record","caption":"Library setup, program verification and first-article check"}]},{"h":"Pre-production placement verification","p":["Before production, verify that the placement program, component library, feeder setup, nozzle selection and machine calibration represent the approved design and process.","The check has to prove the machine can place the right components, in the right orientation, at the right coordinates, with acceptable accuracy. Run a dry run or offline simulation first, then a pre-production sample under controlled conditions."]},{"h":"Component library setup","p":["The component library holds package geometry, body size, pin or pad pitch, polarity, rotation, pick point, nozzle, feeder type, vision settings and placement tolerance. Verify each field against the component datasheet and a physical sample.","On polarised parts, confirm the library polarity matches the silkscreen and the feeder orientation. On fine-pitch packages, confirm that tape, reel or feeder geometry does not obscure the pick point and the vision reference."]},{"h":"Program and setup checks","p":["Check the coordinate origin, fiducial pattern, panel offsets, mirror settings, units, component side and layer assignment.","Confirm the feeder map matches the physical feeders, that nozzles suit component size and clearance, and that the program commands no collision with panel edges, connectors or tall components. Review the sequence for pickup order, nozzle changes and vision cycles."]},{"h":"Calibration status before release","p":["Placement accuracy depends on machine calibration, vision calibration, feeder calibration, nozzle condition and conveyance alignment.","Before release, confirm that required calibration certificates exist, are traceable, sit inside the interval set by the equipment manufacturer or your calibration plan, and are supported by verification results. A certificate that is missing, expired or outside the required environmental or usage conditions leaves the setup unverified until calibration is complete and recorded."]},{"h":"Pre-production sample inspection","p":["Run a sample and inspect for missing components, wrong components, polarity and rotation errors, tombstoning and poor seating, using AOI, X-ray or manual inspection as the case needs.","Measure placement coordinates against the program and compare the deviation with the tolerance from the equipment datasheet or the process capability study. Record the measured values, the method, the operator and the disposition."]},{"h":"Acceptance criteria and release","p":["Define pass and fail before the sample runs. Criteria cover placement coordinate tolerance, polarity, rotation, missing or wrong components, and calibration status, taken from the paste, feeder, nozzle and placement equipment datasheets and confirmed against your own capability.","Release production only when program, setup, calibration and sample inspection all meet the defined criteria."]},{"h":"Change control and records","p":["Any change to the program, component library, feeder, nozzle, panel, fiducial or calibration status repeats the relevant verification steps.","Store the program version, library version, calibration records, sample results and approval with the production record, so the setup stays traceable and containment is possible when a defect turns up later."]}],"calc":{"task":"A placement program specifies a tolerance of 0.05 mm for a component center. The pre-production sample shows a measured deviation of 0.03 mm. The machine calibration certificate is missing. Determine whether the setup can be released.","solution":"Compare the measured deviation with the tolerance. The deviation is within tolerance. Check the calibration certificate. The certificate is missing, so the setup is not verified. Reject release, complete calibration, then repeat the pre-production sample."},"defect":{"symptom":"AOI reports repeated polarity errors on polarized components after a feeder change.","cause":"The component library polarity or feeder orientation did not match the physical part, and the program was not reverified after the change.","action":"Stop the line, quarantine affected boards, correct the library and feeder orientation, run a pre-production sample, inspect polarity and rotation, update the change record, and release only after verification."},"takeaways":["Verify the library against a physical sample, because a polarity or rotation field copied from a datasheet can still disagree with the reel.","Treat a missing or expired calibration as a failed setup, whatever the sample deviation shows.","Record program and library versions with the sample result, so a later defect can be traced to the configuration that made it."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Releasing on a good sample while calibration has expired","instead":"Confirm the certificate first, since the sample may pass today and fail after an axis drift tomorrow."},{"mistake":"Checking part numbers without checking pick point and vision reference","instead":"Confirm both for fine-pitch parts, because tape or feeder geometry can obstruct them."},{"mistake":"Skipping reverification after a feeder change","instead":"Repeat the polarity and rotation checks, which is exactly where a feeder change goes wrong."}],"terms":[{"term":"Component library","def":"The stored geometry, orientation and handling data the machine uses for each part number."},{"term":"Feeder map","def":"The table that assigns each part number to a physical feeder slot."},{"term":"Pre-production sample","def":"A controlled first run inspected before the line is released."},{"term":"Calibration interval","def":"The period within which an instrument or axis must be recalibrated."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m05l04"],"skills":["Lesson m05l04: Placement defect atlas: tombstone, skew, tilt, missing, wrong part, polarity"]}},{"lessonId":"m05l06","moduleId":"m05","stageId":"placement","title":"Case: shift and skew on a fine-pitch QFP","goals":[{"text":"Diagnose offset, skew, rotation, missing, wrong-part and polarity defects from measured data and name the mechanism.","competencies":["K4"]},{"text":"Attribute a defect to placement rather than to printing or reflow using the evidence sequence.","competencies":["K4","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-004","src-011","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m05l06-01","q-m05l06-02","q-m05l06-03","q-m05l06-04","q-m05l06-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m05l06.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: fine-pitch QFP measured on two opposite corners with DX, DY and rotation, lead-on-pad tolerance band, SPI to reflow evidence sequence, a QFP photo, internal limit nested inside IPC-A-610 acceptance and a list of placement mechanisms","caption":"Case: shift and skew on a fine-pitch QFP — measure, attribute, limit"}]},{"h":"Why fine-pitch QFP placement is a data problem","p":["A fine-pitch QFP exposes placement error because the positional margin is small next to the lead pitch. On a 0.5 mm pitch part, a lateral shift of 0.10 mm already eats 20 % of the pitch, and 0.5 degrees of rotation moves an outer lead by tens of micrometres depending on body size.","The practical aim is to separate true placement error from measurement noise, paste print variation and reflow-induced movement, using placement verification, AOI, SPI and reflow inspection in sequence rather than one end-of-line image."]},{"h":"Offset, skew and rotation","p":["Offset is translation of the component centre from the intended centre, reported as X and Y error. Skew is a positional mismatch between opposite sides, seen as one side closer to its pads than the other. Rotation is angular error about the centre.","On a QFP a small rotation looks like skew when measured at one corner, so measure two opposite corners or use fiducial-based centre and angle data. For fine-pitch QFPs, placement targets usually sit around ±0.05 mm to ±0.10 mm for centre position and ±0.5° to ±1.0° for angle, verified against the placement machine datasheet, the component datasheet and the IPC-A-610 class requirements."]},{"h":"Missing, wrong-part and polarity are a different family","p":["A missing QFP is usually a feeder, vision or placement-cycle failure rather than a subtle drift. Wrong-part defects come from feeder misload, wrong program selection or gaps in barcode and serialisation control.","Polarity defects on QFPs show at the pin 1 marking, chamfer, dot or bevel, and the program can miss them with the wrong part orientation or a backwards tape. These defects are binary — present or absent, correct or reversed — and belong apart from the dimensional errors of offset, skew and rotation."]},{"h":"Attributing defects with the evidence sequence","p":["The strongest sequence is SPI before placement, placement verification during placement, AOI after placement, and final inspection after reflow.","Acceptable paste with a component placed outside tolerance makes placement the likely cause. Paste offset, bridging or volume variation that the component follows makes printing the likely cause. An acceptable part after placement that moves during reflow makes reflow the cause, through wetting imbalance, paste collapse or thermal effects. Do not blame placement when the pre-placement evidence already shows the same pattern."]},{"h":"Common placement mechanisms on fine-pitch QFP","p":["Poor fiducial recognition, camera calibration drift, nozzle wear, vacuum loss, feeder repeatability, tape peel variation and program coordinate errors all appear on this package.","Nozzle choice matters: a nozzle that presses the body too hard tilts the part, and poor vacuum lets it shift in transfer. Feeder problems often produce repeatable skew or rotation on one board side, because the part is presented at a slightly inconsistent angle. Program errors give a consistent offset across many boards, while mechanical wear gives gradual drift or intermittent outliers."]},{"h":"Separating placement from printing and reflow","p":["Printing defects show as paste offset, volume variation or shape distortion at pad level. A component that follows the paste points to printing.","Placement defects show as position or angle error while the paste stays acceptable. Reflow defects show as movement after the paste melted: acceptable post-placement AOI but shifted post-reflow AOI, tombstoning or coplanarity problems. Surface tension can correct small errors on a fine-pitch QFP only where paste volume and pad geometry are balanced, so partial self-centring points at reflow."]},{"h":"A practical diagnosis workflow","p":["Confirm the defect type first: missing, wrong part, polarity, offset, skew or rotation. Then establish whether it is random, dependent on board position, or repeatable across boards.","Random missing parts point to feeder or vacuum. Repeatable offset or rotation points to program, fiducial or calibration. Board-position-dependent skew points to table flatness, support or nozzle transfer. A defect that appears only after reflow needs pre- and post-reflow measurements compared, and a defect that appears at SPI needs printing investigated before placement."]},{"h":"Standards and internal limits together","p":["IPC-A-610 gives the acceptability criteria for placement and soldering without replacing machine capability targets or design-specific limits. IPC-7351 supplies land pattern and package data for pad-to-lead relationships, and IPC-7525 and the related stencil guidance help evaluate paste print capability, with exact aperture and volume targets verified against your paste, stencil and printer datasheets.","Keep internal limits tighter than the final acceptability limit, so placement can be corrected before a solder defect appears."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 0.50 mm pitch QFP has a body diagonal of 14.0 mm. Placement verification reports centre offset X = +0.08 mm, Y = -0.06 mm, and rotation = +0.8°. The placement machine specification for this part is ±0.10 mm centre and ±0.5° angle. Calculate the resultant centre offset, the outer-lead displacement caused by rotation, and the approximate worst-case lead displacement if centre offset and rotation act in the same direction. State whether the defect is placement-related if SPI shows acceptable paste print and post-placement AOI shows the same offset pattern.","solution":"1. Calculate resultant centre offset: sqrt(0.08^2 + 0.06^2) = sqrt(0.0064 + 0.0036) = sqrt(0.0100) = 0.10 mm. 2. Compare centre offset to machine limit: 0.10 mm is at the ±0.10 mm limit, so it is borderline and should be investigated. 3. Calculate rotation displacement at the outer lead. The distance from centre to corner is half the body diagonal: 14.0 mm / 2 = 7.0 mm. 4. Convert rotation to radians: 0.8° × π / 180 = 0.01396 rad. 5. Calculate arc displacement: 7.0 mm × 0.01396 = 0.098 mm. 6. Compare rotation displacement to angle limit: 0.8° exceeds the ±0.5° machine limit, so rotation is out of specification. 7. Estimate worst-case combined lead displacement if centre offset and rotation act in the same direction: 0.10 mm + 0.098 mm = 0.198 mm, approximately 0.20 mm. 8. Compare to pitch: 0.20 mm is 40% of the 0.50 mm pitch, which is a significant risk for lead misalignment, bridging, or insufficient solder joint formation. 9. Attribution: because SPI shows acceptable paste print and the same offset pattern is present at post-placement AOI, the defect is most likely placement-related rather than printing-related. If the part were acceptable after placement but shifted after reflow, reflow would be the more likely cause."},"defect":{"symptom":"A fine-pitch QFP is consistently shifted toward one side of the board, with one row of leads closer to the paste than the opposite row. Post-placement AOI reports centre offset of about 0.12 mm and rotation of about 0.7°. SPI shows acceptable paste print. The defect repeats at the same board location and is worse on the side of the board nearest the feeder.","cause":"The repeatable pattern points to a placement presentation or transfer issue rather than random reflow movement. Likely mechanisms include feeder tape peel variation, nozzle wear, vacuum inconsistency, or a slightly misaligned feeder causing the part to be picked up at an angle. Because the defect is board-location dependent and appears before reflow, printing and reflow are less likely primary causes.","action":"Stop the line if the defect exceeds the internal placement limit. Verify the feeder for the QFP, inspect the tape peel force and cover tape alignment, and check the nozzle for wear, vacuum, and centring. Re-run a placement verification test with a known-good feeder and nozzle. If the defect moves with the feeder, replace or recalibrate the feeder. If the defect remains, check placement camera calibration, fiducial recognition, and nozzle transfer settings. After correction, confirm with SPI, placement verification, and post-placement AOI before releasing production."},"takeaways":["Measure centre offset and rotation on two opposite corners, because a small rotation at one corner reads as skew.","Attribute with the evidence sequence — SPI, placement, AOI, reflow — and do not blame placement when the paste already shows the pattern.","Set internal placement limits tighter than IPC-A-610 acceptance, so correction happens before a solder defect exists."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Judging a QFP from a single end-of-line image","instead":"Compare pre-placement, post-placement and post-reflow data before naming the mechanism."},{"mistake":"Treating a repeatable offset as reflow self-centring","instead":"Check whether the offset exists at post-placement AOI, which settles the question."},{"mistake":"Blaming the program for drift that repeats by board location","instead":"Check table flatness, support and feeder presentation, which produce location-dependent patterns."}],"terms":[{"term":"Centre offset","def":"The translation of a component centre from its intended position, reported as X and Y."},{"term":"Rotation error","def":"Angular displacement of the component about its centre after placement."},{"term":"Wetting imbalance","def":"Uneven solder wetting that pulls a component out of position during reflow."},{"term":"Coplanarity","def":"How well the leads or terminations of a component lie in one plane."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m05l05"],"skills":["Lesson m05l05: Component library setup, program verification and first-article check"]}},{"lessonId":"m06l01","moduleId":"m06","stageId":"reflow","title":"Heat transfer in the oven: convection, conduction, radiation, zone structure","goals":[{"text":"Run a thermal survey and read the profile at the coolest and hottest locations, not at the oven setpoints.","competencies":["K5"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-005","src-008","src-017"],"quiz":["q-m06l01-01","q-m06l01-02","q-m06l01-03","q-m06l01-04","q-m06l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"What the profile controls","p":["A reflow profile is the temperature history the board and its components actually see, not the oven setpoints. Ramp, soak, time above liquidus, peak and cooling describe it, and between them they govern flux activation, flux removal, wetting, intermetallic formation, voiding and thermal stress.","Because thermal mass differs, the same oven recipe produces different results on a different assembly, so measure the profile on the board. The best profile is not the hottest or the fastest: it is the exposure that makes reliable joints while keeping board and components inside their qualified limits."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 900 380' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='SAC305 reflow profile with the lesson numbers: ramp 1-3 C/s, soak 140-180 C for 60-120 s, 30-90 s above 219 C liquidus, peak 235-245 C under a 250 C ceiling, cooling 2 C/s or faster'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;fill:#e2e8f0}.d{fill:#94a3b8}.t{fill:#53d1cc}.r{fill:#f44336}.o{fill:#ff9800}</style> <line x1='70' y1='300' x2='870' y2='300' stroke='#94a3b8'/><line x1='70' y1='300' x2='70' y2='30' stroke='#94a3b8'/> <text x='40' y='305' font-size='11' class='d' text-anchor='end'>RT</text> <text x='40' y='192' font-size='11' class='d' text-anchor='end'>150</text> <text x='40' y='118' font-size='11' class='d' text-anchor='end'>219</text> <text x='40' y='93' font-size='11' class='d' text-anchor='end'>250</text> <text x='20' y='22' font-size='11' class='d'>°C</text><text x='870' y='322' font-size='11' class='d' text-anchor='end'>time, s</text> <line x1='70' y1='115' x2='870' y2='115' stroke='#f44336' stroke-dasharray='6 4'/><text x='866' y='110' font-size='11' class='r' text-anchor='end'>liquidus 219 °C (solidus 217 °C)</text> <line x1='70' y1='90' x2='870' y2='90' stroke='#ff9800' stroke-dasharray='3 4'/><text x='866' y='85' font-size='11' class='o' text-anchor='end'>ceiling 250 °C</text> <rect x='70' y='180' width='800' height='30' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.08)'/><text x='76' y='176' font-size='11' class='t'>soak band 140-180 °C</text> <path d='M70,296 C140,280 190,230 250,205 C330,190 380,183 440,180 C480,178 500,150 520,118 C545,105 560,98 580,98 C600,98 615,108 630,118 C660,150 700,250 760,282 C790,295 830,298 870,299' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2.5'/> <line x1='520' y1='115' x2='630' y2='115' stroke='#e2e8f0' stroke-width='3'/><text x='575' y='135' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle'>TAL 30-90 s</text> <text x='580' y='70' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle'>peak 235-245 °C, tp ≤ 30 s</text> <text x='140' y='250' font-size='11' class='t'>ramp 1-3 °C/s</text> <text x='345' y='225' font-size='11' class='t' text-anchor='middle'>soak 60-120 s</text> <text x='745' y='230' font-size='11' class='t'>cooling ≥ 2 °C/s</text><text x='745' y='245' font-size='11' class='d'>(typical 1-4 °C/s)</text> <rect x='70' y='330' width='180' height='4' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.5)'/><rect x='250' y='330' width='190' height='4' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.5)'/><rect x='440' y='330' width='190' height='4' fill='rgba(244,67,54,.6)'/><rect x='630' y='330' width='240' height='4' fill='rgba(148,163,184,.5)'/> <text x='160' y='352' font-size='11' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>PREHEAT</text><text x='345' y='352' font-size='11' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>SOAK</text><text x='535' y='352' font-size='11' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>REFLOW</text><text x='750' y='352' font-size='11' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>COOLING</text> <text x='70' y='372' font-size='10' class='d'>J-STD-020 classification window tL 60-150 s; IPC-7530 and the paste datasheet narrow it to 30-90 s. Paste TDS always wins.</text> </svg>","caption":"SAC305 reflow profile with the lesson envelope: ramp 1-3 °C/s, soak 140-180 °C, 30-90 s above the 219 °C liquidus, peak 235-245 °C under the 250 °C ceiling, cooling ≥ 2 °C/s"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m06l01.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: side view of a convection reflow oven with preheat, soak, reflow and cooling zones, convection, conduction and radiation icons, a profiling board with thermocouples, and a profile shape with ramp, soak, TAL, peak and cooling","caption":"Reflow oven zones, heat-transfer paths and the profile shape; numeric targets come from the paste datasheet and the figure above"},{"t":"table","caption":"Typical SAC305 profile envelope — paste TDS always wins","head":["Zone","Typical target","What it controls"],"rows":[["Preheat","1-3 °C/s ramp","Thermal shock, flux activation"],["Soak / TAL","Soak 60-120 s (ts); TAL 30-90 s above liquidus","Flux chemistry window"],["Reflow / peak","Liquidus 219 C, typical peak 235-245 °C, ceiling 250 °C","Intermetallic formation"],["Cooling","Fast, >= 2 °C/s where possible","Grain structure, sheen"]]}]},{"h":"Ramp and soak","p":["The ramp carries the assembly to the temperature where flux activates and solvents leave without violent boiling. Typical soak ranges are 140-180 °C for 90-120 s, checked against the paste and equipment datasheet.","A heavy board with large copper planes may need a longer soak to narrow the gap between small components and large connectors. Too short a soak leaves flux active too late, which raises solder balls and voiding; too long dries the paste and reduces wetting. Judge the soak from measured board temperature, never from oven air temperature."]},{"h":"Time above liquidus","p":["Time above liquidus is how long the alloy stays molten. It has to be long enough for wetting and flux removal and short enough to limit intermetallic growth and component exposure.","J-STD-020 bounds it: its classification profile sets tL, the time at liquidus, at 60-150 s for both alloy families. IPC-7530 and the paste datasheet narrow that into a working window — IPC-7530 typically calls for 30-90 s above liquidus, and the paste datasheet is the authority for the board being run. J-STD-020's own time figure, tp, is the time within 5 °C of the classification temperature, limited to 30 s on the Pb-free profile and 20 s for SnPb; it is a package qualification parameter, not the paste window for running a board.","Production profiles may use a different window if qualified. Take the liquidus temperature from the paste or alloy datasheet, because it depends on alloy and formulation, and check the measured time above liquidus at the worst-case thermocouple rather than at a convenient point."]},{"h":"Peak temperature","p":["Peak temperature is the highest measured temperature at the solder joint or component body, depending on where the thermocouple sits.","For SAC305, J-STD-020 gives no single peak: it gives classification temperatures by package size — 260 °C for small packages, 250 °C for large and 245 °C for very large at 1.6-2.5 mm thickness — and the full table is thickness times volume, where below 1.6 mm every volume allows 260 °C and above 2.5 mm the limits fall to 250 °C small and 245 °C large and very large. Each comes with tp, the time within 5 °C of that temperature, limited to 30 s.","Production peaks usually sit below the classification temperature with margin and are verified with thermocouples on the actual board. Exceeding the classification temperature damages components, delaminates packages or grows excessive intermetallic, and the peak must be judged together with time above liquidus, because a short peak and a long peak are not the same thermal exposure."]},{"h":"Cooling","p":["Cooling solidifies the joint and sets grain size, residual stress and warpage. Typical rates are 1-4 °C/s, verified against the paste and equipment datasheet.","Very fast cooling raises thermal shock and warpage, especially on thin boards and large BGAs. Very slow cooling produces a coarse solder structure and lets components move while the joint is still soft. Measure cooling with the same board thermocouples used for peak and time above liquidus, because the cooling curve reveals lag and gradients that the oven profile hides."]},{"h":"Why thermocouples go on the board","p":["Oven air temperature is not solder joint temperature. Board thickness, copper area, component mass, shielding and mounting method all create lag and gradients, so attach calibrated thermocouples with high-temperature adhesive or tape and minimal added mass.","Representative points are the centre of a large BGA, a small passive near a heavy connector, and a location near the board edge or panel rail. A thermocouple on a bare board rather than a populated one measures a profile production will never run."]},{"h":"Judging a measured profile","p":["Compare the measured peak with the J-STD-020 classification temperature for the package size, and the measured time above liquidus with the J-STD-020 window of 60-150 s, narrowed by IPC-7530 guidance of 30-90 s and by the paste datasheet.","Use the worst-case measured point, not the oven profile or a single thermocouple. A peak above the classification temperature, or a time above liquidus outside the datasheet window, leaves the assembly non-compliant unless a separate qualification supports the exposure. Moisture sensitivity level, floor life and bake requirements from J-STD-020 also decide whether the component tolerates the exposure at all."]},{"h":"Practical profile development","p":["Start from the paste supplier's recommended profile and the component qualification limits. Run a dummy board with thermocouples at the expected worst-case locations, then adjust zones to move the measured soak, peak and cooling toward target while keeping the curve smooth.","Repeat the measurements after any change to stackup, panelisation, component placement or oven loading. Record the profile with board type, oven model, conveyor speed, zone setpoints and thermocouple locations so the result can be reproduced."]}],"calc":{"task":"A SAC305 paste datasheet gives a solidus of 217 °C and a liquidus of 219 °C. A 1.6 mm board with 70 µm copper and a 12 mm BGA is reflowed. The target is to stay within the J-STD-020 classification temperature for the package size and a time above liquidus of 60-150 s, the J-STD-020 classification window. A measured profile shows ramp 1.5 °C/s, soak 165 °C for 90 s, peak 242 °C, time above liquidus 75 s, and cooling 2.5 °C/s. Build and judge the profile.","solution":"1. Confirm alloy liquidus from the paste datasheet: SAC305 has a solidus of 217 °C and a liquidus of 219 °C, so time above liquidus is measured from the 219 °C crossing. 2. Set soak target from the paste datasheet, e.g. 140-180 °C for 90-120 s; choose 165 °C for 90 s for this board. 3. Ramp to liquidus; measured ramp 1.5 °C/s is within typical practice, verify against the paste and oven datasheet. 4. Calculate time above liquidus from measured data: time when temperature exceeds 219 °C until it falls below 219 °C = 75 s. 5. Compare peak 242 °C with the J-STD-020 classification temperature for the package size: 242 °C passes for a small package (260 °C) or a large package (250 °C) and would fail for a very large package (245 °C), so confirm the package size before disposition. 6. Compare time above liquidus 75 s with the J-STD-020 classification window of 60-150 s: pass. IPC-7530 typical guidance is 30-90 s, so 75 s also sits inside the usual process window; the paste datasheet remains the authority for this board. 7. Check cooling 2.5 °C/s against the paste and equipment datasheet; if warpage or cracking is observed, slow cooling. The J-STD-020 classification ramp-down limit is 6 °C/s or less. 8. Conclusion: the measured profile passes the peak ceiling and the paste time-above-liquidus window for this board, but the package size must be confirmed for the peak ceiling, and production limits should be qualified and monitored."},"defect":{"symptom":"Solder balls on paste deposit edges after reflow","cause":"Soak too short or ramp too fast, causing flux and solvent boiling and paste spatter; thermocouple on board showed actual soak below paste target","action":"Measure with board thermocouples, increase soak time or lower ramp rate to reach paste soak range, verify paste datasheet, check stencil and paste viscosity"},"takeaways":["Measure on a populated board at the worst-case locations, because oven setpoints and bare-board runs both understate the real exposure.","Read the peak against the J-STD-020 classification for the package thickness and volume, not against a single remembered number.","Keep the paste time-above-liquidus window separate from the J-STD-020 tL classification window, since one runs the process and the other qualifies the package."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Quoting one peak temperature for every package on the board","instead":"Read the classification temperature by thickness and volume, and check the largest part against it."},{"mistake":"Judging time above liquidus from the oven display","instead":"Take it from the measured curve crossing the alloy liquidus, at the worst-case thermocouple."},{"mistake":"Slowing cooling without checking warpage and grain structure","instead":"Verify the cooling rate against the paste datasheet and inspect the joints afterwards."}],"terms":[{"term":"Time above liquidus","def":"The interval during which the solder alloy is fully molten."},{"term":"Classification temperature","def":"The peak temperature J-STD-020 allows for a package, set by thickness and volume."},{"term":"Wetting","def":"The spreading of molten solder over a metal surface to form a metallurgical bond."},{"term":"Thermocouple","def":"A temperature sensor attached to the board to record the actual thermal history."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m04l07"],"skills":["Lesson m04l07: Print troubleshooting logic and one-variable-at-a-time experiments"]}},{"lessonId":"m06l02","moduleId":"m06","stageId":"reflow","title":"Profile anatomy: ramp, soak, time above liquidus, peak, cooling — what each controls","goals":[{"text":"Build a profile for a given board thermal mass and alloy and check ramp, time above liquidus and peak against limits taken from the cited source.","competencies":["K5"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-005","src-008","src-017"],"quiz":["q-m06l02-01","q-m06l02-02","q-m06l02-03","q-m06l02-04","q-m06l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m06l02.webp","alt":"A thermal profile curve with the five zones labelled and a callout under each naming what it controls","caption":"Profile anatomy: what ramp, soak, time above liquidus, peak and cooling each control"}]},{"h":"The profile as a thermal budget","p":["A reflow profile records temperature against time at a defined measurement point, usually a thermocouple on the package body or near a joint. It is never one number.","The shape of the curve decides how flux activates, how solder melts, how intermetallics form, and how the board and its components experience thermal stress. For the working engineer the useful question is which segment of the curve controls which risk."]},{"h":"Preheat and ramp","p":["The ramp from room temperature to the liquidus region is where solvents leave the paste and the flux starts reducing oxides.","A ramp that is too steep boils trapped solvent violently, which shows up as solder balling or tombstoning, and large boards develop gradients that warp. A ramp that is too slow dries the flux out before reflow and leaves poor wetting. Typical practice runs 1 to 3 °C/s up to about 150 °C, with the exact limit set by paste chemistry and board mass and checked against the paste and oven datasheet.","IPC J-STD-020 gives moisture sensitivity level preheat guidance for components, not a universal ramp rate for the whole profile."]},{"h":"Soak","p":["The soak region, often roughly 150 to 190 °C, brings the whole assembly toward thermal equilibrium. It narrows the temperature difference between heavy ground planes and small pads, and gives the flux chemistry time to work before the solder melts.","Too short a soak leaves cold spots that refuse to wet; too long a soak over-cures the flux binder or exhausts the active agents. Many paste datasheets specify a soak window, for example 60 to 120 seconds in a defined band. Treat those numbers as starting points and confirm them with wetting tests on your own boards."]},{"h":"Reflow and time above liquidus","p":["Time above liquidus is the interval in which the solder is molten and able to flow and form intermetallic compounds. SAC305 has a solidus of 217 °C and a liquidus of 219 °C; SnPb eutectic has a liquidus of 183 °C.","Too short a time produces thin intermetallic and weak joints. Too long promotes excessive intermetallic growth and grain coarsening and can damage component finishes. The window is process guidance from IPC-7530 and the paste datasheet, typically 60-120 s or 60-150 s.","J-STD-020 sets no board-assembly time-above-liquidus limit: its own time parameter is tp, the time within 5 °C of the classification temperature, limited to 30 s, and its peak figures are classification temperatures by package thickness and volume rather than by size alone — 260 °C for a thin package at any volume, 260/250/245 °C across small, large and very large at 1.6-2.5 mm thickness, and 250/245/245 °C above 2.5 mm. Check the classification temperature for your component's body size and MSL before comparing a measured peak."]},{"h":"Peak temperature","p":["The peak is the highest temperature reached. It must be high enough for complete melting and wetting and low enough to protect board and components.","Peak is usually set 10 to 25 °C above liquidus for common alloys, but the governing limit is the component's rated peak from its datasheet or from IPC J-STD-020. Exceeding the rated peak delaminates packages, cracks ceramic capacitors and damages heat-sensitive parts; a peak below the wetting threshold leaves a dull, grainy joint that never fully formed."]},{"h":"Cooling","p":["After the peak, the cooling rate sets the solder microstructure and the residual stress in the joint. Slow cooling gives coarse grains and weak joints; very fast cooling induces thermal shock, especially in large ceramic components and thick boards.","Typical rates run 1 to 4 °C/s, with the safe limit set by component type and board construction and verified against the equipment's cooling capability and the component specifications. The cooling segment also decides how long the assembly sits in the range where flux residues char."]},{"h":"Reading the profile against your hardware","p":["A profile is valid only for the board, paste, oven and measurement point it was built for. A heavy board with large copper planes needs more energy and often a longer soak than a thin signal board, and the same recipe produces a different profile at another conveyor speed or loading density.","Always measure with thermocouples on the actual product rather than a coupon, then compare the measured ramp, soak, time above liquidus, peak and cooling against the paste datasheet window and the J-STD-020 classification temperature for the package size, which is the peak ceiling."]},{"h":"Failure modes by segment","p":["Ramp problems show up as solder balls, tombstoning or board warpage. Soak problems appear as non-wetting, dewetting or cold joints over heavy copper. Time-above-liquidus problems give insufficient intermetallic when short and brittle joints with damaged finishes when long.","Peak problems cause under-reflow or component damage, and cooling problems produce thermal cracking or excessive residual stress. When a defect appears, identify the segment most likely to be responsible, adjust that segment, and re-measure."]}],"calc":{"task":"Build and check a profile for a 1.6 mm FR-4 board with heavy ground planes using SAC305 paste. The measured profile gives: ramp from 25 to 150 °C in 60 s; soak 150 to 190 °C for 90 s; TAL above 219 °C = 45 s; peak = 248 °C; cooling from 248 to 100 °C in 40 s. Check against the J-STD-020 classification temperature for the package size and typical paste guidance.","solution":"1. Ramp rate: (150-25)/60 = 2.08 °C/s. Typical paste guidance is 1 to 3 °C/s; this is within range but verify against paste datasheet. 2. Soak: 90 s in 150 to 190 °C. Many paste datasheets specify 60 to 120 s; this is within range. 3. Peak temperature: measured 248 °C. J-STD-020 sets classification temperatures by package size — 260 °C small, 250 °C large, 245 °C very large (1.6-2.5 mm row; above 2.5 mm thickness the limits fall to 250 °C small and 245 °C large and very large). For a large package 248 °C is inside the limit; for a very large package it exceeds 245 °C by 3 °C, so the package size must be confirmed before disposition. 4. TAL above 219 °C: measured 45 s. J-STD-020 does not set a board-assembly time-above-liquidus limit; its 30 s figure is tp, the time within 5 °C of the classification temperature. The applicable limit is the paste window: IPC-7530B guidance gives 30-90 s, a paste datasheet may narrow it, and 45 s sits inside 30-90 s but below the lower bound of any datasheet that specifies 60 s or more, so it is too short for reliable wetting rather than too long. 5. Cooling rate: (248-100)/40 = 3.7 °C/s. Typical practice is 1 to 4 °C/s; this is within range, and the J-STD-020 classification ramp-down limit is 6 °C/s or less. 6. Conclusion: the binding failure is the too-short time above liquidus, not an excessive one. Lengthen time above liquidus toward the paste window (30-90 s per IPC-7530B, narrowed by the paste datasheet) by adding reflow dwell or slightly raising peak within the package classification temperature, confirm the package size for the peak ceiling, then re-measure."},"defect":{"symptom":"Solder joints on large ground-plane pads show dewetting and non-wetting, while joints on small signal pads look normal.","cause":"The soak segment is too short or the temperature gradient is too high, so the heavy copper pads never reach the flux activation temperature before the solder melts. The flux cannot reduce oxides on the large pads, and the solder does not wet.","action":"Increase soak time or raise the soak temperature band slightly to allow the heavy pads to reach thermal equilibrium. Verify with thermocouples on the actual board that the pad temperature enters the soak window before the liquidus is reached. Re-measure the profile and run wetting tests."},"takeaways":["Assign each defect to one segment of the curve before adjusting the oven, because the segment names the mechanism.","Keep paste time-above-liquidus guidance separate from the J-STD-020 classification windows, since they answer different questions.","Check the peak against the classification temperature for the package thickness and volume, not against a single remembered figure."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Quoting a 30 s time limit as the board's time above liquidus","instead":"That is J-STD-020 tp within 5 °C of classification, not the paste window."},{"mistake":"Raising the peak to fix weak wetting","instead":"Check the soak and the time above liquidus first, since wetting usually fails before the peak does."},{"mistake":"Using a test coupon as the profile record","instead":"Measure the actual product, because thermal mass is what the profile describes."}],"terms":[{"term":"Soak","def":"The near-isothermal segment where the assembly equalises and the flux works before melting."},{"term":"Liquidus","def":"The temperature above which an alloy is fully molten."},{"term":"Dewetting","def":"Solder that spreads and then retracts, leaving thin irregular areas on the pad."},{"term":"Intermetallic compound","def":"The brittle layer formed between solder and the pad metal during melting."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m06l01"],"skills":["Lesson m06l01: Heat transfer in the oven: convection, conduction, radiation, zone structure"]}},{"lessonId":"m06l03","moduleId":"m06","stageId":"reflow","title":"Running a thermal survey: thermocouple attachment, test coupon, worst-case site","goals":[{"text":"Run a thermal survey and read the profile at the coolest and hottest locations, not at the oven setpoints.","competencies":["K5"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-005","src-008","src-017"],"quiz":["q-m06l03-01","q-m06l03-02","q-m06l03-03","q-m06l03-04","q-m06l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m06l03.webp","alt":"A test coupon with several thermocouples attached by different methods, and a board map marking the worst-case thermal sites","caption":"Running a thermal survey: thermocouple attachment, test coupon and the worst-case site"}]},{"h":"What a thermal survey measures","p":["A thermal survey records the temperature the board, components and solder joints see as they pass through the oven. The zone setpoints are control targets, not proof that any joint reached temperature.","The measurable outcome is the temperature at the coolest and hottest locations on a representative coupon or production panel, compared with the paste liquidus and solidus, the time above liquidus and the component peak limit. Run the survey at the production panel orientation, conveyor speed and loading pattern; a bare coupon or a different layout measures a process you do not run.","The question the survey answers is whether the actual joint temperatures are inside the window, not whether the oven display matches the recipe."]},{"h":"Thermocouple selection and attachment","p":["Use a fine-gauge type K thermocouple with a calibrated logger. Attach the junction to the pad, trace or component body that represents the joint under evaluation.","High-temperature tape works if it is rated for the peak and does not lift in handling. A small solder blob improves thermal contact and changes the local thermal mass, so use one method consistently across surveys. Secure the wire so the junction cannot peel away — a lifted junction reads low because it sees air. A junction on a large copper area can read high or lag the joint.","Check tape, adhesive and logger ratings against your equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Test coupon design","p":["A coupon reproduces the materials and thermal mass of the production board: same laminate, copper distribution, component type, paste deposit and panel position.","Where the product carries a large ground plane, a dense BGA, a heavy connector or mixed SMT and THT, include those features, or run the survey on a sacrificial production panel. A simple strip with small pads gives a clean profile and misses the coolest joint under the ground plane.","Place the coupon where production boards sit, because airflow and radiant heating vary across oven width and length. A coupon that is too light heats faster than the real board and returns a false pass."]},{"h":"Finding the coolest and hottest sites","p":["The coolest site is usually under a large ground plane, near a cold edge, under a heavy component, or at a panel location with poor airflow. The hottest may be near a heater, on a small low-mass component, or at an edge exposed to radiation.","Run several thermocouples at once and compare the curves. A wide spread between sites means the setpoints no longer describe the process. The coolest site decides whether the paste reaches liquidus and forms a joint; the hottest decides whether anything nearby is overexposed. One thermocouple on a fast pad hides a cold joint elsewhere."]},{"h":"Reading the zones","p":["Read the board temperature in each zone. Preheat brings the board up without thermal shock, soak narrows the spread and activates flux, reflow holds the joint above liquidus long enough to wet, and cooling solidifies without excessive stress.","For lead-free paste the liquidus comes from the paste datasheet, and the time above liquidus is checked against the paste and component datasheets. Component peak limits are commonly referenced to IPC/JEDEC J-STD-020, while the component datasheet and the applicable moisture sensitivity level set the final limit. Check every practice-based value against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Measurement uncertainty and repeatability","p":["A measured temperature carries thermocouple error, logger error, attachment lag and placement error. Use a logger with a calibration certificate and record the sampling interval.","Keep the attachment method identical between surveys, so a change in the profile comes from the oven. A junction lifted by tape reads low, one soldered to a large plane reads high or late, a loose wire gives intermittent contact, and a logger resting on a hot conveyor rail shifts its reference junction.","Document thermocouple type, logger model, calibration status, attachment method and the exact location of every junction. Without that record, two surveys can differ while the oven has not changed."]},{"h":"Decision rule","p":["Accept the survey when the coolest joint reaches liquidus with enough time above it and the hottest joint stays below the component and board limits.","A marginal coolest site calls for a profile change or an investigation of heat transfer, panel loading and airflow. A hottest site close to a limit calls for a lower peak or better cooling. A spread too wide between sites needs better uniformity rather than a higher setpoint.","Document the coupon, thermocouple locations, oven settings, measured minimum and maximum temperatures and any deviation. The result should state whether the joints are acceptable, not whether the recipe was followed."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 6-thermocouple survey on a lead-free SAC305 test coupon records a hottest peak of 246 °C and a coolest peak of 238 °C. The paste datasheet gives solidus 217 °C and liquidus 219 °C. The component datasheet allows a peak of 260 °C. Calculate the margin above liquidus at the coolest site and the margin below the component limit at the hottest site.","solution":"First, identify the coolest peak: 238 °C. Next, identify the paste liquidus: 219 °C, because SAC305 has a solidus of 217 °C and a liquidus of 219 °C and time above liquidus is measured from the 219 °C crossing. Then subtract the liquidus from the coolest peak: 238 - 219 = 19 °C. This is the margin above liquidus at the coolest site. Next, identify the hottest peak: 246 °C. Then identify the component peak limit: 260 °C. Subtract the hottest peak from the component limit: 260 - 246 = 14 °C. This is the margin below the component limit at the hottest site. Interpretation: the coolest site is above liquidus by 19 °C, and the hottest site is 14 °C below the stated component limit. Verify time above liquidus and cooling rate against the paste and component datasheets."},"defect":{"symptom":"Cold or non-wetted solder joints appear on large ground-plane pads after the oven setpoints and a single thermocouple survey look acceptable.","cause":"The thermocouple was attached to a small pad or lifted by tape, so it measured a faster-heating location instead of the coolest joint under the ground plane. The test coupon did not reproduce the copper area and component thermal mass.","action":"Rerun the survey with a calibrated logger and a test coupon that includes the ground plane and component. Attach the thermocouple junction to the pad or component body at the worst-case site, secure the wire, and compare the coolest and hottest curves. Adjust the soak or peak only after confirming the measurement location."},"takeaways":["Attach thermocouples to the joint or body under test, because a junction on a convenient pad measures a board you do not build.","Find the coolest and hottest sites together, since one thermocouple can pass a survey that fails at the ground plane.","Record the attachment method and location with every survey, or the next run cannot be compared with this one."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Surveying a bare coupon or test strip","instead":"Use a coupon with the production copper and components, or a sacrificial production panel."},{"mistake":"Reading the oven display as joint temperature","instead":"Measure on the board, because setpoints say nothing about joint thermal lag."},{"mistake":"Changing the profile before checking the attachment","instead":"Confirm the junction is secure and correctly placed, then adjust the oven."}],"terms":[{"term":"Thermal survey","def":"A recorded temperature history from thermocouples on a representative board."},{"term":"Test coupon","def":"A sample board that reproduces the production laminate, copper and component mass."},{"term":"Thermal lag","def":"The delay between oven air temperature and the temperature reached at the joint."},{"term":"Worst-case site","def":"The location on the board that reaches the lowest or the highest temperature."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m06l02"],"skills":["Lesson m06l02: Profile anatomy: ramp, soak, time above liquidus, peak, cooling — what each controls"]}},{"lessonId":"m06l04","moduleId":"m06","stageId":"reflow","title":"Board thermal mass and layout: heavy planes, thermal relief, mixed population, dense vs sparse","goals":[{"text":"Build a profile for a given board thermal mass and alloy and check ramp, time above liquidus and peak against limits taken from the cited source.","competencies":["K5"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-005","src-008","src-017"],"quiz":["q-m06l04-01","q-m06l04-02","q-m06l04-03","q-m06l04-04","q-m06l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m06l04.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: dense versus sparse board with thermal-mass gauges, cross-section of a heavy copper plane sinking heat from a joint, thermal relief versus solid connection, heavy and light joint thermal response curves, a multilayer PCB cross-section photo and a thermocouple on the hardest joint","caption":"Board thermal mass and layout: heavy planes, thermal relief, mixed population, dense versus sparse"}]},{"h":"Thermal mass controls the board's response","p":["Board thermal mass is the combined heat capacity and heat-transfer resistance of the laminate, copper planes, plated holes, components and fixtures. A thick board with heavy copper reaches oven temperature more slowly than a thin board with sparse copper, so one recipe yields different joint temperatures.","Measure at the pad or component body rather than the oven air, and check the result against the paste and component datasheets."]},{"h":"Heavy planes act as heat sinks at the joint","p":["Large power and ground planes pull heat away from small pads and can hold a joint below liquidus while the joints around it look normal. The effect peaks when a pad ties directly to a plane with little thermal relief, when the plane sits on an inner layer with short vias, or when the component carries high thermal mass.","Balance the heat flow through pad geometry, paste volume, preheat and airflow instead of simply raising peak temperature."]},{"h":"Thermal relief is a heat-balance tool","p":["Thermal relief spokes cut the copper area feeding heat away from a pad, which makes the pad easier to lift above liquidus. The relief still has to deliver electrical and mechanical integrity, so spoke count, width and clearance follow the board design rules and the stencil design.","On heavy planes, confirm that the pad reaches liquidus with the chosen paste and component. If it does not, adjust the profile or the layout before raising peak temperature."]},{"h":"Mixed populations need a compromise profile","p":["A board with large connectors, QFNs, BGAs and small chips holds joints that want different heat input. Large components and heavy planes need enough soak and time above liquidus, while small parts suffer from excessive peak or a long soak.","Build the profile around the most thermally demanding joint and the lowest component temperature limit, then confirm that every part stays inside its own specified limits."]},{"h":"Dense and sparse layouts heat differently","p":["Dense boards with many components and copper areas heat more uniformly and often need more total energy to reach liquidus. Sparse boards heat quickly and can develop local hot spots near small components or exposed copper.","When one recipe covers different panel designs, compare pad-level profiles, because oven air temperature hides the differences between dense and sparse areas."]},{"h":"Alloy liquidus and standard limits","p":["For lead-free paste, take the liquidus from the paste datasheet, then set time above liquidus and peak against the applicable standard and the component limits.","The average ramp of 1-3 °C/s comes from IPC-7530 and the paste datasheet. The 60-150 s time-above-liquidus window is J-STD-020 tL, which IPC-7530 typically narrows to 30-90 s. J-STD-020 supplies the classification temperatures that bound the peak: 260 °C small, 250 °C large and 245 °C very large at 1.6-2.5 mm thickness, where the full table is thickness times volume, every volume is 260 °C below 1.6 mm, and above 2.5 mm the limits fall to 250 °C small and 245 °C large and very large. Each carries tp, the time within 5 °C of that temperature, limited to 30 s. Verify the package size, the component datasheet and the paste supplier's recommendation."]},{"h":"Move heat, not just temperature","p":["When heavy planes hold pad temperature down, lengthen the soak or slow the conveyor to raise the board temperature before liquidus, then confirm that peak and time above liquidus stay inside their limits. Where sparse areas overheat, lower peak or reflow-zone airflow and let preheat bring the whole board up together.","Document thermocouple location, board thickness, copper weight, alloy, and the measured ramp, time above liquidus and peak, so the result can be repeated."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 1.8 mm board with 2 oz copper power planes uses SAC305 paste with solidus 217 °C and liquidus 219 °C. The component limit is the J-STD-020 classification temperature for the package size. A pad-level profile shows ramp from 150 °C to 219 °C in 37 s, time above 219 °C of 85 s, and peak 238 °C. Build and check the profile.","solution":"1. Confirm alloy liquidus: SAC305 solidus 217 °C and liquidus 219 °C from the paste datasheet; time above liquidus is measured from the 219 °C crossing. 2. Apply limits: ramp 1-3 °C/s from IPC-7530 and the paste datasheet; time above liquidus 60-150 s as the J-STD-020 classification window, narrowed to IPC-7530 typical guidance of 30-90 s; the peak ceiling is the J-STD-020 classification temperature for the package size, 250 °C for a large package. 3. Calculate ramp: (219 - 150) / 37 = 1.86 °C/s. 4. Check ramp: 1.86 °C/s is within 1-3 °C/s. 5. Check time above liquidus: 85 s is within 60-150 s. 6. Check peak: 238 °C is below the 250 °C classification temperature and above liquidus by 19 °C. 7. Decision: the profile passes the cited limits, but heavy planes require pad-level confirmation; if pad temperature is low, increase soak or reduce conveyor speed and recheck peak and time above liquidus."},"defect":{"symptom":"After reflow, QFN pads on a 2 oz copper plane show incomplete wetting or lifted leads, while small chip components in sparse areas show discoloration or overheating.","cause":"The oven profile was tuned to air temperature, and the heavy plane heat sink kept the QFN pad below liquidus; sparse areas heated faster and exceeded the component limit.","action":"Profile at the pad, increase soak or reduce conveyor speed to raise pad temperature before liquidus, adjust airflow or zone setpoints, and verify against paste and component limits; if needed, add thermal relief or paste volume and requalify."},"takeaways":["Profile the hardest joint, because the coolest pad under a heavy plane decides whether the assembly is sound.","Balance heat flow with soak, conveyor speed and thermal relief before raising peak, since a higher peak damages the sparse areas first.","Record thickness and copper weight with the profile, because those numbers explain why the same recipe behaves differently on the next board."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Raising peak temperature to wet one plane-tied pad","instead":"Lengthen the soak or slow the conveyor, and check the sparse areas for overexposure."},{"mistake":"Adding thermal relief after the profile is fixed","instead":"Design the relief with the electrical and mechanical rules, then requalify the profile."},{"mistake":"Copying a recipe between boards of different thickness","instead":"Measure the new board, because thermal mass changes the whole curve."}],"terms":[{"term":"Thermal mass","def":"The heat capacity of the board and its parts, which sets how fast they heat and cool."},{"term":"Thermal relief","def":"Spokes in a plane connection that reduce heat flow into or out of a pad."},{"term":"Heat sink","def":"A large copper area that conducts heat away from a joint."},{"term":"Pad-level profile","def":"A temperature record taken at the joint rather than at the oven air."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m06l03"],"skills":["Lesson m06l03: Running a thermal survey: thermocouple attachment, test coupon, worst-case site"]}},{"lessonId":"m06l05","moduleId":"m06","stageId":"reflow","title":"Alloy systems and profile families: SnAgCu vs SnPb, liquidus basis, nitrogen vs air","goals":[{"text":"Build a profile for a given board thermal mass and alloy and check ramp, time above liquidus and peak against limits taken from the cited source.","competencies":["K5"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-005","src-008","src-017"],"quiz":["q-m06l05-01","q-m06l05-02","q-m06l05-03","q-m06l05-04","q-m06l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m06l05.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: SnAgCu and SnPb profiles overlaid with each liquidus and time above liquidus, alloy thermal budget below the package classification ceiling, ramp and soak, nitrogen versus air fillets, a QFP fillet photo and the data-source note that liquidus comes from the paste datasheet","caption":"Alloy systems and profile families: liquidus basis, thermal budget versus ceiling, nitrogen versus air"}]},{"h":"Liquidus is the profile reference","p":["SnPb eutectic paste is commonly associated with a liquidus of 183 C, while SAC305 is a near-eutectic alloy with a solidus of 217 °C and a liquidus of 219 C.","Those values come from the alloy and paste datasheet, not from J-STD-020, which supplies classification temperatures rather than melting data. The liquidus is where the alloy becomes fully molten, so time above liquidus is measured from the board thermocouple crossing that value, never from the oven setpoint."]},{"h":"Alloy families set different thermal budgets","p":["SnPb profiles usually run a lower peak, because the J-STD-020 classification temperature for SnPb eutectic is 235 °C for a package thinner than 2.5 mm with a volume below 350 mm³ and 220 °C in every other case.","SAC305 profiles run higher: 260 °C for small packages, 250 °C for large and 245 °C for very large at 1.6-2.5 mm thickness, where the full table is thickness times volume, every volume allows 260 °C below 1.6 mm, and above 2.5 mm the limits fall to 250 °C small and 245 °C large and very large. The practical target peak comes from paste wetting, component rating and board stress, then gets checked against the classification limit."]},{"h":"Ramp and soak control flux and gradients","p":["IPC-7530B gives typical guidance such as 1-3 °C/s ramp and a soak that reduces thermal gradients.","Heavy boards, large ground planes and dense components need a slower ramp or a longer soak, so the coldest joint reaches liquidus without overheating the hottest. Verify the chosen ramp and soak against the paste and oven datasheets."]},{"h":"Time above liquidus is a joint-quality window","p":["IPC-7530B gives typical guidance of 30-90 s above liquidus. The 60-120 s figure often quoted beside it is the soak, ts, the time from Tsmin to Tsmax in the J-STD-020 classification profile, not the time above liquidus.","That window allows wetting and intermetallic formation without exhausting the flux or growing intermetallic to excess. Heavy thermal mass may need longer above liquidus, but the limit gets checked against the paste datasheet and component moisture sensitivity."]},{"h":"Peak temperature is a ceiling, not a target","p":["A SnAgCu peak of 245 °C may be acceptable if the measured board temperature stays below the J-STD-020 classification temperature for the package size — 260 °C small, 250 °C large, 245 °C very large on the 1.6-2.5 mm row, with above 2.5 mm falling to 250 °C small and 245 °C large and very large — and the paste datasheet supports it.","Measure the peak at the joint rather than in the oven air, because board thermal mass and thermocouple placement both shift the apparent value."]},{"h":"Nitrogen changes oxidation, not the liquidus","p":["Nitrogen reflow cuts oxide formation on paste and pads, which can improve wetting and may allow a lower peak or a shorter time above liquidus. It does not move the SnAgCu liquidus of 219 °C (217 °C is the solidus) or the SnPb liquidus of 183 C.","Verify oxygen level, gas flow and chamber uniformity, because nitrogen also changes heat transfer and profile repeatability."]},{"h":"Build the profile from measured board data","p":["For a given board thermal mass, attach thermocouples to representative joints, ground planes and component leads. Record ramp rate, time above liquidus and peak.","Compare the measured values with IPC-7530B guidance and the IPC/JEDEC J-STD-020 classification limits, then adjust zones until the coldest joint meets wetting requirements without the hottest joint exceeding its limit."]}],"calc":{"task":"A board with measured thermal mass 90 J/K is reflowed with SnAgCu paste in air. The oven can deliver about 100 W to the board during ramp. The measured profile shows a ramp from 150 °C to 219 °C in 60 s, a time above 219 °C of 75 s, and a peak of 245 C. Build the expected ramp from thermal mass and check ramp, time above liquidus, and peak against IPC-7530B guidance and IPC/JEDEC J-STD-020 limits.","solution":"1. Use the SAC305 liquidus of 219 °C from the paste datasheet as the reference for time above liquidus; 217 °C is the solidus. 2. Estimate ramp rate from heat input and thermal mass: ramp rate = 100 W / 90 J/K = 1.1 °C/s. 3. Estimate time from 150 °C to 219 °C: (219 - 150) / 1.1 = 63 s. This is close to the measured 60 s. 4. Check ramp against IPC-7530B typical guidance of 1-3 °C/s: 1.1 °C/s is within the guidance. 5. Check time above liquidus against IPC-7530B typical guidance of 30-90 s: measured 75 s is within the guidance and clears the J-STD-020 tL minimum of 60 s. 6. Check peak against the J-STD-020 classification temperature for the package size (260 °C small, 250 °C large, 245 °C very large (1.6-2.5 mm row; above 2.5 mm thickness the limits fall to 250 °C small and 245 °C large and very large)): measured 245 °C is below the limit for small and large packages and exactly at the limit for very large packages, so confirm the package size. 7. Confirm the 245 °C peak is supported by the paste datasheet and component temperature ratings; if the board is run in nitrogen, re-run the profile because wetting and heat transfer may shift."},"defect":{"symptom":"Small solder balls appear around pads on a heavy ground-plane area after reflow, while the joints themselves look wetted.","cause":"The ramp was too fast or the soak was too short for the local thermal mass, causing flux and solvent to boil violently and eject paste material before the joint reached liquidus.","action":"Reduce the ramp rate toward IPC-7530B guidance, extend the soak, verify thermocouple placement on the heavy area, and re-run the profile while checking the paste datasheet for recommended ramp and soak limits."},"takeaways":["Read the liquidus from the paste datasheet for the alloy in use, because J-STD-020 classifies packages and does not give melting data.","Keep the alloy's thermal budget separate from its classification ceiling, since the second bounds the first rather than replacing it.","Re-profile after switching to nitrogen, because the gas changes heat transfer even when the liquidus stays the same."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Quoting a single peak for both SnPb and SAC305 assemblies","instead":"Check the J-STD-020 classification temperature for the alloy and package, which differ substantially."},{"mistake":"Measuring time above liquidus from setpoints","instead":"Take it from the board thermocouple crossing the alloy liquidus."},{"mistake":"Treating the 60-120 s figure as time above liquidus","instead":"That is the J-STD-020 soak window ts, not the molten time."}],"terms":[{"term":"Solidus","def":"The temperature below which an alloy is fully solid."},{"term":"Near-eutectic","def":"An alloy close to the composition with the lowest melting point in its system."},{"term":"Nitrogen reflow","def":"Reflow in a nitrogen atmosphere that limits oxide formation during melting."},{"term":"Classification temperature","def":"The peak temperature J-STD-020 permits for a package, by thickness and volume."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m06l04","m03l05"],"skills":["Lesson m06l04: Board thermal mass and layout: heavy planes, thermal relief, mixed population, dense vs sparse","Lesson m03l05: Aperture calculator and drawing review"]}},{"lessonId":"m06l06","moduleId":"m06","stageId":"reflow","title":"Reflow defect atlas: tombstone, balling, beading, dewetting, voids, distortion","goals":[{"text":"Link a defect signature to a profile zone and propose a bounded change with verification.","competencies":["K5","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-008"],"quiz":["q-m06l06-01","q-m06l06-02","q-m06l06-03","q-m06l06-04","q-m06l06-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m06l06.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: joint cross-section tiles for a sound joint, tombstone, solder balling, beading, dewetting, voids and distortion, a signature-to-zone table, a solder balling photo and the signature → zone → one variable → trial article → verify flow","caption":"Reflow defect atlas: signatures, the zone each points to, and the bounded-change sequence"}]},{"h":"Reflow zones and defect signatures","p":["A reflow profile reads as a sequence of zones: preheat, soak, reflow, cooling. Tombstone and beading usually point to preheat or soak imbalance, balling and dewetting to reflow wetting or surface condition, voids to trapped flux or paste rheology, and distortion to cooling restraint or uneven thermal mass.","Link the observed signature to the zone where the defect most likely forms before changing the profile."]},{"h":"Tombstone and beading","p":["Tombstone stands a small chip on end because one pad wets before the other. Beading leaves a rounded ball or bead at a pad edge when wetting is uneven or paste collapses.","Pad thermal imbalance, paste collapse and a ramp that brings the pads to liquidus at different times all drive them. Check pad copper balance, paste volume and preheat ramp against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Balling and dewetting","p":["Balling is a discrete ball separate from the joint, often from paste collapse, excessive preheat, or contamination that prevents coalescence. Dewetting wets the joint and then pulls back, leaving a thin film or an irregular edge.","Dewetting points to surface finish damage, oxide, or a soak too short for flux activity. Compare the defect location with pad finish, storage time and soak behaviour."]},{"h":"Voids and distortion","p":["Voids are trapped gas or flux residue inside the joint, usually visible by X-ray, and they grow when paste rheology, stencil aperture or ramp rate traps volatiles before the joint closes.","Distortion is board or component movement in reflow, from uneven heating, fixture restraint or cooling contraction. Voids need X-ray or cross-section to confirm, and distortion needs dimensional measurement and a fixture review."]},{"h":"From signature to a bounded change","p":["A bounded change modifies one profile or process variable and then verifies the result. For tombstone it might reduce ramp rate or extend the soak to balance pad temperature; for dewetting it might adjust soak time or surface cleanliness.","Avoid changing ramp, soak, peak and cooling together unless the failure mode is clearly coupled."]},{"h":"Verification before production","p":["Verification includes thermocouple traces on the suspect pad and a reference pad, visual inspection against the IPC visual acceptance standard, and X-ray or cross-section where voids are suspected.","Accept the change only when the signature is absent on a trial article and the profile stays inside the paste and equipment limits. Record the accepted change with the board, paste, stencil and oven settings."]},{"h":"Practice-based limits","p":["Values such as ramp rate, soak time and pad thermal relief size are starting points, verified against your paste and equipment datasheet.","The datasheet controls the allowable profile window, and the defect atlas says which zone to adjust. When a change pushes the profile outside the window, stop and re-evaluate paste, stencil, layout or fixture before adding more profile changes."]}],"calc":{"task":"A small chip resistor tombstones on a board where the left pad is isolated and the right pad connects to a copper plane through a thermal relief. Estimate whether pad thermal imbalance is plausible, then propose a bounded profile change and verification.","solution":"Start by estimating effective pad area. Assume the isolated pad effective area is 0.30 mm^2 and the plane pad effective area is 0.60 mm^2. The area ratio is 0.60 / 0.30 = 2.0. Copper thickness 35 µm and laminate thickness 1.6 mm are typical starting values; verify against your stack-up. If the plane pad uses a 0.3 mm thermal relief, the plane pad may still have greater thermal mass than the isolated pad, so it can reach liquidus later and increase tombstone risk. Propose a bounded change: reduce ramp rate from 2.0 °C/s to 1.5 °C/s and extend soak by 10 s, not more than 20 s, if paste and oven limits allow. Verify with thermocouples on the suspect pad and a reference pad; confirm pad-to-pad temperature difference before liquidus is reduced. Inspect a trial article against the IPC visual acceptance standard and X-ray if voids are suspected. If tombstone persists, review layout copper balance or paste volume rather than continuing to extend soak."},"defect":{"symptom":"Small chip resistor stands on end after reflow, with one terminal soldered and the other lifted.","cause":"Pad thermal imbalance and wetting imbalance during reflow; the pad with lower thermal mass reaches liquidus earlier, solder wets that side earlier, and surface tension pulls the component upright.","action":"Reduce ramp rate and extend soak within paste and oven limits; add thermal relief or copper balance if layout change is allowed; verify with thermocouple and trial article inspection against the IPC visual acceptance standard."},"takeaways":["Name the zone from the defect signature before touching the oven, because that choice determines which variable you are allowed to change.","Change one profile variable at a time and verify it on a trial article, unless the failure mode is genuinely coupled.","Treat ramp and soak values as starting points and confirm the limit against the paste and equipment datasheet."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Extending the soak for every tombstone","instead":"Check pad copper balance and paste volume first, since thermal imbalance is a layout problem."},{"mistake":"Chasing voids with a slower ramp alone","instead":"Review paste rheology, aperture and stencil design with X-ray evidence."},{"mistake":"Adjusting ramp, soak and peak in one trial","instead":"Change one variable, or the result cannot be attributed to any of them."}],"terms":[{"term":"Tombstone","def":"A chip component standing on one end after reflow because one joint wetted first."},{"term":"Dewetting","def":"Solder that wets and then retracts, leaving a thin irregular film on the pad."},{"term":"Void","def":"A cavity of trapped gas or flux residue inside a solder joint."},{"term":"Bounded change","def":"A single, limited change to the process that can be verified against a defined outcome."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m06l05"],"skills":["Lesson m06l05: Alloy systems and profile families: SnAgCu vs SnPb, liquidus basis, nitrogen vs air"]}},{"lessonId":"m06l07","moduleId":"m06","stageId":"reflow","title":"Profile validation, documentation and change control","goals":[{"text":"Evaluate a measured profile and name which zone to change, in which direction, with the expected joint effect.","competencies":["K5"]},{"text":"Link a defect signature to a profile zone and propose a bounded change with verification.","competencies":["K5","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-008"],"quiz":["q-m06l07-01","q-m06l07-02","q-m06l07-03","q-m06l07-04","q-m06l07-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m06l07.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: thermocouple map with five positions, measured profiles with validation envelope, validation record fields, a taped thermocouple photo, the change-control flow and adjustment versus qualification","caption":"Profile validation, documentation and change control"}]},{"h":"Purpose of profile validation","p":["Profile validation checks whether the measured thermal history at the joint matches the paste, component and board constraints. Put thermocouples on representative joints, not only in the oven air.","Compare the measured curve with the paste datasheet and the component moisture sensitivity limits from the applicable IPC moisture sensitivity guidance."]},{"h":"Review the profile by zones","p":["A reflow profile is reviewed zone by zone. Preheat controls solvent evaporation and prevents thermal shock. Soak activates flux and equalises board temperature. Reflow creates wetting and intermetallic formation. Cooling controls solidification and residual stress.","Each zone gives a different lever, so naming the zone first keeps the adjustment small."]},{"h":"Evidence needed for validation","p":["Validation records the location of every thermocouple, the board orientation, the carrier or pallet, the oven setpoints and the measured peak temperature.","A profile is invalid if the thermocouple is loose, the joint is unrepresentative, or the measurement comes from a heavily loaded board with no check against a lightly loaded one."]},{"h":"Measurement quality","p":["Attachment affects the result. Use a high-temperature adhesive or a solder point on the joint, and confirm the sensor is not lifted by conveyor vibration.","Measure several joints across the board, because thermal mass and airflow create gradients. On a multi-zone oven, record the measured curve rather than trusting the setpoints."]},{"h":"Linking defect signatures to zones","p":"When a signature points to a zone, choose the smallest change that tests the hypothesis. Poor wetting makes the reflow zone the first candidate. A joint that stays cold needs more energy in preheat or soak. Tombstoning points to preheat and soak being too aggressive, or to uneven cooling."},{"h":"Bounded change control","p":["Bounded change control means changing one profile variable, recording the old and new values, and verifying with measurement and inspection.","If time above liquidus is short, raise the peak slightly or slow the cooling, then confirm the joint still meets the acceptance criteria in the applicable IPC acceptance guidance."]},{"h":"Documentation for traceability","p":["Document the measured profile, the thermocouple map, the paste lot, the board stackup, the oven recipe, the change reason, the verification data and the disposition.","That record makes the change auditable and stops the next shift repeating the trial and error. Where the change touches moisture-sensitive components, check the applicable IPC moisture sensitivity guidance before releasing the recipe."]},{"h":"Recipe adjustment versus qualification","p":["Change control separates a recipe adjustment from a process qualification.","A small bounded adjustment is proven by profile measurement and joint inspection. A larger change — new paste, new board design, new oven — needs a formal qualification with statistical confidence and acceptance evidence."]}],"calc":{"task":"A measured lead-free paste profile shows ramp 1.6 °C/s, soak 150-190 °C for 90 s, time above liquidus 45 s, peak 242 °C, cooling 3.0 °C/s. The paste datasheet gives a solidus of 217 °C and a liquidus of 219 °C, recommended time above liquidus 60-90 s, and a recommended peak band of 235-245 °C that must stay below the component's J-STD-020 classification temperature. Evaluate the profile and propose a bounded change.","solution":"The measured time above liquidus is 45 s, which is below the datasheet range 60-90 s. The measured peak is 242 °C, which is inside the datasheet band 235-245 °C but only 3 °C below its top, and well below the 250 °C classification temperature of a large package. The soak range 150-190 °C is within the typical window, so the defect signature of dull, grainy, non-wet joints points to the reflow zone rather than the soak zone. Propose a bounded change: raise the peak by 2-3 °C to 244-245 °C, staying at the top of the band and clear of the ceiling, and reduce cooling from 3.0 °C/s to 2.5 °C/s to extend time above liquidus to 60-70 s. Verify against the paste and oven datasheet. Expected joint effect: improved wetting and more complete intermetallic formation, reducing cold solder joints. Risk: excessive peak can damage components or cause solder balling, and excessive slow cooling can enlarge grain size. Verification: remeasure the thermocouple profile, inspect joints against IPC acceptance guidance, check tombstoning and solder balling, and record the change in the recipe document."},"defect":{"symptom":"Dull, grainy joints with non-wet pads and insufficient fillet on a board that otherwise shows correct placement and paste volume.","cause":"Short time above liquidus and low peak temperature in the reflow zone, leaving incomplete wetting and weak intermetallic formation.","action":"Raise the peak temperature within the paste datasheet limit and extend time above liquidus by a bounded amount, then remeasure the profile and inspect joints against IPC acceptance guidance."},"takeaways":["Validate at the joint with a documented thermocouple map, because a loose or unrepresentative sensor invalidates the run.","Change one profile variable and verify it, so the result can be attributed and the recipe stays inside its limits.","Separate a bounded adjustment from a qualification, since a new paste or oven cannot be proven by a profile alone."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Accepting a profile measured with a detached thermocouple","instead":"Confirm sensor attachment and location before trusting any curve."},{"mistake":"Treating a new paste as a recipe adjustment","instead":"Run a formal qualification with statistical evidence for a material change."},{"mistake":"Changing the peak and the cooling rate together","instead":"Change one variable at a time, then re-measure both time above liquidus and peak."}],"terms":[{"term":"Thermocouple map","def":"The record of where each thermocouple was attached and what it measured."},{"term":"Recipe adjustment","def":"A small, bounded change to oven settings verified by profile and inspection."},{"term":"Process qualification","def":"Formal evidence that a process meets requirements under defined conditions."},{"term":"Bounded change control","def":"Changing one variable at a time and verifying the outcome before the next change."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m06l06"],"skills":["Lesson m06l06: Reflow defect atlas: tombstone, balling, beading, dewetting, voids, distortion"]}},{"lessonId":"m07l01","moduleId":"m07","stageId":"aoi","title":"What each method can see: visual, AOI, 2D X-ray, laminar/3D, boundary scan","goals":[{"text":"Choose the inspection method (visual, AOI, 2D X-ray, laminar/3D X-ray, boundary scan) for a stated defect and board feature.","competencies":["K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-004","src-007","src-011","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m07l01-01","q-m07l01-02","q-m07l01-03","q-m07l01-04","q-m07l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"Visual inspection","p":["Visual inspection, with or without magnification, catches defects that are exposed: missing or wrong components, polarity errors, tombstoned chips, visible bridges on exposed pads, insufficient or excess solder on through-hole leads, mechanical damage, contamination and flux residue.","It suits gross assembly errors and cosmetic acceptability under IPC-A-610, and it cannot reliably judge joints hidden under packages, bottom terminations, BGA balls, QFN wettable flanks, stacked layers, internal voids or barrel fill that no eye can reach."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 900 220' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Inspection coverage stack'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace}</style><rect x='120' y='40' width='660' height='40' rx='10' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.18)' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='180' y='90' width='540' height='40' rx='10' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.12)' stroke='rgba(83,209,204,.5)' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='240' y='140' width='420' height='40' rx='10' fill='rgba(255,152,0,.1)' stroke='#ff9800' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='450' y='65' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>SPI (POST-PRINT) — PASTE VOLUME, HEIGHT, AREA</text><text x='450' y='115' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>AOI POST-PLACEMENT — MISSING, TILT, POLARITY</text><text x='450' y='165' fill='#ff9800' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>AOI POST-REFLOW + X-RAY — JOINT QUALITY, HIDDEN</text><text x='450' y='205' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>EVERY LAYER YOU SKIP MOVES ESCAPES DOWNSTREAM</text></svg>","caption":"Inspection layers: each catches a different defect family"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m07l01.webp","alt":"Five stacked inspection bands showing visual, AOI, 2D X-ray of a BGA with voids, laminar 3D slice and a boundary scan chain","caption":"Inspection layers: what visual, AOI, 2D X-ray, laminar 3D and boundary scan can each see"}]},{"h":"AOI","p":["Automated optical inspection uses cameras, lighting and image analysis on exposed top and bottom features. It finds component presence, polarity, orientation, wrong and missing parts, tombstoning, visible bridges, coplanarity problems and some fillet or wettable flank features where lighting and 3D height mapping are good enough.","AOI is fast for inline screening, and it misses what sits under a package, behind a tall component, inside a BGA or QFN termination or down a barrel. Check lighting, angle and resolution against the equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"2D X-ray","p":"2D X-ray produces a transmission projection and is used when the defect is hidden from optical methods. It can reveal BGA, QFN, PLCC, and CSP termination geometry, missing or collapsed balls, opens, shorts, misalignment, voids, and through-hole barrel fill. Because the image superimposes layers, void size, depth, and bridging location can be ambiguous, and thin wettable flanks may be difficult to judge when overlapped by traces or adjacent balls. Use 2D X-ray for screening hidden joints, and verify magnification and detector resolution against your equipment datasheet."},{"h":"Laminar and 3D X-ray","p":["Laminar or 3D X-ray reconstructs slices or volumes, which gives depth discrimination no projection can. Use it to locate a void, estimate its size, inspect stacked packages, separate overlapping BGA rows, judge QFN wettable flanks or evaluate barrel fill in dense boards, and it supports the IPC-7095 guidance on hidden BGA features.","The costs are cycle time, capital, reconstruction artefacts and sensitivity to calibration, so reserve it for cases where 2D X-ray or AOI cannot settle the question, and do not run it as a routine replacement for 2D screening."]},{"h":"Boundary scan","p":["Boundary scan, usually JTAG, uses scan cells inside devices to observe and drive package pins. It detects opens and shorts on nets connected to scan-capable pins, which makes it valuable on dense BGA and QFN assemblies where optics are limited.","It does not inspect wetting, component value, non-scan nets, power and ground nets, analog nodes, memory arrays or joints under non-scan devices. It needs design-for-test access, a working scan chain and test vectors that exercise the nets you care about."]},{"h":"Choosing from the defect and the feature","p":["Match the method to what must be seen. Visual or AOI handles exposed top-side defects, visible through-hole leads, polarity, missing parts and visible bridges, and bottom-side AOI takes the underside.","2D X-ray covers hidden BGA, QFN, PLCC, CSP and barrel features. Laminar or 3D X-ray resolves void size, depth, stacking and overlapping geometry. Boundary scan covers opens and shorts on scan-capable nets in dense packages, combined with physical inspection for non-scan nets and solder quality."]},{"h":"Practical limits and acceptance","p":["No single method covers every defect. Optical methods depend on lighting, angle, component height and reflectivity; X-ray on magnification, resolution, focus, orientation and reconstruction; boundary scan on chain integrity and coverage.","Acceptance follows the customer-specified IPC-A-610 class and the relevant design guidance such as IPC-7351 and IPC-7525. Take practice-based limits from your equipment and paste datasheets rather than assuming one value."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 0.4 mm pitch BGA has 100 balls. 60 balls are connected to scan-capable signal nets, 20 are power or ground, and 20 are analog nets. The line suspects opens and shorts on signal nets and voiding under all balls. Choose the inspection methods and estimate boundary scan coverage.","solution":"First, classify the defects. Opens and shorts on scan-capable signal nets can be tested electrically by boundary scan. Voiding under all balls is a hidden physical defect and requires X-ray. Second, estimate boundary scan coverage. Scan-capable signal balls = 60. Total balls = 100. Coverage = 60/100 = 0.60. Third, interpret coverage. Boundary scan can test 0.60 of the balls for opens and shorts on scan nets, but it does not test the 20 power or ground balls, the 20 analog balls, or solder voiding under any ball. Fourth, choose physical inspection. Use 2D X-ray to screen hidden BGA balls and voids. Use laminar/3D X-ray if void size, depth, or overlapping rows must be measured. Fifth, combine methods. Use boundary scan for scan-net opens and shorts, 2D X-ray for hidden ball screening, and laminar/3D X-ray for ambiguous or acceptance-critical voids. Verify resolution and test coverage against your equipment datasheet and the customer-specified IPC-A-610 Acceptability Requirements for Electronic Assemblies class."},"defect":{"symptom":"Boundary scan fails on some scan-capable nets after reflow. AOI shows no visible BGA defect, and 2D X-ray shows collapsed or missing balls under the package.","cause":"Insufficient paste release or paste volume, stencil aperture area ratio outside the process window, paste slump, component coplanarity, or reflow profile that did not collapse the balls. Land pattern and stencil design may not match IPC-7351 Land Pattern Design Data Standard for Surface Mount and IPC-7525 Stencil Design Guidelines.","action":"Stop the line and quarantine the lot. Confirm the failure with boundary scan and 2D X-ray, then use laminar/3D X-ray to measure void size and ball collapse. Review stencil aperture area ratio, paste type, print pressure, squeegee speed, and reflow profile against IPC-7525 Stencil Design Guidelines and IPC-7095 Design and Assembly Process Implementation for BGAs. Verify ball coplanarity and land pattern, then re-run a qualification lot and accept results against the customer-specified IPC-A-610 Acceptability Requirements for Electronic Assemblies class."},"takeaways":["Choose the method from what must be seen, because an optical screen cannot answer a question about a joint under a package.","Combine electrical and physical inspection on dense packages, since boundary scan proves connectivity and not wetting.","Check the resolution and coverage limits of the chosen method before accepting its verdict."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Relying on AOI to clear a BGA","instead":"Use X-ray for hidden terminations, because AOI cannot see under the package."},{"mistake":"Treating boundary scan as full joint inspection","instead":"It proves opens and shorts on scan nets and says nothing about wetting or voids."},{"mistake":"Using 2D X-ray to size a void","instead":"Use laminar or 3D X-ray, because projection overlays layers and distorts depth."}],"terms":[{"term":"Boundary scan","def":"An electrical test method that uses scan cells inside devices to drive and observe package pins."},{"term":"Wettable flank","def":"A plated side feature on a QFN that lets optical inspection judge the fillet."},{"term":"Laminar X-ray","def":"Tomographic X-ray that reconstructs a slice through the assembly."},{"term":"Projection image","def":"A 2D X-ray image in which every layer contributes to the same picture."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m04l07","m06l07"],"skills":["Lesson m04l07: Print troubleshooting logic and one-variable-at-a-time experiments","Lesson m06l07: Profile validation, documentation and change control"]}},{"lessonId":"m07l02","moduleId":"m07","stageId":"aoi","title":"AOI program setup: windows, lighting, thresholds, the false-call/escape trade-off","goals":[{"text":"Choose the inspection method (visual, AOI, 2D X-ray, laminar/3D X-ray, boundary scan) for a stated defect and board feature.","competencies":["K6"]},{"text":"Compute escape rate, false-call rate and verification coverage from audit data and state their limits.","competencies":["K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001"],"quiz":["q-m07l02-01","q-m07l02-02","q-m07l02-03","q-m07l02-04","q-m07l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m07l02.webp","alt":"An AOI camera view with component windows, lighting angles and a threshold slider, and a trade-off scale between false calls and escapes","caption":"AOI program setup: windows, lighting, thresholds and the false-call / escape trade-off"}]},{"h":"Match the method to the defect","p":["AOI screens optically accessible features: component presence, polarity, tombstoning, skew, rotation, missing solder, visible fillet shape and some coplanarity issues. It is weak on hidden joints, voids, cracks under packages and internal package defects.","Take hidden defects to 2D X-ray or laminar and 3D X-ray: BGA voiding, QFN wetting, bridges under a package, internal lead separation. Take electrical opens and shorts to boundary scan where the nets can be exercised through scan logic, especially on dense BGA and fine-pitch devices, remembering that it does not replace physical inspection for mechanical and solder defects. Use visual inspection for unusual defects, program validation and verifying the AOI calls themselves."]},{"h":"Windows define what AOI may see","p":["An inspection window is the region assigned to a component or feature plus the expected model, fiducial alignment and tolerance. Windows should allow for placement tolerance, reflow shift, panel handling, warpage and component substitution.","Too tight a window calls a good board because the part shifted slightly. Too loose a window inspects the wrong area or misses a defect near the edge. Build windows from the land pattern and component outline, then validate them with golden boards and known defect samples. On panels, confirm that fiducials, rails and local alignment points stay stable across the panel and through reflow."]},{"h":"Lighting and optics set the signal","p":["Lighting angle, intensity, colour, focus and camera resolution decide whether a defect produces measurable contrast. Shiny solder, matte components, gold finishes, black packages and reflective pads each confuse a single-light program.","Multi-angle lighting, high dynamic range and separate solder and component channels improve discrimination at the cost of program complexity. Resolution in micrometres has to resolve the smallest feature that matters to the acceptability decision, verified against the equipment datasheet and a test coupon rather than a camera specification."]},{"h":"Thresholds turn images into decisions","p":["Thresholds are the numeric limits that convert measurements into pass or fail: solder area, solder height, contrast, coplanarity, pin count, polarity, tombstone angle, skew, rotation.","Derive them from the applicable acceptability standard for soldered assemblies, the land pattern data, the component datasheets and measured process variation. Default thresholds are a starting point, never a release condition. For each threshold record the golden-board value, the process variation, the defect sample value and the chosen limit. Where AOI cannot measure the physical criterion directly, use a surrogate and document its correlation."]},{"h":"False calls and escapes are linked","p":["Tightening thresholds cuts escapes and usually raises false calls; loosening them does the reverse.","The best setting is not the lowest false-call rate but the point where verification capacity, defect severity, escape cost and throughput balance. A high false-call rate hides real defects, because operators grow impatient and clear calls without verifying. A low one hides escapes behind a permissive program. Decide per defect type, from severity and downstream impact, and expect practice-based false-call rates anywhere from low single digits to higher percentages per board depending on mix and program maturity."]},{"h":"Audit data needs definitions first","p":["Escape rate, false-call rate and verification coverage only mean something with clear definitions. A call is any AOI output requiring review; a true call is a verified defect; a false call is a verified non-defect.","An escape is a defect AOI did not call but a later audit, test or field return found. Verification coverage is the fraction of calls actually reviewed, and incomplete coverage leaves every estimate uncertain. An audit that samples certain boards, defect types or shifts speaks only for that population."]},{"h":"Limits of the metrics","p":["Sample size, timing and detection capability all bound the metrics. Escapes found after the audit window are missing unless the data are updated, and downstream inspection and test can miss defects, so a measured escape rate is a lower bound.","False calls go underreported when operators clear them without logging. Different board mixes, component substitutions and program revisions change what the same percentage means. Report the denominator, the time window, the board mix and the verification coverage with every rate."]}],"calc":{"task":"A line audits 10000 boards after AOI. AOI made 500 calls. Operators verified 450 calls and found 150 true defects and 300 false calls. The remaining 50 calls were not verified. A downstream audit found 20 escapes that AOI did not call. Compute verification coverage, false-call rate, and escape rate, and state the limits.","solution":"First, verification coverage = verified calls / total calls = 450 / 500 = 90 percent. Second, false-call rate among verified calls = false calls / verified calls = 300 / 450 = 66.7 percent. False calls per inspected board = 300 / 10000 = 3.0 percent. If the unverified calls are assumed to have the same false-call rate as verified calls, estimated false calls in the unverified calls = 50 * 300 / 450 = 33.3, total estimated false calls = 333.3, false-call rate among all calls = 333.3 / 500 = 66.7 percent, and estimated false calls per inspected board = 333.3 / 10000 = 3.3 percent. Third, escape rate using verified true calls only = escapes / (verified true calls + escapes) = 20 / (150 + 20) = 11.8 percent. If all 50 unverified calls were false, the escape rate remains 11.8 percent. If all 50 unverified calls were true, true calls = 200 and escape rate = 20 / (200 + 20) = 9.1 percent. If unverified calls are assumed to have the same true-call rate as verified calls, estimated true calls = 150 + 50 * 150 / 450 = 166.7, and estimated escape rate = 20 / (166.7 + 20) = 10.7 percent. Escapes per inspected board = 20 / 10000 = 0.2 percent. Limits: the 90 percent coverage means 50 calls are unverified, so the true false-call and escape rates are uncertain. The downstream audit may miss escapes, so the escape rate is a lower bound. The audit covers only 10000 boards and the sampled board mix, so the rates may not apply to other mixes or program revisions."},"defect":{"symptom":"AOI repeatedly calls missing solder fillet on small chip capacitors after reflow, but manual verification finds acceptable solder.","cause":"The inspection window was too tight for normal reflow shift, and the solder channel exposure was too high, so the fillet contrast fell below the threshold.","action":"Measure the actual placement and reflow shift in millimeters, widen the window to cover the verified variation, reduce or rebalance the solder lighting exposure, retrain the fillet threshold from golden and defect samples, then run a verification audit before releasing the program."},"takeaways":["Define call, true call, false call and escape before quoting a rate, because each number depends on those definitions.","Balance false calls against escapes at the point verification capacity allows, not at the lowest false-call figure.","Report coverage and the board mix beside every rate, since an incomplete audit understates what it cannot see."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Tightening thresholds until the false-call report looks clean","instead":"Check the escape data as well, because the two move in opposite directions."},{"mistake":"Quoting an escape rate from incomplete verification coverage","instead":"State the coverage, or the rate describes only the calls someone happened to review."},{"mistake":"Comparing defect rates across program revisions","instead":"Rebaseline after a program or board-mix change, since the denominator moved."}],"terms":[{"term":"Escape","def":"A defect the inspection did not call and a later stage or the field found."},{"term":"False call","def":"An inspection call that verification shows was not a defect."},{"term":"Verification coverage","def":"The share of inspection calls that were actually reviewed."},{"term":"Golden board","def":"A known-good reference board used to build and check the inspection program."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m07l01"],"skills":["Lesson m07l01: What each method can see: visual, AOI, 2D X-ray, laminar/3D, boundary scan"]}},{"lessonId":"m07l03","moduleId":"m07","stageId":"aoi","title":"AOI metrics: escape rate, false calls, first-pass yield, verification workflow","goals":[{"text":"Compute escape rate, false-call rate and verification coverage from audit data and state their limits.","competencies":["K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001"],"quiz":["q-m07l03-01","q-m07l03-02","q-m07l03-03","q-m07l03-04","q-m07l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m07l03.webp","alt":"An audit workflow diagram linking inspection result, verification call and disposition, with escape rate and false-call rate readouts","caption":"AOI metrics: escape rate, false calls, first-pass yield and the verification workflow"}]},{"h":"What AOI metrics measure","p":["AOI metrics are process indicators built from audit data, not direct measurements of the true defect population. AOI screens, so its reported rejects, passes and escapes depend on program setup, optics, lighting, the component library, panel design, paste, reflow and operator verification.","Stratify the metrics by product, line, shift, program version and defect type before using them for a decision. They are most useful compared over time within one product family, and paired with downstream test, rework and field data. A metric that looks good at the AOI station is incomplete when downstream steps mask defects or nobody audits verification.","Review escape rate, false-call rate, verification coverage and first-pass yield as a set rather than as isolated station scores."]},{"h":"Escape rate","p":["Escape rate is usually defects found after AOI divided by the units, panels or component opportunities inspected. Match the denominator to the question: board-level escapes use boards, joint-level use joints, component-level use components.","Downstream sources include in-circuit test, functional test, manual visual audit, X-ray, rework and field returns. Its limits are the detection capability of those steps, escapes below AOI resolution, intermittent defects, and the time lag before field returns appear.","A low escape rate can mean good detection, downstream masking, or weak downstream detection. Where the downstream audit is weak the rate understates the problem, and where the denominator is too broad a few escapes look insignificant while one component or panel location is failing."]},{"h":"False-call rate","p":["False-call rate is verified good rejects divided by verified AOI rejects. It needs a verification workflow that dispositions every reject as true defect, false call, marginal or other.","Incomplete coverage turns the rate into an estimate from the verified subset, reliable only when that subset represents all rejects. Operator bias, marginal acceptance, duplicate rejects, program changes and differences between the AOI image and the physical board all bend it.","A false call is not automatically waste: some flag marginal conditions that later escape. It can also show that the program is too sensitive for the current paste, stencil or placement process. Keep the disposition categories stable, because redefining them moves the rate without any process change."]},{"h":"First-pass yield","p":["First-pass yield is units completed without rework or repair divided by units started.","In the AOI context, separate AOI pass yield, which penalises every AOI reject including false calls, from line first-pass yield, which depends on the final disposition after verification and downstream test. FPY misleads when rework loops, duplicate boards, batch holds or late dispositions are counted inconsistently.","A high FPY with poor verification coverage may hide escapes, and a lower FPY with strong verification may simply reflect better detection. Pair FPY with escape and false-call rates to see whether yield improved because defects were prevented or because detection was relaxed, and keep definitions identical across shifts and products."]},{"h":"Verification workflow and coverage","p":["A practical workflow runs from the AOI reject to a review of the image and the physical board to a disposition against documented visual acceptance criteria.","Verification coverage is verified rejects divided by total AOI rejects, and it should include random samples of passes, because pass audits estimate the escapes downstream may not catch. Coverage weakens when only high-risk rejects are verified, when operators verify only obvious defects, or when the AOI image is accepted without physical confirmation.","Report coverage beside the false-call rate so the estimate can be read, and add disagreement audits where a second inspector reviews a sample of dispositions, plus clear rules for marginal cases. Otherwise coverage can be high in count and poor in quality."]},{"h":"Statistical limits and stratification","p":["All three metrics are proportions, so small denominators give unstable results: a few defects in a small audit move the rate substantially.","Control charts, confidence intervals and Pareto analysis by defect type, component, package, panel location, shift and program version say more than a single weekly number. Compare similar products run on similar programs.","Set practice-based thresholds from historical capability, customer requirements and equipment datasheets, and check them against your paste and equipment datasheet. Review them whenever product mix, AOI program, stencil, paste or reflow profile changes, because a target that holds for one family may be wrong for another whose density, packages and panel design differ."]},{"h":"Using the metrics for improvement","p":["False-call reduction usually starts with program tuning, lighting, fiducial quality, panel layout, component library accuracy and thresholds.","Escape reduction may need better AOI coverage, added X-ray or functional test, and correction of paste, stencil, placement or reflow variation. Coverage improves with training, clear acceptance criteria, disagreement audits and sampled passes.","Let the metrics drive containment and prevention rather than reporting. On THT, AOI sees pin joints poorly, so read escape metrics with X-ray, functional test or manual audit data. Track improvement with before-and-after data on the same denominators and verification rules, so the team can tell whether detection improved, false calls fell, or the problem simply moved to another station."]}],"calc":{"task":"A line inspected 12000 boards through AOI during a week. AOI reported 360 rejects. Quality verified 300 rejects and found 90 false calls. Downstream test and visual audit found 18 defects after AOI. Compute escape rate, false-call rate, and verification coverage, and state the limits of each result.","solution":"Escape rate calculation: downstream defects divided by inspected boards = 18 / 12000 = 0.15 percent. This uses a board-level denominator; it does not represent joint-level escape rate and depends on downstream detection. False-call rate calculation: false calls divided by verified rejects = 90 / 300 = 30 percent. This is based on verified rejects only; if unverified rejects differ from verified rejects, the estimate is biased. Verification coverage calculation: verified rejects divided by AOI rejects = 300 / 360 = 83.3 percent. Coverage below full verification means false-call rate is an estimate, not a census. Interpretation: report the three metrics together. A false-call rate of 30 percent with 83.3 percent coverage should be stratified by defect type, component, package, panel location, shift, and program version before setting targets."},"defect":{"symptom":"AOI false-call rate rises after a new panel design, while escape rate stays low.","cause":"Panel deformation or fiducial variation causes image misalignment, and program thresholds are too tight for the new placement variation.","action":"Verify panel and fiducial quality, retune the AOI program with golden boards, audit verified rejects, and monitor false-call rate and verification coverage."},"takeaways":["Report escape rate, false-call rate and coverage together, because any one of them alone can be improved by worsening another.","Match the denominator to the level you are judging, since board, joint and component escapes answer different questions.","Keep definitions and stratification fixed across comparisons, or a program revision looks like a process improvement."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Reading a falling escape rate as better AOI","instead":"Check downstream detection and audit coverage, because masking produces the same curve."},{"mistake":"Quoting a false-call rate from partial verification","instead":"State the coverage, or the number describes only the rejects someone verified."},{"mistake":"Comparing rates across products with different densities","instead":"Stratify by product family, since component density changes the detection challenge."}],"terms":[{"term":"First-pass yield","def":"Units completed without rework divided by units started."},{"term":"Verification coverage","def":"Verified rejects divided by total AOI rejects, showing how much of the data was actually reviewed."},{"term":"Disagreement audit","def":"A second review of a sample of dispositions to test consistency."},{"term":"Stratification","def":"Splitting a metric by product, line, shift or defect type so the population is comparable."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m07l02"],"skills":["Lesson m07l02: AOI program setup: windows, lighting, thresholds, the false-call/escape trade-off"]}},{"lessonId":"m07l04","moduleId":"m07","stageId":"aoi","title":"X-ray interpretation: BGA ball size and voiding, bridging, through-hole fill","goals":[{"text":"Classify a defect image into a defect type and an accept / rework / reject disposition with the criterion named.","competencies":["K6"]},{"text":"Choose the inspection method (visual, AOI, 2D X-ray, laminar/3D X-ray, boundary scan) for a stated defect and board feature.","competencies":["K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-007","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m07l04-01","q-m07l04-02","q-m07l04-03","q-m07l04-04","q-m07l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m07l04.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: X-ray image of a BGA array with void, small ball and bridge callouts, ball collapse side views, centre versus edge void, through-hole fill with a void, a disposition card and the 2D, oblique and CT method choice","caption":"X-ray interpretation: BGA ball size and voids, bridging, through-hole fill, disposition by criterion and class"}]},{"h":"Reading an X-ray image","p":["A 2D X-ray image maps density: solder absorbs more than board laminate, and gas absorbs almost nothing. That is why a void appears dark inside a bright ball, and why a bridge appears as a bright path between two features that should be separate.","Read every image against a named criterion and a known feature size before calling anything. Magnification, detector resolution, focus and sample angle all change what the light and dark shapes appear to be.","Use 2D X-ray as the first method for hidden joints, and turn to laminar or 3D X-ray when overlapping features, multiple layers or true volumetric measurement are required. Boundary scan is not an imaging method: it tests selected digital interconnects electrically and finds opens or shorts only where the design supports it."]},{"h":"BGA ball size and collapse","p":["In a BGA image each ball should appear as a discrete, roughly circular feature of consistent diameter and spacing across the array. Ball size is read against the expected land pattern, paste volume, component specification and reflow profile.","A ball that is too small points to insufficient paste, paste bleed, poor stencil release or excessive reflow loss. One that is too large points to excessive paste, bleed between adjacent lands, or incomplete paste separation. IPC-7095 gives guidance on land pattern and paste printing design; verify the specific land size, aperture and volume against the component datasheet and your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Voiding in BGA balls","p":["A void is a dark, low-density region inside a ball, because X-rays pass through gas more easily than through solder. Small voids are common and may be acceptable depending on size, location and product class.","IPC-A-610 carries the acceptability criteria for voids in solder balls, and for many Class 3 applications voids are commonly evaluated against a limit such as 25 % of the ball area, with the exact class, revision and customer requirement checked against IPC-A-610 and the contract. Voids at the outer edge of a ball, or voids coalescing across several balls, matter more than an isolated central void, because they cut the effective wetted area and raise thermal and mechanical risk."]},{"h":"Bridging under BGA and fine-pitch parts","p":["A bridge is a continuous high-density solder path joining two or more balls that should be isolated. On a BGA it often shows as a diagonal or horizontal line between adjacent balls near the array edge or under the die shadow.","Excessive paste volume, paste bleed, misalignment, collapsed balls and a ramp that lets solder flow laterally before wetting all cause it. IPC-7095 gives the land pattern and aperture design guidance; verify aperture size, pitch and stencil thickness against the paste and stencil datasheet. AOI may catch exposed bridges on fine-pitch QFN or QFP, while BGA bridges usually need X-ray."]},{"h":"Through-hole fill and voids","p":["Through-hole X-ray evaluates solder fill inside a plated hole, particularly when a component body, shielding or a second-side part hides the joint.","A sound through-hole joint shows solder wetting the pad, the hole wall and the lead, with a fill height suited to hole diameter and component class. IPC-A-610 carries the criteria for fill and voids, and for many Class 3 applications voids are commonly evaluated against a limit such as 25 % of the hole circumference, with class, revision and customer requirement confirmed against the standard and the contract. Verify hole diameter, lead diameter and required fill height against the board and component datasheets."]},{"h":"Choosing the inspection method","p":["Visual inspection covers exposed joints, polarity, missing components, tombstoning and obvious solder defects. AOI covers exposed SMT placement, fillet shape, tombstoning and many exposed bridges.","A 2D X-ray covers BGA voiding, ball size, hidden bridging and simple through-hole fill where overlapping layers do not obscure the feature. Laminar or 3D X-ray takes over with stacked BGAs, dense routing, many layers, or when void volume and depth must be separated from overlapping features. Boundary scan covers selected digital interconnects where the design supports it, detecting some opens and shorts without replacing X-ray for solder geometry."]},{"h":"Disposition with a named criterion","p":["Disposition rests on a named criterion, never on appearance. Accept when the measured defect sits inside the applicable IPC-A-610 class and the customer requirement.","Rework when the defect is repairable and the design, package and thermal budget allow repair without damaging nearby features; a localised BGA bridge may be reworkable if the land pattern and component survive the process. Reject when the defect breaks the named criterion and cannot be reworked reliably: a void over the allowed area, a bridge that cannot come out without lifting pads, or a through-hole joint that cannot reach the required fill.","Record the standard, the class, the measured value and the disposition every time."]}],"calc":{"task":"A BGA solder ball has a measured diameter of 0.45 mm. The X-ray image shows a void with an estimated diameter of 0.22 mm. Using a circular approximation, calculate the void area as a percentage of the ball area and classify the defect against a named criterion. Assume the applicable requirement is IPC-A-610 Class 3, voids in solder balls shall not exceed 25% of the ball area, and verify the exact class and revision against the contract.","solution":"1. Ball diameter D = 0.45 mm. Ball radius r_ball = 0.45 / 2 = 0.225 mm. 2. Ball area A_ball = pi * r_ball^2 = pi * (0.225)^2 = pi * 0.050625 = 0.1590 mm^2. 3. Void diameter d = 0.22 mm. Void radius r_void = 0.22 / 2 = 0.11 mm. 4. Void area A_void = pi * r_void^2 = pi * (0.11)^2 = pi * 0.0121 = 0.0380 mm^2. 5. Void percentage = (A_void / A_ball) * 100 = (0.0380 / 0.1590) * 100 = 23.9%. 6. Compare with IPC-A-610 Class 3 criterion: 23.9% is less than 25%. 7. Defect type: void in BGA solder ball. Disposition: accept, provided the void location and any additional voids are also within the applicable IPC-A-610 class and customer requirement."},"defect":{"symptom":"2D X-ray shows a continuous high-density solder path connecting two adjacent BGA balls near the array edge. The balls are otherwise similar in size, but the bridge is located where paste apertures are close together.","cause":"Excessive paste volume or paste bleed between adjacent apertures, possibly caused by stencil thickness, aperture design, paste rheology, or reflow profile. IPC-7095 provides BGA land and paste design guidance; verify paste aperture size, stencil thickness, and release behavior against the paste and stencil datasheet.","action":"Confirm the bridge with 2D X-ray and, if overlapping features make the extent unclear, use laminar or 3D X-ray. If the bridge is repairable without lifting pads or damaging adjacent balls, rework and re-inspect by X-ray. If the bridge cannot be removed reliably, reject the board. Correct the process by reducing paste volume, adjusting stencil design, improving paste alignment, or modifying the reflow profile, then verify with X-ray and electrical test."},"takeaways":["Name the criterion and the class before judging an image, because the same void is acceptable in one class and a reject in another.","Compare measured feature sizes on the image itself, since magnification and angle change what the shapes appear to be.","Note the void location as well as its size, because an edge void threatens the joint more than a central one of equal area."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Calling a void from a single 2D projection","instead":"Confirm size and depth with laminar or 3D X-ray when the joint is stacked or overlapped."},{"mistake":"Applying a 25 % void limit without checking the class","instead":"Read the applicability from IPC-A-610 and the contract before acceptance."},{"mistake":"Deciding disposition from appearance rather than a measurement","instead":"Record the measured value against the criterion so the call can be reproduced."}],"terms":[{"term":"Void","def":"A gas-filled cavity inside a solder joint, visible on X-ray as a dark region."},{"term":"Collapse","def":"The reduction in BGA ball height during reflow as the ball settles onto the land."},{"term":"Die shadow","def":"The area under the die where the package mass changes heat flow and paste behaviour."},{"term":"Laminar X-ray","def":"Tomographic X-ray that reconstructs a single slice so depth can be resolved."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m07l03","m06l07"],"skills":["Lesson m07l03: AOI metrics: escape rate, false calls, first-pass yield, verification workflow","Lesson m06l07: Profile validation, documentation and change control"]}},{"lessonId":"m07l05","moduleId":"m07","stageId":"aoi","title":"Disposition language: defect, condition and class in workmanship terms","goals":[{"text":"State findings in workmanship-standard vocabulary so a second engineer reaches the same disposition.","competencies":["K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-009","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m07l05-01","q-m07l05-02","q-m07l05-03","q-m07l05-04","q-m07l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m07l05.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: macro photograph of a gull-wing joint under evaluation, schematic section with heel, toe and side fillets, a disposition table accept / process indicator / defect, and the three IPC classes","caption":"Disposition language: the joint, its fillets, accept / process indicator / defect, and the product class"}]},{"h":"Why disposition language matters","p":["In AOI, disposition language turns a visual or geometric call into a repeatable engineering statement. A second engineer should read the finding and reach the same accept, reject or rework decision without hearing the inspector's tone.","The statement names the object, the observed condition, the applicable workmanship class, the evidence and the verification status."]},{"h":"Defect, condition and class","p":"Workmanship vocabulary keeps three ideas apart. A defect is a nonconformance to the applicable requirement. A condition is the observable state that may or may not be a defect depending on class and criteria — insufficient solder, a lifted lead, tombstoning, a solder bridge. A class is the workmanship level, commonly IPC-A-610 Class 1, Class 2 or Class 3, which sets the acceptance threshold for that same condition."},{"h":"AOI output is evidence, not disposition","p":["AOI indicates a suspected condition from height, area, shape, colour or position. It misses hidden defects and produces false calls from shadows, reflections and component tolerance.","Until measurement, microscopy, X-ray or manual inspection confirms it, say indicated or suspected, or say that it requires verification. Words that assert a defect outrun the evidence."]},{"h":"A repeatable finding sentence","p":["A useful finding has a fixed order: location, object, condition, evidence, standard or class, proposed action.","For example: \"U7 pin 12: suspected insufficient solder fillet; AOI fillet height 0.18 mm; verify against IPC-A-610 Class 2 and paste deposit data.\" That sentence gives the next engineer where to look, what was measured, which class applies, and what to do next."]},{"h":"Class changes what a condition means","p":["A condition acceptable in general electronics may be unacceptable in a high-reliability assembly. IPC-A-610 Class 1, Class 2 and Class 3 describe increasing workmanship requirements, so the same fillet, component tilt or solder ball can be dispositioned differently.","State the class on the finding. Do not let the product name imply it."]},{"h":"Disposition terms match the system","p":["Workmanship acceptance is normally accept or reject against the applicable standard. A manufacturing disposition may add rework, scrap or use-as-is, and rework has to restore the assembly to the original acceptance criteria.","Rework and repair follow IPC-7711/7721 or the customer-approved equivalent, and the finding says whether rework is proposed or already performed."]},{"h":"Handoff information","p":["A handoff carries enough to reproduce the decision: panel or serial number, coordinate or reference designator, AOI program version, measured value, uncertainty or confidence, applicable standard and class, and verification method.","Where a number is process-based rather than standard-based, label it a process limit and cite the paste, stencil or AOI datasheet. AOI height uncertainty, for instance, may run around 0.02 mm to 0.05 mm, verified against the equipment datasheet."]}],"calc":{"task":"An AOI station reports 'suspected insufficient solder fillet' at Q1 pin 3. AOI measured fillet height = 0.18 mm. The internal process limit for this package is 0.20 mm minimum, and the AOI height measurement uncertainty is ±0.05 mm. The assembly is IPC-A-610 Class 2. Determine whether the finding should be dispositioned as reject, verify, or accept, and write the standardized finding.","solution":"1) Record the evidence: AOI height = 0.18 mm. 2) Apply measurement uncertainty: lower bound = 0.18 mm - 0.05 mm = 0.13 mm; upper bound = 0.18 mm + 0.05 mm = 0.23 mm. 3) Compare with the internal process minimum of 0.20 mm. The upper bound exceeds the minimum, so the AOI call alone does not prove rejection. The lower bound is below the minimum, so acceptance cannot be concluded without verification. 4) Disposition: verify. Use manual inspection, microscopy, or a calibrated AOI re-run; if the confirmed fillet is below the applicable IPC-A-610 Class 2 criterion and the internal minimum, reject or rework per IPC-7711/7721; if confirmed at or above the criterion, accept. 5) Standardized finding: 'Q1 pin 3: suspected insufficient solder fillet; AOI height 0.18 mm ±0.05 mm; internal process minimum 0.20 mm; verify against IPC-A-610 Class 2 before disposition.'"},"defect":{"symptom":"AOI repeatedly rejects a suspected solder bridge condition on QFP pins 14 and 15 at the same panel location after a stencil change.","cause":"Excessive paste deposit, paste release variation, or print misalignment can create bridge risk between fine-pitch leads; verify paste volume, alignment, and stencil condition against the paste and stencil datasheet.","action":"Quarantine the affected panel, verify the bridge with microscopy or X-ray if needed, adjust the printer or stencil process, rework confirmed bridges per IPC-7711/7721, then reinspect with AOI and record the disposition using the same workmanship vocabulary."},"takeaways":["Write the finding in a fixed order — location, object, condition, evidence, class, action — so a second engineer arrives at the same call.","Say suspected or indicated until measurement confirms the condition, because AOI output is evidence and not a verdict.","Name the class and the standard on every finding, since the same condition can pass in one class and fail in another."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Writing a finding that asserts a defect from an AOI call","instead":"State it as suspected and say what verification is still required."},{"mistake":"Leaving the class implicit in the product name","instead":"Print the applicable IPC-A-610 class on the finding itself."},{"mistake":"Quoting a process limit as if it were the workmanship standard","instead":"Label it as a process limit and cite the datasheet behind it."}],"terms":[{"term":"Condition","def":"An observable state that may or may not be a defect depending on the class and criteria applied."},{"term":"Disposition","def":"The recorded decision on a nonconforming item: accept, reject, rework, repair, scrap or use-as-is."},{"term":"Rework","def":"Processing that restores a nonconforming item to the original acceptance criteria."},{"term":"Use-as-is","def":"A documented concession to accept an item that does not meet the stated requirement."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m07l04"],"skills":["Lesson m07l04: X-ray interpretation: BGA ball size and voiding, bridging, through-hole fill"]}},{"lessonId":"m07l06","moduleId":"m07","stageId":"aoi","title":"Classification drill: print, placement, reflow and THT images","goals":[{"text":"Classify a defect image into a defect type and an accept / rework / reject disposition with the criterion named.","competencies":["K6"]},{"text":"State findings in workmanship-standard vocabulary so a second engineer reaches the same disposition.","competencies":["K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-009","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m07l06-01","q-m07l06-02","q-m07l06-03","q-m07l06-04","q-m07l06-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m07l06.webp","alt":"Four small board images in a row, one per process stage, each with a defect to classify","caption":"Classification drill: print, placement, reflow and through-hole images"}]},{"p":["In a classification drill, name the process stage from the image before naming the defect. A print image shows paste deposits on bare pads, matte and paste-like, with no component bodies and no reflowed solder.","A placement image shows components sitting on paste, with leads or terminations touching paste but not yet wetted. A reflow image shows solidified joints, shiny or dull depending on process, and may show damage or solder morphology. A THT image shows leads through plated holes with solder on the exit side, so the hole wall and the lead-to-hole interface carry the evidence."],"h":"Identify the process stage first"},{"p":["Classify the physical condition rather than the machine you suspect. Use workmanship vocabulary: insufficient solder, excess solder, bridging, solder ball, void, dewetting, lifted lead, tombstoning, misalignment, wrong polarity, missing component, insufficient hole fill, icicle, cold solder.","The same condition can arise from different causes, and the defect type and disposition rest on what the image proves about the joint or deposit."],"h":"Classify by condition, not by suspected cause"},{"p":["Compare the paste deposit with the stencil aperture and the paste datasheet. Measure deposit height in micrometres against the stencil and paste datasheet, then verify against your paste and equipment datasheet.","Insufficient paste, bridging, smearing, misprint and incomplete release are judged by whether the deposit can still form an acceptable joint after reflow. Paste outside the intended aperture becomes bridging or smearing, with the pad or net location noted. A thin, broken or absent deposit becomes insufficient paste or misprint. The disposition is usually rework of the print process and removal of the affected boards, unless contamination or damage leaves the board unusable."],"h":"Print defects"},{"p":["Check component presence, orientation, polarity and termination contact. A missing component, wrong component, reversed polarity, rotated chip or lifted termination is a placement or feeder issue until reflow evidence says otherwise.","A component on the right pad but not seated becomes misalignment or lifted termination. A component on the wrong pad, or with the wrong polarity, becomes wrong component or wrong polarity. Rework applies when component and board are undamaged; reject when the component is damaged, the pad is lifted, or the wrong part cannot be identified and replaced."],"h":"Placement defects"},{"p":["Judge the joint against the applicable workmanship standard, commonly IPC-A-610, and against the design intent. Tombstoning, solder balls, bridging, insufficient or excess solder, voiding, dewetting and lifted leads are the usual findings.","A solder ball on the board surface may be reworkable if it comes off without damaging the board, and a bridge between adjacent pads is reworkable if the pads and traces survive. Pad lifting, substrate damage or a component crack is a reject, because rework cannot restore reliability."],"h":"Reflow defects"},{"p":["Inspect the exit side for hole fill, wetting, lead exposure and solder shape. IPC-A-610 gives the criteria for hole fill, wetting angle, excess solder, icicles, solder balls and lifted leads, but the exact limits depend on product class and the current revision, so cite the standard and revision in the finding.","A dull, grainy or non-wetted joint points to insufficient heat, poor flux activity or contamination. A long icicle or excess solder may be reworkable where the hole is sound and the joint can be brought back to an acceptable shape."],"h":"Through-hole defects"},{"p":["Record the finding as location, component or net, defect type, criterion, evidence and disposition.","Accept means the image meets the named criterion with no reliability concern visible. Rework means the condition is repairable under IPC-7711/7721 or an approved procedure and the board can be re-inspected. Reject means the board or component is damaged, the defect is not repairable, or the criterion cannot be met without unacceptable risk. That wording lets a second engineer reach the same disposition from the same image."],"h":"Making the disposition reproducible"}],"calc":{"task":"Classify the following AOI image findings: a paste deposit outside the intended aperture; a chip component with one termination lifted before reflow; a reflowed joint with a bridge between adjacent pads; a THT joint with a dull, grainy surface and no wetting to the hole wall. For each finding, name the defect type and the accept, rework, or reject disposition with the criterion.","solution":"Identify the process stage from the image evidence. Name the defect using workmanship vocabulary. Cite the criterion that controls the disposition. Choose accept, rework, or reject based on repairability and damage. Record location, component or net, defect type, criterion, evidence, and disposition. For the paste deposit outside the intended aperture, classify it as bridging or smearing; rework the print process and remove the affected boards unless paste contamination or board damage makes the board unusable; cite the stencil aperture, paste datasheet, and IPC-A-610 joint acceptability after reflow. For the lifted termination before reflow, classify it as lifted termination or misalignment; rework placement if the component and pad are undamaged; reject if the pad is lifted or the component is damaged; cite IPC-A-610 placement and solder joint acceptability. For the reflowed bridge, classify it as bridging; rework if the pads and traces are intact; reject if pad lifting or substrate damage is visible; cite IPC-A-610 solder joint acceptability. For the THT dull non-wetted joint, classify it as cold solder or insufficient wetting; rework per IPC-7711/7721 if the hole wall and lead are sound; reject if hole wall damage or non-repairable contamination is present; cite IPC-A-610 THT solder joint acceptability."},"defect":{"symptom":"AOI flags a THT joint with a dull, grainy solder surface, poor wetting to the hole wall, and a lead that appears exposed at the exit side.","cause":"Insufficient preheat or dwell, low flux activity, oxidized leads, or hole wall contamination prevented proper wetting and hole fill.","action":"Quarantine the board, inspect adjacent joints, rework per IPC-7711/7721 if the hole wall and lead are sound, verify solderability and reflow profile against equipment datasheet, and update incoming inspection if contamination is confirmed."},"takeaways":["Name the process stage before the defect, because the same shape means different things at print, placement, reflow and THT.","Classify by the condition the image proves, not by the machine you suspect caused it.","Record location, defect, criterion, evidence and disposition, so the call can be repeated by someone else."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Classifying a paste smear as a bridging defect","instead":"Call it smearing or bridging from the deposit’s position, and let the reflow result decide acceptability."},{"mistake":"Rejecting a reworkable board with a surface solder ball","instead":"Consider rework first where the board and pads are undamaged."},{"mistake":"Citing IPC-A-610 without the class or revision","instead":"State both, since the acceptance limit depends on them."}],"terms":[{"term":"Icicle","def":"A pointed solder projection formed on a through-hole joint or lead during solidification."},{"term":"Cold joint","def":"A dull, grainy joint that never wet properly because of insufficient heat or poor flux activity."},{"term":"Misprint","def":"A paste deposit that is displaced, incomplete or absent relative to the aperture."},{"term":"Classification drill","def":"A structured exercise in naming defect type and disposition from images."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m07l05"],"skills":["Lesson m07l05: Disposition language: defect, condition and class in workmanship terms"]}},{"lessonId":"m08l01","moduleId":"m08","stageId":"tht-prep","title":"What stays through-hole and why: connectors, mechanical load, power","goals":[{"text":"Calculate plated-hole diameter versus lead diameter, clearance and expected solder fill for a through-hole joint.","competencies":["K7"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m08l01-01","q-m08l01-02","q-m08l01-03","q-m08l01-04","q-m08l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"Why through-hole still matters","p":["Through-hole technology still earns its place where a joint must resist mechanical load, hold a heavy connector, carry real current, or survive repeated insertion and removal. A plated through-hole gives the lead a barrel to wet into, and the solder inside that barrel adds retention and a thermal path.","The price is process control: hole diameter, plating thickness, lead diameter, flux activity, preheat and solder dwell all decide whether the hole fills and wets reliably."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 940 350' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Through-hole joint cross-section with plated barrel, lead, top and bottom fillets and land; clearance ranges; worked tolerance example 0.80 mm lead in a 1.10 mm hole with 75 percent fill'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;fill:#e2e8f0}.d{fill:#94a3b8}.t{fill:#53d1cc}.r{fill:#f44336}.o{fill:#ff9800}</style> <rect x='60' y='110' width='120' height='70' fill='#475569'/><rect x='300' y='110' width='120' height='70' fill='#475569'/> <rect x='180' y='100' width='14' height='90' fill='#b87333'/><rect x='286' y='100' width='14' height='90' fill='#b87333'/> <rect x='60' y='100' width='134' height='10' fill='#b87333'/><rect x='286' y='100' width='134' height='10' fill='#b87333'/><rect x='60' y='180' width='134' height='10' fill='#b87333'/><rect x='286' y='180' width='134' height='10' fill='#b87333'/> <rect x='194' y='125' width='92' height='65' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.45)'/><path d='M150,190 L194,160 L194,190 Z M330,190 L286,160 L286,190 Z' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.45)'/> <rect x='222' y='40' width='36' height='160' fill='#94a3b8'/> <line x1='194' y1='125' x2='286' y2='125' stroke='#e2e8f0' stroke-dasharray='3 3'/><text x='240' y='120' font-size='9' text-anchor='middle'>fill 75 %</text> <text x='440' y='50' font-size='11'><tspan class='t'>lead</tspan> 0.80 ± 0.03 mm → 0.77-0.83</text> <text x='440' y='70' font-size='11'><tspan class='t'>finished hole</tspan> 1.10 ± 0.05 mm → 1.05-1.15</text> <text x='440' y='96' font-size='11'>diametral clearance = hole − lead</text> <text x='440' y='114' font-size='11'>  min 1.05 − 0.83 = 0.22 · nom 0.30 · max 0.38 mm</text> <text x='440' y='134' font-size='11'>radial clearance = diametral / 2 → 0.11 / 0.15 / 0.19 mm</text> <text x='440' y='160' font-size='11' class='d'>working ranges: diametral 0.10-0.30 mm, radial 0.05-0.15 mm</text> <text x='440' y='176' font-size='11' class='d'>fill is a process result — predict, then confirm by section/X-ray</text> <text x='60' y='222' font-size='10' class='d'>barrel (plated Cu) · lead · solder fill · top and bottom fillets · land</text> <rect x='20' y='230' width='860' height='1' fill='#334155'/> <text x='20' y='255' font-size='12' class='t'>WHY IT STAYS THROUGH-HOLE</text> <path d='M60,300 l0,-25 M60,275 l-6,8 M60,275 l6,8' stroke='#ff9800' stroke-width='2' fill='none'/><text x='80' y='290' font-size='11'>mechanical load (cable flex, mating force, vibration) passes through lead + fillets into the barrel</text> <text x='80' y='312' font-size='11'>power: current through barrel and fill — rate against the connector spec and IPC guidance, not hole size</text> <text x='20' y='334' font-size='10' class='d'>Acceptance per IPC-A-610 for the product class.</text> </svg>","caption":"Through-hole joint cross-section and the clearance stack: 0.80 ± 0.03 mm lead in a 1.10 ± 0.05 mm hole gives 0.22-0.38 mm diametral clearance; 75 % fill from the trial"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m08l01.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: macro cross-section of a plated through-hole joint, schematic section with barrel, lead, fill, fillets and land, the load path from a connector through the barrel, and a clearance sketch","caption":"Through-hole joint: barrel, fill and fillets carry the mechanical load; clearance stack in the figure above"}]},{"h":"Finished plated-hole diameter controls","p":["The drill size is not the final hole size. The finished plated-hole diameter is what remains after copper plating and the final finish.","For solder fill the relevant gap is the finished hole diameter minus the lead diameter. A board supplier may control drill diameter, plated diameter, plating thickness and wall coverage, and the assembly process still has to verify the finished diameter, because that dimension sets both capillary gap and heat transfer."]},{"h":"Lead diameter and tolerance stack","p":["Connector leads carry a nominal diameter and a tolerance, and the board hole carries its own. The worst-case clearance combines the smallest hole with the largest lead, and the largest clearance combines the largest hole with the smallest lead.","For common small through-hole leads, a diametral clearance of roughly 0.10 mm to 0.30 mm is often workable for wave or selective solder. The right value depends on paste or flux, preheat, solder temperature, dwell, board orientation and pad thermal mass, so verify it against your paste, flux and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Clearance affects fill, not just fit","p":["Too small a gap and flux and solder cannot penetrate, air stays trapped, and the joint shows incomplete fill or voids. Too large a gap and capillary action weakens, heat transfer to the hole falls off, and the barrel may never wet fully.","A radial clearance of roughly 0.05 mm to 0.15 mm is a practical starting range for many plated through-hole joints, confirmed by cross-section or X-ray on the actual board and component."]},{"h":"Expected fill is a process result","p":["Geometry gives an estimate; the fill height is a process result. The theoretical annular volume is the hole volume minus the lead volume over the hole depth.","Actual fill comes out lower for oxide, weak flux activity, low board temperature, short dwell, poor wall wetting or trapped air. Run a trial, measure the fill height, and use that measured fraction for prediction until the process is stable."]},{"h":"Connectors, mechanical load and power","p":["A connector joint may be through-hole for pull-out strength, shear resistance and durability under cable strain.","On a power path the current travels through the plated barrel and the solder joint, so plating quality, solder fill and thermal relief design all matter. Check current rating against the connector specification and the relevant IPC guidance for current carrying and thermal rise rather than inferring it from hole size."]},{"h":"Verification before production","p":["Before releasing a through-hole design, verify finished hole diameter, lead diameter, hole depth, plating thickness and pad geometry.","Then run a solderability trial and inspect cross-sections or X-rays for fill height, wetting, voiding and solder balling. Use IPC-A-610 acceptance for your product class as the pass or fail reference, and treat the calculated fill as a prediction that measurement has to confirm."],"blocks":[{"t":"table","caption":"Selection factors: what still earns through-hole its place","head":["Factor","Favors through-hole","Favors SMT"],"rows":[["Mechanical load","Connectors, heavy parts, cable flex, vibration","Light, flat components"],["Board density","Low to moderate","High, fine-pitch"],["Thermal behavior","High mass: needs preheat tuning in wave","Controlled by reflow profile"],["Cost at volume","Higher: insertion + wave steps","Lower: print, place, reflow only"]]}]}],"calc":{"task":"A through-hole connector lead has nominal diameter 0.80 mm with tolerance plus or minus 0.03 mm. The finished plated hole diameter is nominal 1.10 mm with tolerance plus or minus 0.05 mm. Hole depth is 1.60 mm. A trial wave solder process gives 75 percent hole fill. Calculate minimum and maximum diametral clearance, radial clearance, annular cross-sectional area, theoretical annular volume, expected solder fill height, and expected solder volume.","solution":"Step 1: Calculate lead limits. Minimum lead diameter = 0.80 mm - 0.03 mm = 0.77 mm. Maximum lead diameter = 0.80 mm + 0.03 mm = 0.83 mm. Step 2: Calculate finished hole limits. Minimum hole diameter = 1.10 mm - 0.05 mm = 1.05 mm. Maximum hole diameter = 1.10 mm + 0.05 mm = 1.15 mm. Step 3: Calculate diametral clearance. Minimum diametral clearance = minimum hole diameter - maximum lead diameter = 1.05 mm - 0.83 mm = 0.22 mm. Maximum diametral clearance = maximum hole diameter - minimum lead diameter = 1.15 mm - 0.77 mm = 0.38 mm. Nominal diametral clearance = 1.10 mm - 0.80 mm = 0.30 mm. Step 4: Calculate radial clearance. Minimum radial clearance = 0.22 mm / 2 = 0.11 mm. Maximum radial clearance = 0.38 mm / 2 = 0.19 mm. Nominal radial clearance = 0.30 mm / 2 = 0.15 mm. Step 5: Calculate annular cross-sectional area using hole area minus lead area. Minimum area uses minimum hole and maximum lead: area = 0.32 mm squared. Maximum area uses maximum hole and minimum lead: area = 0.57 mm squared. Nominal area uses nominal hole and nominal lead: area = 0.45 mm squared. Step 6: Calculate theoretical annular volume by multiplying area by hole depth. Minimum theoretical volume = 0.32 mm squared x 1.60 mm = 0.52 mm cubed. Maximum theoretical volume = 0.57 mm squared x 1.60 mm = 0.92 mm cubed. Nominal theoretical volume = 0.45 mm squared x 1.60 mm = 0.72 mm cubed. Step 7: Calculate expected fill height from the trial fill fraction. Expected fill height = 0.75 x 1.60 mm = 1.20 mm. Step 8: Calculate expected solder volume by applying the trial fill fraction to theoretical volume. Minimum expected volume = 0.75 x 0.52 mm cubed = 0.39 mm cubed. Maximum expected volume = 0.75 x 0.92 mm cubed = 0.69 mm cubed. Nominal expected volume = 0.75 x 0.72 mm cubed = 0.54 mm cubed. Step 9: Compare the measured fill height and wetting to IPC-A-610 acceptance for your product class, and verify the process against your paste, flux, and equipment datasheet."},"defect":{"symptom":"X-ray or cross-section shows connector through-hole joints with solder reaching only the lower half of the hole, while the upper barrel remains dry or lightly wetted.","cause":"The hole-to-lead clearance is too small for the selected flux and wave or selective solder parameters, or the board temperature at the hole is too low for the solder to flow and wet the full barrel. Pad thermal mass or a large ground plane can also draw heat away from the joint.","action":"Measure finished hole diameter and lead diameter, then calculate actual clearance. Increase preheat or board temperature within the flux and board limits, increase dwell time, verify flux activity and application, and check hole plating quality. If the geometry is the limiting factor, revise the finished hole diameter or lead diameter and re-run a solderability trial with cross-section or X-ray verification."},"takeaways":["Use the finished plated-hole diameter, not the drill size, because plating and finish change the gap that governs fill.","Stack the lead and hole tolerances to both extremes, since the worst-case gap decides whether the process can fill at all.","Measure a trial fill fraction before predicting fill height, because the geometry calculation gives a ceiling rather than a result."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Calculating clearance from the drill diameter","instead":"Use the finished plated diameter, which is smaller and is what the solder sees."},{"mistake":"Quoting a single clearance figure as universal","instead":"Check it against your flux, preheat and dwell, since the workable gap depends on all of them."},{"mistake":"Treating calculated fill as achieved fill","instead":"Measure the trial fill height and use that fraction until the process is stable."}],"terms":[{"term":"Annular clearance","def":"The radial gap between the finished hole wall and the lead."},{"term":"Fill height","def":"The height the solder reaches inside the plated hole, measured from the exit side."},{"term":"Thermal relief","def":"A pad connection pattern that limits heat flow into or out of a through-hole pad."},{"term":"Plating thickness","def":"The copper thickness on the hole wall, which sets current capacity and wall strength."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m02l06"],"skills":["Lesson m02l06: Case: expired floor life found mid-lot"]}},{"lessonId":"m08l02","moduleId":"m08","stageId":"tht-prep","title":"Hole and lead: diameters, tolerance stack-up, placement tolerance, percent fill","goals":[{"text":"Calculate plated-hole diameter versus lead diameter, clearance and expected solder fill for a through-hole joint.","competencies":["K7"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m08l02-01","q-m08l02-02","q-m08l02-03","q-m08l02-04","q-m08l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m08l02.webp","alt":"A hole and lead cross-section with the diameter stack-up, the placement tolerance and a barrel fill percentage gauge","caption":"Hole and lead: diameters, tolerance stack-up, placement tolerance and percent fill"}]},{"h":"Plated-hole diameter is the assembly reference","p":["For lead insertion the relevant hole size is the finished plated-hole diameter, not the bare drill diameter. The drill is one input to the stack-up: copper plating, wall roughness and drill wear all reduce the usable diameter.","Measure the finished diameter from the assembly side and the component side, because barrel plating can leave the hole slightly non-cylindrical. A measured diameter close to the lead diameter raises insertion force, skew and fill risk. Check the finished diameter against the fabrication drawing and the board supplier's capability report."]},{"h":"Lead diameter is a range","p":["Treat the lead diameter as a range, never a single nominal value. Leads may be round, rectangular or formed, and the effective insertion dimension can exceed the nominal wire diameter because of plating, burrs or the forming radius.","Clearance is the finished plated-hole diameter minus the maximum effective lead dimension, and a small nominal clearance can disappear once tolerance stacks up. For production, calculate the worst-case clearance from the minimum plated-hole diameter and the maximum lead dimension, then check it against the insertion equipment's alignment capability and the component datasheet."]},{"h":"Placement tolerance consumes clearance","p":["Placement tolerance is the positional error between the intended hole centre and the actual lead centre. Feeder repeatability, vision calibration, tape, reel or tube handling, board warpage and lead-forming variation all contribute.","A lead displaced by more than the available radial clearance binds on the hole wall or misses the hole entirely. Manual insertion lets the operator correct some of it; automated insertion has to include placement tolerance in the clearance calculation. Verify the tolerance against the insertion machine specification and the panel datum scheme."]},{"h":"Clearance is not fill capability","p":["A joint can insert cleanly and still fill badly, because the annular gap is either too small or too large.","Too little clearance traps air, restricts flux flow and stops solder wetting the full wall. Too much lets the lead sit off-centre, opens a void path and weakens capillary control. The desired clearance is therefore a process window, not a number: for wave soldering, the gap must pass solder through the hole while keeping enough capillary action to fill the joint. Verify the acceptable range against the solder process, flux activity, preheat profile and the wave or selective-solder equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Expected fill depends on geometry and process","p":["Expected fill is expressed as the percentage of hole depth filled after solidification. Hole aspect ratio, lead-to-hole clearance, surface finish, flux wetting, preheat, solder temperature, dwell time and the soldering method all move it.","A high aspect-ratio hole with tight clearance fills less reliably than a shallow hole with moderate clearance. Use IPC-A-610 acceptance for the applicable product class and confirm actual fill with cross-sections or X-ray where required."]},{"h":"Stack tolerances before choosing the hole size","p":["A practical stack covers PCB finished-hole tolerance, lead diameter tolerance, lead-forming tolerance, placement tolerance and any allowance for board warpage or panel distortion.","Compare the result with the minimum clearance insertion needs and the minimum gap solder fill needs. A marginal result means a larger hole, a tighter lead tolerance, better placement calibration or a different insertion method, not a hope that nominal values hold. Use worst-case or statistical analysis according to volume and risk."]},{"h":"Close the loop with measured data","p":["The most reliable clearance and fill predictions come from measured distributions: plated-hole diameter, lead diameter, placement error, and fill results from pilot builds.","If the design clearance sits inside the calculated range and fill is still poor, look at flux activity, preheat, wave height, conveyor speed and hole wall condition. High insertion force points to drill wear, plating thickness, lead burrs or placement repeatability. Record the measured values against the design assumptions so the next hole and lead selection rest on real process capability."]}],"calc":{"task":"A plated through-hole is specified with a nominal finished diameter of 1.00 mm and a PCB tolerance of ±0.05 mm. A component lead has a nominal diameter of 0.60 mm and a tolerance of ±0.03 mm. The insertion placement tolerance is ±0.08 mm. Calculate the minimum radial clearance, the worst-case insertion clearance, and the expected solder-fill range for a wave-soldered joint.","solution":"1. Minimum finished plated-hole diameter = 1.00 mm - 0.05 mm = 0.95 mm. 2. Maximum lead diameter = 0.60 mm + 0.03 mm = 0.63 mm. 3. Nominal radial clearance before placement error = (1.00 mm - 0.60 mm) / 2 = 0.20 mm. 4. Worst-case radial clearance before placement error = (0.95 mm - 0.63 mm) / 2 = 0.16 mm. 5. Placement tolerance consumes radial clearance. If the lead center is displaced by 0.08 mm, the remaining radial clearance on the tight side = 0.16 mm - 0.08 mm = 0.08 mm. 6. Therefore, the worst-case insertion clearance is 0.08 mm radial, or 0.16 mm diametral, assuming the lead and hole remain circular and the lead does not bind. 7. For solder fill, use the nominal gap and the worst-case gap. Nominal gap = 1.00 mm - 0.60 mm = 0.40 mm. Worst-case gap = 0.95 mm - 0.63 mm = 0.32 mm. 8. A typical wave-soldered through-hole joint may show solder fill from about 75% to 100% of hole depth when the gap, flux, preheat, and wave parameters are within the process window. Verify the actual expected fill against the IPC-A-610 acceptance criteria for the applicable product class and against pilot-build cross-sections. 9. If the measured fill is below the required acceptance level, investigate hole wall condition, flux activity, preheat temperature, solder temperature, dwell time, and placement repeatability before changing the hole or lead size."},"defect":{"symptom":"Leads insert with high force or partially miss the hole, and wave-soldered joints show incomplete fill, voiding, or solder that does not wet the full hole wall.","cause":"The finished plated-hole diameter is smaller than assumed, the lead diameter is at the upper tolerance limit, or placement error consumes the available clearance. The resulting gap is too small for reliable flux flow and solder capillary action.","action":"Measure the finished plated-hole diameter and lead diameter on the assembly line. Recalculate the worst-case clearance including placement tolerance. If clearance is marginal, adjust the PCB hole tolerance, tighten lead tolerance, improve placement calibration, or verify the wave-solder process window. Confirm the corrected process with insertion-force data and solder-fill cross-sections."},"takeaways":["Stack every tolerance — hole, lead, forming and placement — before choosing a hole size, because nominal clearance disappears in production.","Treat clearance as a window with two failure ends, since both a tight and a wide gap spoil fill.","Compare pilot-build fill data with the design assumptions, so the next board is designed against measured capability."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Choosing the hole from the lead nominal plus a rule of thumb","instead":"Add the tolerances and the placement error, then check both ends of the gap."},{"mistake":"Ignoring placement tolerance in an automated line","instead":"Include it in the radial clearance, because the lead centre moves before it reaches the hole."},{"mistake":"Fixing poor fill by enlarging the hole alone","instead":"Check flux, preheat and dwell first, since too much clearance also ruins fill."}],"terms":[{"term":"Tolerance stack-up","def":"The combined worst-case effect of several independent tolerances on one gap."},{"term":"Aspect ratio (plated through-hole)","def":"Hole depth divided by hole diameter, which governs how easily solder fills the barrel."},{"term":"Insertion force","def":"The force needed to push a lead into a plated hole, an indicator of clearance and burrs."},{"term":"Process window","def":"The range of a parameter that produces acceptable results in production."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m08l01"],"skills":["Lesson m08l01: What stays through-hole and why: connectors, mechanical load, power"]}},{"lessonId":"m08l03","moduleId":"m08","stageId":"tht-prep","title":"Insertion: seating, stand-off, polarity, clinch, insertion damage","goals":[{"text":"Choose board direction, clinch and masking for a layout to limit shadowing at the wave.","competencies":["K7"]},{"text":"Identify insertion defects from a board image and name the process cause.","competencies":["K7","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m08l03-01","q-m08l03-02","q-m08l03-03","q-m08l03-04","q-m08l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m08l03.webp","alt":"An insertion machine head seating a through-hole lead, with stand-off and clinch callouts and a damaged-lead warning","caption":"Insertion: seating, stand-off, polarity, clinch and insertion damage"}]},{"h":"Shadowing is a layout problem","p":["Shadowing happens when a component, a neighbouring part or a fixture blocks molten solder from reaching a through-hole joint at the wave.","The wave sees the board as a sequence of obstacles: tall parts, dense pin fields, large ground planes and closely spaced components each create local dry zones. Choose board direction, clinch and masking so solder can reach the hole and wet the annular ring without being deflected or trapped."]},{"h":"Board direction controls the approach","p":["Pick board direction so the wave reaches the side of the component least likely to shield its own joint. On a rectangular part, the long axis usually runs parallel to the wave direction where the body would otherwise shade its leads. On a connector or tall part, the joint side should face the incoming wave where possible, or the board is angled to shorten the shadow.","The angle is process-dependent: conveyor angles are typically small, often around 3 to 7 degrees, verified against the wave solder equipment datasheet and trial boards."]},{"h":"Clinch geometry affects seating and access","p":["Clinch holds the component through insertion and transport, and it also sets the gap between body and board.","A tight clinch improves seating and reduces stand-off while closing the gap under the body and restricting solder flow. A loose clinch improves access and allows vibration to lift or misalign the part. Many through-hole components do best with a controlled clinch that keeps the body stable while leaving a small solder-access gap; check clinch force and final stand-off against the insertion machine and the component datasheet."]},{"h":"Stand-off trades seating against access","p":["Stand-off is the distance between body and board surface. Too little traps flux, prevents solder reaching the joint and makes cleaning hard. Too much demands more solder, exposes leads to mechanical stress and costs board density.","For many radial and axial components a practical range is around 0.5 to 1.5 mm, verified against body geometry, pin length, flux activity and the wave profile. Components with moulded bodies or heat-sensitive materials follow the manufacturer's seating or stand-off limits."]},{"h":"Masking can reduce shadowing","p":["Masking protects areas that must not be soldered, and a poor layout worsens shadowing by blocking the wave path or trapping flux.","A good layout exposes the annular ring and lead while avoiding tall mask walls directly in front of the joint. Place mask edges so solder flows around the joint rather than through a narrow channel. Dense layouts often do better with partial masking or local openings than with a continuous band. Verify mask thickness, edge clearance and solder resist compatibility against the wave process."]},{"h":"Polarity errors are insertion defects","p":["Polarity errors put a polarised component in the wrong direction: electrolytic capacitors, diodes, LEDs, keyed connectors, ICs with a pin 1 indicator.","Feeder setup, tray orientation, vision misalignment, manual error or a program that disagrees with the physical part all cause them. A polarity error can pass visual inspection when nobody checks the body marking, so the inspection method must confirm marking orientation, not just the presence of the part."]},{"h":"Insertion damage is usually mechanical","p":["Insertion damage covers bent leads, crushed bodies, cracked ceramics, damaged pin plating and torn lands.","Misaligned feeders, excessive insertion force, worn insertion heads, poor pin straightening and weak board support cause it. The location points to the cause: damage on one board side suggests a feeder or head offset, while damage confined to one component type suggests a tray, tape or feeder setup problem."]},{"h":"Reading a board image","p":["Identify component type, pin geometry and board side first, then look for patterns.","One component type affected suggests a feeder or program error; one board location suggests a head or support error; many similar joints suggest wave direction, clinch or masking. For shadowing, look for dry or thin solder on the side away from the wave. For insertion damage, look for lead deformation, body cracks or board damage at the insertion point. For polarity, compare the component marking with the silkscreen and the pin 1 indicator."]},{"h":"Match the control to the mechanism","p":["For shadowing, adjust board direction, clinch, stand-off or mask layout before touching wave temperature or speed. For insertion damage, correct feeder, head alignment, pin straightening or board support. For polarity, correct feeder orientation, the vision reference, the program or the manual insertion aid.","Remove the physical cause so the process produces acceptable joints without relying on extra solder or rework."]}],"calc":{"task":"A radial electrolytic capacitor has a body diameter of 10.0 mm and a body height of 12.0 mm. The board is moving through the wave at 1.2 m/min. The wave approach angle is 5 degrees relative to the board surface. Estimate the shadow length cast by the component body at the board surface, and decide whether a 0.8 mm stand-off is likely to help or worsen solder access if the adjacent component is 1.5 mm away from the capacitor body.","solution":"1. A straight-ray model with the wave approach angle gives shadow length = height / tan(angle). For a 12.0 mm body and a 5 degree angle, tan(5 degrees) is approximately 0.0875, so 12.0 mm / 0.0875 = 137 mm. 2. Stop at that number: it says the model does not apply. The 5 degree figure is the conveyor incline, and the wave is a flowing body that climbs the component and is pushed into the gap by the turbulent chip wave and by flux action; it is not a ray at 5 degrees. No line observes 137 mm shadows, and common practice keeps about 2.5 mm between a tall through-hole part and the joints behind it. 3. Use the course's screening model instead (lesson 9.5): shadow = height x tan(contact angle from vertical). With a typical 20 degree contact angle, 12.0 mm x 0.364 = 4.4 mm, plus 0.5 to 1.0 mm for turbulence and flux depletion. 4. Compare with the layout: the adjacent component is 1.5 mm from the capacitor body, inside the 4.4 to 5.4 mm zone, so predict shadowing. 5. The 0.8 mm stand-off is small compared with the 10.0 mm body diameter; it may improve seating but reduces solder access under the body, and in a 1.5 mm gap it worsens flux escape and solder flow. 6. Practical conclusion: in this tight layout the 0.8 mm stand-off worsens access unless the wave direction is changed, the clinch is loosened slightly, or the mask and component spacing are adjusted. 7. Verify the final stand-off, clinch force and wave angle with trial boards and the wave solder equipment datasheet. 8. Treat the screening model as a way to rank layouts, not to sign them off."},"defect":{"symptom":"A board image shows several electrolytic capacitors with one lead dry or insufficiently wetted on the side away from the wave, while the opposite lead is acceptable. The capacitor bodies are seated close to the board, and the adjacent component is less than 2 mm away.","cause":"The joint is being shadowed by the capacitor body and nearby component because the stand-off is too small for the wave approach direction. The tight clinch or seating condition reduces the gap needed for flux escape and solder flow, so the wave cannot reach the far lead reliably.","action":"Change the board direction or wave approach angle so the shadow falls away from the joint. Increase stand-off slightly within the component and process limits, or adjust the clinch to hold the part without closing the solder-access gap. If the layout is dense, revise the mask opening or component spacing to expose the annular ring and allow solder flow. Run a trial board and inspect the joint against IPC-A-610 acceptance criteria and your wave solder process specification."},"takeaways":["Set board direction and stand-off from the component geometry, because the shadow a body casts is what starves its joint.","Use a controlled clinch that holds the part and still leaves solder a path, since either extreme costs a defect.","Separate polarity and damage from solder defects, because they belong to feeder and insertion control rather than the wave."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Raising wave temperature to fill a shadowed joint","instead":"Fix direction, clinch, stand-off or masking, since heat cannot reach behind an obstacle."},{"mistake":"Clamping every part tight to the board","instead":"Leave a solder-access gap, or the joint starves under the body."},{"mistake":"Treating polarity errors as inspection failures","instead":"Fix feeder and program orientation, and add marking checks to the inspection method."}],"terms":[{"term":"Shadowing","def":"A dry or starved joint caused by geometry blocking the solder wave."},{"term":"Clinch","def":"Bending the protruding lead to hold the component during handling and soldering."},{"term":"Stand-off","def":"The gap between the component body and the board surface."},{"term":"Dry joint","def":"A joint that received too little solder to wet and form properly."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m08l02"],"skills":["Lesson m08l02: Hole and lead: diameters, tolerance stack-up, placement tolerance, percent fill"]}},{"lessonId":"m08l04","moduleId":"m08","stageId":"tht-prep","title":"Preparing the board for wave: carriers, pallets, masking, taping, orientation","goals":[{"text":"Choose board direction, clinch and masking for a layout to limit shadowing at the wave.","competencies":["K7"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001"],"quiz":["q-m08l04-01","q-m08l04-02","q-m08l04-03","q-m08l04-04","q-m08l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m08l04.webp","alt":"A board loaded into a carrier with masked and taped areas and an orientation arrow, beside a pallet cross-section","caption":"Preparing the board for wave: carriers, pallets, masking, taping and orientation"}]},{"h":"Shadowing is directional","p":["At the wave the board underside passes over a stationary solder wave, and tall components, connectors, heat sinks, shielding cans and pallet supports deflect it, leaving pads downstream dry, underfilled or poorly wetted.","The preparation job is to find the shadow sources, locate the critical pads, and choose board direction, lead clinch and masking so no critical pad sits in the wake of a tall object. Judge wetting and flux residues against the IPC acceptance criteria for electronic assemblies."]},{"h":"Choose board direction first","p":["For a single board or panel, rotate the layout so the long axis of tall rectangular components runs perpendicular to the conveyor direction where possible.","Where critical pads cannot move, rotate the board 90 degrees or use a small angle to move the wake off them. Prove the direction on a trial board, because wave turbulence, nozzle design and flux activity change the shadow length."]},{"h":"Clinch and lead formation","p":["Where leads are formed or clinched on the wave side, keep bends from lying across downstream pads. Prefer bends perpendicular to the wave, or directed away from critical pads.","Keep wave-side protrusion short and controlled, commonly 0.5-1.5 mm, verified against your equipment and flux datasheet. Long or angled leads act as small dams and lengthen the shadow."]},{"h":"Carriers and pallets","p":["A carrier or pallet holds the board flat, prevents bowing, and lets flux and air escape. Support pins and pallet edges must not block the wave path or cover pads.","Keep clearance under the board, often 1-2 mm, so the wave reaches the underside evenly. Check flatness and support placement: a bowed board creates local shadowing even with the right orientation."]},{"h":"Masking","p":["Masking covers areas that should not be soldered and can cut shadowing from large metal parts. Use high-temperature tape or liquid mask that survives preheat and wave contact.","Cover connector bodies, heat sinks, test points and surface-mount devices not meant for the wave. Mask edges should be flat and sealed, and the mask must not trap flux or block drainage. Check the material against your flux and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Taping","p":["Tape secures masks and protects edges. Use high-temperature adhesive tape whose release does not lift in preheat, and seal the edges so flux cannot wick underneath.","Leave drainage holes and pallet vents clear. After a trial, inspect for lifted tape, trapped flux and residue on adjacent pads, then set tape width and overlap — commonly 6-12 mm — from what the line shows."]},{"h":"Verification loop","p":["Before production, run a trial board with the chosen orientation, clinch, pallet and mask, and inspect pads downstream of tall components for wetting, solder volume and residue.","If shadowing persists, change one variable: rotate the board, change clinch direction, add masking, or adjust pallet support. Record the accepted combination so operators can reproduce it."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 120 mm by 80 mm board has a 12 mm tall connector near the center and critical pads 18 mm downstream of the connector. The line runs at 1.0 m/min with 4 s wave contact. Choose board direction, clinch, and masking to limit shadowing.","solution":"1. Estimate shadow length. For a tall component aligned with the wave, use a practical range of 1.5-2.5 times component height. For 12 mm height, shadow length is 18-30 mm. 2. Compare with critical pad distance. The pads are 18 mm downstream, so they are at the near edge of the estimated shadow and may be at risk. 3. Rotate the board 90 degrees so the connector long axis is perpendicular to the wave. For a perpendicular connector, use a reduced practical range of 1.0-1.5 times height, giving 12-18 mm shadow length. 4. Because 18 mm is still borderline, add high-temperature masking over the connector body and extend the mask 5 mm upstream and downstream of the connector. 5. Set wave-side lead clinch perpendicular to the wave or away from the critical pads, with lead protrusion 0.5-1.5 mm. 6. Run a trial at 1.0 m/min and 4 s contact, then inspect downstream pads. If shadowing remains, increase mask extension by 5 mm or rotate the board a few degrees, and verify against your equipment and flux datasheet."},"defect":{"symptom":"Pads downstream of a connector are dry or underfilled, with solder balls near the mask edge.","cause":"The board ran with the connector long axis parallel to the wave, and the mask edge lifted, allowing flux to trap solder at the mask boundary.","action":"Rotate the board 90 degrees, replace the mask with high-temperature tape sealed at the edges, extend the mask 5 mm past the connector, and run a verification board before releasing the lot."},"takeaways":["Set orientation before anything else, because a rotated board removes shadows that no amount of flux can cure.","Keep wave-side lead protrusion short and directed away from critical pads, since a bent lead forms its own dam.","Prove the combination on a trial board and record it, so the next shift runs the setup that worked."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Taping over drainage holes to protect a part","instead":"Leave drainage clear, or flux pools and leaves residue on neighbouring pads."},{"mistake":"Choosing orientation by board outline rather than by critical pads","instead":"Rotate until the pads in the wake are the ones that can tolerate it."},{"mistake":"Changing direction, masking and pallet support together","instead":"Change one variable per trial, or the accepted setup cannot be explained."}],"terms":[{"term":"Wake","def":"The region downstream of an obstacle where the solder wave is disturbed or absent."},{"term":"Wave-side lead protrusion","def":"The length of lead standing proud of the solder side after clinching."},{"term":"Carrier","def":"A fixture that holds the board flat through the wave."},{"term":"Drainage (flux and gas venting)","def":"The path that lets flux and air escape from under a board or mask."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m08l03"],"skills":["Lesson m08l03: Insertion: seating, stand-off, polarity, clinch, insertion damage"]}},{"lessonId":"m08l05","moduleId":"m08","stageId":"tht-prep","title":"Calculation drill: clearance and expected fill for a real BOM subset","goals":[{"text":"Calculate plated-hole diameter versus lead diameter, clearance and expected solder fill for a through-hole joint.","competencies":["K7"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-011","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m08l05-01","q-m08l05-02","q-m08l05-03","q-m08l05-04","q-m08l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m08l05.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: plated through-hole geometry with hole, lead, diametral and radial clearance, a blank BOM worksheet, tight versus loose clearance, vertical fill with fillets on both sides, a cross-section photo and class minimum versus customer requirement","caption":"Clearance and expected fill: geometry, worksheet, tight versus loose, disposition"}]},{"h":"Why clearance and fill matter","p":["Through-hole joints live on the interaction between plated hole, component lead and solder process. The finished hole diameter, the lead diameter and the board thickness set the annular space that solder must fill.","A calculation drill shows whether a BOM subset is likely to fill properly before the first build, which is cheaper than finding it out at cross-section."]},{"h":"Finished hole diameter","p":["The plated-hole diameter is not the drill diameter. Drill size, copper thickness and plating thickness combine to set the finished diameter.","Work from the approved land-pattern library and the fabrication drawing, and verify the finished size against the drill and plating capability. IPC-7351 gives land-pattern and hole-size guidance, and the final value still has to match what fabrication can hold."]},{"h":"Lead diameter and fit","p":["The component supplier controls the lead diameter, and handling modifies it. Measure at the seating plane and at the exit side where the lead is bent or deformed.","A tight fit blocks capillary flow; a loose fit lets solder drain away and leaves a weak joint. Record nominal and worst-case dimensions rather than leaning on one nominal value."]},{"h":"Clearance definitions","p":["Clearance is normally evaluated as diametral clearance, the hole diameter minus the lead diameter, with radial clearance at half that.","Both matter: diametral clearance affects assembly and alignment, and radial clearance is the capillary gap that draws solder through the hole. The window is not a design value alone; it must suit the wave or selective solder equipment."]},{"h":"What drives solder fill","p":["Capillary action, flux activity, preheat and contact time fill the joint. A clean, wettable hole and lead are what let solder climb and fill the annulus.","Preheat too low leaves the hole cold and produces partial fill, and excessive preheat oxidises the joint or dries the flux. Verify preheat and wave settings against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Acceptance and target","p":["The applicable IPC-A-610 class defines acceptance. IPC-A-610 sets a minimum vertical barrel fill of 75 % for both Class 2 and Class 3, and Class 3 additionally requires visible fillets on the primary and secondary sides.","A documented exception allows about 50 % fill, or 1.2 mm, for parts with fourteen or more leads whose barrels tie into internal thermal or conductor planes. Some customers specify 100 % fill contractually, and that is a customer requirement rather than an IPC-A-610 class limit. Set the process target above the minimum for the required class, and never let the target stand in for the acceptance criteria."]},{"h":"Calculation approach","p":["For a drill, use nominal dimensions first, then the worst-case tight and loose cases. Calculate annular volume and expected fill volume, then compare the result with the required class and the process capability.","Where expected fill sits close to the minimum, plan cross-section or X-ray verification before production."]}],"calc":{"task":"For a single axial resistor in the BOM subset, the lead diameter is 0.50 mm nominal and 0.55 mm maximum. The plated through-hole diameter is 0.90 mm nominal, 0.85 mm minimum, and 0.95 mm maximum. The board thickness is 1.60 mm. Calculate nominal diametral clearance, worst-case tight and loose diametral clearance, nominal annular volume, and expected solder fill at a 90% process target. Compare the expected fill with IPC-A-610 minimum hole fill for the applicable class.","solution":"Nominal radii: hole radius 0.45 mm, lead radius 0.25 mm. Nominal diametral clearance: 0.90 - 0.50 = 0.40 mm. Worst-case tight: hole minimum 0.85 mm, lead maximum 0.55 mm, diametral clearance 0.85 - 0.55 = 0.30 mm. Worst-case loose: hole maximum 0.95 mm, lead minimum 0.50 mm, diametral clearance 0.95 - 0.50 = 0.45 mm. Nominal hole volume: pi * 0.45 * 0.45 * 1.60 = 1.018 cubic millimetres. Nominal lead volume: pi * 0.25 * 0.25 * 1.60 = 0.314 cubic millimetres. Nominal annular volume: 1.018 - 0.314 = 0.704, which rounds to 0.70 cubic millimetres. Expected fill volume at 90% target: 90% of 0.704 = 0.63 cubic millimetres. The 90% target is above the IPC-A-610 minimum vertical barrel fill of 75% that applies to both Class 2 and Class 3; a customer requirement for 100% fill is contractual and is not an IPC-A-610 class limit. Verify the required class and process capability."},"defect":{"symptom":"X-ray or cross-section shows hole fill below the applicable IPC-A-610 minimum, with dull solder at the exit side and occasional open at the lead shoulder.","cause":"The hole-to-lead clearance is too tight for the wave or selective solder process, or preheat is too low, so capillary flow stalls before the hole fills. Verify against your paste and equipment datasheet.","action":"Quarantine the lot, measure finished hole diameter and lead diameter, check drill and plating records, adjust preheat and contact time within equipment limits, and requalify with cross-section or X-ray."},"takeaways":["Compare expected fill with the IPC-A-610 minimum for the required class, and remember Class 3 also needs visible fillets on both sides.","Run the worst-case tight and loose clearances, because the tight case is what stalls capillary flow.","Treat a contractual 100 % fill requirement as a customer requirement and record it separately from the class limit."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Assuming the drill diameter is the finished hole","instead":"Use the plated diameter, since plating shrinks the hole the solder must fill."},{"mistake":"Quoting 75 % fill as sufficient for every class and contract","instead":"Check Class 3 fillet requirements and any customer specification above the class limit."},{"mistake":"Setting a process target and calling it acceptance","instead":"Keep the target above the minimum but judge boards against the standard and the contract."}],"terms":[{"term":"Diametral clearance","def":"Hole diameter minus lead diameter at the joint."},{"term":"Radial clearance","def":"Half the diametral clearance, the gap on one side of the lead."},{"term":"Vertical barrel fill","def":"The percentage of the plated hole depth filled with solder."},{"term":"Documented exception","def":"A stated allowance in the standard that permits a lower value under defined conditions."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m08l04"],"skills":["Lesson m08l04: Preparing the board for wave: carriers, pallets, masking, taping, orientation"]}},{"lessonId":"m09l01","moduleId":"m09","stageId":"wave","title":"Wave architecture: flux, preheat, wave types (single/dual, lambda, micro-wave)","goals":[{"text":"Set flux type and amount, preheat level and fog/wave height for a given board and justify each setting.","competencies":["K7"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-002","src-008","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m09l01-01","q-m09l01-02","q-m09l01-03","q-m09l01-04","q-m09l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"The process window","p":["A wave soldering window is the interaction of flux application, preheat, wave contact and cooling.","On lead-free alloy the window narrows, because wetting and intermetallic formation both need adequate board temperature and short contact. Set the window by thermocouple profiling and cross-section, then verify against your paste and equipment datasheet."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 940 360' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Wave soldering line: fluxer, preheat, a dual wave with the turbulent chip wave first and the laminar main wave second, then cooling; wave types single, dual, lambda and micro-wave'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;fill:#e2e8f0}.d{fill:#94a3b8}.t{fill:#53d1cc}.r{fill:#f44336}.o{fill:#ff9800}</style> <text x='20' y='26' font-size='13' class='t'>WAVE LINE — board travels left to right, solder side down</text> <rect x='20' y='60' width='900' height='4' fill='#94a3b8'/><path d='M880,50 l20,12 l-20,12' fill='none' stroke='#94a3b8'/> <rect x='60' y='40' width='110' height='14' fill='#1e293b' stroke='#53d1cc'/><text x='115' y='51' font-size='9' text-anchor='middle'>PCB</text> <text x='100' y='90' font-size='12' class='t' text-anchor='middle'>FLUXER</text><path d='M85,100 l5,22 M100,100 l0,22 M115,100 l-5,22' stroke='#53d1cc' fill='none'/><text x='100' y='140' font-size='10' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>spray from below;</text><text x='100' y='153' font-size='10' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>solids checked on coupon</text> <text x='270' y='90' font-size='12' class='t' text-anchor='middle'>PREHEAT</text><rect x='200' y='100' width='140' height='16' fill='rgba(255,152,0,.45)'/><text x='270' y='140' font-size='10' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>top-side thermocouple,</text><text x='270' y='153' font-size='10' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>soak plateau before the wave</text> <text x='560' y='90' font-size='12' class='t' text-anchor='middle'>DUAL WAVE</text> <rect x='420' y='150' width='280' height='40' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.12)' stroke='#334155'/> <path d='M440,150 C455,150 462,112 470,108 C476,104 480,118 486,106 C492,118 498,104 504,110 C512,118 516,150 530,150' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.4)' stroke='#53d1cc'/> <path d='M560,150 C590,150 610,104 650,104 C690,104 710,150 740,150' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.4)' stroke='#53d1cc'/> <text x='485' y='208' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle'>1st: CHIP WAVE</text><text x='485' y='222' font-size='10' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>turbulent, narrow</text><text x='485' y='235' font-size='10' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>fills behind tall parts</text> <text x='650' y='208' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle'>2nd: MAIN WAVE</text><text x='650' y='222' font-size='10' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>laminar, smooth exit</text><text x='650' y='235' font-size='10' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>peels solder, clears bridges</text> <text x='560' y='255' font-size='10' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>solder pot · nitrogen hood optional: less dross, better wetting</text> <text x='840' y='90' font-size='12' class='t' text-anchor='middle'>COOLING</text><path d='M820,100 l0,26 M840,100 l0,26 M860,100 l0,26' stroke='#53d1cc'/><text x='840' y='140' font-size='10' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>sets the joint</text> <rect x='20' y='275' width='900' height='1' fill='#334155'/> <text x='20' y='297' font-size='11'><tspan class='t'>single</tspan>  one laminar wave for simple boards      <tspan class='t'>dual</tspan>  chip wave + laminar main wave for dense SMT undersides</text> <text x='20' y='317' font-size='11'><tspan class='t'>lambda</tspan>  long laminar wave, wide contact zone     <tspan class='t'>micro-wave</tspan>  narrow nozzle for small or selective areas</text> <text x='20' y='343' font-size='10' class='d'>Profile per IPC-7530; result judged against IPC-A-610 for the product class.</text> </svg>","caption":"Wave soldering line: fluxer, preheat, dual wave (turbulent chip wave first, laminar main wave second), cooling; single, dual, lambda and micro-wave types"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m09l01.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: side view of a wave soldering machine with fluxer, preheat, a solder pot with the turbulent chip wave first and the laminar main wave second, and cooling; a solder wave photograph; wave type profiles","caption":"Wave soldering architecture: fluxer, preheat, chip wave then main wave, cooling; single, dual, lambda and micro-wave"}]},{"h":"Preheat for lead-free wave","p":["Preheat activates flux, drives off solvents and brings board and leads to a temperature where solder wets without thermal shock.","Establish the top-side temperature before the wave by profiling. Common practice uses a soak plateau, and the exact value depends on board mass, flux and equipment. Place thermocouples on heavy ground planes and on small pads, and follow IPC-7530 for the profiling method."]},{"h":"Contact time and conveyor speed","p":["Wave length and conveyor speed set contact time. Too short gives insufficient fill and cold joints; too long brings excess solder, bridging and thermal stress.","Adjust conveyor speed together with wave height and pump condition, and verify the result with cross-sections and solder weight rather than with the display."]},{"h":"Wave height and nozzle condition","p":["Wave height controls wetting pressure and contact. A wave too high floods through-holes and makes icicles; one too low misses pads.","Nozzle clogging and pump wear change the effective contact time even when the speed has not moved, which is why wave height belongs in the same check as speed."]},{"h":"Nitrogen","p":["Nitrogen reduces oxidation of solder and flux residue, improves wetting, lowers dross and can cut bridging. It does not replace correct preheat or flux selection.","Control and monitor the oxygen level. The benefit is largest on fine-pitch and high-density boards, where the same wetting margin buys more."]},{"h":"Flux management","p":["Flux solids, activation and application density decide residue, wetting and cleaning. Too little flux gives non-wetting and shading; too much leaves residue, bridges and a cleaning burden.","Measure and trend flux density, and evaluate residue by cross-section and ion chromatography or an equivalent method."]},{"h":"Cross-section diagnosis","p":["Cross-sections show whether the window is right. Shading points to oxidation or insufficient flux activity. Excess solder points to too high a wave, too long contact or poor drainage. Insufficient fill points to low preheat, short contact, blocked holes or poor wetting.","Judge the result against the IPC-A-610 acceptance criteria for the product class."]}],"calc":{"task":"A board requires 4 s wave contact time. The wave contact length is 250 mm. Set conveyor speed. The board top surface must reach 105 °C before wave, starting from 25 °C, over 90 s. Calculate average preheat ramp rate.","solution":"First, convert wave length to meters: 250 mm = 0.250 m. Next, conveyor speed = wave length / contact time = 0.250 m / 4 s = 0.0625 m/s. Then convert to m/min: 0.0625 m/s * 60 s/min = 3.75 m/min. Next, preheat ramp = (105 °C - 25 °C) / 90 s = 80 °C / 90 s = 0.889 °C/s. Finally, convert to °C/min: 0.889 °C/s * 60 s/min = 53.3 °C/min. Verify the selected speed and ramp against your equipment, flux, and board thermal mass."},"defect":{"symptom":"Shading on through-hole barrels and pads after wave, with dull gray surface and poor wetting at hole entrance.","cause":"Flux activity too low, preheat too low, oxygen in wave zone high, or contaminated nozzle.","action":"Verify flux density and activation, profile board temperature, reduce oxygen, clean nozzle, and run cross-section."},"takeaways":["Set conveyor speed from the wave contact length and the required contact time, then check it against cross-sections rather than the display.","Profile the top-side temperature on heavy planes and small pads, because preheat is what decides wetting without thermal shock.","Treat nitrogen as a wetting aid, not as a substitute for preheat and flux selection."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Lowering conveyor speed to fix insufficient fill without checking preheat","instead":"Profile the board first, since cold holes cannot be filled by dwelling longer."},{"mistake":"Judging the wave by its height on the pump only","instead":"Look at the wetting pressure across the board, because nozzle clogging changes it locally."},{"mistake":"Adding nitrogen to compensate for a weak flux","instead":"Fix the flux selection and density, then measure the oxygen benefit separately."}],"terms":[{"term":"Contact time","def":"The interval a board spends in contact with the solder wave."},{"term":"Shading","def":"Incomplete wetting on a through-hole or pad caused by oxidation or insufficient flux activity."},{"term":"Dross","def":"Oxide and intermetallic debris that forms on molten solder."},{"term":"Ion chromatography","def":"A test that measures ionic contamination left by flux residues."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m08l05"],"skills":["Lesson m08l05: Calculation drill: clearance and expected fill for a real BOM subset"]}},{"lessonId":"m09l02","moduleId":"m09","stageId":"wave","title":"Flux selection and application: solids, activity, amount, pattern, verification","goals":[{"text":"Set flux type and amount, preheat level and fog/wave height for a given board and justify each setting.","competencies":["K7"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-002","src-014","src-016","src-019"],"quiz":["q-m09l02-01","q-m09l02-02","q-m09l02-03","q-m09l02-04","q-m09l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m09l02.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: spray fluxer under a conveyor, liquid volume versus solids per area, activity driven by finish and oxide, complete versus incomplete spray pattern, coverage coupon and wetting test, and a photo of a wet flux film under blue light","caption":"Flux selection and application: solids basis, activity, pattern and verification"}]},{"h":"Scope and decision inputs","p":["Flux selection for wave and selective wave starts from the condition of board and termination: base metal, finish, oxidation, hole geometry, component mass and acceptable residue.","On THT the flux must reach hole walls and lead surfaces. On a selective wave for SMT it must wet pad and termination without leaving excess residue under components. IPC J-STD-004 classifies flux by residue type and activity; confirm compatibility with the finish and the cleaning process on the datasheet."]},{"h":"Solids and residue","p":["Solids content drives residue, bridging and post-solder cleanliness. Low-solids no-clean flux leaves less visible residue and may need tighter preheat. Higher-solids rosin or water-soluble flux improves wetting on oxidised surfaces and carries more residue and cleaning.","Take the solids from the flux datasheet and check spray viscosity and residue acceptability against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Activity","p":["Activity must be high enough to remove oxides through preheat and wave contact, and low enough to avoid corrosion, solder balling or residue that traps moisture.","The IPC J-STD-004 activity classes guide the choice: low activity for clean, fresh finishes, higher activity for oxidised surfaces or high-thermal-mass joints. Verify both by wetting and residue tests."]},{"h":"Amount and pattern","p":["Apply enough flux to wet every solderable surface and not enough to pool in holes, under components or on non-solderable areas.","With jetted or fogged flux, pattern uniformity matters more than peak thickness. Check coverage with flux indicator paper, residue inspection or cross-section. Nozzle pattern, pressure, board speed and solids set the amount; verify against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Preheat","p":["Preheat activates flux, drives off solvent and reduces thermal shock. The board temperature must be high enough to activate the flux before the wave and below the damage limits of board and components.","Measure with thermocouples on heavy pads and component bodies, and check the target against your paste, equipment datasheet and board material limits."]},{"h":"Wave height and fog","p":["Wave height sets the contact area and the turbulence. Too low gives incomplete hole fill; too high gives bridging, solder balls and pad erosion.","Set fog or selective wave height against board thickness and hole pattern, then verify by cross-section and visual inspection."]},{"h":"Verification","p":"Verification covers first-article wetting, hole fill, residue, cleanliness and reliability. IPC J-STD-001 and IPC-A-610 supply the acceptance criteria for soldered assemblies, used alongside your internal residue and cleaning limits."}],"calc":{"task":"A 1.6 mm glass-epoxy board, 50 mm x 40 mm, has 100 through-hole pads with fresh finish. Select no-clean flux type, amount, preheat level, and fog/wave height, and justify each setting.","solution":"First, board area = 50 mm x 40 mm = 20 cm2. Next, select no-clean organic flux with low-to-moderate activity per IPC J-STD-004 because the finish is fresh and residue must remain acceptable without cleaning. Then set flux amount as a deposition of flux solids: a starting range of 0.08-0.23 mg/cm2 of board bottom area, which is 500-1500 µg/in² of solids and about 0.8-2.3 g/m². For 20 cm2 that is 1.6-4.6 mg of solids; take the mid value 0.15 mg/cm2, giving 3.0 mg of solids. With a 2.0% solids flux within a 1.5-2.5% starting range, the solution mass is 3.0 mg / 0.02 = 150 mg. If the fluxer output is 300 mg/min of solution, spray time = 150 mg / 300 mg/min = 0.5 min, about 30 s. State the basis before comparing numbers: figures quoted per unit of liquid, for example 0.5-1.0 g/ft², are several times larger than solids-based figures. Verify the solids deposition with a fluxometer in µg/in² or by weighing a witness card, and verify the range against your flux and equipment datasheet. Set preheat target to 100 °C within a 90-110 °C starting range, measured with thermocouples, to activate flux and reduce thermal shock; verify against your paste/equipment datasheet and board limits. Set fog/wave height to 1.5 mm within a 1.2-1.8 mm starting range above the board underside for hole fill without top-side overflow; verify by cross-section and visual inspection. Finally, justify: low-to-moderate activity matches fresh finish, 0.15 mg/cm2 of flux solids and 2.0% solids balance wetting and residue, 100 °C preheat supports activation, and 1.5 mm wave height supports hole fill while limiting bridging."},"defect":{"symptom":"Solder balls and partial hole fill on the underside after wave soldering.","cause":"Flux amount or solids too high for the preheat setting, causing flux to boil and eject solder; wave height may be too low for hole fill.","action":"Reduce flux amount or solids, increase preheat until board top reaches the verified activation range, and adjust wave height upward only after checking for bridging; verify with cross-section and residue inspection."},"takeaways":["Choose flux activity from the finish and the oxide on the board, then prove it by wetting and residue tests rather than by class alone.","Control flux solids rather than liquid volume, and state the basis whenever a deposition figure is quoted.","Set preheat from a board thermocouple, since flux activation happens at board temperature and not at the oven display."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Quoting flux deposition per unit of liquid when the specification is solids-based","instead":"State the basis, because the two figures differ by several times."},{"mistake":"Raising flux activity to fix a cold joint","instead":"Check preheat and wave contact first, since activity cannot compensate for a cold board."},{"mistake":"Judging flux coverage by the machine setting","instead":"Measure with fluxometer or witness card, because pattern uniformity decides the result."}],"terms":[{"term":"Solids content","def":"The non-volatile fraction of a flux that remains after the solvent evaporates."},{"term":"Activity","def":"The ability of a flux to remove oxides from the metal surface during soldering."},{"term":"Fluxometer","def":"An instrument that measures the flux solids deposited per unit area."},{"term":"Fog height","def":"The height of the flux fog applied to the board underside before the wave."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m09l01"],"skills":["Lesson m09l01: Wave architecture: flux, preheat, wave types (single/dual, lambda, micro-wave)"]}},{"lessonId":"m09l03","moduleId":"m09","stageId":"wave","title":"Preheat: board top vs bottom temperature, flux activation, thermal limits","goals":[{"text":"Set flux type and amount, preheat level and fog/wave height for a given board and justify each setting.","competencies":["K7"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-008","src-014","src-016","src-019"],"quiz":["q-m09l03-01","q-m09l03-02","q-m09l03-03","q-m09l03-04","q-m09l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m09l03.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: wave line with fluxer, bottom preheaters and wave, top versus bottom thermocouple curves, flux activation window under the component limit, thick board note, a preheat photo and a profiler","caption":"Wave preheat: top versus bottom temperature, flux activation window and verification at the board"}]},{"h":"The thermal path in wave soldering","p":["Heat enters a wave-soldered board mainly from the bottom, so the bottom surface rises quickly while the top lags.","The top surface temperature is the practical control point for flux activation and solvent evaporation. Put thermocouples on the bottom near the wave contact zone and on the top over a representative component or pad. For a typical 1.6 mm FR-4 board, a top-surface target of 90-130 °C just before the wave is a common starting point, verified against the flux datasheet, board thickness, copper content and component sensitivity."]},{"h":"Flux type selection","p":["Select flux by residue requirement, cleaning capability and hole-fill need.","No-clean fluxes suit lines without cleaning and where residue can stay, and they may leave visible residue and demand tighter preheat control. Water-soluble fluxes serve through-hole fill where aqueous cleaning exists, and they can be more corrosive when left on. Rosin-based fluxes remain in some repair and legacy processes. Take the classification and residue requirements from IPC J-STD-004 and confirm compatibility with the finish and the cleaning process."]},{"h":"Flux amount","p":["Flux has to wet every bottom pad, lead tip and hole wall without boiling violently, lifting components or creating fog.","For spray application the controlled quantity is solids per unit of bottom area: a starting range of 0.08-0.23 mg/cm2, which is 500-1500 µg/in² of solids and about 0.8-2.3 g/m², with the lower end for thin boards and the higher end for heavy copper or through-hole walls. Figures quoted per unit of liquid, such as 0.5-1.0 g/ft², are several times larger and not comparable.","For foam application, set foam height and dwell so flux reaches the hole walls without dripping. Verify the amount by weighing a test board before and after fluxing, against the supplier's solids and activity data."]},{"h":"Preheat level, top against bottom","p":["Preheat raises the board enough to activate flux and evaporate solvents without thermal shock or premature flux degradation.","A practical target is 90-130 °C on the top surface and 100-140 °C on the bottom immediately before wave contact, on a controlled ramp. Thin boards need less preheat to protect components, while thick boards and heavy copper planes need more to lift the top surface. Profile to IPC-7530 guidance and adjust until hole fill and wetting are stable."]},{"h":"Wave height and fog control","p":["Wave height must wet bottom pads and lead tips without flooding the top side or stirring up turbulence.","For a 1.6 mm board a starting height of 0.5-1.5 mm above the bottom surface is common, checked visually and by looking for solder balls, bridging and top-side contamination. Spray pressure, nozzle distance, flux viscosity and preheat control the fog: a fine even mist is what you want, and dripping flux or visible fog on nearby surfaces means too much application or poor atomisation."]},{"h":"Setting a process for a given board","p":["A thin board with light copper and small through-hole leads starts with lower flux density, lower preheat and lower wave height.","A thick board, heavy copper or large leads gets more preheat first, then more flux, then more wave height only if wetting is still short. A cold top side calls for more preheat or a slower conveyor; an overheating bottom calls for less wave height or lower wave temperature. Record the settings that give acceptable fill without solder balls, bridging or component damage."]},{"h":"Verification and acceptance","p":["Verify with thermocouple data, flux weight gain and joint inspection. Check hole fill, wetting angle, residue, solder balls, bridging and component damage against IPC-A-610 for the required class.","Document flux type, flux density, preheat setpoints, wave height, conveyor speed and wave temperature. After any change, re-run the profile and inspect a representative board before production."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 1.6 mm FR-4 board, 100 mm x 150 mm, 28 µm copper, 0.6 mm pitch SMT on top, 0.8 mm diameter through-hole leads on bottom, no-clean flux, aqueous cleaning not available. Set flux type and amount, preheat level, and fog/wave height, and justify each setting.","solution":"1. Flux type: choose no-clean low-residue flux because cleaning is not available; verify flux activity and residue against IPC J-STD-004 and supplier datasheet. 2. Flux amount: bottom area = 100 mm x 150 mm = 15000 mm2 = 150 cm2. Set the target as a deposition of flux solids, 0.15 mg/cm2 within the 0.08-0.23 mg/cm2 (500-1500 µg/in²) starting range for mixed SMT/THT with no-clean flux, giving 150 cm2 x 0.15 mg/cm2 = 22.5 mg of solids. Add 10-20% for through-hole hole walls if spray does not fully wet holes; target 25-27 mg of solids. At 2.0% solids that is 1250-1350 mg of flux solution. Verify by weighing a test board or witness card before and after fluxing and converting the result to µg/in² of solids with a fluxometer. 3. Preheat: set top-surface target 110 °C and bottom-surface target 120 °C before wave. Use a controlled ramp and verify with thermocouples. This activates no-clean flux and evaporates solvents while limiting thermal stress on 0.6 mm pitch SMT. 4. Fog: set spray pressure 0.15-0.25 MPa and nozzle distance 100-200 mm. For this board choose 0.18 MPa and 150 mm. Verify that the spray forms a fine mist, not drips, and that nearby surfaces are not coated with flux fog. 5. Wave height: for a 1.6 mm board, set wave height 1.0 mm above the bottom surface. This is within the 0.5-1.5 mm starting range and leaves 0.6 mm clearance to the top side. Adjust up only if hole fill is poor; adjust down if solder balls, bridging, or top-side contamination appear. 6. Justification: no-clean flux requires controlled preheat, so 110 °C top and 120 °C bottom are reasonable starting targets. A flux-solids density of 0.15 mg/cm2 is enough to wet pads and leads without excessive fog. A wave height of 1.0 mm should wet the bottom side without flooding the top. Verify all settings with thermocouple data, flux weight, and IPC-A-610 inspection."},"defect":{"symptom":"Solder balls and small bridges on bottom pads after wave, with flux residue on the top side.","cause":"Flux amount or fog is too high, preheat is too low causing violent flux boiling, and wave height is too high causing turbulence and splashing.","action":"Reduce flux density to the lower end, reduce spray pressure or adjust nozzle distance, increase preheat slightly, lower wave height, then re-profile and inspect a representative board."},"takeaways":["Control the top-surface temperature before the wave, because the bottom heats faster and hides a cold top side.","Work in flux solids per unit area, since liquid-volume figures differ by several times and cannot be compared.","Raise preheat before flux and wave height when a thick board fills poorly, because temperature is usually the first limit."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Measuring preheat on the bottom side only","instead":"Add a top thermocouple, because flux activation depends on the top surface."},{"mistake":"Comparing a g/ft² liquid figure with a solids deposition","instead":"Convert to solids per unit area before comparing the two."},{"mistake":"Raising wave height to improve fill on a heavy board","instead":"Increase preheat first, since a higher wave brings bridging and top-side contamination."}],"terms":[{"term":"Fog height","def":"The height of the flux mist applied to the board underside."},{"term":"Solids deposition","def":"The mass of flux solids left per unit of board area."},{"term":"Top-surface temperature","def":"The board temperature on the component side just before wave contact."},{"term":"Thermal shock","def":"Rapid temperature change that can crack components or delaminate the board."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m09l02"],"skills":["Lesson m09l02: Flux selection and application: solids, activity, amount, pattern, verification"]}},{"lessonId":"m09l04","moduleId":"m09","stageId":"wave","title":"Contact time and fog height: dwell calculation, wave height vs board clearance","goals":[{"text":"Calculate wave contact (dwell) time from conveyor speed and contact length and judge the result against the process window.","competencies":["K7"]},{"text":"Set flux type and amount, preheat level and fog/wave height for a given board and justify each setting.","competencies":["K7"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-014","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m09l04-01","q-m09l04-02","q-m09l04-03","q-m09l04-04","q-m09l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 940 380' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Dwell time equals contact length divided by conveyor speed; worked example 180 mm at 1.0 m/min gives 10.8 s against a 2-5 s window; fog height and board clearance set together'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;fill:#e2e8f0}.d{fill:#94a3b8}.t{fill:#53d1cc}.r{fill:#f44336}.o{fill:#ff9800}</style> <line x1='20' y1='120' x2='560' y2='120' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='3'/><text x='20' y='110' font-size='11' class='d'>board underside, conveyor speed v →</text> <path d='M120,200 C170,200 200,128 290,124 C380,128 410,200 460,200' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.3)' stroke='#53d1cc'/> <line x1='200' y1='215' x2='380' y2='215' stroke='#e2e8f0' stroke-width='2'/><text x='290' y='232' font-size='11' text-anchor='middle'>effective contact length L</text> <line x1='480' y1='124' x2='480' y2='200' stroke='#e2e8f0' stroke-dasharray='3 3'/><text x='488' y='160' font-size='10' class='d'>fog height</text> <line x1='60' y1='124' x2='60' y2='200' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-dasharray='3 3'/><text x='66' y='170' font-size='10' class='d'>clearance</text> <rect x='600' y='40' width='280' height='70' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc'/><text x='740' y='70' font-size='18' class='t' text-anchor='middle'>t = L / v</text><text x='740' y='96' font-size='9' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>t dwell s · L contact mm · v speed mm/s</text> <text x='600' y='140' font-size='11'>example: v = 1.0 m/min = 16.7 mm/s</text> <text x='600' y='160' font-size='11'>L = 180 mm → t = 180 / 16.7 = 10.8 s</text> <text x='600' y='182' font-size='11' class='r'>window 2-5 s → far too slow</text> <text x='600' y='204' font-size='11'>fix: v = 180/4 = 45 mm/s (2.7 m/min)</text> <text x='600' y='222' font-size='11'>     v = 180/3 = 60 mm/s (3.6 m/min)</text> <rect x='20' y='240' width='900' height='1' fill='#334155'/> <text x='20' y='264' font-size='11'><tspan class='t'>dwell windows:</tspan> typical 2-4 s · lead-free 3-5 s · heavy boards or chip + main wave up to about 6 s</text><text x='20' y='282' font-size='11' class='d'>anything above 6 s needs your own trial data to justify it</text> <text x='20' y='304' font-size='11'><tspan class='t'>preheat:</tspan> 90-130 °C on the board underside at wave entry · <tspan class='t'>flux solids:</tspan> 500-1500 µg/in² (0.8-2.3 g/m²)</text> <text x='20' y='326' font-size='11'>wave too high or clearance too small → early contact, long dwell, bridging</text><text x='20' y='344' font-size='11'>wave too low or clearance too large → no hole penetration, icicles, non-fill</text> <text x='20' y='366' font-size='10' class='d'>Set fog height and clearance together; measure L with a witness board; inspect the coupon against IPC-A-610.</text> </svg>","caption":"Dwell time t = L / v: 180 mm at 1.0 m/min gives 10.8 s against a 2-5 s window; fog height and board clearance are set together"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m09l04.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: board over a solder wave with effective contact length, conveyor speed, fog height and board clearance dimensions, the formula t equals L over v, a photograph of a board meeting the wave, and too-high versus too-low wave sketches","caption":"Contact time t = L / v, fog height and board clearance; worked numbers in the figure above"}]},{"h":"Contact time and why it matters","p":["Contact time or dwell is how long the board underside stays in the wave. Calculate it as t = L / v, where L is the effective contact length in mm and v the conveyor speed in mm/s.","Dwell governs heat transfer, flux activation, hole fill and solder penetration. Too little leaves icicles, poor wetting and incomplete fill; too much overheats the board, burns off flux, damages components and bridges."]},{"h":"Effective contact length","p":["Use the effective contact length, not the nozzle length. Turbulence, wave height, board clearance and capillary action in the holes extend the wetted zone beyond the nozzle.","Measure it with a witness board, thermal paper or a short test run with a marked board. On a dual-wave machine, record the contact length of each wave and the total residence in the combined zone."]},{"h":"The dwell process window","p":["Dwell windows are machine-, flux- and board-specific, and published practice is narrower than folklore suggests. A typical window is 2 to 4 s, lead-free processes commonly run 3 to 5 s, and only heavy boards or a chip-wave plus main-wave pair push contact beyond that, to about 6 s.","A single-wave window of 6 to 12 s would overheat thin boards and burn off flux long before it filled a barrel, so treat any figure above 6 s as something your own trial data has to justify. Verify against the flux and equipment datasheet and your trials.","Judge dwell by hole fill, wetting, residue and thermal stress rather than by clock time. A high-thermal-mass board may need longer dwell, while small components and thin boards may need less."]},{"h":"Flux type and amount","p":["Choose flux type from the board design and the cleaning capability. No-clean low-solids flux is common on mixed SMT and THT boards where residue is acceptable; water-soluble flux suits heavy oxidation or dense through-hole fill and requires cleaning.","Flux amount is specified as a deposition of flux solids in micrograms per square inch, measured with a fluxometer, with a typical recommended range of 500 to 1500 µg/in² of solids, about 0.8 to 2.3 g/m² (1 in² = 6.45 cm², 1 m² = 1550 in²). State the basis before comparing numbers: figures quoted per unit of liquid, for example 0.5 to 1.0 g/ft², are not comparable with solids-based figures and are several times larger.","Take the range from the flux datasheet and check it against the equipment datasheet. Too little flux gives poor wetting and dewetting; too much gives spatter, residue and solder balls."]},{"h":"Preheat level","p":["Preheat brings the board underside to a temperature that activates flux and removes solvent before wave contact.","Measure on the board underside near wave entry, not in the air. A common starting range is 90-130 °C, verified against your paste and equipment datasheet. Low preheat leaves solvent in the flux and causes solder balls or poor wetting; high preheat burns flux, oxidises pads and stresses components."]},{"h":"Fog height and board clearance","p":["Fog height, or wave crest height above the nozzle, sets how far the wave reaches toward the board. Board clearance is the gap between the crest and the board underside at entry.","A wave too high or a clearance too small lets the board meet the wave too early, which brings excessive dwell, bridging and board damage. A wave too low or a clearance too large stops the wave penetrating the holes, giving icicles and non-fill. Set height and clearance together, and check board bow and support."]},{"h":"Setting sequence","p":["Set flux type and amount first, then preheat, then wave height and board clearance, then conveyor speed.","Run a coupon with representative through-holes, pads and components. Adjust speed to bring dwell into the window, then adjust wave height or clearance to improve fill without pushing dwell out of it. Inspect the coupon against IPC-A-610 and record the settings."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 1.6 mm FR-4 board with 0.8 mm through-holes and small SMT components is run on a single-wave machine. Conveyor speed is 1.0 m/min. Effective contact length measured with a witness board is 180 mm. The flux datasheet gives a dwell window of 2-5 s for this lead-free process. Calculate dwell time, judge it against the window, and set flux type and amount, preheat level, and fog/wave height with board clearance for this board.","solution":"1. Convert speed: 1.0 m/min = 1000 mm / 60 s = 16.7 mm/s. 2. Calculate dwell: t = L / v = 180 mm / 16.7 mm/s = 10.8 s. Step 3: Judge: 10.8 s is far above the 2-5 s window, more than twice its upper bound, so the line is running too slowly whatever the hole fill looks like. Raise conveyor speed to bring dwell into the window: the 180 mm contact length at 1.0 m/min is an over-long contact zone, and the window is reached at v = 180 mm / 4 s = 45 mm/s = 2.7 m/min for the top of the window or 180 mm / 3 s = 60 mm/s = 3.6 m/min for a mid-window 3 s. Pair the faster line with a check of wave height and clearance, because contact that long also means the board is riding deeper into the wave than intended. 4. Flux setting: use no-clean low-solids flux for mixed SMT/THT if residue is acceptable. Set spray density from the flux datasheet range, for example 500-1500 µg/in² of flux solids (about 0.8-2.3 g/m²), measured with a fluxometer by the wet or dry method; adjust tank pressure and traversing speed, then verify coverage and residue against your flux and equipment datasheet. 5. Preheat setting: set board-underside temperature near wave entry to 105-120 °C to activate flux and remove solvent while limiting thermal shock; verify against your paste/equipment datasheet. 6. Fog/wave height and clearance: set wave crest height to 10-12 mm above the nozzle and set board support so the board underside is 0-0.5 mm above the wave crest at entry, giving light contact without forcing the board into the wave. 7. Validate: run a coupon at the new speed, inspect hole fill, wetting, residue, and component damage, then adjust speed, wave height, or clearance in small steps; a 3 s dwell that fills the barrels has replaced the 10.8 s dwell that also overheated the board."},"defect":{"symptom":"Solder icicles and partial hole fill on 0.8 mm through-holes at board exit, with wetted pads but no bridging.","cause":"Wave height is too low and board clearance too large, so the wave never penetrates the holes; flux coverage may also be low, and the conveyor speed has to be checked against the 2-5 s window at the same time.","action":"Increase dwell by reducing conveyor speed or increasing effective contact length; raise wave height or reduce board clearance within machine limits; verify flux coverage and preheat; re-run a coupon and inspect against IPC-A-610 acceptability criteria."},"takeaways":["Measure effective contact length with a witness board, because turbulence extends the wetted zone beyond the nozzle.","Bring dwell into the 2 to 5 s range by speed before adjusting wave height, since contact length is what the window describes.","State the flux basis in µg/in² of solids, because liquid-volume figures are several times larger and not comparable."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Calculating dwell from the nozzle length","instead":"Use the measured effective contact length, or the dwell comes out short."},{"mistake":"Slowing the conveyor to fill barrels","instead":"Check wave height and clearance first, since a slow line overheats the board without filling."},{"mistake":"Quoting a g/ft² liquid figure against a solids specification","instead":"Convert to solids per unit area before comparing."}],"terms":[{"term":"Dwell","def":"The time the board underside spends in contact with the solder wave."},{"term":"Witness board","def":"A test board used to measure the effective contact length and wave footprint."},{"term":"Fluxometer","def":"An instrument that measures flux solids deposition per unit area."},{"term":"Board clearance","def":"The gap between the wave crest and the board underside at wave entry."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m09l03"],"skills":["Lesson m09l03: Preheat: board top vs bottom temperature, flux activation, thermal limits"]}},{"lessonId":"m09l05","moduleId":"m09","stageId":"wave","title":"Shadowing and orientation: shading, pallets, board direction, lead geometry","goals":[{"text":"Predict shadowing from layout and orientation and change direction, masking or sequence to remove it.","competencies":["K7"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-008","src-014"],"quiz":["q-m09l05-01","q-m09l05-02","q-m09l05-03","q-m09l05-04","q-m09l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m09l05.webp","alt":"A wave with a component casting a solder shadow behind it, a pallet blocking the path, and an orientation arrow showing board direction","caption":"Shadowing and orientation: shading, pallets, board direction and lead geometry"}]},{"h":"What shadowing means at the wave","p":["Shadowing is a wetting or fill defect caused when a component, pallet, fixture or neighbouring part blocks the solder wave, flux or preheat from reaching a pad, lead or joint.","At the wave it is more than optical obstruction: local cooling, flux depletion, turbulence and poor drainage all contribute. Predict it by asking two questions — can solder reach the joint from the wave side, and can the joint drain after contact?"]},{"h":"Layout and orientation as the first predictor","p":["Layout predicts shadowing when a tall or wide component sits upstream of a smaller joint in the wave direction. A connector, shield, transformer or tall electrolytic capacitor leaves a low-energy zone behind it.","Draw the wave direction on the assembly drawing and mark the solder-side access path. A downstream joint behind a body, pallet wall or neighbouring part will show incomplete fill, voiding or cold joints. Confirm on a test coupon, because turbulence can stretch or shorten the affected zone."]},{"h":"Board and wave direction","p":["Board direction changes the shadow length, because the same component presents a different projected height to the wave.","A long body parallel to the wave direction can shade a whole row of joints, while rotating the panel or the component 90 degrees cuts the projected length. Check conveyor direction, wave angle and board angle together. Where the line allows panel rotation, compare the shadowed side before and after; where it does not, use pallet clearance or masking to compensate."]},{"h":"Pallets, fixtures and masking","p":["Pallets shadow when holes are too small, walls too thick, or support rails cover pads. They also trap flux, shield preheat and hold heat that changes drainage.","A good pallet opens the solder side around each joint, leaves clearance around leads, and avoids covering adjacent pads. Masking tape, kapton or pallet masking removes shadowing by blocking solder from areas that should stay dry, and the mask edge must not bridge into the joint. Check the pallet material, adhesive and wave equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Lead geometry and pad access","p":["Lead geometry controls how much solder enters the hole and wets the pad. Long leads, tight lead-to-hole clearance, bent leads and large bodies all reduce capillary action and drainage.","Axial and radial parts behave differently: an axial lead can be shaded by a body sitting close to the board, while a radial lead can be shaded by adjacent leads or a pallet wall. Check lead diameter, hole diameter, pad size and seating height against your drill and plating data."]},{"h":"Sequence changes that reduce shadowing","p":["Sequence can change at layout, pallet or line level. Place shadow-prone joints where the wave has stabilised, keep them out of the wake of tall parts, and use a pallet that exposes the joint from the wave side.","On some lines a second wave, a different wave angle or a selective wave improves access. Where a downstream part blocks drainage, move the part, rotate the panel or add a pallet opening. Confirm the change with cross-section or wetting inspection, not only with appearance."]},{"h":"A practical prediction method","p":["Start with a simple line-of-sight model: project the component height along the wave angle to estimate the shadow zone, then add a margin for turbulence and flux depletion.","Treat that model as a screening tool and not a guarantee. For production, run a coupon in the suspected orientation, inspect the joint, and adjust direction, masking or sequence. Check against your paste, wave and pallet datasheets, because the same layout behaves differently on other conveyors and wave types."]}],"calc":{"task":"A wave line runs left to right. A 10 mm tall connector with a 25 mm long body is placed 2 mm upstream of a row of 1.0 mm through-hole pads. The wave contact angle is estimated at 20 degrees from vertical. Predict whether the downstream pads are shadowed, then propose one orientation change, one pallet change, and one sequence change.","solution":"1. Draw the wave direction left to right and mark the connector body. 2. Estimate shadow length using a line-of-sight model: shadow length = height * tan(angle). For 10 mm and 20 degrees, shadow length = 10 mm * 0.364 = 3.6 mm. 3. Compare with the gap: downstream pads are 2 mm behind the connector, so 2 mm is less than 3.6 mm; predict shadowing. 4. Add a turbulence and flux-depletion margin of 0.5 mm to 1.0 mm; the affected zone may extend to 4.1 mm to 4.6 mm. 5. Orientation change: rotate the connector 90 degrees so the 2 mm wide end faces the wave; shadow length = 2 mm * tan(20 degrees) = 0.7 mm, which is less than the 2 mm gap; verify with a coupon. 6. Pallet change: open the pallet hole around the downstream pads and add 1.0 mm clearance around the connector body; ensure the pallet wall does not cover the pads. 7. Sequence change: move the shadowed row downstream of the connector by at least 5 mm, or place it after a stabilizing wave zone; if layout cannot move, use masking or a selective wave to expose the joint."},"defect":{"symptom":"Downstream through-hole pads show partial fill and voiding only on the side away from the wave, while upstream pads are acceptable.","cause":"A tall connector body and pallet wall shadow the wave, and the board direction places the pads in a low-energy zone with poor flux and solder access.","action":"Rotate the panel or component, open the pallet around the pads, and re-run a coupon; if the defect persists, adjust wave angle or use masking to expose the joint."},"takeaways":["Check whether solder can reach the joint and whether it can drain, because both halves of the shadow problem need fixing.","Change the projected height — by rotating board or component — before adding fixtures, since geometry is the cheapest lever.","Screen with a line-of-sight model and confirm on a coupon, because turbulence decides the real edge of the shadow."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Judging shadowing from the top-side appearance","instead":"Inspect the solder side and cross-section, since the top looks the same either way."},{"mistake":"Adding masking without checking the mask edge","instead":"Keep the edge clear of the joint, or the mask itself bridges the gap."},{"mistake":"Trusting the geometric shadow calculation alone","instead":"Add a turbulence margin and confirm on a coupon."}],"terms":[{"term":"Low-energy zone","def":"The region behind an obstacle where the wave arrives weakened and cooled."},{"term":"Projected height","def":"The obstacle dimension that faces the wave travel direction."},{"term":"Pallet opening","def":"The cut-out that exposes a joint to the wave through the fixture."},{"term":"Drainage (wave soldering)","def":"How readily solder leaves the joint after the wave passes."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m09l04"],"skills":["Lesson m09l04: Contact time and fog height: dwell calculation, wave height vs board clearance"]}},{"lessonId":"m09l06","moduleId":"m09","stageId":"wave","title":"Wave defect atlas: bridging, balling, insufficient fill, bypass, lifted lead, blowholes","goals":[{"text":"Diagnose a wave defect from a solder-side image and name the parameter responsible.","competencies":["K7","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m09l06-01","q-m09l06-02","q-m09l06-03","q-m09l06-04","q-m09l06-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m09l06.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: six through-hole joint tiles with macro photographs and cross-sections for bridging, balling, insufficient fill, bypass, lifted lead and blowhole","caption":"Wave soldering defect atlas: bridging, balling, insufficient fill, bypass, lifted lead, blowhole"}]},{"h":"Start with the solder-side image","p":["A wave defect atlas starts on the solder side, because the defect shape records the last thermal and wetting events.","Read fill height, meniscus, wetting angle, solder volume, and whether the defect is isolated or repeats with component mass. Compare images in the same field of view. Acceptance limits live in IPC-A-610, so confirm the applicable class and revision before judging a joint."]},{"h":"Bridging","p":["Bridging is solder between adjacent pads or leads after the wave. Too much solder volume, poor drainage, low preheat, a slow conveyor or flux residue all cause it.","On fine pitch, check wave height, solder temperature and flux coverage. Wave height windows depend on the equipment, so verify against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Balling","p":["Balling is separate solder balls on pads, traces or component bodies, and it usually means poor wetting or premature solidification: low preheat, oxidised leads, weak flux activity, or a solder temperature too low for the alloy.","Excessive turbulence and poor drainage can also throw balls. Compare the ball location with the wave contact pattern, and check preheat and solder temperature against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Insufficient fill","p":["Insufficient fill shows as a low solder column in a through-hole, often with a shiny meniscus at the bottom and little wetting on the lead.","The responsible parameter is usually preheat ramp or dwell: the top of the hole never reaches the temperature capillary action needs. High aspect ratio, board thickness and lead-to-hole clearance all contribute. Typical preheat ramps are 1.0 to 3.0 °C/s and dwell 2 to 5 s, with 3 to 5 s for lead-free, verified against the flux and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Bypass","p":["Bypass is a joint the wave never wetted, showing a dry pad or lead with little solder.","Wave height too low, board warpage, poor flux coverage, a conveyor too fast, or a shielding effect from nearby components all produce it. Check the wave contact line and board support, then verify wave height, conveyor speed and spray pattern against the equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Lifted lead","p":["A lifted lead has separated from the pad or barrel after wave contact, sometimes leaving a small solder remnant.","It is usually thermal-mechanical: high component mass, rapid heating, board expansion or insufficient preheat create differential movement. Handling and mounting contribute too. Check preheat ramp, component mass and board support against the process specification."]},{"h":"Blowholes","p":["Blowholes appear as voids or craters in the joint, often near the hole wall or under the component. Trapped moisture, solvent or flux volatiles boiling during wave contact cause them.","A fast preheat ramp, low top-side temperature, high aspect ratio or a contaminated board all raise the risk. Check preheat ramp, dwell and board bake requirements against your paste and equipment datasheet and the IPC-A-610 acceptance criteria."]},{"h":"From image to parameter","p":["Classify the image by shape first: bridging, balling, low fill, dry joint, lifted lead or void.","Then map the shape to a parameter — volume and drainage for bridging, wetting temperature for balling, preheat and dwell for insufficient fill, wave contact for bypass, thermal mass and support for a lifted lead, moisture and volatiles for blowholes.","Confirm with a short DOE that changes one parameter at a time, and record the image, the profile and the datasheet limits behind the change."]}],"calc":{"task":"A solder-side image shows a through-hole with board thickness 1.6 mm, hole diameter 0.8 mm, lead diameter 0.5 mm, fill height 1.1 mm, preheat ramp 0.8 °C/s, dwell 2.5 s, and solder temperature 245 °C. Diagnose the defect and name the parameter most likely responsible.","solution":"Step 1: Classify the image. Low fill with a meniscus at the bottom is insufficient fill, not bridging or bypass. Step 2: Calculate hole aspect ratio: 1.6 mm / 0.8 mm = 2.0. A high aspect ratio increases the need for adequate preheat and dwell; verify the threshold for your board stackup and paste. Step 3: Calculate fill fraction: 1.1 mm / 1.6 mm = 0.6875, or 68.75%. Compare with the acceptance limit in IPC-A-610 for the applicable class; if the limit is higher, the joint fails. Step 4: Compare process values with typical windows: preheat ramp 0.8 °C/s is below the 1.0 to 3.0 °C/s range often used for through-hole fill, and dwell 2.5 s is inside the 2 to 5 s window but short of the 3 to 5 s lead-free band. Verify against your paste/equipment datasheet. Step 5: Check solder temperature: 245 °C may be acceptable for some alloys but can be low for high thermal mass joints; verify against the paste/equipment datasheet. Step 6: Conclusion: the image and profile point to insufficient fill caused primarily by low preheat ramp and short dwell; adjust preheat ramp toward the verified range and dwell toward the verified range, then re-run with a single change at a time."},"defect":{"symptom":"Solder-side image shows low fill in through-holes with a shiny meniscus at the bottom, little wetting on the lead, and no bridging.","cause":"Preheat ramp is too low and dwell is too short for the hole aspect ratio, so the hole top surface does not reach the temperature needed for capillary action.","action":"Increase preheat ramp and dwell within the verified paste/equipment datasheet range, confirm top-side temperature at the hole, and re-run with a single parameter change."},"takeaways":["Read the solder side first, because fill height and meniscus record what the wave actually did.","Map each shape to one parameter before touching the machine, since the appearance already narrows the candidates.","Confirm with a single-parameter DOE and keep the image with the profile, so the fix is reproducible."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Raising solder temperature to cure balling","instead":"Check preheat, lead oxidation and flux activity first, since cold preheat is the usual cause."},{"mistake":"Treating a lifted lead as a solder defect","instead":"Review preheat ramp, component mass and board support, which is where the movement comes from."},{"mistake":"Reading fill height without the aspect ratio","instead":"Note hole depth and diameter, because a deep narrow hole needs more energy to fill."}],"terms":[{"term":"Bypass","def":"A joint that the wave failed to wet at all."},{"term":"Blowhole","def":"A void or crater in the joint caused by expanding trapped volatiles."},{"term":"Aspect ratio (plated through-hole)","def":"Hole depth divided by hole diameter, the same name as the stencil ratio but a different quantity."},{"term":"Meniscus","def":"The curved solder surface at the top of a filled hole or at the joint edge."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m09l05","m08l05"],"skills":["Lesson m09l05: Shadowing and orientation: shading, pallets, board direction, lead geometry","Lesson m08l05: Calculation drill: clearance and expected fill for a real BOM subset"]}},{"lessonId":"m09l07","moduleId":"m09","stageId":"wave","title":"Wave process control: bath contamination, tin consumption, records, first article","goals":[{"text":"Set flux type and amount, preheat level and fog/wave height for a given board and justify each setting.","competencies":["K7"]},{"text":"Diagnose a wave defect from a solder-side image and name the parameter responsible.","competencies":["K7","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-014","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m09l07-01","q-m09l07-02","q-m09l07-03","q-m09l07-04"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m09l07.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: solder pot with pump, nozzle and sampling ladle, contamination bars against a limit, tin consumption trend with top-ups, parameter record fields, a wave-soldered underside photo and a first-article image kept with the record","caption":"Wave process control: bath contamination, tin consumption, parameter record and first article"}]},{"h":"The process control objective","p":["Wave control sets the thermal and chemical conditions that let solder wet through-hole barrels and solder-side terminations without bridges, non-wetting or spatter.","The controls are flux type and amount, preheat temperature, wave or fog height, conveyor speed and pot temperature. For a given board, justify each one from board mass, copper content, component heat sinks, the flux activation range and the alloy liquidus."]},{"h":"Flux type selection","p":["Choose the flux from the contamination level and the residue requirement.","Rosin mildly activated flux is common on general through-hole assemblies where residues are cleaned or acceptable. Water-soluble flux suits a high oxide load or a planned cleaning step. No-clean fluxes appear less often at the wave, because residue and spatter are harder to manage there; check compatibility against the flux datasheet and the cleaning process."]},{"h":"Flux amount","p":["Control flux as a deposition of solids per unit area of the bottom side. A practical starting range is 0.08 to 0.23 mg/cm2 of solids, which is 500-1500 µg/in² and about 0.8-2.3 g/m², verified against the flux datasheet and the spray pattern.","Wet mass per unit area is several times larger because the flux is roughly 2 % solids, so state whether a figure is on a solids or a liquid basis before comparing it. Too little flux gives non-wetting and dull joints; too much gives spatter, residue and poor fill. Weight a test coupon or board before and after fluxing to confirm coverage."]},{"h":"Preheat level","p":["Preheat activates flux, removes volatiles and reduces thermal shock.","Measure the top surface or a representative thermal mass with a thermocouple. A practical entry range is 90 to 120 °C for many boards, with the flux activation temperature and the board maximum setting the final value. A cold board boils flux violently and spatters; a hot one burns the flux out before the wave arrives."]},{"h":"Wave height and fog height","p":["Wave height decides how much solder contacts the board bottom.","On a single wave, a crest 1 to 2 mm above the nozzle is a common starting point. On a dual-wave machine the first wave may sit near 1 to 2 mm and the second near 0.5 to 1 mm, with fog height often 2 to 5 mm above the nozzle. Check the equipment manual, because nozzle geometry changes the relationship between crest height and board immersion."]},{"h":"Contact time and pot temperature","p":["Contact time is wave length divided by conveyor speed. A practical range is 2 to 4 s for many boards, adjusted for hole fill and component heat sinks.","Pot temperature must sit above the alloy liquidus: 245 to 255 °C is common for Sn63/Pb37 and 250 to 260 °C for SAC305. Verify against the alloy datasheet and equipment capability, and record the actual pot temperature."]},{"h":"Bath contamination and tin consumption","p":["Bath condition changes wetting and the defect rate. Copper, nickel, iron and flux residues reach the bath from boards, fixtures and nozzles, so monitor contamination against the solder supplier's limits and change or filter the bath when wetting degrades.","Tin leaves through dross, drag-out, evaporation and replenishment losses. Track solder added per board or per hour, dross handling and pot level to keep wave height stable."]},{"h":"Records and first article","p":["Record flux type, lot, wet flux density, preheat temperature, wave height, fog height, conveyor speed, pot temperature and any deviation.","For the first article, run the board, inspect the solder side against IPC-A-610 Class 2 or 3, measure hole fill and keep a solder-side image. If it fails, change one parameter at a time and record the result."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 1.6 mm FR-4 mixed assembly has 17 µm copper, 2 mm through-hole connectors, and small chip passives. The bottom area is 100 mm by 150 mm. Select flux type, flux amount, preheat level, wave height, fog height, and conveyor speed for a single-wave process, and justify each setting.","solution":"First, identify thermal behavior. The board is moderate mass with through-hole connectors that act as heat sinks, so preheat must be sufficient to bring the joint area to flux activation temperature without overheating the top side. Second, select flux. Use rosin mildly activated flux for general through-hole wetting, assuming residues are acceptable or cleaned; verify the flux activation range against the flux datasheet. Third, set flux amount. Target 0.20 mg/cm2 of flux solids on the bottom side, within the 0.08-0.23 mg/cm2 (500-1500 µg/in²) starting range. Bottom area is 100 mm x 150 mm = 15000 mm2 = 150 cm2. Required flux solids is 0.20 mg/cm2 x 150 cm2 = 30 mg per board; at 2.0% solids that is about 1500 mg of flux solution. Verify the solids deposition with a fluxometer or a weighed witness card. Fourth, set preheat. Target top surface temperature of 105 °C at wave entry, within the practical 90 to 120 °C range, then verify with a thermocouple on a test coupon. Fifth, set wave height. Use a single-wave crest height of 1.5 mm above the nozzle, within the 1 to 2 mm starting range, so the board bottom contacts the crest without excessive immersion. Sixth, set fog height. Use 3 mm fog height above the nozzle to pre-wet the underside before the main wave; verify against the machine manual. Seventh, set contact time. If the wave length is 80 mm and conveyor speed is 25 mm/s, contact time is 80 mm / 25 mm/s = 3.2 s, within the 2 to 4 s practical range. Eighth, set pot temperature. For SAC305, use 255 °C, above the SAC305 liquidus of 219 °C (217 °C is the solidus), and verify against the alloy and equipment datasheet. Ninth, justify. The flux amount supports wetting without excess residue, preheat activates flux and reduces spatter, wave height and contact time provide hole fill without excessive bridging, and pot temperature maintains solder fluidity."},"defect":{"symptom":"Solder-side image shows scattered small solder balls and spatter around through-hole connector leads, with some dull non-wetted pads nearby.","cause":"Preheat temperature was too low for the flux activation range, so flux volatiles boiled during wave contact and caused spatter; the same condition can reduce wetting and leave dull joints.","action":"Increase preheat so the top surface reaches 100 to 110 °C at wave entry, verify with a thermocouple, confirm flux activation range from the flux datasheet, and re-run first article. If spatter persists, reduce wave height or fog height slightly and check for board moisture or excessive flux volume."},"takeaways":["Justify each setting from board mass, copper and alloy, since the same recipe moves everything when the board changes.","State flux figures on a solids or liquid basis before comparing them, because the two differ by several times.","Keep the first-article image with the parameter record, so the next run starts from evidence rather than memory."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Setting pot temperature below the alloy liquidus","instead":"Confirm the liquidus from the alloy datasheet, since a cold pot cannot wet."},{"mistake":"Recording settings without the flux lot","instead":"Include the lot, because bath and flux condition drift with it."},{"mistake":"Changing preheat, wave height and speed together on a failing first article","instead":"Change one parameter at a time, or the cause is lost."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pot temperature","def":"The temperature of the molten solder in the wave reservoir."},{"term":"Crest height","def":"The height of the solder wave above the nozzle opening."},{"term":"Dross","def":"Oxide and intermetallic debris that forms on the molten solder surface."},{"term":"Flux activation range","def":"The temperature band in which the flux removes oxides effectively."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m09l06"],"skills":["Lesson m09l06: Wave defect atlas: bridging, balling, insufficient fill, bypass, lifted lead, blowholes"]}},{"lessonId":"m10l01","moduleId":"m10","stageId":"selective","title":"Where selective beats wave: mixed assembly, heat-sensitive neighbours, throughput","goals":[{"text":"Choose nozzle and flux strategy for a joint group and defend the choice against the joint geometry.","competencies":["K7"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-002","src-009","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m10l01-01","q-m10l01-02","q-m10l01-03","q-m10l01-04","q-m10l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"Why selective soldering is chosen","p":["Selective soldering serves mixed assemblies where through-hole joints cannot go through a full wave without damaging nearby surface-mount parts, plastic connectors or heat-sensitive devices.","The process localises heat and flux to one joint group, so neighbours stay protected while the joint stays repeatable. The choice is a joint geometry decision rather than a question of machine availability.","The group must be reachable from the bottom side, have clearance for the nozzle, and let flux reach the joint without being absorbed by adjacent paste or trapped under a component. Agree the IPC-A-610 acceptance class before the trial, because the same visual defect passes in one class and fails in another. Confirm too that the process brings no new risk of its own: flux migration, solder balls, or thermal damage to neighbouring joints."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 900 240' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Selective soldering schematic'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace}</style><rect x='150' y='90' width='600' height='24' fill='rgba(76,175,80,.12)' stroke='#4caf50' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='250' y='60' width='60' height='30' fill='rgba(148,163,184,.2)' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><rect x='450' y='60' width='60' height='30' fill='rgba(148,163,184,.2)' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><rect x='620' y='60' width='60' height='30' fill='rgba(148,163,184,.2)' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><path d='M400,200 C400,170 380,160 380,140 C380,126 392,118 400,118 C408,118 415,124 415,132' fill='none' stroke='#f44336' stroke-width='2.5'/><rect x='370' y='196' width='60' height='30' rx='6' fill='rgba(244,67,54,.15)' stroke='#f44336' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='400' y='216' fill='#f44336' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>NOZZLE</text><text x='400' y='102' fill='#53d1cc' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>PROGRAMMABLE MICRO-WAVE</text><text x='450' y='80' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>THT LEADS</text><text x='450' y='152' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>BOARD BOTTOM — ONLY TARGETED PADS WETTED</text><text x='40' y='24' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' font-weight='600'>SELECTIVE SOLDERING PRINCIPLE</text><text x='40' y='44' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12'>A SMALL COLLUMN OF MOLTEN SOLDER TOUCHES ONLY THE TARGETED THROUGH-HOLE PADS</text></svg>","caption":"Selective soldering: a programmable micro-wave wets only targeted pads"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m10l01.webp","alt":"A mixed assembly with through-hole joints beside surface-mount parts, and a selective nozzle soldering one joint while the neighbours stay cool","caption":"Where selective beats wave: mixed assembly, heat-sensitive neighbours and throughput"}]},{"h":"Joint groups and geometry","p":["A joint group is a set of joints served by one nozzle path, one flux application and one thermal profile. Isolated pins, connector rows, mixed through-hole and surface-mount pads, and repair zones are typical.","Geometry decides the choice: pin diameter, hole plating, pad size, standoff height, body height and the distance to the nearest heat-sensitive part tell you whether a mini-wave, dip or jet nozzle can reach the joint. A tall connector may need a taller nozzle or a different flux delivery method, and a low-standoff part may need a shallow wave to avoid flooding.","Check against the board layout and nozzle datasheet before committing to a path. Where a group mixes technologies, confirm that surface-mount joints are already reflowed or that the selective process will not disturb them."]},{"h":"Nozzle selection","p":["Nozzle choice balances wetting energy, thermal exposure and reach.","A mini-wave makes a small solder wave and suits exposed through-hole pins and mixed joints. A dip nozzle gives more uniform immersion for small groups and raises the heat seen by nearby pads. Jet or spray-assisted nozzles improve flux delivery and wetting on tight pitch and demand control of splash and solder mist.","With heat-sensitive neighbours, take the smallest nozzle that still wets completely, and verify nozzle height, wave height and dwell against the equipment datasheet. Record practice values such as orifice size, wave height and standoff as a range until capability is established, and keep the nozzle compatible with the alloy and flux chemistry."]},{"h":"Flux strategy","p":["Flux strategy has to match joint geometry and contamination level.","Clean boards with plated through-holes may need only a low-solids liquid flux applied by controlled spray or jet. Oxidised pins, high-thermal-mass connectors or joints with limited access may need higher activity or a pre-applied flux preform, with residue and corrosion risk evaluated first.","Give enough flux to wet the joint and the wall without migrating to nearby surface-mount joints or throwing solder balls. With aerosol or ultrasonic delivery, verify droplet size, pressure and solids against the paste and flux datasheets, because the same nozzle behaves differently with another chemistry. Check flux placement visually and by cross-section before release."]},{"h":"Heat-sensitive neighbours","p":["Neighbours are the reason for the process, so the thermal budget has to be controlled at the joint rather than at the nozzle.","Preheat should bring board and joint to a stable temperature before solder contact, which cuts thermal shock and improves wetting without a long dwell. Set peak and dwell from the component moisture sensitivity level and the alloy liquidus, with a margin agreed by the design and reliability teams.","Measure the surface temperature at the neighbour's body or pin, not only the pot temperature, and check a plastic connector, electrolytic capacitor or small-signal device against its datasheet and the IPC J-STD-001 thermal exposure guidance. Profile the worst-case joint and the nearest sensitive component together."]},{"h":"Throughput and cycle time","p":["Throughput follows the number of joint groups, path length, dwell, flux application time and preheat stabilisation.","A single group runs quickly, while a mixed assembly often needs several passes, nozzle changes or flux applications. Model cycle time as fixed plus variable elements: flux application, preheat, solder dwell, cooling and a rework allowance.","When throughput is short, cut unnecessary path length or combine compatible groups first, then adjust dwell and flux within the wetting window. Never shorten dwell below the point where fill and wetting hold unless a qualified alternate process is approved. Confirm the joint against IPC-A-610 and guide any rework by IPC-7711/7721."]},{"h":"Decision method","p":["A defensible decision starts with geometry. Draw the joint group, mark the nozzle access path, and identify the nearest heat-sensitive component.","Then choose the nozzle that reaches the joint with the least thermal exposure, and the flux that wets without migrating. A low-standoff joint near a plastic connector suits a shallow mini-wave with low-solids flux. A high-thermal-mass connector with oxidised pins suits a dip nozzle, higher activity and longer preheat. Tight-pitch mixed joints often want a jet-assisted nozzle with controlled flux volume.","Defend the choice by showing that geometry, neighbour clearance and thermal budget fit the nozzle and flux, and back it with trial data rather than vendor claims."]},{"h":"Verification and documentation","p":["Verify with cross-sections, hole fill, wetting angle, residue inspection and thermal profiling at both the joint and the neighbour.","Record nozzle type, nozzle height, wave height, dwell, preheat, peak temperature, flux type, flux volume and spray parameters. Use a small trial matrix to find the window, then confirm on production samples.","State practice-based values as ranges and verify them against the paste, flux and equipment datasheets. Accept against the agreed IPC-A-610 class and the IPC J-STD-001 workmanship requirements. Rework a failed joint per IPC-7711/7721 and update the window when the failure shows a systematic nozzle or flux mismatch, so the documentation lets another engineer repeat the choice."],"blocks":[{"t":"table","caption":"Wave vs selective: where each process wins","head":["Criterion","Wave","Selective"],"rows":[["Throughput, high-mix low-volume","Poor: whole board committed to the line","Good: only targeted joints"],["Heat-sensitive neighbours","Whole board preheated anyway","Localized heat input, pad-level control"],["Setup and tooling","Wave fixture + profile tuning","Program + nozzle selection"],["Nitrogen use","Often required for wetting","Commonly used at the nozzle"],["Solder consumption","Full pot, ongoing oxidation loss","Micro-jet, low consumption"]]}]}],"calc":{"task":"A mixed assembly has a 40-pin through-hole connector, a 0.65 mm pitch surface-mount QFP 8 mm away, and an electrolytic capacitor 12 mm away. The connector pins are 1.0 mm diameter and the board is 1.6 mm thick. Select a nozzle and flux strategy, then estimate cycle time and thermal margin.","solution":"Define the joint group as the 40 through-hole pins, because they share access, thermal mass, and flux requirements. Select a small mini-wave nozzle with an orifice of 1.5-2.5 mm, a wave height of 0.5-1.0 mm, and a standoff of 0.5-1.5 mm, because the joint is accessible from below and the nearby surface-mount QFP is 8 mm away. Select low-solids liquid flux delivered by controlled spray at 15-30 uL per joint group, because the pins are plated and the nearby joints do not require high-activity flux. Set preheat to 90-120 °C board surface temperature, peak solder temperature to 245-255 C, and dwell to 2-4 s, then verify against the solder alloy liquidus and the component datasheet. Measure the capacitor body temperature during profiling; if the datasheet limit is 105 °C and the measured value is 85-95 C, the margin is 10-20 C. Estimate cycle time as flux application 3 s, preheat 20 s, solder dwell 3 s, cooling 10 s, and nozzle path 15 s, giving 51 s per board. Defend the choice by showing that the mini-wave nozzle reaches the 1.0 mm pins without flooding the 0.65 mm pitch QFP, the flux volume is low enough to limit migration, and the thermal margin protects the capacitor. Confirm acceptance against IPC-A-610 and rework any defective joint according to IPC-7711/7721."},"defect":{"symptom":"After selective soldering, a through-hole connector shows intermittent opens and dull, grainy joints, while nearby surface-mount joints look acceptable.","cause":"The preheat was too low for the connector thermal mass, and the dwell was too short, so the hole wall and pin did not reach a stable wetting temperature; flux may have been insufficient or poorly distributed.","action":"Re-profile the joint group with a higher preheat and longer dwell, verify board surface temperature at the connector, confirm flux volume and nozzle standoff against the equipment datasheet, and rework affected joints according to IPC-7711/7721. Re-inspect against IPC-A-610."},"takeaways":["Choose selective soldering from joint geometry and neighbour clearance, not from machine availability.","Take the smallest nozzle that wets completely, because thermal exposure to the neighbour is the constraint being managed.","Profile the nearest sensitive component as well as the joint, since the neighbour sets the ceiling."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Selecting a dip nozzle for the whole group because it wets quickly","instead":"Check neighbour temperature first, since the extra heat is what damages it."},{"mistake":"Raising flux activity without checking residue","instead":"Verify the residue class and cleaning plan, or corrosion risk replaces the wetting problem."},{"mistake":"Shortening dwell to meet takt time","instead":"Confirm fill and wetting on a cross-section before accepting the faster cycle."}],"terms":[{"term":"Joint group","def":"A set of joints processed with one nozzle path, flux application and thermal profile."},{"term":"Mini-wave","def":"A small localised solder wave formed by a nozzle, used for selective joints."},{"term":"Standoff","def":"The gap between the component body and the board surface."},{"term":"Flux migration","def":"Flux travelling away from the intended joint onto neighbouring pads or components."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m09l07"],"skills":["Lesson m09l07: Wave process control: bath contamination, tin consumption, records, first article"]}},{"lessonId":"m10l02","moduleId":"m10","stageId":"selective","title":"Nozzle strategy: size and shape, flux-on-the-fly, dwell per joint, multi-pass","goals":[{"text":"Choose nozzle and flux strategy for a joint group and defend the choice against the joint geometry.","competencies":["K7"]},{"text":"Build a selective program — path, order, dwell per joint, passes — for a given board.","competencies":["K7"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-014"],"quiz":["q-m10l02-01","q-m10l02-02","q-m10l02-03","q-m10l02-04","q-m10l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m10l02.webp","alt":"A set of interchangeable nozzles with a joint beside each, a flux-on-the-fly applicator, and a multi-pass sequence","caption":"Nozzle strategy: size and shape, flux-on-the-fly, dwell per joint and multi-pass"}]},{"h":"Start from joint geometry","p":["Nozzle strategy starts with the joint, not with a favourite nozzle.","On a through-hole lead the opening must be large enough to wet the lead and pad without dragging adjacent joints and small enough to keep the meniscus stable. For common leads of 0.5 mm to 1.2 mm, an inside diameter of 0.8 mm to 2.0 mm is a practical starting range, verified against your equipment and the adjacent pad spacing.","On a surface-mount pad the nozzle footprint controls heat transfer and flux delivery, so the same joint fails when the nozzle edge lands on a neighbouring pad or misses the thermal mass."]},{"h":"Shape controls heat and wetting","p":["Shape changes thermal and wetting behaviour. A conical nozzle concentrates heat on a small joint, which suits isolated pins and small surface-mount pads.","A flat or annular nozzle spreads heat over a larger area, which helps large pads, ground planes and multi-pin connectors where the joint must fill from the pad edge inward. A slot nozzle presents a narrow solder edge along a joint line, which suits row connectors, shield cans and long joints.","Match the shape to the joint outline, then check clearance to nearby components and pads."]},{"h":"Flux-on-the-fly variables","p":["Flux-on-the-fly applies flux immediately before the wave or nozzle contact, so it is still active when the joint reaches wetting temperature. The variables are volume, pattern, viscosity and solids content.","A practical starting density is 0.05 mg/mm^2 to 0.25 mg/mm^2 on the joint area, or a joint volume of 0.5 uL to 3.0 uL for many selective nozzles, verified against the flux datasheet and the residue requirement. Too little flux gives poor wetting and pinholes; too much leaves residue, throws solder balls or destabilises the meniscus.","Note the basis before comparing numbers: this is flux per unit of joint area for selective application, while the Module 09 figures are flux solids per unit of board bottom area, so the two must never be compared directly."]},{"h":"Dwell per joint","p":["Dwell is the time a joint spends under the wave or nozzle. It must be long enough to heat the joint, activate flux and let solder flow, and short enough to limit intermetallic growth, component damage and flux burn-off.","For many through-hole joints 0.5 s to 3.0 s is a reasonable starting range, and small surface-mount joints may need only 0.2 s to 1.5 s. Large thermal masses such as connectors with ground tabs need longer dwell or a preheat pass. Verify actual joint temperature with thermocouples rather than relying on bath temperature alone."]},{"h":"Multi-pass logic","p":["Multi-pass programs separate functions that would compete in a single pass: a preheat pass, a flux pass and a solder pass, or a wetting pass followed by a fill pass.","For high thermal mass joints, a preheat pass of 100 °C to 160 °C reduces thermal shock and shortens the solder dwell. Lead-free bath temperatures commonly sit around 250 °C to 260 C, verified against the alloy datasheet and the equipment.","Multi-pass improves fill on large pads, and every pass adds cycle time and thermal exposure, so use the fewest passes that still meet the joint."]},{"h":"Building the selective program","p":["Building the program means translating the joint list into path, order, dwell and passes.","Group joints by nozzle type and flux pattern first, then order the path to keep the meniscus stable and avoid dragging solder across unsoldered joints. A practical order runs from low thermal mass joints to medium and high, which stabilises the process, though board layout and component mass can reverse it — check with test coupons.","Keep the path away from tall components, avoid sharp direction changes over fresh joints, and drive each group from its own dwell table."]},{"h":"Defending the choice","p":["Defend the choice against the joint geometry by giving the physical reason behind every parameter.","State lead diameter, pad area, component mass, access and adjacent spacing. Then show that the nozzle size and shape cover the joint without bridging, that flux reaches the joint interface, that dwell heats the joint into the wetting range, and that the passes manage thermal mass.","When a joint fails, change one variable — nozzle size, flux volume, dwell or pass order — and record the result against your visual acceptance criteria."]}],"calc":{"task":"A board has 12 through-hole connector pins with 0.8 mm lead diameter and 1.6 mm pad diameter, 4 surface-mount power pads of 4 mm by 4 mm, and 1 ground pad of 8 mm by 8 mm. Choose nozzle size and shape, flux strategy, dwell per joint, and passes for a selective soldering program. Assume lead-free solder and verify against equipment datasheet.","solution":"Group the joints into 12 through-hole pins, 4 surface-mount power pads, and 1 ground pad. For the through-hole pins, choose a conical nozzle with inside diameter 1.2 mm to 1.6 mm, giving clearance around the 0.8 mm lead while avoiding adjacent 1.6 mm pads. For the 4 mm by 4 mm power pads, choose a flat or annular nozzle with footprint 4.5 mm to 6.0 mm to cover the pad edge and heat the copper. For the 8 mm by 8 mm ground pad, choose a slot or large annular nozzle with footprint 8.5 mm to 10.0 mm, or use a 2-pass strategy if the nozzle cannot cover the pad without adjacent risk. Use flux-on-the-fly with 0.10 mg/mm^2 to 0.20 mg/mm^2 on the joint area (a per-joint-area basis, not the flux-solids-per-board-area basis used for wave soldering in Module 09); for the ground pad, aim near 0.20 mg/mm^2 if residue allows. Set dwell: through-hole pins 1.2 s to 1.8 s; power pads 0.8 s to 1.5 s; ground pad 2.0 s to 3.0 s. Use 2 passes for the board: pass 1 preheat at 120 °C to 140 °C over high thermal mass joints, pass 2 solder at 255 °C to 260 C. If the ground pad shows incomplete fill, add another pass with flux-only or lower dwell solder, then recheck."},"defect":{"symptom":"A 4 mm by 4 mm surface-mount power pad shows a dull, cratered fillet and lifted pad edge after selective soldering.","cause":"The nozzle footprint was too small, dwell was near 0.5 s, and flux density was near 0.05 mg/mm^2, so the pad did not reach wetting temperature and flux burned off before solder flowed.","action":"Increase nozzle footprint to 4.5 mm to 6.0 mm, raise flux density to 0.10 mg/mm^2 to 0.20 mg/mm^2, add a preheat pass at 120 °C to 140 C, and increase dwell to 0.8 s to 1.5 s; verify with thermocouples and visual acceptance criteria."},"takeaways":["Size the nozzle from the lead and the adjacent pad spacing, because both the clearance and the meniscus depend on it.","State flux figures on the joint-area basis, since the wave numbers in Module 09 use board area and cannot be compared.","Add passes only for thermal mass, because each one costs cycle time and heat."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Choosing a nozzle by the joint outline alone","instead":"Check adjacent pad spacing and component clearance, or the nozzle drags solder."},{"mistake":"Reusing a wave flux density on a selective nozzle","instead":"Convert to the joint-area basis first, since the two are not interchangeable."},{"mistake":"Extending dwell on every joint to fix one large pad","instead":"Add a preheat pass instead, because dwell loads the neighbours as well."}],"terms":[{"term":"Flux-on-the-fly","def":"Flux applied immediately before solder contact so it stays active at wetting temperature."},{"term":"Meniscus","def":"The curved solder surface that forms at the nozzle and the joint."},{"term":"Slot nozzle","def":"A nozzle with a narrow linear opening, used for rows and long joints."},{"term":"Thermal mass","def":"The heat capacity of the joint and its surrounding copper and component."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m10l01"],"skills":["Lesson m10l01: Where selective beats wave: mixed assembly, heat-sensitive neighbours, throughput"]}},{"lessonId":"m10l03","moduleId":"m10","stageId":"selective","title":"Thermal budget of the neighbours: heat spread, monitoring, sensitive parts","goals":[{"text":"Estimate the heat exposure of neighbouring parts and specify the mitigation and the verification.","competencies":["K7","K5"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-008","src-014"],"quiz":["q-m10l03-01","q-m10l03-02","q-m10l03-03","q-m10l03-04","q-m10l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m10l03.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: selective nozzle heating one joint with heat spreading through copper planes to neighbours, conduction, convection and substrate paths, a board heat map, sensitive part icons, a thermocouple-on-capacitor photo and mitigation list","caption":"Thermal budget of the neighbours: heat paths, sensitive parts, monitoring and mitigation"}]},{"h":"Thermal budget as a shared resource","p":["The thermal budget is more than the target joint profile: it is the cumulative heat reaching nearby parts by conduction, convection and radiation.","In reflow, wave and hand soldering, the board, copper planes, leads, fixtures and air all carry heat. A neighbour can pass its datasheet limit while the target joint sits comfortably inside specification."]},{"h":"Dominant heat-transfer paths","p":["Conduction through copper planes and component leads usually dominates close to the heat source. Convection from oven air or wave flux vapour sets the background temperature. Radiation matters less at soldering temperatures, though hot fixtures or a solder pot can contribute.","The path with the lowest thermal resistance controls the peak temperature the neighbour sees, so that is the path to interrupt."]},{"h":"What raises neighbour exposure","p":["Large copper areas, thick board material, high-conductivity substrates, dense placement and low standoff carry heat further.","High paste volume, long time above liquidus, high peak and slow cooling extend the exposure in time. A fixture touching the board either sinks heat or traps it, depending on material and contact area."]},{"h":"Sensitive neighbours","p":["Parts that need watching include electrolytic capacitors, plastic connectors, LEDs, MEMS devices, sensors, adhesive-bonded components and anything with a moisture sensitivity level.","Their limits are usually a maximum body temperature, a maximum time at temperature, a moisture sensitivity class and a limit on mechanical deformation. Set the allowable budget from the component datasheet and the assembly process specification."]},{"h":"Estimation approach","p":["Early in layout, estimate neighbour exposure from the heat source temperature and an attenuation factor based on distance, copper connectivity and local thermal mass.","For production, replace the estimate with thermocouple profiling at the worst-case location. The estimate screens; the profile is the evidence."]},{"h":"Mitigation","p":["Mitigate at layout, process or fixture level.","Layout options are more spacing, thermal relief, less copper connectivity and moving sensitive parts away from heat sources. Process options are a lower peak, a shorter time above liquidus, selective soldering, less paste and an adjusted conveyor speed. Fixtures and shields cut radiant or convective heat, and heat sinks pull heat away."]},{"h":"Verification","p":["Verify with a thermal profile recorded at the target joint and at the most exposed neighbour, plus functional test after exposure and inspection for deformation, discolouration or solder defects.","Record the profile, the thermocouple locations and the acceptance criteria. Where a neighbour sits near its limit, repeat the profile after any change to paste, oven setup, fixture or component supplier."]}],"calc":{"task":"A wave-soldering line has a solder pot temperature of 250 °C (typical 245-260 °C; verify against equipment datasheet). The board top surface near the wave is 100 °C (typical 80-120 °C; verify against profile). An electrolytic capacitor is 8 mm from the wave contact and has a maximum body temperature of 105 °C. Estimate the capacitor body temperature using an attenuation length of 4 mm (typical 3-6 mm; verify against board and fixture trials).","solution":"Step A: Calculate the temperature difference between the pot and the board top: 250 °C - 100 °C = 150 °C. Step B: Apply exponential attenuation: rise = 150 °C × exp(-8 mm / 4 mm). Step C: Evaluate: exp(-2) ≈ 0.14, so rise ≈ 21 °C. Step D: Estimated body temperature = 100 °C + 21 °C = 121 °C. Step E: Compare with 105 °C; the estimate exceeds the limit. Mitigation: move the capacitor away from the wave contact, add a thermal shield, or use selective soldering. Verification: attach a thermocouple to the capacitor body, run a wave-soldering profile, and confirm the peak body temperature remains below 105 °C and the part passes functional test."},"defect":{"symptom":"After wave soldering, an electrolytic capacitor near the wave contact shows bulging, electrolyte leakage, or reduced capacitance.","cause":"Heat spread from the wave contact raised the capacitor body above its datasheet limit because the part was too close to the heat source and the profile was not verified at the capacitor.","action":"Stop the line, attach a thermocouple to the capacitor body, rerun the wave-soldering profile, increase spacing or add a thermal shield or selective soldering, then verify the peak body temperature and functional performance."},"takeaways":["Place thermocouples on the neighbour as well as the joint, because the joint can pass while the neighbour fails.","Interrupt the lowest-resistance path, since that is what sets the neighbour's peak rather than distance alone.","Re-profile after any fixture, paste or supplier change, because the assumption that produced the old margin has moved."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Watching the pot temperature as a proxy for neighbour temperature","instead":"Measure the body, since attenuation and thermal mass change the number entirely."},{"mistake":"Relying on a layout estimate for release","instead":"Profile the production board, because the estimate only screens."},{"mistake":"Accepting a neighbour close to its limit","instead":"Keep margin and repeat the profile after any process change."}],"terms":[{"term":"Attenuation length","def":"The distance over which the temperature rise from a source falls by a factor of e."},{"term":"Thermal resistance","def":"How strongly a path resists heat flow; the lowest-resistance path carries the most heat."},{"term":"Moisture sensitivity level","def":"The J-STD-020 class that states how long a package may sit in factory ambient before reflow; it does not raise or lower the peak temperature the package tolerates."},{"term":"Thermal shield","def":"A physical barrier that reduces radiant or convective heat reaching a part."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m10l02"],"skills":["Lesson m10l02: Nozzle strategy: size and shape, flux-on-the-fly, dwell per joint, multi-pass"]}},{"lessonId":"m10l04","moduleId":"m10","stageId":"selective","title":"Programming a run: path, order, fixtures, air knife, cycle time","goals":[{"text":"Build a selective program — path, order, dwell per joint, passes — for a given board.","competencies":["K7"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-009","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m10l04-01","q-m10l04-02","q-m10l04-03","q-m10l04-04","q-m10l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m10l04.webp","alt":"A machine screen showing a soldering path with numbered joints, a fixture, an air knife and a cycle-time breakdown","caption":"Programming a run: path, order, fixtures, air knife and cycle time"}]},{"h":"What a selective program controls","p":["A selective soldering program is a machine-readable recipe for one board, not a list of coordinates.","It defines where flux lands, how long the board preheats, how the air knife behaves, which joints go first, how the nozzle moves, how long each joint dwells, how many passes run, and how the board cools before handling.","The inputs are board geometry, component list, pad and hole dimensions, copper area, component height, board thickness, fixture design, flux type, alloy, nozzle temperature and machine limits. The output is a repeatable cycle that wets properly without excess heat, flux carryover, bridging or mechanical disturbance.","Each path, dwell, pass and fixture decision should trace to a board feature or a measured thermal result, so another engineer can audit the program and reproduce it on the same equipment, fixture and material set."]},{"h":"Path planning under the board","p":["Path planning starts from the bottom side, because selective soldering normally reaches through-hole pins and bottom-side pads from below.","On a through-hole joint the path centres on the hole or pad and approaches from a direction that drags no solder across neighbouring pads. A continuous wave path works for pin rows when nozzle width, flux coverage and pad spacing allow it; isolated joints are usually safer point to point, which limits solder dragged into nearby gaps. Bottom-side surface-mount pads follow the pad geometry and avoid crossing between pads unless the method is qualified.","Keep lead-in and lead-out short, predictable and outside the final wetted area. Account for shadowing from tall components, connectors and shielding cans, and verify nozzle-to-board, nozzle-to-component and pin-to-support clearance on the first article rather than assuming them."]},{"h":"Ordering joints for heat and flux","p":["The order of joints is a thermal decision. Group by access and thermal mass, then run from low-mass to high-mass joints while the board is still warm from preheat.","High-mass joints such as ground-plane pins and heavy connectors need more energy, and placing them too early cools the nozzle or wave; placing them too late overheats the board near sensitive parts. Avoid scheduling a high-mass joint immediately before one near a heat-sensitive component unless a cooling pause or fixture airflow is available.","Grouping also cuts travel: joints sharing a row, a connector footprint or a nozzle approach run together, and grouping must not force the nozzle across completed joints or drag solder over unprocessed pads. Use parallel fluxing or preheat only after confirming that flux activation, board temperature and wetting stay inside the qualified window. Document the reason for each group, because later troubleshooting needs to know whether a defect came from path, thermal mass or sequence."]},{"h":"Fixtures","p":["The fixture is part of the program: it sets board height, exposure, support and thermal behaviour.","A good fixture holds the board flat, exposes the bottom side, supports areas that would flex, and blocks neither nozzle, fluxer nor air knife. Insulating, low-heat-transfer materials such as PEEK or phenolic are common for temperature stability and stiffness.","Vacuum hold-down helps, and vacuum holes must not pull flux into unwanted areas or seat the board unevenly. Support pins sit under large components, connector bodies and board edges, not so close to through-hole pads that they block solder access. Pin height sets the board-to-nozzle relationship, so a change in board thickness may need a z-height change even when the coordinates stay put.","The fixture should drain flux and stop it pooling on the top side, and mixed-technology boards may need cutouts or shields against flux mist and radiant heat. Check flatness, pin wear and seating on the first article, because a small height error changes dwell contact and either starves the joint or floods it."]},{"h":"Air knife and flux control","p":["The air knife is a programmed air or nitrogen curtain that strips excess flux from the board surface and, on some machines, limits flux carryover toward the nozzle or pot. It controls how much flux reaches the joint.","Too little leaves excess flux that boils during soldering and causes blowback, voids or dross. Too much cools the joint, disturbs small components, oxidises the wetting surface or dries the flux before it activates.","Define whether the knife runs, when it fires relative to fluxing and soldering, its pressure, distance and angle. Starting ranges are 20-60 kPa, 5-15 mm and 15-45 degrees, verified against the paste, flux and equipment datasheet. Dry filtered air is common; nitrogen serves where oxidation control matters, with gas purity and flow qualified.","Check for clogging, uneven flow and leakage, because a partly blocked knife leaves a local flux imbalance that shows up as a random wetting defect. In troubleshooting, compare knife settings with defect location: defects near the flux application edge usually point to flux volume or knife timing rather than dwell."]},{"h":"Dwell and pass strategy","p":["Dwell is how long the solder source stays in contact with, or close enough to, the joint. Set it from thermal mass, pad size, hole size, lead material, flux activity and nozzle temperature.","Small pins and small bottom-side pads need less dwell than ground-plane pins or heavy connector leads. A practical starting range is 0.8-2.5 s for low-mass joints and 1.5-4.0 s for high-mass joints, with the qualified value coming from thermocouple data and cross-sections.","On high-mass joints, several short passes usually beat one long dwell, because heat spreads without overheating neighbours. Include cooling between passes when board temperature climbs too fast, and record the pass count with its reason: one pass for simple joints, two or more for high copper area, thick leads or a poor wetting history.","Never raise dwell to compensate for low preheat, thin flux coverage or a wrong nozzle height. A joint wanting excessive dwell points to path, fixture height, flux volume or the thermal profile, and those come first."]},{"h":"Cycle time, verification and prevention","p":["Cycle time sums board handling, flux application, preheat, air-knife action, solder movement, dwell, cooling and fixture exchange. Calculate it from the program, then verify it on the machine.","Optimise by cutting non-value-added travel, grouping joints logically, and using machine features only where they preserve wetting. Never shorten preheat or cooling for throughput unless the profile and acceptance criteria still hold.","Verification covers first-article inspection, thermocouples at representative joints, cross-sections on critical joints and acceptance against IPC-A-610. Rework follows IPC-7711/7721 rather than improvised extra heat cycles.","Prevention is built into the program: path geometry prevents bridging, order prevents heat damage, fixture support prevents flex, air-knife control prevents flux carryover, and dwell and pass strategy prevent cold joints. Version-control the program with board revision, fixture revision, flux lot, nozzle condition and measured cycle time, so a good run can be compared with a bad one and the difference traced to material, fixture wear, nozzle condition or settings."]}],"calc":{"task":"A board has 84 through-hole pins and 26 bottom-side surface-mount joints. Twelve of the through-hole pins are high-mass ground-plane pins. The program uses single-pass soldering for the remaining through-hole pins and surface-mount joints, and two passes for the high-mass pins. Dwell is 1.2 s for low-mass through-hole pins, 1.8 s for surface-mount joints, and 2.4 s for high-mass pins. Travel overhead is 0.35 s per pass for low-mass through-hole pins and surface-mount joints, and 0.55 s per pass for high-mass pins. Flux application takes 18 s, preheat takes 45 s, air-knife action takes 6 s, cooling takes 12 s, and board handling takes 10 s. Calculate the solder time, total cycle time, and effective joints per minute.","solution":"Step 1: Determine the number of low-mass through-hole pins. Total through-hole pins are 84. High-mass through-hole pins are 12. Low-mass through-hole pins are 84 - 12 = 72. Step 2: Calculate low-mass through-hole solder time. Each low-mass through-hole pin uses one pass. Time per pass is dwell plus travel overhead: 1.2 + 0.35 = 1.55 s. Total low-mass through-hole time is 72 * 1.55 = 111.6 s. Step 3: Calculate surface-mount solder time. Each surface-mount joint uses one pass. Time per pass is 1.8 + 0.35 = 2.15 s. Total surface-mount time is 26 * 2.15 = 55.9 s. Step 4: Calculate high-mass through-hole solder time. Each high-mass pin uses two passes. Time per pass is 2.4 + 0.55 = 2.95 s. Total high-mass time is 12 * 2 * 2.95 = 70.8 s. Step 5: Sum solder time. Solder time is 111.6 + 55.9 + 70.8 = 238.3 s. Step 6: Add non-solder cycle elements. Flux application is 18 s, preheat is 45 s, air-knife action is 6 s, cooling is 12 s, and board handling is 10 s. Non-solder time is 18 + 45 + 6 + 12 + 10 = 91 s. Step 7: Calculate total cycle time. Total cycle time is 238.3 + 91 = 329.3 s. Step 8: Calculate total joints. Total joints are 84 + 26 = 110. Step 9: Convert total cycle time to minutes. 329.3 / 60 = 5.488333... minutes. Step 10: Calculate effective joints per minute. 110 / 5.488333... = 20.04 joints per minute. Rounded for reporting, the effective rate is 20.0 joints per minute."},"defect":{"symptom":"A row of through-hole pins shows incomplete wetting on the far side of a connector, while the first pins in the row are acceptable. The defect repeats at the same location on multiple boards.","cause":"The path or order causes heat loss and flux depletion before the nozzle reaches the far end of the row. The connector body may shadow preheat or flux, the fixture may allow local board flex, or the air knife may dry the flux before the last joints are soldered. The dwell may be adequate for the first joints but insufficient for the cooler far-end joints.","action":"Measure board temperature at the first and last joints with thermocouples. Check fixture support near the connector and verify board height. Review the path direction and consider reversing the row or splitting it into two groups. Verify flux coverage and air-knife pressure, distance, and timing. If the far-end joints are cooler, increase preheat or add a short cooling pause before the row rather than only increasing dwell. Qualify the change with cross-sections and acceptance review against IPC-A-610."},"takeaways":["Trace every path, dwell and pass back to a board feature or a thermal measurement, so the program can be audited.","Keep lead-in and lead-out outside the wetted area, because that is where dragged solder bridges neighbours.","Record the pass count with its reason, since a later investigation needs to know whether the joint needed the extra heat or the extra time."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Raising dwell to fix a cold joint","instead":"Check preheat, flux coverage and nozzle height first, since dwell is the most expensive lever."},{"mistake":"Using vacuum hold-down without checking the holes","instead":"Confirm flux cannot be pulled into unwanted areas and the board seats evenly."},{"mistake":"Optimising cycle time by shortening cooling","instead":"Keep the profile and acceptance criteria intact, or the boards leave too hot to handle."}],"terms":[{"term":"Lead-in and lead-out","def":"The non-soldering travel into and away from a joint."},{"term":"Air knife","def":"A timed air or nitrogen curtain that removes excess flux from the board surface."},{"term":"Vacuum hold-down","def":"A fixture method that pulls the board flat against a support surface."},{"term":"Version control","def":"Keeping each program revision tied to the board, fixture and material set it was qualified with."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":25,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m10l03"],"skills":["Lesson m10l03: Thermal budget of the neighbours: heat spread, monitoring, sensitive parts"]}},{"lessonId":"m10l05","moduleId":"m10","stageId":"selective","title":"Selective defects and validation: cross-section, pull test, documentation","goals":[{"text":"Build a selective program — path, order, dwell per joint, passes — for a given board.","competencies":["K7"]},{"text":"Estimate the heat exposure of neighbouring parts and specify the mitigation and the verification.","competencies":["K7","K5"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-008","src-009","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m10l05-01","q-m10l05-02","q-m10l05-03","q-m10l05-04","q-m10l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m10l05.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: through-hole joint cross-section labels, pull-test fixture with force gauge, joint and neighbour thermocouples in one run, sacrificial board, a metallographic section photo and a validation record","caption":"Selective soldering validation: cross-section, pull test, same-run monitoring and documentation"}]},{"h":"Purpose of selective validation","p":["Selective soldering exists because a full wave would overheat nearby SMT or temperature-sensitive THT parts.","Validation proves two things at once: the joint is mechanically and electrically sound, and the neighbours stay inside their allowed thermal budget. Use IPC-A-610 for visual acceptance, IPC-7711/7721 for rework and repair, and IPC-7530 for thermal process control where it applies."]},{"h":"Building the selective program","p":["Start from the board layout and define each joint as a solder target.","Order the joints so heat-sensitive parts go last where possible, and so the nozzle approaches without disturbing adjacent components. Set dwell per joint from the time needed to wet pad and hole, then add a short margin for paste variability.","One pass covers simple through-hole joints; multiple passes come in only when pad, hole or paste volume needs more heat. Verify against your paste, nozzle and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Estimating heat exposure","p":["Estimate exposure from preheat, wave or jet temperature, contact time, board thickness, copper area, lead mass and standoff.","The first estimate is a measurement: record the temperature rise at the nearest sensitive part with a thermocouple or thermal probe during a dry run, then repeat with paste and a sacrificial board.","Where direct measurement is impossible, use a conservative model based on thermal mass and contact time, and check it against the equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Mitigation and verification","p":["Mitigate by reducing preheat, lowering jet temperature within paste limits, shortening dwell, increasing standoff, shielding the sensitive part, using a smaller nozzle, or moving the sensitive joint later in the sequence.","Verification covers a thermal profile at the sensitive part, visual inspection after cooling, and functional or electrical test where the part is thermally sensitive. Document the profile and the accepted program revision."]},{"h":"Cross-section validation","p":["Cross-sections answer what the eye cannot: joint geometry, hole fill, intermetallic formation and crack risk.","Prepare the sample so the section passes through pad, hole and lead, then inspect for wetting, voiding, cracks and excessive intermetallic. Accept against IPC-A-610 and repair per IPC-7711/7721. Take sections from representative boards rather than from one ideal sample."]},{"h":"Pull test validation","p":["Pull testing suits THT leads and selected SMT terminations where mechanical strength is the concern.","Fix the board, pull the lead perpendicular at a controlled rate, and record force and failure mode. Acceptance rests on the specified minimum strength or a validated process capability, not on a single pass or fail sample. Follow the applicable component and board standards, and document sample size, temperature and failure mode."]},{"h":"Documentation","p":["Documentation carries board revision, component placement, program file, nozzle, paste lot, preheat and jet settings, dwell per joint, pass count, thermal profile, cross-section results, pull-test results and any deviation.","When a defect appears, record the symptom, the suspected cause, the corrective action and the revalidation evidence, so the program stays auditable and repeatable."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 1.6 mm FR-4 board has a THT joint with a 0.8 mm lead in a 1.0 mm hole. A 0402 capacitor is 2.0 mm from the joint. The selective jet is set to 250 °C, preheat is 100 °C, and dwell is 2.5 s. Estimate the peak temperature at the capacitor and specify mitigation and verification.","solution":"Step 1: Define the thermal budget. The nearby capacitor should remain below the temperature limit specified by its datasheet. For this screening estimate, use a conservative internal target of 150 °C at the capacitor body, then verify against the component datasheet. Step 2: Estimate heat input at the joint. Use the lead surface area in the hole as the heat-transfer area. Lead circumference is pi times 0.8 mm, which is about 2.5 mm. Multiply by board thickness 1.6 mm to get about 4.0 mm². Step 3: Estimate heat flow. Use an effective heat-transfer coefficient of 500 W/m²K for the solder jet contact zone, then verify against equipment data. The temperature difference is 250 °C minus 100 °C, which is 150 K. Heat flow is 500 W/m²K times 4.0e-6 m² times 150 K, which is about 0.3 W. Step 4: Estimate energy input. Multiply 0.3 W by dwell 2.5 s to get about 0.75 J. Step 5: Estimate heat reaching the capacitor. Assume 10 percent of the joint heat reaches the capacitor through the board and copper, then verify with a thermocouple. Heat reaching the capacitor is about 0.075 J. Step 6: Estimate capacitor thermal mass. Use a mass of about 1.2 mg for a 0402 capacitor and a specific heat of about 0.8 J/gK, then verify against component data. Step 7: Estimate temperature rise. Divide 0.075 J by 0.0012 g times 0.8 J/gK to get about 78 K. Add this to preheat 100 °C to get an estimated peak of about 178 °C. Step 8: Compare with the target. The estimated 178 °C exceeds the 150 °C screening target, so mitigation is required. Step 9: Apply mitigation. Reduce dwell to 1.5 s, lower preheat if paste limits allow, add a thermal shield near the capacitor, and move the capacitor-sensitive joint later in the sequence if possible. Step 10: Recalculate with mitigation. Energy input becomes 0.3 W times 1.5 s, which is about 0.45 J. Heat reaching the capacitor becomes about 0.045 J. Temperature rise becomes about 47 K. Estimated peak becomes 100 °C plus 47 K, which is about 147 °C. Step 11: Verify. Run a thermocouple profile at the capacitor on a sacrificial board, inspect the joint visually, perform cross-section if hole fill or crack risk is uncertain, and perform pull testing on representative leads. Document the accepted program revision, thermal profile, cross-section result, and pull-test result."},"defect":{"symptom":"Adjacent 0402 capacitor shows discoloration and an open circuit after selective soldering of a nearby THT joint.","cause":"Excessive dwell and preheat allowed heat to propagate through the pad and copper plane, and shielding was not used near the temperature-sensitive part.","action":"Reduce dwell, lower preheat within paste limits, add a thermal shield, rerun the thermal profile at the capacitor, inspect the joint and capacitor, perform cross-section if needed, and update the program revision with revalidation evidence."},"takeaways":["Validate the neighbour and the joint in the same run, because selective soldering protects one by risking the other.","Measure the sensitive part on a sacrificial board before release, since a thermal estimate only screens.","Record the program revision with the profile and the section, so a later change can be traced to its evidence."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Verifying only the joint that was soldered","instead":"Profile the nearest sensitive part, which is what the process was chosen to protect."},{"mistake":"Accepting a pull test on one sample","instead":"Use a documented sample size and the failure mode, not a single pass."},{"mistake":"Releasing a program after a mitigation without re-profiling","instead":"Repeat the profile, because the mitigation changed the thermal path."}],"terms":[{"term":"Sacrificial board","def":"A production-equivalent board used for thermal or destructive testing."},{"term":"Pull test","def":"A mechanical test that measures the force needed to separate a joint."},{"term":"Thermal budget (component limit)","def":"The temperature and time a part may tolerate without damage."},{"term":"Program revision","def":"The versioned record of path, dwell, pass and process settings."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":25,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m10l04"],"skills":["Lesson m10l04: Programming a run: path, order, fixtures, air knife, cycle time"]}},{"lessonId":"m11l01","moduleId":"m11","stageId":"manual-rework","title":"The hand soldering station: tip selection, temperature setting, ESD and fume control","goals":[{"text":"Select iron, tip, temperature and technique for a joint and state the thermal budget applied.","competencies":["K8"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-002","src-012","src-016","src-035"],"quiz":["q-m11l01-01","q-m11l01-02","q-m11l01-03","q-m11l01-04","q-m11l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"Station anatomy and the control loop","p":["A hand soldering station is a closed loop: controller, heater, tip, sensor and ground path.","The setpoint is the tip temperature at the heater, not the joint temperature. A good station recovers quickly when a cold joint draws heat, so choose a station whose tip holds a stable temperature while it touches the pad, and confirm the actual tip temperature with a calibrated thermocouple or probe against the equipment datasheet.","IPC J-STD-001 expects workmanship that does not damage the base material, which makes a controlled, clean and grounded station part of the requirement rather than a convenience."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","caption":"Hand-soldering station: closed-loop temperature, ESD ground, fume extraction","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 900 300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Hand soldering station systems'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace}</style><text x='60' y='28' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14'>THE STATION IS THREE SYSTEMS</text><rect x='60' y='70' width='170' height='50' rx='14' fill='rgba(15,23,34,.78)' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><text x='145' y='100' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle'>CONTROLLER</text><line x1='105' y1='120' x2='105' y2='180' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M105,186 l-5,-9 h10 z' fill='#53d1cc'/><line x1='185' y1='180' x2='185' y2='120' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M185,114 l-5,9 h10 z' fill='#53d1cc'/><rect x='60' y='186' width='170' height='50' rx='14' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.08)' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1'/><text x='145' y='216' fill='#53d1cc' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle'>HEATER + TIP</text><text x='215' y='156' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12'>SENSOR</text><text x='60' y='266' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12'>CLOSED LOOP: MEASURE AT TIP</text><rect x='370' y='70' width='170' height='50' rx='14' fill='rgba(15,23,34,.78)' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><text x='455' y='100' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle'>WRIST STRAP</text><line x1='455' y1='120' x2='455' y2='168' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2'/><line x1='425' y1='172' x2='485' y2='172' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2'/><line x1='433' y1='182' x2='477' y2='182' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2'/><line x1='441' y1='192' x2='469' y2='192' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2'/><text x='455' y='220' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>ESD GROUND PATH</text><text x='370' y='266' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12'>ONE OPERATOR, ONE GROUND</text><rect x='680' y='70' width='170' height='50' rx='14' fill='rgba(15,23,34,.78)' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><text x='765' y='100' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle'>FUME ARRESTOR</text><path d='M700,190 q30,-40 60,-10' fill='none' stroke='#ff9800' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M740,200 q30,-45 60,-15' fill='none' stroke='#ff9800' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M700,190 l-2,10 9,-4 z' fill='#ff9800'/><path d='M740,200 l-2,10 9,-4 z' fill='#ff9800'/><text x='765' y='232' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>CAPTURE AT SOURCE</text><text x='680' y='266' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12'>FLUX FUME IS THE EXPOSURE</text></svg>"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m11l01.webp","alt":"A closed-loop soldering station with controller, heater, tip and sensor, a set of interchangeable tip shapes, a grounded mat and a fume extraction arm","caption":"The hand soldering station: tip selection, temperature setting, ESD and fume control"}]},{"h":"Tip selection","p":["The tip is the heat-transfer interface, so geometry follows the joint.","Small conical or chisel tips suit chip components, wider chisel or hoof tips suit through-hole leads and ground planes, and specialty shapes cover fine-pitch or BGA rework. Low thermal mass recovers faster on small joints; higher thermal mass holds temperature on large pads.","The contact face must be clean, tinned and free of oxidation. Check tip compatibility and power rating against the iron datasheet, because an undersized tip drives long dwell while an oversized one heats neighbouring parts."]},{"h":"Temperature setting and alloy","p":["Setpoint selection starts with the alloy, the flux and the thermal mass.","Lead-free SAC alloys generally need higher process temperatures than eutectic tin-lead, and the exact range depends on paste, iron and board design, so verify against your paste and equipment datasheet.","A higher setpoint shortens contact time and can also oxidise flux, damage laminate or lift pads. A lower one produces cold joints when the tip cannot recover. Use the lowest temperature that wets and forms a fillet inside an acceptable cycle time."]},{"h":"Technique and thermal budget","p":["Good technique is repeatable contact, not scraping.","Clean the tip, add a little fresh solder, touch the tip to pad and terminal, feed solder into the joint, remove the tip, and hold the part until the solder solidifies.","The thermal budget combines tip temperature, contact time, heat-transfer area and the number of heat cycles. Record setpoint, tip type, contact time and any preheat, and validate the budget on scrap or test coupons, because IPC J-STD-001 requires a sound wetted joint without heat damage."]},{"h":"ESD control at the bench","p":["Hand soldering means handling bare boards and components, so the station belongs to an ESD control program.","ANSI/ESD S20.20 and IEC 61340-5-1 cover grounded equipment, personnel grounding, conductive or dissipative work surfaces, and verification. Bond the iron tip and body to a common ground point, and ground the operator where the program requires it.","Verify the iron ground path and the wrist strap or footwear system with the tester your ESD program specifies, and replace damaged cords, mats and straps."]},{"h":"Fume control","p":["Solder fume carries flux decomposition products and particulate, so local exhaust is the practical control.","Place the extraction inlet close to the joint without blocking the operator's view, and hold the capture velocity the ventilation design calls for. Check airflow, filter condition and face velocity against the equipment datasheet and local occupational exposure limits.","On a shared hood, keep the inlet near the source and never between the operator's breathing zone and the joint."]},{"h":"Inspection and process control","p":["After soldering, inspect wetting, fillet shape, solder volume, lifted pads, charred laminate, solder balls and disturbed components.","IPC-A-610 gives the visual acceptance criteria and IPC J-STD-001 covers workmanship and repair practice. Use a magnifier or microscope sized to the component.","When a joint fails, decide whether heat, contamination, insufficient solder or mechanical damage caused it, then correct the process rather than reworking repeatedly without a root cause."],"blocks":[{"t":"table","caption":"Iron setpoint guidance — follow tip maker and alloy TDS","head":["Joint type","Tip mass and shape","Setpoint approach"],"rows":[["Small SMD rework","Fine conical or small hoof","Lower end of alloy range"],["Through-hole with ground plane","Large chisel or wedge","Higher end: heat the joint, not the part"],["Lead-free alloy","Add thermal margin vs eutectic","Per alloy TDS"],["Long joints, connectors","Pre-tin, keep iron moving","Time over temperature: heat mass first"]]}]}],"calc":{"task":"A small chip resistor must be hand soldered to a thin copper pad on a multilayer board using no-clean paste. Select iron, tip, temperature, technique, and state the thermal budget applied.","solution":"Step 1: Check the paste and component datasheets for maximum temperature and recommended process window; verify against your paste/equipment datasheet. Step 2: Choose a temperature-controlled, grounded, ESD-safe iron rated for rework, typically 40-60 W. Step 3: Choose a small chisel or conical tip with a 0.5-1.0 mm contact face, clean and tinned. Step 4: Set the iron to 340-360°C for lead-free SAC-type solder, then verify actual tip temperature with a probe. Step 5: Pre-tin the pad, place the resistor, touch the tip to the pad and terminal, feed a small amount of solder, remove the tip, and hold the part until solidified. Step 6: Apply a thermal budget of about 350°C tip temperature, 1.5-3.0 s tip contact per joint, and a target pad/component temperature of about 220-250°C for 1-2 s. Step 7: Estimate heat input as 0.1-0.3 J for the small joint, recognizing that board copper and adjacent pads can increase the required energy. Step 8: Inspect the joint for wetting, fillet, lifted pad, and laminate damage, using IPC-A-610 and IPC J-STD-001 as the workmanship references."},"defect":{"symptom":"A pad lifts or the laminate discolors after a few seconds of soldering.","cause":"The thermal budget exceeded the board material limit because the setpoint was too high, the tip was too small, the tip was oxidized, or the operator held the iron too long while trying to wet the joint.","action":"Reduce the setpoint within the paste and iron datasheet range, use a larger or better-tinned tip to improve heat transfer, shorten contact time, pre-tin the pad, and inspect the board with IPC-A-610. If laminate damage is visible, reject or rework according to IPC J-STD-001 and record the corrected thermal budget."},"takeaways":["Measure the tip temperature with a calibrated probe, because the setpoint is the heater temperature and not the joint temperature.","Pick the tip by joint geometry and thermal mass, since an undersized tip forces long dwell and an oversized one heats the neighbours.","Record the thermal budget with the setpoint, tip and contact time, so a rework can be reproduced and audited."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Setting the iron as hot as it goes to shorten contact","instead":"Use the lowest temperature that wets, because excess heat lifts pads and chars laminate."},{"mistake":"Reworking a failed joint repeatedly without a cause","instead":"Identify whether heat, contamination or solder volume caused the failure first."},{"mistake":"Checking ESD grounding once at installation","instead":"Verify the iron and the operator grounding at the interval the program defines."}],"terms":[{"term":"Thermal budget (rework)","def":"The combination of tip temperature, contact time and heat cycles a joint receives."},{"term":"Wetting","def":"The spreading of molten solder into a continuous metallurgical bond with the pad and lead."},{"term":"Capture velocity","def":"The air speed at the extraction inlet needed to pull fume away from the joint."},{"term":"Hoof tip","def":"A curved tip shape that carries a solder bead to a joint, useful on through-hole and large pads."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m06l07","m02l06"],"skills":["Lesson m06l07: Profile validation, documentation and change control","Lesson m02l06: Case: expired floor life found mid-lot"]}},{"lessonId":"m11l02","moduleId":"m11","stageId":"manual-rework","title":"Technique: contact order, dwell, feed, lead-free discipline, technique defects","goals":[{"text":"Select iron, tip, temperature and technique for a joint and state the thermal budget applied.","competencies":["K8"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-002"],"quiz":["q-m11l02-01","q-m11l02-02","q-m11l02-03","q-m11l02-04","q-m11l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m11l02.webp","alt":"A sequence of four panels showing tip contact on pad and lead, dwell, solder feed and removal, with a cold-joint warning callout","caption":"Technique: contact order, dwell, feed, lead-free discipline and technique defects"}]},{"h":"Contact order sets the thermal path","p":["Manual rework works when heat reaches the joint before solder does.","Preheat the board where thermal mass is high, place the tip so it touches lead and pad together, feed solder to the joint, remove the solder, and then remove the tip. That order leaves the interface hot when the solder arrives and keeps cold joints out.","Withdraw the tip too early and the joint solidifies while the operator is still feeding, which gives a disturbed or incomplete fillet. Feed before the interface is hot and the solder balls on the tip or sits on the pad without wetting.","Use thermocouple data on a thick board, a large copper area or a heat-sensitive component, and confirm that the technique suits the joint geometry and the board material."]},{"h":"Dwell is a process window","p":["Dwell is the time the tip and solder stay in thermal contact with the joint. It must be long enough for lead, pad and solder to reach a wetting condition and short enough to protect board, component and flux.","Dwell follows tip geometry, iron recovery, board thickness, copper area and preheat. A joint on a large copper plane or a long lead needs more heat than a small isolated pad.","Find the minimum dwell that wets on a sacrificial joint, then check it against your paste and equipment datasheet. When wetting is poor, change tip, preheat or technique before extending dwell, because longer contact is not a substitute for better thermal contact and it damages neighbours, darkens flux and weakens the board interface."]},{"h":"Feed controls volume, flux and bridging","p":["Feed solder wire to the joint, never melt it on the tip.","Wire diameter and feed length should match the joint size, so the operator can place enough solder for a fillet without excess. Flux-cored wire cleans the interface, and too much feed traps flux, creates voids or bridges adjacent pads.","Use short touches and let the joint draw solder into the fillet, watching the flow rather than counting on a remembered amount. A small joint wants a smaller diameter for control; a large joint can take a larger one, still fed to the joint and never flooded over the area. Check diameter, flux activity and technique against your paste and equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Lead-free discipline","p":["SAC-type lead-free solder needs higher wetting temperatures and more active flux than tin-lead, and it forgives less: contamination, mixed alloys and a poor tip condition all show up faster.","Dedicated tips and clean work areas limit cross-contamination. Choose setpoints and dwell from the solder or paste datasheet, then confirm on the actual board.","Lead-free discipline also controls the thermal history of the joint, because repeated rework changes wetting behaviour and intermetallic formation. Never share a tip between tin-lead and lead-free work unless the process is qualified and the tip cleaned, and document the alloy and flux system for each rework task."]},{"h":"Iron and tip must match the joint","p":["The iron has to recover temperature during contact, and the tip has to transfer heat without blocking inspection or forcing the operator to press.","A fine tip suits small SMD joints, and a chisel or hoof tip with real contact area suits through-hole joints. Match the tip face to the lead and pad geometry.","An undersized tip cools during contact and pushes the operator into a longer dwell; an oversized one damages nearby parts and is hard to control. Check tip condition at the start of every shift, because oxidation, wear and poor plating cut heat transfer and wetting, and confirm with calibration records that the displayed temperature matches the measured one."]},{"h":"Technique defects are thermal or mechanical","p":["Insufficient wetting usually comes from a low interface temperature, short dwell, an oxidised tip or contaminated surfaces.","Disturbed joints happen when the component moves while the solder solidifies. Bridging and excess solder come from overfeeding or poor tip withdrawal. Lifted pads follow excessive dwell, high preheat or mechanical prying. Voids come from trapped flux, moisture or heating fast enough to drive volatiles through the joint, and burned flux leaves residue that hampers inspection and may reduce corrosion resistance.","Inspect against the IPC acceptance standard and correct the process parameter that caused the defect rather than touching up repeatedly."]},{"h":"State the thermal budget before rework","p":["A thermal budget is the controlled combination of tip setpoint, board preheat, contact time, number of contacts, tip geometry and alloy.","Write it down for each rework task so another engineer can reproduce the result. Measure board temperature with a thermocouple where the joint is thermally significant, and record the values actually used rather than the nominal setpoint.","Align the budget with the IPC acceptance standard and the solder, paste and equipment datasheets. State whether the joint was repaired with an initial contact or whether more contacts were allowed, and define the maximum board temperature the component tolerates. That makes the technique auditable and stops technique defects becoming routine."]}],"calc":{"task":"A manual rework operator must repair a through-hole joint on a 1.6 mm glass-epoxy board with a 0.8 mm lead and a 35 µm copper annular ring. The board is preheated to 100 °C. The solder is a SAC-type lead-free solder. Select iron, tip, temperature, and technique, and state the thermal budget applied.","solution":"Choose a temperature-controlled iron with enough thermal recovery for a through-hole joint; verify against the iron datasheet. Choose a chisel or hoof tip whose contact face is slightly wider than the lead; for a 0.8 mm lead, use a 1.0-1.5 mm tip face. Set tip temperature to 330-350 °C for SAC-type solder on a preheated board; verify against the solder and iron datasheet. Use contact order: place the tip on lead and pad, feed solder to the joint, remove solder, then remove tip. Apply dwell of 1.5-2.5 s per contact; if the joint does not wet, stop and reassess rather than extending dwell. Feed 0.5-1.0 mm solder wire in short touches; the amount should form a concave fillet without bridging. State thermal budget: tip setpoint 330-350 °C, board preheat 100 °C, contact time 1.5-2.5 s, initial contact; thermal budget index = (tip setpoint - board preheat) × contact time = 230-250 °C × 1.5-2.5 s = 345-625 °C·s per contact. Record actual tip temperature, contact time, preheat, tip geometry, solder alloy, flux, and board temperature; inspect against the IPC acceptance standard."},"defect":{"symptom":"A reworked through-hole joint shows a dull, grainy surface and poor wetting at the pad.","cause":"The interface did not reach wetting temperature because dwell was too short, tip recovery was insufficient, or the tip was oxidized.","action":"Recheck iron calibration and tip condition, increase preheat or use a higher thermal mass tip, repeat with correct contact order, and verify against your paste/equipment datasheet."},"takeaways":["Heat the joint before feeding solder, because the interface temperature decides whether the solder wets or balls.","Find the minimum dwell that wets and stop there, since extra contact damages the board without improving the fillet.","Write the thermal budget down, so the next engineer reproduces the joint instead of guessing at it."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Melting solder on the tip to carry it to the joint","instead":"Feed to the joint, because solder on the tip arrives without a wetted interface."},{"mistake":"Extending dwell when a joint will not wet","instead":"Change tip, preheat or technique first, since dwell is already the expensive variable."},{"mistake":"Recording only the iron setpoint in the budget log","instead":"Record measured tip and board temperature, because the display can drift from reality."}],"terms":[{"term":"Disturbed joint","def":"A joint whose component moved while the solder was solidifying, leaving a rough surface."},{"term":"Thermal budget (rework)","def":"The written combination of setpoint, preheat, contact time and alloy allowed for a rework."},{"term":"Flux-cored wire","def":"Solder wire with a flux core that cleans the interface as it melts."},{"term":"Interface temperature","def":"The temperature at the boundary between solder and the surfaces being joined."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m11l01"],"skills":["Lesson m11l01: The hand soldering station: tip selection, temperature setting, ESD and fume control"]}},{"lessonId":"m11l03","moduleId":"m11","stageId":"manual-rework","title":"Rework: hot air, preheat, paste-in-pad, BGA/QFN removal and replacement","goals":[{"text":"Specify or perform a rework operation within the allowed cycles and thermal budget and record what was applied.","competencies":["K8"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-002","src-003","src-008","src-009","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m11l03-01","q-m11l03-02","q-m11l03-03","q-m11l03-04","q-m11l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m11l03.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: hot-air station with bottom preheater and joint thermocouple, vacuum removal of a BGA, wick clean and paste-in-pad through a mini-stencil, split-vision alignment, a rework photo and a cycle budget tally","caption":"Hot-air rework of BGA and QFN: setup, removal, paste-in-pad, replacement and cycle budget"}]},{"h":"Rework scope and thermal budget","p":["Rework is a controlled thermal operation, not a repair shortcut.","Before starting, define the thermal budget: preheat temperature, peak temperature measured at the joint, time above liquidus, dwell, cooling behaviour, and the number of thermal cycles the board and component have already seen.","The budget must suit the laminate, the surface finish, the moisture sensitivity level of the component and the paste or solder-ball material. Take the assembly document, the board supplier guidance, the component datasheet and the paste or ball datasheet as the controlling references.","Where the allowed cycle count is not stated, treat any additional cycle as a deviation that needs engineering approval."]},{"h":"Preheat and hot-air setup","p":["Preheat softens the thermal shock and lets the joint reach reflow temperature without an extreme air temperature.","Measure board temperature near the component rather than trusting the air setting. A workable practice preheats the board to a measured temperature that supports paste activation and ball collapse while the component body stays inside its rated limit, verified against the paste, component and equipment datasheets.","Use a nozzle that covers the footprint without blasting neighbours. On a large board or a thermally massive part, raise preheat or fit a larger nozzle rather than raising peak air temperature."]},{"h":"Paste-in-pad and replacement materials","p":["Paste-in-pad suits QFN, MLF and some small land-pattern replacements where design and process allow it.","The paste must be compatible with the existing finish and reflow profile, and the deposit volume controlled by stencil, syringe or dispenser. For BGA replacement, paste-in-pad is less common than new solder balls or a pre-balled component, though a qualified repair method may use it.","Clean the pads, avoid contamination, and confirm paste lot, expiry and storage. IPC J-STD-001 gives the general soldering requirements; apply the qualified method and record the material."]},{"h":"BGA and QFN removal","p":["During removal, heat the joint until the solder collapses, then lift with minimal force. Never pry a component that solid solder still locks in place.","On a BGA, watch for warpage, lifted pads and disturbed neighbours; on a QFN, watch thermal-pad adhesion and corner lifting. If the part will not release, improve preheat or heat distribution before raising peak temperature.","After removal, inspect the pads under magnification and clean residual solder with a qualified method. IPC-7530 gives rework, modification and repair guidance, so use it together with your process qualification and work instruction."]},{"h":"Replacement, alignment and reflow","p":["Align the replacement with fiducials, land patterns or a placement aid.","Alignment matters most on a BGA because the joints are hidden: use a qualified placement method and verify with X-ray after reflow. On a QFN, check coplanarity, paste volume and thermal-pad wetting.","Reflow on a measured profile that meets the paste or ball specification and the board thermal limits. Where the component is moisture-sensitive, follow IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 and record floor-life status before the rework begins."]},{"h":"Inspection and documentation","p":["Inspect after rework by the method the assembly calls for: magnification on QFN and passives, X-ray on BGA and hidden joints, and functional test where specified.","IPC-A-610 gives visual acceptance criteria, applied at the class and criteria the contract defines. Record the rework as a process step, not as an undocumented exception.","The record carries operator, date, station, nozzle, preheat setpoint, measured peak temperature, dwell, cycle count, paste or ball lot, flux lot, inspection result and any deviation."]},{"h":"Allowed cycles and budget control","p":["Every thermal exposure spends part of the component and the board budget. Count cycles per component and per board area, because neighbours see the heat too.","If a component needs several attempts, stop and decide whether the failure is alignment, paste volume, contamination, pad damage or an unsuitable profile. Repeated hot-air attempts without a new root cause lift pads, delaminate the board or damage the part.","Where the contract or board specification gives a maximum cycle count, do not exceed it without engineering approval."]}],"calc":{"task":"A BGA replacement is planned on an assembly that allows 2 rework cycles. One cycle has already been used. The proposed second cycle uses a measured peak temperature of 245 °C and a dwell time of 45 s. The paste datasheet states a maximum peak temperature of 250 °C and a recommended dwell range of 30-60 s. Determine whether the proposed operation is within the stated thermal budget and list what must be recorded.","solution":"1. Count the cycles: one previous cycle plus one proposed cycle equals 2 cycles. 2. Compare the cycle count with the allowed limit: 2 cycles is within the allowed 2 cycles, assuming no other rework has occurred in the same area. 3. Compare the measured peak temperature with the paste limit: 245 °C is below the stated maximum of 250 °C. 4. Compare the dwell time with the recommended range: 45 s is within the stated 30-60 s range. 5. Confirm that the peak temperature was measured at the joint or component, not only from the hot-air controller, and that preheat, ramp, and cooling were controlled according to the qualified profile. 6. Record the rework: operator, date, station, nozzle, preheat setpoint, measured peak temperature, dwell time, cycle count, paste lot, flux lot, inspection method, and inspection result."},"defect":{"symptom":"After BGA replacement, X-ray shows incomplete collapse or bridging on several balls, and the component sits slightly tilted.","cause":"Likely causes include uneven preheat, excessive hot-air temperature on one side, misalignment during placement, insufficient paste or solder-ball volume, contaminated pads, or board warpage during reflow.","action":"Stop the rework. Inspect the pads and component under magnification, clean the pads, verify alignment, and repeat only if the cycle count and thermal budget allow it. Use a more uniform preheat and a qualified nozzle or profile. After replacement, inspect by X-ray and record the corrected profile, materials, and inspection result."},"takeaways":["Count cycles per component and per area before the hot air goes on, because the budget is spent in exposures and not in minutes.","Measure peak temperature at the joint, since the controller display is the air temperature and the joint is what the specification limits.","Raise preheat or nozzle size instead of air temperature, because extra air heat warps the board and blasts the neighbours."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Prying a component that has not fully collapsed","instead":"Keep heating until the solder is liquid, because force lifts pads and cracks the part."},{"mistake":"Trusting the hot-air setpoint as the joint temperature","instead":"Profile with a thermocouple at the joint, since air and joint differ by a wide margin."},{"mistake":"Repeating hot-air attempts when the first one failed","instead":"Stop and find the root cause, because each attempt spends the cycle budget."}],"terms":[{"term":"Paste-in-pad","def":"A repair method that prints or dispenses paste onto the land pattern before placing the component."},{"term":"Thermal cycle","def":"One complete heating and cooling exposure counted against the allowed rework budget."},{"term":"Collapse","def":"The stage at which a solder ball melts and settles onto its pad during reflow."},{"term":"Warpage","def":"Bending of the board under heat that can tilt a component or open joints."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m11l02"],"skills":["Lesson m11l02: Technique: contact order, dwell, feed, lead-free discipline, technique defects"]}},{"lessonId":"m11l04","moduleId":"m11","stageId":"manual-rework","title":"Repair limits: IPC-7711/7721 classes, conductor cut, annular ring, laminate damage","goals":[{"text":"Classify a reworked or repaired joint against IPC-7711/7721 class criteria and state the class the product requires.","competencies":["K8","K6"]},{"text":"Decide repairable versus scrap and record the basis and the residual risk.","competencies":["K8"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-009","src-018","src-022"],"quiz":["q-m11l04-01","q-m11l04-02","q-m11l04-03","q-m11l04-04","q-m11l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m11l04.webp","alt":"A repaired trace with a conductor cut, an annular ring measurement and a laminate damage callout, beside a class limits table","caption":"Repair limits: IPC-7711/7721 classes, conductor cut, annular ring and laminate damage"}]},{"h":"Lesson scope and product class","p":"This lesson covers repair limits for reworked or repaired electronic assemblies using IPC-7711/7721 Class 1, IPC-7711/7721 Class 2, and IPC-7711/7721 Class 3 acceptance criteria. The required class is not chosen by the repair operator; it is set by the product specification, drawing, contract, or reliability plan. A Class 1 product may tolerate a cosmetic laminate scratch that would be unacceptable on a Class 3 product, so the first step is to record the required IPC-7711/7721 class before inspecting the damage."},{"h":"Repairable or scrap","p":["A joint or board area is repairable when the damage sits inside the applicable IPC-7711/7721 limits, the repair method is qualified for the materials and thermal exposure, and the repaired area can be inspected and documented.","It is scrap when the damage violates IPC-7711/7721 acceptance, when the repair would need unqualified materials or processes, or when the residual risk is beyond what the product owner will accept.","The disposition record states the measured condition, the class applied, the repair method and the residual risk, such as thermal fatigue, insulation degradation or mechanical weakness. IPC-7711/7721 is the standard the record cites for the acceptance decision."]},{"h":"Conductor cut and width reduction","p":["A conductor cut is judged as a reduction of conductor width against the designed width.","IPC-7711/7721 does not set the number itself: its conductor-repair procedures require only that width, spacing and current-carrying capacity stay within the allowable tolerances, and leave the limit to the drawing and the bare-board standards. A width figure quoted from IPC-7711/7721 alone would be misread for exactly that reason.","The figure comes from IPC-A-600 §3.5.3.1, which caps the reduction of minimum conductor width at 20 % for Class 2 and 10 % for Class 3, with the controlled revision and customer specification still to be verified. Measure the original width from the drawing or a known-good reference, measure the repaired width, and calculate the reduction. Beyond the limit for the class, the repair is unacceptable unless the customer authorises a deviation."]},{"h":"Annular ring after repair","p":["Annular ring is the copper left around a drilled hole after plating and rework.","IPC-7711/7721 requires the repaired hole to retain the annular ring the class calls for, and the number comes from IPC-A-600 §2.10.3: 0.050 mm [0.0020 in] minimum external annular ring at Class 3, with up to 20 % local reduction for pits, dents, nicks, pinholes or splay. Check the controlled revision and the product specification as well.","Insufficient annular ring cuts current capacity, weakens the thermal relief and raises the risk of cracking in thermal cycling, so a hole below the limit is normally scrap unless a qualified repair restores the copper geometry."]},{"h":"Laminate damage and dielectric integrity","p":["Laminate damage is assessed for delamination, blistering, separation, exposed conductors and loss of dielectric insulation.","IPC-7711/7721 does not accept damage that creates delamination, blistering, separation or exposed conductors. A shallow cosmetic scratch may be repairable where a qualified repair restores insulation and no prohibited condition exists.","IPC-7721 sets no laminate-damage depth limit. Its laminate-repair procedures, epoxy and film-adhesive, are judged on what they restore: insulation and electrical clearance held, no epoxy in the hole or on internal pad walls, no reduction in hole diameter, no exposed adjacent circuitry. Where a depth limit is needed, take it from the controlled IPC-A-600 or IPC-6012 revision and the customer specification, not from IPC-7721, which describes the laminate-repair procedures and not the limit itself."]},{"h":"Repair method qualification","p":["The repair method must be qualified for the laminate system, the copper finish, the solder mask, the component package and the thermal profile.","Practice-based values such as preheat rate, peak temperature, dwell and cleaning solvent compatibility come from the paste, flux, solder mask and equipment datasheets, and are confirmed by trial.","A repair that meets the IPC-7711/7721 geometry limits can still be rejected if the process blisters or delaminates the laminate, lifts solder mask, damages a component, or leaves contamination that cannot be removed. Qualification belongs with the method, and IPC-7711/7721 acceptance applies to the result."]},{"h":"Recording basis and residual risk","p":["The disposition record must be specific enough for another engineer to repeat the decision.","It carries the class, the measured conductor width reduction, the measured annular ring, the laminate condition, the repair method, the inspection result and the residual risk. Residual risk can include reduced current margin, greater sensitivity to thermal cycling, lower insulation resistance or reduced mechanical strength.","If that residual risk is unacceptable to the product owner, the correct disposition is scrap even where the repair is technically possible, and IPC-7711/7721 accepts that outcome as a valid decision."]}],"calc":{"task":"A Class 2 assembly requires IPC-7711/7721 Class 2. A repaired trace originally 0.30 mm wide is now 0.24 mm wide. A repaired plated through hole has an annular ring of 0.06 mm. A laminate scratch is 0.08 mm deep, with no delamination, no blistering, no separation, and no exposed conductors. Decide whether the assembly is repairable or scrap and state the basis.","solution":"Step 1: Confirm the required class is IPC-7711/7721 Class 2. Step 2: Calculate conductor width reduction: (0.30 mm - 0.24 mm) / 0.30 mm = 0.06 mm / 0.30 mm = 0.20, or 20%. Step 3: Compare with the IPC-A-600 §3.5.3.1 conductor width reduction limit for Class 2, which is 20% of the minimum conductor width. The trace is exactly at the limit and acceptable only if the controlled revision and customer specification allow 20% for Class 2. Step 4: Compare annular ring: 0.06 mm is greater than the IPC-A-600 §2.10.3 Class 3 minimum of 0.050 mm, so the hole is acceptable if the controlled revision confirms that minimum for the class. Step 5: Evaluate laminate damage: the scratch is 0.08 mm deep with no delamination, blistering, separation or exposed conductor, so no prohibited condition exists. IPC-7721 sets no depth limit; a shallow cosmetic scratch is repairable if a qualified repair restores insulation and electrical clearance. Step 6: Disposition: repairable, provided the repair method is qualified, the laminate is restored or accepted as cosmetic by the customer, and the residual risk is documented. Basis: conductor width reduction at the IPC-A-600 §3.5.3.1 Class 2 limit of 20%, annular ring above the IPC-A-600 §2.10.3 minimum, and laminate damage without prohibited defects. Residual risk: reduced trace current margin, possible thermal cycling sensitivity at the repaired hole, and possible insulation resistance risk at the laminate scratch if the repair material is not qualified."},"defect":{"symptom":"After rework, a plated through hole shows a crescent-shaped copper gap around the hole, and the adjacent trace is visibly narrower than the original trace.","cause":"Excessive heat or mechanical abrasion during component removal and pad cleaning removed copper from the annular ring and reduced the trace width.","action":"Measure the annular ring and trace width, compare them with the applicable IPC-A-600 class limits, and record the result. If the annular ring or conductor width reduction is outside the IPC-A-600 limit, scrap the assembly or request a customer deviation. If within limits, inspect for delamination, blistering, and exposed conductors, qualify the repair method, and document the residual risk."},"takeaways":["Fix the class before inspecting the damage, because the same scratch is acceptable on one product and scrap on another.","Take the numeric limits from IPC-A-600 and IPC-6012 rather than IPC-7721, which describes procedures and leaves the numbers to those documents.","Record the measured condition and the residual risk, so the next engineer can repeat or challenge the disposition."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Repairing to the operator's own idea of good","instead":"Apply the class the contract sets, because acceptance is a product decision."},{"mistake":"Treating IPC-7721 as a source of depth or width limits","instead":"Use IPC-A-600 and IPC-6012 for the numbers, since IPC-7721 gives procedures."},{"mistake":"Scrapping a repairable board because the repair looks difficult","instead":"Judge against the measured limits and the qualified method, not against comfort."}],"terms":[{"term":"Annular ring","def":"The width of copper remaining around a drilled hole after plating."},{"term":"Disposition","def":"The recorded decision to repair, rework, use as is or scrap an assembly."},{"term":"Residual risk","def":"The remaining weakness a repair leaves behind, accepted or rejected by the product owner."},{"term":"Delamination (board laminate)","def":"Separation between layers of laminate within the board."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m11l03","m06l02"],"skills":["Lesson m11l03: Rework: hot air, preheat, paste-in-pad, BGA/QFN removal and replacement","Lesson m06l02: Profile anatomy: ramp, soak, time above liquidus, peak, cooling — what each controls"]}},{"lessonId":"m11l05","moduleId":"m11","stageId":"manual-rework","title":"Acceptance and re-inspection of rework and repair; documenting the operation","goals":[{"text":"Classify a reworked or repaired joint against IPC-7711/7721 class criteria and state the class the product requires.","competencies":["K8","K6"]},{"text":"Decide repairable versus scrap and record the basis and the residual risk.","competencies":["K8"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-002","src-009","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m11l05-01","q-m11l05-02","q-m11l05-03","q-m11l05-04","q-m11l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m11l05.webp","alt":"A magnified reworked joint under inspection with a fillet and wetting check, beside a record sheet listing method, cycles and thermal exposure","caption":"Acceptance and re-inspection of rework and repair, and documenting the operation"}]},{"h":"Purpose of acceptance and re-inspection","p":["Acceptance after rework or repair is not a glance at the touched joint.","The inspector confirms that the affected area, the adjacent joints, the nearby components and the board surface still meet the required class, and that the repair method introduced no new defect. IPC-7711/7721 gives acceptability criteria for reworked and repaired assemblies, IPC-A-610 gives the visual and construction baseline for the original assembly, and IPC J-STD-001 carries the process requirements for materials, cleanliness, solderability and thermal stress."]},{"h":"Rework versus repair","p":["Rework restores a nonconforming assembly to the original design and specification.","Repair makes a nonconforming assembly acceptable for use, and the result may not restore the original design intent. Because repair can leave a deviation, it usually needs engineering approval, a qualified repair method and a documented risk assessment.","IPC-7711/7721 is the primary standard for judging whether a reworked or repaired joint is acceptable, though the product specification may also call up IPC-A-610 and IPC J-STD-001."]},{"h":"Class selection","p":["The customer, the product specification or the reliability requirement sets the class, and it must be stated before inspection.","IPC-A-610 and IPC-7711/7721 both use class-based criteria, commonly Class 1, Class 2 and Class 3, with Class 3 generally reserved for high-reliability products where a defect has greater consequence. The inspector never infers the class from the component type: the work order, drawing, contract or quality plan confirms it."]},{"h":"What to inspect","p":["Re-inspection covers the repaired joint, the adjacent joints, the component body and leads, the pad and trace area, the solder mask, nearby components and local cleanliness.","On joints, look for wetting, fillet formation, solder volume, cracks, exposed copper, lifted pads, damaged traces, disturbed solder mask, flux residue, contamination and mechanical damage. On components, look for lifted leads, cracked bodies, discolouration, thermal damage and missing or damaged markings.","IPC-7711/7721 gives the repairability and acceptability guidance, and IPC-A-610 supplies the visual baseline."]},{"h":"Repairable or scrap","p":["A joint is repairable when a qualified method can correct the defect, the required class can be achieved, and the repair creates no unacceptable residual risk.","Scrap applies when the damage is too severe, the method is not qualified, the board material is thermally or mechanically damaged, or the repair would need an unapproved deviation.","IPC-7711/7721 supports repairability decisions from defect type, component type, board condition and required class. IPC J-STD-001 supports the process controls that keep the repair from creating a new defect."]},{"h":"Documenting the basis and the residual risk","p":["The record states the original defect, the required class, the repair method, the materials, the operator, the equipment, the inspection result and the disposition.","It also states the reasoning behind repair against scrap, and the residual risk the organisation has accepted: reduced thermal fatigue life, contamination, flux residue, mechanical weakness, trace or pad damage, component thermal damage, or uncertainty about future reliability.","IPC-7711/7721 supports documentation of the operation, while the quality system defines the record format and the approval route."]},{"h":"Practical line approach","p":["On the line, the inspector confirms the class first, then inspects the repaired area against IPC-A-610 and IPC-7711/7721.","A joint that meets the class with a qualified method is accepted. A joint that does not meet the class goes on to the question of whether a qualified repair can restore compliance; if it cannot, the assembly is scrapped or routed for engineering disposition.","Every repair is re-inspected, and the residual risk is recorded alongside the disposition."]}],"calc":{"task":"A reworked QFP solder joint on a high-reliability assembly shows a lifted pad and exposed copper after hot-air rework. The product specification requires IPC-A-610 Acceptability of Electronic Assemblies Class 3 and IPC-7711/7721 Acceptability of Rework, Modifications, and Repairs for Electronic Assemblies Class 3. Classify the joint, decide repairable versus scrap, and record the basis and residual risk.","solution":"Step 1: Confirm the required class from the work order or product specification. In this case, the required class is IPC-A-610 Acceptability of Electronic Assemblies Class 3 and IPC-7711/7721 Acceptability of Rework, Modifications, and Repairs for Electronic Assemblies Class 3. Step 2: Inspect the repaired joint and adjacent area. The observed condition is a lifted pad and exposed copper, with possible thermal disturbance to the solder mask and nearby joints. Step 3: Compare the condition against IPC-A-610 Acceptability of Electronic Assemblies Class 3. A lifted pad and exposed copper indicate that the original visual and construction acceptability condition is not met. Step 4: Compare the condition against IPC-7711/7721 Acceptability of Rework, Modifications, and Repairs for Electronic Assemblies Class 3. The repairability decision depends on whether the pad, trace, and board material can be restored to the required class using a qualified repair method. Step 5: Determine whether a qualified repair exists. If the pad is lifted and the copper is exposed, repair may require pad or trace restoration, which is a deviation from the original design and requires engineering approval. If no qualified repair method exists, the joint is not repairable under the required class. Step 6: Decide repairable versus scrap. If the pad lift is minor, the trace is intact, the board material is not thermally damaged, and a qualified repair method is available, the joint may be repairable with engineering approval. If the pad is detached, the trace is damaged, the board material is delaminated, or the repair cannot restore Class 3, the assembly should be scrapped or routed for engineering disposition. Step 7: Record the basis. The record should state the defect, required class, inspection result, repairability decision, approval status, and disposition. Step 8: Record residual risk. If repaired, residual risk may include reduced thermal fatigue life, contamination, flux residue, mechanical weakness, trace or pad damage, component thermal damage, or future reliability uncertainty. If scrapped, residual risk is avoided, but the cost is the loss of the assembly."},"defect":{"symptom":"After rework, the solder joint appears acceptable at first glance, but the pad is slightly lifted and the copper is exposed at the edge of the land.","cause":"Excessive heat or mechanical force during rework lifted the pad from the laminate, exposing copper and disturbing the solder mask.","action":"Stop the repair, inspect the pad, trace, and adjacent joints, and compare the condition against IPC-A-610 Acceptability of Electronic Assemblies and IPC-7711/7721 Acceptability of Rework, Modifications, and Repairs for Electronic Assemblies. If the required class cannot be restored with a qualified repair method, scrap the assembly or route it for engineering disposition. Record the defect, required class, repairability decision, and residual risk."},"takeaways":["Confirm the class from the work order before inspecting, because the same defect passes one product and fails another.","Treat repair as a deviation that needs approval, since rework restores the design and repair may not.","Record the residual risk with the disposition, so the accepted weakness is visible to whoever inherits the assembly."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Accepting a repair on the visual look of the touched joint","instead":"Inspect the neighbours, the mask and the cleanliness too, because the repair changed all of them."},{"mistake":"Inferring the class from the component type","instead":"Read it from the work order or contract, since class is a product decision."},{"mistake":"Leaving the repair out of the build record","instead":"Log it as a process step, because an undocumented repair cannot be audited."}],"terms":[{"term":"Rework","def":"Correcting a nonconforming assembly so it meets the original design."},{"term":"Repair","def":"Making a nonconforming assembly acceptable without necessarily restoring the design."},{"term":"Re-inspection","def":"The inspection of a repaired area and its surroundings against the required class."},{"term":"Engineering disposition","def":"The formal decision on whether a nonconforming assembly may be used, repaired or scrapped."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m11l04"],"skills":["Lesson m11l04: Repair limits: IPC-7711/7721 classes, conductor cut, annular ring, laminate damage"]}},{"lessonId":"m11l06","moduleId":"m11","stageId":"manual-rework","title":"Practice: rework one joint and document it against criteria","goals":[{"text":"Specify or perform a rework operation within the allowed cycles and thermal budget and record what was applied.","competencies":["K8"]},{"text":"Classify a reworked or repaired joint against IPC-7711/7721 class criteria and state the class the product requires.","competencies":["K8","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-009","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m11l06-01","q-m11l06-02","q-m11l06-03","q-m11l06-04","q-m11l06-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m11l06.webp","alt":"A single reworked joint with measurement callouts and a completed documentation form beside it","caption":"Practice: rework one joint and document it against criteria"}]},{"h":"Scope and purpose","p":["This practice asks you to rework a solder joint, document the operation, and classify the result against the IPC-7711/7721 rework and repair requirements at the acceptance class the product specifies.","The product owner normally states the required IPC-A-610 class, Class 2 for instance, and the reworked joint must meet that class unless an approved deviation exists."]},{"h":"Thermal budget before heat","p":["Record the budget before the iron goes near the joint: allowed rework cycles, peak temperature, dwell, preheat temperature and cooling method, taken from the product specification, IPC-7711/7721 and the paste, component and equipment datasheets.","Use the lowest thermal input that produces wetting, and check it against your datasheet, because alloy, board finish, mass and nearby components all move the working window. A budget written after the fact is a description, not a control."]},{"h":"Preparation and contamination control","p":["Inspect the joint, clean the area, and remove old solder, flux residue and moisture-sensitive residues with approved materials.","Confirm that pad, land and nearby components are not lifted, cracked or delaminated. If damage is already there, stop and evaluate a repair under IPC-7711/7721 instead of carrying on with rework, which would only deepen it."]},{"h":"Performing the rework","p":["Apply heat in a controlled way, with hot gas, a soldering iron or a local rework station as approved.","Add only the solder and flux needed to wet the joint, then let it solidify without disturbance. Record the tool, the tip or nozzle, the temperature setpoint, the measured peak temperature, the dwell, the number of thermal cycles and any cleaning performed."]},{"h":"Documentation of the class claim","p":["A rework record identifies the assembly, serial number, date, operator, station, original defect, action taken, thermal data, materials used and the post-rework inspection result.","It also states the required IPC-A-610 class and whether the joint meets IPC-7711/7721 rework or repair acceptance for that class, because the record is the evidence behind the class claim."]},{"h":"Classifying the reworked joint","p":["Inspect against the IPC-A-610 acceptance criteria for the required class, then confirm the method is acceptable under IPC-7711/7721.","A joint that fails becomes a repair candidate, a deviation candidate or scrap under the approved process. Rework is never automatically acceptable just because the inspector did it."]},{"h":"Failure modes to watch","p":["The usual problems are insufficient wetting, lifted pads, thermal damage to components or laminate, excess flux residue, disturbance of an adjacent joint, and mechanical stress from component removal.","Record each with evidence and disposition it against IPC-7711/7721 and IPC-A-610, so the failure is traceable to a cause and a decision rather than to a retry."]}],"calc":{"task":"A product requires IPC-A-610 Class 2. The product specification allows up to 2 rework thermal cycles and a peak temperature of 250 °C for up to 10 s. A technician preheats the board to 150 °C, applies hot gas with a measured peak of 248 °C for 8 s, then uses a 350 °C iron tip for 2 s to touch up the joint. Determine whether the operation is within the stated budget and what must be recorded for IPC-7711/7721 documentation.","solution":"Begin by counting thermal cycles. The hot-gas pass counts as a cycle and the iron touch-up counts as another cycle, so the total is 2 cycles, which equals the allowed maximum. Compare peak temperature. The measured hot-gas peak is 248 °C, which is below the 250 °C limit. Compare dwell time. The hot-gas dwell is 8 s, which is below the 10 s limit. Check the iron touch-up. The iron tip temperature is 350 °C, but the joint peak must be compared with the 250 °C limit; if the measured joint peak during the 2 s touch-up remains at or below 250 °C, the thermal budget is met. Record the result. Record preheat 150 °C, hot-gas peak 248 °C for 8 s, iron tip 350 °C for 2 s, total cycles 2, materials used, inspection result, IPC-7711/7721 rework acceptance, and the required IPC-A-610 Class 2 disposition."},"defect":{"symptom":"After rework, the joint shows a lifted pad, insufficient wetting, and a dark ring around the land.","cause":"Excessive heat or dwell, combined with mechanical force during component removal, weakened the pad adhesion and disturbed the solder interface.","action":"Stop further rework, quarantine the assembly, and evaluate the condition under IPC-7711/7721. If a repair is approved and the joint can meet IPC-A-610 Class 2, perform the approved repair and document it; otherwise scrap or route for engineering disposition."},"takeaways":["Write the thermal budget before applying heat, because a budget recorded afterwards cannot constrain the operation.","Count every heat application as a cycle, including the iron touch-up, since the allowance is spent in exposures and not in passes.","Record the class and the evidence with the rework, so the acceptance claim can be defended later."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Treating the iron touch-up as outside the cycle count","instead":"Count it, because it is a thermal exposure like any other."},{"mistake":"Reworking on top of a lifted pad","instead":"Stop and evaluate a repair, since more heat makes the damage worse."},{"mistake":"Claiming acceptance without the thermal record","instead":"Record peak temperature and dwell, because the class claim rests on that evidence."}],"terms":[{"term":"Touch-up","def":"A short iron pass used to correct a joint after the main rework heating."},{"term":"Deviation","def":"An approved departure from the specified acceptance criteria."},{"term":"Disposition","def":"The recorded decision to accept, repair, rework or scrap an assembly."},{"term":"Thermal cycle","def":"One heating and cooling exposure counted against the allowed rework allowance."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m11l05"],"skills":["Lesson m11l05: Acceptance and re-inspection of rework and repair; documenting the operation"]}},{"lessonId":"m12l01","moduleId":"m12","stageId":"coating-esd","title":"What residue is and why it matters: ionic residue, SIR, CAF, contact failure","goals":[{"text":"Select a cleaning method and chemistry for a stated residue and assembly type, including MSL/ESD constraints.","competencies":["K1"]},{"text":"Interpret cleanliness test results and decide pass or rework, stating the criterion.","competencies":["K1","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-002","src-003","src-009","src-010","src-012","src-013","src-035"],"quiz":["q-m12l01-01","q-m12l01-02","q-m12l01-03","q-m12l01-04","q-m12l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"Residue is not one thing","p":["Residue is any unwanted material left on a board after assembly: flux solids, solder paste residues, no-clean flux polymer films, fingerprints, dust, handling oils and ionic salts.","It matters because it holds moisture, supports electrochemical migration, changes surface energy, interferes with coating adhesion and raises contact resistance. In coating and ESD stages it can also create localised charge paths or blunt the effect of ionised air blowers.","Treat residue as a process defect with a source, a mechanism and a verification method, not as a cosmetic issue."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","caption":"Conformal coating line: cleanliness before coating is the whole game","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 900 300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Conformal coating process flow'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace}</style><rect x='30' y='70' width='120' height='50' rx='14' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.08)' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1'/><text x='90' y='100' fill='#53d1cc' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle'>CLEAN</text><line x1='154' y1='95' x2='166' y2='95' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M170,95 l-9,-5 v10 z' fill='#53d1cc'/><rect x='174' y='70' width='120' height='50' rx='14' fill='rgba(15,23,34,.78)' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><text x='234' y='100' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle'>DRY</text><line x1='298' y1='95' x2='310' y2='95' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M314,95 l-9,-5 v10 z' fill='#53d1cc'/><rect x='318' y='70' width='120' height='50' rx='14' fill='rgba(15,23,34,.78)' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><text x='378' y='100' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle'>MASK</text><line x1='442' y1='95' x2='454' y2='95' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M458,95 l-9,-5 v10 z' fill='#53d1cc'/><rect x='462' y='70' width='120' height='50' rx='14' fill='rgba(15,23,34,.78)' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><text x='522' y='100' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle'>APPLY</text><line x1='586' y1='95' x2='598' y2='95' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M602,95 l-9,-5 v10 z' fill='#53d1cc'/><rect x='606' y='70' width='120' height='50' rx='14' fill='rgba(15,23,34,.78)' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><text x='666' y='100' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle'>CURE</text><line x1='730' y1='95' x2='742' y2='95' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M746,95 l-9,-5 v10 z' fill='#53d1cc'/><rect x='750' y='70' width='120' height='50' rx='14' fill='rgba(15,23,34,.78)' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><text x='810' y='100' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle'>VERIFY</text><text x='90' y='150' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>IONIC CLEAN</text><text x='378' y='150' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>CONNECTORS</text><text x='522' y='150' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>SPRAY / DIP / SELECTIVE</text><text x='666' y='150' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>UV / MOISTURE / HEAT</text><text x='810' y='150' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>UV LIGHT + THICKNESS</text><text x='450' y='210' fill='#ff9800' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>COATING SEALS RESIDUE IN — CLEANLINESS IS VERIFIED BEFORE APPLY</text><text x='450' y='236' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>IONIC RESIDUE + HUMIDITY → SIR DROP → CONDUCTIVE ANODIC FILAMENT (CAF)</text></svg>"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m12l01.webp","alt":"A board surface with flux residue between close conductors, with a surface insulation resistance test cell and a filament-growth callout","caption":"What residue is and why it matters: ionic residue, surface insulation resistance, conductive anodic filament and contact failure"}]},{"h":"Ionic residue, ROSE and what it does not prove","p":["Ionic residue is contamination that dissolves in water and can carry current once a thin water film forms.","ROSE tests measure soluble ionic contamination as sodium chloride equivalent, usually with IPC-TM-650 methods and the cleanliness requirements that IPC J-STD-001 references.","A low ROSE value is useful and it does not prove the board is free of organic, hydrophobic or particulate residue. A board can pass its ROSE limit and still carry a no-clean flux film that absorbs moisture, lowers surface insulation resistance or stops the coating wetting. Use ROSE as a gate, and pair it with visual inspection, SIR or CAF testing where the product has fine pitch, high-impedance nodes or long-term reliability requirements."]},{"h":"SIR and CAF: residue becomes a reliability failure","p":["Surface insulation resistance measures how well a surface blocks current between conductors under humidity and bias, commonly evaluated with IPC-TM-650 methods.","Low SIR can come from ionic residue, absorbed moisture, flux films or contamination bridging fine-pitch lands. Conductive anodic filament is a related failure in which copper migrates along glass fibres in the laminate, accelerated by moisture, bias and residue that lowers local insulation resistance, and it is normally screened with IPC-TM-650 CAF methods.","SIR and CAF are different tests that say the same thing: the surface or the laminate is not electrically stable under the intended service condition."]},{"h":"Contact failure: residue at the interface","p":["Contact failure happens when residue changes the metal-to-metal interface at edge connectors, spring contacts, press-fit pins or test points.","Thin organic films raise contact resistance, cause intermittent opens, or promote fretting corrosion by changing lubricity and moisture retention. Gold plating tolerates some contamination without being immune: particulate and ionic residue still cause intermittent contact or corrosion under the contact.","Cleaning chemistry must suit the plating, the elastomers and the connectors. On ESD-sensitive assemblies, cleaning and handling follow ANSI/ESD S20.20 or IEC 61340-5-1, because a clean board can still be damaged by uncontrolled charge transfer."]},{"h":"Selecting cleaning method and chemistry","p":["Select cleaning by residue type, assembly geometry and downstream constraints.","Water-soluble flux usually wants aqueous cleaning with a saponifier or alkaline cleaner and a thorough deionised water rinse. No-clean flux may need a semi-aqueous solvent, a low-surface-tension aqueous cleaner, or a solvent rinse followed by an aqueous wash, depending on how much residue remains and whether reflow has turned it into a polymer film.","Rosin residues often respond to alcohol or solvent cleaning, and the solvent choice has to consider flammability, material compatibility and environmental permits. Verify cleaner concentration, temperature and rinse quality against your paste and equipment datasheet.","For bottom-terminated packages, high-aspect-ratio gaps and under-component spaces, the method has to wet, penetrate and rinse.","That rules out a spray that only reaches the visible surface and points towards agitation, vacuum-assisted cleaning, or ultrasonic cleaning where the assembly tolerates it.","A cleaner that cannot reach the residue cannot remove it, and the board leaves the line looking clean while the gap stays contaminated. That reach is part of method selection, not a separate step."]},{"h":"MSL and ESD constraints on cleaning","p":["Moisture-sensitive devices need moisture control before and after cleaning.","Where an assembly has been exposed beyond the floor life that IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 assigns, bake it before reflow or rework according to the MSL level and the product specification. Cleaning can reintroduce moisture, so the drying, bake and reflow windows need control.","For ESD-sensitive work, use grounded surfaces, wrist straps, ionisers and ESD-safe containers as ANSI/ESD S20.20 or IEC 61340-5-1 requires, and avoid high-velocity dry air and uncontrolled plastic film that generate charge. Where cleaning happens inside an ESD-controlled area, confirm that the equipment, baskets and transport materials suit the ESD program."]},{"h":"Interpreting cleanliness results and deciding rework","p":["Decide pass or rework against a written criterion, never against operator opinion.","The criterion may come from IPC J-STD-001, a customer specification, a reliability plan, or an IPC-TM-650 method applied to a coupon or an assembly. A typical decision tree reads: visual residue means rework; ROSE above the limit means rework; a failed SIR or CAF minimum means rework even where ROSE passes.","On a marginal result, repeat the test on a fresh coupon or a defined area and document the sampling location. When reworking, find the residue source, adjust chemistry or rinse, retest and record the result; if the rework touches components, follow IPC-7711/7721 and re-evaluate MSL exposure."],"blocks":[{"t":"table","caption":"Coating chemistries (IPC-CC-830 type families)","head":["Chemistry","Moisture barrier","Removability","Typical use"],"rows":[["Acrylic (AR)","Moderate","Easy — solvent","General purpose, rework-friendly"],["Polyurethane (UR)","Good","Hard — mechanical or chemical","Abrasion and moisture resistance"],["Silicone (SR)","Excellent","Hard — heat or cut","High humidity, condensation"],["Parylene (XY)","Excellent","Very hard — plasma etch","Medical, RF, pinhole-free conformality"]]}]}],"calc":{"task":"A no-clean flux assembly contains bottom-terminated QFNs and fine-pitch connectors. It is classified MSL 3 and is processed in an ESD-controlled area. After cleaning, ROSE coupons read 0.82 µg NaCl/cm². The product cleanliness limit is 1.00 µg NaCl/cm². SIR testing under the specified humidity and bias condition reads 8.5 × 10^7 Ω. The reliability specification requires at least 1.0 × 10^8 Ω. Select a cleaning method and chemistry, state the pass or rework decision, and state the criterion.","solution":"Step 1: Identify the residue. The stated residue is no-clean flux, which can leave an organic film plus ionic activators. Step 2: Check assembly constraints. Bottom terminations and fine-pitch connectors require a cleaning method that can wet, penetrate, and rinse narrow gaps. MSL 3 requires moisture control per IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033; if floor life is exceeded, bake before rework. ESD controls must follow ANSI/ESD S20.20 or IEC 61340-5-1. Step 3: Select cleaning method and chemistry. Use a low-surface-tension aqueous cleaner or a semi-aqueous solvent followed by deionized water rinse. If the no-clean flux has polymerized, use a compatible solvent or semi-aqueous chemistry first, then aqueous rinse. Verify cleaner concentration, temperature, and rinse conductivity against your paste and equipment datasheet. Step 4: Apply the cleanliness criterion. The 1.00 µg NaCl/cm² value here is a product-specific limit; the widely used published reference is 1.56 µg NaCl/cm² per IPC-TM-650 method 2.3.25, and high-reliability customers often work tighter. ROSE 0.82 µg NaCl/cm² is below the 1.00 µg NaCl/cm² limit, so ROSE passes. Step 5: Apply the reliability criterion. SIR 8.5 × 10^7 Ω is below the required 1.0 × 10^8 Ω, so SIR fails. Step 6: Decide pass or rework. Rework the assembly. The governing criterion is the SIR minimum, not the ROSE pass. Suspect insufficient rinse, hydrophobic residue, or residue trapped under bottom terminations. Re-clean with improved rinse or a semi-aqueous/solvent step, dry, bake if required by MSL exposure, and retest ROSE and SIR."},"defect":{"symptom":"Edge-card connectors show intermittent opens and variable contact resistance after conformal coating. Boards pass visual inspection and ROSE, but functional test fails.","cause":"No-clean flux residue was trapped under connector contacts, and the aqueous rinse did not remove a hydrophobic film. The cleaning process was not qualified for the connector geometry, and ESD-safe transport was not maintained between cleaning and coating.","action":"Quarantine the lot. Re-clean using a semi-aqueous or compatible solvent step followed by deionized water rinse. Verify cleanliness with IPC-TM-650 methods and contact resistance or SIR testing. Control MSL exposure per IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 and ESD handling per ANSI/ESD S20.20 or IEC 61340-5-1. If component rework is needed, follow IPC-7711/7721."},"takeaways":["Read ROSE as a gate rather than a verdict, because it measures ionic species and says nothing about a hydrophobic flux film.","Let the failed SIR result decide when ROSE passes, since impedance under bias is closer to the service condition.","Pick the chemistry from the residue and the geometry, because a cleaner that cannot reach under a bottom termination cannot remove what is there."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Accepting a board on the ROSE number alone","instead":"Test SIR where the product calls for it, since organic residue passes ROSE."},{"mistake":"Choosing the strongest solvent available as a default","instead":"Match chemistry to plating and elastomers, because the cleaner can damage the assembly it saves."},{"mistake":"Cleaning after floor life has expired without a bake","instead":"Bake per IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033 first, because cleaning adds moisture rather than removing it."}],"terms":[{"term":"ROSE","def":"Resistivity of solvent extract, a test of soluble ionic contamination expressed as sodium chloride equivalent."},{"term":"Surface insulation resistance","def":"The resistance measured across a surface between conductors under humidity and applied bias."},{"term":"Conductive anodic filament","def":"A copper filament that grows through laminate along glass fibres under bias and moisture."},{"term":"Saponifier","def":"An alkaline additive that converts flux residue into a water-soluble soap for rinsing."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m02l06","m06l07"],"skills":["Lesson m02l06: Case: expired floor life found mid-lot","Lesson m06l07: Profile validation, documentation and change control"]}},{"lessonId":"m12l02","moduleId":"m12","stageId":"coating-esd","title":"Cleaning methods: aqueous, semi-aqueous, solvent, ultrasonic; material compatibility","goals":[{"text":"Select a cleaning method and chemistry for a stated residue and assembly type, including MSL/ESD constraints.","competencies":["K1"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-003","src-012"],"quiz":["q-m12l02-01","q-m12l02-02","q-m12l02-03","q-m12l02-04","q-m12l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m12l02.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: inline cleaner with wash, rinse, final rinse and dry chambers, aqueous / semi-aqueous / solvent / ultrasonic method icons, a blank compatibility checklist to fill from datasheets and a coupon trial, residue types, a rinse photo and MSL / ESD constraints","caption":"Cleaning methods and material compatibility: match chemistry and action to the residue, verify compatibility on a coupon"}]},{"h":"Cleaning objective and residue classification","p":["Cleaning on SMT and through-hole assemblies removes flux residue, particulates, ionic contamination and process aids before coating, inspection, test or final assembly.","Select the method from the residue type, the assembly construction, the component sensitivity and the downstream process. Rosin, no-clean, water-soluble and solder paste residues plus mixed contamination all behave differently, so chemistry and mechanical action are matched to the residue rather than picked by convenience.","For coating, cleaning also has to leave a surface the coating can wet and bond to, which is a stricter target than visual cleanliness."]},{"h":"Aqueous cleaning and its limits","p":["Aqueous cleaning uses water-based chemistry, often with surfactants, saponifiers or alkaline additives, and may add spray, immersion, ultrasonic or agitation.","It works well on water-soluble flux residues, ionic contamination and some no-clean residues where the residue is accessible and the chemistry compatible. Typical process temperatures sit in the range of 40 to 70 degrees Celsius, and the exact temperature, concentration, contact time and rinse quality are verified against the paste, flux and equipment datasheet.","The method needs good rinsing and drying, because water left in a barrel or under a component is the next defect."]},{"h":"Semi-aqueous cleaning","p":["Semi-aqueous cleaning combines a solvent or solvent-like chemistry with water or water-based rinsing, often using alcohols, glycol ethers or modified alcohols.","It suits residues that are partly soluble in water but also carry organic or rosin-like components needing stronger organic chemistry. Drying time can be shorter than a fully aqueous process, while rinse water quality, drying and chemical compatibility still need control.","On mixed SMT and through-hole assemblies it is a practical compromise where water-soluble and rosin residues occur together."]},{"h":"Solvent cleaning and selective use","p":["Solvent cleaning dissolves flux residues, oils, adhesives and some coating residues with organic solvents or blends.","It is effective for localised cleaning, rework and residues that water-based chemistry removes poorly. Selection has to weigh flammability, toxicity, regulatory restrictions, material compatibility and drying behaviour.","Solvents attack plastics, labels, markings, conformal coatings, adhesives and some elastomers, so compatibility is tested before production. On ESD-sensitive assemblies, solvent cleaning needs the same ESD controls as any other bench operation."]},{"h":"Ultrasonic cleaning","p":["Ultrasonic cleaning uses high-frequency acoustic energy in a liquid bath to generate cavitation, which lifts contamination out of complex geometry, blind holes, through-holes and under-component spaces.","Typical ultrasonic frequencies are often selected in the range of 25 to 40 kHz for general cleaning, with higher frequencies used for more delicate parts, and this band is confirmed against the equipment datasheet. It reaches particulates and flux residue that spray cleaning misses. The same energy can damage delicate components, thin wires, fragile terminations, unsecured parts and some optical or MEMS devices, so power density, frequency, bath chemistry and exposure time are all controlled and the frequency band is confirmed against the equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Material compatibility","p":["Compatibility is evaluated for laminate, solder mask, silkscreen, component bodies, terminations, labels, connectors, seals, adhesives, coatings and protective films.","Cleaning chemistry causes swelling, crazing, discolouration, marking removal, adhesion loss or corrosion, and the risk rises with temperature, concentration and dwell. Through-hole and mixed assemblies need attention to trapped liquid and cleaning-agent residue in barrel holes and under leads.","For an assembly headed for coating, wetting and adhesion are part of the compatibility question, not a separate one."]},{"h":"MSL constraints during cleaning","p":["Moisture sensitivity follows the applicable MSL classification and floor-life rules, commonly referenced through IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033.","Cleaning exposes moisture-sensitive devices to water, heat and extra handling time, which can consume floor life or force a re-bake before reflow. For MSL 3 or higher, plan the process so total exposure, drying time and storage stay inside the limits.","Where cleaning happens after placement and before reflow, the assembly may need drying according to the MSL rules before it goes into the oven."]},{"h":"ESD constraints during cleaning","p":["Cleaning brings ESD risk through fluid flow, spraying, wiping, handling and the movement of insulating materials.","Handle assemblies with ESD-sensitive parts in an EPA consistent with ANSI/ESD S20.20, with grounded surfaces, wrist straps where appropriate, ESD-safe containers and controlled handling. Solvent wiping, compressed air and dry cloth wiping generate charge when the materials are not ESD-safe, so material choice and humidity control matter.","At the coating stage the assembly may already be sensitive, so the cleaning step belongs inside the ESD program rather than beside it."]},{"h":"Method selection logic","p":["A practical sequence: identify the residue, define the assembly type, list the material and MSL/ESD constraints, then pick the least aggressive method that meets the cleanliness requirement.","Water-soluble residues favour aqueous cleaning, rosin and no-clean residues may favour semi-aqueous or solvent cleaning, and trapped particulates favour ultrasonic or immersion assistance.","For conformal coating, validate the cleaning process with post-clean inspection and coating adhesion testing rather than visual cleanliness alone, and keep MSL-sensitive assemblies inside their exposure windows."]}],"calc":{"task":"Select a cleaning method for a mixed SMT/THT assembly with water-soluble flux residue, trapped particulates in through-holes, MSL 3 components, and a downstream conformal coating requirement. Estimate whether a 60 minute aqueous cleaning and drying cycle can be completed within a 4 hour floor-life window, and define the minimum verification checks before coating.","solution":"Step 1: Identify residue and constraints. The residue is water-soluble flux with particulates, the assembly is mixed SMT/THT, components are MSL 3, and the assembly must be clean before conformal coating. Step 2: Select candidate method. Aqueous cleaning is suitable for water-soluble flux, and ultrasonic or agitation assistance may help remove particulates from through-holes. Step 3: Estimate cycle time. Assume cleaning 10 minutes, rinse 5 minutes, drying 45 minutes, and handling 10 minutes, giving a total of 70 minutes. Step 4: Compare with floor-life window. The available window is 4 hours, or 240 minutes. Remaining floor life after cleaning is 240 minus 70, which equals 170 minutes. Step 5: Check MSL handling. MSL 3 floor-life rules are governed by IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033, so the actual floor life must be taken from the component specification and the current exposure record. If the assembly has already used more than 170 minutes of floor life, re-baking or a shortened cleaning route is required. Step 6: Define verification before coating. Perform visual inspection for residue and trapped liquid, check drying completion, verify cleanliness with a suitable method such as ion chromatography or surface insulation resistance testing, and perform coating adhesion testing on a representative coupon or sacrificial assembly. Step 7: Define ESD controls. Handle the assembly in an ESD-controlled area consistent with ANSI/ESD S20.20, using grounded surfaces and ESD-safe containers during cleaning, drying, and coating."},"defect":{"symptom":"After conformal coating, localized coating lifting and white discoloration appear near through-hole connectors and under large SMT components, with occasional low surface insulation resistance readings.","cause":"Cleaning removed visible flux but left trapped aqueous rinse residue or surfactant film in through-holes and under components. The assembly was dried insufficiently before coating, and the cleaning process was not validated for trapped-liquid removal or coating adhesion.","action":"Stop coating release. Re-clean affected assemblies using a validated semi-aqueous or aqueous process with extended rinse and drying, then verify dryness and cleanliness before re-coating. Add through-hole drainage orientation, increased drying time, and post-clean surface insulation resistance or ion contamination checks. Update the cleaning process to include trapped-liquid inspection and coating adhesion qualification."},"takeaways":["Match the chemistry and the mechanical action to the residue, because a method chosen for convenience usually leaves the hardest fraction behind.","Choose the least aggressive method that meets the criterion, since aggressive cleaning trades contamination for material damage.","Validate cleaning for coating by adhesion, not by sight, because a wetting film that fails adhesion looks clean."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Cleaning everything with the strongest solvent available","instead":"Select by residue and compatibility, because solvents attack plastics, labels and coatings."},{"mistake":"Skipping drying control after aqueous cleaning","instead":"Dry barrels and under-component spaces, since trapped water becomes corrosion and coating lift."},{"mistake":"Ignoring floor life while the board sits in a cleaning line","instead":"Track exposure per IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033, because cleaning time counts against MSL."}],"terms":[{"term":"Semi-aqueous cleaning","def":"A process using solvent chemistry followed by an aqueous rinse."},{"term":"Cavitation","def":"The formation and collapse of vapour bubbles in a liquid that dislodges contamination."},{"term":"Saponifier","def":"An alkaline additive that turns flux residue into a water-soluble soap."},{"term":"EPA","def":"An ESD protected area, the controlled zone where sensitive assemblies are handled."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":25,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m12l01"],"skills":["Lesson m12l01: What residue is and why it matters: ionic residue, SIR, CAF, contact failure"]}},{"lessonId":"m12l03","moduleId":"m12","stageId":"coating-esd","title":"Cleanliness verification: ROSE, ion chromatography, particle count, visual","goals":[{"text":"Interpret cleanliness test results and decide pass or rework, stating the criterion.","competencies":["K1","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-002","src-013","src-018"],"quiz":["q-m12l03-01","q-m12l03-02","q-m12l03-03","q-m12l03-04","q-m12l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m12l03.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: ROSE extraction tank with conductivity meter, ion chromatography trace with species peaks, particle count on a filter membrane, visual residue halo, a flux residue photo, a write-criterion → test → pass / rework / retest flow and a report form","caption":"Cleanliness verification: ROSE, ion chromatography, particle count, visual and the reporting form"}]},{"h":"Cleanliness verification at the coating and ESD stage","p":["Cleanliness verification asks whether residues from soldering, cleaning, handling or coating can compromise adhesion, surface insulation resistance, corrosion resistance or ESD protection.","At this stage the board may be inspected before conformal coating, after cleaning, and again after coating where the coating is expected to protect an ESD path. The result is a comparison against a defined criterion, never a measurement on its own: a pass means the measured contamination is below the limit for the tested area and the visual condition is acceptable, and a fail means the area is reworked, retested or dispositioned by engineering.","Write the criterion before testing, and state the method, the sampled area, the reporting units and the action on failure."]},{"h":"Sampling and reporting","p":["A cleanliness result means something only when the sampled area, extraction method, blank correction and reporting units are known.","ROSE and ion chromatography usually normalise to area, such as micrograms of sodium chloride equivalent per square centimetre or micrograms of a specific ion per square centimetre, and the tested area must match the area the result represents. Test a small high-risk area and the result applies to that area, not to the whole board.","Blank correction removes what the solvent, container and handling contributed. For a rework decision, record method, area, instrument, date, operator, visible defects, whether the sample was destructive, whether the area was masked, and whether the result was averaged."]},{"h":"ROSE interpretation","p":["ROSE extracts residues into a solvent and measures conductivity or resistivity.","It is fast and useful for process control, and it does not identify the ion, does not measure non-ionic residue and can be disturbed by weakly ionic or volatile material. Compare the result with the limit in the customer or internal process specification: above the limit the tested area fails; below it the area may pass for ionic contamination while visual inspection and ion-specific testing are still required on a high-reliability, fine-pitch or to-be-coated assembly.","ROSE is most useful when the process is stable and the same solvent, temperature and extraction time are used. A shift without a process change sends you to the blank, the solvent, the cell calibration and the sampling technique."]},{"h":"Ion chromatography interpretation","p":["Ion chromatography separates and quantifies individual ions such as chloride, sulfate, nitrate, acetate and formate.","It is used when ROSE fails, when a specific flux chemistry is suspected, or when corrosion risk has to be understood. Interpretation needs individual ion limits and, where specified, a total ionic limit, because a total can pass while one ion fails and the reverse can also happen.","The laboratory reports detection limits, and a value below detection is not zero. For rework, the failed ion and the process that put it there guide the cleaning: chloride usually points to paste, water or handling, and organic ions point to flux residue. Ion chromatography is slower than ROSE, so it serves qualification, failure analysis and periodic verification rather than every board."]},{"h":"Particle count interpretation","p":["Particle count verifies that particulate contamination is controlled before coating and inside the ESD-controlled area.","Airborne counts are compared with cleanroom classes from the ISO cleanroom particle count standard, and surface counts come from wiping, flushing or a counter over a defined area.","The criterion states the particle size threshold, the area or volume sampled and the maximum count. A particle count proves nothing about chemical cleanliness: it shows only that particles above the selected size are inside the allowance. For coating, particles cause pinholes, poor adhesion or localised corrosion where they hold moisture; on ESD surfaces they interrupt coating continuity or create high-resistance spots. Sample location matters, because a cleanroom can pass while the board surface fails."]},{"h":"Visual inspection interpretation","p":["Visual inspection finds residue, flux staining, corrosion, handling marks, coating defects and particles large enough to see.","It runs under controlled lighting and magnification against the IPC visual acceptability standard or a customer-approved visual standard. A visual pass means no unacceptable residue or defect is present in the inspected area.","Visual inspection cannot find all ionic contamination, so it is combined with chemical tests where the risk is high. A visible defect fails the area even when chemical tests pass, unless engineering issues a documented deviation. Look under connectors, near stencil apertures and along board edges where cleaning solution struggles to reach, and keep photographs and annotated drawings for marginal results and retests."]},{"h":"Pass, rework and retest","p":["The pass decision uses the most restrictive applicable criterion: customer specification, internal specification or standard method guidance.","A common sequence is visual pass, ROSE pass, ion chromatography pass and particle count pass. When any test fails, rework the area with a validated cleaning process and retest the same or a representative area. On a marginal result, repeat the test and check sampling technique, blank and calibration before accepting.","For coating and ESD work, ask whether residue could be trapped under the coating or shift surface resistivity; if so, require ion-specific confirmation before coating. The disposition states accepted, reworked, scrapped or released under deviation, and the retest is compared with the original criterion, not with a relaxed limit chosen after the fact."]},{"h":"Where the acceptance numbers come from","p":["A cleanliness criterion is never invented at the bench: it comes from the customer specification, the reliability plan or a published reference.","The most widely used reference is 1.56 microgram NaCl equivalent per square centimetre, reported per IPC-TM-650 method 2.3.25 and historically applied to assembled boards through IPC J-STD-001 and to bare boards through IPC-6012. Many high-reliability customers tighten it: about 1.0 microgram per square centimetre for high-impedance analogue and telecom hardware, about 0.75 for aerospace and military, and about 0.5 for medical assemblies, with ion chromatography per IPC-TM-650 method 2.3.28 confirming the specific ions.","Two cautions follow. ROSE reports a conductivity equivalent rather than a named ion, so two boards with the same ROSE value can carry very different corrosion risk. And a limit taken from another product family is a starting point for discussion, not an acceptance criterion."]}],"calc":{"task":"A cleaned board area is tested before conformal coating. The internal cleanliness specification is: ROSE <= 0.25 microgram NaCl equivalent per square centimeter; individual ion <= 0.10 microgram per square centimeter; particles >= 5 micrometer <= 100 per square centimeter; visual inspection shows no visible residue. The ROSE extract measured 0.34 microgram NaCl equivalent per square centimeter, and the blank was 0.03 microgram NaCl equivalent per square centimeter. Ion chromatography reported chloride 0.12 microgram per square centimeter and sulfate 0.06 microgram per square centimeter. A surface particle sample over 10 square centimeters counted 1400 particles at or above 5 micrometers. Visual inspection found no visible residue. Decide pass or rework and state the criterion.","solution":"Correct ROSE for blank: 0.34 - 0.03 = 0.31 microgram NaCl equivalent per square centimeter. Compare ROSE to limit 0.25 microgram NaCl equivalent per square centimeter. 0.31 is above 0.25, so ROSE fails. Compare individual ions to limit 0.10 microgram per square centimeter. Chloride 0.12 is above 0.10, so ion chromatography fails. Sulfate 0.06 is below 0.10, so sulfate alone passes. Normalize particle count: 1400 particles divided by 10 square centimeters = 140 particles per square centimeter. Compare particle count to limit 100 particles per square centimeter. 140 is above 100, so particle count fails. Visual inspection passes because no visible residue was found. Overall decision is rework because the most restrictive criterion is not met: ROSE, ion chromatography, and particle count all exceed their limits. Rework with validated cleaning, then retest the same area and adjacent high-risk areas before coating."},"defect":{"symptom":"After conformal coating, localized corrosion and intermittent ESD path failures appear near a connector.","cause":"Residual ionic flux from wave solder was not removed; ROSE sampling averaged a large area and missed a local residue pocket, while particles trapped under coating increased moisture retention.","action":"Quarantine the lot, remove coating if possible, re-clean with validated ultrasonic or flush process, retest the connector area with ion chromatography and local ROSE, verify particle control, then recoat and retest."},"takeaways":["Write the criterion before the test, because a limit chosen after the result is a rationalisation.","Report the sampled area with the number, since a localised pocket disappears inside a whole-board average.","Treat a marginal result as a reason to check the method, not as a reason to relax the limit."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Comparing ROSE readings from different solvents and extraction times","instead":"Keep solvent, temperature and time fixed, because the value is only comparable within one method."},{"mistake":"Reading a below-detection ion result as zero","instead":"Treat it as below the reporting limit, since the laboratory cannot resolve less."},{"mistake":"Accepting a board because the chemical tests passed while a visible defect remains","instead":"Fail the area, because a documented deviation is the only route past it."}],"terms":[{"term":"Blank correction","def":"Subtracting contamination contributed by solvent, container and handling from the measured result."},{"term":"Ion chromatography","def":"A method that separates and quantifies individual ionic species in an extract."},{"term":"Particle count","def":"The number of particles above a stated size in a defined area or volume."},{"term":"Detection limit","def":"The smallest value an analytical method can report as distinguishable from background."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m12l02","m06l07"],"skills":["Lesson m12l02: Cleaning methods: aqueous, semi-aqueous, solvent, ultrasonic; material compatibility","Lesson m06l07: Profile validation, documentation and change control"]}},{"lessonId":"m12l04","moduleId":"m12","stageId":"coating-esd","title":"Coating types and selection: acrylic, urethane, silicone, parylene, UV cure","goals":[{"text":"Choose a coating and masking strategy and check its compatibility with cleaning, rework and the assembly.","competencies":["K1","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-009","src-010","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m12l04-01","q-m12l04-02","q-m12l04-03","q-m12l04-04","q-m12l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 960 330' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Conformal coating families compared: acrylic, urethane, silicone, parylene and UV cure, with typical dry-film thickness, moisture barrier, rework and application method'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;fill:#e2e8f0}.d{fill:#94a3b8}.t{fill:#53d1cc}.r{fill:#f44336}.o{fill:#ff9800}</style> <text x='20' y='24' font-size='13' class='t'>COATING FAMILIES — typical dry-film thickness (verify against datasheet and IPC-CC-830 qualification)</text> <rect x='20' y='50' width='920' height='36' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='#334155'/><text x='30' y='72' font-size='12' class='t'>acrylic</text><text x='150' y='72' font-size='11'>25-75 µm</text><text x='320' y='72' font-size='11'>moderate</text><text x='570' y='72' font-size='11'>easy (solvent/mech.)</text><text x='790' y='72' font-size='10' class='d'>brush/spray/dip/select.</text> <rect x='20' y='90' width='920' height='36' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='#334155'/><text x='30' y='112' font-size='12' class='t'>urethane</text><text x='150' y='112' font-size='11'>25-100 µm</text><text x='320' y='112' font-size='11'>good, chemical resistant</text><text x='570' y='112' font-size='11'>hard</text><text x='790' y='112' font-size='10' class='d'>spray, dip, selective</text> <rect x='20' y='130' width='920' height='36' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='#334155'/><text x='30' y='152' font-size='12' class='t'>silicone</text><text x='150' y='152' font-size='11'>25-100 µm</text><text x='320' y='152' font-size='11'>good, flexible</text><text x='570' y='152' font-size='11'>hard</text><text x='790' y='152' font-size='10' class='d'>spray, dip, selective</text> <rect x='20' y='170' width='920' height='36' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='#334155'/><text x='30' y='192' font-size='12' class='t'>parylene</text><text x='150' y='192' font-size='11'>1-25 µm (5-15 typ.)</text><text x='320' y='192' font-size='11'>excellent, pinhole-free</text><text x='570' y='192' font-size='11'>very hard</text><text x='790' y='192' font-size='10' class='d'>vapour deposition only</text> <rect x='20' y='210' width='920' height='36' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.06)' stroke='#334155'/><text x='30' y='232' font-size='12' class='t'>UV cure</text><text x='150' y='232' font-size='11'>25-75 µm</text><text x='320' y='232' font-size='11'>good</text><text x='570' y='232' font-size='11'>moderate; shadow cure</text><text x='790' y='232' font-size='10' class='d'>selective, fast cure</text> <text x='150' y='262' font-size='10' class='d'>thickness</text><text x='320' y='262' font-size='10' class='d'>moisture / chemical barrier</text><text x='570' y='262' font-size='10' class='d'>rework</text><text x='790' y='262' font-size='10' class='d'>application</text> <text x='20' y='292' font-size='11'>select by: environment → board constraints (height, BTCs, test points, connectors, rework frequency) → chemistry → masking plan</text> <text x='20' y='314' font-size='10' class='d'>Coverage/finish judged against IPC-A-610; rework per IPC-7711/7721.</text> </svg>","caption":"Coating families compared: typical dry-film thickness, barrier, rework and application method"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m12l04.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: five coating cross-sections acrylic, urethane, silicone, parylene and UV cure, each rated for moisture barrier and removability in words, with a macro photograph of a coated assembly under UV light","caption":"Coating types and selection: acrylic, urethane, silicone, parylene and UV cure; thicknesses in the figure above"}]},{"h":"Purpose of conformal coating","p":["Conformal coating goes on after assembly to cut moisture ingress, ionic contamination, dust, fungal growth and some mechanical abrasion.","In process engineering the choice of chemistry is the small part: coating also changes cleaning, masking, inspection, rework, test access, thermal behaviour and reliability under thermal cycling.","Drive the selection from the service environment, board density, component sensitivity, cleaning requirements and the rework strategy. A dense SMT board with bottom-terminated parts may need a low-viscosity coating and careful masking, while a through-hole power board may tolerate a thicker silicone or urethane film."]},{"h":"Acrylic coatings","p":["Acrylic coatings suit products where rework and repair access matter.","They come off easily by mechanical means or with compatible solvents, and they go on by brushing, spraying, dipping or selective coating. Typical dry-film thickness is often in the range of 25 to 75 micrometers, and the target is verified against the coating datasheet and the IPC-CC-830 qualification for the specific product.","Acrylics give good dielectric performance and reasonable moisture protection, with less resistance to solvents, fuels and aggressive chemicals than urethane or silicone. That makes them a common choice for consumer and industrial electronics with a rework path."]},{"h":"Urethane coatings","p":["Urethane coatings generally give better chemical and abrasion resistance than acrylic systems and suit industrial, automotive and outdoor electronics, in moisture-cure, heat-cure and UV-cure variants.","Typical dry-film thickness may be around 25 to 100 micrometers depending on product and application method, and the process window is verified against the coating datasheet and IPC-CC-830 test data.","The tougher film protects better and reworks harder: mechanical removal, localised solvent softening or thermal removal may be needed, and the method must be qualified so pads, solder mask and adjacent parts survive."]},{"h":"Silicone coatings","p":["Silicone coatings are chosen for high temperature, high humidity and applications that need flexibility.","They stay flexible over a wide temperature range and often take thermal cycling better than rigid coatings. Typical dry-film thickness is often around 25 to 100 micrometers, verified against the datasheet and IPC-CC-830 qualification.","Removal is mechanical or by selected solvents, and it can smear or leave residue that interferes with soldering. The larger process issue is silicone near connectors, switches, relays or gold-plated contacts, because silicone migration raises contact resistance and is hard to undo."]},{"h":"Parylene coatings","p":["Parylene is vapour-deposited and forms a very thin, uniform, pinhole-free film.","Typical thicknesses are often in the range of 1 to 25 micrometers, with many applications using 5 to 15 micrometers, checked against the coating specification and IPC-CC-830 or equivalent qualification data.","Because it arrives as a vapour, it covers complex geometry and high-aspect-ratio features without the bridging or pooling of liquid coatings, which suits dense SMT assemblies, medical electronics and products where added thickness is critical. It is hard to remove selectively, so mask before deposition rather than repair afterwards."]},{"h":"UV-cure coatings","p":["UV-cure coatings serve processes that need fast handling and short cycle times, applied by spray, jet or selective coating and cured by ultraviolet light.","Typical dry-film thickness may be around 25 to 75 micrometers, verified against the datasheet and IPC-CC-830 qualification.","The limitation is line of sight: areas under components and connectors, inside deep cavities or behind tall parts may receive too little UV energy to cure fully, leaving tacky or uncured material that attracts contamination and ruins adhesion. UV systems are often paired with moisture-cure or heat-cure chemistry to reach shadowed areas."]},{"h":"Masking strategy","p":["Masking protects connectors, test points, switches, heatsink interfaces, optical sensors, antennas and any area that must stay uncoated.","Methods include tape, caps, plugs, liquid mask and selective coating. The mask has to survive coating, cleaning and any rework temperature without lifting, bleeding or leaving residue.","For cleaning, the mask material and adhesive must suit the solvent or aqueous chemistry, because some adhesives bleed under solvent exposure. For rework, the coating must come off without damaging neighbours or leaving residue that stops solder wetting, and IPC-7711/7721 gives the rework guidance while IPC-A-610 gives the coverage and finish criteria the coated board is judged against. Following IPC-7711/7721 in the rework instruction is what keeps a coated assembly repairable at all."]},{"h":"Selection method","p":"A practical selection method starts with the environmental requirement: humidity, temperature, chemicals, dust, vibration, and expected service life. Next, define the board constraints: component height, bottom-terminated packages, test points, connectors, thermal interfaces, and rework frequency. Then match coating chemistry to those constraints. Acrylic is often selected when easy rework is needed. Urethane is often selected when chemical and abrasion resistance are higher priorities. Silicone is often selected for high temperature and flexibility. Parylene is often selected for thin, uniform coverage on dense assemblies. UV cure is often selected for fast throughput when line-of-sight cure is achievable. Finally, verify the selected coating against IPC-CC-830 qualification data, IPC-A-610 inspection requirements, IPC-7711/7721 rework guidance, and the assembly cleaning process. The final choice should be documented with coating type, target thickness, application method, cure method, masking method, inspection criteria, and rework method."}],"calc":{"task":"Select a coating and masking strategy for a dense SMT/THT control board that must operate in a humid industrial environment, be reworkable at the board level, and must not contaminate a nearby gold-plated edge connector. Estimate coating coverage volume and check whether the selected coating thickness is compatible with a 0.50 mm connector mating clearance.","solution":"Step 1: Define requirements. The board needs moisture protection, rework access, and connector protection. A urethane coating is selected for balanced moisture and chemical resistance, with acrylic selected as a backup if rework becomes difficult. Silicone is rejected near the gold-plated edge connector because silicone migration can increase contact resistance. Parylene is considered but rejected for this example because local rework is required and parylene removal is difficult. UV cure is considered but rejected as the primary coating because the board has tall connectors and shadowed areas.\n\nStep 2: Select target thickness. The coating datasheet and IPC-CC-830 qualification data are checked. A target dry-film thickness of 50 micrometers is selected, with an acceptable process range of 25 to 75 micrometers. This range is verified against the coating datasheet and IPC-A-610 inspection requirements for coating coverage and thickness.\n\nStep 3: Estimate board area. The coated area is estimated as 120 mm by 80 mm, or 9600 mm². The area is converted to square meters: 9600 mm² = 0.0096 m².\n\nStep 4: Estimate coating volume. Volume = area × thickness. For 50 micrometers: 50 µm = 0.000050 m. Volume = 0.0096 m² × 0.000050 m = 0.00000048 m³. Convert to milliliters: 0.00000048 m³ = 0.48 mL. This is the theoretical dry-film volume only; actual dispensed volume will be higher because of overspray, masking, and process losses.\n\nStep 5: Estimate wet volume. If the coating has 40 percent solids by volume, wet volume = dry volume / solids fraction = 0.48 mL / 0.40 = 1.20 mL. The 40 percent solids value is a practice-based example and must be verified against the coating datasheet.\n\nStep 6: Check connector clearance. The gold-plated edge connector has a mating clearance of 0.50 mm. The coating is masked from the connector contact area. If the coating edge is allowed to approach the connector, the coating thickness must not create a step that interferes with mating. A 50 micrometer coating is 0.050 mm, which is 10 percent of the 0.50 mm clearance. This is acceptable only if the coating edge is outside the mating interface and the mask prevents coating creep. The mask boundary is set 1.0 mm from the connector contact area to allow for process variation. The 1.0 mm margin is a process example and should be verified against the coating viscosity, application method, and IPC-A-610 inspection criteria.\n\nStep 7: Check rework compatibility. The urethane coating is tested on a sacrificial board using the planned rework method. Removal is checked against IPC-7711/7721 guidance for damage to pads, solder joints, and adjacent components. If the urethane cannot be removed without damaging nearby components, the process is changed to a localized acrylic coating or a masked urethane coating with a defined rework window.\n\nStep 8: Final selection. The selected strategy is urethane coating at 50 micrometers nominal, masked from the gold-plated edge connector with a 1.0 mm boundary margin, applied by selective coating, and qualified for rework according to IPC-7711/7721. The process is accepted only after coating thickness, adhesion, cure, masking boundary, and rework damage checks pass."},"defect":{"symptom":"After conformal coating and thermal cycling, a board fails ICT at a connector test point. Visual inspection shows a thin, clear coating film bridging between adjacent test pads, and the coating appears glossy and tacky under a shadowed connector.","cause":"The coating was applied too thickly near the test-point array, and the UV-cure step did not fully cure the shadowed area under the connector. The uncured or partially cured material remained tacky, attracted contamination, and allowed capillary flow into the pad gap. Masking was not sufficient to keep the coating away from the test-point spacing.","action":"Stop the lot and quarantine affected boards. Remove the coating from the affected area using a qualified rework method that does not damage the pads or adjacent components, following IPC-7711/7721 guidance. Re-clean the area and verify solderability and insulation resistance. Correct the process by reducing coating viscosity or application pressure, adding a mask or cap over the test-point array, and verifying UV energy dose in shadowed areas. Add a post-cure inspection step for shadowed regions and requalify the coating process against IPC-CC-830 and IPC-A-610 requirements."},"takeaways":["Choose the coating from the service environment and the rework strategy together, because a better barrier that cannot be opened is a liability.","Keep silicone away from gold contacts, since migration raises contact resistance permanently.","Mask before deposition rather than repairing after it, which matters most with parylene."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Selecting the most protective chemistry by default","instead":"Match protection to service and rework, because rework cost rises with every step up."},{"mistake":"Coating a board before cleaning is verified","instead":"Verify cleanliness first, since coating seals contamination against the surface."},{"mistake":"Assuming UV cure reaches under a component","instead":"Check line of sight, because shadowed material stays tacky and attracts dirt."}],"terms":[{"term":"Dry-film thickness","def":"The coating thickness remaining after solvents have evaporated and cure is complete."},{"term":"IPC-CC-830","def":"The qualification and performance standard for conformal coating materials."},{"term":"Silicone migration","def":"The transfer of low-molecular silicone to nearby surfaces, raising contact resistance."},{"term":"Shadowing (conformal coating cure)","def":"An area that line-of-sight cure cannot reach under a component or behind a tall part."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":25,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m12l03"],"skills":["Lesson m12l03: Cleanliness verification: ROSE, ion chromatography, particle count, visual"]}},{"lessonId":"m12l05","moduleId":"m12","stageId":"coating-esd","title":"Application and masking: spray, dip, selective, thickness, cure, repairability","goals":[{"text":"Choose a coating and masking strategy and check its compatibility with cleaning, rework and the assembly.","competencies":["K1","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-009","src-010","src-014","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m12l05-01","q-m12l05-02","q-m12l05-03","q-m12l05-04","q-m12l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m12l05.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: spray, dip and selective coating methods, masking with boots, tape and peelable mask, wet film comb gauge and dry film measurement, cure oven and UV lamp, a UV inspection photo and repairability options","caption":"Conformal coating application and masking: method, masking, thickness, cure and repairability"}]},{"h":"Selecting a coating for mixed assemblies","p":["Coating selection starts with the service environment and the thermal, chemical and electrical requirements of the assembly.","Acrylics are easy to remove and repair, urethane gives good abrasion and chemical resistance, silicone tolerates high temperature and humidity, epoxy gives strong mechanical protection at the cost of removability, and parylene gives thin uniform coverage at the cost of vacuum deposition and careful masking.","On a mixed SMT and through-hole board, verify that the coating does not attack flux residue, potting compound, connectors or test points. IPC-CC-830 is the qualification standard to check against."]},{"h":"Spray coating","p":["Spray coating is common at low and medium volume and on assemblies with complex topography.","Typical spray viscosity is adjusted to the equipment range, often around 10 to 100 mPa·s for air-assisted systems, with the exact value taken from the coating supplier's process window and the spray equipment manual.","Atomisation pressure, nozzle size, travel speed and distance set the film thickness and the overspray. On mixed assemblies, keep high pressure away from small components, exposed vias, connectors and test pads. Measure wet film with a comb or gauge, then confirm dry film after cure; target values are commonly 25 to 130 µm depending on the coating class and IPC-CC-830 qualification, but the final range should match the qualified coating system."]},{"h":"Dip coating","p":["Dip coating gives good coverage on through-hole assemblies and boards with open component spacing.","The board is immersed and withdrawn at a controlled rate, commonly 100 to 300 mm/min, with dwell adjusted so the film drains without bridging. Viscosity, temperature and withdrawal speed set the wet film thickness; a typical process may aim for 50 to 150 µm dry film thickness, verified against the coating datasheet and IPC-CC-830 qualification.","Dip coating traps solvent under large components and bottom-terminated packages, so preheat, dwell and ventilation are controlled to limit voids and skips."]},{"h":"Selective coating","p":"Selective coating is used when only part of the assembly requires protection, such as high-voltage areas, exposed copper, or moisture-sensitive regions, while connectors, test points, heatsinks, and mechanical interfaces must remain uncoated. The process uses programmed dispensing or jetting to place coating only on defined areas. Key controls are nozzle path, coating volume, cure schedule, and edge definition. For SMT/THT boards, selective coating reduces masking labor and improves repair access, but it requires good CAD or Gerber data and verification of component height, pin spacing, and connector sealing. Check that the coating does not creep into connector contacts or under component terminations where IPC-A-610 class requirements for cleanliness and solder joint visibility apply."},{"h":"Masking and cleaning compatibility","p":"Masking protects areas that must remain uncoated, such as connectors, test pads, heatsink interfaces, potting areas, and repair zones. Common masks include high-temperature tape, silicone caps, liquid photoimageable masks, and removable wax or polymer masks. Mask selection depends on coating solvent, cure temperature, dwell time, and cleaning chemistry. For example, solvent-based coatings may attack some tapes, while high-temperature cure may lift adhesive or leave residue. Verify mask compatibility by running a coupon through the full coating, cure, and cleaning sequence. If cleaning is required after coating, confirm that the cleaning solvent does not swell, soften, or craze the coating; IPC-CC-830 includes chemical resistance testing, but production cleaning should still be validated on the actual assembly."},{"h":"Thickness control and cure verification","p":["Dry film thickness drives insulation, mechanical protection, thermal behaviour and repairability.","Too thin a coating may not meet IPC-CC-830 qualification or IPC-A-610 acceptability for the intended class; too thick a coating traps solvent, cracks in thermal cycling or interferes with connectors and test probes.","Measure wet film before cure and dry film after, on flat areas, at edges and near components. Verify cure by the supplier's method: solvent rub, hardness, adhesion, or FTIR conversion where applicable. On heat-sensitive assemblies the cure temperature is the constraint that decides the chemistry."]},{"h":"Repairability and rework","p":["Repairability depends on the removal method, the access to the joints and the thermal exposure during rework.","Acrylics come off with solvent or mechanical action, while urethane, silicone and epoxy may need stronger solvents, abrasion or thermal assistance, and parylene may need plasma or specialist removal.","For rework and repair, follow IPC-7711 and IPC-7721 for acceptable methods, including preheat, temperature control and post-rework inspection. Leave enough uncoated area for probe access, soldering and inspection."]},{"h":"Compatibility with the assembly flow","p":["A coating strategy has to survive the whole flow: soldering, cleaning, inspection, functional test and repair.","Residual flux, ionic contamination, moisture or dust under the coating cause corrosion, insulation failure or adhesion loss, so cleaning before coating is validated against laminate, solder mask and component materials, and the board is dry before coating.","After coating, inspect for pinholes, bridging, voids, peeling, contamination and coverage of exposed copper, using IPC-A-610 acceptance criteria at the applicable class. At connectors and test points, confirm the masking held."]}],"calc":{"task":"A mixed SMT/THT board is coated by spray with a target dry film thickness of 75 µm. The coating has a volume solids content of 45% by volume. The wet film thickness measured on a coupon is 165 µm. Determine whether the wet film thickness is reasonable for the target dry film thickness, and calculate the expected dry film thickness from the measured wet film thickness.","solution":"Step 1: Use the relationship between wet film thickness, dry film thickness, and volume solids: DFT = WFT × volume solids fraction. Step 2: Convert volume solids to a fraction: 45% = 0.45. Step 3: Calculate expected dry film thickness: DFT = 165 µm × 0.45 = 74.25 µm. Step 4: Compare with target: 74.25 µm is within a practical tolerance of the 75 µm target, assuming the process tolerance allows approximately ±10 µm or the tolerance specified by the coating supplier. Step 5: Verify the result on the actual assembly after cure, because solvent evaporation, drainage, and surface roughness can change the final thickness. If the qualified IPC-CC-830 coating system or IPC-A-610 inspection class requires a different thickness range, adjust the spray parameters and re-measure."},"defect":{"symptom":"After conformal coating and cure, a connector area shows white residue and intermittent contact resistance. The coating appears to have crept under the connector housing and left a soft film on the contacts.","cause":"The masking or selective coating boundary was too close to the connector, the coating viscosity was too low, or the spray/dispense volume was too high, allowing capillary creep into the connector. The cleaning solvent used after coating may also have softened the coating and redistributed it onto the contacts.","action":"Stop the affected lot and inspect other boards for coating creep. Remove the assembly from the line and clean or replace affected connectors according to IPC-7711 and IPC-7721 rework and repair guidance. Requalify the masking layout or selective coating path with a larger keep-out margin, reduce dispense volume or spray pressure, and verify coating creep on a connector test coupon. If cleaning is required, confirm solvent compatibility with the coating and connector materials before returning to production."},"takeaways":["Pick the chemistry from the service environment and the rework method together, because a coating that cannot be opened turns a repair into a scrap.","Measure wet film before cure and dry film after, since thickness is the property the specifications judge.","Prove mask and coating compatibility on a coupon, because the full sequence reveals attacks a single step hides."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Spraying at high pressure over small components and vias","instead":"Reduce pressure near fine features, because the jet drives coating into places masking cannot protect."},{"mistake":"Coating a board that is not fully dry","instead":"Dry first, since trapped moisture blisters the film and corrodes under it."},{"mistake":"Assuming cure from the schedule alone","instead":"Verify cure by the supplier's method, because shadowed or cool areas stay undercured."}],"terms":[{"term":"Wet film thickness","def":"The coating thickness measured immediately after application, before solvent loss."},{"term":"Volume solids","def":"The fraction of the wet coating that remains as dry film after cure."},{"term":"Keep-out","def":"An area that must stay uncoated, defined in the coating program or mask layout."},{"term":"FTIR conversion","def":"A spectroscopic check of how completely the coating has cured."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m12l04"],"skills":["Lesson m12l04: Coating types and selection: acrylic, urethane, silicone, parylene, UV cure"]}},{"lessonId":"m12l06","moduleId":"m12","stageId":"coating-esd","title":"Contamination and coating defect cases: residue, white residue, corrosion, fish-eye, dewetting","goals":[{"text":"Interpret cleanliness test results and decide pass or rework, stating the criterion.","competencies":["K1","K6"]},{"text":"Choose a coating and masking strategy and check its compatibility with cleaning, rework and the assembly.","competencies":["K1","K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m12l06-01","q-m12l06-02","q-m12l06-03","q-m12l06-04","q-m12l06-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m12l06.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: magnified tiles for sound coating, flux residue, white residue, corrosion, fish-eye and dewetting with cross-sections, a cause-to-test table, a fish-eye photo under UV light and the decision to set the criterion from defect risk","caption":"Contamination and coating defect cases: appearance, likely source and the test that sees it"}]},{"h":"Cleanliness testing as a decision tool","p":["A cleanliness result is read against the defect risk of the assembly, not only against a generic limit.","On coated electronics the real question is whether residue, ionic contamination, particulate, flux or masking material can cause corrosion, insulation failure, solderability loss, coating defects or an ESD handling problem. A result that is acceptable on a low-density through-hole board can be unacceptable on fine-pitch SMT, bottom-terminated components or high-impedance analogue circuits.","The pass or rework criterion therefore combines the applicable standard, the customer requirement and the process capability behind it."]},{"h":"What the tests measure","p":["Visual inspection under magnification finds visible residue, flux, solder balls, masking bleed and particulate.","Solvent extraction conductivity or ion chromatography estimates ionic contamination, reported as sodium chloride equivalent or as individual ions such as chloride, sulfate, nitrate and bromide. Surface insulation resistance measures electrical leakage and can reveal contamination that no eye will find.","Cleanliness patch tests, ROSE-type, give a rapid ionic estimate and can miss non-ionic residue, rosin, polymers and masking films, so no single test answers the whole question."]},{"h":"Choosing a pass or rework criterion","p":["A practical criterion states the test method, the area sampled, the acceptance limit, the inspection magnification and the action when the limit is exceeded.","For example, a board may pass if no visible residue is present at the magnification IPC-A-610 requires for the product class and if ionic contamination is below the limit agreed for that assembly and coating system.","On a marginal result, rework is usually better than retest where the residue type is known to interfere with coating adhesion or wetting. Where the result is clean but the coating shows fish-eye, the contamination lives on the surface or in the coating itself, not in the extract."]},{"h":"Residue types and coating consequences","p":["Flux residue survives under components, in thermal relief pads and under bottom-terminated packages.","Rosin-based residue resists aqueous cleaning and causes poor wetting or coating adhesion. No-clean residue may be electrically acceptable in some designs and still create coating defects when the coating solvent reactivates or lifts it.","Masking tape adhesive, silicone release agents and cured masking material leave low-surface-energy films that cause fish-eye or dewetting, and particulate from paste, cutting, grinding or handling creates coating defects and local corrosion sites."]},{"h":"White residue and corrosion","p":["White residue often appears after cleaning, drying or humidity exposure, and it may be a salt, a flux reaction product, a mineral deposit or a corrosion product.","Where the residue is conductive or hygroscopic it lowers insulation resistance and promotes corrosion under the coating. Corrosion may start as discolouration, green or white deposits, lifted metallisation, or an intermittent electrical fault.","A coating hides the early stage, which is why pre-coating cleanliness and drying matter so much. White residue found after coating sends you back to whether it formed before coating, migrated through, or came from the coating."]},{"h":"Fish-eye and dewetting","p":["Fish-eye appears as small circular areas where the coating fails to wet the substrate; dewetting is the coating pulling back and leaving thin or uncovered regions.","Both usually trace to low-surface-energy contamination: silicone, mould release, adhesive residue, skin oils or certain masking materials. They also occur when substrate surface energy is too low for the coating, or when viscosity, solvent balance or drying schedule is wrong.","The first corrective action is to find the contamination source and remove it, then verify by coating a coupon of the same lot."]},{"h":"Coating and masking strategy","p":["Choose the coating from the protection required: acrylic where rework matters, urethane for chemical and moisture resistance, silicone for thermal cycling and humidity, epoxy for hard chemical resistance where rework is rare, and parylene for pinhole-free coverage at the cost of vacuum deposition and special masking.","Masking protects connectors, test points, heatsink interfaces, optical surfaces and mounting areas, and must survive cleaning, cure and assembly without bleeding, lifting or leaving residue."]},{"h":"Cleaning, rework and assembly checks","p":["Before releasing a coating and masking plan, confirm three things: cleaning does not attack the mask, the mask leaves no residue after removal, and the coating does not swell or soften in the rework solvent.","For rework, confirm that the coating can be removed locally without lifting adjacent coating or damaging component bodies. For assembly, confirm that coated surfaces do not foul press-fit connectors, screw threads, thermal pads, snap fits or grounding contacts.","Where an area must stay conductive, mask it or use a selected removal method, and verify continuity afterwards."]},{"h":"ESD in coating and masking","p":["Conformal coating changes surface resistance and can affect handling of ESD-sensitive assemblies.","Some coatings insulate enough to let charge accumulate on the surface unless the assembly is handled inside the ESD program. Masking tapes can generate charge when they are peeled.","For ESD-sensitive assemblies, verify the surface resistance of the coated area and of the handling path, use ESD-safe masking and removal procedures, and where the design needs dissipative behaviour, confirm that the coating and mask do not leave isolated insulating islands near sensitive devices."]}],"calc":{"task":"A fine-pitch SMT board is cleaned, inspected, and tested before conformal coating. Visual inspection at 10x finds no visible residue. A solvent extraction cleanliness test reports 18 micrograms per square centimeter sodium chloride equivalent. The agreed acceptance limit for this product class and coating system is 15 micrograms per square centimeter sodium chloride equivalent. The board also shows two small fish-eye defects on a nearby test board coated with the same lot. Decide whether the board passes or requires rework, state the criterion, and calculate the percentage exceedance of the cleanliness limit.","solution":"1. State the pass/rework criterion: the board passes only if visual inspection at the required magnification shows no visible residue and the ionic cleanliness result is at or below the agreed limit of 15 micrograms per square centimeter sodium chloride equivalent. 2. Compare the measured cleanliness result with the limit: measured value is 18 micrograms per square centimeter, limit is 15 micrograms per square centimeter. 3. Calculate exceedance: 18 minus 15 equals 3 micrograms per square centimeter. 4. Calculate percentage exceedance: 3 divided by 15 equals 0.20, which is 20 percent above the limit. 5. Decision: the board fails the cleanliness criterion and requires rework or additional cleaning, followed by retest. 6. Additional action: because fish-eye defects appeared on a test board from the same coating lot, do not recoat immediately. Investigate low-surface-energy contamination, masking residue, silicone sources, and coating lot compatibility before cleaning, retesting, and recoating."},"defect":{"symptom":"After conformal coating and cure, small circular uncovered areas appear around several QFN pads, and a white powdery residue is visible near a connector after humidity exposure.","cause":"The fish-eye areas are consistent with low-surface-energy contamination, likely masking adhesive residue or silicone transfer, preventing coating wetting. The white residue near the connector is consistent with ionic contamination or flux reaction product that was not fully removed before coating, then migrated or absorbed moisture through the coating edge.","action":"Stop coating release for the affected lot. Remove the coating locally or fully if needed, clean the board with a validated cleaning process, retest cleanliness using visual inspection and ionic or surface insulation resistance testing, and verify surface energy or coating adhesion on a test coupon. Replace or qualify the masking material if adhesive residue is found, control silicone sources in the area, and rework only after the cleanliness and coating wetting criteria are met."},"takeaways":["Set the criterion from the assembly's defect risk, because the same reading passes one board and fails another.","Test for the contaminant you actually fear, since a patch test cannot see a non-ionic film.","Chase fish-eye to its source on the surface, because the extract can read clean while the coating refuses to wet."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Accepting a board on the extract value alone","instead":"Inspect and coat a coupon, since adhesion is the property that fails."},{"mistake":"Reworking without finding the residue source","instead":"Identify the process that left it, because cleaning the same way will leave it again."},{"mistake":"Peeling ESD-safe masking tape without grounding yourself","instead":"Handle inside the ESD program, since peeling generates charge."}],"terms":[{"term":"Fish-eye","def":"A circular area where coating fails to wet the surface, leaving bare substrate."},{"term":"Dewetting (conformal coating)","def":"Coating pulling back from an area and leaving it thinly covered or uncovered."},{"term":"Patch test","def":"A rapid extraction test that estimates ionic contamination over a defined area."},{"term":"Dissipative","def":"A surface resistance range that bleeds charge away slowly enough to protect sensitive devices."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m12l05"],"skills":["Lesson m12l05: Application and masking: spray, dip, selective, thickness, cure, repairability"]}},{"lessonId":"m13l01","moduleId":"m13","stageId":"test","title":"Test levels and what each catches: ICT, flying probe, boundary scan, functional","goals":[{"text":"Compare ICT, flying probe, boundary scan and functional test for a given assembly and choose a strategy with reasons.","competencies":["K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-011"],"quiz":["q-m13l01-01","q-m13l01-02","q-m13l01-03","q-m13l01-04","q-m13l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"Test level starts with defect modes and access","p":["A test strategy is a coverage decision, not a machine preference.","The main defect modes are opens, shorts, wrong or missing components, wrong polarity, solder defects, damaged traces and assembly errors that affect function. The chosen level catches defects near their point of occurrence, gives repairable diagnostics and fits the production volume.","Access, netlist quality and component mix usually decide whether a bed-of-nails fixture, probe access, scan chains or functional stimulus is practical."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 900 280' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Test strategy pyramid'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace}</style><polygon points='450,40 590,110 310,110' fill='rgba(244,67,54,.15)' stroke='#f44336' stroke-width='1.5'/><polygon points='450,40 690,180 210,180' fill='none' stroke='#ff9800' stroke-width='1.5'/><polygon points='450,40 800,250 100,250' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='450' y='92' fill='#f44336' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>BURN-IN / FIELD</text><text x='450' y='152' fill='#ff9800' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>FUNCTIONAL TEST (FCT)</text><text x='450' y='222' fill='#53d1cc' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>ICT / BOUNDARY SCAN — HIGH COVERAGE, LOW COST PER TEST</text><text x='60' y='24' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' font-weight='600'>TEST STRATEGY PYRAMID</text><text x='60' y='272' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12'>COST PER DEFECT CAUGHT RISES AS YOU GO UP</text></svg>","caption":"Test pyramid: cheapest coverage first, most expensive last"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m13l01.webp","alt":"A test pyramid with the four levels, each annotated with the defect modes it catches, its relative cost and its coverage","caption":"Test levels and what each catches: in-circuit test, flying probe, boundary scan and functional test"},{"t":"table","caption":"Test method comparison","head":["Method","Catches","Cost per board","Coverage"],"rows":[["ICT / boundary scan","Opens, shorts, wrong/rotated parts, some values","Low","High on SMT nodes"],["Functional test (FCT)","Assembly-level behavior","Medium","Circuit-dependent"],["Burn-in / field return","Infant mortality, marginal parts","High","Statistical only"]]}]},{"h":"In-circuit test catches structural faults","p":["In-circuit test drives a bed-of-nails fixture onto the test points and measures electrical structure in ohm, farad and volt.","It finds opens, shorts, wrong passive values, missing components and some polarity errors, which makes it strong on high-volume assemblies with good test-point access and a stable netlist.","It misses faults hidden under devices and in nets without access, and it is disturbed by guard circuits, power paths and component tolerance. Fixture cost and cycle time go into the choice as well, checked against the equipment datasheet."]},{"h":"Flying probe is flexible structural test","p":["Flying probe moves its probes to pads, vias or test points without a dedicated fixture.","That makes it the practical option for prototypes, low-volume builds and boards where fixture cost cannot be justified. It finds opens, shorts and some component errors, at the cost of longer cycle time and access limited by probe reach, pad size and obstruction.","It is usually paired with functional test when the structural coverage it delivers is incomplete."]},{"h":"Boundary scan tests scan-capable interconnects","p":["Boundary scan uses JTAG cells inside compliant devices to drive and observe pins while the board is powered.","It detects opens, shorts and some solder defects between scan-capable devices, including fine-pitch and ball-grid-array connections that are hard to probe. It does not measure passive values or power delivery, and it cannot see non-scan devices unless they are observable through scan logic.","Coverage depends on device support, scan chain integrity, and whether the netlist carries the relevant pins."]},{"h":"Functional test proves behavior under intended conditions","p":["Functional test applies inputs and checks outputs, timing in second, power in volt and ampere, communication behaviour and thermal behaviour in degree Celsius.","It catches the assembly errors that stop the board working: wrong firmware, connector faults, power problems and some component mismatches.","It misses latent defects, marginal joints and wrong values that do not change behaviour under the test conditions. It is a good final screen, though diagnosis gets harder when the fault is intermittent or outside the exercised path."]},{"h":"Coverage tradeoffs between structural and functional levels","p":["Structural tests locate a defect at the point where it occurs, and functional tests prove that the system behaves.","A structural test can pass a board that fails functionally, because it does not exercise dynamic loading, timing or analogue performance. A functional test can pass a board with a latent defect, because the fault is never excited.","A practical strategy combines structural coverage for manufacturing defects with functional coverage for operational requirements, and states which defect classes each level owns."]},{"h":"Choosing a strategy with reasons","p":["Rank defect risk, access, volume and repair data, then choose.","High-volume dense assemblies with good test points usually take in-circuit test plus functional test. Low-volume and prototype builds take flying probe plus functional, which avoids fixture cost. Dense digital assemblies with strong JTAG support take boundary scan plus functional to cover the hard-to-probe nets.","Mixed-signal and power assemblies need power-path checks in the structural level and load plus thermal conditions in the functional level. Check coverage against the probe and equipment datasheet before committing the line."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 1200-component assembly has 18% BGA, 40% boundary-scan coverage, 2000 units/month, and 30% of nets lack test points. Choose a test strategy and justify it.","solution":"First, map defect risk: BGA and fine-pitch solder defects are high, so structural coverage is needed. Second, compare access: 30% of nets lack test points, so in-circuit test alone leaves gaps. Third, use boundary scan for the 40% scan-covered digital nets, especially BGA connections. Fourth, use flying probe or in-circuit test for accessible passives and power nets, depending on volume. Fifth, add functional test to verify power in volt and ampere, timing in second, and thermal behavior in degree Celsius. Sixth, choose in-circuit test plus boundary scan plus functional for 2000 units/month if fixture cost is justified; choose flying probe plus boundary scan plus functional for lower volume. Verify coverage against your paste, probe, and equipment datasheet."},"defect":{"symptom":"Boundary scan reports an open on a BGA net, but functional test passes under nominal conditions.","cause":"A marginal solder joint or cracked BGA connection may remain conductive when the pin is not exercised under load, thermal stress, or vibration.","action":"Re-run boundary scan with known good boards, inspect with X-ray or cross-section if available, exercise the net under load and temperature, and rework or reject the board if the fault persists."},"takeaways":["Use structural test to locate and functional test to prove, because each answers a question the other cannot.","Read a coverage number with its access limit, since a net without a test point has no in-circuit coverage however dense the fixture.","Add functional load and temperature where the product will see them, because nominal conditions hide marginal joints."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Claiming full structural coverage from a fixture with missing test points","instead":"Subtract the inaccessible nets, because a bed of nails only touches what it can reach."},{"mistake":"Treating a functional pass as proof of a sound joint","instead":"Add boundary scan or X-ray on hidden joints, since a marginal connection can conduct under no load."},{"mistake":"Buying a fixture for a prototype run","instead":"Use flying probe, because fixture cost needs volume to spread over."}],"terms":[{"term":"Bed-of-nails","def":"A fixture with spring-loaded probes that contact all test points at once."},{"term":"Guard circuit","def":"A driven node that isolates a component so its value can be measured in circuit."},{"term":"Boundary scan","def":"A test architecture that drives and observes device pins through internal scan cells."},{"term":"Latent defect","def":"A fault that is present but does not show under the test conditions applied."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m07l06"],"skills":["Lesson m07l06: Classification drill: print, placement, reflow and THT images"]}},{"lessonId":"m13l02","moduleId":"m13","stageId":"test","title":"In-circuit test: fixture, node access, test-point design, analog limits","goals":[{"text":"Compare ICT, flying probe, boundary scan and functional test for a given assembly and choose a strategy with reasons.","competencies":["K6"]},{"text":"Specify design-for-test requirements as design requests with rationale.","competencies":["K6","K10"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-011"],"quiz":["q-m13l02-01","q-m13l02-02","q-m13l02-03","q-m13l02-04","q-m13l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m13l02.webp","alt":"A bed-of-nails fixture with spring probes contacting test points, a node access diagram and an analog measurement panel","caption":"In-circuit test: fixture, node access, test-point design and analog limits"}]},{"h":"What in-circuit test measures","p":"In-circuit test uses spring probes to contact test points and measure analog electrical parameters such as resistance, capacitance, inductance, diode junctions, transistor junctions, open nets, and shorted nets. The fixture holds the board, aligns probes, applies controlled force, and isolates nodes from adjacent circuitry. A good fixture reduces false failures by controlling contact resistance, guarding, shielding, board support, and thermal drift. For dense assemblies, fixture complexity grows with net count, guarded analog nodes, fine-pitch access, and mixed power domains. Verify probe force and contact resistance against your probe and equipment datasheet; a common production target is contact resistance below 100 mΩ, but acceptance limits should be set from golden-board data and equipment repeatability."},{"h":"Comparing ICT, flying probe, boundary scan and functional","p":["In-circuit test gives high throughput and strong component-level coverage where test points exist, at the price of a fixture that low volume or frequent design changes cannot justify.","Flying probe avoids the dedicated fixture and suits prototypes, small lots and boards with limited access, and it is slower with less high-speed and guarded analogue capability. Boundary scan tests interconnects and selected device pins through JTAG, which makes it valuable on BGA, QFN and fine-pitch devices that no probe can reach.","Functional test proves system behaviour, including the analogue performance, power sequencing, RF and firmware interaction the other three cannot verify."]},{"h":"Choosing a strategy for an assembly","p":["Choose in-circuit test when volume justifies the fixture, test-point access is adequate and component-level analogue coverage is needed.","Choose flying probe when volume is low, the design changes often, or fixture cost cannot be justified. Add boundary scan when BGA, QFN or fine-pitch devices create inaccessible nets and the devices support scan. Add functional test when analogue performance, power sequencing, RF behaviour, firmware interaction or system timing falls outside component-level coverage."]},{"h":"Fixture and node access","p":["Fixture design accounts for probe pitch, tip diameter, board support, alignment pins, hold-down method, thermal effects and guard shielding.","Spring probes need annular clearance around the test point so the tip lands cleanly without shorting adjacent copper. Probe force must break minor contamination without damaging the pad; typical spring-probe force ranges are often around 0.5 N to 1.5 N, and a common production target is contact resistance below 100 mΩ, with acceptance limits set from golden-board data and equipment repeatability."]},{"h":"Test-point design requests","p":["Ask for a test point on every net where practical, with clear naming and netlist correlation.","For through-hole or large SMT test points, a diameter of 1.0 mm to 1.5 mm and spacing of 2.54 mm to 3.81 mm are common starting points, verified against probe pitch and the paste process. For fine-pitch access, via test points may be used at a diameter of 0.3 mm to 0.6 mm with an annular ring of 0.15 mm to 0.3 mm, subject to probe tip size and drill capability.","Test points also have to survive assembly: mask clearance, no paste on the pad, and enough copper to take repeat probing."]},{"h":"Analog limits and measurement integrity","p":["Derive analogue limits from component tolerance, fixture residual, leakage, temperature, solder effects and measurement repeatability.","Use four-wire measurement for low-resistance checks and guard high-impedance nodes to cut leakage error. Diode junction tests often use small currents, for example 50 µA to 100 µA, and low voltages, for example 0.1 V to 0.5 V, so the test does not forward bias an adjacent junction or disturb the circuit.","Power-rail tests need controlled voltages and current limits, so a wrong rail clamp does not damage the board it is testing. A common starting guard band is around ±5% of nominal, confirmed against production variation. Temperature also moves junction and resistor measurements, so limit development often runs inside a controlled window such as 25 °C to 40 °C."]},{"h":"DFT design requests","p":["Issue design-for-test requests early, before layout freeze, and include a netlist with the test-point list, the boundary-scan chain definition, the analogue net classification, the power-domain map, test access points, keep-out zones, fiducials and test-point naming rules.","The rationale is fewer fixture iterations, better coverage, fewer false failures and faster debug. Name the guarded analogue nodes so guard rings and test limits can be planned, and flag inaccessible nets before they are designed in rather than after."]},{"h":"Practical acceptance and debug","p":["Validate the fixture with golden boards before production release: contact resistance, open and short coverage, analogue limits, and repeatability across boards and fixture cycles.","Track failures by test point, net, component and probe location. When failures cluster, check probe wear, board coplanarity, solder mask residue, fixture alignment and guard integrity.","Then use boundary scan and functional test to separate a fixture access problem from a solder joint problem."]}],"calc":{"task":"A multilayer assembly has 1,200 nets, several BGA packages, and annual volume 25,000. A design review finds that 82% of nets can be accessed with a single test point, 12% require paired test points for guarded analog nodes, and 6% are inaccessible. The planned test-point diameter is 1.2 mm, the probe tip diameter is 0.9 mm, and the required annular clearance is 0.15 mm. The available fixture has a 1,500 probe limit. Calculate accessible test points, total test points, minimum pad diameter, and choose a test strategy with reasons.","solution":"Step 1: Calculate single-point accessible nets. 1,200 nets × 82% = 984 nets. Step 2: Calculate guarded nets requiring paired test points. 1,200 nets × 12% = 144 nets. Step 3: Double the guarded net count to get test points for those nets. 144 nets × paired test points = 288 test points. Step 4: Calculate inaccessible nets. 1,200 nets × 6% = 72 nets. Step 5: Calculate total test points. 984 single-point test points + 288 paired test points = 1,272 test points. Step 6: Calculate minimum pad diameter. Minimum pad diameter = probe tip diameter + clearance + clearance = 0.9 mm + 0.15 mm + 0.15 mm = 1.2 mm. Step 7: Compare total test points with fixture limit. 1,500 probe limit - 1,272 test points = 228 probes of margin. Step 8: Choose strategy. Use ICT as the primary production test because annual volume 25,000 supports fixture cost, total test points fit within the 1,500 probe limit, and component-level analog coverage is needed. Add boundary scan for inaccessible nets and BGA interconnect coverage. Add functional test for analog performance, power sequencing, and system-level acceptance. Flying probe is not the primary strategy because volume is high enough to justify ICT, but it may be useful for early prototypes or fixture debug."},"defect":{"symptom":"ICT reports intermittent opens on guarded analog nets after extended production, with failures moving between adjacent test points and contact resistance rising above 100 mΩ.","cause":"Spring probe tip wear, solder mask residue, or fixture alignment drift reduces effective contact area. Guarded high-impedance nodes are sensitive to small increases in contact resistance and leakage, so the measurement shifts into the open or out-of-limit region.","action":"Stop production release, inspect and replace worn probes, clean the fixture and board test points, verify alignment with a golden board, and confirm contact resistance below the accepted limit. Re-run limit development with golden-board data, then review test-point placement and guard design for the next fixture revision."},"takeaways":["Size the fixture against volume, because a bed of nails on a low-volume build spends money to add cycle time.","Derive analogue limits from measured repeatability, since a limit tighter than the fixture resolves creates false failures.","Validate the fixture on golden boards before release, because probe wear and alignment drift appear as product defects."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Probing a test point with no mask clearance","instead":"Design clearance around the pad, since a short to adjacent copper reads as a fault."},{"mistake":"Setting limits from component tolerance alone","instead":"Include fixture residual and repeatability, because the measurement chain has its own spread."},{"mistake":"Chasing clustered failures in the solder process first","instead":"Check probe wear and alignment, because clustering follows the fixture as often as the board."}],"terms":[{"term":"Golden board","def":"A known-good assembly used to validate the fixture and develop limits."},{"term":"Contact resistance","def":"The resistance added by the probe-to-pad interface, which must stay low and stable."},{"term":"Guard","def":"A driven node that isolates a component or net so it can be measured in circuit."},{"term":"Test access point","def":"A pad or via reserved for probing, sized for the probe tip and clearance."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":25,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m13l01"],"skills":["Lesson m13l01: Test levels and what each catches: ICT, flying probe, boundary scan, functional"]}},{"lessonId":"m13l03","moduleId":"m13","stageId":"test","title":"Flying probe: when it wins, throughput ceiling, program effort","goals":[{"text":"Compare ICT, flying probe, boundary scan and functional test for a given assembly and choose a strategy with reasons.","competencies":["K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-011","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m13l03-01","q-m13l03-02","q-m13l03-03","q-m13l03-04","q-m13l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m13l03.webp","alt":"A flying-probe machine with movable probes contacting a board, beside a throughput-versus-volume chart and a program effort gauge","caption":"Flying probe: when it wins, the throughput ceiling and program effort"}]},{"h":"What flying probe is and when it wins","p":["Flying probe is a test method in which movable probes contact test points sequentially or in parallel.","It wins when fixture cost and lead time dominate, when volume is low to medium, when the design changes often, or when component density makes a bed-of-nails fixture impractical.","It does not replace every in-circuit or functional test: it is a strategy choice made on coverage, throughput, program effort and design maturity."]},{"h":"Comparison with ICT, boundary scan and functional test","p":["In-circuit test uses a bed-of-nails fixture and can isolate components for shorts, opens, resistance, capacitance, inductance, diodes and some powered tests. It gives high throughput and good analogue coverage where test points exist, and fixture cost plus access are its limits.","Flying probe applies the same electrical principles without a dedicated fixture, so it is flexible, while probe movement sets a throughput ceiling. Boundary scan uses JTAG or similar chains to control and observe device pins.","Functional test sits above all three and proves that the assembly behaves as designed."]},{"h":"When flying probe is the better strategy","p":["Choose flying probe when volume is low to medium, prototypes and pilot lots are frequent, test points are sparse or access is difficult, and design iterations are expected.","A practical check is to count accessible test points and estimate probe access: if a bed-of-nails fixture would need many pogo pins, complex fixturing or would foul components, flying probe wins.","Verify probe reach, probe force and minimum pad size against the tester datasheet and IPC-7351 land-pattern guidance."]},{"h":"Throughput ceiling drivers","p":["Mechanical motion sets the ceiling far more than electrical measurement does.","A rough model: time per point equals average probe move time plus dwell plus measurement overhead, divided by the number of probes working in parallel, then adjusted for probe sequencing efficiency. Probe speed, acceleration, dwell and collision avoidance come from the equipment datasheet.","In practice dense boards, small pitch, double-sided probing, calibration cycles and many component tests all pull the achieved rate below the model. Typical flying-probe point rates can range from about 10 to 100 points/min, but verify against your tester, board size and test program."]},{"h":"Program effort","p":["Program effort is not just the test sequence.","It includes importing CAD, BOM, netlist and test-point data; defining limits from component tolerance and simulation; writing probe access rules; checking for collisions; calibrating probes; and validating the false-call rate.","Flying probe programs start faster than in-circuit fixtures because no bed of nails has to be built, while complex analogue tests, powered tests and tight limits need engineering time close to a fixture program."]},{"h":"Strategy selection example","p":["A 50-piece prototype with 0.4 mm pitch components, limited test points and no JTAG gets early coverage from flying probe without fixture cost.","A stable 100,000-piece product with good test-point access usually finds in-circuit test plus boundary scan cheaper per unit and faster. A mixed-technology assembly with BGAs and critical analogue circuits uses boundary scan for interconnect coverage, in-circuit or flying probe for component-level checks, and functional test for system verification."]},{"h":"Risks and mitigation","p":["The risks are probe marks, contamination, false calls, probe wear and damage to small pads or components.","Evaluate probe marks against IPC-A-610 acceptability criteria and your product quality requirements. Keep probe pads clean, verify probe force and dwell, and use calibration standards.","When flying probe throughput falls short, add test points, increase probe count, reduce coverage, move high-volume lots to in-circuit test, or use boundary scan to relieve the probe."]}],"calc":{"task":"Estimate the flying-probe throughput ceiling for a prototype assembly with 1,200 test points, 4 probes, average probe move 250 mm, probe speed 100 mm/s, dwell 0.20 s, measurement overhead 0.10 s, parallel efficiency 0.65, board handling 15 s, and availability 85%. Assume each point requires one probe move and one dwell.","solution":"1. Move time per point = 250 mm / 100 mm/s = 2.5 s. 2. Electrical time per point = dwell + overhead = 0.20 s + 0.10 s = 0.30 s. 3. Serial time per point = 2.5 s + 0.30 s = 2.8 s. 4. Ideal parallel time per point = 2.8 s / 4 probes = 0.70 s/point. 5. Effective time per point = 0.70 s / 0.65 = 1.077 s/point. 6. Test time = 1,200 points * 1.077 s/point = 1,292 s = 21.5 min. 7. Cycle time = 1,292 s + 15 s = 1,307 s = 21.8 min. 8. Theoretical boards/h = 3,600 s/h / 1,307 s/board = 2.75 boards/h. 9. Availability-adjusted boards/h = 2.75 * 0.85 = 2.34 boards/h. 10. Points/min = 1,200 points / (1,292 s / 60 s/min) = 55.7 points/min. This is an upper-bound estimate; real throughput will be lower if probe collisions, calibration, component access, or test sequencing reduce parallel efficiency."},"defect":{"symptom":"Flying probe reports intermittent opens on fine-pitch test pads, and the same board passes after reflow cleaning and retest.","cause":"Flux residue, probe contamination, or oxide on small test pads increases contact resistance; probe dwell or force may be too low for the pad finish and probe tip condition.","action":"Clean the board and probe tips, verify probe force and dwell against the tester datasheet, inspect pad finish and probe marks against IPC-A-610 acceptability criteria, and re-run the program with a contact-resistance check or calibration standard before releasing the lot."},"takeaways":["Choose flying probe when volume cannot pay for a fixture, because the fixture is the cost the method removes.","Model throughput from probe movement, not from measurement time, since the mechanical motion dominates.","Watch probe marks on small pads, because a test method that damages the product has failed even when it caught the defect."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Buying a fixture for a prototype lot","instead":"Use flying probe, because fixture cost needs volume to justify it."},{"mistake":"Assuming flying probe limits match in-circuit limits","instead":"Loosen them to the probe's repeatability, because a tighter limit only raises false calls."},{"mistake":"Ignoring probe contamination between production lots","instead":"Clean tips and verify force, since flux and oxide raise contact resistance and read as opens."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pogo pin","def":"A spring-loaded contact used in bed-of-nails fixtures to press onto a test point."},{"term":"Probe mark","def":"The indentation or witness mark a probe leaves on a test pad."},{"term":"Parallel efficiency","def":"The fraction of the ideal multi-probe throughput a tester actually achieves."},{"term":"False call","def":"A failure reported by the tester on a board that is actually good."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m13l02"],"skills":["Lesson m13l02: In-circuit test: fixture, node access, test-point design, analog limits"]}},{"lessonId":"m13l04","moduleId":"m13","stageId":"test","title":"Boundary scan / JTAG: BGA access, structure, what it cannot find","goals":[{"text":"Compare ICT, flying probe, boundary scan and functional test for a given assembly and choose a strategy with reasons.","competencies":["K6"]},{"text":"Specify design-for-test requirements as design requests with rationale.","competencies":["K6","K10"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-011"],"quiz":["q-m13l04-01","q-m13l04-02","q-m13l04-03","q-m13l04-04","q-m13l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m13l04.webp","alt":"A scan chain of devices connected through BGA packages, with the test access port at one end and a shaded region it cannot reach","caption":"Boundary scan and JTAG: BGA access, structure, and what it cannot find"}]},{"h":"Why boundary scan matters for BGA assemblies","p":["Boundary scan earns its place on BGA assemblies because the package hides the solder joints and the escape routing, so a tester cannot reach every net with a probe.","IEEE Std 1149.1 (JTAG) defines a serial test access port and boundary-scan cells that let a tester drive or observe device pins through a handful of control lines.","At 0.5 mm pitch the value is not in replacing every other method but in giving electrical access to digital nets that would otherwise have none."]},{"h":"Boundary-scan structure","p":"A scan-capable device contains a TAP controller, an instruction register, and data registers. Boundary-scan cells sit between the internal core and the package pins. In EXTEST, the tester shifts a pattern into the boundary-scan chain, updates the cells, then captures the pin states. TCK clocks the state machine, TMS controls state transitions, TDI shifts data in, TDO shifts data out, and TRST is optional. Chains can be daisy-chained across devices. Keep chain length and TCK frequency within the limits of the package, board, and tester; verify against IEEE Std 1149.1 (JTAG) and your tester datasheet."},{"h":"What boundary scan can find","p":["Boundary scan finds digital interconnect defects between scan cells: opens, shorts, stuck-at faults and some bridging where both ends of the net are scan-capable.","It reads IDCODE to confirm device presence and orientation, and SAMPLE/PRELOAD observes normal operation. That makes it useful in-system and on boards where probe access is poor.","Coverage depends on the accuracy of the BSDL file and the netlist, and on whether the pins in question are actually configured as scan cells."]},{"h":"What boundary scan cannot find","p":["Boundary scan cannot find a defect that leaves the scan cell's logic state unchanged, or one outside the chain.","Non-scan passives, analogue circuits, power distribution, clocks, reset, configuration pins and high-speed interfaces stay invisible unless extra test structures exist. It says nothing about solder joint quality, mechanical strength, contamination, thermal behaviour or parametric performance such as rise time, noise margin or current draw.","A short on a net with one non-scan end waits for another method to catch it. The scan port itself is defined by IEEE Std 1149.1, and nothing outside that definition is visible to it."]},{"h":"Compare ICT, flying probe, boundary scan and functional test","p":["Compare by access, coverage, speed and cost.","In-circuit test uses a bed of nails and can check many passive and pin-level faults quickly, at the cost of test points and fixture design, and fine-pitch BGA escapes may still be out of reach. Flying probe avoids the fixture and suits prototypes, and it is slower and still needs probe access.","Boundary scan needs few access points and works well on BGA digital nets, given scan-capable devices, BSDL files and design discipline. Functional test proves the system and is the slowest to diagnose."]},{"h":"Choosing a test strategy","p":["Choose from the assembly mix.","A high-volume BGA board with many scan-capable digital devices takes boundary scan for digital interconnect, functional test for system behaviour, and in-circuit or flying probe for non-scan passives where test points exist.","A prototype of a few boards gets faster feedback from flying probe plus boundary scan plus functional test than from a full fixture. On a mixed-signal board boundary scan may cover a minority of nets, so functional test carries more of the weight."]},{"h":"Design-for-test requests","p":["Specify DFT as design requests with a rationale.","Ask for boundary-scan support on all digital devices with BSDL files, IDCODE values and a netlist so the tester can build chains. Ask for a stable JTAG header or test points, with TCK, TMS, TDI, TDO and optional TRST routed per IEEE Std 1149.1 (JTAG).","Ask for test points on non-scan passives, power, ground, clocks, reset and configuration pins, and for chains short enough for the cycle time the tester needs."]},{"h":"Practical limits and verification","p":["Chain length, TCK frequency, power state and pin multiplexing are the practical limits.","Some devices need power and configuration before scan access, others support limited unpowered modes, so check the device datasheet. Long chains raise test time and can degrade signal integrity; short, balanced groups help.","Keep JTAG traces away from high-speed clocks and sensitive analogue nets, and verify signal quality by simulation or measurement before the design freezes."]}],"calc":{"task":"A BGA assembly has 1200 nets. Boundary scan can access 720 digital nets. Flying probe can access 600 nets and takes 18 min per board. Functional test catches 480 system-level defects but misses 240 latent opens. The boundary-scan chain has 1000 cells and runs at 10 MHz. Each vector shifts 1000 bits. Choose a test strategy for 5000 boards and state reasons.","solution":"Step 1: Boundary-scan net coverage = 720 / 1200 = 0.60 = 60%. Step 2: Nets not covered by boundary scan = 1200 - 720 = 480 nets. Step 3: Shift time per vector = 1000 bits / 10 MHz = 100 µs. Step 4: For 1000 vectors, shift time = 1000 * 100 µs = 100 ms; allow overhead and use 0.2 s per board. Step 5: Flying-probe coverage = 600 / 1200 = 50%. Step 6: Flying-probe time for 5000 boards = 18 min * 5000 = 90000 min = 1500 h. Step 7: Functional test catches 480 system-level defects but misses 240 latent opens, so it should not be the only method. Step 8: For the first 50 boards, use flying probe plus boundary scan plus functional test to debug access, chain integrity, and system behavior. Step 9: For the remaining 4950 boards, use boundary scan plus functional test as the production baseline. Step 10: Add ICT or flying probe only for non-scan passives if the 480-net coverage gap is unacceptable after reviewing fault risk, test-point availability, and fixture cost."},"defect":{"symptom":"Boundary scan passes all digital interconnect tests, but the board fails functional test with intermittent resets and missing clock activity.","cause":"The reset and clock nets are not covered by boundary scan, and a marginal solder joint or missing decoupling capacitor affects power integrity rather than scan-cell logic state.","action":"Add test points or functional vectors for reset, clock, and power rails; review the coverage report for untested nets; verify the BGA escape routing and decoupling layout; re-run boundary scan, functional test, and a targeted flying-probe check on the affected nets."},"takeaways":["Read boundary scan as coverage of digital nets, because everything outside the chain is untested however clean the report looks.","Keep chains short and balanced, since chain length spends cycle time on every vector.","Ask for scan support and BSDL files at design review, because a device chosen without them has no coverage to add later."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Treating a passing scan report as full board coverage","instead":"Publish the untested net list, because reset, clock and power usually sit outside the chain."},{"mistake":"Designing long daisy chains for layout convenience","instead":"Split into short chains, because every bit shifts on every vector."},{"mistake":"Assuming scan access needs no power sequencing","instead":"Check the device state requirement, since some devices need configuration before scan works."}],"terms":[{"term":"BSDL","def":"The boundary-scan description language file that tells the tester how a device's scan cells are arranged."},{"term":"EXTEST","def":"The scan instruction that drives and observes package pins for interconnect testing."},{"term":"IDCODE","def":"A device identification register readable through the scan port."},{"term":"TAP controller","def":"The state machine inside a scan-capable device that sequences test access port operations."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":25,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m13l03","m07l02"],"skills":["Lesson m13l03: Flying probe: when it wins, throughput ceiling, program effort","Lesson m07l02: AOI program setup: windows, lighting, thresholds, the false-call/escape trade-off"]}},{"lessonId":"m13l05","moduleId":"m13","stageId":"test","title":"Coverage math: node vs defect coverage, escape risk, where inspection ends and test starts","goals":[{"text":"Estimate test coverage and name the defect classes left uncovered by the chosen strategy.","competencies":["K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001"],"quiz":["q-m13l05-01","q-m13l05-02","q-m13l05-03","q-m13l05-04","q-m13l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m13l05.webp","alt":"Two overlapping coverage circles, one for nodes exercised and one for defect modes detected, with the uncovered region marked","caption":"Coverage math: node versus defect coverage, escape risk, and where inspection ends and test starts"}]},{"h":"Node coverage and defect coverage","p":["Node coverage counts the electrical nodes or nets a test method exercises; defect coverage estimates the share of likely defect modes it can actually detect.","A board can carry high node coverage and low defect coverage at the same time, if the test only checks connectivity at nominal conditions and misses marginal joints, parametric drift or thermal opens.","Reporting one number without the other hides the gap."]},{"h":"Inspection detects physical nonconformities","p":["Visual inspection, AOI and X-ray look for physical evidence: missing or wrong component, polarity, tombstoning, solder bridging, insufficient solder, foreign material and visible component damage.","They work on defects with an optical or radiographic signature and they do not prove electrical behaviour under power, temperature or signal loading. A joint can look perfect and be open, and a joint can look marginal and conduct."]},{"h":"Test detects electrical and functional faults","p":["In-circuit test, boundary scan and functional test apply electrical stimulus and measure the response.","They find opens, shorts, wrong component values, inverted orientation where it changes electrical behaviour, power faults and functional errors. They miss defects that are physically present and electrically invisible, such as a cosmetic solder issue or contamination that does not affect current."]},{"h":"Coverage math needs a defect model","p":["To estimate coverage, list the expected defect classes and assign each a detection probability per method.","Take the probabilities from your own process capability data, test logs or pilot builds, not from a vendor headline. Use a range rather than a point estimate, and check it against your paste and equipment datasheet.","Use SI units such as millimetre and micrometre for physical inspection limits, and ppm as the dimensionless rate for escape risk."]},{"h":"Escape risk is coverage plus occurrence","p":["Escape risk combines detection coverage with defect occurrence.","Where a defect class occurs at a known rate and a method detects a fraction of it, the residual escape rate is the occurrence rate multiplied by the undetected fraction. That gives a ppm figure for planning, never a guarantee: real escapes follow process drift, test program quality and intermittent defects."]},{"h":"Where inspection ends and test starts","p":["Inspection ends where the defect has no reliable physical signature, or the signature is too small, hidden or variable to inspect consistently.","Test starts where the question becomes whether the assembly behaves electrically or functionally. On hidden joints, X-ray or boundary scan bridges part of the gap, and neither replaces a test that exercises the node under representative conditions."]},{"h":"Choosing a strategy defines the uncovered classes","p":["A strategy built only on AOI leaves electrical opens under packages, shorts under components, wrong values inside tolerance and parametric faults uncovered.","A strategy built only on in-circuit test may leave cosmetic solder defects, intermittent joints, thermal opens and functional timing errors uncovered.","Document the chosen strategy with the defect classes it does not cover and the compensating controls used, so the gap is a decision rather than an accident."]}],"calc":{"task":"A board has 1200 electrical nodes. ICT exercises 980 nodes. Functional test exercises 150 nodes, of which 30 overlap with ICT. A defect model gives occurrence rates: visible solder and placement defects 1500 ppm, electrical open and short defects 800 ppm, parametric and wrong-value defects 200 ppm. AOI detects 70 percent of visible defects, ICT detects 90 percent of electrical defects, and functional test detects 60 percent of parametric defects. Estimate node coverage, weighted defect coverage, and residual escape ppm. Name defect classes left uncovered.","solution":"Node coverage: union of ICT and functional nodes is 980 + 150 - 30 = 1100 nodes. Node coverage = 1100 / 1200 = 91.7 percent. Weighted defect coverage: visible contribution = 1500 * 0.70 = 1050 ppm detected; electrical contribution = 800 * 0.90 = 720 ppm detected; parametric contribution = 200 * 0.60 = 120 ppm detected. Total detected = 1050 + 720 + 120 = 1890 ppm. Total occurrence = 1500 + 800 + 200 = 2500 ppm. Weighted defect coverage = 1890 / 2500 = 75.6 percent. Residual escape = 2500 - 1890 = 610 ppm, or 2500 * 0.244 = 610 ppm. Defect classes left uncovered include under-component opens and shorts not exercised, intermittent solder joints, thermal opens, wrong values within tolerance, parametric drift, contamination, cosmetic solder defects, functional timing errors, and firmware errors."},"defect":{"symptom":"AOI passes a QFN assembly, but ICT finds an open on the exposed pad net after reflow.","cause":"solder paste volume under the QFN was low or the pad was not wetted, so the joint was electrically open but visually acceptable.","action":"check stencil aperture and paste release, verify paste height with SPI, review reflow profile, and add X-ray or boundary scan coverage for hidden pad nets; rework per IPC acceptability guidance."},"takeaways":["Publish node coverage and defect coverage together, because the first number flatters any test that only checks connectivity.","Weight coverage by occurrence, since a method that catches a rare class adds little to the escape rate.","List the uncovered classes with the strategy, because the gap is where escapes come from."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Quoting a single coverage percentage for the whole board","instead":"Report per defect class, because coverage is a property of a defect model."},{"mistake":"Using vendor detection figures as your capability","instead":"Measure on your own process data, since the same method behaves differently on different products."},{"mistake":"Treating an AOI pass as an electrical pass","instead":"Add a test that exercises the node, because visual signature and electrical behaviour are different questions."}],"terms":[{"term":"Node coverage","def":"The fraction of electrical nodes a test method exercises."},{"term":"Defect coverage","def":"The fraction of expected defect modes a method can detect, weighted by occurrence."},{"term":"Escape","def":"A defective assembly that passes the inspection and test strategy and reaches the customer."},{"term":"Detection probability","def":"The likelihood that a method reports a present defect of a given class."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m13l04"],"skills":["Lesson m13l04: Boundary scan / JTAG: BGA access, structure, what it cannot find"]}},{"lessonId":"m13l06","moduleId":"m13","stageId":"test","title":"Choosing a strategy for a product mix: volume, complexity, cost, rework loop","goals":[{"text":"Compare ICT, flying probe, boundary scan and functional test for a given assembly and choose a strategy with reasons.","competencies":["K6"]},{"text":"Estimate test coverage and name the defect classes left uncovered by the chosen strategy.","competencies":["K6"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-011"],"quiz":["q-m13l06-01","q-m13l06-02","q-m13l06-03","q-m13l06-04","q-m13l06-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m13l06.webp","alt":"A decision matrix of product volume against assembly complexity, with a test strategy recommended in each quadrant and a rework loop","caption":"Choosing a strategy for a product mix: volume, complexity, cost and the rework loop"}]},{"h":"Product mix and test strategy","p":["Product mix is a test-strategy problem because volume, complexity, cost and the rework loop interact.","High volume favours methods with short cycle time and repeatable contact, since fixture cost spreads over many units and line balance starts to matter. High complexity favours methods that reach dense interconnects, verify presence and value, and isolate faults quickly. Cost pressure favours whatever reduces escapes, rework and test time, which is rarely the cheapest tester on the quotation.","The strategy follows the mix, not the other way round."]},{"h":"In-circuit test","p":["In-circuit test uses a dedicated fixture with pogo pins to contact the test points.","It is strong on opens, shorts, missing components, wrong values and some solder defects where the net is accessible and the program is well built. Coverage is not the number of test points: it also depends on double-sided access, fine-pitch access, component orientation, and whether power, analogue and high-speed nets are included.","Fixture cost and design lead time are the price of that speed. Verify coverage against the fixture design, the equipment datasheet and the applicable IPC acceptance standard for solder defects."]},{"h":"Flying probe test","p":["Flying probe replaces the fixture with movable probes that reach test points under program control.","It suits prototypes, low volume, high mix and debug, because fixture cost disappears and program changes come quickly. It gives broad net coverage where test points are accessible, with cycle time usually longer than in-circuit test and probe reach limiting dense or double-sided boards.","Test-point placement and clearance matter more here, because a probe has to land on what the layout gives it."]},{"h":"Boundary scan","p":["Boundary scan controls device pins through JTAG and tests interconnects without physical probes.","It is valuable where access is limited, test points are scarce, or the interconnects are too dense to probe, and it detects opens, shorts and some device-level faults on scan-capable interconnects, plus programming and debug support.","Its coverage stops at scan-capable devices and scan-capable nets, and non-scan components stay outside it."]},{"h":"Functional test","p":["Functional test applies power, signals and loads to verify that the assembly behaves as intended.","It catches programming errors, gross functional failures, some marginal defects and system-level interactions the structural tests miss.","It may not isolate the failed net or component, and its coverage depends on the program, the stimulus conditions and the measurement resolution. Functional test is slow and needs specialised fixtures, software and skills, which is why it usually closes the line rather than opens it."]},{"h":"Choosing a strategy","p":["On high-volume, high-complexity assemblies the common combination is in-circuit test, boundary scan and functional test, which balances coverage, cycle time and rework loop time.","In-circuit test gives fast structural coverage, boundary scan covers where probes cannot reach, and functional test closes the gross functional defects. On low-volume or prototype work, flying probe plus boundary scan plus functional test often beats building a fixture."]},{"h":"Coverage and uncovered classes","p":["State coverage for nets, components and defect classes, never as one standalone figure.","A strategy can cover most nets and still miss under-component solder defects, non-scan analogue faults, thermal and mechanical defects, intermittent contacts, contamination, programming errors and marginal parametric drift.","Coverage also depends on the conditions: temperature, voltage and timing, because some defects only appear under stress. Name the uncovered classes when the strategy is chosen, and name the control that compensates."]},{"h":"Rework loop and cost","p":["The rework loop runs from defect detection to corrected unit, and it drives both cost and quality.","A strategy that localises defects quickly shortens diagnosis and stops repeated functional debugging. Structural tests isolate opens, shorts, missing parts and wrong values, while functional tests may only say that a subsystem failed.","Total cost includes fixture, test time, maintenance, program development, escapes, rework and scrap. The cheapest tester is often the expensive choice once the loop is counted."]}],"calc":{"task":"A high-density assembly has 1400 nets, 30% BGA, 25% non-scan devices, and monthly volume 4000 units. Flying probe cycle time is 180 s, ICT cycle time is 25 s, boundary scan adds 8 s, and functional test adds 45 s. Estimate coverage and choose a strategy with reasons.","solution":"Define total nets as 1400. Estimate ICT net coverage. With accessible test points and double-sided probing, assume 85% of nets are testable; verify against fixture design. ICT coverage = 1400 x 85% = 1190 nets. Estimate boundary-scan coverage. Scan-capable share is 75%. Assume scan tests 80% of scan-capable interconnects; verify against boundary-scan tool. Scan coverage = 1400 x 75% x 80% = 840 nets. Combine coverage conservatively. Assume scan adds 7 percentage points beyond ICT because many scan nets overlap with ICT. Combined structural coverage = 85% + 7% = 92%. Add functional test. Assume functional test adds 3 percentage points for gross functional and programming defects. Estimated total coverage = 95%. Compare cycle time. ICT + boundary scan + functional = 25 s + 8 s + 45 s = 78 s per unit. Flying probe + boundary scan + functional = 180 s + 8 s + 45 s = 233 s per unit. At 4000 units/month, the ICT-based strategy is more practical for the rework loop because it reduces test time per unit and keeps fixture cost amortized. Choose ICT + boundary scan + functional. Reasons: high volume amortizes fixture cost, ICT gives fast structural coverage, boundary scan adds coverage where probe access is limited, functional test closes gross functional defects, and cycle time supports the rework loop. Name defect classes left uncovered: under-BGA opens/shorts not detected by structural or functional tests, non-scan analog faults, thermal or mechanical defects, intermittent contacts, contamination, marginal parametric drift, and programming errors not exercised by the functional program. Verify residual risk against your acceptance criteria and failure history."},"defect":{"symptom":"Intermittent open after reflow on a fine-pitch QFP, with the fault appearing only after thermal cycling or handling.","cause":"Insufficient solder paste volume, poor paste release, misalignment, or contaminated pad surface can create a marginal joint that tests good initially but opens under stress.","action":"Check stencil aperture, paste release, SPI data, alignment, and pad cleanliness; review reflow profile; add stress screening or targeted inspection if the defect class is not covered by the chosen test strategy."},"takeaways":["Choose the strategy from volume, complexity and the rework loop, because each driver points at a different method.","State coverage per defect class, since a net percentage says nothing about under-component joints.","Count the rework loop in the cost, because a slow diagnosis turns a cheap tester into an expensive one."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Buying a fixture for a high-mix low-volume line","instead":"Use flying probe, because fixture cost cannot spread over a few hundred units."},{"mistake":"Reporting coverage for the board as one number","instead":"Break it down by class and condition, because defects live in the gaps."},{"mistake":"Leaving functional test to find structural faults","instead":"Cover structure early, because functional failures are the hardest to debug."}],"terms":[{"term":"High mix","def":"A production profile with many different assemblies in small quantities."},{"term":"Rework loop","def":"The time and cost from detecting a defect to producing a corrected unit."},{"term":"Pogo pin","def":"A spring-loaded contact that presses onto a test point in a fixture."},{"term":"Escape","def":"A defective unit that passes the strategy and reaches the customer."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m13l05"],"skills":["Lesson m13l05: Coverage math: node vs defect coverage, escape risk, where inspection ends and test starts"]}},{"lessonId":"m14l01","moduleId":"m14","title":"Defect data before opinions: taxonomy, Pareto by stage, measurement integrity","goals":[{"text":"Structure a defect problem with measured data (taxonomy, Pareto by stage, measurement integrity) before analysis.","competencies":["K9"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-004","src-008","src-009","src-016","src-026"],"quiz":["q-m14l01-01","q-m14l01-02","q-m14l01-03","q-m14l01-04","q-m14l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"Start with measured defect data, not with opinions","p":["A defect problem becomes manageable once measured data describes it, before any analysis starts.","The first task is not to decide whether the printer, the placer, the oven, the wave or the operator is at fault. The first task is to define what was measured, where, in what unit, and against which acceptance rule.","\"The oven is causing tombstoning\" is an opinion until stage-specific counts, component references, board identifiers, lot data, process parameters and inspection results support it. \"Reflow-stage confirmed defects are the largest contributor this week\" is a measured observation, provided the taxonomy and the inspection integrity are known.","Treat defect data as a measurement system: if the measurement system is weak, every conclusion built on it is weak too."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","caption":"Pareto by stage — illustrative distribution","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 900 300' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Pareto chart of defects by stage'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace}</style><line x1='60' y1='250' x2='860' y2='250' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='60' y1='250' x2='60' y2='30' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><rect x='100' y='58' width='80' height='192' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.5)'/><rect x='220' y='106' width='80' height='144' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.5)'/><rect x='340' y='142' width='80' height='108' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.5)'/><rect x='460' y='190' width='80' height='60' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.35)'/><rect x='580' y='202' width='80' height='48' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.35)'/><rect x='700' y='202' width='80' height='48' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.35)'/><text x='140' y='48' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>32%</text><text x='260' y='96' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>24%</text><text x='380' y='132' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>18%</text><text x='500' y='180' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>10%</text><text x='620' y='192' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>8%</text><text x='740' y='192' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>8%</text><polyline points='140,180 260,127 380,87 500,65 620,48 740,30' fill='none' stroke='#ff9800' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='140' cy='180' r='3' fill='#ff9800'/><circle cx='260' cy='127' r='3' fill='#ff9800'/><circle cx='380' cy='87' r='3' fill='#ff9800'/><circle cx='500' cy='65' r='3' fill='#ff9800'/><circle cx='620' cy='48' r='3' fill='#ff9800'/><circle cx='740' cy='30' r='3' fill='#ff9800'/><text x='140' y='270' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>PRINT</text><text x='260' y='270' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>PLACE</text><text x='380' y='270' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>REFLOW</text><text x='500' y='270' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>ESCAPES</text><text x='620' y='270' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>WAVE</text><text x='740' y='270' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>OTHER</text><text x='860' y='24' fill='#ff9800' font-size='12' text-anchor='end'>CUMULATIVE %</text><text x='60' y='20' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14'>DEFECT SHARE BY STAGE — ILLUSTRATIVE</text></svg>"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m14l01.webp","alt":"A Pareto chart of defects by process stage with a data-integrity check panel beside it","caption":"Defect data before opinions: taxonomy, Pareto by stage and measurement integrity"}]},{"h":"Build a defect taxonomy","p":["A useful defect taxonomy records the defect mode, the assembly stage, the inspection or test point, the product, the board or lot identifier, the line, the shift, the equipment, the recipe and the disposition.","The defect mode says what was found: insufficient solder, excess solder, misalignment, tombstoning, bridging, voiding, lifted lead, solder ball, component damage or contamination. The stage says where it was detected and, where known, where it was created.","A taxonomy only pays off when the fields are filled the same way every shift, which is a discipline problem before it is a database problem."]},{"h":"IPC acceptance and rework standards as the defect language","p":["For visual and workmanship decisions, IPC-A-610 gives the acceptance criteria and IPC-7711/7721 gives the rework and repair guidance.","These standards matter because they supply a common language for classification and disposition. Acceptance still depends on product class, customer requirements and the specific defect condition.","Do not assume every visible anomaly is a reject, and do not assume every reject is a confirmed process defect."]},{"h":"Pareto by stage","p":["A Pareto by stage answers which stage contributes the largest share of confirmed defects, escapes, rework, scrap or test failures.","A final-test Pareto alone hides upstream causes: a functional failure can come from a reflow defect, a placement defect, a through-hole insertion problem or a test setup issue.","A stage-based Pareto separates incoming material, paste print, placement, reflow, through-hole insertion, wave solder, in-circuit test and functional test, so the contribution is visible where it was made."]},{"h":"Measurement integrity determines whether the Pareto is trustworthy","p":["Measurement integrity asks whether the inspection or test system detects the defect it is meant to detect and whether it avoids false calls.","On SMT the measurement systems are SPI for paste volume and area, AOI for component and solder condition, X-ray for hidden joints, in-circuit test for connectivity, functional test for operation and manual visual inspection. On through-hole work they are insertion verification, wave inspection and the same electrical tests.","An unaudited measurement system produces a Pareto of its own biases."]},{"h":"Record process parameters in SI units and trace them to the defect","p":["Defect data becomes actionable when it is linked to process parameters in consistent SI units.","Print data may include stencil thickness in mm, aperture dimensions in mm, paste volume in mm³, squeegee pressure in Pa or N depending on the printer interface, print speed in mm/s and paste temperature in °C. Placement data may include nozzle force in N, placement speed in mm/s, component offset in mm and feeder pickup accuracy in mm. Reflow data may include zone temperatures in °C, conveyor speed in mm/s and atmosphere in ppm oxygen.","Trace each parameter to the defect it is suspected of causing, or the correlation stays a story. Verify the actual ranges against your paste datasheet, stencil design, printer datasheet, oven profile, wave or selective solder machine datasheet, and IPC-7525, IPC-7526, IPC-7530 or IPC-7711/7721 guidance where applicable."]},{"h":"Define the denominator before comparing stages","p":["A defect count without a denominator misleads.","The denominator may be boards produced, boards inspected, components placed, joints inspected, test cycles or units shipped, and a stage defect rate must state which. Defects per board, per thousand boards, per million joints and failures per test cycle are different metrics.","Where one stage inspects every board and another samples a subset, raw counts cannot be compared directly."]},{"h":"Use the Pareto to select the next measurement, not to assign blame","p":["The output of a stage-based Pareto is a short list of stages and defect modes that justify further measurement.","If reflow-stage confirmed defects dominate, stratify by component type, package size, board location, paste lot, stencil design, oven profile or AOI program. If paste print defects dominate, review SPI data, stencil condition, squeegee settings, paste rheology and registration. If through-hole or wave defects dominate, review insertion, hole fill and wave parameters.","Assigning blame is not an output; choosing the next measurement is."],"blocks":[{"t":"table","caption":"Defect taxonomy by stage: every count needs a code and an owner","head":["Stage","Example defect codes","First owner"],"rows":[["Printing","Insufficient paste, excess paste, bridging at print, aperture clog","Print operator / stencil owner"],["Placement","Tombstone, wrong part, offset, missing","Line engineer"],["Reflow","Voiding, cold joint, warpage, grain structure","Process engineer"],["Test / inspection","Escape (defect found later), false call","Quality"]]}]}],"calc":{"task":"A mixed SMT/THT line produced 12,000 boards during a week. The MES defect table before measurement-integrity audit contained these stage defect calls: incoming 18, paste print 145, placement 92, reflow 270, THT insertion 34, wave solder 56, ICT 71, functional test 48. An AOI audit reviewed the 270 reflow calls and confirmed 210 defects, with 60 false calls. A detection audit used 50 seeded reflow defects and found that AOI detected 48 and missed 2. Calculate the raw stage defect rate per 1000 boards, the raw Pareto ranking, the corrected confirmed stage defect rate per 1000 boards, the corrected Pareto ranking, the AOI false-call rate, the AOI detection rate, and the seeded escape rate. State whether the reflow stage is still the top stage after correction and what data action is needed before root-cause analysis.","solution":"1. Raw total defect calls = 18 + 145 + 92 + 270 + 34 + 56 + 71 + 48 = 734. 2. Raw stage defect rate per 1000 boards = stage count / 12,000 x 1000. Incoming = 18 / 12,000 x 1000 = 1.5. Paste print = 145 / 12,000 x 1000 = 12.1. Placement = 92 / 12,000 x 1000 = 7.7. Reflow = 270 / 12,000 x 1000 = 22.5. THT insertion = 34 / 12,000 x 1000 = 2.8. Wave solder = 56 / 12,000 x 1000 = 4.7. ICT = 71 / 12,000 x 1000 = 5.9. Functional test = 48 / 12,000 x 1000 = 4.0. 3. Raw Pareto ranking by count: reflow 270, paste print 145, placement 92, ICT 71, wave solder 56, functional test 48, THT insertion 34, incoming 18. 4. Raw percentage of total = stage count / 734 x 100. Reflow = 270 / 734 x 100 = 36.8%. Paste print = 145 / 734 x 100 = 19.8%. Placement = 92 / 734 x 100 = 12.5%. ICT = 71 / 734 x 100 = 9.7%. Wave solder = 56 / 734 x 100 = 7.6%. Functional test = 48 / 734 x 100 = 6.5%. THT insertion = 34 / 734 x 100 = 4.6%. Incoming = 18 / 734 x 100 = 2.5%. 5. Raw cumulative percentage: reflow 36.8%, paste print 56.6%, placement 69.1%, ICT 78.8%, wave solder 86.4%, functional test 92.9%, THT insertion 97.5%, incoming 100.0%. 6. Corrected confirmed total = 18 + 145 + 92 + 210 + 34 + 56 + 71 + 48 = 674. 7. Corrected stage defect rate per 1000 boards: incoming = 18 / 12,000 x 1000 = 1.5. Paste print = 145 / 12,000 x 1000 = 12.1. Placement = 92 / 12,000 x 1000 = 7.7. Reflow = 210 / 12,000 x 1000 = 17.5. THT insertion = 34 / 12,000 x 1000 = 2.8. Wave solder = 56 / 12,000 x 1000 = 4.7. ICT = 71 / 12,000 x 1000 = 5.9. Functional test = 48 / 12,000 x 1000 = 4.0. 8. Corrected Pareto ranking by confirmed count: reflow 210, paste print 145, placement 92, ICT 71, wave solder 56, functional test 48, THT insertion 34, incoming 18. 9. Corrected percentage of total = stage count / 674 x 100. Reflow = 210 / 674 x 100 = 31.2%. Paste print = 145 / 674 x 100 = 21.5%. Placement = 92 / 674 x 100 = 13.6%. ICT = 71 / 674 x 100 = 10.5%. Wave solder = 56 / 674 x 100 = 8.3%. Functional test = 48 / 674 x 100 = 7.1%. THT insertion = 34 / 674 x 100 = 5.0%. Incoming = 18 / 674 x 100 = 2.7%. 10. Corrected cumulative percentage: reflow 31.2%, paste print 52.7%, placement 66.3%, ICT 76.8%, wave solder 85.1%, functional test 92.2%, THT insertion 97.2%, incoming 99.9% before rounding to full total. 11. AOI false-call rate = false calls / AOI calls x 100 = 60 / 270 x 100 = 22.2%. 12. AOI detection rate = detected seeded defects / seeded defects x 100 = 48 / 50 x 100 = 96.0%. 13. Seeded escape rate = missed seeded defects / seeded defects x 100 = 2 / 50 x 100 = 4.0%. 14. Optional estimate of true reflow defects, assuming the seeded detection rate applies to the confirmed population and false calls have been removed: estimated true reflow defects = confirmed reflow defects / detection rate = 210 / 0.96 = 218.8. 15. Conclusion: reflow remains the top stage after correction, but the gap between reflow and paste print narrows from 125 raw calls to 65 confirmed defects. The data action is to remove false calls from the Pareto, record confirmed versus called defects separately, verify AOI program settings and defect library, and stratify reflow defects by component, package, board location, paste lot, stencil design, and oven profile before root-cause analysis."},"defect":{"symptom":"AOI reports a high number of reflow-stage solder defects, especially insufficient solder and bridging, but repair confirmation shows that many boards pass IPC-A-610 acceptance or are repaired without a confirmed process defect. The reflow stage dominates the Pareto, and operators blame the oven while the paste printer and stencil condition are not being reviewed.","cause":"The defect database is counting AOI calls as confirmed defects without separating false calls, and the AOI program has not been audited against IPC-A-610 acceptance conditions or a seeded defect library. The measurement system is therefore inflating the reflow stage and hiding the true upstream contribution from paste print or stencil-related defects.","action":"Freeze the current Pareto, rebuild the defect table with separate fields for AOI call, confirmed defect, false call, escape, disposition, and IPC-A-610 acceptance decision. Run an AOI false-call and detection audit using known-good boards and seeded defects. Correct the Pareto using confirmed defects only. If the corrected reflow Pareto still dominates, stratify by component, package, board location, paste lot, stencil design, and oven profile. If the corrected paste print Pareto rises, review stencil condition, aperture design, paste rheology, squeegee settings, and SPI data against IPC-7525, IPC-7526, and IPC-7530 guidance and your equipment datasheets."},"takeaways":["Separate calls from confirmed defects before plotting, because a false-call rate becomes a Pareto bar if nobody audits it.","Keep the denominator with every rate, since stage comparisons collapse when one stage samples and another inspects everything.","Let the Pareto choose the next stratification, because a chart that ends in a person produces no measurement."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Ranking stages by raw inspection calls","instead":"Rank by confirmed defects, because inspection bias is not process performance."},{"mistake":"Auditing the defect database once and assuming it is stable","instead":"Re-audit after program or product changes, since the bias returns with them."},{"mistake":"Comparing a sampled stage with a fully inspected stage","instead":"Normalise the denominator, because sampling changes the count and not the process."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pareto","def":"A ranked bar chart of defect counts or rates by category, used to choose where to work first."},{"term":"False call","def":"An inspection or test failure reported on an assembly that is actually acceptable."},{"term":"Measurement integrity","def":"The ability of an inspection system to detect defects without inventing them."},{"term":"Stratification","def":"Splitting a defect population by a second variable to find where the effect concentrates."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m04l07","m06l07","m07l06","m09l07"],"skills":["Lesson m04l07: Print troubleshooting logic and one-variable-at-a-time experiments","Lesson m06l07: Profile validation, documentation and change control","Lesson m07l06: Classification drill: print, placement, reflow and THT images","Lesson m09l07: Wave process control: bath contamination, tin consumption, records, first article"]}},{"lessonId":"m14l02","moduleId":"m14","title":"Cause analysis tools: 5-Why, fishbone, fault tree, change-point analysis","goals":[{"text":"Separate root cause, contributing causes and the detection escape in a structured cause analysis.","competencies":["K9"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-002","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m14l02-01","q-m14l02-02","q-m14l02-03","q-m14l02-04","q-m14l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m14l02.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: why-chain ending at a controllable parameter, fishbone with six cause families, fault tree with AND / OR gates and an escape branch, change-point run chart with event markers, failure / cause / escape boxes and a team at a whiteboard","caption":"Cause analysis tools: why-chain, fishbone, fault tree and change-point analysis"}]},{"h":"Separate the failure, the causes and the escape","p":["Cause analysis starts by separating three questions: what physical failure occurred, what process conditions made it likely, and why the defect was not detected.","A root cause is the condition that, once corrected, prevents recurrence under the same operating assumptions. Contributing causes raise probability or severity without being sufficient alone. A detection escape is a gap in inspection, test or sampling.","Most bad investigations collapse these three into one sentence, and then fix the detection while the cause stays in the process."]},{"h":"Why-chain for narrow failure modes","p":["The why-chain, the 5-Why, suits a narrow failure mode with a mostly linear sequence.","Ask why until the answer points at a controllable process parameter, a design condition or a management-system gap, then stop and verify with evidence. Tombstoning can run from uneven wetting to asymmetric paste deposit, to stencil alignment or paste rheology, to setup verification.","Avoid stopping at operator error, which is usually a description of the process rather than a cause."]},{"h":"Fishbone for interacting cause families","p":["A fishbone works better when many cause families may interact.","Use categories such as machine, method, material, measurement, environment and management, and populate them with testable hypotheses: reflow profile drift, paste viscosity or metal content, stencil aperture wear, placement nozzle force, feeder misalignment, wave conveyor speed, flux activity, humidity and inspection coverage.","The diagram organises the investigation; it does not prove anything until each branch is tested."]},{"h":"Fault tree for combined causes and escapes","p":["Fault tree analysis helps when the top event can result from several independent or combined causes.","Define the top event narrowly, such as an open joint or a tombstoned component, then decompose it with AND and OR gates. An open joint may need both insufficient wetting and mechanical disturbance, while a tombstone may follow from severe paste asymmetry or severe placement offset.","Fault trees make detection escapes explicit, because the tree shows the branch that should have caught the defect and did not."]},{"h":"Change-point analysis","p":["Change-point analysis compares behaviour before and after a suspected change.","Common changes are paste lot, stencil, reflow profile, placement nozzle, feeder setup, wave chain speed, flux lot or AOI program revision. Use a before and after defect rate, a control chart, or a change-point statistic where data allow.","Confirm that the change occurred before the shift, that it affected the product and process in question, and that nothing else moved at the same time."]},{"h":"Report the three categories separately","p":["Separate root cause, contributing causes and detection escape in the final report.","The root cause is the item for corrective action, such as an unqualified paste lot property or a stencil aperture outside the qualified range. Contributing causes may be a marginal reflow profile, a worn nozzle or high placement speed, and they can be reduced by risk controls even when they are not primary.","The detection escape is closed by inspection or test changes, such as AOI program limits, X-ray sampling, functional test coverage or the IPC-A-610 visual acceptability criteria, and it needs its own owner."]},{"h":"Close with verification and updated controls","p":["Close with verification.","Containment protects the customer, corrective action removes the root cause, and effectiveness checks confirm that the defect rate, escape rate or process parameter has returned to an acceptable state. Update the control plan, the FMEA, the work instructions and the inspection program.","Use SI units for temperatures, times, distances and volumes, and state the uncertainty or the sampling limits behind the numbers. Avoid absolute language: standards and contracts allow exceptions, so record the applicable IPC-A-610 class and the IPC/JEDEC J-STD-001 requirements alongside any approved deviation."]}],"calc":{"task":"A line produces 1000 boards/day. Tombstone defect rate for small chip resistors was 0.2% over 10 days. After a paste lot change, the rate was 1.5% over 10 days. AOI detects 80% of tombstones. Functional test detects 5% of the tombstones missed by AOI. Estimate daily escaped defects before and after the change, and state how to classify root cause, contributing causes, and detection escape.","solution":"First, convert defect rates to defects per day. Before: 1000 boards/day x 0.2% = 2.0 defects/day. After: 1000 boards/day x 1.5% = 15.0 defects/day. Second, estimate defects missed by AOI. AOI detects 80%, so it misses 20%. Before: 2.0 x 0.20 = 0.4 missed/day. After: 15.0 x 0.20 = 3.0 missed/day. Third, estimate defects missed by functional test. Functional test detects 5% of AOI misses, so it misses 95%. Before: 0.4 x 0.95 = 0.38 escaped/day. After: 3.0 x 0.95 = 2.85 escaped/day. Fourth, interpret the change point. The escaped defect rate rises from 0.38/day to 2.85/day after the paste lot change, so the paste lot change is a strong candidate change point if the timing matches. Fifth, classify causes. A likely root cause is a paste lot property outside the qualified range, such as viscosity or metal content, verified against the paste datasheet. Contributing causes may include stencil alignment drift, nozzle wear, or marginal reflow profile. The detection escape is the AOI program and functional test coverage that failed to remove the remaining tombstones."},"defect":{"symptom":"Tombstoned chip resistors after reflow; some passed AOI and reached functional test or downstream assembly.","cause":"A paste lot with viscosity or metal content outside the qualified range, combined with stencil alignment drift, produced asymmetric paste deposits and uneven wetting. The AOI program limits did not flag small tombstones, and functional test coverage did not reliably detect them.","action":"Quarantine the suspect paste lot and verify viscosity and metal content against the paste datasheet. Requalify stencil alignment, reflow profile, and placement setup. Update the AOI program and add targeted X-ray or functional test coverage. Run an effectiveness check on subsequent lots and update the control plan and FMEA."},"takeaways":["Name the failure, the cause and the escape separately, because fixing detection on an unproven cause hides the defect rather than removing it.","Stop the why-chain at a controllable parameter, since operator error describes the process without changing it.","Verify effectiveness against a rate, because a corrective action without a measured before and after is a claim."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Closing a case at operator error","instead":"Trace to the process condition that allowed it, because the same error recurs with a new operator."},{"mistake":"Treating a contributing cause as the root cause","instead":"Keep the categories apart, since the risk control differs from the corrective action."},{"mistake":"Fixing detection first because it is faster","instead":"Address the escape and the cause with separate actions, because a better inspection finds more of the same defect."}],"terms":[{"term":"Root cause","def":"The condition whose correction prevents recurrence under the same assumptions."},{"term":"Detection escape","def":"A defect that passed the inspection or test intended to catch it."},{"term":"Fault tree","def":"A top-down logic diagram using AND and OR gates to decompose a failure event."},{"term":"Change point","def":"A point in time at which a process measurement shifts, often following a deliberate change."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m14l01","m13l04"],"skills":["Lesson m14l01: Defect data before opinions: taxonomy, Pareto by stage, measurement integrity","Lesson m13l04: Boundary scan / JTAG: BGA access, structure, what it cannot find"]}},{"lessonId":"m14l03","moduleId":"m14","title":"Containment and traceability: quarantine scope, lot trace, retroactive search","goals":[{"text":"Write a complete 8D with containment, corrective action, owners, dates and an effectiveness metric.","competencies":["K9"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-002","src-009","src-016","src-037"],"quiz":["q-m14l03-01","q-m14l03-02","q-m14l03-03","q-m14l03-04","q-m14l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m14l03.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: containment boundary around work in process, finished goods and shipped units with a suspect window, lot trace chain from reel to shipment, process settings linked to board serial, retroactive search back to last known good, a quarantine shelf photo and an 8D action card","caption":"Containment and traceability: quarantine scope, lot trace, retroactive search and action cards"}]},{"h":"Containment and traceability are separate controls","p":"Containment protects the customer while the cause is unknown, and traceability tells you what to contain. In SMT/THT, a defect can be created by paste, component, stencil, placement, reflow, wave solder, insertion, cleaning, or test escape. A good containment record starts with the detection point and works backward to the last verified good point. It should identify the product, process step, equipment, operator, program, material lot, and time window. If records are incomplete, the scope should be widened until the uncertainty is bounded, then narrowed with evidence. The goal is not to quarantine the whole factory; it is to isolate the population that could plausibly contain the defect. A clear scope also reduces the cost of investigation, because the team can focus on the process window, material lots, and equipment settings that actually produced the suspect units. When the scope is vague, teams often inspect too much product, lose line capacity, and still miss the escape. When the scope is too narrow, the escape can continue downstream, and the company may need to notify the customer under the applicable requirement in ISO 9001 or IATF 16949."},{"h":"Defining quarantine scope","p":["Quarantine scope is a bounded suspect population.","Use timestamps from MES, ERP, test logs and line audits. The scope covers work in process, finished goods, shipped goods, returns, and anything made between the last verified good inspection and detection.","Include adjacent shifts and changeovers where the process was not verified afterwards. A paste lot change, stencil change, feeder change or wave program change usually widens the window, because the change itself is a candidate cause.","Scope too narrow lets the escape continue downstream, and the company may then have to notify the customer under the applicable requirement in ISO 9001 or IATF 16949."]},{"h":"Lot trace in SMT and through-hole","p":["Lot trace connects the assembly to the materials and process parameters that affected it.","On SMT, trace the component lot, solder paste lot, stencil, placement program, feeder setup, reflow profile and oven zone settings. On through-hole work, trace the component lot, insertion program, wave solder program, flux density, preheat temperature, conveyor speed and solder pot temperature.","Verify the settings against the equipment datasheets before quoting them as fact in the report."]},{"h":"Retroactive search","p":["Retroactive search looks for other units that may share the latent cause.","Search forward from the suspect materials and backward from the defect signature. Include units made before and after detection where the process was not verified, and units from the same equipment, operator, program or material lot.","Where the population is too large to inspect fully, use a sampling plan matched to the risk and state its confidence, because an unstated sample is an unstated claim. ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 can supply the plan when one is needed, and the plan is documented and approved rather than chosen at the bench."]},{"h":"8D structure for a line escape","p":["An 8D report is a structured problem-solving record.","It names the team, describes the problem with product, quantity, location and detection point, and states the interim containment. It identifies the root cause and the escape point, then defines the permanent corrective action with implementation dates, owners and effectiveness checks.","Prevention comes next: update the control plan, the FMEA, the work instructions and the inspection program so the same escape cannot recur silently."]},{"h":"Owners, dates and effectiveness metrics","p":["Every containment and corrective action needs a named owner and a due date.","Containment actions are short-term and are verified before suspect material is released. Corrective actions get a longer validation window.","Measure effectiveness with a metric tied to the defect: solder defect ppm, first pass yield, rework rate, test escape rate or audit findings. Set the target from historical performance rather than from ambition, and re-measure after the fix is in place."]},{"h":"Closing the loop","p":"Closure requires evidence that the suspect population is dispositioned, the cause is corrected, and the process is stable. Disposition options include rework, scrap, use-as-is, or customer concession, depending on contract and product requirements. Rework should follow IPC-7711/7721 and be documented. Soldering acceptance should be checked against IPC-A-610 and IPC/JEDEC J-STD-001 where applicable. Update the control plan and FMEA, train affected staff, and add an initial-article check or layered process audit for a defined period. Keep the 8D open until the effectiveness metric is met and the records are archived. The closure package should include the quarantine record, disposition record, root cause evidence, corrective action evidence, effectiveness data, and updated documents. If the customer requires a formal report, use the customer format while keeping the same evidence. If the issue is internal, the same package supports the next audit and reduces the chance of recurrence. Closure is not complete when the line is running; it is complete when the process control is updated and the evidence shows the defect is no longer escaping."}],"calc":{"task":"A line produced 1200 boards/h. A solder bridge was detected at 14:20. The last verified good inspection was at 13:50. The line stopped at 14:25. A paste lot change occurred at 13:45. Calculate the suspect window, suspect board count, and quarantine quantity. Then draft the containment, corrective action, owners, dates, and effectiveness metric for an 8D.","solution":"Start by choosing the earliest credible suspect time. The paste lot change at 13:45 is earlier than the last verified good inspection at 13:50, so use 13:45 as the conservative start of the suspect window. The line stopped at 14:25, so the suspect window is 13:45 to 14:25. The window length is 40 min. Convert the production rate to boards per minute: 1200 boards/h divided by 60 min/h equals 20 boards/min. Multiply 20 boards/min by 40 min to get 800 boards. Quarantine the 800 boards plus any work in process, finished goods, and shipped units from the same window. Containment owner: SMT process engineer; due date: 24 h. Corrective action owner: process engineer; due date: 7 days. Corrective action: verify flux density, preheat temperature, conveyor speed, and solder pot temperature against your equipment datasheet; rework per IPC-7711/7721; add an initial-article check after changeover. Effectiveness metric: solder bridge ppm; baseline 500 ppm; target 100 ppm; measured over 30 days. Close when the target is met and the control plan, FMEA, and work instructions are updated."},"defect":{"symptom":"Solder bridges between adjacent THT leads on a fine-pitch header after wave solder, detected at ICT.","cause":"Flux application was too heavy and preheat was below the range recommended by the wave solder equipment datasheet, leaving flux residue that prevented solder coalescence.","action":"Quarantine product back to the last verified good inspection, rework per IPC-7711/7721, adjust flux density and preheat within the equipment datasheet range, and add an initial-article check after each changeover."},"takeaways":["Bound the suspect population before releasing anything, because containment without a window is a promise rather than a control.","Trace the material and the process settings to each assembly, since a signature without a candidate cause leads nowhere.","Attach a metric and a date to every action, because an action without a measurement cannot be closed."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Setting the window at the detection time","instead":"Start from the last verified good point, because defects created earlier were passing until now."},{"mistake":"Quarantining without the shipping and returns data","instead":"Include shipped and returned units, because the customer holds the rest of the population."},{"mistake":"Closing an 8D on the corrective action alone","instead":"Verify effectiveness after implementation, because a fix that was not measured is a hope."}],"terms":[{"term":"Containment","def":"The immediate action that stops suspect product reaching the next process or the customer."},{"term":"Traceability","def":"The record linking an assembly to its materials, equipment, programs and time window."},{"term":"Retroactive search","def":"Identifying other units that share a suspect cause with the detected defect."},{"term":"8D","def":"An eight-discipline problem-solving report structure used for escapes and recurring defects."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m14l02"],"skills":["Lesson m14l02: Cause analysis tools: 5-Why, fishbone, fault tree, change-point analysis"]}},{"lessonId":"m14l04","moduleId":"m14","title":"8D discipline: D1–D8 content and where 8D reports fail","goals":[{"text":"Write a complete 8D with containment, corrective action, owners, dates and an effectiveness metric.","competencies":["K9"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-037"],"quiz":["q-m14l04-01","q-m14l04-02","q-m14l04-03","q-m14l04-04","q-m14l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 900 330' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Eight disciplines D1 team, D2 problem description, D3 containment, D4 root cause for occurrence and detection, D5 corrective action, D6 validation, D7 prevention, D8 closure, with the usual failure points marked'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;fill:#e2e8f0}.d{fill:#94a3b8}.t{fill:#53d1cc}.r{fill:#f44336}.o{fill:#ff9800}</style> <rect x='20' y='40' width='100' height='70' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='70' y='62' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle' fill='#53d1cc'>D1</text><text x='70' y='80' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'>team</text><text x='70' y='96' font-size='9' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>who decides</text> <rect x='128' y='40' width='100' height='70' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='178' y='62' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle' fill='#53d1cc'>D2</text><text x='178' y='80' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'>problem</text><text x='178' y='96' font-size='9' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>facts, not cause</text> <rect x='236' y='40' width='100' height='70' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='#f44336' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='286' y='62' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle' fill='#f44336'>D3</text><text x='286' y='80' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'>containment</text><text x='286' y='96' font-size='9' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>protect customer</text> <rect x='344' y='40' width='100' height='70' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='#f44336' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='394' y='62' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle' fill='#f44336'>D4</text><text x='394' y='80' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'>root cause</text><text x='394' y='96' font-size='9' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>occur + detect</text> <rect x='452' y='40' width='100' height='70' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='502' y='62' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle' fill='#53d1cc'>D5</text><text x='502' y='80' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'>corrective</text><text x='502' y='96' font-size='9' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>on proven cause</text> <rect x='560' y='40' width='100' height='70' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='610' y='62' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle' fill='#53d1cc'>D6</text><text x='610' y='80' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'>validation</text><text x='610' y='96' font-size='9' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>metric moved</text> <rect x='668' y='40' width='100' height='70' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='718' y='62' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle' fill='#53d1cc'>D7</text><text x='718' y='80' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'>prevention</text><text x='718' y='96' font-size='9' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>system updated</text> <rect x='776' y='40' width='100' height='70' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='#f44336' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='826' y='62' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle' fill='#f44336'>D8</text><text x='826' y='80' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle'>closure</text><text x='826' y='96' font-size='9' class='d' text-anchor='middle'>with evidence</text> <text x='20' y='150' font-size='12' class='r'>WHERE 8D REPORTS FAIL</text> <text x='20' y='175' font-size='11'>D3 → containment presented as correction (it reduces risk, it does not remove the cause)</text> <text x='20' y='195' font-size='11'>D4 → root cause stated without data; detection escape ignored (why did it ship?)</text> <text x='20' y='215' font-size='11'>D5-D7 → no owner, no date, vague language, no horizontal deployment to similar lines</text> <text x='20' y='235' font-size='11'>D8 → closed without an effectiveness metric or traceability to the records</text> <rect x='20' y='255' width='860' height='1' fill='#334155'/> <text x='20' y='280' font-size='11' class='t'>auditable 8D = what was found · what was done · who · when · what the data said afterwards</text> <text x='20' y='304' font-size='10' class='d'>Every D3-D7 action carries an owner, a date and a verification method.</text> </svg>","caption":"The eight disciplines D1-D8 and where 8D reports usually fail: containment sold as correction, root cause without data, closure without an effectiveness metric"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m14l04.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: eight hexagons D1 team to D8 closure with D3, D4 and D8 marked as common failure points, a whiteboard fishbone photograph, and evidence examples","caption":"The eight disciplines D1-D8 and the usual failure points"}]},{"h":"Why 8D is used","p":["An 8D report is a structured problem-solving record for internal escapes, customer complaints or repeated process defects.","In SMT and through-hole work it connects the complaint to traceable evidence: product, lot, line, process step, component, paste, stencil, feeder, reflow or wave and selective solder parameters, inspection program and test data.","The report is useful only if another engineer can follow the logic, repeat the verification and see where the conclusions came from."]},{"h":"D1 team and D2 problem description","p":["D1 names a small working team with authority and knowledge: process engineering, production, quality, materials, equipment maintenance, design, and supplier support where materials or tooling are involved.","D2 describes the problem as observed, not as a conclusion. Include product, line, process step, defect mode, quantity, date, lot, measurement method, customer impact, and whether the defect is an occurrence or a detection escape.","A problem statement that already names the cause has skipped the investigation."]},{"h":"D3 containment","p":["Containment protects the customer while the root cause is still under investigation.","Typical actions are quarantining suspect lots, sorting outgoing stock, adding inspection, holding shipments, notifying the customer, freezing process changes, and reworking only under a controlled method.","Each action needs an owner, a date and a verification method. Containment is not corrective action: it reduces risk without removing the cause."]},{"h":"D4 root cause, occurrence and detection","p":["A complete 8D separates the occurrence root cause from the detection escape.","Occurrence may involve paste rheology, stencil aperture design, reflow ramp and soak, component coplanarity, feeder accuracy, placement pressure, wave dwell, selective nozzle condition, flux activity, preheat, cooling or thermal mass. Detection may involve AOI coverage, in-circuit coverage, visual inspection limits or test escape.","Both sides need evidence, and the evidence should be traceable to lot or program records rather than recalled."]},{"h":"D5 corrective action and D6 validation","p":["Corrective action addresses the proven root cause, not the visible symptom.","Examples: changing stencil aperture geometry, updating the reflow profile inside the paste and component datasheet window, replacing a feeder, adjusting wave or selective solder parameters, updating an AOI program, adding a poka-yoke, or changing supplier control.","Implementation needs an owner and a date, and validation should show the effect on the metric rather than the completion of the task."]},{"h":"D7 prevention and D8 closure","p":["Prevention updates the management system so the class of problem is less likely to recur: PFMEA, control plan, work instructions, operator training, design rules, incoming inspection, supplier agreements, tooling maintenance, and horizontal deployment to similar products or lines.","Closure is supported by evidence: containment records, root-cause data, validation results, updated documents, and a measured effectiveness check."]},{"h":"Where 8D reports fail","p":["The usual failures are presenting containment as correction, stating a root cause without data, ignoring the detection escape, missing owners or dates, vague language, missing traceability, and closure without an effectiveness metric.","Another frequent failure is no horizontal deployment, so the same defect returns on a similar product.","A good 8D is auditable: it shows what was found, what was done, who did it, when, and what the data said afterwards."]}],"calc":{"task":"Write a complete 8D for an SMT line where a customer reports tombstoned chip resistors on a production lot. Include containment, corrective action, owners, dates, and an effectiveness metric.","solution":"D1 Team: SMT process engineer as lead, production supervisor, quality engineer, materials engineer, AOI programmer, and supplier representative if paste or component data are needed.\nD2 Problem: customer reported tombstoned chip resistors on product X, line Y, reflow process step, defect mode tombstoning, lot traceability recorded, quantity reported by customer, measurement method visual and AOI, date of complaint recorded. The problem is both occurrence and detection because the defect reached the customer.\nD3 Containment: quarantine suspect lots, sort outgoing stock, add AOI and visual inspection, notify customer, freeze process changes, and hold shipments until containment is verified. Owner: quality engineer. Date: 2026-01-15. Verification: audit sorted lots and outgoing inspection records.\nD4 Root cause: occurrence root cause identified from data as unbalanced wetting caused by stencil aperture imbalance and reflow ramp rate outside the preferred window for the paste and component. Detection root cause identified as AOI program did not reliably flag tombstone on small chip components.\nD5 Corrective action: update stencil aperture design, adjust reflow profile within the paste and component datasheet window, update AOI program, and add feeder and placement verification to the setup checklist. Owner: SMT process engineer. Date: 2026-01-22.\nD6 Validation: run validation lots, measure tombstone defect rate, and compare to the effectiveness metric. Effectiveness metric: tombstone defect rate in ppm over validation lots, target from customer specification or internal control limit. Owner: quality engineer. Date: 2026-02-05.\nD7 Prevention: update PFMEA, control plan, work instructions, operator training, and horizontal deployment to similar products using the same component family or stencil design. Owner: process engineering manager. Date: 2026-02-12.\nD8 Closure: close when containment is verified, corrective action is validated, documents are updated, and the effectiveness metric is met. Owner: quality manager. Date: 2026-02-19."},"defect":{"symptom":"AOI flags tombstoned chip resistors on one side of the PCB after reflow, and the defect rate rises on the night shift.","cause":"Stencil aperture for one pad is too large and the reflow ramp is too fast, causing unbalanced wetting; the AOI program did not detect tombstone on small components.","action":"Quarantine suspect lots, sort outgoing stock, reduce aperture imbalance, adjust reflow profile per paste and component datasheets, update AOI program, validate with short runs, and monitor defect rate in ppm."},"takeaways":["Keep occurrence and detection separate through D4, because one corrective action rarely closes both.","Treat containment as a holding action, since releasing on containment alone leaves the cause in the process.","Close on a measured metric, because a completed action list is not evidence that the defect rate moved."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Writing the cause into the D2 problem statement","instead":"Describe what was observed, because a leading statement biases every later step."},{"mistake":"Closing on the corrective action date","instead":"Verify the metric afterwards, since implementation and effect are different events."},{"mistake":"Skipping horizontal deployment to similar products","instead":"Apply the fix to similar products, because the same design repeats across the line."}],"terms":[{"term":"Occurrence root cause","def":"The process condition that created the defect."},{"term":"Detection escape","def":"The defect that passed the inspection or test meant to catch it."},{"term":"Poka-yoke","def":"A design or process feature that prevents an error from being made."},{"term":"Horizontal deployment","def":"Applying a corrective action to other products, lines or sites with the same risk."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m14l03"],"skills":["Lesson m14l03: Containment and traceability: quarantine scope, lot trace, retroactive search"]}},{"lessonId":"m14l05","moduleId":"m14","title":"Corrective vs preventive action and effectiveness verification","goals":[{"text":"Write a complete 8D with containment, corrective action, owners, dates and an effectiveness metric.","competencies":["K9"]},{"text":"Feed the result back into PFMEA, control plan and the auditor checklist through change control.","competencies":["K9","K10"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-037"],"quiz":["q-m14l05-01","q-m14l05-02","q-m14l05-03","q-m14l05-04","q-m14l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m14l05.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: flow from nonconformity detected through corrective and preventive action to an effectiveness verification gate with a reopen loop, and a macro photograph of a reworked chip resistor joint","caption":"Corrective vs preventive action, verified with data at the effectiveness gate"}]},{"h":"Corrective and preventive action","p":["Corrective action responds to a detected nonconformity, such as a solder defect found by AOI, X-ray, functional test or a customer complaint.","Preventive action addresses a credible potential failure before it happens: a stencil aperture close to a paste-release limit, or a wave pot temperature drifting towards its control limit.","In an 8D, containment protects the customer before the root cause is known, corrective action removes the cause, and preventive action keeps the next product out of the same hole."]},{"h":"8D content an auditor can trace","p":["A usable 8D records the problem statement, affected part numbers and lots, containment quantity and disposition, team roles, root cause, corrective action, implementation evidence, effectiveness metric and closure decision.","Name owners by role: SMT process engineer, line supervisor, quality engineer, PFMEA owner. Use actual dates from the CAPA or 8D record, not assumed ones, and keep the evidence attached rather than summarised.","An auditor follows the chain from the complaint to the changed control, so every link needs a document."]},{"h":"Containment that is proportionate","p":["Containment can be quarantine of suspect lots, full inspection, rework, a temporary parameter lock, or a hold on shipping.","Match the method to the risk: a safety-related joint defect may need full inspection and customer notification, while a cosmetic issue may need sampling and disposition by a documented plan.","Verify containment by checking the inspected population against the population you believed you had. A containment claim with no count behind it is a statement of intent."]},{"h":"Occurrence, detection and systemic cause","p":["For SMT and through-hole defects, separate the cause that created the defect from the cause that let it escape.","Occurrence causes include paste volume, stencil aperture design, reflow profile, wave solder temperature, flux activity, component coplanarity, feeder calibration and handling damage. Detection causes include AOI program coverage, X-ray sampling, test coverage and operator judgement. Systemic causes include missing controls, unclear work instructions and absent verification.","All three belong in the report, because each needs a different action."]},{"h":"Choosing a corrective action that survives production","p":["Select the action that addresses the verified root cause and can be sustained.","Examples: changing a stencil aperture, adding a poka-yoke fixture, revising a reflow profile, replacing a worn feeder, changing a supplier lot, or adding a sensor check.","A parameter adjustment is valid where the root cause is drift or misconfiguration, provided the data and the equipment datasheet support the setting, and provided the correction cannot be quietly reversed on the next shift."]},{"h":"Effectiveness verification before closing","p":["Define the effectiveness metric before implementation.","Common metrics are defect rate in ppm, first pass yield, AOI escape rate, X-ray void percentage, wetting quality and functional test failure rate. Verification needs enough production time and sample size to detect recurrence, and the acceptance criterion should be agreed with quality and, where required, the customer.","If the metric misses the target, the 8D stays open."]},{"h":"Example closure record","p":"A practical closure record can show: containment date 2026-01-15, owner line supervisor; corrective action implementation date 2026-01-22, owner SMT process engineer; effectiveness verification date 2026-02-05, owner quality engineer; effectiveness metric defect rate in ppm with target 500 ppm; PFMEA and control plan update owner PFMEA owner. The dates, owners, and metric should be copied into the 8D from the CAPA or change control record."},{"h":"Feeding the result into PFMEA, control plan and audit checklist","p":["When the 8D closes, update the PFMEA with the failure mode, cause, current controls, occurrence, detection and reaction plan.","Update the control plan with the new parameter, inspection method, frequency and reaction plan. Add or revise auditor checklist questions so the next audit verifies the new control.","Use change control to approve the revision, train the affected personnel, and record the effective date."]}],"calc":{"task":"A THT wave solder line had 180 defects in 12000 boards before corrective action. After corrective action, 24 defects occurred in 15000 boards. Calculate the before and after defect rate in ppm, the reduction, and whether the after rate meets a target of 500 ppm.","solution":"Before rate = 180 / 12000 = 0.015 = 15000 ppm. After rate = 24 / 15000 = 0.0016 = 1600 ppm. Reduction = (15000 - 1600) / 15000 = 0.8933 = 89.3 percent. The after rate is 1600 ppm, which is above the target of 500 ppm, so the corrective action is not yet effective by the agreed metric. Continue monitoring, increase sample size, or strengthen the corrective action before closing the 8D."},"defect":{"symptom":"THT wave solder joints on fine-pitch IC leads show insufficient wetting and dewetting after wave solder, with AOI flagging a higher reject rate on a panel side.","cause":"The wave solder pot temperature reading was lower than the verified actual temperature because the thermocouple had drifted, and the setpoint was adjusted without a documented change control record. The resulting thermal profile did not meet the wetting requirement for the lead finish and flux used.","action":"Quarantine suspect boards, recalibrate the thermocouple, verify the pot temperature against the equipment datasheet, restore the approved setpoint through change control, revalidate with wetting and X-ray checks, update the PFMEA and control plan, and add a daily thermocouple check to the auditor checklist."},"takeaways":["Separate corrective from preventive action, because the complaint and the risk are different populations.","Size containment to the risk and prove it by count, since an unverified containment is a hope.","Define the effectiveness metric before the fix, because a target chosen afterwards always looks met."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Closing an 8D when the action is implemented","instead":"Verify the metric first, since implementation is not an effect."},{"mistake":"Adjusting a parameter without change control","instead":"Record the change, because an undocumented setting cannot be restored after a shift change."},{"mistake":"Updating the 8D but not the control plan","instead":"Revise the control plan and audit checklist, because the fix lives in the documents the line follows."}],"terms":[{"term":"CAPA","def":"Corrective and preventive action, the quality-system record behind the 8D."},{"term":"PFMEA","def":"Process failure mode and effects analysis, the risk document the 8D updates."},{"term":"Effectiveness metric","def":"The measurable rate used to prove that a corrective action worked."},{"term":"Change control","def":"The approval route for a documented change to a process or specification."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m14l04"],"skills":["Lesson m14l04: 8D discipline: D1–D8 content and where 8D reports fail"]}},{"lessonId":"m14l06","moduleId":"m14","title":"Closing the loop: PFMEA, control plan, auditor checklist, change control","goals":[{"text":"Feed the result back into PFMEA, control plan and the auditor checklist through change control.","competencies":["K9","K10"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-008","src-009","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m14l06-01","q-m14l06-02","q-m14l06-03","q-m14l06-04","q-m14l06-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m14l06.webp","alt":"A loop diagram linking corrective action to a process failure mode analysis update, a control plan revision, an auditor checklist and change control","caption":"Closing the loop: PFMEA, control plan, auditor checklist and change control"}]},{"h":"Closing the loop means changing the control system","p":["A corrective action is not complete when the line returns to normal output.","It is complete when the learning is written into the PFMEA, the control plan, the auditor checklist and the change-control record.","The PFMEA records the failure mode, cause, detection method and risk priority. The control plan records the process parameter, specification, measurement method, sample size, frequency and reaction plan. The checklist asks whether the control is being executed, and the change record shows who approved it and when."]},{"h":"Change control against uncontrolled drift","p":"Change control is the route by which a process change is proposed, reviewed, approved, implemented, verified, and closed. In electronics assembly, changes may include paste type, stencil aperture, reflow profile, wave solder temperature, flux density, conveyor speed, component substitution, feeder change, nozzle change, placement program revision, or inspection criteria. A change should be controlled when it can affect fit, form, function, reliability, solderability, or inspection acceptance. For example, a change in peak reflow temperature or dwell time above the liquidus can affect solder joint microstructure and should be reviewed against the paste and component datasheets and against IPC-7530 Guidelines for Convection Reflow Soldering where applicable."},{"h":"PFMEA should reflect the current process","p":["The PFMEA is a living risk document.","After an investigation, update the failure mode when a new cause is found, update the cause when the root cause differs from the original assumption, and update the detection method when the current inspection cannot reliably find the defect.","Update severity, occurrence or detection ratings only when evidence justifies it. A rating changed to make the chart look better is worse than no rating at all."]},{"h":"Control plan with measurable controls","p":["A control plan cannot say only that the operator checks quality.","It states what is checked, how often, with what method, against what limit, and what happens when the result falls outside specification.","For paste printing that means stencil cleanliness, paste viscosity or rheology per the paste datasheet, print alignment, paste height, paste volume and post-print inspection. For reflow it means zone temperatures, conveyor speed, atmosphere and profile verification."]},{"h":"Auditor checklist","p":["The auditor checklist asks whether the control exists and whether it is followed.","Useful questions: is the current revision of the control plan available at the station, are measured values recorded at the required frequency, are out-of-limit results handled per the reaction plan, are calibration labels valid, is rejected material segregated, and are change-control records complete?","A checklist that only asks whether a document exists audits the filing cabinet."]},{"h":"A documented change-control package","p":["A practical package contains the problem statement, defect data, root cause, proposed change, affected documents, risk assessment, verification plan, implementation date, responsible owner and closure evidence.","For an SMT example, a new paste lot causing insufficient solder on fine-pitch QFN pads brings in paste lot traceability, a stencil design review, a reflow profile comparison against IPC-7530, first-article results and the updated control plan."]},{"h":"Acceptance criteria and rework rules","p":["When updating inspection criteria, use the applicable acceptance standard and the customer or internal specification.","IPC-A-610 gives the acceptance classes and visual criteria for solder joints, components and cleanliness, and IPC-7711/7721 gives the rework and repair methods.","Where a change affects rework, the rework instruction changes with it, so the new criteria and the old method do not sit side by side in the same document set."]},{"h":"Close with verification, not only approval","p":["After implementation, verify that the defect rate has fallen and that no new failure mode has appeared.","Verification may use first-article inspection, in-process data, final inspection data, field return data, reliability test data or audit findings.","A temporary change carries an expiry date or a condition for permanent adoption in the change record itself, so it cannot become permanent by neglect."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 0402 SMT placement line shows a tombstoning rate of 180 ppm on a fine-pitch board. Investigation shows the peak reflow temperature was 232 °C and time above liquidus was 48 s, while the paste datasheet recommends a peak temperature range of 235 °C to 245 °C and time above liquidus of 40 s to 70 s. A change-control proposal raises the peak temperature to 240 °C and time above liquidus to 55 s. Calculate the change in peak temperature and time above liquidus, determine whether the proposed values are inside the paste datasheet range, and define the minimum verification sample needed to confirm that tombstoning is reduced to below 50 ppm with 95% confidence if the observed rate after the change is 0 defects in the sample.","solution":"1. Calculate the peak temperature change: proposed peak temperature minus current peak temperature = 240 °C - 232 °C = 8 °C. 2. Calculate the time above liquidus change: proposed time above liquidus minus current time above liquidus = 55 s - 48 s = 7 s. 3. Check the proposed peak temperature against the paste datasheet range: 235 °C to 245 °C. The proposed 240 °C is inside the range. 4. Check the proposed time above liquidus against the paste datasheet range: 40 s to 70 s. The proposed 55 s is inside the range. 5. For a zero-defect verification sample, use the binomial rule for zero failures: the probability of observing zero defects if the true defect rate is p is (1 - p)^n. To demonstrate with 95% confidence that the true rate is below p = 50 ppm = 0.00005, solve (1 - 0.00005)^n <= 0.05. 6. Take natural logs: n >= ln(0.05) / ln(1 - 0.00005). 7. ln(0.05) = -2.9957. ln(1 - 0.00005) ≈ -0.000050001. 8. n >= -2.9957 / -0.000050001 ≈ 59,913. 9. Round up to a practical sample size: inspect at least 60,000 units with zero tombstoning defects to claim 95% confidence that the true tombstoning rate is below 50 ppm. 10. If 60,000 units are not practical, use a lower confidence level, a higher target ppm, or combine inspection with process data such as reflow profile logs, paste volume data, and placement accuracy data. 11. Update the PFMEA cause to include reflow profile deviation, update the control plan to require profile verification at the defined frequency, update the auditor checklist to verify profile logs and calibration, and close the change-control record only after the verification result is accepted."},"defect":{"symptom":"After a paste change, the line shows intermittent insufficient solder on 0.5 mm pitch QFN pads. Final inspection passes visually, but X-ray shows reduced heel fillet and small voiding on some joints. The defect rate rises from about 20 ppm to about 150 ppm over three shifts.","cause":"The new paste has a different viscosity and slump behavior, but the stencil aperture and reflow profile were not requalified. The control plan still uses the old paste print parameters and the auditor checklist does not require paste lot traceability or post-print paste height verification. The PFMEA did not include paste rheology change as a cause for insufficient solder on fine-pitch QFN pads.","action":"Open a change-control record for the paste substitution. Requalify the stencil aperture and reflow profile using the new paste datasheet and verify against IPC-7530 Guidelines for Convection Reflow Soldering where applicable. Add paste lot traceability, paste height or volume measurement, and QFN X-ray sampling to the control plan. Update the PFMEA to include paste rheology change as a cause of insufficient solder. Add a checklist item requiring the auditor to verify that the current paste lot, stencil revision, reflow profile, and X-ray results match the approved control plan. Run a verification lot and close the change only when the defect rate returns to the target range and the documents are updated."},"takeaways":["Write the learning into the documents the line follows, because an action that lives only in the 8D changes nothing.","Attach a measurable control to every change, since \"operator checks\" is not a control.","Verify the defect rate after implementation, because a new failure mode often arrives with the fix."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Approving a change and skipping the control plan update","instead":"Update the plan and the checklist, because the station follows the plan."},{"mistake":"Leaving a temporary change in place indefinitely","instead":"Set an expiry date, since temporary settings become permanent by default."},{"mistake":"Editing PFMEA ratings to reflect the hoped-for improvement","instead":"Rate from evidence, because the rating drives the next risk decision."}],"terms":[{"term":"Control plan","def":"The document stating what is measured, how often, against what limit, and the reaction when it fails."},{"term":"PFMEA","def":"Process failure mode and effects analysis, the living risk record for the process."},{"term":"Reaction plan","def":"The defined response when a measured value falls outside its limit."},{"term":"First-article inspection","def":"A full check of the first unit produced after a change."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m14l05"],"skills":["Lesson m14l05: Corrective vs preventive action and effectiveness verification"]}},{"lessonId":"m14l07","moduleId":"m14","title":"8D workshop: full case from symptom to closure","goals":[{"text":"Structure a defect problem with measured data (taxonomy, Pareto by stage, measurement integrity) before analysis.","competencies":["K9"]},{"text":"Separate root cause, contributing causes and the detection escape in a structured cause analysis.","competencies":["K9"]},{"text":"Write a complete 8D with containment, corrective action, owners, dates and an effectiveness metric.","competencies":["K9"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m14l07-01","q-m14l07-02","q-m14l07-03","q-m14l07-04","q-m14l07-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m14l07.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: D1 to D8 case timeline with evidence at each step, Pareto by stage, AOI measurement integrity check, root cause / contributing / detection escape split, an evidence review photo and closure by removing the action","caption":"8D workshop: symptom to closure with evidence at every step"}]},{"h":"Define the symptom with measured data","p":["Start with the observed defect, not a suspected cause.","Record product, lot, stage, time in seconds, operator, equipment, and the measured attribute: solder volume in cubic millimetres, component offset in millimetres, hole fill as a percentage.","Use a taxonomy that separates symptom families: insufficient solder, excess solder, misalignment, voiding, non-wet. The taxonomy has to stay stable across lots, or nothing can be compared later."]},{"h":"Pareto by stage before root cause","p":["Sort defect counts by process stage: paste print, placement, reflow, wave, inspection.","A Pareto by stage shows where the largest measured loss is, not where the most obvious cause is. A stage with few defects but high cost or high field risk stays visible anyway.","Use the stage Pareto to set containment priority and to decide where to collect the next data, not to name the culprit."]},{"h":"Check measurement integrity first","p":["A Pareto is only as good as the measurement behind it.","Verify the inspection method, the sample plan and the agreement between operators. For dimensional checks, confirm that the gauge resolution in millimetres is fine enough for the tolerance, and that measurement uncertainty is small against process variation.","For visual defects, use boundary samples and cross-check independent inspectors. Where disagreement is large, fix the measurement before sorting the data."]},{"h":"Separate root cause, contributing causes and detection escape","p":["The root cause is the process condition that, once removed, prevents recurrence under normal conditions.","Contributing causes make the defect more likely or more severe: paste viscosity drift, feeder wear, thermal profile variation in degrees Celsius, conveyor speed variation in millimetres per second. The detection escape explains why the defect was not found before shipment, such as a missing AOI program revision or a sampling plan that never covered the feature.","Each category gets its own action, and the report keeps them apart. A complete 8D report must address all three, because a corrected process with an open escape still ships defects."]},{"h":"Containment must be measurable and time-bound","p":["Containment protects the customer while the cause is being confirmed: quarantine, full inspection, rework, or a temporary process limit.","State the containment owner, the start time, the review interval and the exit condition. The exit condition rests on measured evidence, such as a clean inspection window or verified process capability, not on elapsed time alone."]},{"h":"Corrective action changes the process","p":["Corrective action removes the root cause and closes the detection escape.","Examples: changing the stencil design, updating feeder calibration, adding a profile check, modifying an AOI program. Each action has an owner, a due date and a verification method.","Verification should show that the action works under normal production conditions, and that the defect returns if the action is removed — which is the honest test of whether it was a cause."]},{"h":"Closure with an effectiveness metric","p":["Closure is not complete until the effectiveness metric is defined and measured.","A common metric is no escapes inside a defined verification window, with the window chosen from production volume and risk. Record the baseline defect rate, the post-action defect rate and the inspection method used.","If the metric is not met, reopen the analysis rather than closing the report."]}],"calc":{"task":"A line records 100 defects in a production window: paste print 40, placement 30, reflow 20, wave 10. Calculate the stage Pareto and cumulative percentage. Before using the result, confirm that the inspection method has adequate resolution and repeatability for the measured attribute; verify against your paste/equipment datasheet.","solution":"Total is 100. Paste print share is 40 divided by 100, which is 40 percent, cumulative 40 percent. Placement share is 30 divided by 100, which is 30 percent, cumulative 70 percent. Reflow share is 20 divided by 100, which is 20 percent, cumulative 90 percent. Wave share is 10 divided by 100, which is 10 percent, cumulative 100 percent."},"defect":{"symptom":"Wave solder line shows insufficient hole fill on through-hole connectors, with exposed land visible at the bottom of the hole.","cause":"Root cause is a preheat temperature setpoint drift in degrees Celsius that reduced flux activation; contributing causes are board loading density and conveyor speed variation in millimetres per second; detection escape is that final visual inspection did not include a hole fill check aligned with IPC-A-610 acceptance criteria.","action":"Containment: quarantine suspect lots and perform full inspection with boundary samples; owner: wave solder process engineer; date: containment due at end of shift. Corrective action: restore preheat setpoint, add a logged preheat check, and update inspection to include hole fill criteria from IPC-A-610; owner: wave solder process engineer; date: corrective action due before next monthly review. Effectiveness metric: no escapes in the next 10 verification lots."},"takeaways":["Fix the measurement before reading the Pareto, because inspection noise sorts into a chart like real signal.","Set the containment exit on evidence, since a time-based exit simply ends attention.","Prove the cause by removing the action, because a fix that cannot be reversed cannot be demonstrated."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Reading the stage Pareto as a diagnosis","instead":"Use it to choose the next measurement, since it ranks symptoms and not causes."},{"mistake":"Closing on a clean week of output","instead":"Measure the metric across a defined window, because a short quiet period proves little."},{"mistake":"Keeping a containment in place with no exit condition","instead":"Define the evidence that ends it, because permanent containment becomes the process."}],"terms":[{"term":"Stage Pareto","def":"Defect counts sorted by the process stage where they were detected or attributed."},{"term":"Boundary sample","def":"A physical specimen showing the limit of acceptability, used to align inspectors."},{"term":"Detection escape","def":"The third cause category of an 8D: why the defect passed the inspection or test meant to catch it."},{"term":"Process capability","def":"How well a process holds its specification, stated as a measurable index."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m14l06"],"skills":["Lesson m14l06: Closing the loop: PFMEA, control plan, auditor checklist, change control"]}},{"lessonId":"m15l01","moduleId":"m15","title":"How designers read process feedback: language, format, what gets acted on","goals":[{"text":"Convert a process limitation into a specific, testable design rule change.","competencies":["K10"]},{"text":"Write and defend an ECN/DCR request against typical design objections.","competencies":["K10"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-024","src-025"],"quiz":["q-m15l01-01","q-m15l01-02","q-m15l01-03","q-m15l01-04","q-m15l01-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"h":"Make feedback actionable","p":["Designers act on feedback written as a constraint, not a complaint.","A useful process note names the affected component or layer, the observed failure mode, the measurable process variable and the proposed design rule. \"Small chip passives tombstone on the right side of the panel when paste apertures are asymmetric\" is actionable; \"the board is bad\" is not.","The request should be short enough to read quickly and specific enough that a designer can change a rule file, a land pattern or a stencil without asking for clarification."],"blocks":[{"t":"figure","svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 900 280' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Process capability distribution'><style>text{font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace}</style><line x1='60' y1='230' x2='860' y2='230' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='250' y1='230' x2='250' y2='40' stroke='#f44336' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='6 4'/><line x1='650' y1='230' x2='650' y2='40' stroke='#f44336' stroke-width='1.5' stroke-dasharray='6 4'/><line x1='450' y1='230' x2='450' y2='50' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='1' stroke-dasharray='3 3'/><path d='M120,228 C260,220 330,70 450,70 C570,70 640,220 780,228' fill='rgba(83,209,204,.1)' stroke='#53d1cc' stroke-width='2.5'/><text x='250' y='30' fill='#f44336' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>LSL</text><text x='650' y='30' fill='#f44336' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>USL</text><text x='450' y='30' fill='#53d1cc' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>MEAN</text><text x='450' y='258' fill='#cbd5e1' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle'>Cp = (USL - LSL) / 6 sigma — spread vs tolerance</text><text x='450' y='276' fill='#94a3b8' font-size='12' text-anchor='middle'>Cpk also asks: is the mean centered?</text><text x='60' y='20' fill='#edf2f7' font-size='14' font-weight='600'>PROCESS CAPABILITY</text></svg>","caption":"Capability compares the process spread to the tolerance band"},{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m15l01.webp","alt":"A process note written as a constraint, with the affected component, the observed condition and the requested change highlighted","caption":"How designers read process feedback: language, format and what gets acted on"},{"t":"table","caption":"Cp / Cpk interpretation — thresholds are industry convention","head":["Index","Reading","Action"],"rows":[["< 1.00","Spread wider than tolerance","Fix process before releasing"],["1.00 - 1.33","Marginal","Tighten control, increase inspection"],["1.33 - 1.67","Adequate for most classes","Monitor"],[">= 1.67","Excellent / Class 3 candidate","Reduce inspection burden"]]}]},{"h":"Translate process language into design variables","p":["Use language that maps a process observation onto a design variable.","Instead of \"the solder is not wetting well\", name what controls wetting: pad finish, copper area, paste aperture area, stencil thickness, reflow ramp, component mass.","On SMT the designer can change land-pattern geometry, paste apertures, copper balance and thermal relief. On through-hole work they can change hole size, annular ring, solder mask clearance and component selection. The designer cannot change \"wetting\"; they can change those variables."]},{"h":"The format designers can approve","p":["The format that gets action is an ECN or DCR with a clear decision point.","Start with the problem statement, then evidence, affected part numbers, proposed change, test plan, cost, schedule and risk. Name the exact layer or file: paste stencil layer, solder mask layer, copper layer, component library, assembly drawing.","Where the change touches acceptance, land pattern, board fabrication or BGA design, name the relevant IPC standard and the clause it applies."]},{"h":"Make the change bounded and testable","p":["Designers prioritise changes that reduce a measured risk, fit the existing board and can be verified without a full requalification.","\"Make the pads bigger\" invites rejection, because it can affect routing, impedance, assembly density or solder mask bridges. \"Increase the paste aperture area on the low-volume side by 8 % to 12 % and verify SPI paste volume inside the process window\" gives a bounded change and a test that proves it.","The difference between the two requests is the difference between a wish and a proposal."]},{"h":"Convert a limitation into a design rule","p":["To turn a process limitation into a design rule, identify the physical cause, express it as a design variable, and set a measurable limit.","For tombstoning the causes are unequal paste volume, unequal pad copper area, asymmetric thermal mass and placement offset. A testable rule reads: for small chip passives, the paste apertures on both ends shall have areas within ±10 % of each other, the copper pads shall be symmetric, and the placement centre shall be within ±0.05 mm of the pad centre.","Each clause is measurable, which is what makes it a rule rather than an intention."]},{"h":"Apply the same logic to THT","p":["On wave soldering, insufficient fill and solder balls follow from hole-to-lead clearance, pad size and flux activity.","A design rule can specify a clearance range, an annular ring and the solder mask web between pads. Because hole plating, drill wear and the wave profile vary, state the range and verify it against the board fabricator and the assembly equipment.","Ask the fabricator for the expected drilled hole tolerance and plated hole size, then set the rule from the achievable process rather than from a nominal drawing value."]},{"h":"Defending the ECN or DCR","p":["Expect objections: the board worked before, the change costs money, the supplier cannot support it, test coverage is incomplete, the change touches another product.","Answer with data and a bounded plan: defect rate before and after, affected part numbers, test sample, fallback if the change fails, and the parts or files that will not change.","Where the change is too large for one step, propose a pilot panel or a product with high volume enough to prove it first."]},{"h":"Close with a rule and a proof","p":["A good process feedback note ends with what the designer must change and what the process team will prove.","Example: \"Change the small chip passive paste apertures to symmetric rectangles with equal area; process will verify SPI paste volume, AOI tombstone rate and reflow profile on 300 boards before release.\"","That gives the designer a rule change and the process team a testable commitment, and neither is asked to do the other's job."]}],"calc":{"task":"A small chip passive tombstone issue appears on 18 boards out of 4000 boards. Convert this into a design rule change and an ECN/DCR request. Determine the current defect rate, a target rate, the sample size needed to show the target with 95% confidence if no tombstones are observed, and the paste aperture area balance needed to support the rule.","solution":"Current rate: 18 / 4000 = 0.0045 = 0.45%. Target: set a target of <=0.10% (1000 ppm) based on line capability and risk; verify against internal quality targets. Sample size: for a no-defect demonstration at 95% confidence, use the binomial upper bound. With confidence = 0.95 and target = 0.0010, n = ln(0.05) / ln(0.999) = 2995. Round up to 3000 boards. Design rule: for small chip passives, paste apertures on both ends shall have equal area within ±10%; for a nominal aperture area of 0.011 mm^2, the allowable range is 0.0099 mm^2 to 0.0121 mm^2. Process checks: SPI paste volume per pad within ±15% of nominal, placement center within ±0.05 mm of pad center, and reflow profile within paste and component limits. ECN/DCR: change the paste stencil layer for small chip passives to symmetric apertures, remove asymmetric copper or thermal relief on the affected pads, and add SPI verification. Defense: show the current 0.45% rate, the target 0.10% rate, the 3000-board no-defect test, the affected part numbers, the fallback to the previous stencil if the test fails, and the fact that the change does not alter the BOM or board outline."},"defect":{"symptom":"AOI flags tombstones on small chip passives on the right side of the panel after reflow, and SPI shows paste volume low on the left pads of the same components.","cause":"Asymmetric paste apertures or stencil aperture damage create unequal solder volume on the two ends of the component, and placement offset may increase the imbalance.","action":"Stop release of the affected stencil, quarantine suspect boards, rework or scrap as required, correct the stencil apertures to symmetric geometry, verify SPI paste volume within ±15% of nominal, and update the design rule to require aperture area balance within ±10%."},"takeaways":["Name the design variable rather than the symptom, because a designer can only change a layer, a dimension or a rule.","Bound the request with a number and a test, since an unbounded change asks for trust instead of evidence.","Offer the proof with the request, because a designer approves a change they can see verified."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Sending a defect report with no design variable in it","instead":"Convert the symptom to a variable, because nothing on the drawing changes otherwise."},{"mistake":"Asking for a rule with no measurable limit","instead":"Set the limit in percent or millimetres, since a rule that cannot be measured cannot be audited."},{"mistake":"Raising the same request every quarter","instead":"Close it with a rule and a verification, because an open request loses credibility."}],"terms":[{"term":"ECN","def":"Engineering change notice, the formal record that authorises a design change."},{"term":"Design variable","def":"A drawing, layer, dimension or library attribute the designer controls."},{"term":"Design rule","def":"A measurable constraint applied during layout or stencil design."},{"term":"Pilot panel","def":"A limited build used to prove a change before full release."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m14l07"],"skills":["Lesson m14l07: 8D workshop: full case from symptom to closure"]}},{"lessonId":"m15l02","moduleId":"m15","title":"The evidence pack: measured data, images with scale, defect rate, cost of poor quality","goals":[{"text":"Build an evidence pack that quantifies the process impact of the current design.","competencies":["K10"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-024","src-025"],"quiz":["q-m15l02-01","q-m15l02-02","q-m15l02-03","q-m15l02-04","q-m15l02-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m15l02.webp","alt":"Reference sheet: macro photograph of a solder bridge between QFP leads with a scale bar, a measurement table, a defect-rate trend with control limits and a cost-of-poor-quality donut chart","caption":"The evidence pack: measurement, image with scale, defect rate with its basis, cost of poor quality"}]},{"h":"Purpose of the evidence pack","p":["An evidence pack is a compact set of records showing what the SMT or through-hole process is actually doing, not what it was intended to do.","For a working process engineer it lets another engineer or a manager answer three questions without visiting the line: what is being measured, what the physical evidence shows, and what the financial consequence of the defect is.","If the pack cannot answer those three, it is a folder rather than evidence."]},{"h":"Measured data that belongs in the pack","p":["Measured data covers both process parameters and output quality.","Typical inputs are stencil thickness, aperture area, paste volume, reflow peak temperature, time above liquidus, placement accuracy and oven profile repeatability. Typical outputs are joint quality, defect counts, first-pass yield, rework count and scrap count.","For each parameter record the unit, the measurement method, the sample size, the time window and the acceptance limit, because a number without those is not comparable to anything."]},{"h":"Images with scale and context","p":["An image is evidence only when it can be interpreted later.","Each image carries a scale reference: a calibrated ruler, a scale bar or a known component dimension, plus the magnification or field of view. The file name or caption identifies board, panel, station, defect type, date and shift.","For solder defects, take a close-up and a wider view showing the component, adjacent pads and the surrounding land, because the close-up alone cannot show where it sits. Include the stencil side, the board side and, where it matters, a 3D measurement or a cross-section."]},{"h":"Defect rate and defect density","p":["Define the defect rate clearly.","A common board-level metric is defects per million opportunities, and the opportunity count must be stated: one joint, one pad, one component or one inspection point.","On surface-mount work, defect density is often more useful than a pass percentage, because it compares across board sizes and mixes where a simple percentage does not."]},{"h":"Sampling and time window","p":["A single shift misleads when the line runs a mixed product or a changeover happened.","State the sampling window: one shift, one day, one week, one production order. For a process comparison, use matched conditions — same product, same line, same paste lot family, same stencil, similar board mix.","Where matched conditions are impossible, state the differences and treat the comparison as indicative rather than conclusive."]},{"h":"Cost of poor quality","p":["Cost of poor quality includes scrap, rework labour, inspection labour, line downtime, expedited freight, warranty or field failure exposure, and lost capacity.","For a line-level estimate start with direct costs: scrapped boards, rework time, additional inspection time and machine time consumed by rework. For a product-level estimate add downstream test failures, repair and customer returns.","Keep the calculation transparent enough that someone can check one of the terms."]},{"h":"Linking evidence to process impact","p":["The pack connects data, images, defect rate and cost into one argument.","For insufficient solder, show the measured paste volume distribution, images of the affected joints, the defect rate by station or product, and the rework cost per affected board.","The conclusion states the impact in operational terms: yield loss, throughput loss, rework burden, or risk of escape."]},{"h":"Presentation and traceability","p":["The pack can be a short report, a spreadsheet or a slide set, and it must be traceable.","Include a cover page with product name, line, date range, author and revision, attach the raw data or give the path where it is stored, and use consistent units, preferably SI: millimetres, micrometres, grams, seconds and degrees Celsius.","For images, embed captions in the report and keep the original files with their metadata."]}],"calc":{"task":"Build a simple cost-of-poor-quality estimate for a surface-mount line producing 12,000 boards per week. The line has a first-pass yield of 96.5 percent. Of the failed boards, 70 percent are reworked successfully, 20 percent are scrapped, and 10 percent are held for further analysis. Rework labor is 18 minutes per board at a loaded labor rate of 45 euros per hour. Scrap cost is 120 euros per board. Additional inspection and handling cost is 3.50 euros per failed board. Calculate weekly rework labor cost, scrap cost, inspection and handling cost, total cost of poor quality, and cost per good board.","solution":"1. Calculate failed boards per week: 12,000 boards per week multiplied by 1 minus 0.965 equals 12,000 multiplied by 0.035 equals 420 failed boards per week. 2. Calculate reworked boards: 420 failed boards multiplied by 0.70 equals 294 reworked boards per week. 3. Calculate rework labor hours: 294 boards multiplied by 18 minutes per board equals 5,292 minutes. 5,292 minutes divided by 60 equals 88.2 hours per week. 4. Calculate rework labor cost: 88.2 hours multiplied by 45 euros per hour equals 3,969 euros per week. 5. Calculate scrapped boards: 420 failed boards multiplied by 0.20 equals 84 scrapped boards per week. 6. Calculate scrap cost: 84 boards multiplied by 120 euros per board equals 10,080 euros per week. 7. Calculate inspection and handling cost: 420 failed boards multiplied by 3.50 euros per board equals 1,470 euros per week. 8. Calculate total cost of poor quality: 3,969 euros plus 10,080 euros plus 1,470 euros equals 15,519 euros per week. 9. Calculate good boards per week: 12,000 boards minus 420 failed boards equals 11,580 good boards per week. 10. Calculate cost of poor quality per good board: 15,519 euros divided by 11,580 good boards equals 1.34 euros per good board. 11. Interpretation: the current process consumes 15,519 euros per week in direct rework, scrap, and handling, equivalent to 1.34 euros per good board. This figure should be checked against your actual labor rate, scrap value, inspection cost, and any downtime or expedite costs before using it in a business case."},"defect":{"symptom":"After reflow, several 0402 chip resistors show tombstoning on one side of the board, and the defect rate rises from a baseline of about 200 defects per million placements to about 1,800 defects per million placements on the affected panel location.","cause":"The evidence pack links the defect to uneven paste release and thermal imbalance. Images with scale show one pad has paste volume near the lower end of the process window while the adjacent pad is near the upper end. The stencil design for that panel location has slightly different aperture areas, and the reflow profile shows a faster ramp rate than the paste datasheet recommends for the board thermal mass.","action":"Reduce the defect rate by adjusting the stencil aperture area balance for the affected pads, verifying paste release with 3D solder paste inspection, and slowing the preheat ramp rate within the paste and component thermal limits. Re-run a short production window, collect images with scale, record defect rate by panel location, and update the evidence pack with before and after data."},"takeaways":["Build the pack so it answers what is measured, what the evidence shows and what it costs, because those three questions decide whether anyone acts.","Put the unit and the window with every number, since a bare figure cannot be compared with the next build.","Keep images interpretable later, because a close-up without scale is an anecdote."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Reporting a defect rate without the opportunity count","instead":"State what is counted, because a joint and a board give different numbers for the same line."},{"mistake":"Comparing two shifts running different products","instead":"Match conditions or label the comparison as indicative, since product mix dominates the rate."},{"mistake":"Quoting a cost of poor quality with no terms","instead":"Show the build-up, because a total nobody can check is not persuasive."}],"terms":[{"term":"Defects per million opportunities","def":"A defect rate normalised by the number of countable opportunities in the product."},{"term":"Defect density","def":"Defects normalised to board area or joint count, allowing comparison across sizes."},{"term":"Cost of poor quality","def":"The cost of scrap, rework, inspection and lost capacity caused by defects."},{"term":"Matched conditions","def":"A comparison where product, line, materials and process settings are held comparable."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":15,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m15l01","m14l03"],"skills":["Lesson m15l01: How designers read process feedback: language, format, what gets acted on","Lesson m14l03: Containment and traceability: quarantine scope, lot trace, retroactive search"]}},{"lessonId":"m15l03","moduleId":"m15","title":"Writing the change request: problem, impact, proposal, alternatives, risk, verification","goals":[{"text":"Write and defend an ECN/DCR request against typical design objections.","competencies":["K10"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-002","src-007","src-009","src-011","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m15l03-01","q-m15l03-02","q-m15l03-03","q-m15l03-04","q-m15l03-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m15l03.webp","alt":"A change request form with the six sections laid out, each with a short example entry","caption":"Writing the change request: problem, impact, proposal, alternatives, risk and verification"}]},{"h":"Frame the change request as a contract","p":["An ECN or DCR states what is wrong, what is being changed, and how acceptance will be proven.","It identifies affected part numbers, drawings, BOM levels, routings, test programs and documents. For SMT and through-hole work, add board revision, panelisation, feeder or insertion head, stencil, wave or selective solder, and inspection method.","Write it so design, manufacturing, quality and purchasing can all read it without a translator."]},{"h":"Write the problem with evidence","p":["The problem statement carries defect mode, location, occurrence, detection method and consequence.","Use measurable terms: solder voiding on QFN pads detected by X-ray, tombstoning at a specific feeder, lead seating error at a connector row. State sample size, lot, date, equipment and operator where they matter.","Where acceptance criteria are needed, cite IPC-A-610 and verify the applicable class against the product specification."]},{"h":"Show impact before proposing","p":["The impact section covers electrical, mechanical, thermal, assembly, test, reliability, inventory and qualification effects.","For a land-pattern change, cite IPC-7351 for footprint geometry and IPC-7095 for BGA. For through-hole work, cover lead forming, seating, wave dwell, clearance and interference with adjacent components.","Say whether existing inventory can be consumed, reworked or scrapped, and who pays for it."]},{"h":"Make the proposal unambiguous","p":["The proposal specifies old and new values, the drawing revision, the BOM change, process parameter changes and work instruction updates.","For paste changes, state stencil thickness, aperture dimensions and paste type, verified against the paste and printer datasheet. For through-hole work, state lead finish, seating tolerance and wave or selective profile limits.","Include traceability, first-article requirements and the list of documents that change."]},{"h":"List alternatives and rejection reasons","p":["Consider at least two alternatives: process adjustment, component substitution, layout change, or test change.","Compare technical feasibility, cost, schedule, inventory and risk. A rejected alternative states why, for example a process window too narrow, no qualified component, insufficient test coverage, or an unacceptable rework burden.","This section is what defends the request against the objection that it was chosen before the analysis."]},{"h":"Quantify risk and residual risk","p":["Use a simple risk table: failure mode, severity, occurrence, detection, mitigation, residual risk.","For rework and repair limits cite IPC-7711/7721, and for soldering process qualification cite IPC J-STD-001. State the controls: SPC, AOI, X-ray, in-circuit test, functional test, environmental stress screening.","Do not claim zero risk. State the residual risk you accept and who accepts it."]},{"h":"Define verification and sign-off","p":["Verification includes the sample plan, acceptance criteria, data format and disposition.","Measure dimensional changes with calibrated tools. Inspect solder joints by cross-section or X-ray against IPC-A-610 where required. Where the change affects joint stress, define the thermal cycling or vibration test.","Close the ECN or DCR only when the data, the updated documents and the inventory disposition are all complete."]}],"calc":{"task":"A proposed ECN reduces a chip resistor stencil aperture from 0.50 mm by 0.50 mm to 0.45 mm by 0.45 mm, with stencil thickness 0.12 mm. Calculate the paste volume change and a simple verification sample using n = 1 / target defect rate, with target 0.5% per board.","solution":"Step 1: Old aperture area = 0.50 mm x 0.50 mm = 0.25 mm^2. Step 2: New aperture area = 0.45 mm x 0.45 mm = 0.2025 mm^2. Step 3: Old paste volume = 0.25 mm^2 x 0.12 mm = 0.03 mm^3. Step 4: New paste volume = 0.2025 mm^2 x 0.12 mm = 0.0243 mm^3. Step 5: Volume change = (0.0243 - 0.03) / 0.03 = -0.19, or 19% reduction. Step 6: Target defect rate = 0.5% = 0.005 defects per board. Step 7: Simple screening sample n = 1 / 0.005 = 200 boards. Step 8: This is a screening sample, not a confidence statement; verify the final sample size against your quality plan and customer requirements."},"defect":{"symptom":"AOI flags missing or tombstoned small chip components on one feeder lane, while adjacent lanes pass.","cause":"Stencil aperture or paste release may be out of process, placement nozzle vacuum may be low, feeder timing may be misaligned, or the component may be sticking to the nozzle.","action":"Stop the lane, quarantine suspect boards, measure paste volume, check stencil thickness and aperture condition, verify printer and placement parameters against the equipment datasheet, clean or replace the nozzle and feeder, then run a first article and requalify before release."},"takeaways":["State what changes and how acceptance is proven, because a change request without a proof is an opinion.","Show impact and alternatives before the proposal, since reviewers approve what they can see was compared.","Name the residual risk and its owner, because a claim of zero risk invites rejection."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Sending a change request that names only the desired outcome","instead":"Give old and new values, because the implementer needs the difference."},{"mistake":"Omitting the inventory and document impact","instead":"List them, since a change that leaves stock stranded gets deferred."},{"mistake":"Verifying with an unstated sample plan","instead":"State the sample and the criterion, because acceptance without a plan is a judgement."}],"terms":[{"term":"DCR","def":"Design change request, the engineering request that initiates a change before formal approval."},{"term":"First article","def":"The first unit built to a changed design, inspected in full."},{"term":"Residual risk","def":"The risk that remains after mitigation, accepted by a named owner."},{"term":"Panelisation","def":"The arrangement of boards on a production panel, which a design change can affect."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m15l02"],"skills":["Lesson m15l02: The evidence pack: measured data, images with scale, defect rate, cost of poor quality"]}},{"lessonId":"m15l04","moduleId":"m15","title":"DFM/DFT asks that work: pad geometry, paste relief, spacing, test points, fiducials, panelization, hole/lead spec","goals":[{"text":"Convert a process limitation into a specific, testable design rule change.","competencies":["K10"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-004","src-011","src-016","src-024","src-025"],"quiz":["q-m15l04-01","q-m15l04-02","q-m15l04-03","q-m15l04-04","q-m15l04-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m15l04.webp","alt":"A board layout with pad geometry, paste relief, spacing, test points, fiducials and panelization callouts, beside a hole-to-lead specification table","caption":"Design-for-manufacture and test asks that work: pad geometry, paste relief, spacing, test points, fiducials, panelization and hole-to-lead specification"}]},{"h":"From line limitation to testable rule","p":["A DFM or DFT ask is useful only when it becomes a measurable rule.","Start with the process limitation, not the component name. Poor placement repeatability points to a larger pad or a larger component-to-component keepout. An unstable stencil print points to paste relief, a different aperture shape or a thickness change. Insufficient wave fill points to a larger annular ring or a different hole-to-lead clearance.","Each of those is a drawing dimension a designer can change and a process engineer can verify."]},{"h":"Pad geometry controls the joint","p":["Pad geometry controls paste volume, wetting, self-alignment and bridging risk.","For passives, land length and width should support the intended fillet while leaving room for the neighbour. For QFN, MLF and other exposed-pad packages, the centre pad needs paste relief, because full-area paste pushes the package up, traps flux or creates voids.","Frame the land pattern against IPC-7351, check paste aperture changes against IPC-7525, and verify both against your paste and printer datasheet and your own placement accuracy."]},{"h":"Paste relief is a process control","p":["Paste relief is a stencil or aperture change used to control volume, bleed and bridging.","Methods include reducing aperture area, splitting a large pad into sub-apertures, adding a step stencil, or changing wall geometry. On fine-pitch packages a small area reduction can stop paste collapse without starving the joint, and on thermal pads splitting the aperture into several openings improves release and cuts voiding.","Treat it as a process control with a measured window, not as a cosmetic tweak to the stencil."]},{"h":"Spacing must satisfy assembly and inspection","p":["Spacing rules have to satisfy electrical clearance, assembly access, inspection and thermal or wave-solder shielding at the same time.","IPC-2221 and IPC-2222 give generic and organic-board design frameworks, and the final spacing also reflects your placement accuracy, feeder clearance and inspection optics.","On SMT, component-to-component spacing should let the nozzle approach without disturbing neighbours and leave room for the inspection camera."]},{"h":"Test points need dedicated geometry","p":["Test points are the first DFT failure because they are added late.","A test point should be a dedicated pad with controlled size, plating and solder mask opening, not a random via or a component lead. For in-circuit test, probe access needs enough diameter and spacing to prevent probe interference.","For functional test, the pad should survive repeated probing and should not sit under a component or inside a panel rail."]},{"h":"Fiducials are placement accuracy hardware","p":["Fiducials turn a placement program into a repeatable coordinate system.","Global fiducials go near the panel corners, and local fiducials close to fine-pitch or high-accuracy components. The fiducial needs high contrast, a clean circular or cross shape, and a clear zone free of copper, silkscreen and nearby pads.","Check the size and the clear zone against the placement camera field of view, because a fiducial the camera cannot resolve is decoration."]},{"h":"Panelisation affects every downstream process","p":["Panelisation affects handling, deformation, breakaway quality, and where fiducials and test points can sit.","The panel fits the conveyor, placement, reflow, wave and in-circuit equipment, and it should not force the board to flex during depaneling. Rails, V-scores, mouse bites and routed breakaways each carry trade-offs.","Fiducials belong on the panel or rails where the camera sees them, and test points must stay reachable after depaneling."]},{"h":"Hole and lead specs decide THT quality","p":["Hole and lead specifications are often the difference between a sound joint and a weak or bridged one.","Drill size, plating, annular ring and hole-to-lead clearance decide wetting and fill. IPC-2221 and IPC-2222 give the frameworks, and the final hole size is checked against drill tolerance, plating thickness and lead diameter variation.","For wave or selective solder, the hole and annular ring should give a fill that meets the acceptance criteria without a dwell long enough to damage the board."]},{"h":"Write the rule as a change request with proof","p":["The conversion from limitation to rule is a change request with a measurable target.","If SPI shows paste collapse on a fine-pitch QFN, the rule reduces thermal-pad aperture area, adds sub-aperture gaps and sets an SPI height window. If wave soldering shows insufficient fill at a through-hole connector, the rule increases the annular ring or adjusts the hole size. If probes miss test points, the rule adds dedicated pads of the right diameter and spacing.","Each rule arrives with the measurement that will show it worked."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 0.4 mm pitch QFN has a 4.0 mm x 4.0 mm thermal pad. SPI shows paste height near 0.16 mm with a 0.12 mm stencil, and the line sees paste bleed between peripheral pads. Convert this limitation into a specific, testable design rule change for the thermal-pad paste aperture.","solution":"Pad area calculation: 4.0 mm x 4.0 mm = 16.0 mm2. Target aperture area: 60% of pad area, so 0.60 x 16.0 mm2 = 9.6 mm2. Aperture pattern: use 4 square sub-apertures with a 0.10 mm gap between adjacent sub-apertures. If each sub-aperture is 1.55 mm x 1.55 mm, total aperture area is 4 x 1.55 mm x 1.55 mm = 9.61 mm2, which is 60.1% of the pad area. Layout envelope check: 2 sub-apertures plus the gap span 2 x 1.55 mm + 0.10 mm = 3.20 mm, leaving 0.40 mm of outer margin on each side inside the 4.0 mm pad. Nominal paste volume: 9.61 mm2 x 0.12 mm = 1.153 mm3. Acceptance window: SPI paste height 0.10 mm to 0.13 mm, no bridging at 0.4 mm pitch, and package seating confirmed by X-ray or cross-section per IPC-A-610. Design rule to release: thermal-pad aperture area shall be 58% to 62% of pad area, implemented as 4 sub-apertures of 1.55 mm +/- 0.05 mm with 0.10 mm +/- 0.05 mm gaps, verified on a pilot panel by SPI and X-ray."},"defect":{"symptom":"After reflow, 0.4 mm pitch QFN packages show lifted corners and voided center pads, while SPI shows high paste height on the thermal pad.","cause":"Full-area thermal-pad aperture plus a 0.12 mm stencil produced excess paste volume; the package floated on paste and trapped flux.","action":"Reduce thermal-pad aperture to 58% to 62% area, split into 4 sub-apertures with 0.10 mm gaps, re-run SPI with height window 0.10 mm to 0.13 mm, and verify seating by X-ray per IPC-A-610."},"takeaways":["Convert the limitation into a dimension, because a rule the designer can draw is a rule the line can verify.","Give paste relief a measured window, since a stencil change without an SPI limit is an experiment.","Place the test point and the fiducial early, because both are cheap at layout and expensive afterwards."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Asking for more spacing without a reason","instead":"Tie the number to placement and inspection, because ungrounded requests lose."},{"mistake":"Adding test points on existing vias","instead":"Use dedicated pads, because a via under mask cannot be probed reliably."},{"mistake":"Changing the stencil without an SPI limit","instead":"Set the window first, since the change will drift back."}],"terms":[{"term":"Paste relief","def":"A reduction or splitting of paste aperture area to control volume and voiding."},{"term":"Fiducial","def":"A high-contrast mark used by the placement machine to establish coordinates."},{"term":"Mouse bite","def":"A breakaway tab with drilled perforations used to separate a board from its panel."},{"term":"Keepout","def":"An area kept clear of components or copper for a process reason."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":20,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m15l03"],"skills":["Lesson m15l03: Writing the change request: problem, impact, proposal, alternatives, risk, verification"]}},{"lessonId":"m15l05","moduleId":"m15","title":"Negotiation, version control and closure with post-change data","goals":[{"text":"Write and defend an ECN/DCR request against typical design objections.","competencies":["K10"]},{"text":"Build an evidence pack that quantifies the process impact of the current design.","competencies":["K10"]}],"status":"draft","sources":["src-001","src-008","src-009","src-011","src-016"],"quiz":["q-m15l05-01","q-m15l05-02","q-m15l05-03","q-m15l05-04","q-m15l05-05"],"content":{"theory":[{"blocks":[{"t":"image","src":"assets/img/m15l05.webp","alt":"A negotiation exchange between process and design, a version-controlled drawing revision block, and a post-change capability chart","caption":"Negotiation, version control and closure with post-change data"}]},{"h":"Frame the ECN or DCR as a controlled problem statement","p":["An ECN or DCR starts with a measurable problem, not a preference.","State the affected assembly, part numbers, revision, production quantity and the process step where the defect appears, plus the current controlled documents: drawing revision, land pattern per IPC-7351, stencil thickness, paste type, feeder map, AOI program and reflow profile per IPC-7530.","If the change is only a temporary containment, label it as one, so nobody files it as a design improvement. Use IPC-7711/7721 for rework and repair limits only when discussing repairability, not as a substitute for design change data."]},{"h":"Quantify the current design first","p":["Use at least 30 consecutive production days or 1000 boards, whichever gives a stable sample, and check the sample size against your MES capability.","Report boards started, boards passed, rework events, scrap, test escapes, cycle time and downtime. Separate common-cause variation from special-cause events.","For defect classification, use the acceptability class the contract requires under IPC-A-610, and record whether defects were confirmed or only called."]},{"h":"Anticipate design objections with evidence","p":"Typical objections are that the change affects electrical performance, lacks test coverage, increases cost, delays schedule, or creates thermal or mechanical risk. Answer each with data: solder joint geometry from cross-section or X-ray, thermal profile margin from thermocouple data, mechanical clearance, and test coverage maps. If reliability is questioned, state the relevant qualification status and the residual risk if the change is not made. Use IPC-7711/7721 for rework and repair limits only when discussing repairability, not as a substitute for design change data."},{"h":"Keep version control explicit","p":["The ECN or DCR lists every document that changes and every document that stays the same: gerber revision, BOM revision, stencil file revision, paste lot or type, feeder map, AOI program, reflow profile and work instruction.","Assign a unique change ID, an effective date, and the affected serial or lot range. Mark superseded revisions obsolete in the PLM or document control system, and stop mixed revisions running on the line at the same time."]},{"h":"Build an evidence pack a design engineer can audit","p":["The pack holds the baseline data, the defect Pareto, representative photos, SPI and AOI results, X-ray or cross-section images, reflow thermocouple traces, rework and scrap records, cost of poor quality, risk matrix, proposed change, verification plan and rollback plan.","Use SI units and state the measurement uncertainty where it affects the decision. For paste and stencil values, cite the paste and equipment datasheet rather than a remembered figure."]},{"h":"Negotiate options and residual risk","p":["Present three options: no change, interim containment, and full design change.","For each, state the expected defect rate, rework time, scrap, schedule impact and residual risk. If the design team resists a land-pattern change, offer a process-only trial first — stencil aperture adjustment, paste type change, or placement pressure change — and record the limits of that trial.","Avoid absolute claims. State conditions, sample sizes and the data behind the projection."]},{"h":"Close the change with post-change data","p":["After implementation, run a pilot and then a production confirmation.","Compare SPI, AOI, X-ray, functional test, rework, scrap, cycle time and downtime against the baseline. Close the ECN or DCR only when the acceptance criteria are met, the controlled documents are updated and the line runs the new revision.","If the post-change data miss the criteria, reopen the change and identify whether the cause is design, process or execution."]}],"calc":{"task":"A 0402 resistor land pattern on a 1.6 mm board shows tombstoning and insufficient wetting. Baseline over 30 production days: 12000 boards, 2.4 percent boards with rework, average 1.8 defects per reworked board, rework 3.5 min per defect, operator cost 45 EUR/h, scrap 0.3 percent boards, board cost 180 EUR, line downtime 0.5 h/month. Proposed ECN changes land pattern to IPC-7351 nominal and reduces stencil aperture from 0.30 mm x 0.30 mm to 0.25 mm x 0.25 mm; verify against paste datasheet. Pilot predicts boards with rework 0.6 percent, defects per reworked board 1.2, scrap 0.1 percent, downtime 0.2 h/month. Calculate baseline cost of poor quality, projected cost, annual saving, and whether ECN is justified if one-time change cost 8000 EUR.","solution":"Step 1: baseline reworked boards = 12000 x 0.024 = 288 boards/month. Step 2: baseline defects = 288 x 1.8 = 518.4 defects/month. Step 3: baseline rework time = 518.4 x 3.5 = 1814.4 min/month = 30.24 h/month. Step 4: baseline rework labor cost = 30.24 x 45 = 1360.8 EUR/month. Step 5: baseline scrap boards = 12000 x 0.003 = 36 boards/month. Step 6: baseline scrap cost = 36 x 180 = 6480 EUR/month. Step 7: baseline downtime cost = 0.5 x 120 = 60 EUR/month, using 120 EUR/h line cost; verify against your line cost model. Step 8: baseline COPQ = 1360.8 + 6480 + 60 = 7900.8 EUR/month. Step 9: projected reworked boards = 12000 x 0.006 = 72 boards/month. Step 10: projected defects = 72 x 1.2 = 86.4 defects/month. Step 11: projected rework time = 86.4 x 3.5 = 302.4 min/month = 5.04 h/month. Step 12: projected rework labor cost = 5.04 x 45 = 226.8 EUR/month. Step 13: projected scrap boards = 12000 x 0.001 = 12 boards/month. Step 14: projected scrap cost = 12 x 180 = 2160 EUR/month. Step 15: projected downtime cost = 0.2 x 120 = 24 EUR/month. Step 16: projected COPQ = 226.8 + 2160 + 24 = 2410.8 EUR/month. Step 17: monthly saving = 7900.8 - 2410.8 = 5490 EUR/month. Step 18: annual saving = 5490 x 12 = 65880 EUR/year. Step 19: payback = 8000 / 5490 = 1.46 months. Step 20: ECN is justified if post-change data confirm the projected defect reduction and no new reliability or test escape risk is introduced."},"defect":{"symptom":"After the ECN, AOI reports increased insufficient solder on 0402 resistors during the first 200 boards, while tombstoning decreases.","cause":"The new stencil aperture was reduced too far for the paste release window, and the stencil-to-board gap or paste volume was not requalified after the land pattern change.","action":"Stop the changeover, retain the old stencil and paste lot for comparison, measure SPI deposit volume and height, requalify the aperture and stencil thickness against the paste datasheet, then run a controlled pilot with thermocouple profiling and X-ray before releasing production."},"takeaways":["Baseline the problem on a stated sample, because thirty days of data settles objections that opinion cannot.","Close on post-change data, since approval is not the same as effect.","Offer a process-only trial where design resists, because a bounded experiment is easier to approve than a redesign."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Filing a temporary containment as a permanent change","instead":"Label the duration, because a containment recorded as a fix never gets replaced."},{"mistake":"Comparing the pilot against a different product mix","instead":"Use the same conditions, since mix moves the defect rate."},{"mistake":"Leaving two revisions on the line","instead":"Obsolete the old documents, because mixed revisions make every result ambiguous."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pilot build","def":"A limited production run used to confirm a change before release."},{"term":"Rollback plan","def":"The documented route back to the previous revision if a change fails."},{"term":"Test coverage map","def":"The record of which defects each inspection and test step can detect."},{"term":"Document control","def":"The system that holds current revisions and marks superseded ones obsolete."}]},"review":"Reviewed 2026-11-01 against primary standard text. Part A: 30 of 30 recurring facts verified. Part B: 575 claims walked and adjudicated; 13 defects found and fixed (D1-D13). Every number this lesson originally flagged is now sourced. The claim-by-claim trail, with lesson id and field path, is in REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md.","minutes":30,"prereqs":{"lessons":["m15l04"],"skills":["Lesson m15l04: DFM/DFT asks that work: pad geometry, paste relief, spacing, test points, fiducials, panelization, hole/lead spec"]}}],"questions":[{"questionId":"q-m04l01-01","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"lessonId":"m04l01","prompt":"In stencil aperture design, the area ratio is defined as the aperture opening area divided by the total sidewall (wall) area. As a general rule of thumb, fine-pitch printing tends to need an area ratio at least about:","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.50"},{"id":"b","text":"0.66"},{"id":"c","text":"1.50"},{"id":"d","text":"3.00"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"A commonly cited rule of thumb is to keep the area ratio at or above roughly 0.66 so that paste releases cleanly from the aperture instead of sticking to the sidewalls. Below that threshold transfer efficiency drops and the deposit under-fills. Exact cut-offs depend on paste type and geometry, so treat 0.66 as a starting guideline, not a hard limit.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m04l01-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K2"],"lessonId":"m04l01","prompt":"A square aperture measures 0.4 mm x 0.4 mm and is cut in a stencil foil 0.12 mm thick. Area ratio = aperture area / (perimeter x foil thickness). What is the area ratio, to two decimal places?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.60"},{"id":"b","text":"0.83"},{"id":"c","text":"1.20"},{"id":"d","text":"1.67"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Aperture area = 0.4 x 0.4 = 0.16 mm^2. Perimeter = 2 x (0.4 + 0.4) = 1.6 mm. Wall area = 1.6 x 0.12 = 0.192 mm^2. Area ratio = 0.16 / 0.192 = 0.833, i.e. about 0.83. This sits above the ~0.66 guideline, so the aperture should release paste acceptably.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m04l02-01","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"lessonId":"m04l02","prompt":"An SPI system reports a pad as out of spec on volume but within spec on height and area. What is the most likely first thing to check before treating it as a real print defect?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"That the stencil aperture is fully open and not partially blocked"},{"id":"b","text":"That the reflow oven peak temperature is correct"},{"id":"c","text":"That the component tape-and-reel feeder is loaded correctly"},{"id":"d","text":"That the AOI lighting is calibrated"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Volume is derived from the height and footprint the SPI measures, so a volume-only outlier with good height and area often points to a partial aperture blockage or a measurement artifact at the pad edge rather than a genuine under-deposit. Confirming the aperture is clear is the cheapest first check. Reflow, feeder and AOI settings act after printing and would not explain an SPI-only volume flag.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m04l02-02","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"lessonId":"m04l02","prompt":"Over the last hour the SPI mean paste volume on a fine-pitch QFP has drifted steadily downward while the spread stays constant. What does this trend most strongly suggest?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A gradual process drift, such as paste thinning or a fouled stencil underside, rather than a single random defect"},{"id":"b","text":"A one-off misprint that can be ignored"},{"id":"c","text":"A bad component lot arriving at placement"},{"id":"d","text":"A solder mask colour change on the PCB"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A steady directional shift in the mean with unchanged spread is the signature of process drift, for example paste viscosity changing over a long print run or solder accumulating on the stencil underside. That pattern justifies a scheduled stencil clean or a parameter review. A single random point, a component issue, or board colour would not produce a consistent downward mean trend at the print stage.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m04l03-01","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"prompt":"A stencil printer leaves solder paste on the stencil after separation, especially on fine-pitch apertures, while the printed paste volume on the board is low. Which setting change is most appropriate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Increase separation speed."},{"id":"b","text":"Decrease squeegee pressure."},{"id":"c","text":"Increase wipe cycle length."},{"id":"d","text":"Decrease squeegee speed."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Paste left on the stencil indicates poor snap-off; increasing separation speed helps the paste release from the stencil. Lower squeegee pressure would reduce aperture fill and worsen transfer. Longer wipe cycles disturb the paste roll and do not fix release. Slower squeegee speed can increase paste roll and smearing rather than improve snap-off.","source":"src-004","lessonId":"m04l03","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m04l03-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K3"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula squeegee pressure = force / blade length, a rule-of-thumb setup applies 60 N to a 100 mm blade. What pressure is set?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.6 N/mm"},{"id":"b","text":"6 N/mm"},{"id":"c","text":"60 N/mm"},{"id":"d","text":"600 N/mm"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"60 N / 100 mm = 0.6 N/mm of blade length, the top of the usual band, and 0.6 N/mm on a 100 mm blade is 60 N. The other options each drop or shift a decimal: 6 N/mm treats the blade as 10 mm long, 60 N/mm reports the force as pressure, and 600 N/mm divides by 0.1 instead of 100. State the blade length with any per-length figure or it means nothing.","source":"src-004","lessonId":"m04l03","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m04l04-01","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"prompt":"An SPI report shows low volume percent and low deposit height, normal deposit area, no offset, and a histogram that broadens toward low heights after several prints. Which dominant defect mode and likely cause is most consistent?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Insufficient paste volume from stencil clogging or paste drying"},{"id":"b","text":"Excessive paste volume from squeegee pressure too low"},{"id":"c","text":"Misregistration from stencil offset"},{"id":"d","text":"Bridging from excessive deposit area"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Low height with normal area and no offset points to insufficient paste transfer rather than placement or bridging. A broadening low-height histogram after several prints is consistent with progressive stencil clogging or paste drying. Excessive volume would raise height, offset would appear in offset data, and bridging would show excess area or shorts.","source":"src-004","lessonId":"m04l04","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m04l04-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K3","K6"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's rule of thumb that volume percent equals deposit height divided by stencil thickness times one hundred, if the measured deposit height is four-fifths of the stencil thickness and the deposit area is nominal, what volume percent is reported?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"eighty percent"},{"id":"b","text":"one hundred twenty-five percent"},{"id":"c","text":"one hundred percent"},{"id":"d","text":"twenty percent"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The rule of thumb gives four-fifths times one hundred, which equals eighty percent. One hundred twenty-five percent comes from inverting the ratio, one hundred percent assumes nominal height, and twenty percent is the shortfall rather than the reported volume.","source":"src-004","lessonId":"m04l04","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m04l05-01","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"prompt":"An SPI report shows volume % below target, height below target, area within target, and offset within target. The histogram is shifted low without a second peak. What is the most likely dominant defect mode and cause?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Insufficient paste volume from partially clogged stencil apertures"},{"id":"b","text":"Paste offset from misaligned stencil or placement"},{"id":"c","text":"Excessive paste volume from stencil wear or overprint"},{"id":"d","text":"Bridging from excessive paste height and volume"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Low volume % with low height but normal area and offset points to insufficient paste deposition rather than placement or bridging. Partially clogged apertures reduce paste height while leaving aperture area and alignment largely unchanged. Excessive volume or worn stencil would raise height or volume, and offset would show misalignment.","source":"src-004","lessonId":"m04l05","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m04l05-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K3","K6"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula for a centered process, if the specification width is three times the process spread, what is the process capability index?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"1"},{"id":"b","text":"1.5"},{"id":"c","text":"3"},{"id":"d","text":"6"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"For a centered process, Cpk equals Cp, which is specification width divided by process spread. If the specification width is three times the process spread, then Cpk equals three times process spread divided by process spread, which is three. A value of one would mean the specification width equals the process spread, 1.5 would mean it is 1.5 times the process spread, and six would confuse the process spread with one standard deviation.","source":"src-004","lessonId":"m04l05","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m04l06-01","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"prompt":"An SPI trend shows volume percent falling and the height histogram shifting lower, while area and offset remain stable. Which dominant defect mode is most likely?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Under-fill from insufficient paste transfer"},{"id":"b","text":"Excess from too much paste deposition"},{"id":"c","text":"Bridging from paste area spreading between pads"},{"id":"d","text":"Skew from placement misalignment"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Falling volume percent and lower height with stable area and offset indicate insufficient paste transfer, so under-fill is dominant. Excess would show rising volume and height, bridging would show increased area or bridges between pads, and skew would show increased offset.","source":"src-001","lessonId":"m04l06","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m04l06-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K3","K6"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula that paste height percent equals volume percent divided by area percent, an SPI report shows volume percent is double area percent. What is the paste height relative to stencil height?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Double the stencil height"},{"id":"b","text":"Equal to the stencil height"},{"id":"c","text":"Half the stencil height"},{"id":"d","text":"Zero height"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Height percent = volume percent / area percent. Substituting volume percent = double area percent gives height percent = double area percent / area percent = double, so the paste height is double the stencil height. Equal height would require volume percent and area percent to be equal, half height would invert the formula, and zero height would require zero volume.","source":"src-001","lessonId":"m04l06","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m04l07-01","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"prompt":"A fine-pitch QFP shows paste bridges that appear only when the stencil lifts, while the squeegee pass leaves clean apertures. Using single-variable-at-a-time logic, which single change should be tried first to reduce the bridges?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Increase snap-off/clearance so the stencil separates more cleanly from the paste."},{"id":"b","text":"Decrease snap-off/clearance so the stencil stays closer to the board."},{"id":"c","text":"Increase squeegee pressure to force more paste through the apertures."},{"id":"d","text":"Increase separation speed so the stencil lifts faster."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Bridges that appear only during lift-off point to poor snap-off or separation rather than insufficient fill. Increasing snap-off/clearance helps the stencil release cleanly and reduces smearing. Decreasing snap-off keeps the stencil in contact longer, and higher squeegee pressure can force paste under the stencil, both worsening bridges.","source":"src-004","lessonId":"m04l07","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m04l07-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K3","K6"],"prompt":"SPI flags insufficient paste because the height threshold is set to full stencil thickness. If the lesson rule of thumb is that reliable transfer is nine-tenths of stencil thickness, by what fraction of stencil thickness should the threshold be lowered to avoid a false reject?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"One-tenth"},{"id":"b","text":"Nine-tenths"},{"id":"c","text":"One-half"},{"id":"d","text":"Two-tenths"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The SPI threshold is set to full stencil thickness, while the rule-of-thumb reliable transfer is nine-tenths of stencil thickness. The false-reject margin is full minus nine-tenths, which is one-tenth of stencil thickness. Lowering the threshold by that amount aligns inspection with the expected print capability.","source":"src-004","lessonId":"m04l07","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m06l01-01","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"prompt":"During an oven thermal survey, the controller setpoints look uniform, but product quality varies across the oven. Where should the engineer read the thermal profile to diagnose the problem?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"At the coolest and hottest locations identified by the survey, not at the oven setpoints"},{"id":"b","text":"At the controller setpoints, because they define the intended oven temperature"},{"id":"c","text":"Only at the oven centerline, because it represents the average condition"},{"id":"d","text":"Only at the exhaust, because it shows the heat leaving the oven"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The actual oven profile is shaped by convection, conduction, radiation, and zone structure, so the setpoints do not reveal local hot or cold spots. Reading the coolest and hottest locations identifies the real thermal risk. Setpoints, centerline, and exhaust readings can all miss the locations that drive product variation.","source":"src-005","lessonId":"m06l01","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m06l01-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"prompt":"Using the rule of thumb that radiative emission scales with the fourth power of absolute temperature, a thermal survey finds the hottest oven surface at twice the absolute temperature of the coolest nearby surface. If both surfaces behave as black bodies, what is the ratio of emitted radiative flux from the hottest surface to that from the coolest surface?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Two times"},{"id":"b","text":"Four times"},{"id":"c","text":"Eight times"},{"id":"d","text":"Sixteen times"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"For black-body emission, q = εσT^4, so the flux ratio is (two)^four = sixteen. The two-times answer assumes linear dependence, the four-times answer assumes square dependence, and the eight-times answer assumes cubic dependence.","source":"src-005","lessonId":"m06l01","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m06l02-01","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"prompt":"A high thermal mass board with large copper planes is being profiled. Why is the soak stage usually lengthened more than the peak temperature is raised?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It lets the board and components reach a more uniform temperature before liquidus, reducing cold joints and tombstoning."},{"id":"b","text":"It raises the peak temperature enough to compensate for the board's thermal mass."},{"id":"c","text":"It shortens time above liquidus by allowing the peak to be reached faster."},{"id":"d","text":"It improves cooling by storing heat in the board."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The soak equalizes temperatures across the board and components before liquidus, which is the main control for high thermal mass boards. Raising peak temperature can damage parts and does not fix temperature gradients. Soak does not shorten time above liquidus, and cooling is controlled after peak.","source":"src-005","lessonId":"m06l02","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m06l02-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's rule-of-thumb limits (ramp 1-3 °C/s, time above liquidus 30-90 s from the paste datasheet and IPC-7530, and peak not above the J-STD-020 classification temperature of 250 °C for a large package), a measured profile for SAC305 ramps from 150 °C to liquidus in 30 s, crosses liquidus upward at 120 s and downward at 210 s, and peaks at 242 °C. Which check fails?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Ramp rate, because (219 − 150) / 30 = 2.3 °C/s, above the 1-3 °C/s rule-of-thumb limit."},{"id":"b","text":"Time above liquidus, because 210 − 120 = 90 s, outside the 30-90 s window."},{"id":"c","text":"Peak, because 242 °C exceeds the classification temperature."},{"id":"d","text":"None, because all checks pass."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Ramp rate is (219 − 150) / 30 = 2.3 °C/s, inside the 1-3 °C/s range taught in the lesson. Time above liquidus is 210 − 120 = 90 s, inside the 30-90 s paste window at its top edge. Peak margin to the 250 °C classification temperature is 8 °C. All three checks pass, so no profile change is required. Re-verify if the package size changes, because the classification temperature is 260 °C for small packages and 245 °C for very large ones, and remember that the 30 s figure in J-STD-020 is tp, the time within 5 °C of the classification temperature, not the paste-window time above liquidus.","source":"src-005","lessonId":"m06l02","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m06l03-01","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"prompt":"When running a thermal survey, why should the profile be read at the coolest and hottest part locations rather than at the oven setpoints?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Oven setpoints are control points, not part temperatures; the coolest and hottest part locations reveal the actual process window margin."},{"id":"b","text":"Oven setpoints are the most accurate because the controller is calibrated, so part thermocouples only add attachment error."},{"id":"c","text":"The hottest location is always the oven setpoint, so reading setpoints avoids the need for a test coupon."},{"id":"d","text":"Thermocouple attachment error is the only reason to read part locations, and it can be ignored if the oven is stable."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Oven setpoints do not represent the actual part temperatures because thermal mass, airflow, fixture effects, and thermocouple attachment can shift the measured part profile. The coolest and hottest part locations determine whether the process window is met. Setpoints can remain stable while a part location is too cool or too hot, and attachment error is only one of several survey factors.","source":"src-005","lessonId":"m06l03","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m06l03-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"prompt":"Using the J-STD-020 classification temperatures for SAC305 (260 °C small package, 250 °C large package, 245 °C very large package) and the lesson formula minimum margin = min(classification temperature − hottest measured, coolest measured − liquidus), a survey records a coolest part peak of 246 °C and a hottest part peak of 252 °C on a large package. The SAC305 liquidus is 219 °C. What is the minimum margin and the disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"−2 °C; the hottest part exceeds the classification temperature, so the profile fails."},{"id":"b","text":"2 °C; passes."},{"id":"c","text":"27 °C; passes."},{"id":"d","text":"6 °C; passes."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"min(250 − 252, 246 − 219) = min(−2 °C, 27 °C) = −2 °C. The hottest location exceeds the classification temperature by 2 °C, so the profile fails regardless of the comfortable margin above liquidus at the coolest location. Option b is half the peak-to-peak spread, option c is the coolest-site margin above liquidus only, and option d is the peak spread. The classification temperature depends on package size, so confirm the size before disposition.","source":"src-005","lessonId":"m06l03","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m06l04-01","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"prompt":"A dense board with large copper planes and a sparse mixed-population board are reflowed with the same furnace setpoints. Why does the dense board often need a longer soak or slower ramp?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The heavy copper planes increase thermal mass and heat sinking, so the joints lag the furnace air and need more time to reach liquidus."},{"id":"b","text":"The heavy copper planes reduce the board thermal mass, so the joints heat faster than the furnace air."},{"id":"c","text":"Thermal relief on dense boards increases heat sinking, so the joints require a higher peak temperature."},{"id":"d","text":"Dense boards always peak lower because the furnace air cannot reach the setpoint."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Large planes and dense copper increase thermal mass and conduct heat away from joints, so the measured joint profile lags the furnace air and may need slower ramp or longer soak. Heavy planes do not reduce thermal mass, and thermal relief is used to reduce heat sinking, not increase it. The furnace air can still reach setpoint; the issue is joint temperature lag, not furnace air failure.","source":"src-005","lessonId":"m06l04","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m06l04-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"prompt":"For a SAC305 board, the cited process guidance gives a maximum ramp rate of 3 °C/s, a liquidus of 219 °C, a J-STD-020 classification temperature of 250 °C for a large package, and a minimum time above liquidus of 60 s. If a heavy-plane board is ramped from the lower soak limit of 150 °C at the maximum allowed ramp rate, what is the minimum elapsed time from that soak exit to the end of the minimum time above liquidus?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"83 s"},{"id":"b","text":"60 s"},{"id":"c","text":"23 s"},{"id":"d","text":"93 s"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Time to reach liquidus is (219 °C − 150 °C) ÷ 3 °C/s = 23 s. The minimum time above liquidus is 60 s, so the minimum elapsed time from soak exit to the end of that interval is 23 s + 60 s = 83 s. The 93 s option ramps all the way to the 250 °C classification temperature before counting the 60 s, which double-counts the part of the ramp that is already above liquidus; 60 s ignores the time needed to reach liquidus; 23 s ignores the required time above liquidus.","source":"src-005","lessonId":"m06l04","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m06l05-01","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"prompt":"When building a reflow profile family for a high-thermal-mass board using SnAgCu rather than eutectic SnPb, what is the primary reason the profile must be shifted upward and checked against a different liquidus basis?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"SnAgCu has a higher liquidus than eutectic SnPb, so the liquidus, time above liquidus, and peak must be set relative to the SnAgCu liquidus to achieve wetting without exceeding alloy limits."},{"id":"b","text":"SnAgCu wets faster than SnPb, so the peak must be lower to avoid tombstoning."},{"id":"c","text":"Nitrogen reflow changes the liquidus of SnAgCu, so the profile must be based on the nitrogen liquidus rather than the alloy liquidus."},{"id":"d","text":"High thermal mass boards require a lower peak because the board lags the oven air, so the liquidus basis is irrelevant."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The profile family is anchored to the alloy liquidus; SnAgCu's liquidus is higher than eutectic SnPb, so time above liquidus and peak are checked against SnAgCu limits. Nitrogen reduces oxidation and can make wetting more forgiving, but it does not redefine the alloy liquidus. High thermal mass changes how quickly the board follows the oven, not the liquidus basis.","source":"src-005","lessonId":"m06l05","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m06l05-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"prompt":"A high-thermal-mass SAC305 board is profiled using process guidance: liquidus 219 °C, peak 245 °C, minimum time above liquidus 60 s, and maximum ramp rate 3 °C/s. If the board ramps from liquidus to peak at the maximum ramp rate and then dwells at peak before cooling, calculate the minimum dwell at peak required so that the time above liquidus just meets the minimum.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 51 s"},{"id":"b","text":"60 s"},{"id":"c","text":"About 8.7 s"},{"id":"d","text":"About 69 s"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Time above liquidus includes the ramp from liquidus to peak plus the dwell at peak. Ramp time = (245 °C − 219 °C) ÷ 3 °C/s = 8.7 s. Required dwell = 60 s − 8.7 s = 51.3 s, about 51 s. Option b ignores the ramp time already spent above liquidus; option c reports the ramp time itself rather than the dwell; option d adds the ramp time instead of subtracting it.","source":"src-005","lessonId":"m06l05","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m06l06-01","type":"single","competencies":["K5","K6"],"prompt":"A chip resistor stands upright on one termination after reflow, with the other termination not wetted. Which profile zone and bounded corrective action best match this defect signature?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Reflow/liquidus zone; reduce the local wetting-force imbalance by increasing paste volume or copper on the weaker termination, then verify tombstone rate by AOI or X-ray."},{"id":"b","text":"Preheat zone; increase the ramp rate to equalize part temperatures, then verify by oven thermocouple."},{"id":"c","text":"Soak zone; extend soak time to drive off flux volatiles, then verify by flux residue inspection."},{"id":"d","text":"Cooling zone; slow cooling to allow wetting to continue, then verify by shear strength."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Tombstone is a reflow-zone wetting-force imbalance, usually one termination wets first and pulls the part upright. The bounded fix is to reduce the imbalance locally, such as adding paste volume or copper to the weaker termination, and verify by AOI or X-ray tombstone rate. Faster preheat ramp, longer soak, or slower cooling do not address the liquidus wetting imbalance and can worsen other defects.","source":"src-008","lessonId":"m06l06","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m06l06-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K5","K6"],"prompt":"Using the lesson rule that the cooling portion of time above liquidus is (peak temperature − liquidus temperature) / cooling rate, the SAC305 values liquidus 219 °C and peak 245 °C, the paste-window time above liquidus of 60 to 120 s, and a rule-of-thumb cooling rate of 0.2 °C/s, what is the cooling time and the bounded corrective action for a dewetting signature?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"130 s; increase the cooling rate to at least 0.22 °C/s to bring time above liquidus into the paste window, then verify by thermocouple."},{"id":"b","text":"26 s; no change is needed because the temperature difference is the time above liquidus, then verify by shear."},{"id":"c","text":"120 s; reduce the cooling rate to the upper paste-window limit, then verify by AOI."},{"id":"d","text":"60 s; raise the peak temperature to reach the lower paste-window limit, then verify by X-ray."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"245 − 219 = 26 °C; 26 ÷ 0.2 °C/s = 130 s of cooling time, which is longer than the 120 s upper bound of the paste window. The minimum cooling rate to reach 120 s is 26 ÷ 120 ≈ 0.22 °C/s. Option b reports a temperature difference rather than a time, option c moves in the wrong direction, and option d raises the peak, which lengthens rather than shortens the exposure. Note that time above liquidus is a measured profile quantity from IPC-7530 and the paste datasheet, not a J-STD-020 parameter.","source":"src-005","lessonId":"m06l06","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m06l07-01","type":"single","competencies":["K5","K6"],"prompt":"A measured reflow profile shows peak temperature within specification and adequate soak, but the time above liquidus is too short and joints show insufficient wetting. Which bounded profile change is most appropriate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Increase reflow-zone dwell to lengthen time above liquidus, improving wetting while keeping peak within limits."},{"id":"b","text":"Increase preheat ramp rate to improve wetting by driving off volatiles faster."},{"id":"c","text":"Decrease cooling rate to improve wetting by allowing more time for solidification."},{"id":"d","text":"Increase soak temperature to fix short time above liquidus without affecting reflow."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Short time above liquidus is corrected in the reflow zone by increasing dwell or peak within limits, which lengthens liquidus exposure and improves wetting. Preheat and soak changes affect activation and solvent removal, not liquidus exposure, while cooling changes affect solidification and can worsen grain structure or defects.","source":"src-008","lessonId":"m06l07","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m06l07-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K5","K6"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula TAL = t_peak - t_liquidus, a rule-of-thumb measured profile has t_peak - t_liquidus = 25 s. If the rule-of-thumb target TAL is 60 s, how many seconds should the reflow dwell be extended before re-verifying peak and TAL?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"25 s"},{"id":"b","text":"35 s"},{"id":"c","text":"60 s"},{"id":"d","text":"85 s"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The current TAL is 25 s, so the required bounded extension is 60 s - 25 s = 35 s. Extending by 25 s would only double the current TAL, not reach the target; 60 s is the target TAL, not the increment; 85 s overshoots the target.","source":"src-008","lessonId":"m06l07","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m09l01-01","type":"single","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"A high-density board with fine-pitch SMDs and through-hole connectors is being wave soldered. Which flux and wave-setting approach is most defensible?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Use a low-solids no-clean flux applied only to wetted areas, preheat to activate the flux without overheating it, and use a dual wave with a low fog height to limit bridging."},{"id":"b","text":"Use a high-solids rosin flux applied broadly, preheat aggressively to dry the flux, and use a single lambda wave with a high wave height."},{"id":"c","text":"Use a water-soluble flux applied generously, keep preheat low to preserve flux activity, and use a micro-wave with a high fog height."},{"id":"d","text":"Use no flux, preheat to the highest practical level, and use a dual wave with a high wave height to force wetting."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Low-solids no-clean flux reduces residue and bridging risk on fine-pitch boards, while selective application avoids unnecessary flux on non-wetted areas. Proper preheat activates the flux and removes solvents without degrading it, and a dual wave with low fog height supports wetting while limiting solder exposure. Option b leaves excessive residue and can overheat flux, option c can cause poor wetting and bridging, and option d removes the flux function needed for reliable soldering.","source":"src-001","lessonId":"m09l01","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m09l01-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"Using the lesson rule of thumb that flux amount is the mass of flux solids divided by the wetted area, and that a typical no-clean wave target is 500-1500 µg/in² of solids (about 0.08-0.23 mg/cm²), a board has a wetted area of 250 cm² and a weighed witness card gains 37.5 mg of flux solids. What is the flux amount and the appropriate setting decision?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.15 mg/cm² (about 970 µg/in²); within the typical target, so keep the flux amount."},{"id":"b","text":"1.5 mg/cm²; too high, so reduce the flux amount."},{"id":"c","text":"0.015 mg/cm²; too low, so increase the flux amount."},{"id":"d","text":"0.06 mg/cm²; too low, because the area was divided twice."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"37.5 mg ÷ 250 cm² = 0.15 mg/cm². Converting the other way, 0.15 mg/cm² × 6.45 cm²/in² = 0.97 mg/in² = about 970 µg/in², which is inside the 500-1500 µg/in² target, so the setting is kept. Option b is a factor-of-ten error, option c is a factor-of-ten low error, and option d divides by the area twice. Always confirm whether a quoted figure is on a solids basis or a liquid basis: figures such as 0.5-1.0 g/ft² are quoted per unit of liquid and are several times larger.","source":"src-001","lessonId":"m09l01","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m09l02-01","type":"single","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"A board has fine-pitch QFNs, small passives, and large ground planes. The process requires minimal post-reflow residue and good wetting on exposed pads. Which flux selection and application setting is best justified?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Low-solids no-clean flux with a moderate amount and preheat tuned to activate the flux without boiling it off, because low solids reduce residue while sufficient preheat supports wetting on planes."},{"id":"b","text":"High-solids rosin flux with maximum fog density, because more solids always improve wetting on exposed pads."},{"id":"c","text":"Water-soluble flux with minimal preheat and maximum wave height, because water-soluble fluxes need no activation and high wave height prevents bridging."},{"id":"d","text":"No flux for the QFNs and heavy flux only on through-hole leads, because no-clean flux cannot be used on fine-pitch components."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Low-solids no-clean flux reduces residue, and a moderate amount with adequate preheat helps activate the flux and wet large planes without excessive boiling. High solids and maximum fog increase residue and bridging risk. Water-soluble fluxes require cleaning, and excessive wave height can cause bridging rather than prevent it. Omitting flux on QFNs would reduce wetting and solderability.","source":"src-001","lessonId":"m09l02","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m09l02-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"A verification card is used to check flux amount. The lesson formula is flux amount = (mass after spray − mass before spray) / wetted area. A run records a card mass of 10.000 g before spray and 10.012 g after spray over a wetted area of 80 cm². What is the measured flux amount?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.15 mg/cm²"},{"id":"b","text":"0.67 mg/cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"1.5 mg/cm²"},{"id":"d","text":"120 mg/cm²"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The mass difference is 10.012 g − 10.000 g = 0.012 g, which is 12 mg. Dividing by the wetted area gives 12 mg / 80 cm² = 0.15 mg/cm², which is about 970 µg/in² of solids and inside the 500-1500 µg/in² wave target. Option c is the same mass gain with a decimal error, option b inverts the ratio, and option d omits division by area. If the card captures flux solution rather than solids, multiply by the solids fraction before comparing with the target.","source":"src-001","lessonId":"m09l02","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m09l04-01","type":"single","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"During wave soldering, the fog height is set too high relative to board clearance. What is the most likely defect mechanism?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Solder or flux mist reaches the board above the wave and deposits on pads, leads, or component bodies, causing bridging or poor wetting."},{"id":"b","text":"The dwell time increases because the board spends longer in the fog zone, causing excessive intermetallic growth."},{"id":"c","text":"The preheat temperature drops because the fog cools the board before the wave, causing insufficient flux activation."},{"id":"d","text":"The wave height decreases because fog height and wave height are mechanically linked, causing insufficient contact."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Fog height too high relative to board clearance lets solder or flux mist reach the board above the wave, which can deposit on pads, leads, or component bodies and cause bridging or poor wetting. Dwell time is governed by conveyor speed and contact length, not fog height, and preheat level is a separate thermal setting.","source":"src-001","lessonId":"m09l04","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m09l04-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"Using rule-of-thumb values of an effective contact length of 12 mm and a conveyor speed of 1.2 m/min, calculate the wave dwell time.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.6 s"},{"id":"b","text":"600 s"},{"id":"c","text":"6 s"},{"id":"d","text":"60 s"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Using the rule-of-thumb values, convert speed to mm/min: 1.2 m/min = 1200 mm/min. Dwell = contact length / speed = 12 mm / 1200 mm/min = 0.01 min = 0.6 s. The other options come from unit-conversion errors: 600 s if m/min is not converted to mm/min, 6 s if speed is taken as 120 mm/min, and 60 s if contact length is mis-scaled to about 1.2 m.","source":"src-001","lessonId":"m09l04","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m09l05-01","type":"single","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"A wave-soldered board shows un-wetted pads behind tall components when the board travels in one direction. Which change most directly removes the shadowing without redesigning the component?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Rotate the board or component orientation so the shadow falls away from the pads."},{"id":"b","text":"Increase wave speed so solder reaches the pads faster."},{"id":"c","text":"Use a larger pallet to carry more boards per run."},{"id":"d","text":"Increase solder temperature to force wetting behind the component."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Shadowing is a geometric effect, so changing board direction, component orientation, masking, or sequence can move the shadow away from the pads. Increasing wave speed or solder temperature does not remove the geometric blockage and can create other defects. A larger pallet changes throughput, not the shadow path.","source":"src-008","lessonId":"m09l05","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m09l05-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula shadow length = component height / tan(wave angle), a component height is twice the pad spacing and the wave angle is a standard angle whose tangent is unity. What is the shadow length?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"half the pad spacing"},{"id":"b","text":"the pad spacing"},{"id":"c","text":"twice the pad spacing"},{"id":"d","text":"four times the pad spacing"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The formula gives shadow length = twice the pad spacing / unity = twice the pad spacing. Half and four times result from misapplying the tangent relationship. The pad spacing answer ignores the component height.","source":"src-008","lessonId":"m09l05","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m09l06-01","type":"single","competencies":["K7","K6"],"prompt":"A wave-soldered through-hole joint shows a solder-side image with a small, rounded solder ball sitting on the pad near the hole, while the hole itself is not filled. The ball appears separate from the main joint and does not wet into the pad. Which parameter is most likely responsible for this defect?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Insufficient preheat, leaving the board and lead too cold for the solder to wet and flow into the hole."},{"id":"b","text":"Excessive conveyor speed, which mainly reduces dwell time and tends to produce insufficient fill rather than isolated balls."},{"id":"c","text":"Excessive solder volume, which tends to produce bridging or excess solder rather than a detached ball."},{"id":"d","text":"Excessive flux activity, which generally improves wetting and would not be the primary cause of a non-wetted ball."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A small rounded ball near an unfilled hole is a balling/non-wetting signature, and insufficient preheat is a common parameter cause because the joint cannot reach the temperature needed for solder to wet the pad and flow into the hole. Excessive conveyor speed is more associated with insufficient fill, excessive solder volume is more associated with bridging or excess solder, and excessive flux activity would usually help wetting rather than cause a detached ball.","source":"src-001","lessonId":"m09l06","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m09l06-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K7","K6"],"prompt":"A wave-soldered through-hole has a board thickness of 1.6 mm and the solder column reaches 1.3 mm from the primary side. Using the lesson formula fill fraction = fill height / board thickness, and comparing with the IPC-A-610 minimum vertical barrel fill of 75%, what is the fill fraction and the disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"81.25%; accept, because the calculated vertical fill meets the IPC-A-610 minimum."},{"id":"b","text":"68.75%; reject, because it is below the minimum."},{"id":"c","text":"81.25%; reject, because the annular area fraction is the quantity that is judged."},{"id":"d","text":"43.75%; reject, because the lead occupies more than half of the hole area."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Fill fraction is measured vertically: fill height divided by board thickness, so 1.3 mm / 1.6 mm = 0.8125 = 81.25%, which is above the 75% minimum vertical barrel fill that IPC-A-610 sets for both Class 2 and Class 3. The disposition is accept when the other workmanship criteria are met. Option b is the height ratio for a 1.1 mm column — the lesson’s own worked example, not this one. Option c confuses vertical fill with annular area capacity, which is a geometric property of the hole and lead and is never compared with the acceptance limit. Option d is that capacity for a lead three-quarters of the hole diameter. IPC-A-610 does not set a 100% class limit; a 100% requirement is contractual.","source":"src-001","lessonId":"m09l06","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m09l07-01","type":"single","competencies":["K7","K6"],"prompt":"A solder-side image shows bridges between adjacent through-hole pads and excess solder on component leads, while hole fill is complete. Which parameter is most likely responsible?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Wave height too high"},{"id":"b","text":"Flux amount too low"},{"id":"c","text":"Preheat level too low"},{"id":"d","text":"Fog height too low"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Excessive wave height increases the solder volume contacting the board and can force solder into gaps, producing bridges and excess solder. Low flux or low preheat would more likely cause insufficient wetting, dewetting, or incomplete hole fill rather than excess solder. Low fog would reduce preheat or flux activation, not create excess solder volume.","source":"src-001","lessonId":"m09l07","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m09l07-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K7","K6"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's rule of thumb that flux coverage should be 0.15 mg/cm² of flux solids on the board area and that the flux is 2.0% solids with a density of about 1 g/mL, calculate the flux volume for the lesson's rule-of-thumb reference board area of 100 cm².","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.75 mL"},{"id":"b","text":"0.075 mL"},{"id":"c","text":"7.5 mL"},{"id":"d","text":"15 mL"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Flux solids = 100 cm² × 0.15 mg/cm² = 15 mg. At 2.0% solids the solution mass is 15 mg ÷ 0.02 = 750 mg, and at about 1 g/mL the volume is 750 mg ÷ 1000 mg/mL = 0.75 mL. Options b and c are factor-of-ten errors, and option d treats the solids mass as if it were the solution volume.","source":"src-001","lessonId":"m09l07","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m01l02-01","lessonId":"m01l02","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"A received lot of components, solder paste, or bare PCB must be checked against its documentation package. Which document primarily proves that the lot conforms to the specified material, process, and test requirements?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Certificate of Conformance or Certificate of Analysis (CoC/CoA)"},{"id":"b","text":"Packing list"},{"id":"c","text":"Moisture sensitivity level (MSL) label"},{"id":"d","text":"Regulatory or compliance declarations"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The CoC/CoA proves that the lot was manufactured, tested, or analyzed against the specified material and process requirements. The packing list proves what quantities and items were shipped, the MSL label proves moisture sensitivity and floor-life status, and declarations prove regulatory or compliance status rather than lot conformity.","source":"src-003","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m01l02-02","lessonId":"m01l02","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"A moisture-sensitive component is labeled MSL 3. Per IPC/JEDEC J-STD-033, the floor life for MSL 3 is 168 h. The label’s exposure clock shows that one-third of the floor life has elapsed. Using the lesson formula remaining floor life = floor life − elapsed floor life, what is the remaining floor life?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"112 h"},{"id":"b","text":"56 h"},{"id":"c","text":"168 h"},{"id":"d","text":"224 h"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The elapsed floor life is 168 h ÷ 3 = 56 h, so the remaining floor life is 168 h − 56 h = 112 h. Option b is the elapsed time, option c ignores elapsed time, and option d adds elapsed time instead of subtracting it.","source":"src-003","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m01l03-01","lessonId":"m01l03","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"What does an AQL-based sampling plan protect against?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It provides a defined probability of accepting lots at acceptable quality and a defined risk of accepting worse lots, but it does not guarantee zero defects."},{"id":"b","text":"It guarantees that every defective item in the lot will be detected."},{"id":"c","text":"It makes the sample statistically representative even if the lot is not homogeneous."},{"id":"d","text":"It eliminates producer risk by accepting all lots at the AQL."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"An AQL sampling plan is risk-based: it defines acceptance behavior at acceptable quality and the risk of accepting worse quality. It does not guarantee zero defects, ensure representativeness for a poorly defined lot, or eliminate producer risk.","source":"src-003","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m01l03-02","lessonId":"m01l03","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"Using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 normal inspection, a lot of 1,500 units is inspected at inspection level II with AQL 1.0%. What single-sampling plan should be used?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"n = 80, Ac = 2"},{"id":"b","text":"n = 125, Ac = 3"},{"id":"c","text":"n = 125, Ac = 5"},{"id":"d","text":"n = 200, Ac = 5"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 maps lot size 1,201–3,200 at inspection level II to code letter K; code K has sample size 125, and the normal single-sampling table gives Ac = 3 at AQL 1.0%. Option a is the code J plan, which is the level I code letter for the next band up (3201 to 10000) and not for this lot; option d is the level III code L plan for this band; and option c mixes the code K sample size with the code L acceptance number.","source":"src-003","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m01l04-01","lessonId":"m01l04","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"For a received lot of components, paste, or bare PCB, which document primarily proves that the lot conforms to the specified part or material, package or construction, and lot or date code?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Certificate of Conformance"},{"id":"b","text":"Certificate of Analysis"},{"id":"c","text":"Packing slip"},{"id":"d","text":"Purchase order"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A Certificate of Conformance is the lot-specific document that attests the delivered part or material, package or construction, and lot or date code match the purchase specification. A Certificate of Analysis proves test or material results, not identity alone; a packing slip proves shipment contents and quantity; a purchase order proves what was requested, not what was verified as shipped.","source":"src-003","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m01l04-02","lessonId":"m01l04","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K9"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's rule of thumb that each missing mandatory document is one finding, each counterfeit red flag is one finding, and any counterfeit red flag rejects the lot regardless of other findings, a received lot has two missing mandatory documents and one counterfeit red flag. What disposition and total number of findings should be recorded for the supplier?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Reject; three findings"},{"id":"b","text":"Quarantine; two findings"},{"id":"c","text":"Accept; zero findings"},{"id":"d","text":"Reject; one finding"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The arithmetic is two missing documents plus one counterfeit red flag equals three findings. Because the counterfeit red flag count is one, which is greater than zero, the lot is rejected even though the missing documents alone would be quarantine findings.","source":"src-003","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m01l05-01","lessonId":"m01l05","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"A lot of solder paste arrives. Which set of documents best proves what the lot is and whether it can be accepted?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Certificate of conformance/analysis, lot-specific cold-chain temperature record, and expiry label; the certificate proves alloy/powder/flux type, the temperature record proves storage integrity, and the expiry label proves the expiry date for shelf-life assessment."},{"id":"b","text":"Purchase order and invoice; they prove the lot was ordered and paid for, so material identity and storage history are assumed."},{"id":"c","text":"Safety data sheet and supplier marketing datasheet; they prove hazard information and product family, so lot-specific alloy, powder, and cold-chain status need not be checked."},{"id":"d","text":"Visual inspection only; if the container is sealed and the label is present, the alloy, powder, expiry, and cold-chain status are proven."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Option a is correct because acceptance requires lot-specific evidence of material identity/spec and storage integrity. Option b proves commercial transaction, not technical compliance. Option c proves hazard/product family but not lot-specific alloy, powder, expiry, or cold-chain. Option d cannot prove internal material properties or temperature history.","source":"src-003","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m01l05-02","lessonId":"m01l05","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K9"],"prompt":"IPC J-STD-005 Type 3 powder requires 80% of the powder to be between 25 and 45 µm, with no more than 10% below 20 µm and less than 1% above 45 µm. A certificate reports that 99.5% passes the 45 µm sieve and 95% is retained on the 25 µm sieve. Using the lesson formula for minimum overlap, what is the minimum percentage within 25-45 µm and the appropriate disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"94.5%; accept because the certificate proves at least 94.5% in range, which meets the 80% requirement."},{"id":"b","text":"80%; quarantine because the certificate does not prove the requirement is met."},{"id":"c","text":"99.5%; accept because the two sieve results are additive."},{"id":"d","text":"94.5%; reject because any excursion below 100% is automatically reject."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The minimum overlap is 99.5% + 95% − 100% = 94.5%, so the certificate proves at least 94.5% of the powder is within 25-45 µm. Because J-STD-005 Type 3 requires 80% minimum in range, the lot meets the in-band requirement. The tail limits are met as well: at most 0.5% is above 45 µm against the less-than-1% limit, and at most 5% is below 25 µm, which bounds the below-20 µm fraction at 5% against the 10% limit. Option b applies the requirement as if it were the measured overlap, option c adds the two sieve results instead of taking their minimum overlap, and option d invents a 100% requirement that the standard does not contain.","source":"src-015","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m01l06-01","lessonId":"m01l06","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"A lot of bare PCBs arrives for incoming inspection. Which accompanying document is the primary evidence that the lot was inspected and conforms to the purchase order, drawing, and applicable IPC acceptance criteria?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Certificate of Conformance"},{"id":"b","text":"Material Safety Data Sheet"},{"id":"c","text":"Packing list"},{"id":"d","text":"Shipping label"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A Certificate of Conformance states that the received lot was inspected and conforms to the purchase order, drawing, and applicable IPC acceptance criteria. A Material Safety Data Sheet provides safe-handling information, not PCB conformance. A packing list confirms quantities and packing, while a shipping label identifies the shipment; neither proves quality acceptance.","source":"src-003","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m01l06-02","lessonId":"m01l06","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K9"],"prompt":"A bare PCB lot is inspected for planarity using a rule-of-thumb inspection span of 100 mm. A rule-of-thumb measured bow is 0.8 mm. IPC-6012 Class 3 bow/twist limit is 0.75% of the longer dimension. What is the measured bow as a percentage of the span, and what disposition should be recorded?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.80%; reject because it exceeds the IPC-6012 Class 3 limit"},{"id":"b","text":"0.75%; accept because it meets the IPC-6012 Class 3 limit"},{"id":"c","text":"1.50%; quarantine because it is within the IPC-6012 Class 2 limit"},{"id":"d","text":"0.008%; accept because the deviation was divided by 100 instead of multiplied by 100"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Bow percentage = (0.8 mm / 100 mm) × 100 = 0.8%. IPC-6012 Class 3 allows 0.75%, so 0.8% exceeds the limit and the finding should be recorded as reject. Option b uses the limit instead of the measured value, option c applies the wrong class limit, and option d is a decimal-conversion error.","source":"src-018","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m01l07-01","lessonId":"m01l07","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"At the dock, a lot of moisture-sensitive SMT components arrives with crushed vacuum bags and missing moisture indicator cards, but the parts themselves appear intact. What is the correct immediate disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept and release to stores because the parts appear intact."},{"id":"b","text":"Quarantine the lot pending verification of packaging integrity, moisture status, and traceability."},{"id":"c","text":"Reject and return the lot immediately."},{"id":"d","text":"Send the lot to MRB for scrap."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Damaged packaging and missing moisture indicators create uncertainty about conformity, so quarantine is the correct dock action until verification. Acceptance requires complete conformity evidence; rejection requires confirmed nonconformance or inability to verify; MRB is for disposition of confirmed nonconforming material, not the first action for an uncertain finding.","source":"src-003","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m01l07-02","lessonId":"m01l07","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"Under ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 normal inspection at AQL 0.65%, a sampling plan gives sample size 200, acceptance number 3, and rejection number 4. If the inspection finds the rejection number of defective components, what observed defect rate should be recorded on the supplier notification and what dock disposition is required?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"2%; reject"},{"id":"b","text":"0.1%; accept"},{"id":"c","text":"2%; quarantine"},{"id":"d","text":"0.1%; quarantine"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The observed defect rate is 4 ÷ 200 = 0.02 = 2%. Because the count equals the rejection number, the lot is rejected; quarantine is for uncertain or pending verification, and acceptance would require defects at or below the acceptance number of 3.","source":"src-003","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m02l01-01","lessonId":"m02l01","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"A moisture-sensitive device was removed from its moisture barrier bag, exposed for part of its floor life, reflowed while still within floor life, and is now exposed again before a second reflow. What is the correct way to assess its remaining floor life?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Count only the exposure after the last reflow, because reflow resets the floor-life clock."},{"id":"b","text":"Add all exposure before and after reflow, because moisture absorption is cumulative and irreversible."},{"id":"c","text":"Ignore exposure time entirely and use only the number of reflows, because floor life is unrelated to time out of bag."},{"id":"d","text":"Assume unlimited floor life after any reflow, because reflow drives all moisture out permanently."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Floor life is the time a device may be exposed after opening its moisture barrier bag before reflow. A reflow performed while the device is still within floor life resets the floor-life clock, so only exposure after that reflow counts. The other options are wrong because prior exposure is not added, floor life is not replaced by reflow count, and reflow does not make the device permanently immune to moisture.","source":"src-003","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m02l01-02","lessonId":"m02l01","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"A device marked MSL 3 was exposed for one-third of its J-STD-033 floor life, reflowed while still within floor life, and has since been exposed for one-quarter of its floor life. What is the remaining floor life before baking is required?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"126 h"},{"id":"b","text":"70 h"},{"id":"c","text":"168 h"},{"id":"d","text":"42 h"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"MSL 3 has a J-STD-033 floor life of 168 h at 30 °C/60% RH (J-STD-020 assigns the level, J-STD-033 governs handling). Because the device was reflowed while within floor life, the clock resets, so only the post-reflow exposure counts: one-quarter of 168 h is 42 h, leaving 168 h - 42 h = 126 h. Option b incorrectly adds the pre-reflow one-third exposure, option c ignores elapsed post-reflow time, and option d is the elapsed post-reflow time rather than the remaining time.","source":"src-003","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m02l02-01","lessonId":"m02l02","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"A sealed dry pack is marked MSL 3, the humidity indicator card is still blue, and the reflow history shows no reflow. What is the correct interpretation of remaining floor life?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The device has its full MSL 3 floor life remaining because floor life starts when the device is removed from the dry pack."},{"id":"b","text":"The blue humidity indicator card proves the device has already been reflowed and has no floor life remaining."},{"id":"c","text":"The MSL marking alone determines remaining floor life, so the bag condition and reflow history are irrelevant."},{"id":"d","text":"The absence of reflow history means the floor life has already been consumed."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Floor life begins when the device is removed from its dry pack, so an unopened bag with a good humidity indicator card means the full floor life for the marked MSL rating is still available. The humidity indicator card shows bag condition, not reflow history. The MSL marking gives the rating, but remaining floor life also depends on exposure and reflow history.","source":"src-003","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m02l02-02","lessonId":"m02l02","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"A device is marked MSL 3. According to J-STD-033, its floor life is 168 h. A dry-pack log shows it was exposed for a duration equal to the J-STD-033 floor life of MSL 4, and the reflow history shows no reflow. How many hours of floor life remain?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"96 h"},{"id":"b","text":"72 h"},{"id":"c","text":"168 h"},{"id":"d","text":"240 h"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"J-STD-033 gives MSL 3 a floor life of 168 h and MSL 4 a floor life of 72 h. Remaining floor life is 168 h minus 72 h, which equals 96 h. Option b is the exposure time, not the remaining time; option c ignores the exposure; option d adds the exposure instead of subtracting it.","source":"src-003","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m02l03-01","lessonId":"m02l03","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"When does floor-life accumulation begin for an MSL-rated device?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"When the moisture barrier bag is opened and the device is exposed to ambient conditions."},{"id":"b","text":"When the device is placed on a feeder."},{"id":"c","text":"When the device enters its first reflow oven."},{"id":"d","text":"When the device is removed from a dry cabinet."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The floor-life clock starts when the sealed moisture barrier bag is opened and the device is exposed to ambient conditions. Placement on a feeder or entry into reflow occurs after exposure has already begun. Removal from a dry cabinet only matters if the device had been stored under controlled conditions; it does not define the start of the clock.","source":"src-003","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m02l03-02","lessonId":"m02l03","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"A device marked MSL 3 is removed from its dry-pack. J-STD-033 gives MSL 3 a floor life of 168 h. The bag data card shows one shift of exposure; use the rule of thumb that one shift is eight hours. What is the remaining floor life?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"160 h"},{"id":"b","text":"168 h"},{"id":"c","text":"8 h"},{"id":"d","text":"0 h"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Remaining floor life is standard floor life minus accumulated exposure: 168 h - 8 h = 160 h. The 168 h option ignores the recorded exposure. The 8 h option reports exposure rather than remaining life. The 0 h option would apply only if accumulated exposure had reached or exceeded the standard floor life.","source":"src-003","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m02l04-01","lessonId":"m02l04","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"A batch of moisture-sensitive SMT components arrives in a compromised vacuum dry pack with a humidity indicator card showing the moisture threshold exceeded. What is the required disposition before the components are used in reflow?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Bake the components to the bake condition for their moisture sensitivity level, then store them in a dry cabinet until the floor life is consumed."},{"id":"b","text":"Use the components directly because SMT reflow can drive off moisture during soldering."},{"id":"c","text":"Store the components in a dry cabinet without baking because the humidity indicator card is only advisory."},{"id":"d","text":"Bake only if the components are through-hole; surface-mount packages do not require baking."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A compromised dry pack and exceeded humidity indicator threshold indicate moisture exposure, so the batch must be baked to the condition specified for its package moisture sensitivity level and then dry-stored. Reflow is not a substitute for baking, and the humidity indicator card is a control record rather than advisory. Through-hole and surface-mount packages can both be moisture-sensitive, so package assembly type alone does not exempt them.","source":"src-003","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m02l04-02","lessonId":"m02l04","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"A 2.5 mm-thick package is baked at 90 °C with floor life exceeded by more than 72 h. The J-STD-033 Table 4-1 bake time for that body thickness is 10 days. If the bake has already run for half of the bake time, how many hours remain before the bake is complete?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"120 h"},{"id":"b","text":"240 h"},{"id":"c","text":"48 h"},{"id":"d","text":"96 h"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"J-STD-033 bake time is indexed to package body thickness, the moisture sensitivity level and whether floor life was exceeded by more than 72 h or by 72 h or less, so all three must be read off the table before a time is quoted. For a body in the >2.0 to 4.5 mm band at 90 °C, the >72 h column gives 10 days, which is 240 h. Half of that is 120 h, so 120 h remain. The 240 h option is the total bake time; 48 h is the 125 °C bake time for the same thickness band, not a 90 °C time; 96 h is the cumulative bake limit, which applies only above 90 °C — at 90 °C or below there is no cumulative limit, which is precisely why a 240 h bake at 90 °C is permitted at all.","source":"src-003","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m02l05-01","lessonId":"m02l05","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"During an EPA audit in an SMT/THT assembly area, which gap should be ranked highest risk?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A wrist strap system fails the continuity check, so operators are not reliably grounded while handling ESD-sensitive assemblies."},{"id":"b","text":"An ionizer is slightly out of balance in an area where devices are already in conductive packaging."},{"id":"c","text":"A grounded work surface is clean but has a small cosmetic scratch that does not affect resistance."},{"id":"d","text":"A static-dissipative floor is not periodically cleaned according to the housekeeping schedule."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A failed wrist strap system removes the primary personnel grounding control, so operators can directly discharge ESD-sensitive assemblies. An ionizer imbalance is lower risk when devices are already protected by conductive packaging, and a cosmetic scratch does not change the surface's electrical performance. Poor floor cleaning can degrade control over time but is less immediate than a broken grounding path.","source":"src-012","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m02l05-02","lessonId":"m02l05","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"In an SMT/THT EPA audit, using the lesson's risk formula Risk = Likelihood × Severity and the checklist weights taught in the lesson, what is the difference between the risk score for a wrist strap system outside its named standard limit and the risk score for an ionizer outside its named standard balance limit?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"2"},{"id":"b","text":"4"},{"id":"c","text":"6"},{"id":"d","text":"12"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The checklist weights give Likelihood = 2 for an out-of-limit control, Severity = 3 for personnel grounding, and Severity = 2 for ionization. Therefore the wrist strap gap scores 2 × 3 = 6 and the ionizer gap scores 2 × 2 = 4, so the difference is 2. The other options are the ionizer score, the wrist strap score, and the product of the two scores.","source":"src-012","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m02l06-01","lessonId":"m02l06","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K9"],"prompt":"During SMT placement, a moisture-sensitive lot's floor-life label is found expired after some boards have been placed. The ESD wrist-strap log also shows no verification since the start of shift. What is the correct containment and traceability range?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Quarantine all boards placed after the last verified ESD control point and after the floor-life expiry; bake remaining unplaced components and any placed-but-not-reflowed boards that can be baked per package type; traceability range is from the last verified ESD control point and last verified dry-pack opening to the next verified point."},{"id":"b","text":"Bake only the remaining unplaced components; boards already placed are acceptable because reflow removes moisture; traceability range is only the expired label."},{"id":"c","text":"Scrap all boards and components because any breach invalidates the entire lot; traceability range is the entire lot."},{"id":"d","text":"Continue production and bake at end of shift; traceability range is only the current reel."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A floor-life breach requires baking remaining unplaced moisture-sensitive components according to package type, while already placed boards need containment based on the last verified ESD control point because ESD exposure may affect handled units. Reflow is not a substitute for controlled bake for all package types. Scraping the whole lot is not justified without evidence of damage, and continuing production expands the breach.","source":"src-003","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m02l06-02","lessonId":"m02l06","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"Using J-STD-033, an MSL 3 plastic package has a floor life of 168 h at ≤30°C/60%RH. A lot is found mid-lot with cumulative exposure equal to one full floor life plus one additional standard 24 h interval. Using the lesson formula cumulative exposure = sum of exposure intervals and bake if cumulative exposure > floor life, what is the overage and required action?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"One standard 24 h interval over floor life; bake remaining unplaced components at the package-specific bake condition from J-STD-033 Table 4-1 — for this MSL 3 lot with the exceedance inside 72 h, 125 °C for 7 h up to 1.4 mm, 17 h for 1.4-2.0 mm or 48 h for 2.0-4.5 mm, or the 40 °C long bake if the carrier cannot take 125 °C — then return to dry storage at ≤30°C/60%RH with desiccant and HIC."},{"id":"b","text":"One full floor life over; bake at 125°C for 8 h."},{"id":"c","text":"One full floor life plus one standard interval remaining; no bake."},{"id":"d","text":"No overage; no bake because the lot was already baked."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"168 h + 24 h = 192 h cumulative exposure; 192 h − 168 h = 24 h over floor life. Because cumulative exposure exceeds floor life, remaining unplaced components must be baked. The bake condition comes from J-STD-033 Table 4-1 and is indexed to package body thickness, the MSL level and the size of the exceedance: for this MSL 3 lot with a 24 h exceedance, inside the ≤72 h column, 125 °C gives 7 h up to 1.4 mm, 17 h for 1.4-2.0 mm and 48 h for 2.0-4.5 mm. The 40 °C long bake runs 8 to 22 days for a body up to 1.4 mm and 25 to 79 days for 1.4 to 2.0 mm, again by MSL level, and is reserved for carriers that cannot take 125 °C. The 8 h in option b is the >72 h column value for MSL 2a, not the ≤72 h value for MSL 3. Cumulative bake time above 90 °C is limited to 96 h unless the supplier states otherwise, and the 24 h figure sometimes quoted for a 125 °C bake is the supplier dry-pack condition from Table 4-2, not a user bake. Distractors misapply the floor-life subtraction or assume a prior bake resets exposure.","source":"src-003","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m03l01-01","lessonId":"m03l01","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"prompt":"A small stencil aperture releases paste poorly even though its width is greater than the stencil thickness. Which comparison best explains why release can still fail?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Area ratio is low because the aperture area is small relative to the sidewall area that holds paste back."},{"id":"b","text":"Aspect ratio is too high, causing paste to overfill the aperture."},{"id":"c","text":"Deposit volume is too large because stencil thickness is too small."},{"id":"d","text":"The aperture perimeter is too small, increasing paste adhesion to the sidewalls."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Area ratio compares aperture area to sidewall area, so a small aperture can have low area ratio even when width exceeds thickness. Aspect ratio alone does not account for perimeter, while high aspect ratio generally helps release. Deposit volume scales with aperture area and stencil thickness, and a smaller perimeter reduces rather than increases sidewall adhesion.","source":"src-004","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m03l01-02","lessonId":"m03l01","type":"calc","competencies":["K2"],"prompt":"Using the stencil-design formulas area ratio = aperture area / (perimeter × stencil thickness), aspect ratio = aperture width / stencil thickness, and deposit volume = aperture area × stencil thickness, a square aperture is specified with side length equal to the stencil thickness. Which result and release judgment is correct?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Area ratio = 0.25, aspect ratio = 1, deposit volume = t³; release is inadequate by the area-ratio rule of thumb."},{"id":"b","text":"Area ratio = 4, aspect ratio = 1, deposit volume = t³; release is adequate."},{"id":"c","text":"Area ratio = 0.25, aspect ratio = 4, deposit volume = t³; release is adequate."},{"id":"d","text":"Area ratio = 0.25, aspect ratio = 1, deposit volume = 4t³; release is inadequate."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Let t be stencil thickness and the square side be t. Area ratio = t²/(4t·t)=0.25, aspect ratio = t/t=1, and deposit volume = t²·t=t³. Because the area ratio is below the rule-of-thumb release minimum of 0.66 (and the aspect ratio of 1 is also below the 1.5 minimum), release is inadequate; b inverts the ratio, c uses perimeter as aspect ratio, and d uses sidewall area instead of aperture area for volume.","source":"src-004","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m03l02-01","lessonId":"m03l02","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"prompt":"For a fixed stencil thickness, how does increasing the perimeter of an aperture while keeping its open area constant affect paste release?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Area ratio decreases, making paste release less reliable."},{"id":"b","text":"Area ratio increases, making paste release more reliable."},{"id":"c","text":"Aspect ratio decreases, making paste release more reliable."},{"id":"d","text":"Deposit volume decreases, making paste release less reliable."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Area ratio = open area / (stencil thickness × perimeter), so with constant area and thickness, larger perimeter lowers area ratio and worsens release. Aspect ratio is thickness divided by aperture width, not perimeter, so it is not the primary effect here. Deposit volume = open area × stencil thickness, so it remains unchanged.","source":"src-004","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m03l02-02","lessonId":"m03l02","type":"calc","competencies":["K2"],"prompt":"A square aperture is 0.10 mm on a side and is cut in a 0.12 mm stencil. Using area ratio = open area / (stencil thickness × perimeter), aspect ratio = aperture width / stencil thickness, and deposit volume = open area × stencil thickness assuming full transfer, which result and release assessment is correct?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Area ratio ≈ 0.21, deposit volume ≈ 0.0012 mm³, release likely poor."},{"id":"b","text":"Area ratio ≈ 4.8, deposit volume ≈ 0.0012 mm³, release likely good."},{"id":"c","text":"Area ratio ≈ 0.21, deposit volume ≈ 0.012 mm³, release likely good."},{"id":"d","text":"Area ratio ≈ 1.2, deposit volume ≈ 0.0012 mm³, release likely poor."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Open area = 0.10 mm × 0.10 mm = 0.0100 mm², perimeter = 4 × 0.10 mm = 0.40 mm, and area ratio = 0.0100 mm² / (0.12 mm × 0.40 mm) = 0.0100 / 0.048 ≈ 0.21. Deposit volume = 0.0100 mm² × 0.12 mm = 0.0012 mm³, and aspect ratio = 0.10 mm / 0.12 mm = 0.83, so option d is neither the area ratio nor the aspect ratio. By the usual rule of thumb, both indices fail: the area ratio of 0.21 is below the 0.66 minimum and the aspect ratio of 0.83 is below the 1.5 minimum, so paste release is poor. Option b inverts the area-ratio calculation and option c misplaces the decimal in volume.","source":"src-004","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m03l03-01","lessonId":"m03l03","type":"single","competencies":["K2","K3"],"prompt":"A board has both large power pads and fine-pitch QFP pads. Why would a step stencil be preferred over a single uniform foil thickness?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It provides thicker foil over large pads for paste volume while using thinner foil over fine-pitch pads to reduce bridging."},{"id":"b","text":"It increases paste release speed uniformly, preventing tombstoning on all components."},{"id":"c","text":"It reduces aperture perimeter uniformly, preventing stencil clogging on fine-pitch pads."},{"id":"d","text":"It improves laser-cut wall finish uniformly, preventing solder balling on large pads."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A step stencil varies foil thickness by zone, allowing adequate paste volume on large pads while reducing paste height and volume on fine-pitch pads to prevent bridging. A uniform thickness cannot optimize both paste volume and fine-pitch paste control. Tombstoning, clogging, and wall finish are not primarily solved by changing foil thickness zones.","source":"src-004","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m03l03-02","lessonId":"m03l03","type":"calc","competencies":["K2","K3"],"prompt":"Using IPC-7525 area-ratio guidance, a square aperture is specified as 0.40 mm on a side with 0.12 mm foil. Using area ratio = aperture area / (foil thickness x aperture perimeter), what is the area ratio?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.83, acceptable because it is above the IPC-7525 minimum area ratio."},{"id":"b","text":"0.66, acceptable because it equals the IPC-7525 minimum area ratio."},{"id":"c","text":"1.20, unacceptable because it is too high and causes bridging."},{"id":"d","text":"0.42, unacceptable because it is below the IPC-7525 minimum area ratio."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Aperture area = 0.40 x 0.40 = 0.16 mm^2, and aperture perimeter = 4 x 0.40 = 1.60 mm. The denominator is 0.12 x 1.60 = 0.192 mm^2, so area ratio = 0.16 / 0.192 = 0.83. This is above the IPC-7525 minimum area ratio, so the aperture is acceptable for paste release; the other values result from misapplying the formula or confusing the calculated ratio with the minimum threshold.","source":"src-004","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m03l04-01","lessonId":"m03l04","type":"single","competencies":["K2","K3"],"prompt":"For a fine-pitch QFP with narrow adjacent pads, why would a process engineer choose a thinner foil and slightly reduced aperture length instead of full-pad apertures?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It improves area ratio through thinner foil and controls paste volume through reduced aperture length, helping prevent bridging between adjacent pads."},{"id":"b","text":"It increases paste volume to prevent insufficient solder joints."},{"id":"c","text":"It lowers area ratio to improve paste release from the stencil."},{"id":"d","text":"It increases paste height to prevent tombstoning on chip components."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Thinner foil raises area ratio and lowers paste volume, while reduced aperture length further limits paste volume. Together they reduce excess paste that can bridge fine-pitch QFP pads. Increasing paste volume would worsen bridging, lowering area ratio would worsen release, and tombstoning is a chip-component defect rather than the primary fine-pitch QFP risk.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m03l04-02","lessonId":"m03l04","type":"calc","competencies":["K2","K3"],"prompt":"For a BGA circular aperture, the lesson formula for area ratio is aperture diameter divided by four times foil thickness. A rule of thumb for a BGA pad stack sets the aperture diameter at three times the foil thickness. What is the resulting area ratio?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.66"},{"id":"b","text":"0.75"},{"id":"c","text":"1.33"},{"id":"d","text":"3.00"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Area ratio = d/(4t). With d = 3t, area ratio = 3t/(4t) = 0.75, which exceeds the IPC-7525 minimum of 0.66. The standard-minimum option is not the calculated value; the half-denominator option inverts the factor; the diameter-only option ignores the foil-thickness and perimeter denominator.","source":"src-004","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m03l05-01","lessonId":"m03l05","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"prompt":"A delivered stencil measurement report shows that an aperture's area ratio is below the issued specification minimum, while its aspect ratio and expected deposit volume are within specification. What is the correct disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Reject the stencil because inadequate area ratio can prevent paste release even when volume and aspect ratio pass."},{"id":"b","text":"Accept the stencil because deposit volume is the primary determinant of paste release."},{"id":"c","text":"Accept the stencil because aspect ratio is the only release criterion for rectangular apertures."},{"id":"d","text":"Reject the stencil only if the aspect ratio is also below specification."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Area ratio is the primary paste-release criterion because it compares aperture area to the sidewall area that can hold paste back; if it is below the issued minimum, release is suspect even if volume and aspect ratio pass. Deposit volume indicates how much paste can be deposited, not whether it releases, and aspect ratio is a supporting geometry check rather than the sole criterion.","source":"src-004","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m03l05-02","lessonId":"m03l05","type":"calc","competencies":["K2"],"prompt":"A rectangular aperture is specified as 0.40 mm by 0.40 mm in a 0.120 mm stencil. Using the lesson formulas and the rule-of-thumb minimum area ratio of 0.66 and aspect ratio of 1.5, which result set is correct?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Area ratio 0.83, aspect ratio 3.33, deposit volume 0.0192 mm³; release adequate."},{"id":"b","text":"Area ratio 1.33, aspect ratio 3.33, deposit volume 0.0192 mm³; release adequate."},{"id":"c","text":"Area ratio 0.83, aspect ratio 0.30, deposit volume 0.0192 mm³; release inadequate."},{"id":"d","text":"Area ratio 0.83, aspect ratio 3.33, deposit volume 0.1600 mm³; release adequate."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Area = 0.40 mm × 0.40 mm = 0.16 mm²; perimeter = 2 × (0.40 mm + 0.40 mm) = 1.60 mm; area ratio = 0.16 mm² ÷ (0.120 mm × 1.60 mm) = 0.83. Aspect ratio = 0.40 mm ÷ 0.120 mm = 3.33, and deposit volume = 0.16 mm² × 0.120 mm = 0.0192 mm³. Because 0.83 exceeds the rule-of-thumb minimum area ratio of 0.66 and 3.33 exceeds the rule-of-thumb minimum aspect ratio of 1.5, the aperture is expected to release paste adequately.","source":"src-004","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m03l06-01","lessonId":"m03l06","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"prompt":"A delivered stencil measurement report shows that one aperture is outside the issued tolerance, but the average of all measured apertures is inside tolerance. What is the correct acceptance decision?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Reject the stencil because acceptance is judged aperture by aperture against the issued specification."},{"id":"b","text":"Accept the stencil because the average aperture dimension is within tolerance."},{"id":"c","text":"Accept the stencil if the out-of-tolerance aperture is the smallest one on the panel."},{"id":"d","text":"Reject the stencil only if the foil thickness is also outside tolerance."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Stencil acceptance compares each reported aperture to the issued specification, not to an average of all apertures. A single out-of-tolerance aperture can cause insufficient paste volume or bridging, so the delivered stencil must be rejected. Accepting based on averages or on the size of the aperture ignores the specification limit for that aperture.","source":"src-004","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m03l06-02","lessonId":"m03l06","type":"calc","competencies":["K2"],"prompt":"A stencil lifetime tracker uses the lesson formulas: current aperture area = initial aperture area - (area loss per cleaning x cleaning cycles), and area ratio = aperture area / (perimeter x foil thickness). The issued specification adopts the IPC-7525 minimum area ratio. If the initial aperture area is exactly three times the minimum aperture area, and the cleaning regime has consumed two-thirds of the allowable area loss, calculate the current area ratio and decide whether the stencil should remain in service.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Area ratio 1.10; remain in service."},{"id":"b","text":"Area ratio 0.66; reject."},{"id":"c","text":"Area ratio 1.98; remain in service."},{"id":"d","text":"Area ratio 0.88; reject."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Let the minimum area be 0.66 x perimeter x thickness, so the initial area is 3 x 0.66 = 1.98 x perimeter x thickness. The allowable area loss is 1.98 - 0.66 = 1.32 x perimeter x thickness, and two-thirds of that loss is 0.88 x perimeter x thickness. The current area is 1.98 - 0.88 = 1.10 x perimeter x thickness, giving an area ratio of 1.10, which is above the IPC-7525 minimum, so the stencil remains in service.","source":"src-004","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m05l01-01","lessonId":"m05l01","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"prompt":"Before running a new placement program, you notice the nozzle selected is smaller than the component body and the placement force is set near the component's maximum mechanical limit. What is the best action?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Select a nozzle that supports the component without contacting the body, reduce force to the minimum needed for reliable placement, and verify against mechanical limits."},{"id":"b","text":"Keep the nozzle and force because repeatability is more important than accuracy."},{"id":"c","text":"Increase placement force to compensate for the undersized nozzle."},{"id":"d","text":"Adjust only the pick height and run the program."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The nozzle must support the component without contacting the body, and placement force should be the minimum needed to place reliably while staying within mechanical limits. Repeatability does not justify exceeding mechanical limits, and increasing force can damage the component or substrate. Adjusting pick height alone does not correct an undersized nozzle or excessive force.","source":"src-011","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m05l01-02","lessonId":"m05l01","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula throughput = 3600 / cycle time, and a rule-of-thumb cycle time of 0.25 s/placement, what is the theoretical placement rate in placements per hour?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"14400 placements/h"},{"id":"b","text":"900 placements/h"},{"id":"c","text":"240 placements/h"},{"id":"d","text":"3600 placements/h"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson formula is throughput = 3600 / cycle time, so 3600 / 0.25 = 14400 placements/h. Multiplying by 0.25 gives 900, which confuses seconds per placement with placements per second. Dividing by 0.25*60 gives 240, which incorrectly converts to minutes before dividing.","source":"src-011","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m05l02-01","lessonId":"m05l02","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"prompt":"For a brittle SMT component, which approach best selects nozzle, pick height, and placement force within mechanical limits?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Use the largest nozzle available and maximum placement force to ensure the component is picked reliably."},{"id":"b","text":"Select a nozzle that contacts the component's intended pick surface, set pick height to clear the component height plus clearance, and keep placement force below the component's mechanical limit."},{"id":"c","text":"Set pick height below the component top so the nozzle presses the component into the feeder, and increase force if the pick fails."},{"id":"d","text":"Choose nozzle size only by feeder pitch, because placement force is controlled by the machine and does not need component-specific limits."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The component's mechanical limits govern nozzle contact, pick height, and placement force; the nozzle must pick the intended surface without interference, and force must not exceed the component rating. The largest nozzle and maximum force can damage brittle parts, pressing into the feeder causes mispicks or damage, and feeder pitch alone does not define nozzle or force limits.","source":"src-011","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m05l02-02","lessonId":"m05l02","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula F = P × A, a vacuum nozzle with a rule-of-thumb bore diameter of 1.0 mm is set to a rule-of-thumb air pressure of 0.3 MPa. A rule-of-thumb placement-force limit for the component is 0.5 N. What is the approximate placement force, and is it within the limit?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.24 N, within the rule-of-thumb limit."},{"id":"b","text":"0.30 N, within the rule-of-thumb limit."},{"id":"c","text":"0.50 N, within the rule-of-thumb limit."},{"id":"d","text":"0.94 N, exceeds the rule-of-thumb limit."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Using pi*d^2/4, area = pi*1.0^2/4 = 0.785 mm^2, so F = 0.3*0.785 = 0.236 N, about 0.24 N, which is below the rule-of-thumb limit. Option b uses the bore diameter as if it were area, option c reports the limit rather than the calculated force, and option d omits the divide-by-four in the area formula.","source":"src-011","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m05l03-01","lessonId":"m05l03","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"prompt":"During first-article vision verification, every component on the panel shows the same X and Y offset relative to its programmed location, while fiducials are recognized and no component rotation is observed. Which mechanism best explains the measured data?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Board recognition or origin offset applied to the whole panel"},{"id":"b","text":"Expired camera-to-nozzle calibration causing random per-component errors"},{"id":"c","text":"Wrong-part detection due to incorrect feeder mapping"},{"id":"d","text":"Polarity reversal caused by reversed tape direction"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A uniform vector offset across all components indicates the board coordinate origin or fiducial-based board recognition is shifted, not individual placement errors. Expired calibration would typically produce inconsistent or systematic camera/nozzle errors rather than a single panel-wide vector. Wrong-part and polarity defects are detected by component shape or orientation, not by a common positional offset.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m05l03-02","lessonId":"m05l03","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"prompt":"During setup verification, the measured fiducial vector has equal transverse and longitudinal components relative to the nominal vector. Using the lesson formula for skew or rotation, what is the skew angle?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0 degrees"},{"id":"b","text":"45 degrees"},{"id":"c","text":"90 degrees"},{"id":"d","text":"180 degrees"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson formula is skew = atan(transverse component / longitudinal component). With equal components, the ratio is one, so skew = atan(1) = 45 degrees. A zero angle would require no transverse component, 90 degrees would require no longitudinal component, and 180 degrees would indicate reversal along the axis.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m05l04-01","lessonId":"m05l04","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K6"],"prompt":"A measured placement report shows the component center is displaced from the nominal center, but the printed paste deposits are centered and the reflow profile is within limits. Which evidence sequence best attributes the defect to placement rather than to printing or reflow?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Paste deposits are centered, reflow is nominal, and the component center is displaced in the same direction across multiple boards."},{"id":"b","text":"Paste deposits are shifted in the same direction as the component, and reflow shows tombstoning."},{"id":"c","text":"The component is absent from the board and the feeder is empty."},{"id":"d","text":"The component rotates after reflow and the paste volume is low."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson evidence sequence attributes a defect to placement when printing is centered and reflow is nominal while measured component center data show a repeatable offset. Option b points to printing because the paste is shifted, and tombstoning points to reflow. Option c is a missing-part or feeder issue, not a placement offset mechanism. Option d points to reflow because the rotation appears after reflow with low paste.","source":"src-011","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m05l04-02","lessonId":"m05l04","type":"calc","competencies":["K4","K6"],"prompt":"A placement camera measures the component center offset as equal X and Y offsets, each equal to a pad pitch. Using the lesson formula for offset magnitude, what is the total offset?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"a pad pitch"},{"id":"b","text":"square root of two pad pitches"},{"id":"c","text":"two pad pitches"},{"id":"d","text":"half a pad pitch"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson formula is offset magnitude = sqrt(dx^2 + dy^2). With dx = a pad pitch and dy = a pad pitch, offset = sqrt((a pad pitch)^2 + (a pad pitch)^2) = sqrt(two) pad pitches. The other options ignore the vector sum: a pad pitch is only one axis, two pad pitches is an arithmetic sum, and half a pad pitch is not a vector magnitude.","source":"src-011","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m05l05-01","lessonId":"m05l05","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"prompt":"Before releasing a placement program for production, what is the correct verification approach?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Verify the program against the BOM and setup, confirm feeder/nozzle/tooling, check calibration status, then perform the first-article check."},{"id":"b","text":"Start production and use the first board as the first-article check, correcting program errors after the fact."},{"id":"c","text":"Only verify the program if the placement machine reports a feeder error; calibration is handled separately by maintenance."},{"id":"d","text":"Accept the program if the file opens and the component count matches; calibration records are not part of setup verification."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Program verification must confirm the placement program against the BOM and the physical setup before production, and the first-article check validates the result. A missing or expired calibration invalidates the setup because the machine cannot be trusted to place or inspect correctly. Starting production first, relying only on machine alarms, or accepting a file that opens are all incomplete verification.","source":"src-011","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m05l05-02","lessonId":"m05l05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula, blockers = (required components − verified components) + expired calibrations. A rule of thumb is to stop release if blockers are greater than zero. A job has 120 required components, 114 verified components, and one expired calibration. How many blockers are present?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"7"},{"id":"b","text":"6"},{"id":"c","text":"1"},{"id":"d","text":"0"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Using the formula, 120 − 114 = 6 missing components, and 6 + 1 expired calibration = 7 blockers. The rule of thumb is to stop release when blockers are greater than zero. Option b ignores the expired calibration, option c ignores the missing components, and option d ignores both.","source":"src-011","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m05l06-01","lessonId":"m05l06","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K6"],"prompt":"A fine-pitch QFP shows a consistent X/Y offset and a small rotation in post-placement inspection, while pre-placement solder paste inspection shows paste apertures centered and paste volume within limits. After reflow, the same QFP shows lifted leads on one side. Which evidence sequence best attributes the defect to placement rather than to printing or reflow?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Solder paste inspection is good, post-placement inspection shows the same offset and rotation, and reflow inspection shows the resulting lifted leads; the defect appeared after placement."},{"id":"b","text":"Solder paste inspection shows paste offset, post-placement inspection shows good placement, and reflow inspection shows lifted leads; the defect appeared after reflow."},{"id":"c","text":"Solder paste inspection is good, post-placement inspection is good, and reflow inspection shows lifted leads; the defect appeared after placement."},{"id":"d","text":"Solder paste inspection is good, post-placement inspection shows offset, and reflow inspection shows a missing part; the defect appeared after reflow."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The evidence sequence is good solder paste inspection, bad post-placement inspection, then bad reflow inspection, so the defect appeared after placement. If solder paste inspection were bad, printing would be implicated; if post-placement inspection were good, reflow would be implicated; a missing part is a different defect mechanism.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m05l06-02","lessonId":"m05l06","type":"calc","competencies":["K4","K6"],"prompt":"The lesson defines QFP skew as half the difference between the average Y offset of the left-side leads and the average Y offset of the right-side leads. If the left-side average Y offset is +A and the right-side average Y offset is -A, what is the skew?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A"},{"id":"b","text":"the full difference"},{"id":"c","text":"zero"},{"id":"d","text":"half of A"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson's skew definition takes the difference between the left and right average Y offsets and then takes half of that difference. With +A and -A, the difference is A plus A, and half of that is A. The full-difference option omits the half factor, zero would require equal same-sign offsets, and half of A would use only one side's offset.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m07l01-01","lessonId":"m07l01","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"A fine-pitch QFP has suspected solder bridging under the package body, and the area is not visible from the top. Which inspection method is most appropriate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"AOI"},{"id":"b","text":"2D X-ray"},{"id":"c","text":"Laminar/3D X-ray"},{"id":"d","text":"Boundary scan"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Laminar/3D X-ray is best for hidden solder defects under a package because it provides depth information to distinguish bridging from overlapping features. AOI and visual inspection cannot see under the package body. 2D X-ray may reveal some hidden solder features but can lack the depth resolution needed for under-body bridging. Boundary scan tests electrical connectivity, not the physical solder joint shape.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m07l01-02","lessonId":"m07l01","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"Using a rule-of-thumb planning example, a mixed SMT/THT board has 120 defects to be assigned to exactly one primary inspection method: AOI for visible surface defects, X-ray for hidden solder defects, and boundary scan for electrical net defects. AOI is assigned 70 defects and boundary scan is assigned 25 defects. How many defects must be assigned to X-ray?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"25"},{"id":"b","text":"45"},{"id":"c","text":"70"},{"id":"d","text":"120"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Using the rule-of-thumb partition formula, X-ray defects = total defects - AOI defects - boundary scan defects. Arithmetic: 120 - 70 - 25 = 45. AOI covers the visible defects and boundary scan covers the electrical defects, so the remaining hidden solder defects require X-ray.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m07l02-01","lessonId":"m07l02","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"A hidden solder joint under a BGA package must be inspected for opens and excessive voiding, and the board has no test access. Which inspection method is most appropriate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"2D X-ray"},{"id":"b","text":"AOI"},{"id":"c","text":"Visual inspection"},{"id":"d","text":"Boundary scan"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"2D X-ray is appropriate because it can inspect hidden solder joints under a BGA package and detect opens or excessive voiding. AOI and visual inspection cannot see under the package, and boundary scan requires test access and mainly detects electrical faults rather than voiding.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m07l02-02","lessonId":"m07l02","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"Using a rule-of-thumb audit sample of 100 boards, the audit found 20 true defects, 5 escapes, 15 false calls, and 20 verified calls. Which set of rates is correct?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Escape rate 25%, false-call rate 50%, verification coverage 66.7%"},{"id":"b","text":"Escape rate 5%, false-call rate 15%, verification coverage 20%"},{"id":"c","text":"Escape rate 25%, false-call rate 75%, verification coverage 66.7%"},{"id":"d","text":"Escape rate 20%, false-call rate 50%, verification coverage 80%"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Detected true calls are 20 true defects minus 5 escapes, giving 15 detected true calls. Total calls are 15 detected true calls plus 15 false calls, giving 30 calls, so escape rate is 5/20 = 25%, false-call rate is 15/30 = 50%, and verification coverage is 20/30 = 66.7%. Option b incorrectly treats raw counts as percentages, option c incorrectly divides false calls by true defects, and option d uses incorrect denominators.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m07l03-01","lessonId":"m07l03","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"Which statement best describes a key limit of an AOI escape rate estimated from audit data?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It estimates the share of defects missed by AOI among defects present in the audited population, so it depends on the audit sample and the audit method's ability to detect defects."},{"id":"b","text":"It measures how many good boards AOI incorrectly rejects during production."},{"id":"c","text":"It is equivalent to first-pass yield whenever verification coverage is complete."},{"id":"d","text":"It can be improved simply by increasing false calls, because more calls imply better detection."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Escape rate is a missed-defect metric: it compares escapes to the total defects present in the audited population, so it is limited by sampling and by whether the audit method can detect the same defects. False calls are a separate metric and do not reduce escapes. First-pass yield is a broader production outcome and is not the same as escape rate even with full verification.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m07l03-02","lessonId":"m07l03","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"Using a rule-of-thumb audit sample of 100 boards, AOI generated 40 calls, 10 calls were false, and 30 calls were verified. What is the verification coverage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"25%"},{"id":"b","text":"30%"},{"id":"c","text":"75%"},{"id":"d","text":"95%"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Verification coverage is verified calls divided by total calls, so 30 / 40 = 0.75 = 75%. The 25% option is the false-call rate, 10 / 40 = 25%. The 30% option incorrectly divides verified calls by audited boards, 30 / 100, and the 95% option is a yield-like figure, not verification coverage.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m07l04-01","lessonId":"m07l04","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"A BGA X-ray shows overlapping ball shadows and a suspected bridge between adjacent balls. Which inspection method should be used to determine whether the bridge is a real solder bridge or a projection artifact?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Laminar/3D X-ray"},{"id":"b","text":"AOI"},{"id":"c","text":"Boundary scan"},{"id":"d","text":"2D X-ray"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Laminar/3D X-ray separates features by depth, so it can distinguish a real bridge from overlapping ball shadows. AOI cannot see under the BGA package, boundary scan tests electrical connectivity rather than the physical defect, and 2D X-ray can create projection artifacts when balls overlap.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m07l04-02","lessonId":"m07l04","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"A through-hole joint has solder fill height equal to two-thirds of the hole depth. Using the lesson formula fill fraction = solder height / hole depth and the IPC-A-610 Class 3 minimum fill criterion, what is the disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept: fill is 66.7%, which meets IPC-A-610 Class 3 minimum fill."},{"id":"b","text":"Reject: fill is 66.7%, which is below IPC-A-610 Class 3 minimum fill."},{"id":"c","text":"Rework: fill is 33.3%, which is below IPC-A-610 Class 3 minimum fill."},{"id":"d","text":"Accept: fill is 150%, which is above IPC-A-610 Class 3 minimum fill."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Fill fraction = solder height / hole depth = two-thirds = 0.667, so fill = 0.667 × 100 = 66.7%. IPC-A-610 Class 3 requires at least 75% fill, so the joint is reject. The 33.3% and 150% options invert the ratio, and the accept option ignores the Class 3 minimum.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m07l05-01","lessonId":"m07l05","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"An inspector observes a solder joint with insufficient wetting on a Class 2 assembly. The joint is electrically functional, but the condition may lead to a defect. Which disposition statement best follows the lesson's workmanship vocabulary?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Class 2 condition: insufficient wetting at U12 pin 4."},{"id":"b","text":"Class 3 defect: insufficient wetting at U12 pin 4."},{"id":"c","text":"Defect: bad solder joint."},{"id":"d","text":"Condition: solder looks dull."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A condition, or process indicator, is a workmanship finding that may lead to a defect but is not itself a defect, and the class must match the product class. Option b assigns the wrong class and the wrong category. Option c lacks class and uses vague language, while option d lacks class and does not identify the specific finding.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m07l05-02","lessonId":"m07l05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula Defect Rate = Defects / Inspected Units × 100, and the rule of thumb that a lot is dispositioned as a defect only when the defect rate exceeds the IPC-A-610 class number percent, a Class 3 lot has defects equal to the class number and inspected units equal to one hundred. Which disposition statement is correct?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Class 3: defect rate is 3 percent; not a lot defect under the rule of thumb."},{"id":"b","text":"Class 3: defect rate is 30 percent; lot defect."},{"id":"c","text":"Class 2: defect rate is 0.3 percent; not a lot defect."},{"id":"d","text":"Class 3: defect rate is 300 percent; lot defect."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The IPC-A-610 Class 3 number is 3, so defects = 3 and inspected units = 100. Applying the formula gives 3 / 100 × 100 = 3 percent, which does not exceed the Class 3 rule-of-thumb threshold of 3 percent. Option b would require 30 defects per 100 units, option c would require 0.3 defects per 100 units, and option d would require 300 defects per 100 units.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m07l06-01","lessonId":"m07l06","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"A stencil print image shows a pad with insufficient solder paste coverage, but there is no bridging to adjacent pads. Using IPC-A-610 workmanship vocabulary, what defect type and disposition should be recorded?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Insufficient solder paste; accept because the absence of bridging satisfies the print criterion."},{"id":"b","text":"Insufficient solder paste; rework by cleaning and reprinting because paste coverage does not meet the IPC-A-610 print criterion."},{"id":"c","text":"Solder paste bridging; reject because any paste defect is nonconforming."},{"id":"d","text":"Component misalignment; rework because the pad is not fully covered."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The finding is insufficient solder paste coverage, not bridging or misalignment. IPC-A-610 requires adequate paste coverage to form an acceptable solder joint, so the board should be reworked by cleaning and reprinting before reflow. Accepting it would risk insufficient solder after reflow, and calling it bridging or misalignment misstates the defect type.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m07l06-02","lessonId":"m07l06","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"A THT through-hole joint is inspected and the solder fills the hole to exactly the IPC-A-610 minimum hole-fill fraction. Using fill percentage = fill fraction x 100 and disposition = accept when fill percentage is at least the IPC-A-610 minimum, what disposition is supported if all other workmanship criteria are met?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept, because the calculated fill percentage meets the IPC-A-610 minimum."},{"id":"b","text":"Rework, because meeting the minimum still requires additional solder to improve wetting."},{"id":"c","text":"Reject, because any hole-fill calculation requires a safety margin above the minimum."},{"id":"d","text":"Indeterminate, because the fill fraction cannot be converted to a percentage."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"IPC-A-610 minimum hole fill is three-quarters of the hole depth, so fill percentage = 3/4 x 100 = 75%. Since 75% is at least the 75% minimum, the disposition is accept when all other criteria are met. Rework or reject would require fill below the minimum or another nonconformance, and the conversion is defined by the formula.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m08l01-01","lessonId":"m08l01","type":"single","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"Why is the plated-hole diameter specified larger than the lead diameter in a through-hole joint?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"To provide clearance for lead insertion, solder flow, and reliable wetting without forcing the lead into the hole."},{"id":"b","text":"To increase mechanical load by making the hole larger than the lead."},{"id":"c","text":"To reduce the amount of solder needed by eliminating capillary action."},{"id":"d","text":"To make the plated hole electrically isolated from the lead."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The extra diameter is clearance, allowing the lead to enter and solder to wet the hole wall and annular ring. A larger hole does not by itself increase mechanical load; load depends on joint geometry and wetted area. Solder fill relies on capillary action, so eliminating it would harm the joint. The plated hole is intended to be electrically connected to the lead, not isolated.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m08l01-02","lessonId":"m08l01","type":"calc","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"Using a rule-of-thumb diametral clearance of 0.30 mm between lead and plated hole and the IPC-A-610 minimum vertical barrel fill, a rule-of-thumb lead diameter of 0.50 mm is used. What plated-hole diameter and expected solder fill should be specified?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.80 mm plated-hole diameter and at least 75% vertical barrel fill"},{"id":"b","text":"0.80 mm plated-hole diameter and 100% expected solder fill"},{"id":"c","text":"0.20 mm plated-hole diameter and at least 75% fill"},{"id":"d","text":"0.50 mm plated-hole diameter and at least 75% fill"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Plated-hole diameter = lead diameter + diametral clearance = 0.50 mm + 0.30 mm = 0.80 mm. IPC-A-610 sets a minimum vertical barrel fill of 75% for both Class 2 and Class 3, with Class 3 additionally requiring visible fillets on both sides; 100% fill is a customer requirement, not an IPC-A-610 class limit. The 0.30 mm clearance is a design rule of thumb rather than an IPC-2221 requirement, so verify it against your drill, plating and lead tolerances.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m08l02-01","lessonId":"m08l02","type":"single","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"Which tolerance stack-up most directly controls whether a lead can be inserted into a plated through-hole?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Minimum plated-hole diameter minus maximum lead diameter"},{"id":"b","text":"Nominal plated-hole diameter minus nominal lead diameter"},{"id":"c","text":"Maximum plated-hole diameter minus minimum lead diameter"},{"id":"d","text":"Placement tolerance only"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Insertion requires the worst-case diametral clearance, so the smallest plated hole must still accept the largest lead. Nominal values ignore tolerance stack-up, and maximum hole minus minimum lead gives only best-case clearance. Placement tolerance affects alignment, not the physical diameter mismatch.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m08l02-02","lessonId":"m08l02","type":"calc","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"IPC-A-610 Class 2 requires a minimum hole fill of 75%. If the lead diameter is one-half the finished plated-hole diameter, what is the expected annular solder fill using the lesson formula?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"75%"},{"id":"b","text":"50%"},{"id":"c","text":"25%"},{"id":"d","text":"100%"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The annular fill formula is fill = (1 - (lead/hole)^2) × 100. With lead/hole = 1/2, fill = (1 - 1/4) × 100 = 75%. The 50% choice uses linear clearance instead of area, 25% is the lead-area fraction, and 100% ignores the lead.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m08l03-01","lessonId":"m08l03","type":"single","competencies":["K7","K6"],"prompt":"A board image shows a row of un-soldered pads on the trailing side of a tall connector after wave soldering. Which insertion/layout change most directly limits this shadowing defect?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Rotate the board direction and adjust clinch/masking so the connector does not shadow the pads."},{"id":"b","text":"Increase seating force to eliminate stand-off and pull the connector lower."},{"id":"c","text":"Mask the pads to keep solder off the connector body."},{"id":"d","text":"Increase clinch length to raise the connector above the wave."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The un-soldered pads are a shadowing defect: the tall connector blocks wave solder from reaching the trailing pads. Rotating the board and adjusting clinch/masking changes the shadow path and exposes the pads to the wave. Increasing seating force or clinch length does not address the wave shadow and can cause insertion damage, while masking the pads prevents soldering.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m08l03-02","lessonId":"m08l03","type":"calc","competencies":["K7","K6"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula clinch length = board thickness + clinch allowance, and a rule-of-thumb clinch allowance of 0.8 mm, what clinch length should be set for a standard 1.6 mm thick board?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.8 mm"},{"id":"b","text":"1.6 mm"},{"id":"c","text":"2.4 mm"},{"id":"d","text":"3.2 mm"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The formula gives 1.6 mm + 0.8 mm = 2.4 mm. 0.8 mm is only the clinch allowance, 1.6 mm ignores the allowance, and 3.2 mm doubles the board thickness rather than adding the allowance.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m08l04-01","lessonId":"m08l04","type":"single","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"A wave-soldered layout has a tall component upstream of a row of pads when the board is placed on the conveyor. Which preparation best limits shadowing at the wave?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Rotate the board so the tall component is downstream of the pads, use the lowest practical clinch height, and mask or tape non-solder areas while leaving the pads exposed."},{"id":"b","text":"Keep the tall component upstream of the pads, raise the clinch height, and mask the pads to force solder around the component."},{"id":"c","text":"Keep the tall component upstream of the pads, increase wave height, and tape over the pads to reduce turbulence."},{"id":"d","text":"Rotate the board so the tall component is upstream of the pads, use the highest practical clinch height, and mask the entire board."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Shadowing is caused by a tall component blocking the wave from reaching pads behind it, so the board should be oriented with the tall component downstream at the trailing edge and the pads exposed. The clinch should be kept low so the component does not add unnecessary height, and masking or taping should protect non-solder areas while leaving the pads open. Masking or taping the pads prevents solder wetting, and increasing wave height is not a layout control and can cause bridging or damage.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m08l04-02","lessonId":"m08l04","type":"calc","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's rule-of-thumb shadowing score formula, score = (component height + clinch height) / stand-off clearance, and rule-of-thumb layout values, orientation A has upstream component height 10 mm, clinch height 2 mm, and stand-off clearance 4 mm; orientation B has upstream component height 6 mm, clinch height 2 mm, and stand-off clearance 4 mm. What is the score difference between A and B?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"1"},{"id":"b","text":"2"},{"id":"c","text":"3"},{"id":"d","text":"4"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Orientation A scores (10 + 2) / 4 = 12 / 4 = 3, and orientation B scores (6 + 2) / 4 = 8 / 4 = 2, so the difference is 3 - 2 = 1. The option 2 is orientation B's score, option 3 is orientation A's score, and option 4 is the raw height difference rather than the normalized score difference.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m08l05-01","lessonId":"m08l05","type":"single","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"For a through-hole joint, why is clearance alone insufficient to estimate expected solder fill?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Expected fill depends on the area difference between the plated hole and the lead, so the same clearance can produce different fill when the hole and lead diameters differ."},{"id":"b","text":"Clearance is the only geometric variable that controls solder fill, so expected fill can be read directly from the diameter difference."},{"id":"c","text":"Expected fill is determined only by the lead diameter, because the plated-hole diameter is fixed by the drill size."},{"id":"d","text":"Expected fill is determined only by the plated-hole diameter, because the lead diameter does not affect the annular solder area."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson formula uses hole area minus lead area, so fill is an area ratio rather than a linear clearance. Two joints can have the same clearance but different fill because the squared diameter terms change the annular area. The other options incorrectly reduce fill to a single diameter or to linear clearance.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m08l05-02","lessonId":"m08l05","type":"calc","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"A through-hole joint has a lead diameter equal to three-quarters of the plated-hole diameter. The annular cross-section available to solder is the hole area minus the lead area. What fraction of the hole cross-section can solder occupy, and how should that fraction be used?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"43.75%; it is the annular capacity, a geometric property of the hole and lead — it is not an inspection result and is not compared with the IPC-A-610 vertical fill minimum."},{"id":"b","text":"43.75%; it is the expected vertical barrel fill, so it must be compared with the IPC-A-610 75% minimum and the joint rejected."},{"id":"c","text":"56.25%; it is the expected vertical barrel fill, so it must be compared with the IPC-A-610 75% minimum and the joint rejected."},{"id":"d","text":"75.00%; it is the expected vertical barrel fill, so it meets the IPC-A-610 minimum and the joint is accepted."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The annulus occupies 1 − (0.75)² = 1 − 0.5625 = 0.4375, so solder can occupy 43.75% of the hole cross-section. That number is a geometric capacity: it depends only on the hole and lead diameters and says nothing about how far up the barrel the solder actually climbed. IPC-A-610 judges vertical barrel fill — fill height against board or barrel height — so the two are never compared. Option b makes exactly that category error, option c takes the lead’s own area fraction and then makes it too, and option d is the coincidence of choosing the diameters so the area fraction equals the acceptance limit. A 100% requirement is contractual, not an IPC-A-610 class limit.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m09l03-01","lessonId":"m09l03","type":"single","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"During wave soldering preheat, a board shows bottom temperature near flux activation while top temperature remains well below the component thermal limit. What is the best interpretation?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Preheat is adequate for flux activation while preserving top-side thermal margin; maintain settings if flux type, amount, and fog/wave height are appropriate."},{"id":"b","text":"Increase preheat until top temperature equals bottom temperature to ensure uniform flux activation."},{"id":"c","text":"Reduce preheat because top-side components are cold and flux will not activate."},{"id":"d","text":"Increase fog/wave height to compensate for the low top temperature."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Bottom temperature is the primary indicator for flux activation, while top temperature margin protects components; if the flux type, amount, and fog/wave height are appropriate, the settings can be maintained. Forcing top temperature to match bottom temperature can exceed thermal limits, and fog/wave height affects soldering rather than preheat activation.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m09l03-02","lessonId":"m09l03","type":"calc","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"The lesson formula for preheat rise is ΔT = T_target - T_inlet. Using rule-of-thumb values T_inlet = 25°C, T_target = 100°C, and board thermal limit = 125°C, what is the required preheat rise and remaining margin to the limit?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"75°C rise and 25°C margin"},{"id":"b","text":"100°C rise and 25°C margin"},{"id":"c","text":"75°C rise and 125°C margin"},{"id":"d","text":"125°C rise and 0°C margin"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Using the lesson formula ΔT = T_target - T_inlet, the rise is 100°C - 25°C = 75°C. The remaining margin is 125°C - 100°C = 25°C. The distractors confuse the target temperature with the rise or compare the limit with the inlet instead of the target.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m10l01-01","lessonId":"m10l01","type":"single","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"A mixed assembly has a fine-pitch QFN with heat-sensitive neighbours nearby and a large through-hole power connector with high thermal mass. Which nozzle and flux strategy best defends the joint geometry?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Selective soldering with a small nozzle and localized no-clean flux on the fine-pitch joints, then a larger nozzle and longer dwell for the through-hole connector."},{"id":"b","text":"Wave soldering with high flux density because it gives the highest throughput for all joints."},{"id":"c","text":"Selective soldering with a large nozzle and aggressive water-soluble flux across the whole board."},{"id":"d","text":"Hand soldering each joint because hand tools can adapt to mixed geometry."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The small nozzle and localized flux limit heat and flux spread to the fine-pitch joints and protect heat-sensitive neighbours, while the larger nozzle and longer dwell supply enough heat to the high-thermal-mass through-hole connector. Wave soldering exposes the neighbours to board-wide heat, a large nozzle floods fine-pitch joints, and hand soldering is not a repeatable process strategy.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m10l01-02","lessonId":"m10l01","type":"calc","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's selective-soldering time formula, T = N × t_nozzle + t_flux, and the lesson's rule of thumb that a representative joint group has four joints, a nozzle forms one joint per second, and a flux application adds one second per joint group, what is the selective-soldering time for that joint group?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"5 seconds"},{"id":"b","text":"4 seconds"},{"id":"c","text":"1 second"},{"id":"d","text":"8 seconds"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Apply the formula: T = 4 × 1 + 1 = 5 seconds. The 4-second option omits the flux application, the 1-second option omits the nozzle time, and the 8-second option doubles the nozzle time without a rule-of-thumb basis.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m10l02-01","lessonId":"m10l02","type":"single","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"For a joint group of closely spaced fine-pitch THT pins adjacent to SMD pads, which nozzle and flux strategy is most defensible?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Use a small nozzle with a narrow flux pattern and flux-on-the-fly, applying flux only at each target joint."},{"id":"b","text":"Use a large nozzle with a broad flux pattern and pre-flux the whole area to reduce program time."},{"id":"c","text":"Use a wide nozzle with high flux volume to ensure wetting, relying on solder mask to block excess flux."},{"id":"d","text":"Use the same nozzle and fixed dwell for all joints to simplify the selective program."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A small nozzle with a narrow flux pattern confines flux to the intended joints, and flux-on-the-fly prevents flux from spreading to nearby SMD pads. Broad pre-flux or high-volume wide-nozzle strategies increase the risk of bridging, contamination, and poor solderability. Fixed dwell and a single nozzle ignore the geometry-driven need for selective flux placement.","source":"src-014","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m10l02-02","lessonId":"m10l02","type":"calc","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"A selective solder program uses a rule of thumb of one second of dwell per joint per pass. A joint group contains four joints and requires two passes. What is the total dwell time for the group?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"2 s"},{"id":"b","text":"4 s"},{"id":"c","text":"8 s"},{"id":"d","text":"16 s"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Total dwell time is calculated as joints multiplied by dwell per joint per pass multiplied by passes: 4 joints × 1 s/joint/pass × 2 passes = 8 s. The 4 s answer ignores the second pass, the 2 s answer ignores both joints and passes, and the 16 s answer double-counts the pass multiplier.","source":"src-014","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m10l03-01","lessonId":"m10l03","type":"single","competencies":["K7","K5"],"prompt":"When estimating the thermal budget of a neighbouring part, which approach best supports specifying mitigation and verification?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Combine the worst-case heat load, thermal path resistance, and duty cycle, then verify the predicted rise with temperature monitoring."},{"id":"b","text":"Use only the average power of the source because nearby parts respond to long-term averages."},{"id":"c","text":"Assume the neighbouring part is isothermal with the room because air dominates heat spread."},{"id":"d","text":"Select a heat sink after the first temperature excursion because monitoring is the primary mitigation."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The correct approach links heat load, thermal resistance/path, and duty cycle to estimate exposure, then uses monitoring to verify the predicted margin. Average power can miss peak thermal stress, assuming room temperature ignores conduction and radiation paths, and monitoring detects excursions but does not itself mitigate heat spread.","source":"src-008","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m10l03-02","lessonId":"m10l03","type":"calc","competencies":["K7","K5"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula R_th,max = ΔT_allowable / P, and rule-of-thumb values ΔT_allowable = 20 K and P = 4 W, what is the maximum thermal resistance from the heat source to the sensitive neighbouring part?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"5 K/W"},{"id":"b","text":"80 K/W"},{"id":"c","text":"0.2 K/W"},{"id":"d","text":"24 K/W"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Using the stated rule-of-thumb values, R_th,max = 20 K / 4 W = 5 K/W. The 80 K/W option multiplies instead of divides, the 0.2 K/W option inverts the division, and the 24 K/W option adds the values instead of applying the formula.","source":"src-008","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m10l04-01","lessonId":"m10l04","type":"single","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"When building a selective program for a mixed board, why should the path and order be chosen before setting dwell per joint and passes?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"To keep the air knife and fixture clear of obstructions and minimize cycle time while meeting joint quality."},{"id":"b","text":"Because dwell per joint is fixed by the board file and cannot be changed."},{"id":"c","text":"Because the air knife must be programmed after cycle time is known."},{"id":"d","text":"Because fixtures determine the number of passes independently of joint order."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Path and order determine access, simultaneous joints, and air-knife exposure, so dwell and passes are tuned after the route is feasible. Dwell per joint is not fixed by the board file, cycle time is an outcome of the program rather than a prerequisite for air-knife programming, and fixtures influence access but do not set passes independently of order.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m10l04-02","lessonId":"m10l04","type":"calc","competencies":["K7"],"prompt":"Using the lesson cycle-time formula, cycle time = (joints × dwell per joint × passes) ÷ simultaneous joints, and the rule-of-thumb values of a pass per joint and a dwell unit per joint, what is the cycle time for a board with a joint per pad when the air knife processes a pair of joints at once?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The joint count divided by the simultaneous pair, in dwell units."},{"id":"b","text":"The joint count, in dwell units."},{"id":"c","text":"The joint count multiplied by the simultaneous pair, in dwell units."},{"id":"d","text":"The joint count divided by the pad count, in dwell units."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Substitute the rule-of-thumb values into the formula: (joints × dwell unit × pass) ÷ pair = joints ÷ pair, so the cycle time is the joint count divided by the simultaneous pair in dwell units. The other options ignore simultaneous processing, multiply by the pair instead of dividing, or divide by pad count rather than simultaneous joints.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m10l05-01","lessonId":"m10l05","type":"single","competencies":["K7","K5"],"prompt":"When building a selective soldering program for a board with mixed SMT and THT joints, which sequence best controls heat exposure to nearby SMT components?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Approach from the side with the greatest clearance, solder the most heat-sensitive joints first with minimal dwell, then use additional passes only where needed, and verify with thermocouples or infrared imaging."},{"id":"b","text":"Start at the center of the board and use maximum dwell on all joints to ensure wetting, then inspect visually."},{"id":"c","text":"Use a long dwell pass over all joints to reduce cycle time, since neighboring parts are unaffected if the nozzle is small."},{"id":"d","text":"Program the path by component height only, because dwell and passes do not affect heat exposure."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Selective programs should minimize heat input by ordering joints, using short dwell, and adding passes only where needed; verification by thermocouple or infrared imaging confirms neighboring part exposure. Maximum dwell or a long pass increases heat exposure, and path by height ignores thermal effects.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m10l05-02","lessonId":"m10l05","type":"calc","competencies":["K7","K5"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula that heat exposure scales with dwell time and passes, and the rule of thumb that a heat sink halves the remaining exposure, if a mitigation halves dwell time and a heat sink halves the remaining exposure, what is the final exposure relative to baseline?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"one quarter of baseline"},{"id":"b","text":"one half of baseline"},{"id":"c","text":"three quarters of baseline"},{"id":"d","text":"one eighth of baseline"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson formula multiplies exposure factors, so halving dwell gives one half and halving again gives one quarter. One half ignores the heat sink; three quarters subtracts the reductions instead of multiplying them; one eighth applies an extra halving.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m11l01-01","lessonId":"m11l01","type":"single","competencies":["K8"],"prompt":"A through-hole component lead is soldered to a large ground plane that acts as a heat sink. Which selection best controls the thermal budget while still achieving wetting?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Use a larger, higher-thermal-mass tip and a temperature appropriate for the solder, then remove the iron as soon as the joint wets."},{"id":"b","text":"Use a small precision tip and lower the iron temperature to minimize heat, even if contact time must be extended."},{"id":"c","text":"Increase the iron temperature and keep the tip on the joint until the flux fully evaporates."},{"id":"d","text":"Rely on fume extraction and ESD control to reduce the heat delivered to the joint."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A heat-sinking plane requires a tip that can deliver enough heat quickly; a larger tip reduces the time needed to reach wetting, limiting thermal budget. A small tip cools and forces longer contact, increasing heat exposure. Higher temperature plus prolonged contact increases thermal budget and can damage the joint or board. Fume extraction and ESD control protect the operator and devices but do not reduce heat input.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m11l01-02","lessonId":"m11l01","type":"calc","competencies":["K8"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula thermal budget = (tip temperature − ambient temperature) × contact time, and the rule-of-thumb values taught for a lead-free through-hole joint (tip temperature 350°C, ambient temperature 25°C, contact time 3 s), what is the thermal budget?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"975 °C·s"},{"id":"b","text":"1050 °C·s"},{"id":"c","text":"1125 °C·s"},{"id":"d","text":"325 °C·s"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Thermal budget = (350 − 25) × 3 = 325 × 3 = 975 °C·s. Option b multiplies tip temperature by time and ignores ambient temperature. Option c adds ambient temperature instead of subtracting it. Option d gives only the temperature difference and omits contact time.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m11l02-01","lessonId":"m11l02","type":"single","competencies":["K8"],"prompt":"For a lead-free through-hole joint, which hand-soldering sequence best controls the thermal budget while avoiding technique defects?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Place the tip on the pad and lead, feed solder to the joint, remove solder first, then remove the tip."},{"id":"b","text":"Feed solder to the tip, then wipe it onto the joint, and hold the tip until the joint looks shiny."},{"id":"c","text":"Use a high tip temperature and a long dwell so the solder flows without preheating the joint."},{"id":"d","text":"Touch the tip to the solder wire first, then drag the molten solder onto the joint."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Correct sequence heats the joint, feeds solder to the joint, and removes solder before the tip to avoid icicles and poor wetting. Feeding solder to the tip burns flux and creates cold or disturbed joints. High temperature and long dwell exceed the thermal budget and can damage pads or components. Dragging solder from the tip gives insufficient heat transfer and non-wetted joints.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m11l02-02","lessonId":"m11l02","type":"calc","competencies":["K8"],"prompt":"A SAC305 lead-free through-hole joint is hand-soldered on a board preheated to 100 °C. Using the lesson rule-of-thumb margin of about 120 °C above the SAC305 liquidus of 219 °C, which places the setpoint in the 330-350 °C range taught for SAC-type solder on a preheated board, and a dwell of 2 s, what temperature-time thermal budget is applied if thermal budget = tip temperature × dwell time?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"678 °C·s"},{"id":"b","text":"339 °C·s"},{"id":"c","text":"558 °C·s"},{"id":"d","text":"478 °C·s"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Tip temperature = 219 °C + 120 °C = 339 °C, which sits inside the 330-350 °C range taught for SAC-type solder on a preheated board. Thermal budget = 339 °C × 2 s = 678 °C·s. Option b omits the dwell time, option c uses half the margin, and option d subtracts the board preheat, which is already accounted for in the tip setpoint. Note that 217 °C is the SAC305 solidus, not the liquidus.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m11l03-01","lessonId":"m11l03","type":"single","competencies":["K8"],"prompt":"During hot-air removal of a BGA or QFN, why is preheat typically specified before localized hot-air heating is applied?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"To reduce the thermal gradient between the joint and the surrounding board, lowering the risk of board warpage, delamination, or component damage."},{"id":"b","text":"To allow the hot-air peak temperature to exceed the component maximum rating without affecting reliability."},{"id":"c","text":"To replace the need for flux or paste-in-pad material during replacement."},{"id":"d","text":"To cool the joint rapidly so that the time above liquidus is shortened."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Preheat raises the board temperature before localized hot-air heating, reducing thermal gradients and mechanical stress during BGA or QFN removal. It does not permit exceeding component temperature limits, does not replace flux or paste-in-pad requirements, and does not cool the joint; the goal is controlled heating, not rapid cooling.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m11l03-02","lessonId":"m11l03","type":"calc","competencies":["K8"],"prompt":"As a rule of thumb, a BGA replacement consumes two rework cycles, one for removal and one for replacement, and a typical rework allowance for a board is three cycles. If a board is specified with that typical allowance and one BGA replacement is performed, how many rework cycles remain within the thermal budget?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"One cycle"},{"id":"b","text":"Two cycles"},{"id":"c","text":"Three cycles"},{"id":"d","text":"Zero cycles"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Using RemainingCycles = AllowedCycles - UsedCycles, the rule-of-thumb calculation is 3 - 2 = 1. Two cycles would remain only if the replacement alone were counted; three cycles would ignore the consumed removal and replacement cycles; zero cycles would overcount the operation.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m11l04-01","lessonId":"m11l04","type":"single","competencies":["K8","K6"],"prompt":"A Class 3 product has a repaired through-hole joint where the annular ring still meets the IPC-A-600 Class 3 minimum, laminate is not exposed, and no conductor is cut. What is the correct disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Repairable; record the repair basis and residual risk."},{"id":"b","text":"Scrap; any repair automatically invalidates Class 3."},{"id":"c","text":"Accept as Class 2; Class 3 repairs must be downgraded."},{"id":"d","text":"Accept without documentation because the joint meets the minimum."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"IPC-7711/7721 permits repair when the repaired joint meets the required class criteria, and Class 3 requires documented repair basis and residual risk. Scrap is required only when the damage exceeds the class repair limits. There is no automatic downgrade to Class 2, and acceptance without documentation is not acceptable for Class 3.","source":"src-009","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m11l04-02","lessonId":"m11l04","type":"calc","competencies":["K8","K6"],"prompt":"IPC-7711/7721 repair acceptance requires the repaired annular ring to meet the IPC-A-600 Class 3 minimum of 0.05 mm. A repaired through-hole pad has a pad diameter that exceeds the hole diameter by twice this minimum. Using annular ring = (pad diameter - hole diameter)/2, what is the disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Repairable for Class 3; the annular ring equals the minimum."},{"id":"b","text":"Scrap; the annular ring is below the Class 3 minimum."},{"id":"c","text":"Repairable for Class 2 only; Class 3 requires a larger ring."},{"id":"d","text":"Cannot be dispositioned because the hole diameter is not given."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Let the Class 3 minimum annular ring be 0.05 mm. The pad diameter exceeds the hole diameter by 2 × 0.05 mm = 0.10 mm. The annular ring is (0.10 mm)/2 = 0.05 mm, which meets the Class 3 minimum, so the joint is repairable with documentation. This matches the 0.05 mm minimum used in the m11l04 worked example; confirm the value against the controlled revision and the assembly drawing. The hole diameter is not needed separately because it cancels in the formula.","source":"src-009","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m11l05-01","lessonId":"m11l05","type":"single","competencies":["K8","K6"],"prompt":"A reworked solder joint is judged acceptable under IPC-7711/7721 Class 2 criteria. The product specification calls for IPC Class 3. Which disposition is correct?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept the joint because rework is allowed on all IPC classes if visually acceptable."},{"id":"b","text":"Accept the joint because Class 2 criteria are more conservative than Class 3 criteria."},{"id":"c","text":"Reject or scrap the joint unless a formal deviation is approved, because the product requires Class 3 and the rework meets only Class 2."},{"id":"d","text":"Accept the joint because rework resets the joint to Class 1 acceptance."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"IPC-7711/7721 acceptance is class-specific, so a Class 3 product must meet Class 3 rework or repair criteria. Class 2 acceptance is not sufficient unless a formal deviation is approved, which is not stated. Class 1 is less demanding, and rework does not reset the product class.","source":"src-009","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m11l05-02","lessonId":"m11l05","type":"calc","competencies":["K8","K6"],"prompt":"A repair is otherwise repairable, but the residual-risk score must be recorded. The product specification calls for IPC Class 3, and the rework is accepted only to IPC Class 2. Using the lesson's residual-risk formula, residual risk = required class - accepted class, what score should be recorded?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0"},{"id":"b","text":"1"},{"id":"c","text":"2"},{"id":"d","text":"3"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson's residual-risk formula is required class minus accepted class. For a Class 3 product with a Class 2 rework, 3 - 2 = 1. A score of zero would mean the rework meets the required class; two would be a Class 3 requirement versus Class 1; three is the required class alone, not the gap.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m11l06-01","lessonId":"m11l06","type":"single","competencies":["K8","K6"],"prompt":"A joint needs rework on an assembly that must meet IPC-7711/7721 class criteria. Which action best satisfies the lesson's rework and documentation logic?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Perform the rework only if the remaining allowed cycles and thermal budget are sufficient, then record the method, thermal exposure, cycles used, and the IPC-7711/7721 class criteria applied."},{"id":"b","text":"If the joint looks acceptable after rework, the cycle count and thermal budget can be ignored because appearance is the only IPC criterion."},{"id":"c","text":"A repair may be documented as a rework without noting the thermal exposure, provided the operator signs the traveler."},{"id":"d","text":"Any reworked joint automatically meets the product's IPC-7711/7721 class if it passes electrical test."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Correct because IPC-7711/7721 rework acceptance is process-controlled: the remaining cycle count and thermal budget must be checked before rework, and the record must show what was applied and which class criteria were used. Appearance or electrical test alone does not prove the joint meets the required class. A repair is not interchangeable with rework in documentation because the thermal exposure and process history differ.","source":"src-009","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m11l06-02","lessonId":"m11l06","type":"calc","competencies":["K8","K6"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's rule of thumb for IPC-7711/7721 Class 2 (allowed thermal budget = two units; one rework cycle consumes one unit), a Class 2 joint has already consumed one unit. Using the lesson formula remaining cycles = floor((allowed thermal budget - consumed thermal budget) / thermal budget per cycle), how many additional cycles are allowed?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0"},{"id":"b","text":"1"},{"id":"c","text":"2"},{"id":"d","text":"3"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Using the lesson formula remaining cycles = floor((allowed thermal budget - consumed thermal budget) / thermal budget per cycle), the Class 2 rule of thumb gives allowed budget = 2 units and consumed budget = 1 unit, so floor((2 - 1) / 1) = 1. Option a would require consumed budget to equal allowed budget; option c ignores the cycle already consumed; option d ignores the class limit.","source":"src-009","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m12l02-01","lessonId":"m12l02","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"An assembly has water-soluble flux residue, a moisture-sensitive BGA package, and ESD-sensitive components. Which cleaning method and chemistry is most appropriate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Aqueous cleaning with DI water and a compatible saponifier, followed by controlled drying/bake and ESD controls"},{"id":"b","text":"Solvent-only cleaning with a non-polar solvent, because it avoids water and therefore eliminates MSL risk"},{"id":"c","text":"Ultrasonic cleaning with a strong alkaline chemistry and no drying, because ultrasonic energy removes residue without chemistry"},{"id":"d","text":"Semi-aqueous cleaning with a flammable solvent and no ESD controls, because ESD controls are only needed for bare die"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Water-soluble flux residues are best removed with aqueous/DI cleaning plus a compatible saponifier; MSL packages require controlled drying/bake to prevent moisture damage, and ESD-sensitive assemblies require grounded/ionized handling. Solvent-only or non-polar chemistry is less effective for ionic water-soluble residues, and omitting drying or ESD controls creates reliability risks.","source":"src-003","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m12l02-02","lessonId":"m12l02","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"Using the lesson dilution formula, a rule-of-thumb aqueous cleaning sump is maintained at a 5% v/v working concentration. For a rule-of-thumb sump volume of 200 L, how much concentrate is required?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"1 L"},{"id":"b","text":"10 L"},{"id":"c","text":"20 L"},{"id":"d","text":"100 L"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The dilution formula gives concentrate volume = sump volume × working concentration. With a rule-of-thumb 5% v/v concentration, 5% = 0.05, so 200 L × 0.05 = 10 L. The other options correspond to misplacing the decimal or using 0.5%, 10%, or 50%.","source":"src-003","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m12l03-01","lessonId":"m12l03","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K6"],"prompt":"A board passes the ROSE total ionic contamination test, but ion chromatography identifies chloride above the applicable limit. What is the correct disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Rework: the specific ion criterion is failed even though the aggregate ROSE result passes."},{"id":"b","text":"Pass: ROSE is the accepted cleanliness test, so ion chromatography is only diagnostic."},{"id":"c","text":"Pass: visual inspection and particle count are not needed when ROSE passes."},{"id":"d","text":"Rework only if particle count also fails, because particles are the main reliability risk."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"ROSE reports aggregate ionic contamination, while ion chromatography identifies individual ions; if a specific ion exceeds the criterion, the board fails and needs rework. Visual cleanliness and particle count do not override a failed chemical criterion.","source":"src-002","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m12l03-02","lessonId":"m12l03","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K6"],"prompt":"Using IPC-TM-650 2.3.25, a standard 100 cm² test area has an acceptance limit of 1.56 µg NaCl eq/cm² (the same level is about 10.06 µg/in²). If the measured total contamination is 156 µg NaCl eq, what is the disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Pass: 156 µg ÷ 100 cm² = 1.56 µg/cm², which meets the limit."},{"id":"b","text":"Rework: 156 µg ÷ 100 in² = 1.56 µg/in², which exceeds the limit."},{"id":"c","text":"Pass: 156 µg × 100 in² = 15,600 µg/in², which is below the limit."},{"id":"d","text":"Rework: 100 in² ÷ 156 µg = 0.64 in²/µg, which is below the limit."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The cleanliness level is contamination mass divided by test area: 156 µg ÷ 100 cm² = 1.56 µg/cm². The IPC-TM-650 2.3.25 criterion is pass if the level is at or below 1.56 µg/cm², so the board passes. Keep the unit basis straight: 1.56 µg/cm² is the same level as about 10.06 µg/in², so a limit quoted per square inch is roughly 6.45 times larger numerically than the same limit quoted per square centimetre. Multiplying contamination by area or dividing area by contamination uses the wrong formula and wrong units.","source":"src-013","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m12l04-01","lessonId":"m12l04","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K6"],"prompt":"A high-reliability medical device assembly uses fine-pitch components and must survive repeated isopropyl alcohol cleaning and occasional component replacement. Which coating and masking strategy best balances protection and rework?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Use parylene with masked pads and connectors; it provides low-stress, pinhole-free coverage and tolerates cleaning while masked areas remain solderable and reworkable."},{"id":"b","text":"Use thick silicone over the entire board because it is flexible and easy to remove."},{"id":"c","text":"Use UV-cured acrylic over everything because it cures fast and is fully compatible with rework."},{"id":"d","text":"Use urethane over everything because it is chemically resistant and easy to rework."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Parylene is low-stress, pinhole-free, and generally compatible with cleaning, while masking pads and connectors preserves solderability and rework. Thick silicone can be difficult to remove cleanly and may leave residues that interfere with rework or probing. UV-cured acrylic can shadow under components and may be brittle or difficult to rework, and urethane's chemical resistance often comes with harder removal than parylene.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m12l04-02","lessonId":"m12l04","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K6"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's coating-volume formula, wet volume = area × dry film thickness ÷ solids fraction, a representative board area is 0.02 square meters as a rule of thumb. As a rule of thumb, specify a 50 micrometer dry film thickness for acrylic and assume 30 percent solids by volume. What wet volume is required, ignoring transfer losses?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"3.3 milliliters"},{"id":"b","text":"1.0 milliliter"},{"id":"c","text":"10 milliliters"},{"id":"d","text":"33 milliliters"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson formula is wet volume = area × dry film thickness ÷ solids fraction. Using the rule-of-thumb values, dry volume = 0.02 square meters × 50 micrometers = 1 milliliter, and wet volume = 1 milliliter ÷ 0.30 = 3.3 milliliters. Option b omits the solids correction, while c and d result from unit or solids-fraction errors.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m12l05-01","lessonId":"m12l05","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K6"],"prompt":"A mixed assembly contains through-hole connectors that must remain solderable and a high-density BGA area that needs moisture protection. Which application and masking strategy best preserves cleaning, rework, and assembly compatibility?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Use full dip coating with no masking so every surface receives the same protective layer."},{"id":"b","text":"Use selective coating with removable masking on connector and rework areas, then cure only the protected zones."},{"id":"c","text":"Use spray coating over the entire assembly and rely on solvent cleaning to remove coating from connector contacts."},{"id":"d","text":"Use dip coating with permanent masking tape left on connectors to avoid removal during assembly."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Selective coating with removable masking protects only the required zones while leaving connector and rework surfaces accessible for cleaning, soldering, and inspection. Full dip or spray coating can trap residues, block contacts, and make rework difficult, while permanent masking left in place can trap flux residues and interfere with assembly.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m12l05-02","lessonId":"m12l05","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K6"],"prompt":"A conformal coating datasheet gives a rule-of-thumb volume solids fraction of one-half. If the target dry film thickness is D, what wet film thickness should be applied?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"D"},{"id":"b","text":"one-half D"},{"id":"c","text":"two times D"},{"id":"d","text":"four times D"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson formula is dry film thickness equals wet film thickness times volume solids fraction. Rearranging gives wet film thickness equals target dry film thickness divided by solids fraction, so WFT = D ÷ one-half = two times D. The other options multiply by the solids fraction or apply it twice, which would under- or over-estimate the required wet film thickness.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m12l06-01","lessonId":"m12l06","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K6"],"prompt":"A conformal-coated assembly shows fish-eye and dewetting after coating. A cleanliness test shows no ionic contamination, but white residue was previously observed near masked areas. What is the most likely root cause and corrective action?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Ionic flux residue; rework by aqueous cleaning and retest ion concentration."},{"id":"b","text":"Low-surface-energy contamination, such as silicone or oil, from masking or handling; remove the masking source, clean with a compatible solvent, verify surface energy or cleanliness, then recoat."},{"id":"c","text":"Excessive solder mask cure; strip and recoat the board."},{"id":"d","text":"Under-cured conformal coating; bake the assembly longer."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Fish-eye and dewetting are wetting defects usually caused by low-surface-energy contamination, such as silicone or oil, rather than by ionic residue alone. A passing ion test does not rule out nonionic residue, especially white residue left by incompatible masking or cleaning. The corrective action is to remove the masking source, clean with a compatible solvent, verify surface energy or cleanliness, then recoat; options a, c, and d do not address the contamination and wetting mechanism.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m12l06-02","lessonId":"m12l06","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K6"],"prompt":"Using IPC-TM-650 2.3.25 and the traditional ROSE acceptance level of 1.56 µg NaCl eq/cm², and the lesson formula ion concentration = total NaCl-equivalent ion mass / wetted area, a board has a measured ion mass equal to that acceptance level multiplied by the wetted area. What is the calculated ion concentration and the disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"1.56 µg NaCl eq/cm² — meets the traditional ROSE level; record method, area, blank and units, and confirm the process with SIR evidence."},{"id":"b","text":"3.12 µg NaCl eq/cm² — fails, because the area is counted twice."},{"id":"c","text":"0.78 µg NaCl eq/cm² — fails, because the area is divided twice."},{"id":"d","text":"Cannot be dispositioned without the product class."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The concentration is (1.56 µg/cm² × area) / area = 1.56 µg NaCl eq/cm², which meets the traditional ROSE acceptance level. J-STD-001 no longer treats a single ROSE number as a pass/fail criterion for every product: it requires objective evidence, normally SIR data, that the specific process is clean enough for the intended life and environment, and high-reliability customers often work tighter than 1.56 µg/cm². Option b double-counts the area, option c divides by it twice, and option d is wrong because the traditional ROSE level is quoted independently of product class, although a customer or product-specific limit may be tighter.","source":"src-002","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m01l01-01","lessonId":"m01l01","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"prompt":"Which document that must accompany a received lot of components, paste, or bare PCB proves that the supplier verified the lot against the specified material and process requirements?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Certificate of Conformance"},{"id":"b","text":"Packing slip"},{"id":"c","text":"Purchase order"},{"id":"d","text":"Incoming inspection report"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A Certificate of Conformance proves the supplier verified the lot against the specified material and process requirements. A packing slip proves what was shipped, and a purchase order proves the commercial order. An incoming inspection report proves the internal disposition after receipt, not the supplier's original verification.","source":"src-037","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m01l01-02","lessonId":"m01l01","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K9"],"prompt":"A finding is classified as quarantine rather than accept. Using the lesson's rule-of-thumb stage multipliers (incoming = base, assembly = ten times base, field = one hundred times base), if the incoming-stage correction cost is the base cost unit, what is the assembly-stage cost avoided per defect?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"ten base cost units"},{"id":"b","text":"one hundred base cost units"},{"id":"c","text":"the base cost unit"},{"id":"d","text":"one thousand base cost units"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson formula is stage cost equals incoming-stage cost multiplied by the stage multiplier. Quarantine prevents the defect from reaching assembly, so the avoided cost is the base cost unit times the rule-of-thumb assembly multiplier of ten, which equals ten base cost units. The field-stage option is higher, the incoming-stage option is the base, and the remaining option is not the taught multiplier.","source":"src-037","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m13l01-01","lessonId":"m13l01","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"A low-volume prototype has a fine-pitch BGA and a JTAG chain, but many nets lack dedicated test points. Which test strategy best balances fault detection, access, and debug value?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Use flying probe for opens and shorts, boundary scan for scan-capable digital interconnect, and functional test for system behavior."},{"id":"b","text":"Use ICT only, because it is the fastest and most repeatable test level."},{"id":"c","text":"Use boundary scan only, because it can detect all analog, power, and BGA faults."},{"id":"d","text":"Use functional test only, because it verifies the product as it will be used."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Flying probe is useful when a bed-of-nails fixture is impractical, boundary scan adds coverage for scan-capable digital interconnect, and functional test verifies system behavior. ICT is less suitable here because many nets lack test points. Boundary scan cannot cover all analog, power, or non-scan faults, and functional test alone gives poor fault localization and can miss latent defects.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m13l01-02","lessonId":"m13l01","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"A rule of thumb for a mixed assembly is that 50% of defects are assembly defects, 30% are digital logic defects, and 20% are system-level defects. Rule-of-thumb coverage is 80% for ICT on assembly defects, 60% for boundary scan on digital logic defects, and 90% for functional test on system-level defects. If the strategy uses ICT for assembly defects, boundary scan for digital logic defects, and functional test for system-level defects, what is the expected overall defect coverage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"76%"},{"id":"b","text":"80%"},{"id":"c","text":"70%"},{"id":"d","text":"90%"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Weighted coverage is 0.50 × 0.80 + 0.30 × 0.60 + 0.20 × 0.90 = 0.40 + 0.18 + 0.18 = 0.76, or 76%. The 80% answer incorrectly applies ICT coverage to all defect classes. The 70% answer ignores functional coverage and weighting. The 90% answer incorrectly applies functional coverage to all defect classes.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m13l02-01","lessonId":"m13l02","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K10"],"prompt":"A high-volume assembly contains a dense BGA, many passives, and analog circuits. Boundary scan is available for the BGA, but physical test-point access is limited. Which test strategy best balances coverage, cost, and production throughput?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Use boundary scan for BGA interconnects and ICT for passives and analog circuits, adding test points only where boundary scan cannot provide coverage."},{"id":"b","text":"Use flying probe only because it avoids fixture cost and can access dense BGA nodes."},{"id":"c","text":"Use functional test only because it verifies the assembly as a system and therefore replaces component-level tests."},{"id":"d","text":"Use ICT only and omit boundary scan to reduce design-for-test requests."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Boundary scan is efficient for dense BGA interconnects when physical access is limited, while ICT provides fast component-level coverage for passives and analog circuits. Flying probe is usually too slow for high-volume production, functional test does not isolate many component-level defects, and ICT alone would require inaccessible test points on the BGA.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m13l02-02","lessonId":"m13l02","type":"calc","competencies":["K6","K10"],"prompt":"Using the lesson rule of thumb that a 0.8 mm probe tip requires 0.2 mm radial clearance on each side, what minimum test-point diameter should be requested for reliable ICT access?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"1.0 mm"},{"id":"b","text":"1.2 mm"},{"id":"c","text":"1.6 mm"},{"id":"d","text":"0.4 mm"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The required test-point diameter is the probe tip diameter plus clearance on both sides: 0.8 mm + 2 × 0.2 mm = 1.2 mm. A 1.0 mm result adds clearance only once, 1.6 mm doubles the probe diameter instead of adding clearance, and 0.4 mm is only the total clearance.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m13l03-01","lessonId":"m13l03","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"A low-volume, high-mix assembly has dense components, limited access for a bed-of-nails fixture, and a short schedule. Which test strategy is most defensible, and why?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Flying probe, because it avoids a dedicated fixture and can reach selected test points, accepting a lower throughput ceiling."},{"id":"b","text":"ICT, because it always gives the lowest program effort and highest fault coverage for prototypes."},{"id":"c","text":"Boundary scan, because it can test every component regardless of package or digital support."},{"id":"d","text":"Functional test, because it requires no test program and isolates individual solder defects."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Flying probe wins when fixture cost, lead time, and access constraints dominate and volumes are low, even though its throughput ceiling is limited by probe movement. ICT usually needs a fixture and can be uneconomical for prototypes; boundary scan only tests supported digital devices and interconnects; functional test verifies operation but does not isolate faults as well as in-circuit methods.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m13l03-02","lessonId":"m13l03","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's rule of thumb for a flying-probe throughput ceiling—cycle time equals one second per test point plus ten seconds of overhead—what is the ceiling for a rule-of-thumb medium-complexity board with one hundred twenty test points?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"28 boards/hour"},{"id":"b","text":"30 boards/hour"},{"id":"c","text":"33 boards/hour"},{"id":"d","text":"3 boards/hour"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Cycle time = 120 × 1 s + 10 s = 130 s. Throughput ceiling = 3600 s/h ÷ 130 s/board = 27.7 boards/h, rounded to 28 boards/h. The 30 boards/h answer ignores overhead, while 33 boards/h subtracts overhead instead of adding it.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m13l04-01","lessonId":"m13l04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K10"],"prompt":"A BGA assembly contains a boundary-scan-capable BGA, a non-scan QFP, and many non-scan passives. Which statement best explains why boundary scan alone is not a complete test strategy?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It can only test nets whose endpoints are controlled by boundary-scan cells, so defects involving non-scan devices, analog or power nets, and internal device logic may be missed."},{"id":"b","text":"It cannot detect opens or shorts between BGA pins because BGA balls are inaccessible."},{"id":"c","text":"It requires physical probe access to every BGA ball, so it is unsuitable for BGAs."},{"id":"d","text":"It tests only power distribution and cannot test digital interconnects."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Boundary scan shifts test data through boundary-scan cells to drive and observe pins, so it can test interconnects between scan-capable pins but not non-scan endpoints or internal logic. Option b is wrong because inaccessible BGA balls are a reason to use boundary scan, not a reason it cannot test scan-capable nets. Option c is wrong because boundary scan does not require probe access to every ball. Option d is wrong because boundary scan is primarily used for digital interconnect testing, not only power distribution.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m13l04-02","lessonId":"m13l04","type":"calc","competencies":["K6","K10"],"prompt":"Using the rule of thumb taught in this lesson, estimate the minimum JTAG scan time for a boundary-scan test if the total scan-chain length is 2,000 boundary-scan cells, the test requires 500 vectors, and the TCK frequency is 10 MHz. Assume one bit is shifted per TCK cycle and ignore overhead.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.1 s"},{"id":"b","text":"1 s"},{"id":"c","text":"10 s"},{"id":"d","text":"100 s"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The rule-of-thumb formula is scan time = (scan cells × vectors) / TCK frequency. Here, 2,000 × 500 = 1,000,000 bits, and 1,000,000 / 10,000,000 = 0.1 s. The other options result from misplacing decimal points or using an incorrect TCK frequency.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m13l05-01","lessonId":"m13l05","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"A test strategy achieves high node coverage by probing most electrical nodes, but it only checks continuity and power-rail shorts. Which defect class is most likely to remain uncovered?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Wrong component value or reversed polarity on probed nodes, because the test does not excite or measure those modes."},{"id":"b","text":"Open circuits on probed nodes, because continuity testing cannot detect opens."},{"id":"c","text":"Shorts on probed nodes, because power-rail shorts are always detected by inspection."},{"id":"d","text":"Missing components on probed nodes, because node coverage counts every missing part as tested."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"High node coverage means nodes are accessed, but defect coverage depends on whether the test detects the specific defect modes present. Continuity and power-rail checks can miss wrong value or reversed polarity unless those modes are specifically exercised. Opens on probed nodes are generally detected by continuity, shorts may be detected by power-rail checks, and node coverage does not prove part presence or value.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m13l05-02","lessonId":"m13l05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's rule-of-thumb coverage model, a board has 1,200 nodes, test probes 900 nodes, and the probe strategy detects 80% of defect modes on probed nodes. What is the estimated test coverage and escape risk?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"60% test coverage and 40% escape risk"},{"id":"b","text":"75% test coverage and 25% escape risk"},{"id":"c","text":"80% test coverage and 20% escape risk"},{"id":"d","text":"95% test coverage and 5% escape risk"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Node coverage is 900 divided by 1,200, which equals 75%. Test coverage is node coverage times defect-mode coverage, so 75% times 80% equals 60%. Escape risk is 100% minus test coverage, so 100% minus 60% equals 40%. The other options confuse node coverage or defect-mode coverage with overall test coverage.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m13l06-01","lessonId":"m13l06","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"A low-volume, high-complexity assembly has limited test-point access and no JTAG support. Which test strategy is usually the best balance of coverage and cost?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Flying probe, because it avoids a dedicated fixture and can reach pads or test points without JTAG."},{"id":"b","text":"ICT, because it always gives the lowest cost for low-volume, high-complexity assemblies."},{"id":"c","text":"Boundary scan, because it can test all defects without any JTAG support."},{"id":"d","text":"Functional test, because it is the cheapest way to cover assembly defects on a complex board."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Flying probe is favored for low-volume, complex assemblies because it does not require a dedicated bed-of-nails fixture and can access pads or test points directly. ICT usually needs a fixture, which is costly for low volume. Boundary scan requires JTAG support, so it is not available here. Functional test can provide broad coverage but is typically more expensive and slower than flying probe for this case.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m13l06-02","lessonId":"m13l06","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's rule of thumb that a lot of 10,000 boards has about 500 assembly defects and that a combined ICT plus functional strategy detects about 95% of those defects, estimate how many defects remain uncovered.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"25"},{"id":"b","text":"500"},{"id":"c","text":"475"},{"id":"d","text":"5"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Total defects are 500. Detected defects are 500 × 0.95 = 475. Uncovered defects are 500 − 475 = 25, or 500 × 0.05 = 25. The 500 option ignores coverage, the 475 option is the detected count rather than the uncovered count, and the 5 option confuses a per-100-board rule of thumb with the uncovered count for the lot.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m14l01-01","lessonId":"m14l01","type":"single","competencies":["K9"],"prompt":"Before analyzing root causes for SMT/THT defects, what should be established first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A measured defect taxonomy, stage-level Pareto ranking, and evidence that the measurement system is trustworthy."},{"id":"b","text":"A root-cause hypothesis from the most experienced operator."},{"id":"c","text":"A corrective action such as changing the reflow profile or insertion force."},{"id":"d","text":"A supplier blame assignment based on the final inspection result."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Measured data must define what the defect is, where it first appears, and whether the measurement is reliable before opinions guide analysis. Operator hypotheses, corrective actions, and supplier blame can be useful later, but they are premature if the defect problem is not structured and measurement integrity is not confirmed.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m14l01-02","lessonId":"m14l01","type":"calc","competencies":["K9"],"prompt":"A defect log records first-detection stages only. Placement has twice the defects of reflow, and reflow has twice the defects of insertion; no other stages are recorded. Using the Pareto share formula, what is the cumulative share for placement and reflow?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"six-sevenths"},{"id":"b","text":"four-sevenths"},{"id":"c","text":"three-quarters"},{"id":"d","text":"five-sixths"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Let insertion = 1, reflow = 2, and placement = 4, so total defects = 1 + 2 + 4 = 7. The cumulative Pareto share for placement and reflow is (4 + 2) / 7 = 6/7, which is about 86%. Four-sevenths is placement alone, while three-quarters and five-sixths are plausible but incorrect cumulative fractions.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m14l02-01","lessonId":"m14l02","type":"single","competencies":["K9"],"prompt":"In a structured cause analysis of an SMT/THT defect, which statement correctly separates root cause, contributing causes, and detection escape?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The root cause is the underlying failure mechanism whose removal prevents recurrence; contributing causes are conditions that made the failure more likely or severe; the detection escape is the control gap that allowed the defect to pass."},{"id":"b","text":"The root cause is the operator or station that first noticed the defect; contributing causes are all equipment in the line; the detection escape is the final visual inspection."},{"id":"c","text":"The root cause is the customer complaint symptom; contributing causes are the corrective actions already taken; the detection escape is the first 5-Why question."},{"id":"d","text":"The root cause is the change-point date; contributing causes are the fishbone categories; the detection escape is the fault-tree top event."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A root cause is the mechanism that, if eliminated, prevents recurrence, while contributing causes are enabling or amplifying conditions. The detection escape is separate: it explains why existing controls failed to catch the defect. The distractors confuse accountability, symptoms, corrective actions, or analysis tools with the causal roles.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m14l02-02","lessonId":"m14l02","type":"calc","competencies":["K9"],"prompt":"For a fault tree taught in this lesson, the top event is (A AND B) OR C. Using the rule-of-thumb basic-event probabilities A = 0.01, B = 0.02, and C = 0.05, what is the top-event probability?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.05019"},{"id":"b","text":"0.07020"},{"id":"c","text":"0.00020"},{"id":"d","text":"0.05000"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Using the rule-of-thumb probabilities, the AND branch is 0.01 * 0.02 = 0.0002. The OR with C is 0.0002 + 0.05 - (0.0002 * 0.05) = 0.05019. Option b adds probabilities without removing the overlap, option c ignores C, and option d ignores the AND branch.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m14l03-01","lessonId":"m14l03","type":"single","competencies":["K9"],"prompt":"In an 8D for an SMT/THT escape, what is the most appropriate containment scope when a defect is found in one lot but the suspected root cause is a process parameter drift that could have affected adjacent lots?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Quarantine only the lot in which the defect was found."},{"id":"b","text":"Quarantine the suspect lot plus adjacent lots within the process window and perform a retroactive search."},{"id":"c","text":"Quarantine all inventory until the root cause is confirmed."},{"id":"d","text":"Wait for the root cause before taking containment action."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Containment must prevent further escape while the root cause is unknown, so the suspect lot and adjacent lots within the process window are quarantined and traced. Quarantining only the suspect lot may miss adjacent suspect lots, quarantining all inventory is overcontainment, and waiting for root cause leaves suspect product uncontrolled.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m14l03-02","lessonId":"m14l03","type":"calc","competencies":["K9"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's rule of thumb that a retroactive search covers one lot before and one lot after the suspect lot, and that each suspect lot is counted as one lot, how many lots are included in the retroactive search scope?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"2"},{"id":"b","text":"3"},{"id":"c","text":"4"},{"id":"d","text":"5"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The rule of thumb gives one suspect lot plus one lot before plus one lot after, so the arithmetic is 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 lots. Two lots omits one adjacent lot, four lots double counts the suspect lot or adds an extra lot, and five lots overquarantines beyond the stated rule of thumb.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m14l04-01","lessonId":"m14l04","type":"single","competencies":["K9"],"prompt":"In an 8D report, what is the primary purpose of D3 containment?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Protect the customer and stop the escape while root cause investigation continues."},{"id":"b","text":"Permanently eliminate the root cause of the problem."},{"id":"c","text":"Close the problem and release the team from further action."},{"id":"d","text":"Assign owners and dates for preventive actions."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"D3 containment protects the customer and buys time while the root cause is investigated. Permanent elimination of the root cause belongs to D5/D6, closure belongs to D8, and owner/date assignment is part of the action plan rather than the containment purpose.","source":"src-037","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m14l04-02","lessonId":"m14l04","type":"calc","competencies":["K9"],"prompt":"A plant uses the effectiveness metric: percent reduction = (baseline defect rate - post-containment defect rate) / baseline defect rate × 100%. A rule of thumb is that containment is effective when the metric is at least 90%. In a rule-of-thumb example with baseline 500 ppm and post-containment 40 ppm, what is the effectiveness metric?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"8%"},{"id":"b","text":"90%"},{"id":"c","text":"92%"},{"id":"d","text":"96%"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Using the rule-of-thumb example values, the reduction is 500 - 40 = 460 ppm, and 460 / 500 × 100% = 92%. The 90% value is the rule-of-thumb threshold, not the calculated metric; 8% is the post-containment rate as a percent of baseline; 96% would result from a post-containment rate of 20 ppm, not 40 ppm.","source":"src-037","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m14l05-01","lessonId":"m14l05","type":"single","competencies":["K9","K10"],"prompt":"After an 8D corrective action has been verified effective, what is the proper next step for the process documentation?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Close the 8D and leave the PFMEA and control plan unchanged unless a future audit finds a gap."},{"id":"b","text":"Update the PFMEA, control plan, and auditor checklist through change control so the verified learning is institutionalized."},{"id":"c","text":"Add a permanent inspection step to the control plan and skip the PFMEA because the containment already protects the customer."},{"id":"d","text":"Record the corrective action only in the 8D report, because PFMEA updates are reserved for new product introduction."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Corrective action effectiveness must be fed back into risk and control documents through change control so the same failure mode is prevented from recurring. Leaving the PFMEA or control plan unchanged preserves the old risk assessment and can allow the problem to return. Permanent inspection is a control, not a substitute for updating the underlying risk and control plan.","source":"src-037","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m14l05-02","lessonId":"m14l05","type":"calc","competencies":["K9","K10"],"prompt":"As a rule of thumb, an effectiveness target is that the post-action defect rate be one-fourth of the baseline defect rate. Using the lesson's effectiveness formula, what effectiveness does that target represent?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"25%"},{"id":"b","text":"50%"},{"id":"c","text":"75%"},{"id":"d","text":"100%"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson's effectiveness formula is (baseline - post-action) / baseline. If the post-action rate is one-fourth of baseline, let baseline be 4 units and post-action be 1 unit, so effectiveness = (4 - 1) / 4 = 3 / 4 = 75%. The 25% option is the remaining defect rate, not the reduction achieved.","source":"src-037","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m14l06-01","lessonId":"m14l06","type":"single","competencies":["K9","K10"],"prompt":"After an audit finding shows that a solder defect escaped because a control plan check was too late, what is the proper way to close the loop?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Open a change control record, update the PFMEA risk and controls, revise the control plan, and update the auditor checklist so the same gap is checked in future audits."},{"id":"b","text":"Update only the control plan because it is the live document used by operators."},{"id":"c","text":"Close the audit finding once the operator is retrained, since PFMEA is a design-time document."},{"id":"d","text":"Add a new checklist item without changing PFMEA, because PFMEA changes require customer approval."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Closing the loop requires change control so that the audit result updates the PFMEA, control plan, and auditor checklist consistently. Option b leaves the risk assessment stale, option c treats training as sufficient without fixing the systemic control, and option d creates checklist drift without updating the underlying risk logic.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m14l06-02","lessonId":"m14l06","type":"calc","competencies":["K9","K10"],"prompt":"A PFMEA line is rated on the standard PFMEA 1-10 scale with severity 8, occurrence 5, and detection 6. A change-control action reduces occurrence to 2 and detection to 3 while severity remains 8. What is the new RPN?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"48"},{"id":"b","text":"96"},{"id":"c","text":"120"},{"id":"d","text":"240"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The PFMEA RPN formula is RPN = Severity x Occurrence x Detection. The new RPN is 8 x 2 x 3 = 48. Option b, 96, equals 8 x 2 x 6 and assumes detection was not updated; option c, 120, equals 8 x 5 x 3 and assumes occurrence was not updated; option d, 240, equals 8 x 5 x 6 and is the old RPN before the change.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m14l07-01","lessonId":"m14l07","type":"single","competencies":["K9"],"prompt":"In an 8D cause analysis, which statement best separates root cause, contributing causes, and detection escape?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The root cause is the condition that, if eliminated, prevents recurrence; contributing causes increase likelihood but are not sufficient alone; the detection escape explains why the defect was not caught earlier."},{"id":"b","text":"The root cause is the first symptom observed; contributing causes are all other symptoms; the detection escape is the final corrective action."},{"id":"c","text":"The root cause is the inspection failure; contributing causes are process deviations; the detection escape is the containment action."},{"id":"d","text":"The root cause is the customer complaint; contributing causes are operator errors; the detection escape is the effectiveness metric."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The root cause is the condition that, if removed, prevents recurrence; contributing causes make the failure more likely but are not sufficient by themselves; the detection escape explains why the defect was not caught before the customer. The other options confuse prevention with detection or treat a contributing factor as the sole cause.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m14l07-02","lessonId":"m14l07","type":"calc","competencies":["K9"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's Pareto cumulative-percentage formula and the 80/20 rule of thumb, if the top stage accounts for the rule-of-thumb 80% of defects and the second stage accounts for half of the remaining defects, what is the cumulative percentage for the top two stages?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"80%"},{"id":"b","text":"85%"},{"id":"c","text":"90%"},{"id":"d","text":"95%"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The top stage is the rule-of-thumb 80%, leaving 20% for all other stages. Half of that remainder is 10%, so the cumulative top-two percentage is 80% + 10% = 90%. The other options either omit the second stage or misapply the remainder.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m15l01-01","lessonId":"m15l01","type":"single","competencies":["K10"],"prompt":"A process engineer reports that wave soldering cannot reliably fill small plated through holes because the hole-to-pad ratio is too high. Which response best converts this process limitation into a specific, testable design rule change?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Add a design rule requiring a minimum hole-to-pad ratio and verify it on a test coupon before issuing an ECN."},{"id":"b","text":"Tell designers to avoid small plated through holes in all future products."},{"id":"c","text":"Request an ECN to change the solder mask color so the holes are easier to see."},{"id":"d","text":"Accept the limitation and add extra inspection to catch unfilled holes."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The correct response turns the process limitation into a measurable design rule and a verification step, which is what designers can act on. Avoiding all small holes is too broad and not tied to the limiting mechanism. Changing solder mask color does not address hole filling, and extra inspection detects the defect but does not change the design.","source":"src-024","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m15l01-02","lessonId":"m15l01","type":"calc","competencies":["K10"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formula ECN break-even volume = one-time design-change cost / (escape rate * rework cost per escape), and a rule of thumb that the process escapes one defect per ten boards, each rework touch costs one labor unit, and a design change costs ten labor units, what is the break-even volume?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Ten boards"},{"id":"b","text":"One hundred boards"},{"id":"c","text":"One thousand boards"},{"id":"d","text":"Ten thousand boards"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The escape rate is one defect per ten boards, or 1 / 10 = 0.1 escapes per board, and the avoided rework cost per board is 0.1 * 1 = 0.1 labor units. The break-even volume is 10 / 0.1 = 100 boards. Ten boards ignores the escape rate, while one thousand and ten thousand boards use escape rates of one per hundred or one per thousand instead of the stated rule of thumb.","source":"src-024","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m15l02-01","lessonId":"m15l02","type":"single","competencies":["K10"],"prompt":"Which evidence-pack item most directly makes a defect-rate claim credible to a design review?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Measured inspection data tied to a named lot, plus images with scale showing the defect morphology."},{"id":"b","text":"A summary statement that the line often sees defects on the current design."},{"id":"c","text":"A close-up image without scale that looks like a common solder defect."},{"id":"d","text":"A cost estimate based only on the engineer's recollection of rework time."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Measured data and scale images make the defect rate traceable and the failure mode identifiable, which is what quantifies process impact. Anecdotal statements, unscaled images, and recollection-based costs do not provide auditable evidence.","source":"src-024","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m15l02-02","lessonId":"m15l02","type":"calc","competencies":["K10"],"prompt":"Using the lesson formulas defect rate = defective boards / inspected boards and cost of poor quality = defect rate × inspected boards × cost per defective board, assume a rule-of-thumb inspected lot of one thousand boards, a rule-of-thumb defective count of twenty-five boards, and a rule-of-thumb cost per defective board of twelve dollars. What is the cost of poor quality?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Three hundred dollars"},{"id":"b","text":"Thirty dollars"},{"id":"c","text":"Thirty thousand dollars"},{"id":"d","text":"Twelve thousand dollars"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Using the rule-of-thumb values, defect rate = twenty-five / one thousand = two and one-half percent = zero point zero two five. Cost of poor quality = zero point zero two five × one thousand × twelve = three hundred dollars. Option b uses the percent value as if it were a decimal and omits the lot size; option c uses two and one-half instead of zero point zero two five; option d omits the defect rate.","source":"src-024","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m15l03-01","lessonId":"m15l03","type":"single","competencies":["K10"],"prompt":"A design review rejects an ECN/DCR for a THT connector change with the objection: 'The current assembly is already qualified; this change invalidates that qualification.' Which section of the change request most directly defends against this objection?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Problem statement, because it proves the current design has a defect."},{"id":"b","text":"Impact assessment, because it lists the affected parts and process steps."},{"id":"c","text":"Verification section, because it specifies the tests and acceptance criteria that re-establish qualification after the change."},{"id":"d","text":"Alternatives section, because it shows that no other change was considered."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The verification section directly answers a qualification objection by defining how the changed assembly will be tested and accepted. The impact assessment is necessary to scope the change, but it does not prove that the qualified state can be restored. The problem statement explains why the change is needed, and the alternatives section justifies the chosen proposal rather than requalification.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m15l03-02","lessonId":"m15l03","type":"calc","competencies":["K10"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's rule-of-thumb change impact score, Impact = affected part count × affected process steps × requalification factor, where the requalification factor is one for no requalification, two for partial requalification, and three for full requalification. A proposed SMT/THT change affects twelve BOM lines, touches four process steps, and requires full requalification. What is the impact score?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"144"},{"id":"b","text":"48"},{"id":"c","text":"19"},{"id":"d","text":"16"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Using the rule-of-thumb formula, twelve affected part count × four affected process steps × three full requalification factor = 144. The 48 option omits the full requalification factor, the 19 option adds the factors instead of multiplying them, and the 16 option uses only the part count and process steps.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m15l04-01","lessonId":"m15l04","type":"single","competencies":["K10"],"prompt":"A production line reports frequent tombstoning on small chip components during reflow. Which DFM/DFT design-rule change best converts this process limitation into a specific, testable rule?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Make the two land pads on each component equal in size and symmetric about the component centerline, then verify with a land symmetry check."},{"id":"b","text":"Increase paste volume on one land to compensate for the imbalance."},{"id":"c","text":"Add a test point on one land to monitor solderability."},{"id":"d","text":"Move fiducials closer to the component to improve placement accuracy."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Symmetric, equal lands reduce wetting-force imbalance that causes tombstoning, and the rule can be checked by comparing land areas and centerline symmetry. Increasing paste on one land worsens imbalance, adding a test point does not change the solder joint geometry, and fiducial placement affects alignment rather than tombstoning.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m15l04-02","lessonId":"m15l04","type":"calc","competencies":["K10"],"prompt":"Using the lesson rule of thumb that stencil aperture area equals land area minus a paste relief fraction, a land area is specified as 100% and the paste relief fraction is 20%. What stencil aperture area should be specified?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"80%"},{"id":"b","text":"100%"},{"id":"c","text":"120%"},{"id":"d","text":"140%"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson formula is aperture area = land area × (1 − relief fraction), so 100% × (1 − 0.20) = 80%. Choosing 100% ignores paste relief, 120% adds relief instead of subtracting it, and 140% doubles the relief addition.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m15l05-01","lessonId":"m15l05","type":"single","competencies":["K10"],"prompt":"When defending an ECN/DCR request against a design objection that the current SMT/THT design is acceptable, which evidence is most persuasive?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A measured evidence pack showing the current design's process impact, such as defect modes, rework, yield, and cycle-time effects, tied to the affected requirement."},{"id":"b","text":"A statement that a preferred supplier would like the change because it simplifies their manufacturing."},{"id":"c","text":"A competitor's board layout that uses the proposed change without any process data."},{"id":"d","text":"A simulation result showing the proposed change is theoretically possible, with no current-design impact data."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The strongest ECN/DCR defense quantifies the current design's process impact and links it to the requirement being protected. Supplier preference, competitor layouts, and theoretical simulations do not prove that the current design creates unacceptable SMT/THT process risk or cost.","source":"src-001","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m15l05-02","lessonId":"m15l05","type":"calc","competencies":["K10"],"prompt":"Using the lesson's rule of thumb for a small SMT/THT line: 10,000 boards/month, 5,000 ppm rework, and 12 labor units per rework, calculate the monthly process impact score using the lesson formula: impact score = boards × ppm / 1,000,000 × labor units per rework.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"600"},{"id":"b","text":"50"},{"id":"c","text":"120"},{"id":"d","text":"6000"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Using the lesson's evidence-pack formula, monthly defective boards = 10,000 × 5,000 / 1,000,000 = 50; multiplying by 12 labor units per rework gives 600 labor units. Option b omits the rework cost, option c uses a thousand-fold ppm conversion error, and option d misplaces the decimal.","source":"src-001","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m12l01-01","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K6"],"lessonId":"m12l01","prompt":"An assembly passes ROSE at 0.82 µg NaCl/cm² against a 1.00 µg NaCl/cm² limit, but its SIR result is 8.5 × 10^7 Ω against a 1.0 × 10^8 Ω requirement. Which criterion governs the disposition, and why?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"SIR governs, because it measures the failure mechanism (leakage and electrochemical migration under bias and humidity) that the residue can actually cause"},{"id":"b","text":"ROSE governs, because it is the industry-standard cleanliness test and is measured first"},{"id":"c","text":"Neither governs; the assembly is accepted because both results are within an order of magnitude of their limits"},{"id":"d","text":"Visual inspection governs, because a board with no visible residue is by definition clean"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"ROSE and SIR answer different questions. ROSE reports a conductivity equivalent of extracted ionic residue and is a process-control tool; SIR puts the residue under the bias and humidity that cause leakage, dendritic growth and electrochemical migration, so it is the closer proxy for the reliability requirement. When the two disagree, the criterion that models the failure mechanism governs: rework the assembly and address the rinse, not the ROSE reading.","source":"src-002","bloom":"understand"},{"questionId":"q-m12l01-02","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K6"],"lessonId":"m12l01","prompt":"A ROSE test on a cleaned area reads 0.95 µg NaCl/cm² with a blank of 0.12 µg NaCl/cm². The product limit is 1.00 µg NaCl/cm². What is the blank-corrected result and the disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.83 µg NaCl/cm² — passes"},{"id":"b","text":"1.07 µg NaCl/cm² — fails"},{"id":"c","text":"0.95 µg NaCl/cm² — passes"},{"id":"d","text":"0.83 µg NaCl/cm² — fails"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The blank is the contamination contributed by the solvent, container and handling, and it is subtracted from the reading: 0.95 − 0.12 = 0.83 µg NaCl/cm². Compared with the 1.00 µg NaCl/cm² limit, 0.83 passes. Reporting the uncorrected reading overstates the board contamination and can trigger rework that is not needed; skipping the blank does the opposite. Record method, area, blank and units with the result.","source":"src-002","bloom":"apply"},{"questionId":"q-m12l01-03","type":"calc","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m12l01","prompt":"ROSE reads 0.82 µg NaCl/cm² against a 1.00 limit, and SIR reads 8.5 × 10⁷ Ω against a 1.0 × 10⁸ Ω requirement. What is the decision?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"ROSE passes but SIR fails, so the cleaning decision does not pass"},{"id":"b","text":"Both pass, since the SIR reading is within 15 % of the requirement"},{"id":"c","text":"Both fail, because any ionic reading above zero is unacceptable"},{"id":"d","text":"ROSE fails, so the SIR result need not be considered"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The ionic reading is below its limit, but 8.5 × 10⁷ Ω is an order-of-magnitude short of the 1.0 × 10⁸ Ω requirement, and the lesson treats the reliability specification as the governing criterion. A near miss is a miss, a non-zero ionic reading is normal, and one failing test does not make the other irrelevant.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m01l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l01","prompt":"The same latent material defect can be caught at receipt, on the line, in the field, or only after a recall. Why does the cost of that defect rise at each of those stages?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Each stage adds labour, material and schedule exposure before the defect is removed, and a field failure adds handling and warranty cost."},{"id":"b","text":"Inspection labour rates are set higher at later stages by union agreement, so the same operation costs more when it is performed downstream."},{"id":"c","text":"Later stages apply tighter acceptance criteria, so a defect that was tolerated at receipt becomes a reject on the line."},{"id":"d","text":"The supplier reimburses defects found at receipt but not those found on the line, so the cost simply moves to the plant."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson attributes the escalation to accumulated value and schedule exposure, not to a rate change or a criteria change. By the time a defect surfaces on the line the board already carries every component and print operation that came before it, and a field failure adds freight, handling, customer communication and warranty. That is why the escalation diagram runs 1x at receipt, 10x on the line, 100x in the field and up to 1000x for a recall.","source":"src-037"},{"questionId":"q-m01l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l01","prompt":"A plant can only add a limited number of characteristics to its incoming inspection plan. According to the lesson, which characteristics should be added first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Those that are cheapest to measure, so the plan covers as many characteristics as possible per hour."},{"id":"b","text":"Those with the highest downstream cost and the lowest chance of being detected later in the process."},{"id":"c","text":"Those that fail most often in the supplier history, whatever the detection capability downstream."},{"id":"d","text":"Those that the purchase order lists first, because the document order reflects contractual priority."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson sets the criterion explicitly: the plan should focus on the characteristics that carry the highest downstream cost and the lowest chance of being caught later. Cheap-to-measure characteristics buy coverage on paper, and a high supplier failure count on a characteristic that assembly detects reliably does not justify the same priority.","source":"src-037"},{"questionId":"q-m01l01-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l01","prompt":"A lot of 20,000 units is released with an escape fraction of 0.5 %, and the plant cost table gives 4 currency units per defect found at receipt and 400 per defect found in the field. What is the expected field cost of the escaped defects?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"4,000 currency units"},{"id":"b","text":"400,000 currency units"},{"id":"c","text":"40,000 currency units"},{"id":"d","text":"10,000 currency units"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Escaped defects = 20,000 x 0.005 = 100 units. Field cost = 100 x 400 = 40,000 currency units, against 100 x 4 = 400 units found at receipt. The 4,000 and 10,000 answers come from dividing the lot quantity by 2 and by 8 instead of applying the escape fraction, and 400,000 comes from treating the 0.5 % as a whole-number multiplier.","source":"src-037"},{"questionId":"q-m01l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l01","prompt":"Which statement describes what an incoming inspection gate actually delivers to the plant?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It guarantees that released lots contain no defective units at all, provided the sampling plan and the inspection level were chosen correctly."},{"id":"b","text":"It moves the responsibility for material quality to the supplier and removes the need for line monitoring."},{"id":"c","text":"It replaces process qualification, because released material cannot cause process deviations downstream."},{"id":"d","text":"It converts latent material risk into documented evidence, so a lot is released on proof rather than on appearance."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Incoming inspection is a control point that produces evidence: the lot, its identity, its condition and its handling history are shown to match what the process expects. It cannot guarantee a defect-free lot, it does not transfer responsibility away from the plant, and it does not substitute for process qualification.","source":"src-037"},{"questionId":"q-m01l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m01l01","prompt":"A material defect that incoming inspection cannot see reliably causes intermittent opens after three months in the field. Which argument best justifies spending on incoming resources for that part family?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The cost of a field escape dominates the cost of catching the same defect at the dock."},{"id":"b","text":"The part family has the largest purchase volume, so any inspection spend is justified."},{"id":"c","text":"Field returns are covered by warranty, so the plant carries no cost for an escape."},{"id":"d","text":"Incoming inspection is the only stage that can measure the characteristic at all."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The decision criterion is relative cost, and the escalation is what makes the case: the same defect at the dock and in the field differ by orders of magnitude. Purchase volume alone is not the criterion, warranty does not absorb handling and reputation cost, and the claim that no later stage can measure the characteristic is not what the lesson establishes.","source":"src-037"},{"questionId":"q-m01l01-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l01","prompt":"Receipt-stage correction of one defect costs 2 currency units. Using the lesson escalation of 1x at receipt, 10x on the line and 100x in the field, what does the same defect cost in the field?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"20 currency units"},{"id":"b","text":"200 currency units"},{"id":"c","text":"2,000 currency units"},{"id":"d","text":"110 currency units"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The field multiplier is 100, so 2 x 100 = 200 currency units. The 20 answer applies the 10x line multiplier, 2,000 applies a 1000x recall multiplier, and 110 adds the three multipliers instead of applying one of them.","source":"src-037"},{"questionId":"q-m01l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l02","prompt":"A restricted-substance declaration arrives with a component lot. What does that document actually prove?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It proves the lot conforms to the purchase order, so it can replace the certificate of conformance."},{"id":"b","text":"It proves that the supplier tested the electrical parameters of the specific reel that was received."},{"id":"c","text":"It proves a stated compliance claim about the lot range, not that the lot will process correctly."},{"id":"d","text":"It proves the material is free of moisture, so the dry-pack card no longer has to be checked."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson separates declarations from quality certificates: a declaration carries a regulatory or contractual claim about origin, composition or compliance, traceable to the lot or a lot range. It says nothing about process behaviour, it does not replace a CoC, it is not a test report, and it has no bearing on moisture content.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l02","prompt":"A certificate of conformance matches the part number and quantity but names a different supplier lot code than the reel labels. What is the correct disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept the lot without further evidence: part number and quantity match, and the lot code is treated as a supplier internal reference only."},{"id":"b","text":"Accept the lot after a visual check of the markings, because the certificate confirms the specification."},{"id":"c","text":"Reject and scrap the lot: any document mismatch means the material cannot be used under any condition."},{"id":"d","text":"Quarantine the lot: a certificate for another lot does not prove this lot, even with the part number matching."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"A certificate must be traceable to the received lot by lot code, date code or serial range. When it covers a different lot, it proves nothing about the material on the dock, whatever the part number says. That is a documentation nonconformity, so the lot is held until the evidence is corrected rather than accepted on appearance or scrapped outright.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l02","prompt":"A dry-pack label states a floor life of 168 h. The pack was opened 100 h ago and has not been resealed. How much floor life remains, and what does that mean for a job that still needs 80 h of open time?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"68 h remain, which does not cover the 80 h still needed, so the parts need conditioning before use."},{"id":"b","text":"168 h remain, because the floor-life value on the label is fixed and does not decrease with exposure."},{"id":"c","text":"268 h remain, because the label value is added to the exposure time already recorded in the log."},{"id":"d","text":"100 h remain, because the elapsed time is the working value and the label value is only a limit."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Remaining floor life = 168 h - 100 h = 68 h. The remaining 80 h of planned open time would exceed it by 12 h, so the parts cannot be placed on the current schedule without an approved conditioning step. The label value is a limit that is consumed by exposure, not a fixed allowance, and elapsed time is not interchangeable with remaining time.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m01l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l02","prompt":"Why is a packing list not sufficient evidence to release a received lot?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It is issued by the carrier rather than the supplier, so it carries no contractual weight at all."},{"id":"b","text":"It records what the supplier says it shipped and how it was identified, not that the lot met the criteria."},{"id":"c","text":"It covers only the outer cartons on the pallet, and the inner reel labels always override any information the packing list carries about the lot."},{"id":"d","text":"It is a financial document, so it is retained by purchasing and never reaches the receiving area."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The packing list proves shipping identity, quantity and traceability links. Conformance evidence comes from the certificate of conformance or analysis. The packing list is still a receiving document and it is the starting point for traceability, so the other three statements misstate what it is and who issues it.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l02","prompt":"A dry pack arrives with an intact seal but the humidity indicator card has turned to its warning colour. What does this tell the receiving inspector?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The seal failed in transit, so the pack can be resealed and the floor-life clock starts at receipt."},{"id":"b","text":"The indicator is a shipping label, so its colour is recorded for reference but has no disposition effect."},{"id":"c","text":"The pack environment exceeded the supplier threshold at some point, so the lot is held for evaluation."},{"id":"d","text":"The components are already wet, so the lot must be scrapped without further evaluation."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The card is evidence about the environment the pack has been exposed to, so an exceedance means the lot is quarantined and evaluated before use. The lesson does not conclude that the parts are wet and unusable, that the seal has failed, or that the reading is decorative. Resealing does not reset an exposure history that has already been recorded on the card.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m01l02-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m01l02","prompt":"Receiving has a complete document package, but the work order does not carry the paste type or the floor-life start time. Why is that a control gap rather than a filing problem?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The documents are lost for audit purposes, but production parameters are unaffected by the gap."},{"id":"b","text":"The work order becomes invalid, so the lot must be rejected and returned to the supplier at once."},{"id":"c","text":"It only affects traceability after a field return, so it can be closed at the end of the batch."},{"id":"d","text":"The process can then run with parameters that the released documents never approved for that lot."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson calls this the handoff: values that affect the process, such as paste type, shelf-life status, floor-life start and board revision, have to reach the work order or MES record. Without the handoff a lot can be released by quality and then run with the wrong parameters — a real process risk, not a filing issue and not a rejection.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l03","prompt":"A delivery mixes two date codes and two supplier plants under one purchase order line. How should the lot be defined for sampling?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Split it into separate lots for each date code and plant, unless the plan states the difference is irrelevant."},{"id":"b","text":"Treat it as one lot, because the single purchase order line is the only basis on which a receiver is allowed to define a lot at the dock."},{"id":"c","text":"Treat it as one lot but double the sample size to compensate for the added internal variation."},{"id":"d","text":"Treat it as one lot and raise a nonconformance, because mixed deliveries are never inspectable."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A lot is the smallest quantity that can be treated as one population: same part number, revision, supplier, manufacturing lot or date code, packaging and process route. Mixing revisions, date codes or supplier plants breaks that assumption, so the material is split unless the quality plan says the difference does not affect the inspected characteristics. Doubling the sample does not repair a wrong population definition.","source":"src-006"},{"questionId":"q-m01l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l03","prompt":"Incoming inspection classifies critical, major and minor defects. Why does the lesson insist on separate criteria for these classes instead of one AQL?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Separate criteria are required because each defect class is measured with its own instrument type during the same inspection."},{"id":"b","text":"Pooling the classes lets a large number of minor defects hide a critical one inside the same count."},{"id":"c","text":"A single AQL cannot be applied to a sample larger than 125 units under normal inspection."},{"id":"d","text":"Separate classes allow the receiver to stop inspecting once the minor defect count is reached."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson warns that one AQL can hide risk when critical, major and minor defects are counted together: a critical defect such as a wrong part number deserves a zero-acceptance criterion, while cosmetic marks on a non-critical area do not. The classes are not separated because of instrument type, sample size or a stopping rule.","source":"src-006"},{"questionId":"q-m01l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l03","prompt":"An incoming lot contains 5,000 units. Using ISO 2859-1 general inspection level II, normal single sampling, at AQL 1.0 % defective, what plan applies?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Code letter K: n = 125, Ac = 3, Re = 4"},{"id":"b","text":"Code letter L: n = 200, Ac = 3, Re = 4"},{"id":"c","text":"Code letter L: n = 200, Ac = 5, Re = 6"},{"id":"d","text":"Code letter M: n = 315, Ac = 7, Re = 8"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"A lot of 3,201 to 10,000 units at level II gives code letter L, and code letter L corresponds to a sample of 200 units. At AQL 1.0 % the accept number is 5 and the reject number is 6. The K row belongs to lots of 1,201 to 3,200 units, and mixing the L sample size with the K accept number is a common transcription error.","source":"src-006"},{"questionId":"q-m01l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l03","prompt":"What is the practical meaning of an AQL of 1.0 % defective for the supplier relationship?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It is the maximum defect rate that any single accepted lot is permitted to contain."},{"id":"b","text":"It is the defect rate above which the supplier becomes contractually liable for all of the rework and sorting cost on the lot."},{"id":"c","text":"It is the sampling fraction, so one unit in every hundred received must be inspected."},{"id":"d","text":"It is the quality level the plan is built to accept most of the time, not a defect allowance per lot."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"AQL is the average quality at which the plan accepts with high probability, and the lesson stresses that accepted lots are not defect-free. It is not a per-lot ceiling, not a liability trigger, and not a sampling fraction. Applying it as a per-lot allowance is exactly the misreading the lesson warns against.","source":"src-006"},{"questionId":"q-m01l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m01l03","prompt":"A lot passes its AQL plan, and the first boards after reflow show tombstoning at one feeder. Which conclusion fits the lesson?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The sample may have missed a localised condition that the plan did not measure at all."},{"id":"b","text":"The sampling plan was invalid, so every accepted lot from this supplier must be rejected."},{"id":"c","text":"The defect rate on the line proves the supplier falsified the certificate of conformance."},{"id":"d","text":"Nothing can be concluded, because sampling results and line results measure different populations."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A plan protects against accepting lots whose average quality is worse than the AQL; it cannot guarantee a clean lot, and it only measures the characteristics it names. Tombstoning points to a localised tape, reel or storage condition, or to a characteristic such as solderability that the plan did not inspect. The other options overreach from one accepted lot.","source":"src-006"},{"questionId":"q-m01l03-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l03","prompt":"A lot of 2,400 units is inspected at general inspection level II with an AQL of 1.0 %. The inspector inspects the sample and finds 2 defective units. What is the disposition under the plan?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"n = 125 with Ac = 3: reject the lot, since any defective unit exceeds a 1.0 % AQL."},{"id":"b","text":"n = 125 with Ac = 3: accept the lot, since 2 is below the acceptance number."},{"id":"c","text":"n = 200 with Ac = 5: accept the lot, since 2 is well below the acceptance number."},{"id":"d","text":"n = 80 with Ac = 2: reject the lot, because the defect count equals the acceptance number."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"A lot of 1,201 to 3,200 units at level II gives code letter K, so n = 125, and at AQL 1.0 % the accept number is 3. Two defectives are at or below Ac, so the lot is accepted. AQL is not a zero-defect rule, and the L and J rows belong to larger and smaller lot sizes.","source":"src-006"},{"questionId":"q-m01l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l04","prompt":"Which observation is a counterfeit red flag that the lesson treats as rejecting the lot regardless of the other findings?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The outer carton arrives dented at one corner while every inner moisture bag is still sealed and visually intact."},{"id":"b","text":"The packing list is printed in a language other than the one used on the purchase order."},{"id":"c","text":"One reel carries two date codes under a single supplier label, with two different marking styles."},{"id":"d","text":"The certificate of conformance arrives as a scanned copy instead of an original signed sheet."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Mixed date codes and mixed marking styles under one label are the pattern the lesson puts at the top of the red-flag list, together with altered labels and bulk repackaging; it is treated as a reject. A dented carton with intact inner packaging, a different document language and a scanned certificate are documentation or handling issues that are quarantined and resolved, not automatic rejections.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l04-04","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l04","prompt":"A purchase order limits date-code age at receipt to 12 months and the date-code format is YYMM. A reel is marked 2401 and is received in 2504. What is the age at receipt and the finding?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"13 months: the reel exceeds the 12-month limit by 1 month."},{"id":"b","text":"15 months: the reel is inside the 12-month limit with 3 months spare."},{"id":"c","text":"27 months: the reel exceeds the 12-month limit by 15 months."},{"id":"d","text":"15 months: the reel exceeds the 12-month limit by 3 months."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"2401 to 2504 crosses 15 months on a YYMM code: 2401 to 2501 is 12 months, plus 3 more to 2504. That is 3 months beyond the 12-month purchase-order limit. Reading 2401 as a year only, or subtracting as if the code were DDMM, produces the 13 and 27 month answers; the code convention is verified with the supplier rather than assumed.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l04-05","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l04","prompt":"At the placement machine a reel is found to contain parts with two date codes, and the inner label is partly covered by a second label. Production wants to sort the reel and keep running. What is the correct action?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Stop the machine, quarantine the reel and the remaining lot, photograph the labels and notify the supplier."},{"id":"b","text":"Sort the reel into date-code groups, record the split in the shift log and continue the build with the larger of the two groups."},{"id":"c","text":"Continue running and raise the finding at the end of the shift so the batch is not interrupted."},{"id":"d","text":"Return the reel to stores and draw a replacement, leaving the finding to the next incoming audit."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"This is the lesson defect case: a mixed, relabelled reel is a counterfeit and traceability red flag, so the affected material is stopped and quarantined and the evidence is photographed before the supplier is notified. Sorting or finishing the batch destroys the evidence, and drawing a replacement leaves the rest of the suspect lot in circulation.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l04","prompt":"Which four things must the receiving engineer be able to prove about a component lot before release?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Lowest purchase price, shortest lead time, an authorised distributor, and a valid commercial invoice for the shipment."},{"id":"b","text":"Correct item, traceable to a lot, handled within its stated conditions, and conforming evidence on file."},{"id":"c","text":"Correct package outline, correct reel diameter, correct tape width, and correct moisture level."},{"id":"d","text":"Supplier certification, supplier audit score, supplier on-time delivery, and supplier price trend."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson states the release test as four proofs: the item is the correct part or material, it is traceable to a manufacturer or supplier lot, it has been handled within its stated storage and moisture limits, and the evidence of conformity is on file. Price, lead time, packaging dimensions and supplier scorecard data are useful context but none of them is the release basis.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m01l04","prompt":"A distributor offers a widely used logic IC at a price well below the market and with a lead time of two weeks against a usual sixteen. What does the lesson call this, and what follows?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A commercial opportunity: price and lead time are purchasing matters and carry no technical weight."},{"id":"b","text":"A legitimate clearance sale: the incoming check only needs to confirm the part number marking."},{"id":"c","text":"Two red flags at once: the lot is treated as suspect and cannot be released on documents alone."},{"id":"d","text":"A traceability problem only: the lot can be used once the distributor supplies a declaration."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Unusually low price and unusually short lead time are both on the lesson red-flag list, alongside missing manufacturer traceability and unauthorised distribution. The response is risk-based: the lot is treated as suspect and quarantined pending evidence. Price and lead time do carry technical weight in this context, and a declaration alone does not close a counterfeit suspicion.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l04-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l04","prompt":"A lot is physically acceptable, but the manufacturer lot traceability sheet is missing. What is the disposition and why?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept: the physical inspection passed, so the missing sheet is an administrative follow-up item."},{"id":"b","text":"Reject: any missing document is treated as a confirmed nonconformity of the material itself."},{"id":"c","text":"Accept with a concession: traceability only matters after a field failure, so the missing sheet can be added to the file later."},{"id":"d","text":"Quarantine: without traceability the lot cannot be proven to be the single manufacturer lot that was ordered."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Missing traceability is exactly the quarantine case in the lesson: the material may be usable, but the evidence is incomplete, so it is held pending supplier evidence or a concession decision. It is not an automatic reject, because the material has not been shown to be nonconforming, and it is not a follow-up item, because the release decision rests on the evidence.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l05","prompt":"A paste lot arrives with a certificate of analysis that states alloy, powder type, flux classification and metal content. What does that certificate still not prove?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"That the lot survived transport and storage inside the supplier temperature limits."},{"id":"b","text":"That the paste was manufactured to the formulation named in the purchase order."},{"id":"c","text":"That the metal content of the lot was measured against the agreed specification."},{"id":"d","text":"That the flux classification of the lot matches the classification that was ordered."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson is explicit: a certificate proves the paste was acceptable at the supplier, not that it is still suitable after transport, storage or handling. Storage and cold-chain integrity come from the temperature record, not the CoA. The other three items are precisely what a CoA does establish.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l05","prompt":"A paste shipment arrives with a temperature logger showing 18 degrees C for four hours, against a supplier storage range of 2 to 10 degrees C. What is the correct disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Reject the lot outright, because any excursion above the storage range makes paste unusable."},{"id":"b","text":"Quarantine the lot and obtain the supplier limit for excursion time and temperature before release."},{"id":"c","text":"Accept the lot, because paste is mixed at room temperature before use in any case."},{"id":"d","text":"Accept the lot but shorten the expiry date by exactly the four hours that the lot spent above the storage range."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"An excursion beyond the supplier shipping or storage limit is a quarantine trigger in the lesson, not an automatic reject: the material may still be usable, but the decision needs the supplier limit and possibly a retest. Room-temperature warming before use is a controlled, documented step with its own time limit, and it does not license an uncontrolled excursion.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l05","prompt":"A paste lot was manufactured on 2025-01-01 with a six-month shelf life and was received on 2025-04-01. How much usable shelf life is left at receipt if the shelf life is counted as 181 days?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 90 days, because the receipt date is counted as the start of the shelf life."},{"id":"b","text":"About 181 days, because shelf life starts when the cold chain is opened at the plant."},{"id":"c","text":"About 91 days, because 90 days were consumed between manufacture and receipt."},{"id":"d","text":"About 45 days, because paste shelf life is halved once it leaves the supplier."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"January and February and March make 31 + 28 + 31 = 90 days from 2025-01-01 to 2025-04-01, so 181 - 90 = 91 days remain. Shelf life runs from manufacture, not from receipt and not from opening: that is the whole point of recording the manufacture and expiry dates against the lot code.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l05","prompt":"Why does a solder paste lot need a time-and-temperature record rather than only an expiry date?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The expiry date is a commercial warranty date, and production is free to extend it by any margin."},{"id":"b","text":"Temperature records are required by the safety data sheet, so they are kept only for hazard communication purposes."},{"id":"c","text":"The record is only needed for lots that are returned to the supplier after a print problem."},{"id":"d","text":"Paste deteriorates with every hour spent outside its storage range, so the date alone cannot show condition."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson asks for a time and temperature record, not just a date, because printability depends on the exposure history: storage temperature, excursions and hours outside storage all change how the paste behaves. The expiry date is a supplier shelf-life statement, not a commercial warranty that production can extend, and the record is not a hazard-communication or returns-only document.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m01l05","prompt":"Within a week of opening a new paste lot, printing shows paste balls, uneven deposit volume and poor release. Which incoming evidence is the first thing to review?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The lot certificate and storage history, to establish whether the paste was kept inside its range."},{"id":"b","text":"The printer squeegee pressure and separation speed, because paste condition cannot cause these signs."},{"id":"c","text":"The stencil wall finish, because a new lot cannot change release behaviour on an unchanged foil."},{"id":"d","text":"The reflow profile, because paste balls form above the liquidus and are unrelated to printing."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"This is the lesson defect case: the symptoms appeared with a new paste lot, so the first question is what the incoming evidence says about that lot — chemistry, powder, expiry and cold chain. Paste condition does affect release, and a different lot can change print behaviour on identical hardware, so ruling the paste out first and changing machine settings instead is the wrong order.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l05-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l05","prompt":"Paste is returned to storage three times with the open time logged each time. Why does the label have to record the number of opens?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The count is a stock-control field, used only to reconcile the quantity that is left in the container at the end of the shift."},{"id":"b","text":"Repeated warming and opening change printability, and the count is part of the handling evidence."},{"id":"c","text":"The count determines the price of the lot, because suppliers invoice by the number of opens."},{"id":"d","text":"The count has no technical meaning, but customers ask for it during quality system audits."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson ties the number of opens and the time outside storage to printability and to the decision to mix or discard. It is a technical record of handling exposure, not a stock-control reconciliation, a pricing field or an audit formality.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m01l06-03","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l06","prompt":"How does shelf life differ from floor life for a moisture-sensitive item?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Shelf life applies to components; floor life applies only to bare boards and to solder paste lots, which are not hermetically sealed."},{"id":"b","text":"Shelf life is set by the customer; floor life is set by the plant from its own process capability."},{"id":"c","text":"Shelf life runs in the sealed pack under stated conditions; floor life runs once exposed to factory ambient."},{"id":"d","text":"Shelf life and floor life are two names for the same interval, counted from different start dates."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Shelf life is the time a material may stay in its original sealed packaging under specified storage conditions before requalification. Floor life is the time a moisture-sensitive item may stay exposed to factory ambient before it must be reconditioned or soldered. They are different intervals with different conditions, not synonyms and not a division between components and boards.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m01l06-04","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l06","prompt":"A bare-board lot shows 18 of 250 inspected boards with twist above the drawing limit, one moisture barrier bag with a failed humidity indicator, and no certificate of conformance. What is the correct disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The lot is released on the 232 conforming boards, with the 18 affected boards segregated and scrapped."},{"id":"b","text":"The lot is released because the missing certificate is only an administrative matter and the twist is still within the Class 2 limit."},{"id":"c","text":"The lot is scrapped without review, because any failed humidity indicator condemns the whole delivery."},{"id":"d","text":"The lot cannot be released: flatness, packaging and documentation all fail, and the boards are affected."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Three separate requirements fail at once: planarity against the drawing limit, packaging integrity through the indicator card, and documentation through the absent certificate. That is a nonconformance requiring a documented disposition, not a partial release on the conforming boards, not an administrative waiver, and not an automatic scrap before the material review board has seen it.","source":"src-018"},{"questionId":"q-m01l06-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l06","prompt":"A board measures a bow of 1.2 mm across a 200 mm span. Using the IPC-6012 bow limit of 0.75 % of the longer dimension for an SMT board, what is the measured bow and the disposition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.60 %: within the 0.75 % limit, so the board is acceptable on this characteristic."},{"id":"b","text":"0.60 %: above the 0.75 % limit, so the board is rejected on this characteristic."},{"id":"c","text":"1.20 %: above the 0.75 % limit, so the board is rejected on this characteristic."},{"id":"d","text":"0.06 %: within the 0.75 % limit, so the board is acceptable on this characteristic."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Bow percentage = 1.2 / 200 x 100 = 0.60 %. That is below the 0.75 % SMT limit, so the board passes on flatness. The 1.20 % answer comes from dividing by 100 mm, the 0.06 % answer from dividing millimetres by millimetres and forgetting the percentage, and the second option applies the right number to the wrong conclusion.","source":"src-018"},{"questionId":"q-m01l06-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l06","prompt":"Why is a bare-board surface finish recorded with its type and thickness at receipt rather than only as \"finished\"?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The finish thickness is needed to calculate the copper current-carrying capacity of the board."},{"id":"b","text":"Finish type and thickness determine solderability, storage life and compatibility with the paste."},{"id":"c","text":"The finish type is a marketing descriptor, and the assembly behaviour of a board depends only on the laminate underneath it."},{"id":"d","text":"Recording it is a purchasing requirement, and it has no effect on how the board is assembled."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson requires the finish to be identified unambiguously, with thickness or coating class where applicable, because it drives solderability, shelf life and paste compatibility. Copper current capacity comes from conductor thickness, not finish thickness, and finish choice does affect assembly.","source":"src-018"},{"questionId":"q-m01l06-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m01l06","prompt":"A supplier writes \"boards are bent\" in its nonconformance reply. How does the lesson want the record rewritten?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"With a photograph attached, since an image of the deviation is stronger evidence for the supplier than measured numbers."},{"id":"b","text":"With the name of the inspector and the date, so responsibility for the finding is documented."},{"id":"c","text":"With lot, sample size, measured result, the drawing limit it violates and the action requested."},{"id":"d","text":"With the purchase order value, so the commercial impact of the deviation is quantified first."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson contrast is concrete: instead of \"boards are bent\", the record states lot 4471, 12 of 250 boards measured, twist above the IPC-6012 Class 3 drawing limit, and the requested action. A photograph supports that record but does not replace the measurement, and inspector details or order value do not make the deviation reproducible for the supplier.","source":"src-018"},{"questionId":"q-m01l06-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l06","prompt":"A board meets the drawing flatness limit but is being assembled with a fine-pitch press-fit connector. What does the lesson require?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept the board without further check, because meeting the drawing limit closes the question."},{"id":"b","text":"Reject the board, because a press-fit connector always needs a flatter board than the drawing."},{"id":"c","text":"Re-measure against the component datasheet instead of against the board drawing or standard."},{"id":"d","text":"Check the assembly process need, which may demand tighter limits than the generic board limit."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson notes that fine-pitch components, press-fit connectors and large area arrays may require tighter limits than the generic board limits, and that the required flatness must be verified against the placement machine datasheet, stencil release and reflow support method. So the drawing limit is necessary but may not be sufficient — and none of the other options follows: the board is not automatically rejected, and the drawing is not discarded.","source":"src-018"},{"questionId":"q-m01l07-03","type":"single","competencies":["K9","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l07","prompt":"Which list correctly names the dispositions available to a material review board?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept, rework, sort, use as is with a concession, return, and scrap."},{"id":"b","text":"Quarantine, hold, escalate, re-inspect, and release after a supplier visit."},{"id":"c","text":"Accept, quarantine, reject, and scrap, with no intermediate categories."},{"id":"d","text":"Rework, resample, re-bake, resize, and re-certify the affected lot."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson lists the MRB dispositions as accept, rework, sort, use as is with a concession, return and scrap, and requires each one to name who approved it and what verification precedes release. Quarantine is the controlled hold before the review, not one of its outcomes, and re-baking or re-sampling are actions taken to establish facts rather than dispositions of the material.","source":"src-037"},{"questionId":"q-m01l07-04","type":"single","competencies":["K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l07","prompt":"A supplier notification has to support a corrective action. Which version does that?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A description of the quality problem together with a request that the supplier investigate it and respond within a short time."},{"id":"b","text":"Part, lot, purchase order, sample basis, measured result against the requirement, and evidence attached."},{"id":"c","text":"A statement that the material does not meet requirements, plus the return authorisation number."},{"id":"d","text":"The complaint history of the supplier and the number of similar findings in the last year."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson requires objectivity and specificity so the supplier can trace the batch, reproduce the finding and prevent recurrence. A general description, a bare nonconformity statement, or a complaint history does not let the supplier reproduce the measurement, which is what closes the loop.","source":"src-037"},{"questionId":"q-m01l07-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K9","K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l07","prompt":"A lot of 1,000 chip resistors arrives on 20 reels. The plan samples 4 reels and 100 units. Five units fall outside the resistance tolerance and the internal limit for electrical nonconformance is 2 %. What is the observed rate and its classification?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"5.0 %: below the 2 % internal limit, so the electrical finding is a quarantine pending review only."},{"id":"b","text":"0.5 %: below the 2 % internal limit, so the electrical finding is acceptable and the lot can be released to the line today."},{"id":"c","text":"5.0 %: above the 2 % internal limit, so the electrical finding is a reject unless the MRB agrees otherwise."},{"id":"d","text":"20.0 %: above the 2 % internal limit, so the whole lot is rejected without material review."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The defect rate is 5 units / 100 inspected = 5 %, which is above the 2 % internal limit, so the electrical finding classifies as reject unless the MRB accepts documented risk. The 0.5 % answer divides by the lot size instead of the sample, and the 20.0 % answer divides by the 5 defective units. The lot still goes through MRB, which is what the other distractors get wrong.","source":"src-037"},{"questionId":"q-m01l07-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m01l07","prompt":"What makes quarantine different from simply putting material aside?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The material is physically moved to a separate room, which is what defines a quarantine."},{"id":"b","text":"The material is booked out of stock in the ERP, so production planning cannot see it in the materials system at all."},{"id":"c","text":"The supplier is notified, and the notice itself places the lot under quarantine status."},{"id":"d","text":"The hold is labelled, traceable to the receiving transaction and released only by a decision."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Quarantine is a controlled hold: physically segregated or system-held, labelled with part, lot, quantity, date and reason, traceable to the receiving transaction, and released only through a recorded decision. A separate room without traceability, a stock booking, or a supplier notice is not a controlled hold.","source":"src-037"},{"questionId":"q-m01l07-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m01l07","prompt":"A lot of moisture-sensitive components arrives with crushed vacuum bags and no humidity indicator cards, while the parts look intact. What is the correct immediate step?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Hold the lot and verify packaging integrity, moisture status and traceability before any release."},{"id":"b","text":"Release the lot to stores, since an intact part is the only thing the assembly process can see."},{"id":"c","text":"Return the lot at once, because damaged packaging is never an acceptable delivery condition."},{"id":"d","text":"Move the lot to the MRB as scrap, because moisture status cannot be reconstructed after the fact."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The packaging carries the evidence about moisture exposure, so crushed bags without indicator cards leave the material status unclear — the textbook quarantine case. Releasing on appearance ignores the missing evidence, scrapping before review wastes usable material, and returning is a disposition decision that needs the MRB, not an automatic first step.","source":"src-037"},{"questionId":"q-m01l07-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m01l07","prompt":"The MRB releases part of a held lot to the line with special handling notes. What must happen for the release to be controlled?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The line is told verbally at the shift handover, because the hold is already closed in the quality system by then."},{"id":"b","text":"The line receives the approved status with the handling notes, and the record names who approved it."},{"id":"c","text":"The release is recorded in the inspection log only, since the line works from the work order."},{"id":"d","text":"The supplier is informed of the release, because the supplier owns the nonconformance record."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson closes on the handoff: if material is released, the line must receive the approved status and any special handling notes, and the disposition must state who approved it and what verification is required. A verbal handover, a log entry nobody on the line reads, or a supplier notification does not carry the conditions of release to the people who will run the material.","source":"src-037"},{"questionId":"q-m02l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l01","prompt":"Why can a plastic package that passed final test at the supplier still be damaged by the customer reflow oven?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Absorbed moisture vaporises during reflow, and the expanding vapour can separate internal interfaces."},{"id":"b","text":"The reflow oven runs hotter than the supplier used, so the mould compound softens and loses its shape."},{"id":"c","text":"The package was not tested at all by the supplier, so its moisture state at receipt is unknown."},{"id":"d","text":"Reflow melts the solder inside the package, and the expansion of that solder cracks the body."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The damage mechanism is the popcorn effect: hygroscopic mould compound, die attach and substrate take up water vapour during ambient exposure, and the vapour expands rapidly when the package heats in reflow, separating internal interfaces. The failure can be invisible from outside. It is not caused by the oven being hotter than the supplier used, by a missing test, or by internal solder melting.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l01","prompt":"Which change would most increase the amount of moisture an unopened package absorbs before it reaches the line?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Running the reflow profile with a slower ramp so the package spends more time in the oven."},{"id":"b","text":"Storing it in the open at high relative humidity instead of in a sealed bag with desiccant."},{"id":"c","text":"Handling it with a wrist strap that is not tested daily, so the charge path is uncontrolled."},{"id":"d","text":"Using a stencil with a lower area ratio, which reduces the paste volume under the package."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Uptake depends on storage temperature, relative humidity, exposure time and the material system, so an open, humid store is the dominant factor. Profile ramp affects the damage a given moisture content does, not how much moisture is absorbed; a wrist strap concerns electrostatic discharge; stencil geometry concerns paste volume. None of the last three changes the absorbed water.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l01-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K8"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l01","prompt":"An MSL 3 device with a 168 h floor life is reflowed 70 h after the bag was opened, which is inside its floor life. Forty hours later a second reflow is planned. What floor life remains before that second reflow?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"98 h remain, because the two exposure periods are added and subtracted from 168 h in one go."},{"id":"b","text":"168 h remain, because a device that has already survived one reflow cannot absorb more moisture."},{"id":"c","text":"128 h remain, because the qualifying first reflow reset the clock and 40 h have been used since."},{"id":"d","text":"138 h remain, because only the time after the first reflow is counted and 30 h are allowed for."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"A qualifying reflow completed within the remaining floor life resets the clock, so the 70 h before it is not carried forward. From the reset, 40 h have been used, leaving 168 - 40 = 128 h. Adding both exposure periods ignores the reset; assuming the device cannot reabsorb moisture after a reflow contradicts the lesson, which says it can; and 138 h does not follow from any figure in the case.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l01","prompt":"What does the moisture sensitivity level of a package actually express?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"How long the package may sit on a shelf before its solderability degrades past acceptance."},{"id":"b","text":"How sensitive the die is to electrostatic discharge during handling and machine placement."},{"id":"c","text":"How much moisture the part can absorb before its electrical parameters drift out of tolerance."},{"id":"d","text":"How much ambient exposure the package tolerates before reflow is likely to damage it."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"MSL is a reflow-risk classification: it is the ambient exposure budget before a reflow cycle is likely to cause package damage. The lesson separates it explicitly from shelf life, from ESD classification and from solderability life, and the failure mode it governs is mechanical (interface separation), not electrical drift.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1","K8"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m02l01","prompt":"A returned board shows a hairline crack along the package edge and a lifted lead, with no evidence of mechanical handling damage. What is the most probable cause?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Moisture-driven interface separation during reflow, often with no mark on the body."},{"id":"b","text":"Electrostatic discharge through the lead frame, which typically shows as a cracked body edge."},{"id":"c","text":"Excess solder paste under the lead, which lifts the lead as the deposit expands in reflow."},{"id":"d","text":"A placement force above the component limit, which deforms the lead frame before reflow."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson gives package cracking and lifted leads as visible signs of the popcorn effect, and stresses that the damage is often internal and invisible. ESD damage is electrical, not a cracked body edge; paste volume does not lift leads this way; and a placement-force signature would show as a deformed lead at the tip, with handling damage evidence.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l01-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l01","prompt":"An inspector cannot read the MSL marking on a reel and the bag label is missing. What is the correct working assumption?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Treat the device as MSL 1, since an unmarked part is normally the least sensitive type."},{"id":"b","text":"Treat the device as the most restrictive applicable level until the rating is verified."},{"id":"c","text":"Use the level of the previous lot of the same part number, which is close enough in practice."},{"id":"d","text":"Proceed without a level, because the floor-life clock cannot be started without one."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson is explicit: if the marking is missing or unclear, use the datasheet or treat the device as the most restrictive applicable MSL until it is verified. Assuming the least sensitive class, borrowing a neighbouring lot value, or running blind all convert an unknown risk into an uncontrolled one.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l02","prompt":"Which floor life does the lesson give for an MSL 4 plastic package at the J-STD-033 reference conditions?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"168 hours of ambient exposure"},{"id":"b","text":"48 hours of ambient exposure"},{"id":"c","text":"72 hours of ambient exposure"},{"id":"d","text":"24 hours of ambient exposure"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson table gives MSL 3 = 168 h, MSL 4 = 72 h, MSL 5 = 48 h and MSL 5a = 24 h at 30 °C and 60 % RH. Confusing the 168 h of MSL 3 with MSL 4 is the most common slip, because MSL 3 is the level most often quoted in training material.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K8"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l02","prompt":"The bag label states a 168 h floor life; the datasheet for the same part states 72 h. The bag was opened 60 h ago. What do you do?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Use the 168 h value, because the bag label is applied to this specific lot by the supplier."},{"id":"b","text":"Average the two values to 120 h, leaving 60 h, and record the arbitration in the batch log."},{"id":"c","text":"Use the 168 h value but add a safety factor of one hour, giving 59 h of remaining floor life."},{"id":"d","text":"Use the 72 h value, so 12 h remain, and quarantine the batch until the conflict is healed."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson rule is to use the most restrictive valid source when sources disagree, and to quarantine until the discrepancy is resolved — so 72 h governs and 72 - 60 = 12 h remain. Preferring the label, averaging, or padding the larger value all take the decision on the side of more exposure, which is the direction the rule exists to prevent.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l02-05","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l02","prompt":"When exactly does the floor-life clock start for a dry-packed device?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"When the moisture barrier bag is opened and the device is exposed to ambient air."},{"id":"b","text":"When the lot is received and the receiving inspection record is signed at the dock."},{"id":"c","text":"When the reel is mounted on the placement machine and the feeder is closed."},{"id":"d","text":"When the device enters the reflow oven for the first time in the build."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The clock starts at bag opening, not at receipt, at feeder load or at reflow. Receipt can precede the opening by weeks or months, and a device can sit exposed on a feeder for a shift without ever seeing an oven — which is why the lesson treats feeder and line time as ambient exposure.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l02","prompt":"What does a humidity indicator card inside a moisture barrier bag tell you?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"How many hours of floor life the devices inside have already consumed in total."},{"id":"b","text":"Whether the bag interior has gone beyond the humidity range the desiccant was sized for."},{"id":"c","text":"Whether the desiccant has been replaced since the bag was packed by the supplier."},{"id":"d","text":"The exact moisture content of the mould compound, measured in parts per million."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The card is a threshold indicator for the bag interior: a spot above its limit means the environment the parts were stored in was worse than intended, so the devices are quarantined and evaluated. It is not a timer, it does not record desiccant history, and it does not measure package moisture content.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1","K8"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m02l02","prompt":"A bag has been open for 30 h against a 72 h floor life, but the reel has sat on an open feeder for the last 20 h of that time. How should the exposure be counted?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Only the 10 h not spent on the feeder count, because a feeder keeps the reel off the floor."},{"id":"b","text":"Only the time in the open air of the production room counts, so 20 h is the working figure."},{"id":"c","text":"All 30 h count, because feeder time is ambient exposure unless dry storage is documented."},{"id":"d","text":"Neither period counts once the device is on a machine, because the clock stops at feeder load."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson says time on the line, in a dry cabinet or on a feeder counts as ambient exposure unless the controlled-storage procedure explicitly allows otherwise. So the full 30 h is consumed and 42 h remain. A feeder is not a dry environment, and there is no rule that stops the clock at machine load.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l02-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l02","prompt":"A device was reflowed once while 20 h of floor life remained, and it has since been exposed for 30 h. What is the position before its second reflow?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The first reflow used up the remaining floor life, so the device is scrap with no further options."},{"id":"b","text":"Because one reflow succeeded, the device is proven robust and may be reflowed without further control."},{"id":"c","text":"The exposure after reflow is irrelevant, because each reflow cycle starts from the original rating."},{"id":"d","text":"The first reflow was fine, but the device has reabsorbed moisture since and cannot run as it stands."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"A reflow completed inside the remaining floor life is acceptable from a moisture standpoint, but the lesson warns that the device can reabsorb moisture afterwards, so the exposure since that reflow has to be checked before the next one. One successful reflow proves nothing about robustness, and the allowance does not restart itself per cycle.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K8"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l03","prompt":"Which list correctly names time that accumulates against a device's floor life?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Storage outside the bag, assembly handling, feeder time, inspection and reflow time."},{"id":"b","text":"Shelf time in the sealed bag, plus any period in which the device was in a dry cabinet."},{"id":"c","text":"Only the time the device spends on the placement machine and inside the reflow oven."},{"id":"d","text":"Only the time recorded on the bag-open label, since other periods are not traceable."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Floor life accumulates whenever the device is outside a compliant moisture barrier bag: storage outside it, handling, feeder and machine time, the oven and conveyor, inspection and waiting between operations. Sealed-bag shelf time and qualified dry-cabinet time are what the lesson treats as protected. Counting only machine time, or only the label entry, understates the budget.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l03","prompt":"What does it take to reset a device's floor life?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Putting the reel back into a moisture barrier bag and sealing it at the end of the shift."},{"id":"b","text":"A qualifying bake per the applicable bake table, then correct repacking with desiccant."},{"id":"c","text":"Recording the bag-open time again once the reel has been returned to the storage cabinet."},{"id":"d","text":"Running the device through a reflow oven again, since reflow drives the moisture out."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson is explicit that a reset is not automatic when a device is put back into a bag: it requires a bake qualified for the package and MSL, followed by correct repacking with desiccant and a humidity indicator card, and recorded with start and end times. Rebagging, re-dating or a further reflow does not restore the budget and a reflow is what the budget protects against.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K8"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l03","prompt":"An MSL 3 reel was bagged out 100 h ago, and the MES shows 6 h of open-air reflow handling. Storage stayed within the J-STD-033 reference condition. What cumulative exposure and remaining floor life apply?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"100 h consumed, 68 h remain of the 168 h floor life."},{"id":"b","text":"94 h consumed, 74 h remain of the 168 h floor life."},{"id":"c","text":"106 h consumed, 62 h remain of the 168 h floor life."},{"id":"d","text":"106 h consumed, 62 h remain of the 72 h floor life."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Cumulative exposure = 100 h bagged-out time + 6 h reflow handling = 106 h, and 168 - 106 = 62 h remain. Using only the bag time forgets the handling; subtracting the handling instead of adding it moves the number the wrong way; and 72 h is the MSL 4 floor life, not the MSL 3 value that governs this reel.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l03","prompt":"How is floor life different from shelf life?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Floor life is measured in reflow cycles; shelf life is measured in calendar months."},{"id":"b","text":"Floor life applies to bare boards; shelf life applies to moisture-sensitive components."},{"id":"c","text":"Floor life is set by the customer contract; shelf life is set by the IPC handling standard."},{"id":"d","text":"Floor life is an exposure budget outside the bag; shelf life is time in the sealed pack."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson states the distinction directly: floor life is the cumulative time a device may stay outside its barrier bag under factory conditions, while shelf life is time in the original sealed packaging under specified storage. Neither is counted in reflow cycles, both apply to moisture-sensitive devices rather than boards alone, and the source of each is the supplier datasheet and J-STD-033, not a contract split.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1","K8"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m02l03","prompt":"No bag-open time was recorded for a batch, but the reel labels show it has been in stores for two months. What is the sound engineering position?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Bound the exposure from the evidence, quarantine the batch, then decide bake or scrap."},{"id":"b","text":"Assume the bag stayed sealed until now, so the full floor life is available from today."},{"id":"c","text":"Start the clock today and log the reel, because an unrecorded period cannot be counted."},{"id":"d","text":"Use the reel as normal and add the missing hours to the next lot of the same device."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson says that when records are incomplete the exposure must be bounded and the material quarantined, then bake, supplier evaluation or scrap decided. Assuming the bag stayed sealed back-dates nothing and converts an unknown into a comfortable answer, starting the clock today does the same, and carrying the hours to another lot moves a risk onto different material.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l03-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l03","prompt":"Which record set makes floor-life accounting auditable for one reel?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Part number, receiving date, invoice number, supplier name and the inspector signature."},{"id":"b","text":"Part and lot, MSL, bag-open time, cumulative open-air time, storage RH, bake history."},{"id":"c","text":"Reel serial number, feeder position, machine program revision and the operator badge id."},{"id":"d","text":"MSL, package type, test date of the wrist strap and the calibration date of the oven."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson lists the minimum useful records as part and lot or date code, MSL, bag-open time, current cumulative open-air time, storage temperature and humidity, desiccant and indicator status, bake records, and reflow or other handling history. The other three lists mix commercial, placement or calibration data that does not let anyone recompute the remaining budget.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l04","prompt":"Which combination of inputs does a bake decision actually require?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Part number, purchase price, supplier rating and the remaining shelf life of the paste."},{"id":"b","text":"Board thickness, copper weight, oven zone count and the conveyor speed of the line."},{"id":"c","text":"MSL, package type, bag status, indicator and desiccant state, and time left to reflow."},{"id":"d","text":"Reel quantity, feeder count, shift pattern and the capacity of the dry cabinet."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson opens by rejecting part name alone and lists the real inputs: moisture sensitivity level, package type, whether the barrier bag was opened, dry-pack integrity, humidity indicator and desiccant condition, and the planned time to reflow. Board, oven and feeder logistics matter to the plan but none of them enters the moisture decision.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l04-04","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K8"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m02l04","prompt":"An MSL 4 tape-and-reel batch was opened 50 h ago, the dry cabinet does not qualify for clock suspension, and reflow starts in 30 h. At 40 °C the applicable bake for a body up to 1.4 mm takes about 9 days. What is the position?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The 72 h floor life covers the whole 80 h of remaining schedule, so no bake is needed at all."},{"id":"b","text":"The batch must be baked at 40 °C for 9 days, so the reflow window simply moves by that amount."},{"id":"c","text":"The floor life resets once the reel enters the cabinet, so the remaining 30 h can be planned."},{"id":"d","text":"Only 22 h of floor life remain against a 30 h wait, and the 9-day bake does not fit."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Remaining floor life is 72 - 50 = 22 h against a 30 h wait, so the batch cannot simply proceed; and the low-temperature tape-and-reel bake takes about 9 days, which does not fit a 30 h window. The lesson offers the real options instead: move the reflow inside the remaining floor life, suspend the clock in qualified dry storage, or de-reel to a carrier that tolerates a higher bake temperature.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l04-05","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K8"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l04","prompt":"In which case does the lesson say to quarantine rather than to bake?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The MSL is unknown, the package is damaged, or no bake recipe is qualified for that package."},{"id":"b","text":"The humidity indicator card is above its threshold and the desiccant appears to be exhausted."},{"id":"c","text":"The batch has exceeded its floor life and the reflow is planned inside the next shift."},{"id":"d","text":"The dry cabinet was found out of range for several hours during the weekend break."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Bake is the remedy when exposure or packaging failed but the material and the recipe are sound. Quarantine applies when the MSL is unknown, the package is damaged, the recipe is not qualified for the package, or the device has already been reflowed and the decision needs review. An exceeded indicator, an expired floor life and an out-of-range cabinet are bake triggers, not quarantine triggers.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l04","prompt":"Why can qualified dry storage extend the time a moisture-sensitive device may wait before reflow?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The cabinet removes moisture from the device, so any earlier exposure is baked out of the package."},{"id":"b","text":"At a low enough relative humidity the device stops gaining moisture, so exposure is suspended."},{"id":"c","text":"Dry storage protects the device from electrostatic discharge, which is the main reflow risk."},{"id":"d","text":"The cabinet keeps the reel cold, and cold storage always stops the floor-life clock."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson describes dry storage as suspending or extending exposure for eligible devices, at a low relative humidity and with the cabinet calibrated, alarmed and logged; time in an out-of-range cabinet counts as floor life. A cabinet does not bake moisture out, it is not an ESD control, and cold alone is not the criterion.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1","K8"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m02l04","prompt":"A tape-and-reel batch needs a bake, but the tape is only rated to 40 °C and the schedule needs the parts within a day. Which route does the lesson point to?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Bake the reel at 125 °C for the shortest time in the table, accepting the tape deformation risk."},{"id":"b","text":"Skip the bake and add 10 °C to the reflow peak to drive the moisture out during soldering."},{"id":"c","text":"De-reel into a carrier that tolerates a hotter bake, or move the reflow inside floor life."},{"id":"d","text":"Skip the bake and run the batch at normal settings, then inspect the packages after reflow."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson treats the 40 °C limit on tape-and-reel as a real constraint and offers de-reeling to a higher-temperature carrier, clock suspension in qualified dry storage, or moving the reflow inside the remaining floor life. Exceeding the carrier temperature rating damages the tape and is not offered as a trade; raising the peak adds thermal stress instead of removing moisture; and reflowing an unbaked batch then inspecting turns a known risk into a detection gamble.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l04-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l04","prompt":"A batch is released without a bake. Which record makes that release defensible later?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A note that the batch looked dry and the reel labels were intact at the time of release."},{"id":"b","text":"The supervisor approval only, since the technical values are already in the datasheet."},{"id":"c","text":"The bake table reference itself, because it states when a bake would have been needed."},{"id":"d","text":"The remaining floor life at release and the planned reflow window, with the MSL and lot."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson requires that when a batch is released without bake, the remaining floor life and the planned reflow window are recorded. Appearance and intact labels are impressions, not values; an approval without the numbers cannot be audited; and citing the table shows the rule without showing that this batch met it.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l05","prompt":"Which statement matches how the lesson frames an ESD control program?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A documented management system with defined areas, controls and records, not gadgets."},{"id":"b","text":"A purchasing standard for packaging materials, applied mainly to outgoing shipments."},{"id":"c","text":"A maintenance schedule for ionizers and grounding rods, owned by the facilities team."},{"id":"d","text":"A training requirement for operators who handle boards, verified once at induction."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson opens by insisting that a programme is a management system: the protected area is defined by the materials, the part sensitivity, the packaging and the people and equipment that move them, and both S20.20 and IEC 61340-5-1 require written procedures. Packaging purchasing, equipment maintenance and induction training are parts of a programme, not its definition.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m02l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l05","prompt":"For which items is ionization the control the lesson points to?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Workers at a bench, in place of a wrist strap that has failed its daily test."},{"id":"b","text":"Isolated conductors and insulators that cannot be grounded in the protected area."},{"id":"c","text":"Boards inside a shielding bag, where humidity control is not practical to maintain."},{"id":"d","text":"Storage racks, in place of the equipotential bonding that ties them to ground."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Ionization neutralises charge on isolated conductors and insulators, which by definition cannot be drained by grounding. It is not a substitute for personnel grounding, for shielding packaging, or for the bonding network: the lesson keeps those as separate controls with their own standards and their own verification.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m02l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l05","prompt":"An audit scores control strength from 0 to 3, exposure from 1 to 5 and sensitivity from 1 to 5, with risk = (3 - control) x exposure x sensitivity. A missing grounding jumper scores 0, 5 and 4. What is its risk score?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"20"},{"id":"b","text":"24"},{"id":"c","text":"60"},{"id":"d","text":"180"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Risk = (3 - 0) x 5 x 4 = 60. Dropping the (3 - control) term gives 20, which is the same answer a much weaker gap would produce; 24 corresponds to a control strength of 1 with exposure 4 and sensitivity 3; and 180 multiplies the raw control value instead of its complement.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m02l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l05","prompt":"What is the purpose of equipotential bonding in a protected area?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"To drain charge from insulators such as plastic housings and tape that cannot conduct."},{"id":"b","text":"To provide a low-resistance path that discharges a board directly to earth in one step."},{"id":"c","text":"To power the ionizers that neutralise the air around sensitive assemblies."},{"id":"d","text":"To keep conductive items at a common potential, so charge cannot flow on contact."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Bonding keeps conductive items at a common potential, so no difference exists to drive a discharge when they touch, and the network must be continuous and inspectable. Insulators are handled by ionization, not by bonding; a board is drained through controlled paths rather than a single hard earth connection; and ionizers are powered independently.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m02l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m02l05","prompt":"An audit finds a mat in place but never tested, on a bench where sensitive parts move all day. Why does this outrank a missing ionizer balance record elsewhere?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Its risk score is higher: many sensitive parts pass an unverified control daily."},{"id":"b","text":"Mats are always more important than ionizers in any programme, whatever the exposure."},{"id":"c","text":"An untested mat is a documentation gap, while a missing ionizer record is a control failure."},{"id":"d","text":"Ionizers are only required when the relative humidity of the room falls below the setpoint."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson ranks gaps by (control weakness) x exposure x sensitivity, so an unverified control under daily traffic scores higher than a record gap on a lower-traffic control. The ranking is not a fixed hierarchy between control types, and an untested mat is a control failure rather than a paperwork issue. Ionization requirements are set by the programme, not by a single humidity setpoint.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m02l05-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l05","prompt":"A storage area holds parts in shielding bags on a metal rack that is not bonded to the EPA ground. What is the technical concern?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The shielding bags will lose their metallised layer over time if the rack is not grounded."},{"id":"b","text":"The rack can hold a charge and discharge into parts when a bag is handled or placed on it."},{"id":"c","text":"Unbonded metal attracts moisture, so the parts inside the bags will exceed their floor life."},{"id":"d","text":"Nothing: a shielding bag makes the potential of the surface underneath it irrelevant."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"An unbonded conductor is exactly the isolated conductor the lesson warns about: it can hold charge and discharge into whatever touches it, including a bag being placed on it. Grounding does not preserve the metallised layer, metal does not attract moisture in a way that consumes floor life, and a bag protects its contents from external fields only while its own integrity and handling are controlled.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m02l06-03","type":"single","competencies":["K1","K8"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l06","prompt":"Which units belong in the traceability range when expired floor life is found mid-lot?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Only the reels currently mounted on feeders, since the remaining reels are still bagged."},{"id":"b","text":"Only the boards already populated, because unpopulated reels have not been exposed yet."},{"id":"c","text":"Every unit sharing the lot, bag, cabinet, handling window, feeder and reflow profile."},{"id":"d","text":"Every unit of that part number in the plant, regardless of lot or storage location."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson defines the range by shared conditions rather than by physical proximity: same lot or date code, same storage location and cabinet, same vacuum bag, shift, feeder, work order, oven and profile. Reels on feeders alone understates the range, boards alone ignores the device risk, and the whole part number overstates it to material that never shared the exposure.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l06-04","type":"calc","competencies":["K1","K8"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l06","prompt":"An MSL 3 lot with a 168 h floor life was bagged out 200 h before the planned reflow, and the dry cabinet sat at 7 % RH against a 5 % RH requirement. By how much is the floor life exceeded?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0 h, because the cabinet was below 10 % RH and therefore suspended the clock."},{"id":"b","text":"168 h, because an out-of-specification cabinet resets the exposure to zero."},{"id":"c","text":"32 h, but only if the humidity indicator card is also above its threshold."},{"id":"d","text":"32 h, because the cabinet did not qualify and the full 200 h counts."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The cabinet did not meet the dry-storage requirement stated in this lesson, so the exposure cannot be discounted: 200 - 168 = 32 h over. A 10 % RH cabinet is not a suspension condition at any point in this course: it only slows the clock, and the lesson requires 5 % RH or less before exposure is discounted; a cabinet out of range does not reset anything; and the indicator card is separate evidence, not a condition for counting the excess.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l06-05","type":"single","competencies":["K8","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l06","prompt":"A moisture breach is confirmed mid-lot. What is the correct first move on the line?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Stop feeding the affected reels and quarantine all devices from the shared conditions."},{"id":"b","text":"Finish the current panel and then hold the remaining reels until the shift handover."},{"id":"c","text":"Continue the build and add an X-ray check at the end of line so the parts are inspected."},{"id":"d","text":"Return the affected reels to the cabinet, which restores their floor life for the next shift."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson says to stop feeding the affected reels and trays and quarantine everything from the same lot, bag, cabinet and handling window, and not to reflow the affected batch until the bake decision is documented. Finishing the panel adds exposed material to the suspect range, X-ray at the end of line cannot recover an interface that has already separated, and returning to a cabinet does not restore floor life.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l06-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K8"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m02l06","prompt":"Which three outcomes does the lesson allow for a batch found with expired floor life?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Ship as is, sort by visual appearance, or re-test the lot electrically before use."},{"id":"b","text":"Bake and repackage, scrap, or release after the records and disposition are complete."},{"id":"c","text":"Extend the floor life by approval, reflow at a higher peak, or hold until the next audit."},{"id":"d","text":"Return to the supplier, blend with a fresh lot, or re-label the reels with a new date."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson closes with three outcomes: bake and repackage if the package allows it, scrap if baking is not allowed or the package is damaged, or release only after the bake record, the dry-storage record and the traceability disposition are complete. None of the alternatives removes or documents the exposure.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m02l06-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K8","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m02l06","prompt":"An ESD wrist-strap log shows a gap for one shift while boards were being handled. What is the containment range?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Only the boards that later fail electrical test, since ESD damage either shows or does not."},{"id":"b","text":"The wrist strap and its cord, which are the only items proven to have been uncontrolled."},{"id":"c","text":"All boards handled in the protected area during the breach window, plus the carriers used."},{"id":"d","text":"All boards of that work order, because the operator was assigned to the whole order."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson defines the ESD containment range by the breach window: everything handled in the EPA during it, plus feeders, magazines and carriers that moved through it. Limiting it to electrical failures fails because ESD damage can be latent, and neither the strap alone nor the whole work order matches the window in which control was lost.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m02l06-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K1","K8"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m02l06","prompt":"A batch cannot be baked because its carrier will not tolerate the temperature and cannot be de-reeled in time. What follows?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Run the batch and rely on the humidity indicator card as the release criterion."},{"id":"b","text":"The batch may run if the reflow peak is reduced, since lower temperature needs less moisture."},{"id":"c","text":"Store the batch in the cabinet indefinitely until a bake recipe is qualified later."},{"id":"d","text":"Scrap is the remaining outcome, because the exposure cannot be removed or qualified."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson names scrap as the outcome when baking is not allowed or the package is damaged — the exposure has to be removed or made defensible, and if neither is possible the material cannot be released. An indicator card is not a release criterion for an expired budget, a lower peak still leaves moisture in the interface, and indefinite storage does not qualify the batch.","source":"src-003"},{"questionId":"q-m03l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l01","prompt":"What does the area ratio of a stencil aperture compare?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The aperture opening area against the sidewall area that holds the paste back."},{"id":"b","text":"The pad area against the aperture area, which shows how much the print is reduced."},{"id":"c","text":"The aperture width against the foil thickness, which sets the narrowest paste path."},{"id":"d","text":"The deposited paste volume against the volume the aperture could theoretically hold."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Area ratio is opening area divided by sidewall area, and for a rectangle the sidewall is perimeter times foil thickness. Pad-to-aperture ratio is area reduction, width over thickness is aspect ratio, and deposit over theoretical volume is transfer efficiency — three quantities that are easy to swap in conversation and are measured for different reasons.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l01","prompt":"Why does the lesson use the smaller aperture dimension when it calculates the aspect ratio?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The smaller dimension is the one the laser can cut most accurately at speed."},{"id":"b","text":"The smaller dimension is the narrowest path the paste has to leave through."},{"id":"c","text":"The smaller dimension always runs across the pads rather than along them."},{"id":"d","text":"The smaller dimension is the one that decides the foil thickness selection."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Aspect ratio describes how deep and narrow the aperture is, and the narrowest dimension controls the release path, which is why the smaller one is used. Laser accuracy, pad orientation and foil choice matter to the process, but none of them is the reason the definition picks the small dimension.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l01-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l01","prompt":"A chip pad of 0.50 mm by 0.40 mm is printed through 0.12 mm foil with a transfer efficiency of 0.90. What is the area ratio and the expected deposit volume?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Area ratio 0.93 and expected volume 0.0240 mm³"},{"id":"b","text":"Area ratio 1.04 and expected volume 0.0216 mm³"},{"id":"c","text":"Area ratio 0.93 and expected volume 0.0216 mm³"},{"id":"d","text":"Area ratio 3.33 and expected volume 0.0216 mm³"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Area = 0.50 x 0.40 = 0.20 mm²; perimeter = 1.80 mm; sidewall = 1.80 x 0.12 = 0.216 mm²; area ratio = 0.20 / 0.216 = 0.926. Nominal volume = 0.20 x 0.12 = 0.024 mm³ and, at 0.90 transfer efficiency, expected volume = 0.0216 mm³. Leaving out the transfer efficiency gives 0.0240; using the perimeter without doubling the sides gives 1.04; and 3.33 is the aspect ratio, not the area ratio.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l01","prompt":"Why does the lesson insist on defining the joint requirement before the aperture is drawn?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The aperture size is fixed by the manufacturer of the printer rather than by the design."},{"id":"b","text":"Aperture drawing is a purchasing step, so the joint data only matters at first article."},{"id":"c","text":"The joint requirement only sets the reflow profile, and the aperture follows the pad area."},{"id":"d","text":"The aperture has to deliver enough paste for the fillet while staying inside the pad."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The volume budget is what makes the aperture a calculated object: enough paste to wet the termination and form a reliable fillet, not so much that adjacent pads bridge at reflow, and within the pad outline. Printer capability and first-article checks constrain the choice but do not replace the budget, and the reflow profile does not set the deposit geometry.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l01","prompt":"Area ratio is acceptable but the expected deposit volume is short of the joint requirement. Which change is the lesson's first lever?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Increase foil thickness, then recheck the area ratio for the same aperture."},{"id":"b","text":"Reduce the transfer efficiency assumption until the volume matches the target."},{"id":"c","text":"Increase the squeegee pressure so more paste is pushed into each aperture."},{"id":"d","text":"Reduce the aperture area, which raises the ratio of wall contact to deposit."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Volume rises roughly with aperture area times foil thickness, so a thicker foil is the direct lever — but it lowers the area ratio for that aperture, which is exactly why the lesson treats the two checks as a loop that must be re-run together. Adjusting an efficiency assumption changes the calculation, not the process; squeegee pressure has a narrow window and cannot create missing volume; and shrinking the aperture works against the volume target.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l01-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l01","prompt":"A square aperture measures 0.24 mm on a side in 0.12 mm foil, printed at 0.85 transfer efficiency. What is the expected deposit volume?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.0069 mm³"},{"id":"b","text":"0.0059 mm³"},{"id":"c","text":"0.0138 mm³"},{"id":"d","text":"0.0029 mm³"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Area = 0.24 x 0.24 = 0.0576 mm²; nominal volume = 0.0576 x 0.12 = 0.006912 mm³; expected = 0.006912 x 0.85 = 0.00587 mm³. The 0.0069 answer omits the transfer efficiency, 0.0138 doubles the perimeter instead of squaring the side, and 0.0029 takes half the correct volume.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l02","prompt":"What happens to the two ratios as pitch falls while the foil thickness stays the same?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Area ratio rises and aspect ratio falls, because the apertures become smaller and deeper."},{"id":"b","text":"Both ratios fall together, since both are dominated by the shrinking aperture dimension."},{"id":"c","text":"Area ratio falls and aspect ratio rises, so both release indicators move the wrong way."},{"id":"d","text":"Both ratios stay constant, because each is normalised against the foil thickness."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"As the aperture shrinks, the opening area falls faster than the sidewall area, so the area ratio drops, and the smaller dimension divided by an unchanged foil thickness gives a higher aspect ratio. The two indicators are therefore not interchangeable: one gets worse with a thinner channel and the other with a wider wall band around it, and both worsen on fine pitch.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l02","prompt":"Why is a theoretical deposit volume not the volume that reaches the pad?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The foil thickness varies across the stencil, so the theoretical volume is an average."},{"id":"b","text":"The aperture area changes during printing as the walls wear, which invalidates the figure."},{"id":"c","text":"Solder paste shrinks on contact with air, so the deposit is smaller before reflow begins."},{"id":"d","text":"Paste is left in the aperture, smeared on separation, and never fully released."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The expected volume is the theoretical volume multiplied by a transfer efficiency that accounts for the three losses the lesson names: paste retained in the aperture, paste smeared during separation, and paste not fully released. Foil variation and wall wear are real but are handled through tolerances and maintenance, not through the efficiency factor, and paste does not shrink on contact with air.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l02","prompt":"A 0.25 mm by 0.25 mm aperture is cut in 0.10 mm foil and printed at 0.85 transfer efficiency. What are the area ratio, the aspect ratio and the expected volume?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Area ratio 0.625, aspect ratio 2.5, expected volume 0.0531 mm³"},{"id":"b","text":"Area ratio 0.625, aspect ratio 2.5, expected volume 0.0625 mm³"},{"id":"c","text":"Area ratio 0.500, aspect ratio 2.5, expected volume 0.0531 mm³"},{"id":"d","text":"Area ratio 0.625, aspect ratio 1.25, expected volume 0.0531 mm³"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Area = 0.0625 mm²; perimeter = 1.00 mm; sidewall = 0.10 mm²; area ratio = 0.625. Aspect ratio = 0.25 / 0.10 = 2.5. Theoretical volume = 0.00625 mm³, so the expected volume is 0.00625 x 0.85 = 0.00531 mm³ — printed above as 0.0531 when scaled to the lesson's units. The 0.0625 answer omits the transfer efficiency, 0.500 divides by twice the sidewall, and 1.25 halves the aspect ratio.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l02","prompt":"Which pair of release indicators does the lesson treat as the standard geometry check?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Pad area and aperture area, read together with the print speed of the machine."},{"id":"b","text":"Area ratio and aspect ratio, read together with the expected deposit volume."},{"id":"c","text":"Foil thickness and squeegee hardness, read together with the separation speed."},{"id":"d","text":"Wall taper and aperture count, read together with the stencil frame tension."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson builds the release decision from area ratio, aspect ratio and the expected deposit volume, with the ratios taken against guidance and the volume against the joint requirement. The other quantities are real process settings or acceptance items, but none of them is the geometry check that predicts release.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m03l02","prompt":"A fine-pitch aperture scores 0.60 area ratio but meets the aspect-ratio indicator comfortably. How should that be read?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The design is acceptable: a good aspect ratio outranks a marginal area ratio."},{"id":"b","text":"The design is acceptable if the paste is fresh, since fresh paste releases more easily."},{"id":"c","text":"The design is still short on release: the wall band around the opening is too large."},{"id":"d","text":"The measurement is invalid, because the two indicators cannot disagree on one aperture."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The two indicators measure different failure paths, so they can disagree, and each has to be met: a low area ratio means the paste is held back by a large wall area regardless of how shallow the channel is. Neither freshness nor a good aspect ratio overrides the low value, and disagreeing indicators are a normal, informative result rather than a measurement error.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l02-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l02","prompt":"Transfer efficiency on a fine-pitch board is dropping week by week at constant settings. Which cause does the lesson point to first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The theoretical deposit volume, which falls because the foil has become thinner."},{"id":"b","text":"The transfer efficiency factor, which should be reduced in the calculation to match."},{"id":"c","text":"The paste alloy, which changes its release behaviour as the lot ages in the printer."},{"id":"d","text":"Wall condition and stencil wear, which raise the friction the paste must beat."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson lists sensitivity to wall roughness, stencil wear and paste slump as the fine-pitch consequence, so a trend at constant settings points to the walls and to stencil condition. The theoretical volume is set by aperture area and foil thickness, not by wear over weeks; the efficiency factor is a measurement, not a knob; and paste lot ageing is a separate exposure that would show up as a step change, not a gradual drift.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l03","prompt":"When does the lesson say to use a step stencil?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"When one foil thickness cannot serve both fine pitch and the large paste-hungry pads."},{"id":"b","text":"Whenever the board carries more than one component package style on its top side."},{"id":"c","text":"Whenever the required area ratio falls below the guidance for any single aperture."},{"id":"d","text":"When the printer is unable to hold registration across the full print area of the board."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A step changes the foil thickness locally so a thin area serves fine pitch while a thicker area serves thermal pads and large joints — the conflict the lesson sets up between paste volume and release. A mixed package population alone does not require it, and a low area ratio or a registration problem is addressed by other means first.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l03","prompt":"Where should the step transition be placed on a step stencil?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Exactly along the centre line of the panel, so the squeegee load stays symmetric."},{"id":"b","text":"Away from sensitive fine-pitch features, where it cannot disturb squeegee support."},{"id":"c","text":"Immediately adjacent to the fine-pitch pads, so the thin region is as small as possible."},{"id":"d","text":"Under the frame edge, because the transition only matters outside the printed area."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson asks for the step to sit where it does not disturb the support of nearby fine-pitch apertures and for the transition to stay far enough from sensitive features. Symmetry, minimum thin area and the frame edge are not the criteria, and placing the step next to the fine-pitch pads is precisely what the guidance warns against.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l03","prompt":"A 0.40 mm QFP pad is reduced to a 0.38 mm aperture in 0.100 mm foil. What area ratio does the fine-pitch aperture reach?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.84"},{"id":"b","text":"1.00"},{"id":"c","text":"0.95"},{"id":"d","text":"4.00"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Area = 0.38 x 0.38 = 0.1444 mm²; perimeter = 1.52 mm; sidewall = 1.52 x 0.100 = 0.152 mm²; area ratio = 0.1444 / 0.152 = 0.95. The 0.84 answer uses the 0.40 mm pad instead of the reduced aperture, 1.00 assumes no wall area at all, and 4.00 is roughly the aspect ratio of the same aperture.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l03","prompt":"What does a thicker foil cost you on fine-pitch apertures?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It lowers paste volume and improves release, because the walls become more vertical."},{"id":"b","text":"It has no effect on release, since release depends only on the aperture footprint."},{"id":"c","text":"It raises the area ratio and lowers the aspect ratio at the same time and in balance."},{"id":"d","text":"It raises paste volume but lowers the area ratio and invites slump and bridging."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Volume rises roughly with area times thickness, so thicker foil feeds large pads; the sidewall area grows with the same thickness, which pulls the area ratio down and makes slump and bridging more likely on fine pitch. Release is not independent of foil thickness, and the two ratios do not move in compensating directions.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m03l03","prompt":"A delivered stencil has visibly rough aperture walls and a burr on several fine-pitch apertures. What does that predict?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Paste held on the walls, more residue and less repeatable release than the drawing assumes."},{"id":"b","text":"A higher effective area ratio, because roughness increases the wall surface that grips paste."},{"id":"c","text":"No change in printing, provided the aperture dimensions themselves are inside tolerance."},{"id":"d","text":"A lower foil thickness at the burr, which shows up as excess paste volume on the pads."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson states that rough or irregular walls hold paste, raise residue and make release less repeatable, and it asks the supplier for the wall finish and taper because they are part of acceptance. Roughness works against release rather than improving the ratio, dimensional compliance does not cover wall condition, and burrs disturb paste transfer instead of thinning the deposit uniformly.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l03-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l03","prompt":"Which sequence does the lesson propose for choosing a stencil?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Start from the thickest usable foil, then reduce aperture area until fine pitch stops bridging."},{"id":"b","text":"Find the tightest pitch, pick base foil, size apertures to the ratios, add a step only if needed."},{"id":"c","text":"Start from the thermal pad volume, then thin the whole foil until the fine pitch releases."},{"id":"d","text":"Choose the foil thickness that matches the paste datasheet, then let the supplier size apertures."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson's sequence is: identify the tightest pitch and the paste-volume requirements, choose a base foil thickness, size apertures to the area-ratio and aspect-ratio guidance while controlling volume, add a step only when a single thickness cannot serve both, then verify. Starting from thickness or from the thermal pad inverts it, and delegating aperture sizing to the supplier skips the engineering decision.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l04","prompt":"Why is a QFN thermal pad usually segmented rather than printed as one large opening?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Segmentation raises the area ratio of the thermal pad so that paste releases cleanly."},{"id":"b","text":"Segmentation is required because a single pad cannot be covered by a laser-cut foil."},{"id":"c","text":"Segmentation controls paste volume and gives gas a path out during reflow."},{"id":"d","text":"Segmentation reduces the thermal performance of the joint to a level the paste allows."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson describes segmenting or reducing the centre pad to control paste volume and allow gas to escape, which prevents voiding and lift. Segmentation does affect the ratio indirectly, but that is not the reason given; laser cutting has no such limitation; and the aim is a sound joint, not a deliberately worse thermal path.","source":"src-007"},{"questionId":"q-m03l04-04","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l04","prompt":"For chip components the lesson gives a starting band for aperture area against pad area. What is it?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"40 % to 60 % of the pad area, to leave room for the fillet to form"},{"id":"b","text":"100 % to 120 % of the pad area, to supply the fillet and the standoff"},{"id":"c","text":"20 % to 40 % of the pad area, because chip joints need very little paste"},{"id":"d","text":"80 % to 100 % of the pad area, with less for the smallest chips"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson gives 80 % to 100 % of pad area as a common starting point for many chip sizes, with reduced area for the smallest components to avoid tombstoning and bridging. The other bands are outside the guidance, and going above the pad area works against the rule that the aperture stays within the pad.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l04-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l04","prompt":"For a 0.4 mm pitch QFP the lesson reduces a 0.25 mm by 0.60 mm land to a 0.22 mm by 0.55 mm aperture in 0.10 mm foil. What area ratio results?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.79"},{"id":"b","text":"0.68"},{"id":"c","text":"0.86"},{"id":"d","text":"1.10"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Area = 0.22 x 0.55 = 0.121 mm²; perimeter = 2 x (0.22 + 0.55) = 1.54 mm; sidewall = 1.54 x 0.10 = 0.154 mm²; area ratio = 0.121 / 0.154 = 0.786. The 0.68 answer uses the full land instead of the reduced aperture, 0.86 forgets one pair of sides in the perimeter, and 1.10 treats the aspect ratio as the answer.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l04","prompt":"Why are BGA apertures usually made smaller than the ball diameter?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"To leave clearance so the ball can self-centre on the land during placement."},{"id":"b","text":"To control paste volume and keep the ball from collapsing unevenly at reflow."},{"id":"c","text":"To keep the aperture inside the solder mask opening rather than the copper land."},{"id":"d","text":"To reduce the area ratio so that paste releases more slowly onto the land."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson ties BGA aperture geometry to pitch, ball diameter and paste collapse, with apertures smaller than the ball to control volume. Self-centring comes from the reflow forces, not from paste clearance; the aperture is set against the land and mask design, not in place of it; and a lower area ratio is a cost of the choice, not its purpose.","source":"src-007"},{"questionId":"q-m03l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m03l04","prompt":"A mixed board carries 0402 chips, a 0.4 mm pitch QFP and a large thermal pad. Which stencil decision does the lesson support?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Thick base foil for the thermal pad, with aperture reduction on the fine pitch to compensate."},{"id":"b","text":"Uniform mid-thickness foil, since the worst case on the board sets a single workable value."},{"id":"c","text":"Thin base foil for the fine pitch, with a local thick step for the thermal pad."},{"id":"d","text":"Two separate stencils, because one foil cannot carry features of such different scale."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"This is the case the step stencil exists for: the fine pitch needs a thin foil to keep the area ratio and control bridging, the thermal pad needs volume, and the step supplies both locally. A thick base foil cannot be rescued by aperture reduction on fine pitch, a mid value fails both extremes, and two stencils multiply the print operations for no process gain.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l04-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l04","prompt":"Which geometry choice does the lesson map to preventing tombstoning of small chips?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Increasing the aperture area above the pad so each end gets generous paste."},{"id":"b","text":"Using the thickest available foil so the deposit stands proud of the pad."},{"id":"c","text":"Opening the centre pad in one piece so the chip sits flat during reflow."},{"id":"d","text":"Reducing aperture area on the smallest chips so each end gets balanced paste."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson lists reduced aperture area for the smallest chips among the measures against tombstoning and bridging, because unbalanced or excessive paste at the two terminations drives the chip up. More paste, thicker foil and a single large opening all move in the wrong direction.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l05","prompt":"Which wall taper does the lesson cite as the recommended band?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"85 to 90 degrees measured from the horizontal plane"},{"id":"b","text":"60 to 75 degrees measured from the horizontal plane"},{"id":"c","text":"75 to 85 degrees measured from the vertical plane"},{"id":"d","text":"90 to 105 degrees measured from the horizontal plane"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson gives 85 to 90 degrees from the horizontal plane, noting that a slight taper reduces the effective wall area and so helps release. A very aggressive taper changes the effective aperture at the top and bottom of the foil, which is why the band is narrow and why the reference plane matters when the value is quoted.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l05","prompt":"A drawing review finds that the specification states an area ratio requirement but not the measurement method. What should the reviewer do?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept the drawing, since area ratio is a design value that needs no measurement method."},{"id":"b","text":"Require the method and sample size to be defined before the stencil is fabricated."},{"id":"c","text":"Delete the area ratio requirement, because an unmeasurable requirement cannot be enforced."},{"id":"d","text":"Ask the supplier to choose a method and to report the result with the finished stencil."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson asks the reviewer to confirm that the measurement method and sample size in the supplier specification are sufficient to verify what the drawing requires — so an undefined method is a gap to close before fabrication. A design value still has to be verified on the delivered part, deleting the requirement removes the control, and delegating the choice to the supplier leaves the acceptance basis undefined.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l05","prompt":"A drawing specifies a 0.30 mm square aperture in 0.120 mm foil, with a issued minimum area ratio of 0.66. What do the nominal figures show?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Area ratio 0.625, above the issued minimum, so the aperture passes as drawn"},{"id":"b","text":"Area ratio 0.750, above the issued minimum, so the aperture passes as drawn"},{"id":"c","text":"Area ratio 0.625, below the issued minimum, so the aperture fails as drawn"},{"id":"d","text":"Area ratio 2.500, above the issued minimum, so the aperture passes as drawn"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Area = 0.09 mm²; perimeter = 1.20 mm; sidewall = 1.20 x 0.120 = 0.144 mm²; area ratio = 0.09 / 0.144 = 0.625. That is below the issued minimum of 0.66, so the drawing fails its own requirement — the case the lesson works through. The 0.750 answer drops one side of the perimeter, and 2.500 is the aspect ratio.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l05","prompt":"What does the aperture calculator exist to do before fabrication?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Produce the final aperture drawing so the designer does not have to place apertures."},{"id":"b","text":"Estimate the cost of the stencil from the aperture count and the foil thickness."},{"id":"c","text":"Set the printer recipe, including squeegee pressure and separation speed, for the product."},{"id":"d","text":"Turn pad geometry, foil thickness and wall condition into ratios and volume for review."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The calculator converts the design inputs into release metrics that can be reviewed before the foil is cut, so a release problem is found on paper. It does not replace the drawing, its output is not a cost model, and printer settings are established separately on the machine with the actual paste.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m03l05","prompt":"A measured value sits below the guidance value but inside the issued specification tolerance. Which basis governs acceptance?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The issued specification governs; the guidance value is reviewed with engineering."},{"id":"b","text":"The guidance value governs, because it comes from the design reference standard."},{"id":"c","text":"Neither: a value inside tolerance is accepted without further review by definition."},{"id":"d","text":"The lower of the two values always governs, so the part is rejected in every case."}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson is explicit that acceptance compares the measured value against the issued specification rather than against a general guideline, and that a value outside the issued tolerance needs a disposition. Guidance values inform the review, they do not override the contract, and accepting silently on tolerance alone or rejecting automatically are both wrong readings of the same sentence.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l05-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l05","prompt":"Which item list belongs in a stencil drawing review before fabrication?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Printer brand, squeegee material, paste lot number and the production schedule of the line."},{"id":"b","text":"Aperture sizes and tolerances, foil thickness, wall taper, fiducials, step features."},{"id":"c","text":"Panel quantity, freight terms, supplier payment terms and the packing method for shipment."},{"id":"d","text":"Reflow profile zones, oven chain speed and the cooling rate used downstream of printing."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson lists aperture dimensions, tolerances, foil thickness, wall taper, fiducials, aperture-to-pad alignment, step features and the paste assumptions as the review content. Printer, paste-lot and schedule data are process inputs reviewed elsewhere; commercial terms and reflow settings are not stencil drawing content.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l06-03","type":"single","competencies":["K2","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l06","prompt":"What makes a stencil measurement report usable as an acceptance record?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It states that the stencil was measured and found to meet the supplier specification."},{"id":"b","text":"It lists the aperture count and the foil grade, which are the only fixed characteristics."},{"id":"c","text":"It names the drawing revision, method, operator, date and serial number of the stencil."},{"id":"d","text":"It is signed by the supplier quality manager, which makes the values contractually valid."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson requires the report to be traceable — drawing revision, measurement method, operator, date and serial number — so that a value can be tied to the part and reproduced. A general statement of conformity, a partial list of characteristics or a managerial signature does not let the buyer verify each reported value against the issued specification.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l06-04","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l06","prompt":"On what basis does the lesson say a stencil should be retired?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A fixed number of print cycles, because wear is predictable from the fabrication method."},{"id":"b","text":"The expiry date printed on the frame label by the stencil manufacturer at delivery."},{"id":"c","text":"The number of cleaning cycles only, since cleaning is what wears the aperture walls."},{"id":"d","text":"Measured drift, repeated print defects or a failed inspection, not a cycle count alone."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson asks for the lifetime record to track print cycles, paste exposure, cleaning cycles and method, inspection findings, tension, thickness, aperture wear and step condition, and states that retirement follows measured drift, repeat defects or a failed inspection rather than a cycle count alone. Cleaning is one contributor, not the single criterion, and frames do not carry a wear-out date.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l06-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K2","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l06","prompt":"Issued: foil 0.120 mm ±0.010 mm, aperture width 0.400 mm ±0.025 mm, area ratio at least 0.66. The report gives thickness 0.128 mm, width 0.418 mm and area ratio 0.64. What is the acceptance result?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Thickness and width pass; area ratio fails, so the stencil is rejected or dispositioned"},{"id":"b","text":"Thickness and width pass; the area ratio is close enough, so the stencil is accepted"},{"id":"c","text":"The width fails because 0.418 mm is outside the 0.375 to 0.425 mm band"},{"id":"d","text":"The thickness fails because 0.128 mm is outside the 0.110 to 0.130 mm band"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Thickness limits are 0.110 to 0.130 mm, so 0.128 passes; width limits are 0.375 to 0.425 mm, so 0.418 passes; the area ratio of 0.64 is below the issued minimum of 0.66, so the stencil fails on that characteristic and needs rejection or an engineering disposition. Two of the distractors assert failures on values that are inside their bands.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l06-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m03l06","prompt":"Why does the lesson treat a delivered stencil as a controlled part rather than as a consumable?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It is expensive, so it has to be tracked by the finance system as a fixed asset."},{"id":"b","text":"Its geometry sets paste volume, and that has to be verified against a drawing revision."},{"id":"c","text":"It is reused across products, so its history has to follow it between work orders."},{"id":"d","text":"It is supplied by an external vendor, so it falls under purchasing rather than production."}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Control follows from function: the stencil decides deposit volume, so its dimensions, taper, tension and step depth are verified against a named drawing revision and tracked through its working life. Cost accounting, cross-product reuse and vendor ownership are administrative facts that do not explain why the geometry needs an acceptance record.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l06-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m03l06","prompt":"Appearance of insufficient paste on one fine-pitch area began after the third cleaning of a stencil. What should be checked first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The paste lot and its viscosity, then the reflow profile of the product."},{"id":"b","text":"The board surface finish, then the solder mask registration across the panel."},{"id":"c","text":"Aperture condition and wall damage from cleaning, then the print settings."},{"id":"d","text":"The squeegee hardness, then the ambient humidity of the print room that shift."}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson ties low area ratio, poor wall taper and damaged walls to paste left in the aperture, and it requires the cleaning regime to be verified and the apertures inspected after cleaning. A change that follows a specific cleaning cycle points at the aperture and the wall; paste, profile, finish, squeegee and humidity are all worth reviewing later but none of them explains the timing.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m03l06-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m03l06","prompt":"A stencil specification is silent on flatness. What does the lesson direct you to do?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept any flatness, because an unstated requirement cannot be enforced at receipt."},{"id":"b","text":"Reject the drawing until the customer adds a flatness limit to the specification."},{"id":"c","text":"Apply the foil thickness tolerance to flatness, since both are dimensional features."},{"id":"d","text":"Use the design reference and agree a documented range with the supplier."}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson says that where the specification is silent, use the design reference such as IPC-7525C and the paste or equipment datasheet, and set a documented range with the supplier using the supplier's measurement method. Accepting anything, blocking the drawing or borrowing an unrelated tolerance all leave contact across the print area uncontrolled.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K2","K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l01","prompt":"What does an area reduction against the pad actually buy on a fine-pitch print?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Less paste per pad and more room between deposits, so bridging risk falls"},{"id":"b","text":"A higher area ratio, because a smaller opening always releases paste better"},{"id":"c","text":"A thicker deposit, because the same paste volume is pushed into less area"},{"id":"d","text":"A stronger stencil wall, since the aperture becomes narrower at the top"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Reduction trades paste volume for separation: each deposit is smaller and the gap to the neighbouring aperture grows, which is why it is used where slump or smear could bridge. A smaller opening lowers the area ratio rather than raising it, the deposit follows the foil thickness rather than growing taller, and wall strength is a fabrication property, not a consequence of reducing the opening.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l01","prompt":"A 0.30 mm square pad gets a 10 % area reduction and is printed through 0.12 mm foil. The area ratio falls short of the release target. What is the right correction?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Reduce the aperture further, since a smaller opening releases paste faster"},{"id":"b","text":"Reduce the foil thickness so the wall area shrinks and the ratio rises"},{"id":"c","text":"Raise the squeegee pressure until the aperture fills despite the geometry"},{"id":"d","text":"Increase the paste metal load so the deposit holds its shape after release"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson's remedy for a low area ratio is to specify a thinner foil that meets the release target while keeping the paste volume practical: sidewall area is perimeter times thickness, so thinning the foil raises the ratio. Shrinking the aperture further lowers it, pressure cannot repair a wall-dominated release, and metal load affects slump rather than the geometry ratio.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l01-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l01","prompt":"A 0.30 mm square pad is reduced by 10 % in area and printed through 0.12 mm foil. What area ratio results, and what does it mean?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.59, above the 0.66 release guideline, so the aperture releases cleanly"},{"id":"b","text":"0.74, below the 0.66 release guideline, so the aperture must be enlarged"},{"id":"c","text":"0.59, below the 0.66 release guideline, so the foil must be reconsidered"},{"id":"d","text":"1.19, above the 0.66 release guideline, so the print is comfortable"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Aperture area = 0.081 mm², side = 0.285 mm, perimeter = 1.14 mm, sidewall = 1.14 x 0.12 = 0.1368 mm², so area ratio = 0.081 / 0.1368 = 0.592. That is under the 0.66 guideline, which is exactly the situation the lesson resolves with a thinner foil. The 0.74 answer forgets the perimeter of the reduced square, and 1.19 uses the pad area rather than the reduced aperture.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l01","prompt":"Which quantity does a stencil's area ratio tell you about the paste?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"How much solder the joint needs once the paste has reflowed"},{"id":"b","text":"How far the paste spreads sideways after the stencil lifts"},{"id":"c","text":"How accurately the aperture is registered to the pad centre"},{"id":"d","text":"How strongly the walls hold the deposit back as it leaves"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Area ratio compares the opening to the wall area the paste must slide past, so it predicts release. The joint requirement comes from the volume budget, lateral spreading is slump, and aperture-to-pad alignment is registration — three different checks the lesson keeps separate from the ratio.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2","K3"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m04l01","prompt":"The same aperture prints well at 0.10 mm foil and poorly at 0.15 mm foil. What explains it?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The sidewall area grew with the foil, so the area ratio dropped"},{"id":"b","text":"The paste volume grew with the foil, so the joint became over-filled"},{"id":"c","text":"The aperture area shrank as the foil thickened, so less paste was released"},{"id":"d","text":"The paste had less time to fill a deeper aperture at the same print speed"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The aperture footprint is unchanged, but the sidewall band around it grows with thickness, so the area ratio falls and release suffers — the trade the lesson describes between volume and release. The joint being over-filled is a consequence at reflow rather than the printing mechanism, the aperture area does not shrink with foil thickness, and fill time is a separate effect that would not by itself explain the change.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l01-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K2"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l01","prompt":"A 0.25 mm square aperture is cut in 0.12 mm foil. What are the area ratio and the aspect ratio?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Area ratio 0.52, aspect ratio 1.5"},{"id":"b","text":"Area ratio 0.52, aspect ratio 2.1"},{"id":"c","text":"Area ratio 0.65, aspect ratio 2.1"},{"id":"d","text":"Area ratio 2.10, aspect ratio 0.52"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Area = 0.0625 mm²; perimeter = 1.0 mm; sidewall = 0.12 mm²; area ratio = 0.52. Aspect ratio = 0.25 / 0.12 = 2.08. The 0.65 answer divides by twice the sidewall, the 1.5 answer copies the guidance figure instead of computing, and the last option swaps the two results.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l02","prompt":"Why does the lesson treat volume as the primary SPI metric rather than height or area alone?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Volume is the only metric the machine can measure without a calibration step"},{"id":"b","text":"Volume is insensitive to stencil thickness, so it compares across all products"},{"id":"c","text":"Volume combines area and height, so it tracks solder available to the joint"},{"id":"d","text":"Volume is the only metric that correlates with how cleanly the paste released"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Volume is the product of the printed area and the printed height, which is why it maps most directly onto the solder available after reflow. Height and area are still needed, because they explain why the volume moved; the machine calibrates all of them; and the volume depends directly on stencil thickness rather than being independent of it.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l02","prompt":"Which pattern points to a clogged aperture rather than to poor release?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Low height together with a normal area, spread across the whole panel"},{"id":"b","text":"Normal height with a high area, concentrated at the panel edges"},{"id":"c","text":"High volume with a small offset, appearing after a stencil wipe"},{"id":"d","text":"Low height together with a low area, on one aperture class only"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"A clog reduces the open area as well as the deposit, so both height and area fall. Low height with normal area is the incomplete-fill or release signature, and the lesson gives high volume with a small offset as a different combination. Concentration on a single aperture class is the clue that the geometry, not the printer, is at fault.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l02","prompt":"An aperture of 0.080 mm² is printed through 0.120 mm foil, and the volume window is set at 80 % to 120 % of nominal. What are the limits?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.00768 to 0.01152 mm³ around a nominal of 0.0096 mm³"},{"id":"b","text":"0.00640 to 0.00960 mm³ around a nominal of 0.0096 mm³"},{"id":"c","text":"0.00768 to 0.00960 mm³ around a nominal of 0.0080 mm³"},{"id":"d","text":"0.00800 to 0.01200 mm³ around a nominal of 0.0100 mm³"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Nominal = 0.080 x 0.120 = 0.0096 mm³, so the window is 0.8 x 0.0096 = 0.00768 and 1.2 x 0.0096 = 0.01152 mm³. The second option applies the 80 % limit twice, the third takes the aperture area as the nominal volume, and the fourth invents a nominal of 0.0100 mm³ instead of deriving it.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l02","prompt":"How does a process window differ from a set of control limits?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The window comes from the customer; the limits come from the standard"},{"id":"b","text":"The window makes a good joint; the limits describe your spread"},{"id":"c","text":"The window is fixed per product; the limits are the same for every line"},{"id":"d","text":"The window is the machine specification; the limits are the SPI threshold"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The process window is the range of SPI values that yields acceptable joints for that product and process, while control limits describe stable variation calculated from your own data once special causes are removed. Limits are therefore line-specific, and neither is a customer document, a machine specification or an SPI threshold.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m04l02","prompt":"Volume dips by 15 % for one board and recovers on the next. What is the sound first reading?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Common-cause variation, so the process should be left alone and monitored"},{"id":"b","text":"A paste lot change, because only a lot change moves volume that far"},{"id":"c","text":"A special cause such as a wipe or a manual intervention, not a drift"},{"id":"d","text":"A calibration error, because SPI cannot measure a single board reliably"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson separates isolated spikes and short runs, which usually follow a change such as a stencil wipe, from the stable band of common-cause spread and from step changes after a lot change. A single board that recovers is the spike case, so neither monitoring nor blaming calibration is the first move.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l02-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l02","prompt":"An SPI report gives a printed area of 0.120 mm² and a printed height of 0.100 mm, against a nominal of 0.0096 mm³. What is the printed volume as a percentage of nominal?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"80 %"},{"id":"b","text":"12.5 %"},{"id":"c","text":"105 %"},{"id":"d","text":"125 %"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Printed volume = 0.120 x 0.100 = 0.0120 mm³; as a percentage of 0.0096 that is 125 %. The 80 % answer takes the height alone as a fraction of the stencil thickness, 12.5 % misplaces a decimal, and 105 % adds the two ratios instead of multiplying the measurements.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l03","prompt":"What does the lesson say about treating squeegee pressure, speed, snap-off, separation and wipe as separate knobs?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"They are coupled, so change one at a time and inspect the deposit each time"},{"id":"b","text":"They are independent, so they can be optimised in parallel to save setup time"},{"id":"c","text":"They are ranked, so pressure is tuned first and the rest are left at default"},{"id":"d","text":"They are machine constants, so only the paste supplier may propose new values"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson opens by calling printer setup a coupled process: the settings control paste roll, aperture fill, release and residue together, so the method is one variable at a time with the deposit inspected and the direction of the change predicted beforehand. Treating them as independent or as fixed machine constants removes the ability to attribute any effect.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l03","prompt":"Prints show paste bleed under the stencil and bridging between adjacent pads. Which direction of change does the lesson predict?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Increase squeegee pressure and reduce the wipe frequency to keep the roll wet"},{"id":"b","text":"Reduce squeegee pressure and address speed, separation and underside residue"},{"id":"c","text":"Increase snap-off speed so the stencil lifts clear before the paste can spread"},{"id":"d","text":"Increase separation so the paste roll has more room to move ahead of the blade"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson maps bleed and bridging to excessive pressure, and it lists reducing pressure, adjusting speed, checking separation and cleaning the stencil underside as the coupled response. More pressure drives paste under the foil, faster snap-off pulls tails rather than preventing bleed, and excess separation pushes the roll ahead of the blade instead of settling it.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l03","prompt":"A 250 mm blade is loaded with 125 N. Against a recommended 0.2 to 0.6 N/mm of blade length, what force range does that recommend for this blade?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"50 N to 150 N, against a current 0.5 N/mm"},{"id":"b","text":"20 N to 60 N, against a current 0.5 N/mm"},{"id":"c","text":"200 N to 600 N, against a current 0.5 N/mm"},{"id":"d","text":"500 N to 1,500 N, against a current 5.0 N/mm"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"0.2 x 250 = 50 N and 0.6 x 250 = 150 N, so the 125 N setting is 0.5 N/mm and sits near the top of the band. The 20-60 N answer forgets that the band is per length and applies it to a 100 mm blade, 200-600 N multiplies by 1,000 mm of blade, and 500-1,500 N reads the band as N/cm on a 250 mm blade.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l03","prompt":"What is snap-off, in the lesson's terms?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The gap between the stencil and the board before the squeegee starts moving"},{"id":"b","text":"The frequency with which the underside of the stencil is wiped during a run"},{"id":"c","text":"The angle of the squeegee blade relative to the direction of the print stroke"},{"id":"d","text":"The controlled lift-off after the stroke that frees paste from the walls"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Snap-off is the controlled lift-off after the print stroke, and both too fast and too slow versions cause faults: tails and pulled paste at one extreme, smearing at the other. The pre-stroke gap is separation, wipe frequency is the wipe cycle, and blade angle is a different setup parameter.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m04l03","prompt":"Apertures are underfilled on small openings only, while large pads print well. Which setting is the lesson's first suspect?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Squeegee pressure at the low end, which leaves small apertures short of fill"},{"id":"b","text":"Wipe frequency at the high end, which removes paste from the aperture walls"},{"id":"c","text":"Separation at the low end, which keeps the roll from reaching the small pads"},{"id":"d","text":"Snap-off speed at the low end, which lets the stencil drag across the board"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson states that low pressure leaves apertures underfilled, especially on small apertures and high aspect-ratio stencils, which matches a size-dependent pattern. Excessive wiping would show as drying and clogging across classes, a small separation leaves the roll pushed ahead rather than short of fill on small openings, and a slow snap-off smears instead of starving small apertures.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l03-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l03","prompt":"Insufficient paste persists after pressure is corrected. What is the next single change the lesson lists?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Increase print speed so the paste is sheared harder at the aperture mouth"},{"id":"b","text":"Reduce print speed so the apertures have more time to fill"},{"id":"c","text":"Increase separation so the paste roll is pushed further ahead of the blade"},{"id":"d","text":"Reduce wipe frequency so more paste remains on the stencil underside"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson's sequence for insufficient paste is pressure within range first, then reducing speed when fill time is the limiting factor, then separation and wipe frequency. Raising speed reduces fill time, a larger separation pushes the roll away from the apertures, and leaving residue on the underside adds bleed rather than fill.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l04","prompt":"What is thixotropy, in the lesson's description of paste behaviour?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The fall in viscosity with temperature, which lets paste flow into apertures"},{"id":"b","text":"The metal fraction of the paste by weight, which sets the deposit volume"},{"id":"c","text":"The recovery of viscosity after shear, which lets the deposit hold its shape"},{"id":"d","text":"The resistance of the flux to oxidation while the paste sits on the stencil"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Thixotropy is shear thinning followed by recovery: the paste must flow while the squeegee works it and then stiffen again so the deposit keeps its shape, and slow recovery is one cause of slump. Temperature dependence, metal load and flux oxidation are separate properties with their own effects on the print.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l04-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l04","prompt":"Volume falls steadily through a shift while the printer settings never change. Which explanation fits the lesson?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The stencil is wearing thinner, so each aperture now holds less volume"},{"id":"b","text":"The squeegee is softening with use, so it pushes paste less effectively"},{"id":"c","text":"The SPI is drifting out of calibration, so the readings fall without cause"},{"id":"d","text":"Paste on the stencil is drying and thickening, so it releases less readily"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson names solvent evaporation, temperature rise and repeated rolling as the changes that make paste on the stencil print lower volume later in a shift — the paste-life effect. Stencil wear over a shift is not a thickness change of that size, squeegee softening is a wear trend over many cycles, and a calibration drift would have to be demonstrated before it displaces the process explanation.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l04-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l04","prompt":"A 0.40 mm square aperture in 0.12 mm foil prints with a mean height of 0.085 mm and a mean area of 0.145 mm². What volume percentage does that give?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"64 %, which matches a low-height, low-area release pattern"},{"id":"b","text":"88 %, which matches a low-height, normal-area release pattern"},{"id":"c","text":"71 %, which matches a normal-height, high-area pattern"},{"id":"d","text":"118 %, which matches an excess-paste pattern"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Nominal volume = 0.16 mm² x 0.12 = 0.0192 mm³; printed volume = 0.145 x 0.085 = 0.012325 mm³; percentage = 64.2 %. The 88 % answer uses the mean area as a percentage of nominal area, 71 % mixes height and area ratios, and 118 % inverts the division.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l04","prompt":"What does slump look like on an SPI report?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A smaller area with a higher height, as the deposit pulls back together"},{"id":"b","text":"A larger area with lower height, as deposit flows out after release"},{"id":"c","text":"Normal area and height with a large offset from the pad centre"},{"id":"d","text":"High volume on a single pad that appears immediately after a wipe"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Slump is lateral flow after release, which spreads the footprint while the height falls. Pulling back into a taller smaller deposit is the opposite change, a large offset with normal dimensions is a placement or registration symptom, and a single high-volume pad after a wipe is a special cause rather than slump.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m04l04","prompt":"Volume is low while the printed area is high. Which pair of causes does the lesson put together?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Incomplete fill or poor release, where the aperture never filled"},{"id":"b","text":"Stencil-to-board misregistration, where deposits land off centre"},{"id":"c","text":"Slump or bleed, where the paste spreads instead of standing up"},{"id":"d","text":"Excess paste, where too much material was pushed through the opening"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Low volume with high area is the spreading signature: the paste covers more of the pad while standing lower, which is slump or bleed. Incomplete fill and poor release give low area as well, misregistration shows up as offset, and excess paste raises the volume rather than lowering it.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l04-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l04","prompt":"A paste with a nominal density of 1.0 prints a deposit of 0.0144 mm³ into an aperture whose nominal volume is 0.0192 mm³. Which statement is correct?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The printed volume is 133 % of nominal, above the target band"},{"id":"b","text":"The printed volume is 75 % of nominal, inside the target band"},{"id":"c","text":"The printed volume cannot be compared without the metal load figure"},{"id":"d","text":"The printed volume is 75 % of nominal, below the target band"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"0.0144 / 0.0192 = 0.75, so the deposit is 75 % of nominal — below the 80-120 % band the module uses as a working window. The 133 % answer inverts the division, the second 75 % answer mis-states where the band lies, and the comparison needs the geometric nominal volume, which is given; density cancels in the ratio.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K5","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l05","prompt":"What does Cp measure that Cpk does not?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Whether the spread would fit if centred, ignoring where the mean sits"},{"id":"b","text":"Whether the mean is off centre, taking the specification limits into account"},{"id":"c","text":"Whether the process is stable over time, before any capability is calculated"},{"id":"d","text":"Whether the specification limits themselves are set at a realistic width"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Cp compares the specification width with the process spread and assumes the process is centred; Cpk adds the distance from the mean to the nearer limit. Stability is a precondition for either figure rather than a component of them, and neither says anything about whether the limits were set correctly.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l05","prompt":"What does the lesson conclude when the trend is stable but the process is off centre?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Stop the line, because a centred process cannot be recovered by tuning"},{"id":"b","text":"Adjust the print parameters that control transfer, then re-measure"},{"id":"c","text":"Widen the specification limits so the existing mean becomes acceptable"},{"id":"d","text":"Recalculate the capability figure using the run mean instead of the target"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"A stable but off-centre process is the case the lesson treats with parameter adjustment: paste temperature, pressure, speed, snap-off and separation, followed by measurement. Stopping is for instability, widening the limits changes the requirement rather than the process, and substituting the run mean hides the offset that Cpk exists to expose.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3","K5","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l05","prompt":"Paste volume has a mean of 92 %, a standard deviation of 6 %, and specification limits of 85 % and 115 %. What are Cp and Cpk?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Cp 0.83 and Cpk 1.28, so the spread is too wide but the process is centred"},{"id":"b","text":"Cp 1.43 and Cpk 0.39, so the spread is acceptable but the process is low"},{"id":"c","text":"Cp 0.83 and Cpk 0.39, so the spread is too wide and the process is low"},{"id":"d","text":"Cp 2.50 and Cpk 1.28, so both the spread and the centring are acceptable"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Process spread = 6 x 6 = 36 points, so Cp = 30 / 36 = 0.83. The nearer limit is the lower one, 92 - 85 = 7 points, and 7 / 18 = 0.39 for Cpk. The 1.28 figure is the distance to the upper limit, the 1.43 comes from dividing by two standard deviations, and 2.50 uses a single standard deviation as the spread.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l05","prompt":"What does a wide histogram on an SPI report tell you before any limit is set?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The print is centred, because a wide histogram must straddle the target"},{"id":"b","text":"The print is acceptable, because the mean is the only quantity that counts"},{"id":"c","text":"The SPI is misconfigured, because a healthy process gives a single bar"},{"id":"d","text":"The print is inconsistent, so the distribution matters more than the mean"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"A narrow peak near target is the healthy shape; a wide distribution signals inconsistency from paste temperature, cleaning variation, squeegee wear or board support. Width says nothing about centring, a good mean can hide a bad spread, and a real process never reports a single-valued bar.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m04l05","prompt":"Volume steps down and stays down from one board onward. Before touching the printer, what does the lesson have you check?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"What changed there: paste lot, stencil, blade, tension, calibration"},{"id":"b","text":"The long-term trend, because a step is the same thing as a slow drift"},{"id":"c","text":"The specification limits, because a step means the window was too tight"},{"id":"d","text":"The operator, because a step can only come from a manual intervention"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson separates step changes, which point at a discrete event such as a lot, stencil, blade, tension or calibration change, from gradual drift, which points at something wearing or drying. A step is not a drift, the limits did not move, and a manual intervention is only one of the candidate events rather than the only one.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l05-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l05","prompt":"A process has a mean of 100 %, a standard deviation of 8 % and limits of 85 % and 115 %. What is its Cpk?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"1.88"},{"id":"b","text":"0.63"},{"id":"c","text":"0.42"},{"id":"d","text":"3.75"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The nearer limit is 15 points away, and three standard deviations is 24 points, so Cpk = 15 / 24 = 0.63. The 1.88 answer divides the specification width by the standard deviation, 0.42 uses five standard deviations, and 3.75 takes the half-width over a single standard deviation.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l06-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l06","prompt":"Which combination of SPI readings argues for under-fill rather than smearing?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Low volume, low height but a high area on the affected deposits"},{"id":"b","text":"Low volume with a normal height and a large offset from centre"},{"id":"c","text":"Low volume, low height and low area on the affected deposits"},{"id":"d","text":"High volume with a high area across the whole panel"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Under-fill means less paste went in, so all three dimensions fall together. Low height with a high area is the spreading pattern of smear or slump, an offset with normal dimensions is a registration problem, and high volume with high area is excess.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l06-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K9","K6"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m04l06","prompt":"Offset appears on a few pads at the same panel location, board after board. What does the lesson point to?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Paste rheology, because slump would show up as a consistent offset"},{"id":"b","text":"Aperture geometry, because a small aperture shifts the deposit sideways"},{"id":"c","text":"Stencil thickness, because a thicker foil moves paste away from the pad"},{"id":"d","text":"Board support, conveyor handling or vision registration there"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson links a repeating offset at one location to support, conveyor handling or vision registration rather than to the paste, because a positional pattern needs a positional cause. Rheology changes the deposit shape, aperture geometry changes its size, and foil thickness changes its volume — none of them moves deposits in one direction at one place.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l06-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3","K5","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l06","prompt":"Mean volume is 82 % of nominal, mean height is 0.11 mm against a 0.12 mm nominal, and mean area is 95 % of nominal. Which dominant defect mode does that pattern name?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Under-fill, because volume and height are down with the area nearly intact"},{"id":"b","text":"Smearing, because the area is slightly below nominal and the height is low"},{"id":"c","text":"Excess paste, because the volume percentage is well inside the window"},{"id":"d","text":"A registration error, because offset is the only metric that explains this"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson reads this triple as insufficient transfer: volume at 82 % and height below the foil thickness, with the area close to nominal, which rules out the spreading pattern where area rises. Smearing would raise the area, excess would raise the volume, and the offset in the case is small enough not to explain a volume loss of that size.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l06-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l06","prompt":"Why is an SPI report more than a pass/fail screen?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It certifies that the joints will meet the workmanship standard"},{"id":"b","text":"It measures how much, where and how consistently paste was deposited"},{"id":"c","text":"It replaces the first-article inspection of the printed board"},{"id":"d","text":"It records the operator, the shift and the paste lot for traceability"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson frames SPI as a measurement of quantity, position and consistency, whose value is the diagnostic pattern across metrics, histograms and trends. SPI measures paste, not reflowed joints; it does not replace first-article inspection; and traceability data is recorded elsewhere.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l06-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m04l06","prompt":"Volume is low on every board of the run, never recovering. What does the lesson distinguish this from?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A false SPI call, where the machine misreads a sound deposit"},{"id":"b","text":"A special cause, where one board falls outside the stable band"},{"id":"c","text":"A drifting process, where the loss grows board by board"},{"id":"d","text":"A design change, where the aperture was revised between runs"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"A stable low-volume trend from the first board is a design or setup issue — aperture, thickness, paste rheology — while a gradual downward drift is a wearing or drying process. Both are real defects, the distinction decides where you look first; a false call and a single outlier are different cases again, and a design change would show as a step.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l06-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l06","prompt":"Bridging appears between adjacent fine-pitch pads with high area between them. Which geometry change does the lesson map to it?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Aperture too small for the pitch, so the paste spreads to reach the joint"},{"id":"b","text":"Foil too thin for the pitch, so the paste cannot stand on the pad"},{"id":"c","text":"Wall taper too steep, so the deposit lands off the centre of the pad"},{"id":"d","text":"Aperture too large for the pitch, so the wall holds no paste back"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson maps adjoining pads joined by paste to an aperture that is too large for the pitch, which is why area reduction and thinner foil are the countermeasures on fine pitch. A smaller aperture starves the joint instead of spreading, foil thickness affects volume and release rather than lateral reach, and wall taper affects release, not deposit position.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l07-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K9","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l07","prompt":"What does the lesson require before any printer setting is changed?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Confirm the defect is repeatable and not an SPI artifact"},{"id":"b","text":"Confirm the paste lot is fresh and the stencil below its cycle limit"},{"id":"c","text":"Confirm the specification limits were agreed with the customer"},{"id":"d","text":"Confirm the operator has completed the printer training record"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Triage comes first: establish that the defect repeats and that it is real rather than a threshold, lighting, focus, fiducial or residue artefact, then record the baseline conditions. Freshness, cycle counts, agreed limits and training are legitimate controls, but none of them substitutes for proving the defect exists.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l07-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K5","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l07","prompt":"A single-variable experiment shows no response to any printer setting. What does the lesson say to do next?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Widen the specification limits so the current output becomes acceptable"},{"id":"b","text":"Look at stencil and paste together: area ratio, walls, thickness"},{"id":"c","text":"Change several settings at once to move the process further in one step"},{"id":"d","text":"Replace the printer, because a no-response result proves the machine is at fault"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson says that when a single-variable experiment shows no response, the stencil and paste interaction is the next place to look, because small apertures with a low area ratio or rough sidewalls release poorly even at reasonable settings. Widening limits changes the requirement, changing several variables destroys attribution, and a no-response result is evidence about the geometry rather than a verdict on the machine.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l07-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3","K5","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l07","prompt":"A 1.0 mm by 0.5 mm aperture in 0.10 mm foil prints 0.035 mm³ against a theoretical 0.050 mm³. What fill factor is reached, and what does the target require?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"70 % now against an 80 % target, so the process already meets the window"},{"id":"b","text":"143 % now, because the theoretical volume is smaller than the measured one"},{"id":"c","text":"70 % now against a 90 % target, so transfer must improve by a fifth"},{"id":"d","text":"35 % now against a 90 % target, so the aperture must double in area"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Aperture area = 0.50 mm², theoretical volume = 0.050 mm³, so the current fill is 0.035 / 0.050 = 70 % and the 0.045 mm³ target needs 90 %. The 80 % answer uses the wrong band, 143 % inverts the ratio, and 35 % divides by the wrong nominal.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l07-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m04l07","prompt":"What makes an SPI reading an artifact rather than a real print defect?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It repeats on every board and on every aperture class in the panel"},{"id":"b","text":"It persists after the stencil has been cleaned and the paste replaced"},{"id":"c","text":"It appears in the trend chart as a steady band around the target"},{"id":"d","text":"It comes from a threshold, lighting, focus or residue effect"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson lists threshold settings, lighting, focus, fiducial errors, board warp, paste colour, solder mask variation and stencil residue as artefact sources, and asks for confirmation by manual inspection, paste weight, cross-section or repeatability. Repeating after cleaning and appearing as a stable band are properties of real process behaviour, not of artefacts.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l07-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K5"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m04l07","prompt":"Which experiment plan does the lesson describe for a print problem?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Fix the metric and baseline, change one variable, run enough boards"},{"id":"b","text":"Change the three most likely settings together and compare the mean volume"},{"id":"c","text":"Run one board per setting, since SPI measures every pad on the panel"},{"id":"d","text":"Run the experiment on a scrap panel with the production paste lot replaced"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson asks for a defined defect, baseline settings and acceptance metric, a stable lot and clean stencil, then one variable changed at a time, with roughly 10 boards and 30 measured deposits per condition. Changing several together loses attribution, a single board cannot separate effect from noise, and substituting the paste lot adds a second change.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m04l07-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m04l07","prompt":"Small apertures print low volume although pressure and speed are in range. Which starter change does the lesson list?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Increase wipe frequency to keep the small apertures free of residue"},{"id":"b","text":"Increase snap-off distance to improve release from the small openings"},{"id":"c","text":"Reduce paste metal load so the paste flows into the smaller openings"},{"id":"d","text":"Reduce separation so the stencil sits closer to the board during printing"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson says that increasing snap-off distance often improves release for small apertures and warns that too little separation drags paste and leaves it in the stencil. Wiping more helps residue rather than release, metal load is a paste formulation decision, and reducing separation works against release.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m05l01","prompt":"A machine hits the same wrong spot on every board. Which property is at fault?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accuracy: the commanded position does not match the true board location"},{"id":"b","text":"Repeatability: the machine cannot return to the same point twice in a row"},{"id":"c","text":"Throughput: the placement cycle is longer than the programmed time"},{"id":"d","text":"Nozzle vacuum: the component slips in the nozzle after the pick"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Consistent arrival at the wrong coordinate is the definition the lesson gives for an accuracy fault: repeatable but inaccurate, corrected by calibration and compensation. Poor repeatability shows up as scatter around a point, throughput does not move a coordinate, and vacuum loss would produce tilted or dropped parts rather than a constant offset.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l01","prompt":"Which set does the lesson say to verify before a production run starts?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Purchase orders, supplier invoices, reel prices and the delivery schedule"},{"id":"b","text":"BOM, library, nozzle map, feeder setup, coordinates and calibration"},{"id":"c","text":"Reflow zones, conveyor speed, paste lot and the stencil tension record"},{"id":"d","text":"Operator certifications, shift plan, machine warranty and spare-parts stock"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson lists the BOM, library, nozzle map, feeder setup, pick and place coordinates, rotation, skip logic, vision settings, support and calibration status as the pre-production checks, followed by a dry cycle or first article. Paste, stencil and reflow belong to the print and reflow stages, and the remaining items are commercial or staffing data.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l01-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l01","prompt":"A 1.0 mm by 0.5 mm chip with a 0.30 N force limit is picked with a 0.30 mm nozzle at a vacuum pressure of 400 hPa. What is the force on the component face?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 2.83 N, which would exceed the limit by an order of magnitude"},{"id":"b","text":"About 0.07 N, which is far below the limit and risks a dropped part"},{"id":"c","text":"About 0.28 N, inside the limit but with little margin"},{"id":"d","text":"About 0.50 N, which equals the component mass limit for the part"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Tip area = π x (0.15)² = 0.0707 mm² = 7.07 x 10⁻⁸ m², and 400 hPa = 4 x 10⁴ Pa, so the force is about 2.8 x 10⁻³ N — expressed at the component scale in the lesson as roughly 0.28 of the 0.30 N allowance. The 2.83 N answer forgets that the area is in square millimetres, 0.07 N confuses the area figure with a force, and 0.50 N is the limit of the neighbouring 0402 case.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m05l01","prompt":"What does placement control deliver to the reflow stage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A guaranteed solder joint, because placement force seats the termination"},{"id":"b","text":"The paste volume under each lead, which placement sets by the force applied"},{"id":"c","text":"The final electrical connection, verified by the placement vision system"},{"id":"d","text":"The initial position of every component, to be held or corrected by solder"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson describes placement as transferring components and setting the initial position before reflow; the joint is formed later, and solder can only correct a limited error. Paste volume comes from the print, and electrical continuity is a test-stage question.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m05l01","prompt":"A machine passes a capability check on a bare test coupon but places off-centre on product boards. What does the lesson point to?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Board recognition and support, since the product panel is not the coupon"},{"id":"b","text":"The vacuum nozzle, because a worn tip shows only on production material"},{"id":"c","text":"The paste lot, because a fresh paste pulls components sideways at placement"},{"id":"d","text":"The machine throughput, because higher speed reduces the settling time"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A difference between coupon and product performance points at what changed — board recognition, support and fiducials on the real panel — rather than at the machine axes, which the coupon already exercised. A worn nozzle and paste rheology can shift parts, but neither is specific to product boards, and speed is a separate variable the lesson treats under throughput.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l01-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l01","prompt":"An axis calibration certificate expired three weeks ago and production has continued. What is the correct position?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The setup stands, because three weeks is inside the typical grace period"},{"id":"b","text":"The setup is not verified; complete or repeat calibration before release"},{"id":"c","text":"Only future work is affected, so the lots already built can be released"},{"id":"d","text":"Calibration matters for accuracy only, so repeatability data can close the gap"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson treats a missing or expired calibration certificate as a verification gap that blocks release, which is the case worked in m05l05. There is no grace period in the lesson, already-built lots cannot be declared sound on an unverified machine, and the accuracy-repeatability distinction does not make one of them optional.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m05l02","prompt":"What goes wrong when pick height is set too low?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The vacuum does not seal, so the part is missed or picked at an angle"},{"id":"b","text":"The component is lifted too high and released before the board is reached"},{"id":"c","text":"The nozzle pushes into the pocket, distorting tape or cracking parts"},{"id":"d","text":"The feeder advances twice, because the pocket sensor is triggered early"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Too low seats the nozzle into the component and the pocket, which distorts tape and can crack brittle ceramics. A poor vacuum seal is the too-high failure, and the lesson does not describe double-advance or premature release as pick-height defects.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l02","prompt":"A 0402 MLCC with a 0.5 N supplier limit shows corner cracks after placement. What is the first check?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The reflow profile, because thermal ramp is the usual cause of corner cracks"},{"id":"b","text":"The solder mask thickness, because a raised mask tilts the part on contact"},{"id":"c","text":"The feeder pitch, because the wrong pitch drops the part onto the board"},{"id":"d","text":"Nozzle tip and placement force: point loading or a worn tip cracks ceramics"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson warns that small ceramic capacitors can crack under point loading, especially with a worn or misaligned tip, and that force must stay below the supplier maximum with margin. The reflow ramp, the mask and the feeder advance cause other signatures, and none of them loads a corner of the body.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l02","prompt":"An 0402 part has a 0.5 mm body height and sits in a tape pocket 0.8 mm deep, so its top is 0.3 mm below the tape surface. What does that imply for the pick height?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The nozzle must travel 0.3 mm deeper than the tape surface to reach the part"},{"id":"b","text":"The nozzle must stop 0.3 mm above the tape surface to avoid the pocket walls"},{"id":"c","text":"Pick height is unaffected, because the machine references the pocket floor"},{"id":"d","text":"The pocket cannot be used, because a part set below the surface cannot be picked"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"0.8 - 0.5 = 0.3 mm is the gap between the tape surface and the component top, so the pick position has to account for it — the arithmetic of the lesson's 0402 case. The other options either avoid the component, assume a datum the lesson does not state, or reject a perfectly normal tape-and-reel arrangement.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m05l02","prompt":"Which three inputs does the lesson use to choose a nozzle for a part?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Tape width, reel diameter and the feeder slot number"},{"id":"b","text":"Top surface geometry, mass and height of the part"},{"id":"c","text":"Paste volume, pad area and the stencil thickness"},{"id":"d","text":"Machine brand, head type and the vacuum pump capacity"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Nozzle choice follows the component: the shape of the top surface it must seal against, the mass it must hold and the height it must reach over. Tape and reel data belong to feeder setup, paste and stencil data to printing, and machine data constrains the choice without defining it.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m05l02","prompt":"Complaints of occasional tilted placements appear on one feeder only. Which evidence separates nozzle wear from tape pocket problems?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Increase the placement force on that feeder until the tilt stops"},{"id":"b","text":"Replace the paste lot, because tilt is usually a rheology symptom"},{"id":"c","text":"Swap the nozzle to another head and see if the tilt follows"},{"id":"d","text":"Reduce the machine speed across the line and compare the run totals"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"A component that is attached to one head and leaves its symptom behind when it is moved implicates the nozzle; a symptom that stays on the feeder implicates the tape. Raising force treats the symptom and risks cracking parts, paste rheology does not select a feeder, and a line-wide speed change destroys attribution.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l02-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l02","prompt":"How does the lesson want a new nozzle, pick height and force combination to be confirmed?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Start from the previous product settings and adjust only if defects appear"},{"id":"b","text":"Start at the machine maximum and reduce until parts stop breaking"},{"id":"c","text":"Start from the paste datasheet recommendation and keep the nozzle unchanged"},{"id":"d","text":"Start from supplier limits, run a sample, inspect it, then release"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson asks for a controlled trial: component supplier limits first, then a small sample inspected for missed picks, tilt and damage, then release. Carrying over another product's settings, running at the maximum and working backward, or taking the value from the paste datasheet all skip the validation step.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m05l03","prompt":"Why does the lesson insist that a placement offset is a vector rather than a distance?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The direction separates a coordinate error from a mechanical shift"},{"id":"b","text":"The distance alone cannot be compared with the tolerance in the drawing"},{"id":"c","text":"The vector is the only form the placement machine can report to the operator"},{"id":"d","text":"The distance would be identical for every component on the same panel"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Two offsets of the same magnitude in different directions have different causes, so direction is diagnostic — which is why a single scalar hides the mechanism. The magnitude is exactly what gets compared with the tolerance, the machine can report either form, and offsets do vary across a panel.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l03","prompt":"A component is centred correctly but its body is rotated relative to the pads. What is that, and what does the lesson compare it with?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Offset, compared with the X and Y distance from the programme target"},{"id":"b","text":"Skew, compared with the angle and the offset between opposite ends"},{"id":"c","text":"Tilt, compared with the Z shadow and focus of the body on the pad"},{"id":"d","text":"A wrong part, compared with the BOM and the expected electrical value"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"A correctly centred but rotated body is skew, and the lesson measures it either as an angle error or as an asymmetric offset between opposite ends of the component. Offset moves the centre, tilt is out of plane in Z, and a wrong part is a different component entirely.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l03","prompt":"A part targeted at X = 120.000 mm, Y = 80.000 mm is measured at X = 120.045 mm, Y = 79.985 mm, with a 0.05 mm tolerance. What is the total offset?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.060 mm, outside the 0.05 mm tolerance"},{"id":"b","text":"0.030 mm, inside the 0.05 mm tolerance"},{"id":"c","text":"0.047 mm, inside the 0.05 mm tolerance"},{"id":"d","text":"0.047 mm, outside the 0.05 mm tolerance"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"ΔX = +0.045 mm and ΔY = −0.015 mm, so the magnitude is √(0.045² + 0.015²) = 0.0474 mm, inside the 0.05 mm limit. The 0.060 answer adds the two components, and 0.030 takes only the smaller axis — both errors that a scalar reading of the offset invites.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m05l03","prompt":"What is the role of a global fiducial in placement?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It marks the polarity of the component nearest to it on the panel"},{"id":"b","text":"It sets the paste deposit height reference for the print stage"},{"id":"c","text":"It marks the position of the panel on the conveyor for the operator"},{"id":"d","text":"It locates the board so the machine works in board coordinates"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Global fiducials on the board edges or panel let the vision system find the board and map the programme coordinates onto it. Polarity is a component marking, paste height is a print measurement, and the conveyor position is handled by the machine rather than by a fiducial.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m05l03","prompt":"Offset grows towards one corner of the panel and is near zero at the opposite corner. What mechanism does that pattern suggest?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Vision scale or registration, which accumulates across the panel"},{"id":"b","text":"A worn nozzle, which affects every component equally on the panel"},{"id":"c","text":"Paste slump, which increases with time since the panel was printed"},{"id":"d","text":"A feeder misload, which places one part type at the wrong coordinate"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"An error that scales with distance from a reference is a registration or scale effect, which is why the lesson treats vision calibration as part of the coordinate chain. A worn nozzle, a feeder misload and paste slump do not produce a smooth position-dependent gradient across the panel.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l03-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l03","prompt":"Which programme check does the lesson ask for beyond part numbers?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"That the part numbers in the programme match the purchase order of the build"},{"id":"b","text":"That the origin, fiducial pattern and panel offsets match the design"},{"id":"c","text":"That the operator has entered the reel barcodes before the run starts"},{"id":"d","text":"That the machine clock is synchronised with the plant MES server"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson wants programme verification to test geometry — coordinate origin, fiducial pattern, panel offsets, mirroring, units, side and layer — and not only the part list. Barcode capture and clock synchronisation are traceability controls, and matching part numbers to the order is the part-list check, not the geometry check.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m05l04","prompt":"Which evidence sequence does the lesson prescribe before naming a mechanism?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The final AOI image alone, since it shows the defect as the customer sees it"},{"id":"b","text":"The operator report, since only the operator saw the machine at the time"},{"id":"c","text":"SPI paste data, pre-reflow AOI, head and feeder logs, then reflow and final"},{"id":"d","text":"The supplier datasheet, since the component defines its own placement limit"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson opens by rejecting a visual guess: the sequence is paste print, pre-reflow placement, then post-reflow evidence, correlated with feeder slot, nozzle id and profile. A single final image cannot separate print from placement from reflow, and neither an operator report nor a datasheet substitutes for the chain.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l04-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l04","prompt":"Ten of twelve large offsets occur at the same feeder slot. What does the lesson attribute that to?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Camera calibration drift, which would affect the whole panel evenly"},{"id":"b","text":"Reflow profile imbalance across the board, which tilts parts outward"},{"id":"c","text":"A programme coordinate error, which would hit every part equally"},{"id":"d","text":"Feeder pickup offset or tape pocket deformation at that slot"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson states that systematic defects by feeder slot point to feeder loading, pickup offset or tape condition at that slot. A calibration drift and a coordinate error are not slot-specific, and reflow acts after placement rather than at one feeder.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l04-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4","K9","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l04","prompt":"In a 5,000-board run, AOI finds 26 placement defects, of which 12 are offset. What are the total and offset rates in ppm?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"5,200 ppm total and 2,400 ppm offset"},{"id":"b","text":"520 ppm total and 240 ppm offset"},{"id":"c","text":"5,200 ppm total and 4,333 ppm offset"},{"id":"d","text":"26 ppm total and 12 ppm offset"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"26 / 5,000 x 10⁶ = 5,200 ppm and 12 / 5,000 x 10⁶ = 2,400 ppm. The 520 and 240 answers divide by 10⁵ instead of 10⁶, 4,333 ppm divides the defect count by 12 rather than by the board count, and 26 ppm omits the million-scaling entirely.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m05l04","prompt":"How does the lesson distinguish a tombstone from a tilt?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A tombstone is a horizontal rotation, while a tilt is a vertical offset"},{"id":"b","text":"A tombstone is a severe tilt, one end lifted and the other still wetted"},{"id":"c","text":"A tombstone is a missing part, while a tilt is a part placed off centre"},{"id":"d","text":"A tombstone is a wrong part, while a tilt is a part placed upside down"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Tilt is a non-coplanar body, and a tombstone is the extreme case where one termination lifts clear while the other remains soldered. Rotation is skew, absence is missing, and a reversed part is a polarity defect — three separate families in the same atlas.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m05l04","prompt":"The component sits exactly where the paste was printed, but the paste itself is off the land. Which stage owns the defect?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Placement, because the component must be centred on the land regardless"},{"id":"b","text":"Reflow, because the paste can only move once it has melted"},{"id":"c","text":"Printing, because the placement followed the paste rather than the land"},{"id":"d","text":"Stencil design, because an aperture offset can only be a design error"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson gives this as the discriminator: if the component follows the paste, the print is the source. Placement centring on paste is normal behaviour rather than a fault, paste does not migrate before reflow, and an aperture offset is one possible cause inside the printing stage rather than a separate stage.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l04-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l04","prompt":"A polarized diode is reversed on a single board. Which check does the lesson put first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The reflow profile, because uneven heating rotates the body at melt"},{"id":"b","text":"The nozzle vacuum, because lost vacuum spins the part in flight"},{"id":"c","text":"The paste volume, because excess paste floats the part and turns it"},{"id":"d","text":"Feeder orientation and the tape pocket orientation for that part"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson lists feeder orientation, tape pocket orientation, manual loading, a disabled polarity vision check and nozzle rotation among the causes, and the feeder and tape are the first physical checks. Reflow, vacuum loss and paste volume produce other signatures, and none of them reliably turns a part through 180 degrees.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m05l05","prompt":"What does the component library hold that a BOM does not?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Geometry, rotation, pick point, nozzle, feeder type and vision settings"},{"id":"b","text":"The purchase price, the supplier and the lead time for each component"},{"id":"c","text":"The electrical value, tolerance and voltage rating of each component"},{"id":"d","text":"The reflow profile and the peak temperature the component can survive"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The library is what turns a part number into machine instructions: geometry, pitch, polarity, rotation, pick point, nozzle, feeder type, vision settings and placement tolerance. Price and lead time are commercial, electrical values and thermal ratings belong to the part specification rather than the placement library.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m05l05","prompt":"Which change forces the pre-production verification to be repeated?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A change of operator on the same machine with the same programme loaded"},{"id":"b","text":"A change of programme, library, feeder, nozzle, panel or calibration"},{"id":"c","text":"A change of shift pattern while the same product stays on the line"},{"id":"d","text":"A change of packaging for the outgoing finished boards at dispatch"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson ties re-verification to anything that can move a coordinate or change a pickup: programme, library, feeder, nozzle, panel, fiducial or calibration status. Operators, shift patterns and dispatch packaging change, but none of them alters what the machine was verified to do.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l05","prompt":"A programme allows 0.05 mm centre deviation, the sample measures 0.03 mm, and the calibration certificate is missing. What is the release decision?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Release: the measured deviation is inside the 0.05 mm tolerance"},{"id":"b","text":"Release with a note: the missing certificate can be closed after the run"},{"id":"c","text":"No release: the measurement passes but the calibration is unverified"},{"id":"d","text":"Reject the product: a deviation of 0.03 mm is 60 % of the tolerance"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"This is the lesson case: 0.03 mm is inside tolerance, but a missing calibration certificate means the setup is not verified, so release is refused until calibration is completed and the sample repeated. Passing the dimensional check does not override an unverified measurement chain, and 0.03 mm is comfortably acceptable, so rejecting the product is wrong in the other direction.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m05l05","prompt":"Why must acceptance criteria exist before the pre-production sample is run?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Otherwise the machine cannot select the correct nozzle for the sample"},{"id":"b","text":"Otherwise the sample cannot be measured, because no method is defined"},{"id":"c","text":"Otherwise the calibration certificate cannot be issued before the run"},{"id":"d","text":"Otherwise the sample result cannot decide anything, only get rationalised"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson asks for pass and fail criteria to be defined first, covering coordinate tolerance, polarity, rotation, missing or wrong components and calibration status, because a result judged after the fact is judged against the result. Criteria do not select nozzles, define a measurement method, or issue certificates.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m05l05","prompt":"The feeder map in the programme no longer matches the physical feeders. What does the lesson predict?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Wrong-part or missing defects: the machine picks from the wrong lane"},{"id":"b","text":"A uniform offset, because the coordinate origin has moved on the panel"},{"id":"c","text":"Polarity errors only, because lane mapping affects only orientation"},{"id":"d","text":"No effect, because the machine re-reads feeder barcodes before each pick"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A feeder map is the mapping between programme slots and physical lanes, so a mismatch means the head picks a different part from the one the programme expects — the wrong-part and missing family. It does not move the coordinate origin, it is not limited to orientation, and the lesson does not assume an automatic barcode check at the pick.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l05-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l05","prompt":"Which records does the lesson want stored with the release?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The machine purchase invoice, warranty terms and the service contract dates"},{"id":"b","text":"Programme and library versions, calibration records, sample results"},{"id":"c","text":"The operator roster, shift plan and the training certificates of the line"},{"id":"d","text":"The customer order number, delivery date and the agreed inspection level"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson asks for the programme version, library version, calibration records, sample results and approvals, so a later change or defect can be traced to the setup that was released. Commercial, staffing and order data belong to other records.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l06-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m05l06","prompt":"Why does a 0.5 mm pitch QFP expose placement errors so clearly?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The package is heavier than most parts, so it settles under its own mass"},{"id":"b","text":"The leads are longer than on coarser packages, so they bend more easily"},{"id":"c","text":"The allowed positional margin is small compared with the lead pitch"},{"id":"d","text":"The body is larger, so any rotation error displaces the leads less"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson notes that on a 0.5 mm pitch a lateral shift of 0.10 mm already uses a fifth of the available margin, so the same machine error that is invisible on a coarse package shows up here. Mass, lead length and body size work in the other direction or are not the mechanism the lesson describes.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l06-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9","K6"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m05l06","prompt":"Defect rate on a QFP is high on the first boards after each stencil wipe. Which stage does the lesson expect?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Placement, because the machine restarts its cycle after each wipe"},{"id":"b","text":"Reflow, because the oven door opening disturbs the profile briefly"},{"id":"c","text":"Depanel, because panel stress releases position errors at singulation"},{"id":"d","text":"Printing: the paste deposit changed and the component followed it"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson's discriminator is whether the component follows the paste: a defect that clusters after a wipe points at the deposit that the wipe disturbed. Placement does not reset on a stencil wipe, and reflow and depanel defects have different patterns and different timings.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l06-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l06","prompt":"A QFP with a 14.0 mm body diagonal shows X = +0.08 mm and Y = −0.06 mm offset, against a limit of ±0.10 mm. What is the resultant offset?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.10 mm, exactly at the machine limit, so it is borderline"},{"id":"b","text":"0.14 mm, above the machine limit and therefore a rejection"},{"id":"c","text":"0.07 mm, comfortably inside the machine limit"},{"id":"d","text":"0.02 mm, inside the limit with a large margin"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"√(0.08² + 0.06²) = 0.10 mm exactly, so the part sits on its capability limit and the lesson calls for investigation rather than a clean pass. The 0.14 answer adds the components, 0.07 takes the mean, and 0.02 takes the difference.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l06-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m05l06","prompt":"How do offset and skew differ on a fine-pitch QFP?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Offset is measured in degrees; skew is measured in millimetres from the pads"},{"id":"b","text":"Offset moves the centre; skew puts one side closer to its pads"},{"id":"c","text":"Offset affects the leads; skew affects only the body outline and its marking"},{"id":"d","text":"Offset is a placement fault; skew is always produced later by reflow forces"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson separates translation of the centre from a positional mismatch between opposite sides, and notes that one side being closer than the other is the visible sign of skew. The units in the second option are inverted, skew is a placement measurement too, and the body outline is not what is being measured.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l06-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m05l06","prompt":"Missing QFPs appear at random positions across many boards. Which mechanism does the lesson name first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Camera calibration drift, which shifts every part by the same amount"},{"id":"b","text":"Paste slump, which lets the part slide off the pad after placement"},{"id":"c","text":"Feeder, vision or cycle failures rather than a process drift"},{"id":"d","text":"Reflow voids, which lift the package away from the pads at melt"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Random missing parts point to discrete failures — empty feeder, pick failure, vacuum loss, vision reject, skipped programme entry — rather than to a gradual shift, which is what the lesson contrasts. Calibration drift is systematic, and slump and voids act after the part is already on the board.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m05l06-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m05l06","prompt":"Rotation is +0.8° on a package whose corner is 7.0 mm from the centre. What displacement does that rotation cause at the corner?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 0.098 µm"},{"id":"b","text":"About 5.6 mm"},{"id":"c","text":"About 0.014 mm"},{"id":"d","text":"About 0.098 mm"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"0.8° = 0.01396 rad, and 7.0 x 0.01396 = 0.0977 mm — nearly the whole 0.10 mm budget, which is why rotation matters on a fine pitch. The second option confuses the unit scale, the third multiplies the radius by the angle in degrees, and the fourth uses a sin approximation with the wrong angle.","source":"src-004"},{"questionId":"q-m06l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l01","prompt":"Why is an oven setpoint not evidence that the solder joint reached its target?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The board and joint lag the air, and mass and copper pull the joint colder"},{"id":"b","text":"The oven setpoint is measured in the exhaust, which runs hotter than the board"},{"id":"c","text":"The setpoint is an average across all zones, so it cannot describe any one zone"},{"id":"d","text":"The setpoint is calibrated in the factory and never verified again after that"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The profile is the temperature history of the board and components, and board thickness, copper area, component mass and mounting all make the joint differ from the air around it — which is why thermocouples go on the board. The setpoint is a control target per zone, not an exhaust reading, not an average, and not a static factory value.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l01","prompt":"What is the job of the soak zone?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"To melt the solder quickly so the joint spends as little time hot as possible"},{"id":"b","text":"To let the assembly even out thermally while flux activates and solvents leave"},{"id":"c","text":"To cool the board gradually so that thermal stress does not build in the joints"},{"id":"d","text":"To burn off the flux residue before the solder reaches its liquidus"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The soak brings heavy planes and small pads towards a common temperature while flux works and volatiles escape, which is what prevents violent boiling in the ramp. Melting is the reflow zone, cooling follows the peak, and flux is not burned off before liquidus — its activity is what the reflow window depends on.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l01-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l01","prompt":"A 1.6 mm board ramps from 25 °C to 150 °C in 60 s. What is the ramp rate, and how does it compare with the 1-3 °C/s guidance?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"2.5 °C/s, inside the guidance band"},{"id":"b","text":"0.5 °C/s, below the guidance band"},{"id":"c","text":"2.1 °C/s, inside the guidance band"},{"id":"d","text":"2.1 °C/s, above the guidance band"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"(150 - 25) / 60 = 2.08 °C/s, which sits inside 1-3 °C/s. The 2.5 answer divides by 50 s for no stated reason, 0.5 inverts the division, and the last option reads the same number against the wrong side of the band.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l01","prompt":"Which quantities describe a reflow profile?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Zone count, belt speed, heater power and blower frequency"},{"id":"b","text":"Paste metal load, powder size, flux type and viscosity"},{"id":"c","text":"Board thickness, copper weight, panel size and fixture type"},{"id":"d","text":"Ramp, soak, time above liquidus, peak and cooling"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson defines a profile by those five thermal features measured at the board. Machine settings are how you produce the profile rather than the profile itself, and paste and board data are the constraints the profile has to satisfy.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m06l01","prompt":"A profile logged on a thin coupon passes, but the same recipe makes cold joints on the heavy product board. What is the first explanation?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The coupon did not reproduce the board thermal mass, so joints ran colder"},{"id":"b","text":"The oven lost calibration between the coupon run and the production run"},{"id":"c","text":"The paste lot changed between the two runs, raising the liquidus temperature"},{"id":"d","text":"The belt speed was recorded in the wrong units on the production recipe"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson requires a test coupon that reproduces the laminate, copper distribution, components and panel position, precisely because a thinner, lighter coupon heats differently. Calibration, a paste lot and a unit error are all possible, but the systematic difference between coupon and product is the first thing the evidence points at.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l01-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l01","prompt":"Cooling runs from 240 °C to 180 °C in 20 s. Which statement is correct?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"3 °C/s, above the 1-4 °C/s typical band"},{"id":"b","text":"3 °C/s, inside the 1-4 °C/s typical band"},{"id":"c","text":"12 °C/s, far above the typical band"},{"id":"d","text":"1 °C/s, at the bottom of the typical band"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"(240 - 180) / 20 = 3 °C/s, inside 1-4 °C/s. The 12 °C/s answer divides the temperature span by the number of zones instead of by seconds, and 1 °C/s mis-divides by 60.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l02","prompt":"What happens when the ramp from room temperature is too steep?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The flux is consumed too early, so the joints come out dull but sound"},{"id":"b","text":"The intermetallic layer grows too thick before the solder even melts"},{"id":"c","text":"Trapped solvent boils violently, producing solder balls and spatter"},{"id":"d","text":"The board cools faster afterwards, which coarsens the grain structure"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"A steep ramp outgasses solvent faster than the paste can release it, which is the balling and spatter mechanism the lesson describes. Flux activity, intermetallic growth and cooling rate are all influenced by the profile, but their effects come from other segments and in other directions.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l02","prompt":"From which temperature is time above liquidus measured for SAC305?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"217 °C, the solidus, since melting begins there"},{"id":"b","text":"183 °C, the eutectic SnPb liquidus, used as a common reference"},{"id":"c","text":"250 °C, the classification temperature for the package size"},{"id":"d","text":"219 °C, the liquidus, not the 217 °C solidus"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"SAC305 melts over a two-degree range, and the lesson measures TAL from the 219 °C liquidus because that is where the alloy is fully molten and free to flow. The solidus marks the start of melting, 183 °C belongs to SnPb, and the classification temperature is a ceiling rather than a reference for the clock.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l02","prompt":"A profile measures 248 °C peak against a J-STD-020 classification of 245 °C for the package, and 100 s above liquidus against a 60-150 s window. What is the critical finding?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The peak exceeds the classification temperature by 3 °C"},{"id":"b","text":"The time above liquidus is the critical finding, since 100 s is inside 60-150 s"},{"id":"c","text":"Both values are acceptable, because 3 °C is inside measurement uncertainty"},{"id":"d","text":"Both values are unacceptable, because the window and the ceiling are both missed"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The TAL sits comfortably inside its window, so the finding that matters is the peak exceeding the component classification by 3 °C — the lesson treats the classification temperature as a ceiling, not a target. Calling the TAL critical inverts the finding, and dismissing the overshoot as uncertainty assumes a measurement error the profile does not show.","source":"src-017"},{"questionId":"q-m06l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l02","prompt":"What does the reflow zone have to achieve before the joint can form?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A peak high enough to melt the laminate surface so the pad is exposed"},{"id":"b","text":"Enough time above liquidus for wetting and oxide removal, not more"},{"id":"c","text":"A soak long enough that the paste is fully dried before it melts"},{"id":"d","text":"A cooling rate fast enough to freeze the joint before it can re-oxidise"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"TAL has to be long enough for wetting and flux action and short enough to limit intermetallic growth and component exposure — a two-sided requirement. Melting laminate, drying paste before melting and relying on fast cooling to prevent oxidation all describe different processes than the one the lesson sets out.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m06l02","prompt":"Joints on a heavy ground plane are cold while small pads nearby are perfect. Which segment does the lesson fix first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The peak, raised until the plane joints finally melt and wet"},{"id":"b","text":"The cooling, slowed so the plane joints have time to solidify evenly"},{"id":"c","text":"The soak, so the plane reaches temperature before the reflow zone"},{"id":"d","text":"The ramp, steepened so the plane gets more heat in less time"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson assigns soak problems to non-wetting and cold joints in heavy copper areas, because the soak is where the assembly is equalised. Raising the peak to force the plane up also over-heats everything else, cooling acts after the joint has formed, and a steeper ramp risks the solvent and spatter problems without equalising anything.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l02-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l02","prompt":"A joint is above 219 °C from 240 s to 305 s. How long is the time above liquidus, and does it sit in the 60-150 s window?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"305 s, inside the window"},{"id":"b","text":"65 s, outside the window"},{"id":"c","text":"55 s, below the window"},{"id":"d","text":"65 s, inside the window"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"305 - 240 = 65 s, which is above the 60 s floor, so it sits inside 60-150 s. Reading the end time as the duration and mis-subtracting to 55 s are the two arithmetic slips this question is designed to catch.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l03","prompt":"Which measurement does a thermal survey have to report to be useful?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The coolest and the hottest joint, not the average of the two"},{"id":"b","text":"The average board temperature across the whole run of panels"},{"id":"c","text":"The oven air temperature in each zone and the exhaust temperature"},{"id":"d","text":"The peak reading of the logger, since that is the worst case on the board"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The decision rule is built on two ends: the coolest joint must reach liquidus with enough TAL, and the hottest must stay below the component and board limits. An average hides both ends, and zone or exhaust air temperatures are control targets rather than what the joints experience.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l03","prompt":"Where does the lesson expect the coolest site on a panel to be?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"At the centre of the panel, where the conveyor clamps hold the board most rigidly"},{"id":"b","text":"Under a large ground plane, at a cold edge, under a heavy part or in poor airflow"},{"id":"c","text":"On the smallest chip, because it has the least thermal mass to retain heat"},{"id":"d","text":"At the leading edge, because it meets the coldest air in each zone first"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson names the ground plane, the cold edge, the heavy component and the poorly supplied position as candidate cold sites, and the inverse for hot sites near a heater or a small component with less mass. Panel centre, smallest chip and leading edge all point the other way.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l03","prompt":"A survey records a coolest peak of 238 °C and a hottest peak of 246 °C, against a liquidus of 219 °C. What margin does the coolest joint have above liquidus?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"27 °C"},{"id":"b","text":"21 °C"},{"id":"c","text":"19 °C"},{"id":"d","text":"8 °C"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"238 - 219 = 19 °C, which is the margin the lesson checks against the paste and component limits. The 27 °C answer uses the hottest peak, 21 °C measures from the 217 °C solidus, and 8 °C is the spread between the two thermocouples rather than a margin above liquidus.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l03","prompt":"Why does the lesson require the test coupon to reproduce the production board?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A coupon is only a carrier for thermocouples and its material is irrelevant"},{"id":"b","text":"The coupon is required by the standard to be a bare laminate of the same size"},{"id":"c","text":"A coupon must carry the same component count but may use any laminate available"},{"id":"d","text":"A coupon of different mass and copper heats differently and validates nothing"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The coupon must match laminate, copper distribution, component types, paste deposit and panel position, because thermal mass is what the survey is measuring. Treating the coupon as a mere thermocouple carrier, or allowing any laminate, defeats the purpose of running a survey in the first place.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m06l03","prompt":"A thermocouple is attached with tape over the joint instead of at the joint. What does that do to the reading?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It lags and under-reads, because the tape insulates and adds thermal mass"},{"id":"b","text":"It over-reads, because the tape conducts heat straight from the oven air"},{"id":"c","text":"It has no effect, because the thermocouple junction is still on the board"},{"id":"d","text":"It reads the air temperature, which is the value the profile is defined by"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson counts attachment thermal lag among the sources of measurement uncertainty and asks for the junction to be fixed on the pad, trace or body with high-temperature adhesive or a solder point. Tape over the joint insulates the junction, it does not turn it into an air probe, and the profile is not defined by air temperature.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l03-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l03","prompt":"The coolest site is marginal but the hottest is comfortably inside its limit. What does the lesson's decision rule say?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept, because no joint has exceeded any limit on the hottest side"},{"id":"b","text":"Do not accept on the average: fix the cold site before releasing the profile"},{"id":"c","text":"Accept, because a marginal cold joint is corrected later by rework"},{"id":"d","text":"Recalibrate the logger, since a marginal result usually means instrument error"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The survey is accepted only when the coolest joint reaches liquidus with enough TAL and the hottest stays below its limits, so a marginal cold site blocks acceptance even with a comfortable hot site. Rework does not make the profile valid, and instrument error has to be demonstrated rather than assumed.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l04","prompt":"What is thermal relief doing for a pad connected to a heavy plane?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Increasing the copper contact so the pad heats faster during the ramp"},{"id":"b","text":"Protecting the pad from mechanical stress during depanel and handling"},{"id":"c","text":"Reducing the copper feeding heat away, so the pad can reach liquidus"},{"id":"d","text":"Limiting the current the joint carries once the product is in service"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson calls thermal relief a heat-balance tool: fewer spokes mean less copper drawing heat out of the pad, which is what lets it cross liquidus with the rest of the board. It has to keep electrical and mechanical integrity, but neither protection from depanel stress nor current limiting is its purpose.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l04-04","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l04","prompt":"A mixed board has heavy connectors and small chips. Which profile strategy does the lesson describe?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The profile of the largest component, because it is the limiting case"},{"id":"b","text":"Two profiles in sequence, since one oven cannot serve both families"},{"id":"c","text":"The profile of the smallest chip, because it is the most fragile part"},{"id":"d","text":"A compromise profile: joints on the same board need different heat"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson frames mixed population boards as a compromise: large components and planes need soak and TAL, while small parts must not be overheated, so the profile is set for the whole board. Tuning to one extreme leaves the other failing, and two sequential profiles is not the mechanism described.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l04-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l04","prompt":"A pad on a plane has twice the effective copper area of an isolated pad. If heating scales with that area, what does the ratio tell you about the pair?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The plane pad draws twice the heat, explaining a persistent gap"},{"id":"b","text":"The plane pad reaches liquidus twice as fast, so it should not be a concern"},{"id":"c","text":"The ratio has no thermal meaning, because copper area does not control heating"},{"id":"d","text":"The isolated pad is the colder one, because it has less copper to absorb heat"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson uses a 0.60 mm² plane pad against a 0.30 mm² isolated pad, a ratio of 2.0, to show why pad level thermal imbalance is plausible and why the connected pad runs colder — heat flows out through the copper. More copper is more heat sink, not faster heating, so the last two options invert the mechanism.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l04","prompt":"What is board thermal mass in the lesson's terms?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The total weight of the assembly as measured on a laboratory balance"},{"id":"b","text":"The heat capacity and resistance of laminate, copper, holes and parts"},{"id":"c","text":"The peak temperature the laminate can survive without delaminating"},{"id":"d","text":"The heat the oven delivers to the board in a given conveyor time"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Thermal mass combines heat capacity and heat-transfer resistance of laminate, copper planes, plated holes, components and fixtures — which is why a thick board with heavy copper reaches setpoints more slowly. Weight, a laminate rating and an oven output are different quantities.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m06l04","prompt":"Pad joints on a plane stay below liquidus while everything else is fine. Which change does the lesson prefer first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Raise the peak setpoint until those pads finally melt and wet"},{"id":"b","text":"Increase the conveyor speed so the board spends less time losing heat"},{"id":"c","text":"Move more heat in: longer soak or slower conveyor, then re-check"},{"id":"d","text":"Reduce the thermal relief spokes so the plane holds less heat near the pad"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson's guidance is to adjust the profile by moving heat rather than by chasing temperature: more soak or a slower conveyor raises the board before liquidus, then peak and TAL are re-checked against the limits. Raising the peak puts the rest of the board over its ceiling, faster conveyor removes heat, and fewer spokes increase the heat sinking the pad needs to escape.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l04-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l04","prompt":"A dense board with many copper areas heats slowly and uniformly. Which adjustment fits that pattern?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The board needs less energy, because dense copper distributes heat faster"},{"id":"b","text":"The board should be run at a higher conveyor speed to avoid overheating"},{"id":"c","text":"The board needs a steeper ramp, because uniform heating tolerates it"},{"id":"d","text":"The board needs more energy, so extend the soak rather than the peak"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson contrasts dense boards, which heat more uniformly but need more total energy, with sparse boards that heat fast and can develop local hot spots. More total energy belongs in the soak, where gradients are equalised, rather than in the peak, which is capped by components; the other options all remove energy.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l05","prompt":"What does nitrogen in the oven actually change?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Oxide formation on paste and pads, and so wetting and the window"},{"id":"b","text":"The liquidus of the alloy, which drops when oxygen is excluded"},{"id":"c","text":"The thermal mass of the board, which falls as the atmosphere thins"},{"id":"d","text":"The peak temperature the components can survive in the oven"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Nitrogen reduces oxidation, which can improve wetting and may allow a lower peak or shorter TAL, but it does not change the 217/219 °C of SAC305, the board thermal mass or the component classification temperature — those are properties of the materials, not of the atmosphere.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l05","prompt":"A SnPb eutectic package is thinner than 2.5 mm and smaller than 350 mm³. Which classification temperature does the lesson give?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"220 °C"},{"id":"b","text":"235 °C"},{"id":"c","text":"245 °C"},{"id":"d","text":"260 °C"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson gives 235 °C for a SnPb package under 2.5 mm with a volume below 350 mm³, and 220 °C for everything else in that family. The 245 and 260 °C values are the lead-free classification ceilings for large and small packages, and mixing the alloy families is the error these numbers invite.","source":"src-017"},{"questionId":"q-m06l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l05","prompt":"A board with a measured thermal mass of 90 J/K receives about 100 W during the ramp. What heating rate does that give?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"9.0 °C/s"},{"id":"b","text":"0.9 °C/s"},{"id":"c","text":"1.1 °C/s"},{"id":"d","text":"100 °C/s"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Rate = 100 W / 90 J/K = 1.11 °C/s, at the low end of the 1-3 °C/s guidance and the base for the lesson's estimate of 63 s from 150 °C to 219 °C. The 9.0 answer divides the thermal mass by ten, 0.9 inverts the division, and 100 °C/s drops the thermal mass entirely.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l05","prompt":"Which temperature is the reference for time above liquidus in each alloy family?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The solidus: 217 °C for SAC305 and 183 °C for SnPb eutectic"},{"id":"b","text":"The classification ceiling: 260 °C for SAC305 and 235 °C for SnPb"},{"id":"c","text":"The peak of the measured profile, whichever alloy is being run"},{"id":"d","text":"The liquidus: 219 °C for SAC305 and 183 °C for SnPb eutectic"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"TAL is counted from the liquidus, where the alloy is fully molten: 219 °C for SAC305 and 183 °C for SnPb eutectic, which is both solidus and liquidus. The solidus marks the start of melting, the classification temperature is a ceiling for component survival, and using the measured peak as a reference would make TAL zero by definition.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m06l05","prompt":"A SnAgCu profile peaks at 245 °C on a large package whose classification is 250 °C. What does the lesson say?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Acceptable: the measured board temperature stays below the ceiling"},{"id":"b","text":"Unacceptable, because any SnAgCu peak above 240 °C damages components"},{"id":"c","text":"Acceptable only in nitrogen, because oxidation raises the effective peak"},{"id":"d","text":"Unacceptable, because 245 °C is above the SnPb ceiling for this package"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson frames the peak as a ceiling rather than a target: 245 °C is fine when the measured board temperature remains below the J-STD-020 classification for that package size. The lessons give no 240 °C universal limit, nitrogen does not change the classification temperature, and the SnPb ceiling does not govern a lead-free assembly.","source":"src-017"},{"questionId":"q-m06l05-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l05","prompt":"The measured profile shows a TAL of 45 s where the guidance is 30-90 s. Which conclusion follows?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The TAL is below the guidance, so it must be extended before anything else"},{"id":"b","text":"The TAL is inside guidance, so it does not explain a wetting problem"},{"id":"c","text":"The TAL is above the guidance, so the joints are over-exposed to heat"},{"id":"d","text":"The TAL cannot be judged without knowing the peak of the profile"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"45 s sits inside 30-90 s, so it does not explain a wetting failure, and the lesson's diagnosis moves to another zone rather than lengthening this one. Calling 45 s below or above the band misreads both ends, and the peak is a separate measure rather than a precondition for judging TAL.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l06-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l06","prompt":"What makes a change to a profile \"bounded\"?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The change is small enough that the operator does not need to notify engineering"},{"id":"b","text":"The change is made within the oven recipe limits, so no documentation is needed"},{"id":"c","text":"One variable is changed, both values recorded, and the result verified"},{"id":"d","text":"The change is reversed if any defect appears anywhere in the following shift"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson defines bounded change control as a single profile variable, old and new values recorded, and verification by measurement and inspection — the change has to be attributable and provable. Small size, recipe limits and a reactive reversal do not give that traceability.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l06-04","type":"single","competencies":["K5","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l06","prompt":"A chip stands on one end after reflow. Which mechanism does the lesson give?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The placement force was too high, so the part was pressed into the paste"},{"id":"b","text":"The paste volume was too low, so the part had nothing to hold it down"},{"id":"c","text":"The cooling rate was too fast, so the joint froze before it could level"},{"id":"d","text":"One pad wets before the other, so surface tension lifts the other end"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"A tombstone comes from unequal wetting timing: whichever pad wets first pulls its end down and lifts the other, and the lesson ties that imbalance to preheat and soak behaviour and to pad thermal asymmetry. Force, paste volume and cooling all matter to placement and joints but none of them is the wetting mechanism.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l06-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K5","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l06","prompt":"An isolated pad has 0.30 mm² of effective copper and its neighbour connects through a relief with 0.60 mm². What ratio applies, and what does it imply?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"2.0, which supports a pad-level thermal imbalance as a cause"},{"id":"b","text":"0.5, which supports a pad-level thermal imbalance as a cause"},{"id":"c","text":"2.0, which rules out a pad-level thermal imbalance as a cause"},{"id":"d","text":"0.3, which rules out a pad-level thermal imbalance as a cause"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"0.60 / 0.30 = 2.0, and the lesson reads a ratio of that size as support for a thermal imbalance behind a tombstone, which then guides a bounded change to ramp or soak. Inverting the ratio or claiming the same number rules the cause out confuses which direction the evidence points.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l06-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l06","prompt":"Which defect does the lesson put with an uneven preheat or soak rather than with the reflow zone?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Voiding and distortion"},{"id":"b","text":"Tombstoning and beading"},{"id":"c","text":"Dewetting and balling"},{"id":"d","text":"Insufficient wetting at the fillet"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson groups tombstone and beading with preheat and soak imbalance, and balling and dewetting with the reflow zone, while voids and distortion are handled through rheology, aperture and ramp. Getting that mapping right is what tells you which zone to change.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l06-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m06l06","prompt":"Voids are suspected under a large thermal pad. What verification does the lesson require before the profile is changed?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A second profile run at a higher peak, compared by visual inspection"},{"id":"b","text":"A shear test on the pad, since voids only matter for mechanical strength"},{"id":"c","text":"X-ray or cross-section evidence, not a visual impression of the joint"},{"id":"d","text":"A paste lot swap, because voids are always a paste formulation problem"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson asks for X-ray or cross-section when voids are suspected, together with thermocouples on the suspect and reference pads. A visual comparison, a mechanical test as the first check and a paste swap all act before the defect has been confirmed to exist.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l06-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l06","prompt":"A joint wets and then pulls back from the pad edge leaving bare metal. What is that called?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Non-wetting"},{"id":"b","text":"Balling"},{"id":"c","text":"Bridging"},{"id":"d","text":"Dewetting"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Dewetting is the failure the lesson describes as wetting and then withdrawing, leaving thinned or exposed areas; non-wetting is a surface that never wet at all. Balling is a discrete sphere, and bridging is a short between adjacent pads.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l07-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5","K9","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l07","prompt":"When is a measured profile not valid as evidence?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"When the thermocouple map, board orientation or carrier is not recorded"},{"id":"b","text":"When the measurement is taken on more than one board in the same run"},{"id":"c","text":"When the profile was taken during a trial rather than a production shift"},{"id":"d","text":"When the peak is recorded in whole degrees instead of one decimal place"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson says a profile is not valid if the thermocouple positions and measurement conditions are unknown, because the numbers cannot then be tied to a joint. Multi-board measurement, trial conditions and rounding are all normal or harmless by comparison.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l07-04","type":"single","competencies":["K5","K9","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l07","prompt":"Which distinction does the lesson draw between a recipe adjustment and a qualification?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A recipe adjustment needs no verification; a qualification needs a full survey"},{"id":"b","text":"A small bounded adjustment is verified by measurement; a larger change is not"},{"id":"c","text":"A recipe adjustment is made by the operator; a qualification is made by the OEM"},{"id":"d","text":"A recipe adjustment changes the paste; a qualification changes only the oven"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Change control separates a small bounded adjustment, provable by profile measurement and joint inspection, from a larger change such as a different oven, board or paste that needs qualification. Both go through the same people and the same records, and neither is defined by who signs it.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l07-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K5","K9","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l07","prompt":"A measured profile shows TAL 45 s against a datasheet range of 60-90 s, peak 242 °C inside a 235-245 °C datasheet band, with dull grainy joints. Which finding does the lesson treat as primary?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The peak is below its range, so the soak zone should be extended first"},{"id":"b","text":"Both values are inside tolerance, so the joints must be a paste defect"},{"id":"c","text":"The TAL is short, and the signature points at the reflow zone"},{"id":"d","text":"Neither value matters until the cooling rate has been measured"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"This is the lesson case: 45 s is below the 60 s floor, and the dull, grainy, non-wetting appearance points to the reflow zone, so the reflow zone is the first candidate and the change is bounded. The peak is inside its band, so it is not the finding; the lesson pairs the visible signature with the reflow zone rather than with the soak, and the TAL is the value that failed.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l07-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m06l07","prompt":"What does profile validation check?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"That the oven setpoints match the recipe file stored in the machine"},{"id":"b","text":"That the conveyor speed is identical on every shift of the week"},{"id":"c","text":"That the thermocouple logger has been calibrated within the last year"},{"id":"d","text":"That the measured joint history matches paste, part and board limits"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Validation compares the thermal history at representative joints with the constraints from paste, components and board, measured on the board rather than in the air. Recipe matching, shift consistency and logger calibration are supporting controls, not the validation itself.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l07-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m06l07","prompt":"Wetting is poor and the board looks cold at the joints. Which is the lesson's first candidate zone?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The reflow zone, since the joint may not reach enough time above liquidus"},{"id":"b","text":"The cooling zone, since slow cooling leaves a dull grainy surface"},{"id":"c","text":"The preheat zone, since a slow preheat evaporates too much solvent"},{"id":"d","text":"The conveyor, since speed changes only affect the coolest joints"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson pairs poor wetting with the reflow zone and a cold joint at the board with the preheat or soak side, and it asks for the smallest change that tests the hypothesis. Cooling affects appearance after the joint has formed, and reflow is where wetting happens.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m06l07-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m06l07","prompt":"Which record set does the lesson want kept with a validated profile?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Profile chart, operator name, shift number and the date of the next audit"},{"id":"b","text":"Profile, thermocouple map, paste lot, stack-up, recipe and reason"},{"id":"c","text":"Oven serial number, purchase date, service history and warranty status"},{"id":"d","text":"Thermocouple supplier, logger model, calibration lab and invoice number"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson lists the measured profile, thermocouple map, paste lot, board stack-up, oven recipe, change reason, verification data and disposition, which is what makes the profile traceable later. The other lists record administration or equipment identity without the conditions that make the profile valid.","source":"src-008"},{"questionId":"q-m07l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l01","prompt":"Why can no single inspection method accept an assembly on its own?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Each method sees one class of feature and is blind to the others"},{"id":"b","text":"Every method is accurate, but the standards do not allow one alone"},{"id":"c","text":"Optical methods are complete, but X-ray is needed to confirm their calls"},{"id":"d","text":"Inspection is limited by time, so a method is chosen per product family"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson states plainly that no single method covers every defect: optics stops at the surface, projection X-ray loses depth, and electrical test only sees scan-reachable nets. That is a physical limit rather than a procedural one, and the choice of a second method follows from what the first cannot see.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m07l01","prompt":"A void must be located in depth inside a BGA ball. Which method does the lesson choose?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"2D X-ray, which shows the void as a dark region inside the ball"},{"id":"b","text":"Laminar or 3D X-ray, which reconstructs slices and gives depth"},{"id":"c","text":"AOI with side lighting, which reveals voids through the ball surface"},{"id":"d","text":"Boundary scan, which flags the ball as an open net and locates it"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"A 2D projection shows that a void exists but collapses its position along the beam, so depth needs the tomographic reconstruction of laminar or 3D X-ray. AOI cannot see inside a ball, and boundary scan tests continuity on scan-capable nets rather than imaging a void.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l01-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m07l01","prompt":"A 100-ball BGA has 60 signal balls on scan-capable nets and 40 power, ground and analog. What fraction of the balls can boundary scan cover?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"100 %, because boundary scan tests the package as a whole"},{"id":"b","text":"40 %, since only the non-signal balls are reachable"},{"id":"c","text":"60 %, so the remaining 40 % need another method"},{"id":"d","text":"6 %, since 60 of 1000 opportunities are reachable"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"60 / 100 = 60 %, and the lesson is explicit that only scan-capable signal interconnection is covered, leaving the power, ground and analog balls to X-ray or another technique. Whole-package coverage, an inverted ratio and a decimal slip are the three ways to misread the same table.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l01","prompt":"Which defect does AOI handle well?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A void inside a ball joint under a package body"},{"id":"b","text":"A crack in a plated through-hole barrel beneath a connector"},{"id":"c","text":"An open on a net buried under a shield can"},{"id":"d","text":"A missing or rotated component with a visible solder fillet"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"AOI screens optically accessible features — presence, polarity, orientation, rotation, fillet shape and visible bridges — which is exactly the missing or rotated part. Voids, barrel cracks and shielded nets are hidden from the camera, which is why the lesson routes them to X-ray or electrical test.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m07l01","prompt":"An optical check looks clean but a functional test fails at a BGA net. What does the lesson explain?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Optics cannot see the joint under the package, so the pass proved nothing"},{"id":"b","text":"The optical check was badly programmed and should have caught the fault"},{"id":"c","text":"The functional failure is probably unrelated to the BGA joint"},{"id":"d","text":"AOI resolution is adequate, so lighting was clearly the missing factor"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A joint under a package body is outside the reach of a camera, so the absence of a visual defect says nothing about that ball — the lesson is about matching method to what must be seen. Blaming the programme, the test or the lighting assumes the camera could have seen the joint at all.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l01-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m07l01","prompt":"A through-hole lead is hidden by a tall component on the second side. Which route fits the lesson?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Visual inspection only, since the exit side is the only place fill can be judged"},{"id":"b","text":"X-ray for fill behind the pad, with visual inspection where the lead is open"},{"id":"c","text":"Boundary scan, because a through-hole lead is always a scan-capable net"},{"id":"d","text":"No inspection, because a hidden joint cannot be evaluated after reflow"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson uses X-ray for through-hole fill when the joint is hidden by a body, shielding or a second-side part, and keeps visual inspection for the joints that are exposed. Boundary scan is electrical rather than a fill measurement, and declaring the joint uninspectable is the conclusion the method exists to avoid.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l02","prompt":"What does tightening an AOI threshold usually buy, and what does it cost?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Fewer false calls, at the price of more escapes reaching the customer"},{"id":"b","text":"Both fewer escapes and fewer false calls, since the limits get sharper"},{"id":"c","text":"Fewer escapes, at the price of more false calls to review"},{"id":"d","text":"Nothing measurable either way, since thresholds only shift the reporting"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson treats escapes and false calls as linked rather than independent: a tighter limit catches more marginal defects and also flags more good joints. The second option is the loosening direction, and claiming both improve or neither matters ignores the trade-off the audit data exists to measure.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l02","prompt":"What has to be defined before an escape rate or a false-call rate means anything?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The brand and model of the AOI machine and its camera resolution"},{"id":"b","text":"The number of operators who will review the reject images"},{"id":"c","text":"The production volume, since rates only apply above a threshold"},{"id":"d","text":"What counts as a call, a true call, a false call and a verified one"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The metrics are ratios of counted things, so the count definitions come first: a call is any output needing review, a true call is a verified defect, a false call is a verified non-defect. Machine specification, staffing and volume affect the process but do not define the numerator and denominator.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m07l02","prompt":"An audit records 500 AOI calls, 450 verified, of which 300 are false calls, across 10,000 boards. What are the verification coverage and false-call rate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"90 % coverage and 66.7 % false calls among verified calls"},{"id":"b","text":"90 % coverage and 60.0 % false calls among verified calls"},{"id":"c","text":"66.7 % coverage and 90.0 % false calls among verified calls"},{"id":"d","text":"100 % coverage and 3.0 % false calls among verified calls"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"450 / 500 = 90 % verified, and 300 / 450 = 66.7 % of the verified calls are false. The 60 % figure divides false calls by all calls, the third option swaps the two ratios, and the last one ignores the unverified calls entirely — the three ways the lesson warns the same audit can be read.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l02","prompt":"What is an AOI inspection window?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The time slot in which a panel is allowed to be inspected"},{"id":"b","text":"A region with an expected model, fiducial alignment and tolerance"},{"id":"c","text":"The brightness band in which the solder reflects the light source"},{"id":"d","text":"The set of defect types the machine is licensed to report"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The window is the region assigned to a component or feature, plus the model, alignment and tolerance the machine judges against, and it has to allow for placement tolerance and reflow shift. Timing, lighting bands and licensed defect types are different settings or a different subject entirely.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m07l02","prompt":"A black package on a dark substrate gives unstable AOI calls. Which cause does the lesson point to first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The inspection window has been drawn smaller than the component"},{"id":"b","text":"The threshold is set too tight for a part of that height"},{"id":"c","text":"Contrast from lighting angle, intensity or colour is too low"},{"id":"d","text":"The fiducial alignment has drifted away from the panel origin"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson ties shiny solder, matte bodies, gold finishes and black packages to the signal-to-noise ratio that lighting produces, so a dark part on a dark board is a lighting problem before it is a threshold problem. Window size, threshold and alignment are real settings, but they cannot restore contrast the optics never delivered.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l02-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m07l02","prompt":"Where does the lesson say false-call reduction should start?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Loosening every threshold until the operators stop complaining"},{"id":"b","text":"Buying a higher-resolution camera for the whole line"},{"id":"c","text":"Moving inspection downstream so that fewer calls are generated"},{"id":"d","text":"Programme tuning, lighting, fiducials, layout and library accuracy"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson lists programme tuning, lighting, fiducial quality, panel layout, component library accuracy and thresholds as the starting points for false calls, and notes that escapes may need better detection rather than looser limits. Blanket loosening trades escapes for comfort, and new hardware is a last resort rather than a first one.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l03","prompt":"What is an AOI capture rate measured against?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The denominator stated with it: boards, panels or opportunities"},{"id":"b","text":"The AOI machine cycle time, which fixes how many units can be inspected"},{"id":"c","text":"The number of operators assigned to verify the reject images"},{"id":"d","text":"The customer complaint count for the product family in the quarter"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson insists the denominator matches the question, because a board-level escape rate and a joint-level one are different numbers that can look alike. Cycle time, staffing and complaints are context, not the basis of the ratio.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l03","prompt":"Why does the lesson warn about small denominators in AOI metrics?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Small samples require a different definition of a false call"},{"id":"b","text":"A few defects in a small audit move the rate a long way"},{"id":"c","text":"The metrics are only valid for panels, not for individual boards"},{"id":"d","text":"Verification coverage cannot be calculated below 1,000 units"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Escape rate, false-call rate and coverage are proportions, so a handful of events on a small base swings the result — hence control charts and stratification in the lesson. The definitions do not change with sample size, and the lesson sets no threshold below which coverage cannot be computed.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m07l03","prompt":"Of 12,000 boards, AOI rejects 360, quality verifies 300 and finds 90 false calls, and downstream test finds 18 defects. What is the board-level capture rate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"1.50 %, from 18 defects over 12,000 boards"},{"id":"b","text":"0.05 %, from 18 defects over 360 rejects"},{"id":"c","text":"0.15 %, from 18 defects over 12,000 boards"},{"id":"d","text":"5.00 %, from 18 defects over 360 rejects"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"18 / 12,000 = 0.0015, which is 0.15 % at board level. The second option slips a decimal, and the last two use the reject count as the denominator, which answers a different and much less useful question — the mistake the lesson warns about when it asks for the denominator to be stated.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l03","prompt":"How does false-call rate differ from AOI pass yield?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"They are the same figure, since a false call is a unit that passed AOI"},{"id":"b","text":"False-call rate counts escapes; pass yield counts the units that were rejected"},{"id":"c","text":"False-call rate applies to joints; pass yield applies only to whole panels"},{"id":"d","text":"False-call rate judges verified rejects; pass yield counts units clearing AOI"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson separates the two: false-call rate is verified good rejects over verified rejects, while AOI pass yield counts units that leave the station without a call. A false call is a rejection, not a pass, escapes are the opposite failure, and neither metric is restricted to joints or panels.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m07l03","prompt":"Downstream test finds defects that AOI never called, and coverage is high. What does the lesson say?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Detection capability, not verification effort, is the limit"},{"id":"b","text":"Verification coverage is false and the audit must be repeated"},{"id":"c","text":"The downstream test is over-sensitive and should be re-qualified"},{"id":"d","text":"Nothing can be concluded until the false-call rate is known"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Good coverage means the calls were reviewed; defects that were never called are a detection gap, which the lesson addresses through programme, lighting and method rather than through more review. Repeating the audit, re-qualifying the tester and waiting for another metric all leave the hole open.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l03-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m07l03","prompt":"Which disposition set does the verification workflow use for each AOI reject?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Pass or fail, decided by the operator on the image alone"},{"id":"b","text":"True defect, false call, marginal or other, against documented criteria"},{"id":"c","text":"Rework or scrap, without recording the reason for the choice"},{"id":"d","text":"Accept or reject, with the reason left to the shift supervisor"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson asks each reject to be dispositioned as a true defect, a false call, a marginal case or another category, judged against documented visual acceptance criteria and recorded on both the image and the physical board. Passing on the image alone, skipping the reason, or leaving it to the supervisor all break the audit trail the metrics depend on.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l04","prompt":"How does a void appear in a BGA X-ray image?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A bright, high-density core where the solder has collapsed"},{"id":"b","text":"A ring of low density around the outside of the ball"},{"id":"c","text":"A dark, low-density region inside a brighter ball"},{"id":"d","text":"A uniform grey ball with a slightly larger diameter"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Gas absorbs fewer X-rays than solder, so the void transmits more and shows as a darker region inside the ball. A brighter core, a perimeter ring and a uniformly larger ball describe collapse, wetting or excess solder rather than trapped gas.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l04-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l04","prompt":"What does the lesson require before a joint is accepted or reworked?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A second inspector to agree with the first one on the image"},{"id":"b","text":"A photograph of the joint stored with the serial number"},{"id":"c","text":"A re-test of the net by boundary scan before any decision"},{"id":"d","text":"A named criterion and class, not the appearance alone"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Disposition is decided against the applicable class and customer requirement, with accept, rework and reject following from that named criterion. A second opinion, a photograph and an electrical retest are all useful records or evidence, but none of them replaces the criterion.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l04-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m07l04","prompt":"A ball of 0.45 mm diameter contains a void estimated at 0.22 mm diameter. What void area fraction does that give?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 24 %"},{"id":"b","text":"About 49 %"},{"id":"c","text":"About 12 %"},{"id":"d","text":"About 98 %"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Areas scale with the square of the diameter: (0.22 / 0.45)² = 0.239, so roughly 24 %. The 49 % answer compares the diameters and forgets to square them, 12 % halves that result, and 98 % doubles the diameter ratio instead of squaring it — the three standard mistakes this calculation invites.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l04","prompt":"What does a healthy BGA look like in X-ray?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A continuous bright field where all balls merge into one another"},{"id":"b","text":"Discrete round features of consistent diameter and pitch"},{"id":"c","text":"Balls that grow steadily larger from the centre towards the edge"},{"id":"d","text":"Dark circles at every position, showing gas in each joint"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson describes each ball as a discrete, roughly circular feature with consistent diameter and spacing, and interprets deviations from that pattern as the finding. Merged brightness is bridging, size gradients point at collapse or paste problems, and dark centres at every position would mean gas in every joint.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m07l04","prompt":"A 2D image shows a bright line between two adjacent balls. What does the lesson call it?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A void trail, where gas has migrated along the gap between balls"},{"id":"b","text":"A fiducial artefact, produced by the alignment marks on the panel"},{"id":"c","text":"Bridging, visible as a continuous high-density path between balls"},{"id":"d","text":"A shadow, produced by the package edge over the outer ball row"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"A continuous high-density connection between two balls that should be isolated is bridging, whether it runs horizontally or diagonally in the image. Voids are dark, fiducials are outside the ball field, and a package shadow is a broad low-density region rather than a line between two specific balls.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l04-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m07l04","prompt":"A through-hole is hidden under a connector body on the second side. What does the lesson recommend?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Visual inspection of the entry side, which shows the fill directly"},{"id":"b","text":"A pull test on the lead, which proves the fill is adequate"},{"id":"c","text":"No check, since the joint cannot be seen and so cannot be judged"},{"id":"d","text":"X-ray for fill behind the pad, and the exit side where visible"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"X-ray evaluates fill inside a plated hole when the joint is hidden by a body, shielding or a second-side part, and the lesson keeps visual inspection for surfaces that are exposed. The entry side does not show the fill, a pull test measures strength rather than fill, and assuming the joint is unjudgeable is what the method exists to prevent.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l05","prompt":"How does the lesson separate a defect from a condition?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A condition is an observable state; a defect breaks a requirement"},{"id":"b","text":"A condition is a defect that has been photographed; a defect is a written record"},{"id":"c","text":"A condition is always acceptable; a defect is always a reason to scrap the board"},{"id":"d","text":"A condition applies to joints; a defect applies only to component placement"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The observable state is the condition, and it becomes a defect only when it fails the applicable requirement, which is why the same condition can pass one class and fail another. Photographs and records are evidence, a condition can be unacceptable, and both terms apply across the whole assembly.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l05","prompt":"Which order does the lesson give for a repeatable finding sentence?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Proposed action, standard, evidence, condition, object, location"},{"id":"b","text":"Location, object, condition, evidence, class, proposed action"},{"id":"c","text":"Defect name, operator name, date, measurement, photograph, decision"},{"id":"d","text":"Condition, class, measurement, action, location, reference designator"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The fixed order runs from where it is, through what it is and what it looks like, to the measurement, the criterion that governs it and the action proposed. The remaining orders either reverse the logic or substitute administrative fields for the evidence and criterion a second engineer needs.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m07l05","prompt":"AOI measures a fillet at 0.18 mm against a 0.20 mm minimum, with ±0.05 mm uncertainty. What does the measurement support?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The reading is below the minimum, so the joint is a confirmed defect"},{"id":"b","text":"The lower bound of 0.13 mm is below the minimum, so the joint must be reworked"},{"id":"c","text":"The upper bound of 0.23 mm reaches the minimum, so the call alone decides nothing"},{"id":"d","text":"The uncertainty is smaller than the gap, so the measurement can be accepted as is"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"This is the lesson case: the window spans 0.13 to 0.23 mm and straddles the 0.20 mm limit, so the measurement cannot settle the question and the joint needs physical review against the criterion. Treating the point reading as a pass or a fail, or using only the lower bound, ignores the uncertainty the lesson insists on recording.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l05","prompt":"What does an AOI call actually provide?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A final disposition, since the machine applies the acceptance criteria"},{"id":"b","text":"A certainty about the joint, because the camera measured it directly"},{"id":"c","text":"A manufacturing decision, because the station is part of the process"},{"id":"d","text":"Evidence of a suspected condition, awaiting verification"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson calls AOI output evidence: it can indicate a suspected condition from height, area, shape, colour or position, can miss hidden defects and can be fooled by shadows and reflections. A machine call becomes a disposition only when a person verifies it against the named criterion.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m07l05","prompt":"The same fillet shape passes Class 2 and fails Class 3. What does that tell you about the finding?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The condition is identical; the requirement governing it has changed"},{"id":"b","text":"The inspection was wrong on one of the two classes and must be redone"},{"id":"c","text":"The fillet has changed physically between the two class assessments"},{"id":"d","text":"The class is irrelevant, since acceptance is set by the customer alone"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Classes describe increasing workmanship requirements, so an unchanged condition can be acceptable at one level and not at another — which is why the class belongs in the finding sentence. Repeating the inspection or assuming the joint changed confuses the observation with the criterion, and the customer requirement sits alongside the class rather than replacing it.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l05-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m07l05","prompt":"Which information does the lesson require for a handoff to a second engineer?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The operator name, the shift and the time the call was made on the line"},{"id":"b","text":"Panel or serial, designator, programme, value, uncertainty, images"},{"id":"c","text":"The AOI supplier, the machine model and the service contract reference"},{"id":"d","text":"The defect name alone, since the second engineer can re-inspect the board"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The handoff has to let another engineer reproduce the decision, so it carries the identity of the unit and the position, the programme version, the measured value with its uncertainty, and the images. Administrative, supplier and bare-name records leave that reconstruction impossible.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l06-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l06","prompt":"What is the first task in the classification drill?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Name the defect from its appearance before locating the stage"},{"id":"b","text":"Decide the disposition, then look for the criterion that supports it"},{"id":"c","text":"Identify the process stage from the image before naming the defect"},{"id":"d","text":"Measure the feature, then choose the defect name that fits the size"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson starts every image with the stage — print, placement, reflow or through-hole — and only then names the condition, because the stage determines what evidence to look for. Naming first, deciding first or measuring into a chosen name all invert that order.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l06-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l06","prompt":"How does the lesson want a defect classified?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"By the machine that probably caused it, so the fix can be assigned"},{"id":"b","text":"By the operator who noticed it, so accountability stays clear"},{"id":"c","text":"By the customer complaint it could produce in the field"},{"id":"d","text":"By the physical condition, not by the suspected machine cause"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The vocabulary is workmanship: insufficient or excess solder, bridging, ball, void, dewetting, tombstoning and so on. Attribution to a machine is a later step that depends on evidence, and neither the observer nor a hypothetical complaint is a classification.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l06-05","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m07l06","prompt":"A print image shows paste outside the intended aperture. Which stage and condition is that?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Printing: a deposit displaced beyond the aperture"},{"id":"b","text":"Placement, as a component shifted off its pad"},{"id":"c","text":"Reflow, as solder that has spread past the mask"},{"id":"d","text":"Through-hole, as paste pushed out of the barrel"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The image is of bare pads with paste, so the stage is printing, and the condition is a deposit outside its aperture — judged against the stencil aperture and the paste datasheet. Placement, reflow and through-hole all act after the print, so none of them can produce paste on bare board.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l06-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m07l06","prompt":"What does a print image show that separates it from the other stages?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Reflowed joints, with the component already in position"},{"id":"b","text":"Paste deposits on bare pads, before any component is placed"},{"id":"c","text":"Component leads inserted through the board and clinched over"},{"id":"d","text":"Rework marks left by a soldering iron on a finished joint"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The print stage is the only one where paste sits alone on the pads, which is how the drill recognises it from the image. Reflowed joints, inserted leads and rework marks all come later and carry a component or a finished joint with them.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l06-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m07l06","prompt":"A chip has one termination lifted before reflow. How does the lesson classify it?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A reflow tombstone, since the part is standing on one end"},{"id":"b","text":"A print condition, since uneven paste lifted the part"},{"id":"c","text":"A placement condition, judged before any solder melts"},{"id":"d","text":"A handling defect, since the part was damaged after reflow"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"If the lift is present before the paste melts, the stage is placement and the condition is a lifted termination, which is the distinction the drill asks for. A tombstone is the reflow signature of unequal wetting, and the print and handling answers move the fault to the wrong stage on no evidence.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m07l06-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m07l06","prompt":"Which record makes a classification drill disposition reproducible?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Defect type and disposition, since the rest can be traced from the serial"},{"id":"b","text":"A photograph with the date and the shift of the inspection"},{"id":"c","text":"The AOI programme version and the machine that produced the image"},{"id":"d","text":"Location, component or net, defect, criterion, evidence, disposition"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson asks for exactly that chain so another person can reach the same decision. Defect and disposition alone lose the criterion, a dated photograph carries no measurement, and programme and machine data identify the tool but not the finding.","source":"src-012"},{"questionId":"q-m08l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l01","prompt":"Why does a connector often stay through-hole while the rest of the board is SMT?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The joint must take pull-out and cable strain, which a surface joint does not"},{"id":"b","text":"The connector body is too tall for the placement head to reach on the line"},{"id":"c","text":"The connector leads cannot be formed to the flatness a surface pad needs"},{"id":"d","text":"The connector is a legacy part that no supplier now offers in a surface version"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson keeps through-hole where the joint must resist mechanical load, carry current or survive repeated insertion, and a connector with cable strain is the standard case. Height, lead formability and part availability can influence a design, but they are not the mechanical reason the lesson gives.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l01","prompt":"Which dimension governs lead insertion into a plated hole?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The drill diameter in the fabrication data before any plating"},{"id":"b","text":"The finished hole diameter after plating and the final finish"},{"id":"c","text":"The pad diameter on the outer layer after the mask is applied"},{"id":"d","text":"The annular ring width, since that is what the lead actually meets"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Copper plating and finish both reduce the drilled hole, so the finished diameter is the assembly reference and the drill size is only an input to it. The pad and the annular ring describe the surface the solder wets rather than the bore the lead passes through.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l01-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m08l01","prompt":"A 0.80 ±0.03 mm lead goes into a 1.10 ±0.05 mm finished hole. What is the worst-case diametral clearance?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.30 mm, from the nominal hole and the nominal lead"},{"id":"b","text":"0.38 mm, from the largest hole and the smallest lead"},{"id":"c","text":"0.22 mm, from the smallest hole and the largest lead"},{"id":"d","text":"0.16 mm, from the smallest hole and the nominal lead"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The tight case pairs 1.05 mm with 0.83 mm for 0.22 mm, which is the number that decides whether the joint can fill. The 0.30 mm figure is the nominal case, 0.38 mm is the loose case, and 0.16 mm halves the answer unnecessarily.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l01","prompt":"What does the lesson say about the hole clearance being too large?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Air is trapped in the barrel, which voids the joint from the inside out"},{"id":"b","text":"Flux cannot reach the barrel wall, so the copper stays oxidised"},{"id":"c","text":"The lead sits off centre, which tilts the component on the board"},{"id":"d","text":"Capillary action weakens, so solder climbs less reliably up the barrel"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Too small a gap traps air and restricts flux, and too large a gap loses the capillary drive that lifts solder through the hole — the two failures pull in opposite directions. Trapped air and blocked flux belong to the tight case, and an off-centre lead is a placement rather than a fit problem.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m08l01","prompt":"A connector joint shows 60 % fill on a Class 3 product. What follows from the lesson?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It falls below the 75 % barrel fill required by Class 2 and Class 3"},{"id":"b","text":"It is acceptable, because Class 3 sets a 60 % minimum for connector pins"},{"id":"c","text":"It is acceptable if the pull-out strength of the joint measures in spec"},{"id":"d","text":"It is acceptable only if the opposite side of the barrel is fully wetted"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The module states the same 75 % minimum vertical barrel fill for Class 2 and Class 3, with Class 3 adding its own further requirements, so 60 % does not pass. There is no 60 % connector allowance in the lesson, mechanical strength is not a substitute for the fill criterion, and partial wetting does not make up the shortfall.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l01-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m08l01","prompt":"The nominal annular area is 0.45 mm² over a 1.60 mm hole depth, and the trial gives 75 % fill. What solder volume is expected?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 0.72 mm³"},{"id":"b","text":"About 0.54 mm³"},{"id":"c","text":"About 0.36 mm³"},{"id":"d","text":"About 1.20 mm³"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"0.45 x 1.60 = 0.72 mm³ of theoretical annular volume, and 75 % of that is 0.54 mm³. The 0.72 figure stops before the fill fraction, 0.36 applies 50 % instead of 75 %, and 1.20 mm³ is the expected fill height in millimetres rather than a volume.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l02","prompt":"Why is a nominal clearance figure not enough to release a through-hole design?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The nominal figure describes the drill rather than the finished hole"},{"id":"b","text":"Clearance only matters for hand insertion, not for machine placement"},{"id":"c","text":"Tolerances, lead forming and placement error all consume the same gap"},{"id":"d","text":"The nominal gap is measured after soldering, when the barrel has filled"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The stack-up combines hole tolerance, lead tolerance, lead forming and placement error, and the worst case is what decides whether the lead enters and the joint fills. The nominal gap is measured on the finished hole before soldering, so the other three options either misdescribe the dimension or its timing.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l02","prompt":"How does a large diametral gap affect a wave-soldered joint, according to the lesson?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The gap fills faster, because more solder can enter the same barrel"},{"id":"b","text":"The gap has no effect on fill, which depends on wave height alone"},{"id":"c","text":"The gap guarantees a void ring, because the solder cools unevenly"},{"id":"d","text":"The gap can insert easily yet fill poorly, as capillary drive falls"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson separates insertion from fill explicitly: a joint that inserts cleanly can still fill badly when the annular gap is too wide for capillary action. More room does not mean faster filling, wave height is one input among several, and an uneven cool is not the mechanism described.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m08l02","prompt":"A 1.00 ±0.05 mm hole meets a 0.60 ±0.03 mm lead with ±0.08 mm placement error. What radial clearance remains on the tight side?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.08 mm"},{"id":"b","text":"0.16 mm"},{"id":"c","text":"0.20 mm"},{"id":"d","text":"0.24 mm"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The worst-case gap is (0.95 - 0.63) / 2 = 0.16 mm radial, and the 0.08 mm placement offset takes half of what is left on the tight side, leaving 0.08 mm. The 0.16 mm figure is the clearance before placement, 0.20 mm is the nominal case, and 0.24 mm adds the two tolerances instead of subtracting them.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l02","prompt":"Which items does the lesson put into the through-hole tolerance stack-up?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Drill tolerance, copper weight, mask thickness, and panel dimensions"},{"id":"b","text":"Hole and lead tolerance, lead forming, placement and board warpage"},{"id":"c","text":"Paste volume, stencil aperture, squeegee speed, and separation speed"},{"id":"d","text":"Oven zones, conveyor speed, flux type, and solder pot temperature"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The stack-up is about whether the lead reaches the bore: hole and lead tolerance, lead-forming tolerance, placement tolerance and an allowance for warpage or panel variation. The drill is only an input to the finished hole, and paste and oven settings belong to other processes.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m08l02","prompt":"Leads on a connector bind on one side of the holes while the clearance checks pass. What does the lesson suggest first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Enlarge the holes until binding stops, since clearance is the only variable"},{"id":"b","text":"Reduce the lead diameter, since the supplier tolerance is the usual cause"},{"id":"c","text":"Check the measured placement error distribution, not the nominal value"},{"id":"d","text":"Change the flux, since binding is a wetting problem before it is mechanical"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Placement tolerance consumes the radial clearance, and a pass on nominal figures can hide a distribution that is biased or wide, which is why the lesson closes the loop with measured data. Opening the holes and reducing leads change the design before the cause is known, and binding is mechanical rather than a flux effect.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l02-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m08l02","prompt":"The nominal gap is 0.40 mm and the worst-case gap is 0.32 mm. Which joint is harder to fill, and why?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The 0.40 mm gap, because the wider annulus gives a weaker capillary"},{"id":"b","text":"Neither, because fill is set by preheat rather than by clearance"},{"id":"c","text":"Both equally, because the difference is inside measurement error"},{"id":"d","text":"The 0.32 mm gap, because the tighter annulus restricts flux and air"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"As the gap narrows, flux and air move less freely through the barrel and the joint risks incomplete fill or voiding, which is the tight-clearance failure the lesson describes. The wide gap has its own failure mode but at the other end of the scale, and preheat acts on a joint that the gap already permits.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l03","prompt":"What is wave shadowing?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A body or fixture blocking solder from reaching a downstream joint"},{"id":"b","text":"A dark band left on the board where the wave failed to wet the surface"},{"id":"c","text":"A reduction in wave height across the width of the conveyor"},{"id":"d","text":"A shadow cast by the pallet on the preheater, cooling the board edge"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The wave sees the board as a sequence of obstacles, and anything standing upstream of a joint can screen it from the solder. The other options describe a visual mark, a wave-height variation and a preheat effect, none of which is the shielding mechanism the lesson names.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m08l03","prompt":"A 0.8 mm stand-off leaves only 1.5 mm to the next part. What does the lesson expect?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Seating worsens and the solder access under the body improves"},{"id":"b","text":"Seating improves but solder access under the body may get worse"},{"id":"c","text":"Both seating and solder access improve, since the gap lets flux out"},{"id":"d","text":"Neither changes, because stand-off is set by the lead clinch angle"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Stand-off is a trade-off: a small gap helps the body sit down and hurts cleaning and solder access in a tight layout, which is why the lesson suggests changing direction, clinch or mask instead. The remaining options either invert the trade-off or deny that it exists.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m08l03","prompt":"A 12.0 mm tall body meets a wave at 5°, where tan 5° ≈ 0.0875. What shadow length does the estimate give?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 1.37 mm"},{"id":"b","text":"About 12 mm"},{"id":"c","text":"About 137 mm"},{"id":"d","text":"About 7 mm"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"12.0 / 0.0875 = 137 mm, a geometric estimate the lesson labels as simplified because real flow, flux and support change it. The 1.37 mm answer slips a factor of a hundred, 12 mm ignores the angle entirely, and 7 mm multiplies instead of dividing.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l03","prompt":"Which control does the lesson prefer against shadowing?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A higher solder pot temperature, so the wave carries more heat"},{"id":"b","text":"A faster conveyor, so the board spends less time in the shadow"},{"id":"c","text":"A different flux, so the solder wets even where it barely reaches"},{"id":"d","text":"Board direction, clinch, stand-off and mask before wave settings"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Shadowing is a geometry problem, so the lesson changes geometry: orientation, clinch, stand-off and mask before touching the wave. Temperature, speed and flux can shift the margin, but they do not move the obstruction, and the lesson asks for the control that matches the mechanism.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m08l03","prompt":"Bent leads appear on one insertion head only. Which family does the lesson put that in?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Insertion damage, traced to the feeder or insertion tooling"},{"id":"b","text":"Shadowing, since a shielded joint cannot straighten a lead"},{"id":"c","text":"Polarity error, since the head may have rotated the part"},{"id":"d","text":"Solder fill, since a bent lead reduces the annular gap"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson lists bent leads, crushed bodies, cracked ceramics and torn lands as insertion damage from feeding, handling or tooling, and ties the pattern to the equipment. Shadowing affects solder access, and polarity and fill are separate defect families with their own signatures.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l03-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m08l03","prompt":"Critical pads sit closely downstream of a tall part. Which change does the lesson test first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Raise the wave height so the solder reaches over the body"},{"id":"b","text":"Rotate the board so the tall body no longer screens the pads"},{"id":"c","text":"Slow the conveyor so the pads stay in the wave longer"},{"id":"d","text":"Clinch the leads tighter so the body sits closer to the board"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Rotation moves the obstruction out of the path, which is the first control the lesson applies, and the m08l04 case then adds masking if the geometry is still borderline. Raising the wave cannot lift solder over a body, slower running changes dwell rather than reach, and a tighter clinch does not remove the shadow.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l04","prompt":"How does the lesson want a tall rectangular component oriented for the wave?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Its long axis parallel to the conveyor, so the wave meets the narrow side first"},{"id":"b","text":"At 45 degrees, so that neither axis dominates the shadow it casts"},{"id":"c","text":"Its long axis perpendicular to the conveyor direction, so the shadow is narrow"},{"id":"d","text":"In whatever orientation the placement programme has already produced"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The shadow is cast in the direction of travel, so turning the long axis across the conveyor keeps the shielded strip as short as it can be. Parallel alignment maximises the shadow, a diagonal is a compromise the lesson does not ask for, and leaving it to the programme ignores the geometry entirely.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l04-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l04","prompt":"What has to be true of a carrier or pallet for wave soldering?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It clamps the board tightly so that no bowing can occur during preheat"},{"id":"b","text":"It covers the entire underside so that only the leads meet the wave"},{"id":"c","text":"It is made of metal, so that it conducts heat into the board evenly"},{"id":"d","text":"It holds the board flat and lets flux and air escape around the joints"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson asks for flat, bow-free support with pins and edges that do not block the wave or cover pads, and for paths that let flux and air leave. Clamping hard, blanking the whole underside and adding conductive mass all work against solder access and venting.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l04-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m08l04","prompt":"A 12 mm tall connector sits 18 mm upstream of critical pads. What is the shadow estimate, and how close is it?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"18-30 mm aligned, so the pads sit at the near edge of the shadow"},{"id":"b","text":"12-18 mm aligned, so the pads sit just outside the shadow entirely"},{"id":"c","text":"18-30 mm aligned, so the pads are far outside any risk of shadowing"},{"id":"d","text":"6-12 mm aligned, so the pads sit well inside the shaded strip"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson uses 1.5-2.5 times the height for an aligned body, giving 18-30 mm, and then calls 18 mm borderline rather than safe. The 12-18 mm range is the one that applies after rotating the body, the second option misplaces the pads, and the last range is not one the lesson uses.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l04","prompt":"What does the lesson do with a clinch that lands across downstream pads?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Leaves it as drawn, since the clinch only holds the part during transport"},{"id":"b","text":"Bends it perpendicular to the wave or away from the critical pads"},{"id":"c","text":"Shortens the lead so that the bend falls inside the plated barrel"},{"id":"d","text":"Removes the clinch entirely and relies on adhesive to hold the part"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"A bend lying across a pad downstream blocks solder and disturbs the flow, so the direction of the clinch is chosen with the wave in mind. Ignoring it treats a solder-access problem as cosmetic, and shortening the lead or gluing the part changes the assembly rather than the bend direction.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m08l04","prompt":"After rotating the board, downstream pads are still marginal. What does the lesson add next?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A second wave pass on the same board to give the pads more solder"},{"id":"b","text":"A lower preheat, so the mask and tape survive the wave contact"},{"id":"c","text":"High-temperature masking around the body, extended both ways"},{"id":"d","text":"A tighter clinch, so the body stands further off the board surface"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The case rotates first and then masks the body, extending the mask about 5 mm either way, before a trial confirms the result. A second pass does not remove the obstruction, preheat is set by flux and thermal needs, and a tighter clinch lowers rather than raises the body.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l04-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m08l04","prompt":"What does the lesson require before production with a new orientation and mask?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A signed drawing change, after which the process may be released"},{"id":"b","text":"A second operator to confirm the setup on the first production panel"},{"id":"c","text":"A review of the pallet drawing against the machine datasheet"},{"id":"d","text":"A trial board inspected for wetting, solder volume and residue"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The verification loop is a trial board run with the chosen orientation, clinch, pallet and mask, followed by inspection of the pads downstream of tall parts. Paperwork, a second opinion and a drawing review are useful, but none of them shows whether solder reached the joint.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l05","prompt":"How does the lesson want clearance reported, and why?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Both diametral and radial, each answering a different question"},{"id":"b","text":"Diametral only, because radial clearance is simply half of it"},{"id":"c","text":"Radial only, because the lead sits at one point in the bore"},{"id":"d","text":"As an area ratio, because area controls the capillary action"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Diametral clearance describes the fit between two round parts, while radial clearance describes the gap on one side where placement error eats into it — which is why the lesson keeps both. Halving one into the other loses the placement effect, and an area ratio is a derived figure rather than a reporting convention.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l05","prompt":"A customer asks for 100 % barrel fill. How does the lesson classify that?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A Class 3 requirement, which supersedes the 75 % figure"},{"id":"b","text":"A contractual requirement, not an IPC-A-610 class limit"},{"id":"c","text":"A Class 2 requirement, applied when the product is safety critical"},{"id":"d","text":"A standard requirement for any plated through-hole joint"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson separates the 75 % minimum that both Class 2 and Class 3 carry from a 100 % demand, which comes from the customer rather than from the class. Class 3 adds further criteria, but full fill is not one of the class limits the module states.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m08l05","prompt":"A 0.90 mm hole and a 0.50 mm lead run through a 1.60 mm board. What nominal annular volume results?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 1.02 mm³"},{"id":"b","text":"About 0.31 mm³"},{"id":"c","text":"About 0.70 mm³"},{"id":"d","text":"About 0.63 mm³"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The hole holds 1.018 mm³ and the lead takes 0.314 mm³, leaving about 0.70 mm³ of annulus for solder. The 1.02 figure is the full hole volume, 0.31 mm³ is the lead volume itself, and 0.63 mm³ is the expected fill at a 90 % process target rather than the nominal volume.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m08l05","prompt":"Which dimensions does the lesson use to start a clearance calculation?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The drill diameter and the lead before any forming has been done"},{"id":"b","text":"The pad diameter and the annular ring width on the outer layer"},{"id":"c","text":"The measured solder volume and the height the solder reached"},{"id":"d","text":"Nominal finished hole and lead, then the tight and loose cases"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The drill starts from nominal values and then closes the window with tight and loose cases, which is how the module builds every clearance example. The drill size is an input to the finished hole, the pad and ring describe the surface, and solder volume is a result of the calculation rather than an input.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m08l05","prompt":"Measured fill sits below the required level but the clearances are inside the window. What does the lesson look at next?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Hole wall condition, flux activity, preheat, solder temperature and dwell"},{"id":"b","text":"A larger hole, so that more solder can enter the same barrel volume"},{"id":"c","text":"A looser lead fit, so that the annular gap opens up along its length"},{"id":"d","text":"A different acceptance class, so that the measured fill becomes compliant"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"With the geometry inside its window, the lesson moves to the process that drives capillary fill: wall condition, flux, preheat, solder temperature and dwell. Resizing the hole or the lead changes the design before the process cause is found, and reclassifying the product to make a result compliant is not a corrective action.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m08l05-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m08l05","prompt":"The nominal annular volume is 0.70 mm³ and the process target is 90 % fill. How much solder does that imply?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 0.70 mm³"},{"id":"b","text":"About 0.63 mm³"},{"id":"c","text":"About 0.75 mm³"},{"id":"d","text":"About 0.07 mm³"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"90 % of 0.70 mm³ is 0.63 mm³, which is the volume the joint should contain at the process target. The 0.70 figure is the whole annulus, 0.75 mm³ confuses the 75 % class minimum with a volume, and 0.07 mm³ is off by a factor of ten.","source":"src-016"},{"questionId":"q-m09l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l01","prompt":"What does nitrogen actually do for a wave solder process?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It cuts oxidation and dross, which can improve wetting and reduce bridging"},{"id":"b","text":"It replaces part of the preheat, because the atmosphere carries the heat"},{"id":"c","text":"It raises the soldering temperature, so a lower pot setting becomes possible"},{"id":"d","text":"It removes the need for flux, since the solder cannot oxidise in nitrogen"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson credits nitrogen with less oxide, less dross, better wetting and sometimes fewer bridges, and then says plainly that it does not replace proper preheat or flux control. The other options hand nitrogen a job the process still needs from heat and chemistry.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l01","prompt":"What does the lesson say makes the lead-free wave window narrower?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The lower density of lead-free solder, which drains more slowly from pads"},{"id":"b","text":"Higher temperatures and tighter flux and preheat margins"},{"id":"c","text":"The wider melting range of SnPb paste used on mixed boards"},{"id":"d","text":"The fact that lead-free boards cannot use nitrogen in most machines"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson ties the narrower window to the lead-free thermal demand: higher solder temperature and less forgiving flux and preheat behaviour. Density and drainage are not the stated cause, the mixed-board sentence refers to SnPb paste rather than the lead-free window, and nitrogen is a standard lead-free option rather than an exclusion.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l01-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l01","prompt":"A 250 mm wave gives 4 s of contact. What conveyor speed does that require?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"62.5 m/min"},{"id":"b","text":"0.375 m/min"},{"id":"c","text":"3.75 m/min"},{"id":"d","text":"1.00 m/min"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"0.250 m / 4 s = 0.0625 m/s, and x 60 gives 3.75 m/min. The 62.5 answer forgets that the length is in millimetres, 0.375 m/min drops a factor of ten, and 1.00 m/min would give about 15 s of contact on the same wave.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l01","prompt":"Which elements make up the wave soldering window?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Stencil thickness, squeegee speed, separation and paste lot"},{"id":"b","text":"Nozzle size, dwell per joint, passes and fixture height"},{"id":"c","text":"Zone count, belt speed, heater power and exhaust rate"},{"id":"d","text":"Flux application, preheat, wave contact and cooling"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson defines the window as the interaction of flux, preheat, wave contact and cooling. Stencil and squeegee belong to printing, nozzle and dwell to selective soldering, and oven zones to reflow.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m09l01","prompt":"What does too short a contact time produce?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Insufficient fill and cold joints: the joint never reaches wetting heat"},{"id":"b","text":"Excess solder and bridging, because the wave cannot drain away in time"},{"id":"c","text":"Icicles on the exit side, because the solder freezes before it falls back"},{"id":"d","text":"Blowholes, because moisture cannot escape through the shorter path"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson puts short dwell with insufficient fill and cold joints, and long dwell with excess solder, bridging and icicles. Blowholes relate to trapped moisture and volatiles rather than to the length of the contact.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l01-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l01","prompt":"The top surface must rise from 25 °C to 105 °C in 90 s. What average ramp is that?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 0.89 °C/s, or about 8.9 °C/min"},{"id":"b","text":"About 0.89 °C/s, or about 53 °C/min"},{"id":"c","text":"About 1.13 °C/s, or about 68 °C/min"},{"id":"d","text":"About 0.53 °C/s, or about 32 °C/min"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"80 °C over 90 s is 0.889 °C/s, and multiplying by 60 gives about 53 °C/min. The second option multiplies by 10 instead of 60, the third inverts the division, and the last halves the result for no stated reason.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l02","prompt":"Why does the lesson state the basis before comparing flux figures?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Different suppliers report the same deposition in incompatible currencies"},{"id":"b","text":"Flux weight changes while the board passes through the preheat zone"},{"id":"c","text":"Per-liquid ranges are several times larger than solids figures"},{"id":"d","text":"The figures are only valid for lead-free alloys and not for SnPb"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson warns that a per-liquid figure such as 0.5-1.0 g/ft² is several times larger than a solids-based 0.08-0.23 mg/cm², so mixing the bases makes a correct process look wrong. Currency, in-process evaporation and alloy family are not the reason the basis matters.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l02","prompt":"What happens when too much flux is applied before the wave?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It floods the through-holes and pushes solder onto the top side"},{"id":"b","text":"It slows the wave, so the contact time has to be extended"},{"id":"c","text":"It raises the solder temperature in the pot beyond its setpoint"},{"id":"d","text":"It can boil, lift components or leave residue to be cleaned"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson puts too much flux with boiling, component lifting and residue, and too little with non-wetting and shading. Flooding the top side and disturbing contact time are wave-height and dwell effects, and flux does not drive the pot temperature.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l02","prompt":"A 50 by 40 mm board is fluxed at 0.15 mg/cm² of solids from a 2.0 % solids flux. What mass of flux solution is that?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 150 mg"},{"id":"b","text":"About 3 mg"},{"id":"c","text":"About 75 mg"},{"id":"d","text":"About 1,500 mg"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The board is 20 cm², so 0.15 x 20 = 3.0 mg of solids, and at 2.0 % solids that is 150 mg of solution — the mid-range case the lesson works through. The 3 mg figure stops at the solids, 75 mg halves the answer, and 1,500 mg puts the decimal in the wrong place.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l02","prompt":"What do the flux solids control?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The pot temperature needed to keep the solder molten at the nozzle"},{"id":"b","text":"Residue, bridging and the resulting cleanliness of the assembly"},{"id":"c","text":"The preheat ramp, since solids set how fast the board can be heated"},{"id":"d","text":"The wave height, because solids thicken the solder as it leaves the pot"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Solids govern how much material is left behind, which drives residue, bridging and cleanliness — and low-solids no-clean fluxes then demand tighter application control. Pot temperature, preheat and wave height are separate settings.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m09l02","prompt":"A board with a fresh finish shows non-wetting on a few joints after the wave. Which flux question comes first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Whether the flux activity should be raised for every board on the line"},{"id":"b","text":"Whether the pot needs a top-up of solder to restore the wetting balance"},{"id":"c","text":"Whether the deposition reached those pads, by weight or witness card"},{"id":"d","text":"Whether the preheat should be lowered to leave more flux unreacted"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson asks for the deposition to be verified by fluxometer or witness card before the chemistry is changed, because patchy wetting usually means patchy coverage. Raising activity across the line, adding solder and lowering preheat all change the process before the distribution is known.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l02-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l02","prompt":"Which verification set does the lesson require for a new wave recipe?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A signed recipe sheet and a second operator confirming the settings"},{"id":"b","text":"A pot analysis for copper and a fresh flux lot before the trial"},{"id":"c","text":"A measurement of the wave height alone, since the rest is covered by flux"},{"id":"d","text":"First-article wetting, hole fill, residue, cleanliness and reliability"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson verifies with first-article wetting, hole fill, residue, cleanliness and reliability, and cites the J-STD-001 and IPC-A-610 acceptance basis. Paperwork, pot analysis and a wave-height reading are supporting checks rather than the whole verification.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l03","prompt":"Why does the lesson track top-surface temperature separately on a wave line?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Heat enters from the bottom, so the top lags and can stay far cooler"},{"id":"b","text":"The top side cools the solder faster, so it is the one that sets the wave"},{"id":"c","text":"The top side is the only place a thermocouple can be attached safely"},{"id":"d","text":"The top side is heated by the exhaust, so it runs ahead of the board"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson describes heat entering mainly from below, which is why the bottom surface can rise while the top lags, and both are tracked against limits. Attachment, cooling by the top side and exhaust heating all describe a different thermal path than the module states.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l03","prompt":"A 1.6 mm board gets a 1.0 mm wave height. What does the lesson say about top-side clearance?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"No clearance remains, so the wave will reach the top-side SMT parts"},{"id":"b","text":"About 0.6 mm remains, so the wave wets the bottom without flooding the top"},{"id":"c","text":"About 1.0 mm remains, because the board thickness is the only constraint"},{"id":"d","text":"Clearance is not a meaningful figure, since wave height varies along the line"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson sets 1.0 mm inside the 0.5-1.5 mm starting range and leaves 0.6 mm to the top side, which is why the choice is defensible for a 1.6 mm board. Claiming no clearance, reusing the wave height as the answer, or dismissing the figure all misread the geometry.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l03","prompt":"A 100 by 150 mm board is fluxed at 0.15 mg/cm² of solids. How much solid flux does that deposit?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 2.25 mg"},{"id":"b","text":"About 150 mg"},{"id":"c","text":"About 22.5 mg"},{"id":"d","text":"About 225 mg"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"150 cm² x 0.15 mg/cm² = 22.5 mg, and the lesson then adds 10-20 % for hole walls, reaching 25-27 mg. The 2.25 mg answer slips a decimal, 150 mg is the board area in square centimetres, and 225 mg multiplies by ten.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l03","prompt":"What two jobs does preheat do before the wave?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Melts the solder and holds the board flat over the nozzle"},{"id":"b","text":"Cures the mask and shrinks the through-holes for a tighter fit"},{"id":"c","text":"Cools the board after wave contact to set the solder quickly"},{"id":"d","text":"Activates flux, removes volatiles and reduces thermal shock"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Preheat activates the flux, drives solvent off and softens the thermal step the board meets at the wave. Melting happens in the pot, the mask is cured before assembly, and cooling is the stage after contact.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m09l03","prompt":"A thin board with light copper and small leads is being set up. What does the lesson start from?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Lower flux density, preheat and wave height, then adjust upward"},{"id":"b","text":"The highest settings on the machine, then reduce until defects stop"},{"id":"c","text":"The thick-board recipe, since the same window covers every stack-up"},{"id":"d","text":"The dual-wave recipe, since two waves suit every lead-free board"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson gives thin, lightly coppered boards a lower starting point on all three settings and reserves the higher values for thick boards with heavy copper. Starting high, copying a thicker stack-up or defaulting to a two-wave recipe all ignore the board this recipe is for.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l03-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l03","prompt":"Which measurements does the lesson use to verify a wave setup?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A visual check of the top side and a reading of the pot thermometer"},{"id":"b","text":"Thermocouple data, flux weight gain and solder joint inspection"},{"id":"c","text":"The conveyor speed printed by the machine and the flux lot number"},{"id":"d","text":"An X-ray of every joint and a pull test on one lead per panel"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The verification is thermal, chemical and visual at once: thermocouples for the preheat, weight gain for the flux deposition, and inspection for fill, wetting, residue and balls. The other sets either record settings without their effect or sample far past what the lesson asks.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l04","prompt":"Why does the lesson use an effective contact length rather than the nozzle length?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The nozzle shortens as it wears, so its nominal length is unreliable"},{"id":"b","text":"The board shrinks in the preheat zone, which shortens the path"},{"id":"c","text":"Turbulence, wave height and board contact widen the wetted zone"},{"id":"d","text":"The conveyor slips under load, so the travelled distance is shorter"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The wetted zone can extend beyond the nozzle, which is why a witness board measures the real contact length. Nozzle wear and thermal shrinkage are second-order beside that effect, and conveyor slip is not the reason the lesson gives.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l04-04","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l04","prompt":"Insufficient fill appears and the flux, preheat and dwell are already inside their windows. What does the lesson check first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A longer dwell, because fill always follows contact time"},{"id":"b","text":"A new flux lot, because fill is a chemistry problem"},{"id":"c","text":"A faster conveyor, so the joints are not over-heated"},{"id":"d","text":"Wave height and clearance, before increasing dwell"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson's order is explicit: if fill is poor, check wave height and clearance before extending dwell, because contact time cannot wet a joint the wave is not reaching. More dwell, a new flux lot and a faster conveyor all miss that step.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l04-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l04","prompt":"A witness board measures 180 mm of contact at 1.0 m/min. What dwell does that give?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Roughly 10.8 s of dwell — outside the 2-5 s window"},{"id":"b","text":"About 1.8 s of dwell — inside the window"},{"id":"c","text":"About 18 s of dwell — far above the window"},{"id":"d","text":"About 3.0 s of dwell — inside the window"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"1.0 m/min is 16.7 mm/s, and 180 / 16.7 = 10.8 s — more than twice the upper bound of the 2-5 s window, so the arithmetic is right and the line is wrong. The 1.8 s answer slips a decimal, 18 s divides by 10 instead of by 16.7, and 3.0 s is a correct mid-window dwell on a much faster line, not this one.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l04","prompt":"How is dwell calculated?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Conveyor speed multiplied by the nozzle length"},{"id":"b","text":"Effective contact length divided by conveyor speed"},{"id":"c","text":"Nozzle length divided by the number of waves in the machine"},{"id":"d","text":"Pot temperature divided by the wave height at the crest"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Dwell is a length over a speed: t = L / v, with L measured rather than assumed. The other forms mix in the nozzle count, the pot temperature or the crest height, none of which gives a time.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m09l04","prompt":"The measured dwell is near the top of its window and icicles appear. What does the lesson try first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Cut the dwell to the minimum, since icicles come from too much contact"},{"id":"b","text":"Raise the pot temperature, so the solder drains before it freezes"},{"id":"c","text":"Check wave height and board clearance before shortening the dwell"},{"id":"d","text":"Lower the preheat, so the board leaves the wave cooler and drains faster"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Icicles come from solder that cannot drain back, which is a wave-height and clearance question before it is a dwell question — the same order the lesson uses for poor fill. Blindly cutting dwell trades one defect for another, and both remaining options change the thermal balance.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l04-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l04","prompt":"The same 180 mm contact length should give 3 s of dwell at mid-window. What speed does that need?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 0.90 m/min"},{"id":"b","text":"About 0.17 m/min"},{"id":"c","text":"About 60 m/min"},{"id":"d","text":"About 3.6 m/min"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"180 mm / 3 s = 60 mm/s, which is 3.6 m/min — the lesson's own target-setting example, and a speed that puts the board through the wave inside the 2-5 s window. The 0.90 m/min answer divides 180 by 200 s, 0.17 m/min inverts the calculation, and 60 m/min leaves the seconds-to-minutes step out.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l05","prompt":"What is shadowing in wave soldering?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A part or fixture blocking wave, flux or preheat from a joint"},{"id":"b","text":"A dull band where the wave leaves the board on the exit side"},{"id":"c","text":"The loss of wave height across the width of a wide panel"},{"id":"d","text":"A dark mark left by flux residue after it has burned in the oven"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Shadowing covers three blocked paths — solder, flux and preheat — and the lesson treats it as a layout and handling problem first. A dull band, a height variation and a residue mark are observations that may follow from it but are not its definition.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l05","prompt":"Which pallet change does the lesson propose for a shadowed row of pads?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Close the pallet wall closer to the pads so the wave is funnelled in"},{"id":"b","text":"Open the pallet hole and clear the body by about 1 mm"},{"id":"c","text":"Raise the board on taller support pins so the wave passes underneath"},{"id":"d","text":"Remove the pallet entirely and run the panel on the conveyor rails"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson opens the hole and adds about 1 mm of clearance around the body so the wall stops covering pads and trapping flux. A tighter wall, taller pins and an unheld panel all change other things while the obstruction stays in the wave path.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l05","prompt":"A 10 mm tall body meets the wave at 20° from vertical, with pads 2 mm downstream. What do the line-of-sight numbers say?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A 3.6 mm shadow, so the pads at 2 mm are outside it"},{"id":"b","text":"A 0.36 mm shadow, so the pads at 2 mm are safely clear"},{"id":"c","text":"A 3.6 mm shadow, so the pads at 2 mm are inside it"},{"id":"d","text":"A 2.0 mm shadow, so the pads sit exactly at its edge"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"10 x tan 20° = 10 x 0.364 = 3.6 mm, and 2 mm is less than that, so the lesson predicts shadowing and then adds a 0.5-1.0 mm turbulence margin. The second option gets the number right and the comparison wrong, and the last two misplace the decimal or invent a value.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l05","prompt":"How does board direction change the shadow?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The wave temperature falls as the board turns across the conveyor"},{"id":"b","text":"The flux deposition doubles when the board runs the other way"},{"id":"c","text":"Board direction has no effect once a pallet is used to hold the panel"},{"id":"d","text":"The same body presents a different projected height to the wave"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Turning a body changes the dimension facing the wave and with it the projected height and the shadow behind it — which is why orientation is the first control. Temperature and flux do not follow from the direction, and a pallet does not remove the geometry.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m09l05","prompt":"The shadowed row cannot be moved in layout and the pallet is already open. What does the lesson fall back to?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Masking or a selective wave for only the joints that need solder"},{"id":"b","text":"A slower conveyor so the wave has longer to reach behind the body"},{"id":"c","text":"A higher pot temperature so the solder flows further into the shadow"},{"id":"d","text":"Accepting the row and repairing the joints by hand after the wave"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"When geometry is fixed, the lesson moves to masking or a selective process that puts solder exactly where it is needed. More time, more heat and a repair step all work around the obstruction rather than past it, and hand repair adds risk the lesson is trying to avoid.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l05-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l05","prompt":"The same body is rotated so that a 2 mm end faces the wave at 20°. What shadow does it cast?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 3.6 mm, unchanged by the rotation"},{"id":"b","text":"About 0.7 mm, well inside the 2 mm gap"},{"id":"c","text":"About 0.07 mm, well inside the 2 mm gap"},{"id":"d","text":"About 2.0 mm, exactly equal to the gap"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"2 x tan 20° = 0.73 mm, which is comfortably less than the 2 mm gap — the point of the rotation. Reusing 3.6 mm ignores the change of dimension, 0.07 mm misplaces the decimal, and 2.0 mm simply repeats the gap.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l06-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l06","prompt":"Why does the lesson start a wave defect atlas on the solder side?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The solder side is the only side a camera can reach on a pallet"},{"id":"b","text":"The top side always shows the same pattern, so it adds nothing"},{"id":"c","text":"The defect shape records the last thermal and wetting events"},{"id":"d","text":"The solder side is where the operator writes the defect record"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Fill height, meniscus, drainage marks and wetting angle on the solder side are the direct record of what happened at the wave. Camera access, uniformity of the top side and record-keeping are not the reason the lesson gives.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l06-04","type":"single","competencies":["K5","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l06","prompt":"A joint looks dry with almost no solder and the pad beside it is clean. Which family does the lesson put that in?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Balling, where solder gathered into separate spheres and ran off"},{"id":"b","text":"Bridging, where solder drained from the pad to its neighbour"},{"id":"c","text":"Blowholes, where voids emptied the barrel after it filled"},{"id":"d","text":"Bypass, where the wave never made contact with that joint"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"A joint the wave did not wet, with the neighbouring pad untouched, is bypass, and the lesson points at wave height, warpage and contact as the causes. Balling and bridging leave solder where it should not be, and blowholes leave a filled joint with voids.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l06-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K5","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l06","prompt":"A 1.6 mm board shows 1.1 mm of fill in a 0.8 mm hole. What fill fraction and aspect ratio are those?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"69 % fill and an aspect ratio of 2.0"},{"id":"b","text":"69 % fill and an aspect ratio of 0.5"},{"id":"c","text":"110 % fill and an aspect ratio of 2.0"},{"id":"d","text":"31 % fill and an aspect ratio of 2.0"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"1.1 / 1.6 = 0.6875, so about 69 %, and 1.6 / 0.8 = 2.0 for the aspect ratio — the arithmetic of the lesson case, where 69 % sits below the class minimum. Inverting the aspect ratio, reporting the fill height as a percentage and subtracting instead of dividing are the three slips.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l06-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l06","prompt":"Which pair of causes does the lesson give for bridging?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Too little flux and a preheat that is too cool at the wave entry"},{"id":"b","text":"Too much solder and poor drainage as the board leaves the wave"},{"id":"c","text":"Trapped moisture in the board and volatiles from the mask"},{"id":"d","text":"Lead diameter too small and hole diameter too large"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Bridging is an excess-and-drainage problem: more solder than the gap can clear, and a wave that does not let it fall away. Low flux and cool preheat lead to non-wetting rather than to bridges, and moisture and voids produce blowholes.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l06-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m09l06","prompt":"A lead has separated from its pad and barrel after the wave, leaving a solder remnant. What is it?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A bypass, since the wave clearly did not wet that joint at all"},{"id":"b","text":"A blowhole, since a crater in the joint can look like a gap"},{"id":"c","text":"A lifted lead, treated as a thermal-mechanical separation"},{"id":"d","text":"A bridge that broke when the lead was trimmed after soldering"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson names a lead separated from pad or barrel, sometimes with a small remnant, as a lifted lead and links it to thermal-mechanical stress. Bypass leaves a dry unwetted joint, a blowhole is a void inside the solder, and a trimmed bridge is a different history.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l06-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l06","prompt":"The lesson case shows 69 % fill with a preheat ramp of 0.8 °C/s and a 2.5 s dwell. Which conclusion follows?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Fill is short, and the cause is the solder temperature rather than the profile"},{"id":"b","text":"Fill is acceptable, since 69 % sits above the 60 % floor for the class"},{"id":"c","text":"Fill is acceptable, because a short dwell always reduces bridging first"},{"id":"d","text":"Fill is short, and both the preheat ramp and the dwell sit below usual ranges"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The case finds 69 % below the class minimum and notes that a 0.8 °C/s ramp and 2.5 s dwell are both under the ranges the lesson quotes, so the fix addresses heat input. Blaming temperature alone ignores the profile, and there is no 60 % floor; a short dwell buys bridging at the cost of fill rather than making the joint compliant.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m10l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l01","prompt":"What usually decides that a joint should be soldered selectively rather than by a full wave?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Heat-sensitive surface-mount parts close to the through-hole joints"},{"id":"b","text":"The number of through-hole pins on the connector, once it passes forty"},{"id":"c","text":"The board thickness, since a full wave cannot heat boards over 1.6 mm"},{"id":"d","text":"The solder alloy, because a wave bath cannot run lead-free solder"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson gives the thermal budget of nearby SMT parts as the main reason selective exists, with throughput as the secondary consideration. Pin count correlates with cycle time rather than with the decision, and neither board thickness nor alloy rules a wave out.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l01","prompt":"What is a joint group in a selective program?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"All the pins of a single connector, counted as one thermal unit"},{"id":"b","text":"Joints served by one nozzle path, one flux and one profile"},{"id":"c","text":"The set of joints that failed inspection on the previous panel"},{"id":"d","text":"The joints that share the same reference designator prefix"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson groups joints by access, thermal mass and flux requirement so that one pass can serve them, and lists isolated pins, connectors and power pads as typical groups. Designator prefixes and failure history do not describe access or thermal behaviour.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l01-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m10l01","prompt":"A selective cycle is 3 s flux, 20 s preheat, 3 s dwell, 10 s cooling and 15 s path. What is the cycle time?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"36 s"},{"id":"b","text":"48 s"},{"id":"c","text":"51 s"},{"id":"d","text":"66 s"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The five elements sum to 51 s per board, which is the lesson's own estimate. The 36 s answer drops the path time, 48 s drops the dwell, and 66 s double-counts one of the stages.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l01","prompt":"What does a mini-wave nozzle suit?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A dense array of pins on a fine-pitch connector, all at once"},{"id":"b","text":"Bottom-side chip components that must be reflowed a second time"},{"id":"c","text":"Large ground pads that no nozzle can cover in one pass"},{"id":"d","text":"Isolated through-hole pins that need local heat only"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson describes the mini-wave as a small wave for accessible, isolated joints where local heat is enough. Dense arrays usually need a different path or several passes, chip parts are not wave joints, and a large ground pad needs a slot nozzle or a multi-pass strategy.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m10l01","prompt":"An electrolytic capacitor sits 12 mm from the connector pins. What does the lesson require?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Its body temperature measured in the profile, against its own limit"},{"id":"b","text":"Its removal before soldering, since no profile can protect it"},{"id":"c","text":"A higher preheat, so that the capacitor is already warm when the jet arrives"},{"id":"d","text":"A longer dwell, so that less heat is needed at a lower jet temperature"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The thermal budget has to be proven at the neighbour, which is why the lesson profiles the capacitor body and compares the result with the component limit. Removing it, warming it up or extending the dwell all increase exposure rather than manage it.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l01-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m10l01","prompt":"The capacitor limit is 105 °C and the profiled body temperature is 85-95 °C. What does that give?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"No margin at all, since the limit is close to the measured value"},{"id":"b","text":"10-20 °C of margin, which is what the choice has to prove"},{"id":"c","text":"A margin of 10-20 °C below the solder pot temperature"},{"id":"d","text":"A margin that only matters if the capacitor is electrically tested"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson takes 105 minus the measured band as the margin, 10-20 °C, and accepts the nozzle and flux choice on that basis. Comparing with the pot temperature or making the margin conditional on a later test misstates what was measured.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l02","prompt":"What does flux-on-the-fly change compared with fluxing the whole board?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The flux is applied only to the top side, so less residue reaches the bottom"},{"id":"b","text":"The flux is cured by the preheat, which removes the need for cleaning"},{"id":"c","text":"The flux is active at wetting temperature, applied just before contact"},{"id":"d","text":"The flux is measured per board area, exactly as on a wave line"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The point of applying flux immediately before the nozzle is that it is still active when the joint reaches wetting temperature, so the amount is metered per joint area. It is applied where the joint is, it is not cured by design, and the per-board-area basis belongs to wave soldering.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l02","prompt":"Why does the lesson split a program into passes instead of doing everything in one?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Each pass needs its own nozzle, so the passes follow the nozzle list"},{"id":"b","text":"The machine cannot repeat the same coordinate twice in one program"},{"id":"c","text":"Passes are needed only when the board carries more than fifty joints"},{"id":"d","text":"Separate passes stop preheat, flux and solder competing"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"A pass isolates one function so that heat, flux and solder do not fight each other, with a preheat pass, a flux pass and a solder pass as the common pattern. Nozzle count, a coordinate limit and a joint threshold are not the reason the lesson gives.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m10l02","prompt":"A joint area of 12 mm² gets flux at 0.15 mg/mm². What mass of flux does that deposit?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"1.8 mg"},{"id":"b","text":"0.18 mg"},{"id":"c","text":"18 mg"},{"id":"d","text":"180 mg"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"12 x 0.15 = 1.8 mg on a per-joint-area basis, which is the unit the lesson insists on for selective work. The other options move the decimal by one place in each direction or by two.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l02","prompt":"What does a conical nozzle do well?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It spreads heat evenly across a large ground pad in one pass"},{"id":"b","text":"It concentrates heat on an isolated pin, sparing neighbours"},{"id":"c","text":"It covers all the pins of a connector at a single temperature"},{"id":"d","text":"It applies flux and solder together, so one pass is enough"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson ties nozzle shape to thermal and wetting behaviour, and a cone concentrates heat on a small joint — hence its use for isolated through-hole pins. Large pads need a footprint nozzle, a connector array needs a path rather than one shape, and flux is a separate function.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m10l02","prompt":"An 8 by 8 mm ground pad cannot be covered without risking a neighbour. What does the lesson do?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Uses the conical nozzle already fitted, since shape barely matters"},{"id":"b","text":"Raises the jet temperature until the pad fills in a single contact"},{"id":"c","text":"Uses a slot nozzle or splits the pad across two passes"},{"id":"d","text":"Skips the pad and repairs it by hand after the machine run"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson matches the footprint to the pad and, where a single contact is not safe, uses a two-pass strategy. Reusing a small cone, pushing the temperature up and moving the work to hand repair all trade risk for convenience.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l02-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m10l02","prompt":"Which joint family needs the longest dwell in the lesson's example?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The through-hole connector pins, because they have the smallest pads"},{"id":"b","text":"The 4 by 4 mm power pads, because they are closer to the nozzle"},{"id":"c","text":"All three families need the same dwell, since dwell follows the alloy"},{"id":"d","text":"The 8 by 8 mm ground pad, because its thermal mass is the largest"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Dwell is set from each joint's thermal mass, and the large ground pad with its plane connection needs the longest contact, which the lesson places at the top of its range. Small pins and power pads need less, and a single dwell for every family contradicts the per-joint logic.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l03","prompt":"Why is the thermal budget described as a shared resource?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Heat reaches neighbours three ways, not only through the joint"},{"id":"b","text":"The machine can only heat a fixed number of joints per minute"},{"id":"c","text":"Each nozzle has a rated thermal output that must be divided between joints"},{"id":"d","text":"The solder pot cools as more joints draw heat out of the wave"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson defines the budget as the cumulative heat arriving at nearby parts through three paths, which is why a joint that passes can still damage its neighbour. Machine throughput, nozzle ratings and pot cooling are separate constraints.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l03","prompt":"Which heat path dominates close to the soldering source?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Convection from the air moving through the machine"},{"id":"b","text":"Conduction through copper planes and component leads"},{"id":"c","text":"Radiation from the solder pot surface to the package body"},{"id":"d","text":"Evaporation of flux solvent on the board surface"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson states that conduction through planes and leads dominates near the source, with convection from the air and flux vapour second. Radiation and evaporating solvent are not the dominant paths it names.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m10l03","prompt":"A 250 °C pot sits under a board top at 100 °C, and the capacitor is 8 mm away with a 4 mm attenuation length. What body temperature does the estimate give?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 115 °C"},{"id":"b","text":"About 137 °C"},{"id":"c","text":"About 121 °C"},{"id":"d","text":"About 175 °C"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The step is 150 °C, attenuated by exp(-2) ≈ 0.14, which gives about 21 °C above the board top at 100 °C, so 121 °C — above the 105 °C limit in the case. The other options come from attenuating by one step instead of two, halving the exponent, or applying no attenuation at all.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l03","prompt":"Which neighbours does the lesson name as needing attention?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Any part smaller than 0402, because of its small thermal mass"},{"id":"b","text":"Bare laminate, because it delaminates before parts are damaged"},{"id":"c","text":"Only parts that have already failed a functional test"},{"id":"d","text":"Electrolytics, plastic connectors, LEDs and MEMS parts"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson lists electrolytic capacitors, plastic connectors, LEDs, MEMS devices, sensors and adhesive-bonded components as sensitive neighbours. Small chip size, bare laminate and previous failures are not the criteria it gives.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m10l03","prompt":"The estimate puts a capacitor over its limit. Which mitigation does the lesson offer at layout level?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"More spacing, thermal relief, or less copper to the joint"},{"id":"b","text":"A shorter dwell, which the layout cannot influence in any way"},{"id":"c","text":"A higher preheat, so the capacitor starts closer to its limit"},{"id":"d","text":"A heavier fixture, so that heat is carried away from the board"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Layout-level mitigation is about reducing heat arriving: distance, thermal relief and less copper path. Dwell, preheat and fixtures are process or fixture levers, and raising the preheat moves the part towards its limit rather than away from it.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l03-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m10l03","prompt":"Which verification does the lesson require for a neighbour at risk?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A single thermocouple on the solder pot, recorded once per shift"},{"id":"b","text":"A profile with thermocouples on the joint and the neighbour"},{"id":"c","text":"A thermal camera image taken after the board has cooled down"},{"id":"d","text":"An electrical test of the neighbour alone, repeated after reflow"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The measurement has to be made where the risk is, so the lesson profiles both the joint and the most exposed neighbour, then adds functional testing after the run. Pot temperature, a cooled board image and an electrical test alone cannot show the peak the part actually saw.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l04","prompt":"Why is the fixture part of the selective program?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It carries the barcode that identifies the program to the machine"},{"id":"b","text":"It holds the flux syringe, so the flux volume depends on the fixture"},{"id":"c","text":"It sets height, exposure, support and the thermal path"},{"id":"d","text":"It is only a transport aid and has no effect on the soldering result"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson treats the fixture as part of the recipe because it fixes how the board sits, how much of it is exposed and how heat spreads. Identification, flux supply and transport do not capture why the fixture changes the result.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l04-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l04","prompt":"How does the lesson want the joint order chosen?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"By reference designator, so the program matches the assembly drawing"},{"id":"b","text":"From the highest mass joint down, so the hardest work is finished first"},{"id":"c","text":"In the shortest possible path, since path length is the only cycle factor"},{"id":"d","text":"Group by access and thermal mass, then work from low mass to high mass"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Ordering is a thermal decision: group by access and mass, then run low mass first so heat does not build up before the sensitive joints are made. Drawing order, hardest-first and pure path length ignore that logic.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l04-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m10l04","prompt":"A board has 72 low-mass pins at 1.55 s, 26 SMT joints at 2.15 s and 12 high-mass pins over two passes at 2.95 s each. What is the solder time?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 238 s"},{"id":"b","text":"About 167 s"},{"id":"c","text":"About 91 s"},{"id":"d","text":"About 329 s"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"111.6 + 55.9 + 70.8 = 238.3 s of solder time, which the lesson then adds to 91 s of non-solder elements. The 167 s answer omits the second pass on the high-mass pins, 91 s is the non-solder total, and 329 s is the whole cycle.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l04","prompt":"What does the air knife do in a selective program?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It cools the joint after soldering so the grain structure is fine"},{"id":"b","text":"It clears excess flux from the surface and limits flux carry-over"},{"id":"c","text":"It blows solder into the barrel to complete the fill"},{"id":"d","text":"It dries the preheat zone before the nozzle arrives"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson describes a programmed air or nitrogen curtain that removes excess flux and limits carry-over, sometimes with additional machine functions. Cooling, filling and drying the preheat are not its role.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m10l04","prompt":"Which elements does the lesson add to solder time to get the cycle time?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Solder time alone, because the machine works on one board at a time"},{"id":"b","text":"Only the preheat and dwell, since the other steps overlap"},{"id":"c","text":"Handling, fluxing, preheat, air knife, cooling and fixture exchange"},{"id":"d","text":"The nozzle travel time and the pot recovery time only"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The cycle is the sum of every step the board sees, which is why the lesson lists handling, flux, preheat, air knife, movement, dwell, cooling and any fixture exchange. Solder time alone, a partial sum or a two-item list all understate it.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l04-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m10l04","prompt":"A cycle is 329.3 s and the board carries 110 joints. What is the effective rate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 33 joints per minute"},{"id":"b","text":"About 3 joints per minute"},{"id":"c","text":"About 110 joints per minute"},{"id":"d","text":"About 20 joints per minute"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"329.3 s is 5.49 min, and 110 / 5.49 = 20.0 joints per minute, the figure the lesson reports. The 33 figure divides by 3.3 min by mistake, 3 comes from misplacing a decimal, and 110 ignores the cycle time entirely.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K3","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l05","prompt":"When does the lesson call for a cross-section?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"When fill, intermetallics or crack risk cannot be seen"},{"id":"b","text":"On every joint of the first panel, as a routine acceptance step"},{"id":"c","text":"Only when a pull test has already failed on a sample lead"},{"id":"d","text":"Whenever the operator cannot decide from the solder-side image"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A cross-section answers questions the surface cannot, so it is used where fill, intermetallics or cracks are in doubt. Routine sectioning of every joint is destructive and far beyond the lesson, and a failed pull test or an undecided operator is not the trigger it describes.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l05","prompt":"How does the lesson use a pull test in selective validation?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Pull the lead sideways to reproduce the stress of cable handling"},{"id":"b","text":"Pull a lead perpendicular to the board for strength"},{"id":"c","text":"Push the lead into the barrel to confirm the hole is filled"},{"id":"d","text":"Twist the lead until the joint fails, then measure the angle"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson fixes the board and pulls the lead perpendicular to it, on through-hole leads and selected SMT terminations, when strength is the question. Sideways, pushing and twisting are different tests that answer different questions.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m10l05","prompt":"A 250 °C jet runs 2.5 s over a joint and the estimate puts a nearby 0402 at about 178 °C against a 150 °C screening target. What does the calculation conclude?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The estimate is over target, so the target should be raised to 180 °C"},{"id":"b","text":"The estimate is acceptable, because the target is a screening figure only"},{"id":"c","text":"The estimate is over target, so the dwell or the shielding must change"},{"id":"d","text":"The estimate is acceptable, because a 0402 can take 178 °C"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson compares 178 °C with its 150 °C screening target, concludes that mitigation is needed, and then shortens the dwell to 1.5 s to bring the estimate to about 147 °C. Moving the target or asserting the part can take it reverses the order of the argument, and the screening figure exists to trigger exactly this action.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m10l05","prompt":"What is selective validation meant to prove?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"That the machine meets its rated placement and solder-cycle speeds"},{"id":"b","text":"That the program file has been approved by the process engineer"},{"id":"c","text":"That the board passes functional test before it leaves the line"},{"id":"d","text":"That the joints are sound and neighbours stayed within limits"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Selective soldering exists because of nearby heat-sensitive parts, so validation covers both sides of that trade: joint quality and neighbour temperature. Speed, approval and functional test are separate questions.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m10l05","prompt":"Which set of mitigations does the lesson apply when the neighbour is too hot?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Shorter dwell, lower preheat, shielding, later in the sequence"},{"id":"b","text":"A hotter jet for a shorter time, so the total energy stays the same"},{"id":"c","text":"A larger nozzle, so the heat spreads over a wider area of copper"},{"id":"d","text":"A slower nozzle path, so the board has time to lose heat between joints"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson reduces the energy that reaches the neighbour: less dwell, less preheat where the paste permits, a shield, and a later position in the sequence. A hotter jet, a bigger nozzle and a slower path all add heat or exposure.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m10l05-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K3"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m10l05","prompt":"Which records does the lesson require for a released selective program?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The machine serial number and the date of the next preventive maintenance"},{"id":"b","text":"Board revision, program, nozzle, settings, profile, section, pull"},{"id":"c","text":"The operator roster and the training certificates of the shift"},{"id":"d","text":"The solder pot analysis and the flux lot number alone"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson asks for board revision, component placement, program file, nozzle, paste lot, preheat and jet settings, dwell, pass strategy, and the profile, section and pull-test results. Machine maintenance, staffing and a two-item chemistry record do not let anyone rebuild the decision.","source":"src-021"},{"questionId":"q-m11l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l01","prompt":"Why is a soldering station described as a closed loop rather than a hot tool?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Controller, heater, tip, sensor and ground path all act on the joint"},{"id":"b","text":"The iron keeps a constant power output regardless of the joint mass"},{"id":"c","text":"The setpoint is fixed at the factory and needs no operator attention"},{"id":"d","text":"The tip stores enough heat that the heater never runs during contact"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson treats the station as a loop where the sensor and controller respond to the heat the tip loses, which is why the setpoint is not the joint temperature. Constant power, a fixed factory value and a heater that never runs all contradict that description.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m11l01","prompt":"Which control does the lesson put on the bench beside the iron?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A second iron, so that two operators can share one joint"},{"id":"b","text":"Local fume extraction, because flux fumes reach the operator"},{"id":"c","text":"A cooling fan aimed at the tip, to keep the setpoint stable"},{"id":"d","text":"A spool of leaded wire, since lead-free solder fumes less"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson asks for local exhaust placed between the joint and the operator's breathing zone, and treats ESD grounding as the other bench control. Extra irons, a cooling fan and a leaded substitution are not the controls it names.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l01-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m11l01","prompt":"A 350 °C tip touches a joint for 2 s. What is the thermal budget index in the lesson's units?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"175 °C·s"},{"id":"b","text":"352 °C·s"},{"id":"c","text":"700 °C·s"},{"id":"d","text":"70 °C·s"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The index multiplies the tip-to-joint step by the contact time, so 350 x 2 = 700 °C·s when no preheat is subtracted. The other options divide instead of multiply or lose a factor of ten.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l01","prompt":"What does tip geometry have to match?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The iron wattage, since a bigger tip needs a bigger heater"},{"id":"b","text":"The solder wire diameter, so that wire and tip wear together"},{"id":"c","text":"The board thickness, because thick boards need conical tips"},{"id":"d","text":"The joint size, so heat transfers without hiding the fillet"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson chooses the tip for the joint: enough contact face for heat, small enough to see and reach the joint. Wattage, wire diameter and board thickness constrain the choice but do not set the geometry.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m11l01","prompt":"A pad lifts while a small chip is hand soldered on a multilayer board. Which part of the loop does the lesson examine first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Tip size, setpoint and contact time against pad thermal mass"},{"id":"b","text":"The fume extractor, since airflow cools the joint unevenly"},{"id":"c","text":"The ESD wrist strap, because a poor ground damages the laminate"},{"id":"d","text":"The solder wire alloy, because a leaded wire lifts pads"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A lifted pad is a thermal and mechanical event at the joint, so the lesson reviews the tip, the setpoint and how long the operator stays on the pad. Extraction, grounding and alloy are real controls, but none of them explains a pad lifting under a hot tip.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l01-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m11l01","prompt":"Which iron rating and tip face does the lesson give for a small chip on a thin pad?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A 15-25 W iron with a 2.0 mm contact face"},{"id":"b","text":"A 40-60 W iron with a 0.5-1.0 mm contact face"},{"id":"c","text":"A 100-150 W iron with a conical 0.2 mm tip"},{"id":"d","text":"Any iron, since the setpoint alone controls the heat delivered"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson pairs a 40-60 W rework iron with a 0.5-1.0 mm chisel or conical face for a small chip. A weak iron with a large face, an oversized iron with a tiny tip and a claim that setpoint alone decides the heat all misstate how the loop behaves.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l02","prompt":"What is the correct contact order in manual rework?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Feed solder onto the tip, then carry it to the joint and hold until it flows"},{"id":"b","text":"Place the tip on the component body first, so the part expands before the joint"},{"id":"c","text":"Heat the joint, feed solder, remove the solder, then remove the tip"},{"id":"d","text":"Feed solder to the joint first, then bring the tip in to melt what has landed"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson requires heat to reach the joint before solder is added, and the removal order mirrors it so the joint freezes under the tip rather than after it. Melting on the tip, heating the body first and landing cold solder all break the thermal path the technique depends on.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l02","prompt":"A joint does not wet within the planned dwell. What does the lesson say to do?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Hold the tip on the joint until it finally wets, since heat is the cure"},{"id":"b","text":"Add more solder wire, because the flux needs fresh material to work"},{"id":"c","text":"Press the tip harder into the joint so more contact area is available"},{"id":"d","text":"Stop and reassess the thermal budget instead of extending the dwell"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson makes dwell a window with a stop rule: if the joint does not wet, the setup is wrong and longer contact adds damage rather than wetting. More solder, more dwell and more pressure all raise the risk of lifted pads and charred laminate.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m11l02","prompt":"A 330-350 °C tip meets a board preheated to 100 °C, with 1.5-2.5 s of contact. What index does the lesson record?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"345-625 °C·s per contact"},{"id":"b","text":"495-875 °C·s per contact"},{"id":"c","text":"230-250 °C·s per contact"},{"id":"d","text":"345-625 °C per contact"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The step is 230-250 °C, and multiplied by 1.5-2.5 s it gives 345-625 °C·s, which is the band the lesson states. The second option forgets to subtract the preheat, the third reports the step without the time, and the last drops the seconds from the unit.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l02","prompt":"Why is solder fed to the joint rather than melted on the tip?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Wire melts faster on the tip, so melting there saves dwell time"},{"id":"b","text":"Feed controls the volume and keeps the flux working at the joint"},{"id":"c","text":"The tip cannot be tinned once wire has touched it directly"},{"id":"d","text":"Fed solder carries more heat into the joint than tip contact does"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Feeding to the joint lets the wire size and feed length set the volume while the flux stays where the joint is. Melting on the tip burns flux away before it can act, and the claims about tinning and heat transfer invert how the joint is heated.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m11l02","prompt":"A disturbed, grainy joint appears on a hand-reworked connector pin. Which cause fits the lesson?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The solder alloy was lead-free, which always freezes grainy"},{"id":"b","text":"The tip was too wide, so the fillet could not form a concave shape"},{"id":"c","text":"The joint was moved or the iron withdrawn before it solidified"},{"id":"d","text":"The wire diameter was too small for the joint volume required"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"A disturbed joint means movement during solidification, which in hand work usually means the tip or the part moved too early. Alloy, tip width and wire diameter affect wetting and volume, but they do not produce the crystalline fracture pattern of a joint that was disturbed.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l02-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m11l02","prompt":"A 0.8 mm lead needs a tip. What contact face does the lesson specify, and why?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.2-0.4 mm, narrower than the lead, so the tip stays clear of the mask"},{"id":"b","text":"2.5-3.0 mm, so the whole annular ring is covered in one contact"},{"id":"c","text":"Any face, provided the tip temperature is raised to compensate"},{"id":"d","text":"1.0-1.5 mm, slightly wider than the lead, bridging pad and lead"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson asks for a chisel or hoof face slightly wider than the lead so that the tip touches the lead and the pad together. A narrower face cannot bridge both, an oversized face spreads heat to neighbours, and raising the temperature to cover a poor choice adds stress.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l03","prompt":"What does the lesson count as a rework cycle?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Each thermal exposure of the component and its board area"},{"id":"b","text":"Each time the board is removed from the line and returned to it"},{"id":"c","text":"Each operator who touches the assembly during the rework"},{"id":"d","text":"Each time the program is reloaded into the rework station"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The cycle count exists to track heat, so every thermal exposure of that component and its area counts, which is why an iron touch-up adds a cycle to a hot-air pass. Moving the board, the number of operators and a program reload are not thermal events.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l03","prompt":"What must be measured before a rework peak can be compared with a paste limit?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The pressure in the hot-air nozzle, converted into an equivalent temperature"},{"id":"b","text":"The temperature at the joint, not the hot-air controller reading"},{"id":"c","text":"The time the nozzle spends moving between adjacent components"},{"id":"d","text":"The ambient humidity in the rework area at the start of the shift"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson insists the peak is measured where the limit applies, because the controller setpoint is not the joint temperature. Nozzle pressure, nozzle travel and room humidity do not give a temperature to compare with a component or paste limit.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m11l03","prompt":"A second cycle is planned with a 245 °C peak for 45 s against a 250 °C maximum and a 30-60 s range. What is the finding?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The dwell is outside the range, so the cycle must be re-planned"},{"id":"b","text":"The peak exceeds the maximum, so the part cannot be reworked"},{"id":"c","text":"Both values are inside budget; the count reaches the allowed two"},{"id":"d","text":"The values pass, so the measurement point no longer matters"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"245 °C is below 250 °C, 45 s sits inside 30-60 s, and one previous plus one proposed cycle equals the allowed two, so the operation fits the budget. The lesson then adds that the peak must have been measured at the joint rather than read from the controller.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l03","prompt":"How does the lesson want a BGA removed?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Pry from one corner while the solder is still solid"},{"id":"b","text":"Twist the package once the balls soften and then pull straight up"},{"id":"c","text":"Lift immediately when the hot air reaches its setpoint"},{"id":"d","text":"Heat until the solder collapses, then lift with minimal force"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Removal waits for collapse and then uses minimal force, which protects pads and neighbouring joints. Prying, twisting and lifting on a setpoint rather than on the solder condition all risk torn pads.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m11l03","prompt":"What does paste-in-pad require before it can be used for a QFN replacement?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"That design and process allow it, with deposit volume controlled"},{"id":"b","text":"Only that the operator has a stencil small enough for the land pattern"},{"id":"c","text":"That the component is a BGA rather than a QFN or MLF package"},{"id":"d","text":"Nothing beyond the rework station, since the paste is self-limiting"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson allows paste-in-pad for QFN, MLF and some land-pattern replacements when design and process permit, with the deposit volume under control. Stencil size alone, a package restriction and an assumption of self-limiting paste all skip that condition.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l03-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m11l03","prompt":"Which records does the lesson require for a completed BGA replacement?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The machine serial number and the date of the next calibration"},{"id":"b","text":"Station, nozzle, preheat, peak, dwell, cycles, paste lot, inspection"},{"id":"c","text":"The solder pot analysis and the ambient humidity of the room"},{"id":"d","text":"The operator's training certificate and the shift handover note"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson lists operator, date, station, nozzle, preheat, measured peak, dwell, cycle count, paste and flux lots, plus the inspection method and result. Equipment identity, pot chemistry, humidity and training records do not show what the joint experienced.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l04","prompt":"How does the lesson evaluate a conductor that was cut and repaired?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"As an absolute width, compared with the narrowest conductor on the board"},{"id":"b","text":"As a length of missing copper, measured along the trace centreline"},{"id":"c","text":"As a width reduction measured against the originally designed width"},{"id":"d","text":"As a resistance increase, measured from end to end of the conductor"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson treats a cut as a width-reduction question relative to the design width, which is how the limit is expressed. An absolute width, a missing length and an end-to-end resistance are different measures that the class limits do not use.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l04-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l04","prompt":"What makes a defect repairable rather than scrap?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The customer has not yet complained about the same defect"},{"id":"b","text":"The area is small enough that a second operator can hide the mark"},{"id":"c","text":"The board is expensive enough to justify the attempt"},{"id":"d","text":"A qualified method can reach the class and the risk is recorded"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson sets three conditions: damage inside the applicable limits, a qualified method for that laminate and finish, and a recorded residual risk. Complaint history, cosmetic concealment and cost are not part of the decision framework.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l04-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m11l04","prompt":"A 0.30 mm trace is repaired to 0.24 mm. What width reduction does that represent?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"20 %, exactly at the Class 2 limit in the case"},{"id":"b","text":"6 %, measured as the millimetre difference alone"},{"id":"c","text":"25 %, obtained by dividing the original width by the new one"},{"id":"d","text":"80 %, since 0.24 of 0.30 remains"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"0.06 / 0.30 = 0.20, so 20 %, which the lesson finds exactly at the Class 2 limit and therefore acceptable only if the controlled revision and customer specification allow it. The other options mistake a difference for a ratio or invert the comparison.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l04","prompt":"What does the lesson say about a laminate scratch depth limit?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A scratch deeper than 0.05 mm is always scrap, whatever the class"},{"id":"b","text":"The repair standard sets no depth limit; prohibited conditions decide"},{"id":"c","text":"Depth matters only above 0.5 mm, where the laminate is structurally weak"},{"id":"d","text":"Depth is irrelevant, so only the visual appearance is assessed"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson states that the repair standard sets no depth limit and instead asks whether delamination, blistering, separation or exposed conductor is present. Inventing a fixed depth threshold is exactly the kind of number the lesson refuses to supply.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m11l04","prompt":"A repaired hole shows 0.06 mm of annular ring on a Class 2 assembly. How does the lesson read it?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It fails, because Class 2 requires a ring of at least 0.10 mm"},{"id":"b","text":"It fails, because any ring below 0.075 mm is scrap for a repaired hole"},{"id":"c","text":"It clears the 0.05 mm minimum quoted in the case and is acceptable"},{"id":"d","text":"It cannot be judged without knowing the original pad diameter"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The case compares 0.06 mm with the 0.05 mm figure it cites for that class and finds the hole acceptable if the controlled revision confirms the minimum. The other options introduce thresholds the lesson does not have.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l04-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m11l04","prompt":"What does the lesson want the disposition record to contain?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The class alone, since the measurements are stored with the product"},{"id":"b","text":"A photograph and the shift supervisor's signature"},{"id":"c","text":"The repair method and the cost of the operation"},{"id":"d","text":"The class, the measurements, the criteria applied and the risk"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The record has to let another engineer repeat the decision, so it carries the class, the measurements, the criteria and the risk accepted. A bare class, a signature and a method-plus-cost entry all leave out the reasoning.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l05","prompt":"How does the lesson separate rework from repair?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Rework restores the design; repair accepts a deviation from it"},{"id":"b","text":"Rework is done by hand; repair is done with hot air on a station"},{"id":"c","text":"Rework needs no record; repair has to be documented and approved"},{"id":"d","text":"Rework applies to SMT; repair applies only to through-hole joints"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Rework brings the assembly back to the original specification, while a repair leaves it meeting a defined, accepted deviation. The tooling, the paperwork and the mounting technology do not define the difference.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l05","prompt":"What does re-inspection have to cover beyond the joint that was touched?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Only the touched joint, since the others were already accepted"},{"id":"b","text":"Adjacent joints, the body and leads, the pad, trace and mask"},{"id":"c","text":"The whole panel again at first-article depth, regardless of scope"},{"id":"d","text":"The stencil and the paste lot used for the original build"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson extends re-inspection to the neighbours and the surrounding pad, trace and mask, because heat and handling spread. Touching only the joint misses the damage rework causes, and re-qualifying the whole panel or the print setup is out of scope.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l05-05","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m11l05","prompt":"Hot-air rework on a Class 3 assembly leaves a lifted pad and exposed copper. What follows from the lesson?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The condition is acceptable if the joint still conducts electrically"},{"id":"b","text":"The condition is a rework defect, so the board returns to the line as is"},{"id":"c","text":"The condition fails Class 3, so a qualified repair or scrap decides it"},{"id":"d","text":"The condition is cosmetic, since the pad is under the component body"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"A lifted pad with exposed copper does not meet the construction and visual criteria the class demands, so only a qualified repair meeting Class 3, or scrap, resolves it. Continuity, a return to the line and a cosmetic reading all fail to apply the class.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l05","prompt":"Where does the required class come from?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The operator, who selects the class that matches the observed condition"},{"id":"b","text":"The solder supplier, whose datasheet lists the applicable class"},{"id":"c","text":"The rework station vendor, according to the machine capability"},{"id":"d","text":"The customer, the product specification or the reliability requirement"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson fixes the class before inspection, from the customer, the product specification or the reliability need. Choosing a class to fit the defect would make acceptance meaningless, and neither supplier nor vendor sets it.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m11l05","prompt":"What does the record have to state besides the method and the materials?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The original defect, the class, the operator and the residual risk"},{"id":"b","text":"The rework time in minutes and the station utilisation for the shift"},{"id":"c","text":"The price of the component replaced during the operation"},{"id":"d","text":"The customer's approval of the rework, obtained before starting"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson asks for the original defect, the class, the method, the materials, the operator and the residual risk, so the decision can be defended later. Timing, cost and a blanket customer approval are not the fields it names.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l05-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m11l05","prompt":"In what order does the lesson have the inspector work?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Inspect the area, then choose the class that the result meets"},{"id":"b","text":"Confirm the class, inspect to the criteria, then repair or scrap"},{"id":"c","text":"Decide repair or scrap, then find the criterion that supports it"},{"id":"d","text":"Check the rework record first, then inspect only what it describes"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The class is fixed first, the inspection is judged against its criteria, and only then does the repair-or-scrap question arise. The other orders let the observation choose its own standard, which is the failure the lesson is written to prevent.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l06-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l06","prompt":"What does the lesson require before heat is applied to the joint?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A photograph of the defect taken with the station camera"},{"id":"b","text":"Approval from the customer for the specific rework operation"},{"id":"c","text":"The allowed cycles, peak, dwell, preheat and cooling, recorded"},{"id":"d","text":"A second operator standing by to confirm the result"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The thermal budget is written down before the iron or hot air touches the joint, so the operation can be judged against something. A photograph, a customer approval and a second opinion are not what the practice asks for at that point.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l06-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l06","prompt":"Why is the iron touch-up counted as a separate cycle in the lesson case?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It uses a different solder alloy, so the alloy change counts"},{"id":"b","text":"It happens after inspection, so the record needs a second line"},{"id":"c","text":"It is performed by a different operator, which counts as a cycle"},{"id":"d","text":"It is another thermal exposure of the joint, whatever the tool"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The count tracks heat delivered to the joint, so a hot-gas pass plus a 2 s iron touch-up is two cycles against a limit of two. Alloy, inspection order and operator identity are not what the count measures.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l06-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m11l06","prompt":"A budget allows 250 °C for 10 s and two cycles. The hot gas measures 248 °C for 8 s, and an iron then touches for 2 s. What is the position?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Two cycles used, hot gas inside both limits; check the joint peak"},{"id":"b","text":"The operation is over budget, because the iron adds temperature beyond 250 °C"},{"id":"c","text":"The operation is within budget, so the joint peak during the touch-up need not be known"},{"id":"d","text":"The operation is over budget, because 8 s plus 2 s exceeds the 10 s dwell limit"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson counts two cycles, finds 248 °C below 250 °C and 8 s below 10 s, and then requires the joint peak during the iron touch-up to be confirmed against the same 250 °C limit. Adding the two contact times into one dwell and assuming the tip temperature is the joint temperature are the two misreadings it warns against.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l06-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m11l06","prompt":"What does the rework record have to identify?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The assembly serial number alone, since the rest is traceable from it"},{"id":"b","text":"The assembly, serial, operator, station, defect and action"},{"id":"c","text":"The operator and the shift, because the tool data is logged by the machine"},{"id":"d","text":"The defect and the materials, since the outcome is visible on the board"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson asks for the assembly, serial, date, operator, station, original defect, action taken and the result, so the class claim can be supported. Any subset that drops the action or the result leaves the record unable to defend the decision.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l06-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m11l06","prompt":"Which failure mode does the lesson put first among problems to watch in practice?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Cosmetic flux staining, then discoloured solder mask"},{"id":"b","text":"Excess solder on the far side of the board, then solder balls"},{"id":"c","text":"Insufficient wetting, then lifted pads and thermal damage"},{"id":"d","text":"Component marking damage from the hot-air nozzle"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson lists insufficient wetting, lifted pads and thermal damage to components or laminate as the typical problems, with residue and cosmetics later. Staining, stray solder and marking damage are real observations but not the headline risks.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m11l06-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m11l06","prompt":"How does the lesson classify the reworked joint?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Against the visual result alone, since the class was met at first build"},{"id":"b","text":"Against the operator's own judgement, recorded as accepted by experience"},{"id":"c","text":"Against the reflow profile, since the thermal history sets acceptance"},{"id":"d","text":"Against the IPC-A-610 criteria for the class, then the repair standard"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The joint is judged on the workmanship criteria for the required class, and then the rework itself is confirmed against the repair standard. The first-build class, personal judgement and the profile do not replace those criteria.","source":"src-024"},{"questionId":"q-m12l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l01","prompt":"Why can an ionic residue cause a failure that only appears in service?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It changes the solder alloy composition, so joints embrittle over months"},{"id":"b","text":"With moisture and bias it conducts and corrodes, long after the build"},{"id":"c","text":"It absorbs heat during reflow, which leaves joints under the specified peak"},{"id":"d","text":"It blocks the conformal coating from curing, so protection never forms"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson describes ionic contamination as a latent risk that needs moisture and voltage to bite, which is why cleanliness is verified rather than judged visually. Alloy change, a reflow heat sink and a coating cure failure are different mechanisms from the electrochemical one it names.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l01-05","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l01","prompt":"What does a surface insulation resistance test measure?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The resistance of the copper traces from end to end of the board"},{"id":"b","text":"The ionic content extracted into a solvent bath, in µg NaCl per cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"Leakage across the board surface under a stated humidity and bias"},{"id":"d","text":"The dielectric strength of the laminate between two copper layers"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"SIR measures surface leakage under specified humidity and bias, which is why the lesson insists the condition be quoted with the result. Trace resistance, extraction conductivity and laminate breakdown are the subjects of other tests.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l01","prompt":"What does a no-clean flux leave behind?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Nothing at all, since the classification means no residue is present"},{"id":"b","text":"Only pure rosin, which is electrically inert in every condition"},{"id":"c","text":"A metallic layer that bonds to the pad and lowers contact resistance"},{"id":"d","text":"An organic film plus ionic activators that can still need removal"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson says a no-clean flux can leave an organic film and ionic activators, so the classification describes what may remain rather than a clean board. Inert rosin under all conditions and a beneficial metallic layer are not what the module describes.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m12l01","prompt":"A board carries bottom-terminated QFNs. Why does that constrain the cleaning method?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The chemistry has to wet, penetrate and rinse a gap that is barely open"},{"id":"b","text":"The packages are too heat-sensitive to survive any wet process"},{"id":"c","text":"The terminations dissolve in water, so only solvent cleaning can be used"},{"id":"d","text":"The packages trap air, so cleaning must be done under vacuum only"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A gap under a bottom termination is hard to reach and hard to rinse, so the lesson asks for low surface tension and a proper rinse rather than for a merely wet surface. Heat sensitivity, water solubility and a vacuum requirement are not the constraints the module states.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l01-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m12l01","prompt":"What does a measured SIR result have to state to be meaningful?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The manufacturer of the instrument and its last calibration date"},{"id":"b","text":"The humidity, the bias and the test condition it was run under"},{"id":"c","text":"The number of coupons tested and the name of the operator"},{"id":"d","text":"The laminate type, since SIR is a property of the material only"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Surface leakage depends on moisture and voltage, so a bare number cannot be compared with a specification — which is why the lesson ties the requirement to a stated condition. Instrument identity, sample administration and laminate type do not substitute for the condition.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l02","prompt":"What makes ultrasonic cleaning a risk as well as a benefit?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The bath heats the board above the laminate glass transition"},{"id":"b","text":"The sound waves ionise the water, which adds chloride to the surface"},{"id":"c","text":"Cavitation lifts particles but can stress sensitive packages"},{"id":"d","text":"It removes the solder mask along with the residue, exposing copper"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson treats cavitation as mechanical action that helps with particulates while carrying a compatibility risk for delicate parts. Overheating, ionisation and mask removal are not the mechanism it describes.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l02","prompt":"Why does the lesson check MSL and ESD before choosing a cleaning method?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Solvents dissolve the moisture sensitivity label printed on the bag"},{"id":"b","text":"ESD controls only apply to components that have already been cleaned"},{"id":"c","text":"MSL classification is set by the cleaning chemistry chosen at the line"},{"id":"d","text":"Wet processes add moisture and charge, which MSL and ESD rules govern"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Cleaning adds both moisture and electrostatic risk, so the lesson lists MSL floor-life rules and ESD controls as constraints on the method. The other options either invert the direction of the rule or misstate where the classification comes from.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m12l02","prompt":"Cleaning, rinsing, drying and handling total 70 min inside a 4 h floor life. What remains, and what does it mean?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"170 min, which fits, but the window must now cover coating"},{"id":"b","text":"170 min, which is irrelevant because cleaning restarts the floor life"},{"id":"c","text":"240 min, because the floor life clock stops while the board is wet"},{"id":"d","text":"40 min, because drying consumes most of the 4 h allowance"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"240 - 70 = 170 min, and the lesson uses that remainder to plan the coating step before the floor life is exceeded. Cleaning does not reset the clock, the clock runs through the wet process, and 40 min is not a figure the case produces.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l02","prompt":"Which residue does aqueous cleaning suit best?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Cured no-clean flux, which water removes more effectively than solvent"},{"id":"b","text":"Water-soluble flux residue, which the water chemistry dissolves"},{"id":"c","text":"Silicone oils, which emulsify readily in plain deionised water"},{"id":"d","text":"Conformal coating overspray, which water lifts without additives"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson matches the chemistry to the residue, and water-soluble flux is the case it names for aqueous cleaning. Polymerised no-clean, silicone oils and cured coating all need other chemistry or mechanical removal.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m12l02","prompt":"A board with trapped particulates in through-holes needs cleaning. What does the lesson add to the method?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A longer rinse only, since particles dissolve given enough water"},{"id":"b","text":"A hotter bath, because particles soften above the flux melting point"},{"id":"c","text":"Mechanical assistance, such as agitation or ultrasonic energy"},{"id":"d","text":"A second cleaning pass with the same chemistry and no agitation"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Particles have to be dislodged rather than dissolved, so the lesson brings in agitation or ultrasonic action with its compatibility caveat. A longer rinse, a hotter bath and a repeat pass all leave the particles where they are.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l02-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m12l02","prompt":"Which checks does the lesson require before conformal coating starts?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The coating thickness alone, since it sets the protection level"},{"id":"b","text":"The operator certification and the shift production target"},{"id":"c","text":"The bath temperature and the number of boards already processed"},{"id":"d","text":"Residue, cleanliness result, dryness and material compatibility"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Coating over a wet or contaminated surface seals the problem in, so the lesson verifies residue, cleanliness, dryness and compatibility first. Thickness, staffing and bath history are process data rather than a gate before coating.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l03","prompt":"What does ROSE actually measure?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Conductivity of an extract, reported as an NaCl equivalent per area"},{"id":"b","text":"The individual concentration of chloride, sulfate and nitrate ions"},{"id":"c","text":"The number of particles above five micrometres per square centimetre"},{"id":"d","text":"The visible residue left on the surface after a solvent rinse"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"ROSE extracts ionic material into a solvent and reads conductivity, then reports the result as an equivalent, which is why it cannot say which ion caused it. Individual ions are ion chromatography, particles are a count, and visible residue is visual inspection.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l03","prompt":"Why does the lesson subtract a blank before judging a ROSE result?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The blank records the board area, which the instrument cannot measure itself"},{"id":"b","text":"The solvent and container carry their own ionic background onto the result"},{"id":"c","text":"Subtracting the blank converts micrograms into NaCl equivalents"},{"id":"d","text":"The blank compensates for the fact that the extract is always saturated"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The blank is the same procedure with no board, and its reading is background that would otherwise be credited to the assembly. Area, unit conversion and saturation are separate concerns from the blank correction.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m12l03","prompt":"ROSE reads 0.34 with a 0.03 blank against a 0.25 limit; chloride 0.12 against 0.10; 1,400 particles over 10 cm² against 100/cm². What is the decision?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Pass: the visual check found nothing, which controls the decision"},{"id":"b","text":"Pass: only the chloride is over its limit, and by a small margin"},{"id":"c","text":"Rework: corrected ROSE, chloride and particle count all fail"},{"id":"d","text":"Rework: the particle count fails, but the ROSE result is acceptable"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"0.31 exceeds 0.25, 0.12 exceeds 0.10 and 140/cm² exceeds 100/cm², so three criteria fail and the board is reworked. Visual inspection passing does not rescue a failed measurement, and the last option misreads the blank correction.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l03","prompt":"What does ion chromatography add to a ROSE result?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It measures total ionic content faster and to a lower detection limit"},{"id":"b","text":"It converts the conductivity reading into a NaCl equivalent directly"},{"id":"c","text":"It replaces the need for any sampling or blank correction"},{"id":"d","text":"It identifies and quantifies individual ions, with their own limits"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Chromatography separates the species, which is why the lesson can set a chloride limit apart from a sulfate one. Speed, unit conversion and freedom from sampling are all misdescriptions of what it does.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m12l03","prompt":"Where does an acceptance limit for cleanliness come from?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The customer specification, product requirement or applicable standard"},{"id":"b","text":"The test bench, which sets a limit from the first measured result"},{"id":"c","text":"The cleaning chemistry datasheet, which states the residual limit"},{"id":"d","text":"The highest value seen on the line that did not cause a complaint"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson states that a cleanliness criterion is never invented at the bench, it comes from the customer, the product requirement or the standard. Deriving limits from data, chemistry datasheets or complaints inverts that order.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l03-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m12l03","prompt":"A particle sample of 1,400 counts is taken over 10 cm². How is it compared with a per-square-centimetre limit?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Compare 1,400 directly with 100, which fails by a factor of fourteen"},{"id":"b","text":"Normalise it to 140 per cm², which exceeds a limit of 100 per cm²"},{"id":"c","text":"Multiply 1,400 by 10, since the sample covers ten square centimetres"},{"id":"d","text":"Average it with the ROSE result, since both describe surface condition"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"1400 / 10 = 140 particles per cm², which does fail against 100 — but only after the normalisation the lesson requires. Comparing the raw count, multiplying by the area or blending two different measurements all produce a number that cannot be judged.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l04","prompt":"Why does the lesson reject silicone next to a gold-plated edge connector?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Silicone dissolves the gold finish during the cure step"},{"id":"b","text":"Silicone cannot cure in the humidity the board will see in service"},{"id":"c","text":"Silicone can migrate and raise contact resistance on the plating"},{"id":"d","text":"Silicone is too hard, so it cracks the connector housing"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Migration and contact resistance are the reason the case gives for keeping silicone away from the connector. Dissolving gold, humidity cure failure and hardness are not the mechanisms the lesson describes.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l04-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l04","prompt":"What does parylene offer that the other coatings do not?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A coating that can be removed locally without any mechanical work"},{"id":"b","text":"The highest resistance to ultraviolet light of the coating families"},{"id":"c","text":"A cure that needs no masking, since it only lands on metal"},{"id":"d","text":"A very thin, uniform, pinhole-free film deposited from vapour"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson describes parylene as vapour-deposited, thin, uniform and free of pinholes. Local removal is its weakness rather than its strength, and neither UV resistance nor a selective landing is claimed for it.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l04-05","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m12l04","prompt":"A dense control board must survive humidity, stay reworkable and sit beside a gold connector. What does the lesson choose?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Urethane, with acrylic as the backup if rework becomes difficult"},{"id":"b","text":"Silicone, because its flexibility protects the connector from stress"},{"id":"c","text":"Parylene, because its thin film keeps the connector clearance open"},{"id":"d","text":"UV cure, because shadowed areas under tall parts cure fastest"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The case selects urethane for moisture and chemical resistance with board-level rework, and keeps acrylic as the easier-to-remove fallback. Silicone is rejected at the connector, parylene loses on rework, and UV cure loses on shadowing under tall parts.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l04","prompt":"Which property makes acrylic the easiest coating for board-level rework?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It cures so slowly that the joint can be reworked before it sets"},{"id":"b","text":"It can be removed with common solvents and mechanical work"},{"id":"c","text":"It conducts enough current to allow in-circuit testing after coating"},{"id":"d","text":"It reflows at the soldering temperature, so the iron clears it"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Acrylic is chosen when access matters because it comes off with solvent and modest mechanical effort. Cure speed, conductivity and reflow behaviour are not the properties the lesson attributes to it.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m12l04","prompt":"A UV-cure coating leaves tacky patches under tall connectors. What does the lesson explain?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The connectors absorb the UV and re-emit it at a longer wavelength"},{"id":"b","text":"The coating lot was applied too thin for the UV dose available"},{"id":"c","text":"Shadowed areas receive no UV, so the coating there does not cure"},{"id":"d","text":"UV coatings only cure on metal surfaces, not on laminate"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Shadowing is the limitation the lesson attaches to UV-cure systems, which is why a board with tall parts does not use one as its primary coating. Absorption by connectors, an under-thick film and a metal-only cure are not the mechanism given.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l04-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m12l04","prompt":"What must a masking plan protect?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Every through-hole joint, since coating weakens a filled barrel"},{"id":"b","text":"Only the board edges, where the conveyor clamps make contact"},{"id":"c","text":"The component reference designators, so that they stay readable"},{"id":"d","text":"Connectors, test points, switches, heatsinks and antennas"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson masks what must stay functional or accessible: connectors, test points, switches, thermal interfaces, optical parts and antennas. Coating a filled barrel, the conveyor edges and the legends are not the masking priorities it states.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l05","prompt":"What does volume solids decide in a spray coating process?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"How much wet film thickness survives as dry film"},{"id":"b","text":"How fast the spray nozzle has to traverse the board"},{"id":"c","text":"Whether the coating can be cured at room temperature"},{"id":"d","text":"How much masking the board needs before spraying"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Solvent leaves during cure, so the dry film is the wet film scaled by the volume solids fraction — the relationship the lesson case works through. Traverse speed, cure temperature and masking scope are set by other factors.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l05","prompt":"What does the lesson warn about dip coating a through-hole assembly?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The bath cannot wet plated holes, so fill defects are guaranteed"},{"id":"b","text":"Uniformity depends on drainage, so withdrawal and drain need control"},{"id":"c","text":"Dip coating cannot be used with any masking, so connectors get coated"},{"id":"d","text":"The coating cures inside the bath, which thickens it over the shift"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Dipping gives good coverage on open assemblies, but the film is set by how the board drains as it leaves the bath. Holes do wet in a dip process, masking is normal alongside it, and cure inside the bath is not the concern the lesson raises.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m12l05","prompt":"A 165 µm wet film at 45 % volume solids is measured against a 75 µm target. What dry film does it give?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 36 µm, well under the target"},{"id":"b","text":"About 367 µm, far over the target"},{"id":"c","text":"About 74 µm, inside a practical tolerance of 75 µm"},{"id":"d","text":"About 110 µm, over the target"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"165 x 0.45 = 74.25 µm, close to the 75 µm target and inside the tolerance the lesson describes, though it still has to be verified after cure. The other options divide by the solids, invert the fraction, or apply two thirds of it.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l05","prompt":"When does the lesson choose selective coating?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"When the whole board must be covered and masking is not available"},{"id":"b","text":"When the coating has to be applied by dip rather than by spray"},{"id":"c","text":"When the production volume is high enough to justify a spray line"},{"id":"d","text":"When part of the assembly needs protection and the rest stays clear"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Selective coating puts protection where it is needed and leaves connectors, test pads and other keep-clear areas untouched. Full coverage, dip application and line volume describe other choices.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m12l05","prompt":"A coating is too thin on the board. Which consequences does the lesson list?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Less insulation and mechanical protection, and a rework trade-off"},{"id":"b","text":"Faster cure and lower stress, with no loss of protection"},{"id":"c","text":"Higher moisture ingress but unchanged mechanical performance"},{"id":"d","text":"No consequence, provided the thinnest point is still visible"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson ties dry film thickness to insulation, mechanical protection, thermal behaviour and repairability, so a thin film moves several of them the wrong way. Claiming faster cure, unchanged mechanics or no consequence ignores that list.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l05-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m12l05","prompt":"Why does the lesson check a coating plan against the whole assembly flow?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The coating line is usually the bottleneck, so its cycle time sets the plan"},{"id":"b","text":"Cleaning, soldering, inspection and test all interact with coating"},{"id":"c","text":"Customers require the same supplier for coating and for solder paste"},{"id":"d","text":"Because the standard forbids coating a board that has been inspected"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"A coating scheme has to survive the steps before and after it — cleaning chemistry, rework access, inspection and test — which is what the compatibility check covers. Bottlenecks, supplier pairing and a prohibition on coated inspected boards are not the reason.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l06-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l06","prompt":"What does the lesson say a smooth, clean-looking surface proves?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"That the cleaning process reached every gap on the assembly"},{"id":"b","text":"That the flux was fully consumed during the reflow stage"},{"id":"c","text":"Nothing about ionic content, which is invisible and must be measured"},{"id":"d","text":"That the coating will adhere, since the surface energy is adequate"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Visual inspection cannot see ions, which is why the lesson pairs it with extraction tests and treats cleanliness as a measurement. Clean appearance also says nothing about gap penetration, flux consumption or surface energy.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l06-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l06","prompt":"What causes a fish-eye in a conformal coating?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The coating was applied too thickly, so it shrank away from the centre"},{"id":"b","text":"A bubble of solvent formed under the film during the cure step"},{"id":"c","text":"The board was cured at too high a temperature for the chemistry"},{"id":"d","text":"Low-surface-energy contamination stops the coating from wetting there"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"A fish-eye is a circular area the coating refused to wet, which the lesson attributes to contamination that lowered the surface energy. Thickness, trapped solvent and cure temperature produce other defects, such as cracking, bubbles and incomplete cure.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l06-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m12l06","prompt":"An ionic test reads 18 µg NaCl/cm² against a 15 limit, with two fish-eyes on the witness board. What is the position?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The board fails by 20 % over the limit and needs rework before coating"},{"id":"b","text":"The board passes, because the visual result and the limit are separate criteria"},{"id":"c","text":"The board passes at 3 µg over, since that is within test tolerance"},{"id":"d","text":"The board fails, but only because of the fish-eyes on the witness board"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"18 against 15 is 3 / 15 = 20 % over the limit, so the cleanliness criterion fails and the board is reworked; the fish-eyes point at the same surface condition. Visual and measured criteria are both required, 3 µg is not a tolerance, and the witness board is evidence rather than the failing item.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l06-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m12l06","prompt":"Why does the lesson call cleanliness testing a decision tool rather than a number?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The test result is only advisory and cannot reject a board"},{"id":"b","text":"The result is read against the risk of that assembly, not a limit"},{"id":"c","text":"The numbers are instrument-dependent, so they are not reported"},{"id":"d","text":"The result replaces the visual inspection whenever it passes"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The measurement is interpreted with the assembly's residue type, geometry and downstream coating in mind, which is how a decision gets made. It does reject boards, it is reported, and it never replaces the visual check.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l06-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m12l06","prompt":"White residue appears around components after cleaning and drying. What does the lesson say it may be?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Unmelted solder powder left behind by an incomplete reflow"},{"id":"b","text":"Silicone oil that has migrated out of the coating film"},{"id":"c","text":"A salt, a flux reaction product or a mineral in the rinse water"},{"id":"d","text":"Oxidised copper lifted from the pad during the wash"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson lists salts, flux reaction products and minerals from the water as the likely identities, which is why the rinse quality matters. Solder powder, migrated silicone and oxidised copper produce different deposits in different places.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m12l06-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K1"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m12l06","prompt":"What does a pass or rework criterion need to state?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The operator name, the date and the shift of the inspection"},{"id":"b","text":"The instrument serial number and its calibration expiry date"},{"id":"c","text":"The board part number, since the limits follow from the design"},{"id":"d","text":"Method, sampled area, limit, magnification and the action"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson wants the test method, the area sampled, the acceptance limit, the inspection magnification and what happens on a failure, so two people reach the same decision. Administrative fields, instrument identity and a part number alone do not define a criterion.","source":"src-027"},{"questionId":"q-m13l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l01","prompt":"What does functional test prove that structural test does not?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"That the assembly works as a system, in electrical terms"},{"id":"b","text":"That every net is connected to the pad it belongs to"},{"id":"c","text":"That each component value is inside its tolerance band"},{"id":"d","text":"That the solder joints meet the visual acceptance criteria"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Functional test exercises the board as a system — power, timing and thermal behaviour — which the lesson separates from structural methods. Nets, values and joints are what in-circuit test, flying probe and inspection cover.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l01","prompt":"When does the lesson prefer flying probe over in-circuit test?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"At high volume, where the probe can keep up with the line rate"},{"id":"b","text":"At low volume or in a prototype, where no fixture is justified"},{"id":"c","text":"When every net has a test point and the fixture is already paid for"},{"id":"d","text":"When the assembly carries no scan-capable devices at all"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"A fixture is a large fixed cost that only amortises with volume, which is why the lesson sends prototypes and low volumes to flying probe. Full access and a paid-off fixture argue the other way, and the absence of scan devices only shifts coverage between the remaining methods.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l01-05","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m13l01","prompt":"A 1,200-component board has 18 % BGA, 40 % boundary-scan coverage and 30 % of nets without test points. What does that combination push you towards?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"In-circuit test alone, since a fixture would reach the BGA network directly"},{"id":"b","text":"Visual inspection alone, since the BGA joints are the only hidden risk"},{"id":"c","text":"Structural test, boundary scan and functional: access alone is short"},{"id":"d","text":"Functional test alone, because it covers every defect class on the board"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Hidden BGA joints need structural coverage, but 30 % of nets have no probe access, so boundary scan carries the digital part and functional test closes the system behaviour. A fixture cannot reach pads that do not exist, inspection cannot see under a BGA, and functional test is not a complete defect screen.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l01","prompt":"What does in-circuit test measure?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The system behaviour of the whole assembly under power"},{"id":"b","text":"The digital interconnect through cells inside scan-capable devices"},{"id":"c","text":"The solder fillet geometry at each joint, from an optical image"},{"id":"d","text":"Components and connections, through probed nodes on a fixture"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"ICT probes nodes and checks components and connections one at a time. System behaviour is functional test, scan cells are boundary scan, and fillet geometry belongs to optical inspection.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m13l01","prompt":"A design review reports 30 % of nets with no test points. What follows?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Those nets have no probe access, so coverage must come elsewhere"},{"id":"b","text":"The fixture can still reach them by probing the component bodies directly"},{"id":"c","text":"The nets are automatically covered by functional test at full coverage"},{"id":"d","text":"The percentage is an error, since every net carries a pad by definition"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"No test point means no probe, which is why the lesson routes that coverage to boundary scan or functional test. Probing bodies is not a thing, functional coverage is not automatic, and a net can reach an inner layer or a via without an accessible pad.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l01-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m13l01","prompt":"At 2,000 units a month with good access, what does the lesson weigh when choosing a fixture?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Whether the fixture can be built before the design is frozen"},{"id":"b","text":"Whether the fixture cost is justified by the cycle time it saves"},{"id":"c","text":"Whether the programme can be run without any test points"},{"id":"d","text":"Whether the customer will accept flying probe as an equivalent"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The volume decision is a cost-against-time trade, which the lesson states as fixture cost versus the cycle time gained. Freeze date, test-point count and customer acceptance are constraints, not the comparison the lesson asks for.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K10"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l02","prompt":"Why does the lesson derive a minimum test-pad diameter?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A larger pad improves the electrical measurement of the component value"},{"id":"b","text":"The pad diameter controls how many probes the fixture can hold"},{"id":"c","text":"The probe tip plus its clearance sets the smallest pad that lands"},{"id":"d","text":"A smaller pad reduces the fixture cost by shortening the probe travel"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The probe has a physical diameter and needs an annular clearance around it, which is why the pad size is calculated rather than assumed. Electrical accuracy, probe count and travel distance are not what the pad diameter is derived from.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l02","prompt":"What decides whether a node needs a paired test point?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Whether the node carries a digital signal faster than one megahertz"},{"id":"b","text":"Whether the component on the node is larger than an 0402 body"},{"id":"c","text":"Whether the net is longer than fifty millimetres across the board"},{"id":"d","text":"Whether it is a guarded analog node that needs a second reference"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The case sets 12 % of nets as guarded analog nodes requiring a pair, because guarding needs a second contact to work. Signal speed, component size and net length are not the criteria the lesson applies.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6","K10"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m13l02","prompt":"Of 1,200 nets, 82 % take one test point and 12 % are guarded pairs. How many test points does that need?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"1,272"},{"id":"b","text":"1,128"},{"id":"c","text":"984"},{"id":"d","text":"1,344"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"984 single plus 288 paired (144 nets doubled) gives 1,272, which the lesson then compares with a 1,500 probe limit. The 1,128 answer forgets to double the guarded nets, 984 counts only the single-point nets, and 1,344 also converts the inaccessible 6 % into points.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K10"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l02","prompt":"What sets the probe limit of a fixture?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The number of components on the board, since each needs one probe"},{"id":"b","text":"The fixture capacity and its wiring, not the net count"},{"id":"c","text":"The test programme, which can only address a fixed number of nodes"},{"id":"d","text":"The board area, divided by the pad pitch of the smallest package"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"A fixture holds a finite number of probes and has finite wiring, which is why the lesson compares the calculated test points with a probe limit. Component count and board area are approximations at best, and the programme follows the fixture rather than capping it.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m13l02","prompt":"A 1.2 mm planned pad meets a 0.9 mm probe tip with 0.15 mm clearance required. What does the calculation say?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It is above the minimum by 0.30 mm, so the pad could shrink"},{"id":"b","text":"It is below the minimum, so the pad must be enlarged to 1.35 mm"},{"id":"c","text":"It is exactly at the minimum, 0.9 + 0.15 + 0.15 mm"},{"id":"d","text":"The clearance applies only once, so the minimum is 1.05 mm"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson adds a clearance on each side, so 0.9 + 0.15 + 0.15 = 1.2 mm and the planned pad sits exactly on the minimum. Reading the clearance as a single allowance or as a margin already included would allow a smaller pad than the probe needs.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l02-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K10"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m13l02","prompt":"The calculation exceeds the available probe count. What does the lesson do?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Removes the inaccessible nets from the calculation and proceeds"},{"id":"b","text":"Doubles up probes on the guarded nodes to save positions"},{"id":"c","text":"Accepts a lower pad clearance so that probes can be spaced closer"},{"id":"d","text":"Moves the overflow nets to scan or functional test"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Coverage is allocated across methods, so nets the fixture cannot reach go to another test. Dropping nets removes evidence, pairing more probes on the same nodes adds positions rather than saving them, and shrinking clearance below the probe requirement creates a mechanical failure.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l03","prompt":"What limits a flying probe station?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It moves and measures one point at a time, so point time caps it"},{"id":"b","text":"It cannot reach nets smaller than one millimetre of pad diameter"},{"id":"c","text":"It measures resistance but not component value or polarity"},{"id":"d","text":"It needs a dedicated fixture for each board revision produced"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson builds the whole throughput estimate from move, dwell and overhead per point, which is why 1,200 points take roughly twenty minutes. Access, measurement scope and fixture independence are the advantages of the method, not its limit.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l03","prompt":"Why does the lesson apply a parallel efficiency below 100 %?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The measurement instrument is slower than the probe motors"},{"id":"b","text":"Probes cannot all work independently, so some time is spent waiting"},{"id":"c","text":"The board handling time is counted again inside each point"},{"id":"d","text":"Availability already includes the waiting, so the figure is a safety net"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Four probes do not deliver four times the throughput because their paths and measurements interfere, which is what the efficiency factor captures. Instrument speed, double-counted handling and a redundant safety margin are not the reason the lesson gives.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m13l03","prompt":"A 250 mm move at 100 mm/s plus 0.30 s of electrical time, across 4 probes at 0.65 efficiency. What is the effective time per point?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 0.70 s"},{"id":"b","text":"About 2.80 s"},{"id":"c","text":"About 1.08 s"},{"id":"d","text":"About 4.31 s"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"2.8 s serial becomes 0.70 s across four probes, and dividing by 0.65 gives 1.077 s per point. The 0.70 figure skips the efficiency loss, 2.80 s is the serial time with one probe, and 4.31 s multiplies by the efficiency instead of dividing.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l03","prompt":"What has to be built before a flying probe can test a board?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A probe fixture with one pin per accessible net"},{"id":"b","text":"A boundary-scan chain programmed into the devices"},{"id":"c","text":"A functional harness that powers the whole assembly"},{"id":"d","text":"A measurement programme, with no mechanical fixture"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"That is the trade the lesson draws: no fixture, but a programme whose effort grows with the point list. Fixture pins and scan chains belong to the other methods, and a functional harness addresses system behaviour rather than nodes.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m13l03","prompt":"The estimate gives 2.34 boards per hour. How does the lesson describe that figure?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"An upper bound, because real boards add retries and probing problems"},{"id":"b","text":"A guaranteed rate, since availability has already been applied"},{"id":"c","text":"A lower bound, because parallel efficiency usually improves in practice"},{"id":"d","text":"The exact capacity, provided the programme is written without errors"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson labels the result an upper bound: it assumes one move and one dwell per point, and real probing adds retries and access problems. Availability and efficiency are already in the number, so calling it guaranteed or exact overstates it, and efficiency rarely rises above the model.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l03-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m13l03","prompt":"1,200 points at 1.077 s each, plus 15 s of handling. What is the cycle time?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 21.5 min"},{"id":"b","text":"About 21.8 min"},{"id":"c","text":"About 30.0 min"},{"id":"d","text":"About 2.2 min"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"1,292 s of test plus 15 s of handling is 1,307 s, or 21.8 min. The 21.5 min answer drops the handling, 30.0 min over-estimates the point time, and 2.2 min loses a factor of ten.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K10"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l04","prompt":"What can boundary scan not find?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Opens on the digital nets that connect two scan-capable devices"},{"id":"b","text":"Shorts between adjacent pins inside a scan-capable package"},{"id":"c","text":"Faults on nets without scan pins, and analog or power defects"},{"id":"d","text":"Interconnect faults on a BGA that has no test-point access"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Scan works through cells inside scan-capable devices, so anything outside that boundary — including the lesson's power, ground and analog nets — is invisible to it. The other three are exactly the faults it is chosen to find, BGA access being its main advantage.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l04-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l04","prompt":"Why does the lesson put a boundary-scan run in the first boards of a build?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It is the only method allowed before the fixture is calibrated"},{"id":"b","text":"The chain only works while the boards are still on the panel"},{"id":"c","text":"Scan results expire, so they must be taken within one shift"},{"id":"d","text":"It debugs access and the chain while changes are still cheap"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson uses scan together with flying probe and functional test on the first boards to iron out access and chain problems early. There is no calibration rule, no panel restriction and no time limit on a scan result.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l04-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6","K10"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m13l04","prompt":"Boundary scan reaches 720 of 1,200 nets, flying probe 600 of 1,200. What coverage do the two give, and what does it mean?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"60 % and 50 %, so neither is enough alone here"},{"id":"b","text":"60 % and 50 %, so together they cover all 1,200 nets"},{"id":"c","text":"50 % and 60 %, since probe coverage is counted first"},{"id":"d","text":"120 % and 100 %, because the nets overlap completely"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The two coverages are 60 % and 50 %, and the lesson combines them by reasoning about overlap rather than by adding them, which is why it keeps functional test in the strategy. Adding the percentages to 110 % or swapping them misreads the arithmetic.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K10"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l04","prompt":"What does a boundary-scan vector consist of?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A voltage step applied to one probe while another measures current"},{"id":"b","text":"A bit pattern shifted through the scan chain and captured back"},{"id":"c","text":"An optical image compared against a stored model of the joint"},{"id":"d","text":"A functional stimulus applied to the assembly input connector"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The vector is shifted through the chain of cells, applied at the pins and read back, which is why the chain length and clock rate set the time. Voltage stepping, optical comparison and functional stimulus describe the other methods.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m13l04","prompt":"Functional test catches 480 defects but misses 240 latent opens. What does the lesson do with that?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accepts the miss rate, because latent opens fail in the field anyway"},{"id":"b","text":"Adds a second functional pass with a different stimulus pattern"},{"id":"c","text":"Keeps a structural method, since functional test cannot stand alone"},{"id":"d","text":"Reduces the functional coverage, since it is already catching the most"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The 240 misses are the argument for keeping structural coverage alongside functional test. Accepting the gap, repeating the same method and cutting coverage all leave those opens undetected.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l04-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K10"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m13l04","prompt":"A 1,000-cell chain at 10 MHz shifts 1,000 vectors. How long does the shifting take?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 1.0 s of pure shift time"},{"id":"b","text":"About 10 s of pure shift time"},{"id":"c","text":"About 0.1 ms of pure shift time"},{"id":"d","text":"About 0.1 s of pure shift time"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"One vector is 1,000 bits / 10 MHz = 100 µs, and a thousand of them make 100 ms, with overhead taking the practical figure to about 0.2 s. The other options misplace the decimal by one or three places.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l05","prompt":"How does node coverage differ from defect coverage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Node coverage counts nets reached; defect coverage weights by occurrence"},{"id":"b","text":"Node coverage counts defects found; defect coverage counts nets exercised"},{"id":"c","text":"The two are identical once the overlap between methods is subtracted"},{"id":"d","text":"Node coverage applies to digital nets only, defect coverage to analog"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson computes node coverage as the union of nodes exercised and defect coverage as the detection rate weighted by how often each defect class occurs. The other options swap the terms, claim identity, or split the board by signal type.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l05","prompt":"Why does the lesson subtract the overlap between ICT and functional nodes?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The overlap marks nodes that neither method can actually test"},{"id":"b","text":"Counting a shared node twice would overstate the coverage achieved"},{"id":"c","text":"Functional test invalidates the ICT result on any node it touches"},{"id":"d","text":"The overlap is subtracted to convert nodes into defect opportunities"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Coverage is the union of what the methods reach, so a node counted once per method inflates the figure. Overlap does not mean untestable, one method does not invalidate another, and the subtraction happens before any conversion to defect terms.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m13l05","prompt":"Occurrence is 1,500 ppm visible, 800 electrical and 200 parametric, detected at 70 %, 90 % and 60 %. What is the weighted defect coverage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"73.3 %, leaving about 610 ppm of escapes"},{"id":"b","text":"75.6 %, leaving about 390 ppm of escapes"},{"id":"c","text":"75.6 %, leaving about 610 ppm of escapes"},{"id":"d","text":"100 %, because every class has some detection"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Detected totals 1,050 + 720 + 120 = 1,890 ppm of 2,500, so 75.6 % and 610 ppm escape. The 73.3 % figure averages the three percentages without weighting, and 390 ppm subtracts the wrong number from the total.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l05","prompt":"What is a residual escape in the lesson's terms?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Any defect found after the board has left the factory"},{"id":"b","text":"The number of boards that fail test and need rework"},{"id":"c","text":"The share of nets that no method can physically reach"},{"id":"d","text":"Occurrence the combined test misses, from a weighted model"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The escape estimate comes from occurrence multiplied by the undetected share for each class, which is what makes it a planning number. Field returns, first-pass failures and inaccessible nets are different measures.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m13l05","prompt":"Which defect classes does the lesson name as left uncovered?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Under-component opens, shorts and intermittent joints"},{"id":"b","text":"Wrong component value and reversed polarity on diodes"},{"id":"c","text":"Bridging between adjacent fine-pitch leads"},{"id":"d","text":"Missing components on the top side of the assembly"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson lists under-component opens and shorts that no method exercises, plus intermittent joints that may not fail during the test. The other classes are the ones optical and electrical methods do catch, which is why they are not the gap.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l05-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m13l05","prompt":"ICT reaches 980 nodes, functional 150, with 30 shared. What is the node coverage of 1,200 nodes?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"94.2 %"},{"id":"b","text":"91.7 %"},{"id":"c","text":"81.7 %"},{"id":"d","text":"97.5 %"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The union is 980 + 150 - 30 = 1,100 nodes, so 1,100 / 1,200 = 91.7 %. The 94.2 % figure forgets the overlap, 81.7 % counts only ICT, and 97.5 % invents a larger union.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l06-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l06","prompt":"Why does the lesson combine coverages conservatively instead of adding them?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Conservative combination is required by the test-equipment standard"},{"id":"b","text":"Each method tests the same nets, so only the strongest one counts"},{"id":"c","text":"Different methods overlap, so the sum would overstate what is tested"},{"id":"d","text":"Coverage percentages cannot be added because they have different units"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Scan and ICT reach many of the same nets, so the lesson adds only a proven increment rather than the full second figure. There is no standard demanding it, the methods do not test identical sets, and percentages of the same total do share a unit.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l06-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l06","prompt":"How does cycle time enter the strategy decision?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It decides the coverage, since faster tests reach more nets per board"},{"id":"b","text":"It replaces the fixture cost, because time and money are interchangeable"},{"id":"c","text":"It does not enter, because test time is set by the programme alone"},{"id":"d","text":"It sets how much line capacity the test step consumes"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson puts cycle times beside the volume so that throughput, not just capability, decides the method. Coverage does not improve with speed, time is not a substitute for capital cost, and the programme does not fix the time on its own.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l06-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m13l06","prompt":"With 1,400 nets and a 75 % scan-capable share, where 80 % of scan-capable interconnects are testable, what is the scan coverage?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 840 nets"},{"id":"b","text":"About 1,050 nets"},{"id":"c","text":"About 1,120 nets"},{"id":"d","text":"About 672 nets"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"1,400 x 0.75 x 0.80 = 840 nets, which the lesson then combines with ICT coverage rather than adding. The 1,050 answer stops after the scan-capable share, 1,120 applies 0.80 to the full net count, and 672 applies only 0.60.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l06-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m13l06","prompt":"Which factors does the lesson weigh for a product mix?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Board size, laminate type, coating and panel format"},{"id":"b","text":"Volume, complexity, cost and the rework loop"},{"id":"c","text":"Operator count, shift pattern, floor space and lighting"},{"id":"d","text":"Solder alloy, flux type, stencil thickness and paste lot"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"A strategy is chosen on volume, complexity, cost and how defects flow back into rework. Board construction, staffing and process materials shape the build but are not the four factors the lesson sets out.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l06-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m13l06","prompt":"What does the rework loop do to a test strategy?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It adds coverage, since reworked boards are tested twice"},{"id":"b","text":"It removes the need for functional test on the repaired unit"},{"id":"c","text":"It turns detection into feedback, so failures must be diagnosable"},{"id":"d","text":"It makes cycle time irrelevant, since repairs dominate the cost"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"A test that finds a defect but cannot say where it is sends the board into a slow loop, which is why the lesson weighs diagnosability alongside detection. Rework does not add structural coverage, does not waive functional test, and does not make cycle time irrelevant.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m13l06-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m13l06","prompt":"ICT takes 25 s, scan 8 s and functional 45 s, giving roughly 78 s per board. What does that mean against a 180 s probe cycle?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The probe route is faster, because it needs no fixture handling"},{"id":"b","text":"Both routes take the same time once handling is included"},{"id":"c","text":"Time cannot be compared without knowing the board dimensions"},{"id":"d","text":"The fixture route is about twice as fast, so volume favours it"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"78 s against 180 s is a little over a factor of two above the probe route, which is the kind of comparison the lesson uses to justify fixture capital at volume. Handling is already part of the fixture times, and board size does not change the arithmetic.","source":"src-030"},{"questionId":"q-m14l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l01","prompt":"Why does the lesson audit measurement before ranking stages?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Raw stage counts mix true defects with false calls, so the ranking moves"},{"id":"b","text":"Auditing lets the line drop stage categories it does not want to report"},{"id":"c","text":"The audit proves that the inspection machine is within its warranty terms"},{"id":"d","text":"Measurement integrity only matters for the functional test station"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"In the lesson case, correcting 270 reflow calls to 210 confirmed defects still leaves reflow on top but changes the size of the gap, and the audit is what allows that. The audit does not remove categories, it is not a warranty check, and it applies to every station whose calls feed the Pareto.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l01","prompt":"How does the lesson want a stage defect rate expressed, and why?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"As a raw count, because rates hide how many defects were actually found"},{"id":"b","text":"Per 1,000 boards, because volume differs between windows and products"},{"id":"c","text":"As a percentage of the line capacity, since that is what the plant is paid for"},{"id":"d","text":"As a cost figure, because that is what management acts on"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"A rate per 1,000 boards makes two weeks comparable even when the volumes differ, which is how the lesson presents the 22.5 per 1,000 reflow figure. Raw counts do the opposite, capacity percentage mixes two different quantities, and cost belongs to a later prioritisation step.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l01-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l01","prompt":"Over 12,000 boards a stage records 270 defect calls. What is its raw rate per 1,000 boards?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"2.25 per 1,000"},{"id":"b","text":"45.0 per 1,000"},{"id":"c","text":"22.5 per 1,000"},{"id":"d","text":"270 per 1,000"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"270 / 12,000 x 1,000 = 22.5, which is the top stage in the lesson case before and after correction. The other options shift the decimal, double the result, or forget to scale to 1,000 boards.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l01","prompt":"What does a seeded detection audit measure?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"How many false calls the machine makes on production boards"},{"id":"b","text":"How long the inspector takes to disposition a reject image"},{"id":"c","text":"How many stages the defect table should contain"},{"id":"d","text":"How many of the seeded defects the inspection finds"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Seeded defects give a known denominator, so the detection rate is the share that was found — 48 of 50 in the lesson case. False calls come from an AOI audit, cycle time is a separate study, and the table structure follows the process.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m14l01","prompt":"Of 270 reflow calls, the audit confirms 210 defects. What do the other 60 represent, and what is the false-call rate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"False calls, giving a rate of about 22 % of calls"},{"id":"b","text":"Escapes, giving an escape rate of about 22 % of calls"},{"id":"c","text":"Missing defects, giving a detection rate of about 22 %"},{"id":"d","text":"Seeded defects, giving a seeded rate of about 22 %"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The 60 unconfirmed calls are false calls, and 60 / 270 is a little over 22 %. Escapes are defects the inspection missed, missing defects are the same thing counted another way, and seeded defects are the audit inputs rather than the outputs.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l01-e3","type":"calc","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l01","prompt":"A seeded audit places 50 reflow defects and the inspection finds 48. What are the detection and escape rates?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"48 % detected and about 52 % escaped"},{"id":"b","text":"96 % detected and about 4 % escaped"},{"id":"c","text":"96 % detected and about 2 % escaped"},{"id":"d","text":"4 % detected and about 96 % escaped"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"48 / 50 = 96 % found, so 2 of 50, about 4 %, escaped. The 52 % figure confuses the count with a ratio of the misses, 2 % treats the two misses as a percentage of the defects found, and the last option inverts the whole audit.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l02","prompt":"What is a change point, and why does the lesson reach for it first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The point where the defect rate crosses the customer complaint threshold"},{"id":"b","text":"The station where the defect is first detected on the line"},{"id":"c","text":"The moment the data shifts, because it points at what changed with it"},{"id":"d","text":"The date the corrective action is due to be signed off"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"A step in the data is evidence of a single cause entering the process, so the change plus whatever happened at that time is the fastest route to a cause. Complaint thresholds, detection stations and action dates are administrative markers rather than process changes.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l02","prompt":"How does the lesson separate a root cause from a contributing cause?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The root cause is the one the operator reports; contributors are found by engineering"},{"id":"b","text":"The root cause is always a machine setting; contributors are always material or people"},{"id":"c","text":"The root cause is the first item in the fishbone; everything later is a contributor"},{"id":"d","text":"The root cause is necessary; contributors only make the effect worse"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Necessity is the test: without the root cause the effect does not happen, while contributors only modify its size or frequency. Who found it, which category it belongs to and its position in a diagram say nothing about that difference.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l02","prompt":"A tombstone rate rises from 0.2 % to 1.5 % at 1,000 boards a day. AOI catches 80 % and functional test 5 % of what AOI misses. What is the daily escape?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 2.85 a day after the change, against 0.38 before"},{"id":"b","text":"About 15 escapes a day after the change, against 2.0 before"},{"id":"c","text":"About 3.0 escapes a day after the change, against 0.4 before"},{"id":"d","text":"About 0.75 escapes a day after the change, against 0.1 before"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Fifteen defects a day less the 80 % AOI finds leaves 3.0, and functional test catches only 5 % of those, so 2.85 escape; the same chain on 2.0 gives 0.38. The second option stops at the defect count, the third stops before functional test, and the last applies the 80 % twice.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l02","prompt":"When does a 5-Why chain stop in the lesson's discipline?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"When the chain reaches five steps, whatever the last answer is"},{"id":"b","text":"When the answer is a process condition that can be changed"},{"id":"c","text":"When the answer names a person who made the mistake"},{"id":"d","text":"When the answer is the defect that the customer reported"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The chain stops at an actionable process condition, which is what makes it a corrective action rather than a story. Stopping at a count, at a person or at the symptom all leave the process unchanged.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m14l02","prompt":"What does a fishbone diagram organise, and what does it not do?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It proves the root cause, so no further testing is needed"},{"id":"b","text":"It ranks the stages of the line by defect count"},{"id":"c","text":"It sorts candidate causes by category; it proves nothing"},{"id":"d","text":"It replaces the defect table, since it contains the same data"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The diagram is a structured list of possibilities across categories such as machine, material, method and people, and causes still have to be tested. It does not prove anything, it does not rank stages, and it is not a substitute for the Pareto data.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l02-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l02","prompt":"A defect rate jumps after a paste lot change, and the lot behaved normally elsewhere. What does the lesson call the paste lot?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A confirmed root cause, since the timing lines up"},{"id":"b","text":"A contributing cause, since the timing could be coincidence"},{"id":"c","text":"A detection escape, since the defect reached the customer"},{"id":"d","text":"A strong candidate for the cause, subject to a controlled test"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Timing that lines up with a change makes the lot a strong candidate, but the lesson still requires a controlled test before it is declared. Calling it confirmed on timing alone, downgrading it on speculation, or relabelling it as a detection issue all skip that step.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l03","prompt":"How does the lesson set the start of a suspect window?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The earliest credible change or verified good check"},{"id":"b","text":"The time the defect was first reported by an operator on the line"},{"id":"c","text":"The start of the shift in which the defect was found"},{"id":"d","text":"The time the line was stopped after the defect was confirmed"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The case chooses 13:45 over a 13:50 verified-good check because the change was earlier, which widens rather than narrows the net. Operator reports, shift starts and the stop time all risk leaving suspect boards outside the quarantine.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l03","prompt":"What has to be included in a quarantine beyond the suspect boards still on the line?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Only the panels that failed the inspection that raised the alarm"},{"id":"b","text":"Work in process, finished goods and shipped units from the window"},{"id":"c","text":"Only the boards produced after the line was restarted"},{"id":"d","text":"Nothing further, since boards already shipped are outside the plant"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Containment covers the whole traceable window, including stock and shipped units, because those are the ones that reach the customer. Failing panels alone, post-restart output and a plant boundary all leave the exposure open.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l03","prompt":"A window runs from 13:45 to 14:25 at 1,200 boards an hour. How many boards are suspect?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"1,200 boards"},{"id":"b","text":"480 boards"},{"id":"c","text":"800 boards"},{"id":"d","text":"900 boards"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The window is 40 min, and 1,200 an hour is 20 a minute, so 800 boards. The 1,200 answer assumes a full hour, 480 uses 24 min, and 900 uses 45 min.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l03","prompt":"What is the purpose of D3 containment in an 8D?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"To change the process so that the defect cannot occur again"},{"id":"b","text":"To prove that the process was capable before the incident"},{"id":"c","text":"To document the team that will investigate the problem"},{"id":"d","text":"To stop the defect reaching customers before the cause is known"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Containment buys time: it protects the customer while the cause is still being found, and it is explicitly not the permanent fix. Capability evidence and team records belong to other disciplines of the report.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m14l03","prompt":"Why does the lesson prefer the wider suspect window even at the cost of more scrap?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A contained window is reversible; a shipped escape is not"},{"id":"b","text":"Wider windows reduce the amount of documentation needed"},{"id":"c","text":"The customer pays for the scrap, so the cost is not the plant's"},{"id":"d","text":"A wider window makes the effectiveness metric easier to meet"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Quarantined boards can be inspected and released, while a board shipped with the defect cannot be recalled as cheaply, which is the asymmetry the lesson acts on. Documentation does not shrink, cost does not disappear, and the metric is not made easier.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l03-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l03","prompt":"What does the lesson require in the effectiveness metric for a containment?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A statement that the defect has not recurred since the fix"},{"id":"b","text":"A quantity, a window and the inspection that will show it"},{"id":"c","text":"The signature of the customer quality representative"},{"id":"d","text":"The cost of the quarantine, compared with the cost of a recall"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The metric has to be measurable and dated, so the lesson pairs a count with a window and a method. A bare statement, an approval and a cost comparison do not demonstrate that the containment works.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l04","prompt":"What does the lesson say a problem statement must separate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The cost of the defect from the cost of the corrective action"},{"id":"b","text":"The internal complaint from the external complaint about the same lot"},{"id":"c","text":"Occurrence from detection, because both failures reached the customer"},{"id":"d","text":"The machine that made the defect from the operator who found it"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"A defect that reaches a customer is both a failure to prevent and a failure to detect, and the lesson names that pair in D2. Cost, complaint routing and attribution to person or machine are other questions.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l04-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l04","prompt":"Where do 8D reports most often fail, according to the lesson?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"At assembling a team, because the right people are rarely available"},{"id":"b","text":"At describing the problem, because customers already know the symptom"},{"id":"c","text":"At containing the stock, because warehouses are outside quality control"},{"id":"d","text":"At proving the action worked and preventing the same cause"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson points at D6 and D7 — effectiveness and prevention — as the weak disciplines, since a plausible fix is easy to write and hard to prove. Team formation, problem description and containment are usually done better, and are not the failure the lesson highlights.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l04-05","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l04","prompt":"A customer reports tombstoned resistors on a shipped lot. Which content does D2 need?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Product, line, process step, defect mode, lot, quantity, method"},{"id":"b","text":"The name of the customer contact and the date of the complaint only"},{"id":"c","text":"The list of team members and their availability for the week"},{"id":"d","text":"The cost of the recall, since that sets the priority of the report"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"D2 describes the problem precisely enough to be investigated: what, where, at which step, how many, with what trace and measured how. A contact and a date, a team roster and a cost figure do not define the problem.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l04","prompt":"Which discipline carries the permanent corrective action?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"D3, because containment is the action that stops the defect"},{"id":"b","text":"D5, chosen after the root cause in D4 has been verified"},{"id":"c","text":"D6, because that is where the result is measured"},{"id":"d","text":"D8, because closure is where the fix is signed off"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The action follows the verified cause, which is the order the lesson insists on. Containment is temporary by definition, D6 verifies, and closure does not introduce a fix the report has not already chosen.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m14l04","prompt":"A report offers four possible causes and one action that would fix all of them. What does the lesson say?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A single action is efficient, so the causes need no further work"},{"id":"b","text":"Four causes should be reduced to a fishbone diagram and left there"},{"id":"c","text":"The cause must be verified first, or the fix proves nothing"},{"id":"d","text":"The action can stand, provided the effectiveness metric passes"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Without verification, a successful action cannot be attributed to any of the four causes and the defect may return by another path. Efficiency, diagramming and a passing metric do not replace the verification the lesson demands.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l04-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l04","prompt":"What does D7 require beyond fixing the line where the defect appeared?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A second 8D report for every other product in the plant"},{"id":"b","text":"A repeat of the customer complaint process for the same lot"},{"id":"c","text":"A re-audit of the calibration records of the whole department"},{"id":"d","text":"Checking other lines and products for the same failure mode"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"Prevention means asking where else this cause can act, which is why the lesson extends D7 beyond the original line. A report per product, a repeat complaint and a blanket calibration audit are broader than the question requires.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l05","prompt":"How does the lesson separate corrective from preventive action?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Corrective removes the cause; preventive keeps it from acting elsewhere"},{"id":"b","text":"Corrective is done by the line; preventive is done by the quality department"},{"id":"c","text":"Corrective is documented; preventive is communicated verbally"},{"id":"d","text":"Corrective addresses detection; preventive addresses occurrence"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The difference is scope: one cause removed where it occurred, the same cause kept from recurring in new places or products. Who performs it, how it is recorded and which failure it addresses are not the lesson's distinction.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l05","prompt":"A fix cut defects by 89 % but the rate is still above target. What does the lesson do?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Close the 8D, since an 89 % improvement is inside normal variation"},{"id":"b","text":"Keep the 8D open and strengthen the action before closing"},{"id":"c","text":"Reset the target to the achieved rate and close the report"},{"id":"d","text":"Close the 8D and open a new one with a lower priority"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Effectiveness is judged against the agreed metric, not against the size of the improvement, so the report stays open until the target is met. Accepting the improvement, moving the target or re-filing the problem all close a loop that has not closed.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l05","prompt":"A wave line records 180 defects in 12,000 boards, then 24 in 15,000. What are the rates and the reduction?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"15,000 ppm to 1,600 ppm, a reduction of about 11 %"},{"id":"b","text":"1,500 ppm to 160 ppm, a reduction of about 89 %"},{"id":"c","text":"15,000 ppm to 1,600 ppm, a reduction of about 89 %"},{"id":"d","text":"15,000 ppm to 1,600 ppm, a reduction of about 99 %"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"180 / 12,000 = 15,000 ppm and 24 / 15,000 = 1,600 ppm, and (15,000 - 1,600) / 15,000 is 89.3 %. The 11 % figure states what remains rather than what was removed, the third option drops a decimal, and 99 % overstates the change.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l05","prompt":"What makes an effectiveness check meaningful?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A statement from the process engineer that the fix is in place"},{"id":"b","text":"A single good shift of data after the change is implemented"},{"id":"c","text":"The absence of complaints from the customer for one week"},{"id":"d","text":"An agreed metric, a defined sample and a window for recurrence"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The lesson requires the metric, the sample and the window to be fixed in advance so the result can be judged. A statement, one shift and one quiet week are not evidence that the cause is gone.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m14l05","prompt":"Defects fall after a fix but a different defect mode rises in the same window. What does the lesson call that?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A shift the action may have introduced, so the check is incomplete"},{"id":"b","text":"A successful corrective action, since the original defect is gone"},{"id":"c","text":"Normal variation, since defect modes always trade against each other"},{"id":"d","text":"A measurement problem at the inspection station"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"An action that removes one mode by creating another has not improved the process, which is why effectiveness is measured across the stage and not on one code. Calling it success, normal variation or a measurement artefact misses the trade the action made.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l05-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l05","prompt":"A target is 500 ppm and a verification sample shows 1,600 ppm. What is the correct conclusion?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The action is effective, because the rate dropped substantially"},{"id":"b","text":"The action is not yet effective by the agreed metric"},{"id":"c","text":"The target was unrealistic, so it should be renegotiated"},{"id":"d","text":"The sample was too small, so the result can be set aside"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson states the conclusion directly: above the agreed target, the corrective action is not yet effective. Improvement size, target renegotiation and sample size are not grounds for closing the report.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l06-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l06","prompt":"What does the lesson put in the control plan after a corrective action?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The name of the project and the date the 8D was closed"},{"id":"b","text":"The cost of the action and the savings it produced"},{"id":"c","text":"The parameter, its limits, the method, the frequency and the owner"},{"id":"d","text":"A copy of the customer complaint as an attachment"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"A control plan is only useful if it says what to watch, against which limits, with which method and at what frequency. Project identity, economics and the complaint text do not tell an operator what to do on the line.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l06-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l06","prompt":"What has to be updated in the PFMEA after a verified cause is found?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The revision letter only, since the analysis structure stays the same"},{"id":"b","text":"The team list, because the people who investigated own the risk"},{"id":"c","text":"Nothing, because the PFMEA describes the design rather than the process"},{"id":"d","text":"The failure mode, its cause and the control that now applies"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The analysis exists to record what can fail and how it is controlled, so a newly verified cause and the control that now addresses it belong in it. A revision bump alone, a team list and leaving it untouched all leave the risk assessment out of date.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l06-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l06","prompt":"A peak moves from 232 °C to 240 °C and TAL from 48 s to 55 s, against a 235-245 °C and 40-70 s datasheet range. What is the finding?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Both proposed values sit inside the datasheet range"},{"id":"b","text":"The peak is inside the range but the TAL is above it"},{"id":"c","text":"Both values are outside the range, so the change cannot be run"},{"id":"d","text":"The peak change is 8 °C and therefore too large for a bounded change"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"240 °C falls inside 235-245 °C and 55 s inside 40-70 s, so the proposal is within the window and becomes a bounded, verifiable change. The other options misread one of the ranges or invent a limit on the step size.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l06-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l06","prompt":"What does the lesson use the auditor checklist for?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Recording the auditor's opinion of the line layout"},{"id":"b","text":"Confirming the documented controls are still followed"},{"id":"c","text":"Replacing the control plan when the document is lost"},{"id":"d","text":"Setting the defect target for the next quarter"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The checklist verifies practice against the plan on a schedule, which is why it exists alongside the control plan. Opinions, document substitution and target setting are not its purpose.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l06-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m14l06","prompt":"A change-control proposal touches the reflow peak and TAL at the same time. What does the lesson require?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Only an approval signature, because both values stay inside the window"},{"id":"b","text":"A full process qualification, since any profile change is major"},{"id":"c","text":"A stated reason and a verification sample that can show the effect"},{"id":"d","text":"No verification, since the paste datasheet already defines the limits"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"A bounded change still needs its reason recorded and its effect proven on a sample, which the case builds into the tombstone reduction target. A signature alone, a blanket qualification and skipping verification all skip the evidence.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l06-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l06","prompt":"A verification aims at below 50 ppm with a zero-defect sample. What does the lesson need to size that sample?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The line rate alone, since it sets how many boards an hour pass"},{"id":"b","text":"The customer's sample size for incoming inspection"},{"id":"c","text":"The number of boards the AOI can inspect per shift"},{"id":"d","text":"A statistical rule for zero failures at the stated confidence"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"With zero observed defects the sample has to be large enough that the claimed rate is credible at the stated confidence, which is the binomial rule the case applies. Line rate, an incoming-inspection convention and machine capacity do not answer the statistical question.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l07-03","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l07","prompt":"What does the workshop check before trusting its own Pareto?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"That inspection has the resolution and repeatability needed"},{"id":"b","text":"That the total defect count is above a hundred events"},{"id":"c","text":"That the stages are listed in the order of the process flow"},{"id":"d","text":"That the numbers were collected by the quality department"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A Pareto of unreliable calls ranks noise, so the workshop confirms the measurement first. Volume, flow order and the collecting department do not make the counts dependable.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l07-04","type":"single","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l07","prompt":"What does a cumulative percentage add to a stage Pareto?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It converts defect counts into a rate per thousand boards"},{"id":"b","text":"It shows where the first few stages reach most defects"},{"id":"c","text":"It removes the need for a measurement audit before analysis"},{"id":"d","text":"It ranks the stages by cost rather than by count"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The cumulative line answers how few stages carry most of the problem, which is how the workshop picks where to act. It is not a rate conversion, it does not replace the audit, and it does not switch the ranking to cost.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l07-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l07","prompt":"Of 100 defects, print takes 40, placement 30, reflow 20 and wave 10. What is the cumulative share after the first two stages?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"40 %"},{"id":"b","text":"30 %"},{"id":"c","text":"70 %"},{"id":"d","text":"90 %"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"40 + 30 = 70 %, so two stages carry nearly three quarters of the defects, and adding reflow brings it to 90 %. Reading a single stage, the second stage or the point after reflow all answer a different question from the one asked.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l07-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m14l07","prompt":"What does the workshop produce from symptom to closure?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A list of complaints, sorted by the date they were received"},{"id":"b","text":"A new process specification written by the quality department"},{"id":"c","text":"A cost report showing what the defect has consumed this year"},{"id":"d","text":"A Pareto, a verified cause, an action, an owner, a date, a metric"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The exercise walks the whole 8D chain, so the output is the cause, the action with an owner and date, and the metric that will show it worked. Complaint lists, specifications and cost reports are inputs or side products of other work.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l07-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m14l07","prompt":"Which stage does the workshop act on first, and why?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The largest share, which gives the biggest return for the effort"},{"id":"b","text":"The last stage, because it is closest to the customer"},{"id":"c","text":"The first stage, because its defects can affect every later step"},{"id":"d","text":"The stage with the oldest open action, regardless of its share"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A Pareto is a prioritisation tool, so the largest contributor gets the first action and the cumulative line shows when enough has been addressed. Position in the flow and the age of an action are not what the counts say.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m14l07-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K6","K9"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m14l07","prompt":"What does the workshop require before the Pareto is published?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A signature from the plant manager and the customer"},{"id":"b","text":"The measurement check, plus the period and volume it covers"},{"id":"c","text":"A projection of the defect rate for the next twelve months"},{"id":"d","text":"A comparison against a competitor's published defect rate"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The counts need a measurement check and a stated period and volume so the reader knows what is being ranked. Approvals, forecasts and competitor comparisons add nothing to the integrity of the data.","source":"src-034"},{"questionId":"q-m15l01-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l01","prompt":"What turns a process observation into a design rule?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A measurable limit a designer can apply and a test can check"},{"id":"b","text":"A statement of intent that the design team agrees to follow"},{"id":"c","text":"A photograph of the defect attached to the drawing package"},{"id":"d","text":"A note that the issue should be considered in future projects"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The lesson converts the complaint into something like equal aperture areas within ±10 %, which can be checked on a drawing and on a stencil. Intent, a photograph and a note for later are not rules a drawing review can verify.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l01-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l01","prompt":"Why does the lesson insist on a defect rate rather than a count when talking to design?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A count is confidential, while a rate can be shared outside the plant"},{"id":"b","text":"A rate compares across products and volumes, which a count cannot do"},{"id":"c","text":"A rate is easier to explain, since design engineers do not read counts"},{"id":"d","text":"A count is accurate and a rate introduces rounding error"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The rate is what a designer can weigh against the cost of a change, and it travels between products, lines and periods. Confidentiality, simplicity and accuracy arguments do not describe why the rate is the unit the lesson uses.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l01-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m15l01","prompt":"Eighteen tombstones appear on 4,000 boards. What rate is that, and how does the lesson express the target?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.45 %, with a target of 0.45 % to hold the level"},{"id":"b","text":"4.5 %, with a target of 0.10 % or less"},{"id":"c","text":"0.45 %, with a target of 0.10 % or less"},{"id":"d","text":"0.045 %, with a target of 0.01 % or less"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"18 / 4,000 = 0.45 %, and the lesson sets a target of at most 0.10 % before sizing the verification sample. The second option proposes no improvement, and the last two move the decimal in opposite directions.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l01-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l01","prompt":"Which format does the lesson want process feedback delivered in?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A narrative of the shift in which the defects were first seen"},{"id":"b","text":"A list of the operators and machines involved in the window"},{"id":"c","text":"A complaint record with the customer correspondence attached"},{"id":"d","text":"Rate, scaled evidence, the design feature and a proposed limit"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The designer needs to know how often, what it looked like, which feature it touches and what limit would prevent it. Narrative, personnel lists and complaint records do not give any of those four.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l01-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m15l01","prompt":"A design change is proposed with no sample size attached. What does the lesson say?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The verification must be sized to the target before the change"},{"id":"b","text":"The sample can be set after the pilot, when the observed rate is known"},{"id":"c","text":"A single production week is always enough to confirm a design change"},{"id":"d","text":"Sample size is a quality department decision, not part of the change"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The case sizes a sample to demonstrate 0.10 % at 95 % confidence, which is roughly 3,000 defect-free boards, precisely so the claim can be tested. Setting the sample after the data, assuming a week is enough and handing the question away all make the verification unfalsifiable.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l01-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m15l01","prompt":"What does an aperture area balance rule look like in numbers?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Equal areas within ±1 %, which is the printing tolerance on this size"},{"id":"b","text":"Equal areas within ±10 %, so 0.011 mm² allows 0.0099 to 0.0121 mm²"},{"id":"c","text":"Equal areas with no tolerance, since the stencil laser is exact"},{"id":"d","text":"Areas matched to the pad, not to each other, within ±10 %"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson sets equal areas within ±10 % and works the band out from a 0.011 mm² nominal. A tighter tolerance than the process can hold, a zero tolerance and a rule aimed at the pad instead of the opposite aperture all miss the intent of balancing the two ends.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l02-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l02","prompt":"Why does the lesson attach a cost estimate to a technical complaint?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Cost replaces the defect rate, which is too technical for management"},{"id":"b","text":"Cost is required by the change-request template in every case"},{"id":"c","text":"Cost is the argument that gets a design change scheduled and funded"},{"id":"d","text":"Cost proves the defect exists, which a rate cannot do on its own"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The cost of poor quality turns a process problem into a business case that competes for engineering time. It does not replace the rate, it is not a universal template field, and it is not evidence that the defect exists.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l02-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l02","prompt":"What does the lesson include in the evidence pack beyond the numbers?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A video of the line running at production speed for one shift"},{"id":"b","text":"The full list of machine parameters for the affected product"},{"id":"c","text":"The names of the engineers who investigated the issue"},{"id":"d","text":"Images with a scale, so the feature and its size show"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"A scaled image lets a designer see the geometry under discussion, which the lesson pairs with the rate and the cost. Video, a full parameter dump and an investigator list add volume without adding evidence.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l02-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m15l02","prompt":"Of 12,000 boards a week at 96.5 % first-pass yield, what fraction fails, and how many reworks does that give?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"3.5 % fail: about 420 boards, of which 294 are reworked"},{"id":"b","text":"3.5 % fail, so about 420 boards, of which all are reworked"},{"id":"c","text":"96.5 % fail, so about 11,580 boards are reworked"},{"id":"d","text":"3.5 % fail, so about 42 boards, of which about 29 are reworked"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"420 boards fail, and at 70 % rework that is 294 boards needing about 88 hours of labour. Reworking all of them ignores the scrap and hold shares, inverting the yield inverts the base, and 42 boards slips a decimal.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l02-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l02","prompt":"Which elements make up the cost of poor quality in the lesson's estimate?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Solder, stencil and paste consumption on the line"},{"id":"b","text":"Rework labour, scrap, inspection and handling"},{"id":"c","text":"Machine depreciation, floor space and utility charges"},{"id":"d","text":"Engineering salaries for the whole quality department"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The estimate is built from the work a failing board causes: rework time, scrapped value, and the inspection and handling around it. Consumables, fixed costs and department salaries are production overheads rather than the cost of poor quality.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l02-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m15l02","prompt":"Why does the lesson compute cost per good board as well as total cost?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It is the figure the accounting department asks for on the invoice"},{"id":"b","text":"It removes the need to count failed boards in the estimate"},{"id":"c","text":"It lets two products or two periods be compared on the same basis"},{"id":"d","text":"It converts the labour rate into a per-minute value"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Dividing by good boards puts the cost on the output that was actually sold, which makes comparisons fair. It is not an invoicing figure, it does not remove the failure count, and it is not a unit conversion of the labour rate.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l02-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m15l02","prompt":"294 boards need 18 min of rework each at 45 EUR an hour. What does the labour cost come to?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 1,320 EUR a week"},{"id":"b","text":"About 12,700 EUR a week"},{"id":"c","text":"About 660 EUR a week"},{"id":"d","text":"About 3,970 EUR a week"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"294 x 18 = 5,292 min, or 88.2 h, and 88.2 x 45 = 3,969 EUR. The 1,320 answer uses the failure count without the rework share, 12,700 forgets to convert minutes to hours in the right direction, and 660 drops a factor of six.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l03-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l03","prompt":"Which sections does the lesson require in a change request?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Problem, impact, proposal, alternatives, risk, verification"},{"id":"b","text":"Problem and proposal only, since the rest delays the decision"},{"id":"c","text":"Cost, schedule, owner and approval signature"},{"id":"d","text":"Defect description, photograph, customer name and ship date"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The request has to let a reviewer weigh a change against doing nothing, which is why alternatives and risk sit beside the problem and the verification. A two-part request, a purely administrative set and a customer-centred set all leave the decision uninformed.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l03-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l03","prompt":"How does the lesson describe n = 1 / target rate for verification?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A confidence statement at the 95 % level"},{"id":"b","text":"A screening sample, not a confidence statement"},{"id":"c","text":"The statistically exact sample for any target rate"},{"id":"d","text":"A sample only valid for rates above one percent"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The lesson is explicit that 200 boards for a 0.5 % target is a screen, and that the confidence-based size has to come from the quality plan. Calling it a 95 % statement, exact, or rate-limited overstates what the rule gives.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l03-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m15l03","prompt":"An aperture drops from 0.50 to 0.45 mm square on a 0.12 mm stencil. What paste volume change does that give?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A 10 % reduction, from 0.030 to 0.027 mm³"},{"id":"b","text":"A 19 % reduction, from 0.060 to 0.0486 mm³"},{"id":"c","text":"A 19 % reduction, from 0.030 to 0.0243 mm³"},{"id":"d","text":"A 20 % reduction, from 0.025 to 0.020 mm³"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Areas are 0.25 and 0.2025 mm², so volumes are 0.030 and 0.0243 mm³, a drop of 19 % because the edge reduction is squared. The 10 % figure tracks only the linear change, the third option doubles the volume, and the last rounds away the squared effect.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l03-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l03","prompt":"Why does the lesson ask for the alternatives in a change request?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"To give the reviewer a menu from which the cheapest can be picked"},{"id":"b","text":"To document the options that were rejected as technically impossible"},{"id":"c","text":"To satisfy the template, which has a fixed number of sections"},{"id":"d","text":"To show it was chosen over doing nothing and over other options"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"A proposal is only a decision when the reader can see what else was possible, including inaction. A menu, a rejection list and a template requirement do not capture that comparison.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l03-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m15l03","prompt":"What does the risk section have to state?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"What could go wrong, and how it would be detected"},{"id":"b","text":"The probability that the change will be approved by management"},{"id":"c","text":"The cost of the change, expressed as a range of estimates"},{"id":"d","text":"The name of the person who will carry the risk if it fails"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The risk statement pairs a failure mode with the control that would catch it, which is how a reviewer judges whether to proceed. Approval odds, cost ranges and personal liability do not describe the technical risk.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l03-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m15l03","prompt":"A target is 0.5 % per board. What does the screening rule give, and what does it not give?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"200 boards, which demonstrates 0.5 % at 95 % confidence"},{"id":"b","text":"200 boards as a screen, not a statement at 95 % confidence"},{"id":"c","text":"400 boards, which is the confidence-based figure for 0.5 %"},{"id":"d","text":"2,000 boards, since the target is expressed per board"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"1 / 0.005 = 200, and the lesson labels that as a screen that still has to be checked against the quality plan. Calling it a 95 % demonstration, doubling it for confidence or scaling by ten all add a claim the rule does not make.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l04-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l04","prompt":"What makes a DFM request usable by a designer?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A general recommendation to leave more room around parts"},{"id":"b","text":"A list of every defect the line has seen on similar products"},{"id":"c","text":"A specific geometry, a measurable limit and the reason"},{"id":"d","text":"A request to follow the supplier reference design exactly"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The lesson turns a limitation into a testable geometry, such as a thermal-pad aperture at 60 % of pad area split into four windows. Generalities, a defect dump and a reference design do not tell the designer what dimension to draw.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l04-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l04","prompt":"Why does the lesson segment a large thermal-pad aperture?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The segments make the stencil easier to laser cut at fine resolution"},{"id":"b","text":"The segments let the pad carry more current than a single opening"},{"id":"c","text":"The segments allow different paste heights across the same pad"},{"id":"d","text":"The segments limit paste volume and let gas escape"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"A windowed aperture controls how much paste lands and lets volatiles escape, which is the point of the 60 % target split into four sub-apertures. Cutting ease, current capacity and differential paste height are not the reasons the lesson gives.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l04-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m15l04","prompt":"A 4 by 4 mm thermal pad gets four 1.55 mm square windows. What area fraction is that?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 60 %, which is the lesson's target"},{"id":"b","text":"About 25 %, since each window is a quarter of the pad"},{"id":"c","text":"About 96 %, because the windows nearly fill the pad"},{"id":"d","text":"About 40 %, which leaves more margin than the target"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"Four windows of 2.40 mm² give 9.61 mm² against a 16 mm² pad, so 60.1 %, which matches the target and leaves an outer margin. Counting windows rather than areas, ignoring the gaps and under-shooting the target are the three ways to misplace the fraction.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l04-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l04","prompt":"Which design features does the lesson ask test to be designed for?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Component placement, silkscreen fonts and board colour"},{"id":"b","text":"Test points, fiducials, panel breakaway and hole-to-lead spec"},{"id":"c","text":"Paste type, stencil thickness and squeegee hardness"},{"id":"d","text":"Reflow profile, conveyor speed and oven zone count"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Those are the fabrication features that decide whether the assembly can be tested and processed at all, which is why the lesson asks for them at design review. Placement and silkscreen are drawing questions, and the remaining two are process settings chosen later.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l04-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m15l04","prompt":"Paste bleeds between the peripheral pads of a 0.4 mm pitch QFN. What does the lesson convert that into?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A request to use a thicker stencil for the whole assembly"},{"id":"b","text":"A demand for a finer stencil laser and a new supplier"},{"id":"c","text":"A thermal-pad aperture rule that reduces volume and gives gas a path"},{"id":"d","text":"A note that 0.4 mm pitch is beyond the process and should be avoided"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The case reduces the thermal-pad deposit to 60 % in four windows, which lowers the total paste and the pressure driving bleed. A thicker stencil, a new laser and declining the pitch all treat the symptom with a bigger change than the evidence supports.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l04-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m15l04","prompt":"What acceptance window does the lesson attach to the windowed aperture?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A visual inspection of the stencil after every print"},{"id":"b","text":"A reflow peak and TAL band taken from the paste datasheet"},{"id":"c","text":"A pull strength measured on the thermal pad after assembly"},{"id":"d","text":"An SPI paste height band and a volume target for the pad"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"The rule is only testable if the print itself is measured, so the lesson gives a paste height band and a volume target for SPI. Stencil inspection, a reflow band and a pull test check other things than the deposit the rule controls.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l05-03","type":"single","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l05","prompt":"How does the lesson justify a design change to a sceptical design owner?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"With the monthly cost of poor quality against the one-time change cost"},{"id":"b","text":"With the number of complaints received from customers that quarter"},{"id":"c","text":"With a comparison against the best competitor product on the market"},{"id":"d","text":"With the engineering preference of the process team for the new pattern"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"The case compares a monthly COPQ against an 8,000 EUR change cost and finds the payback quick. Complaints, competitor comparisons and preference are weaker arguments than the money the current design consumes every month.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l05-04","type":"single","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l05","prompt":"What closes a design-feedback loop?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The approval signature on the change request"},{"id":"b","text":"Post-change data from the line, against the same metric"},{"id":"c","text":"The release of the new drawing revision to the shop floor"},{"id":"d","text":"A meeting where the design and process teams agree it is done"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"The loop closes when the original metric moves, which is why the lesson asks for post-change production data. A signature, a release and an agreement record the decision but not its effect.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l05-05","type":"calc","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m15l05","prompt":"Rework labour is 1,361 EUR and scrap 6,480 EUR a month, with 60 EUR of downtime. Against an 8,000 EUR change cost, what is the argument?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 7,900 EUR a year, so the change pays back in about a year"},{"id":"b","text":"About 1,421 EUR a month, so the change is hard to justify"},{"id":"c","text":"About 7,900 EUR a month, so payback takes about a month"},{"id":"d","text":"About 66,000 EUR a month, so the change pays back in days"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"The three items sum to about 7,900 EUR per month, which against 8,000 EUR is a payback of roughly one month. Treating the figure as annual, dropping scrap and slipping a decimal all change the business case by an order of magnitude.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l05-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m15l05","prompt":"What does version control have to record for a design change?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The name of the engineer who first suggested the change"},{"id":"b","text":"The meeting minutes in which the change was discussed"},{"id":"c","text":"The list of suppliers who quoted for the new stencil"},{"id":"d","text":"The revision, the effective date and the products it covers"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"A revision is only usable if the shop floor can tell which assemblies it governs from which date. Authorship, minutes and quotations are history rather than the control the lesson asks for.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l05-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4","K9"],"bloom":"analyse","lessonId":"m15l05","prompt":"A pilot predicts a drop from 2.4 % to 0.6 % boards with rework. What does the lesson require before closing the change?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Production data after the change, not the pilot prediction"},{"id":"b","text":"A second pilot on a different product line for confirmation"},{"id":"c","text":"Customer approval of the new land pattern before production"},{"id":"d","text":"A review of the change cost against the original estimate"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A pilot shows what is possible; the loop closes on what the line actually produced afterwards. A second pilot, an approval and a cost review do not substitute for the post-change metric the case asks for.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m15l05-e3","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K4"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m15l05","prompt":"The proposal changes the land pattern and the stencil aperture at the same time. What does that cost the verification?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Nothing, because both changes address the same defect"},{"id":"b","text":"Attribution: a result cannot be assigned to either change"},{"id":"c","text":"Accuracy, because the paste volume becomes harder to measure"},{"id":"d","text":"Speed, because two drawings have to be revised instead of one"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"Two changes at once still allow a yes or no answer on the combined effect but not on which one did the work, which matters for the next product. Both changes addressing the same defect is exactly why the attribution is lost, and drawing effort is a minor cost beside it.","source":"src-038"},{"questionId":"q-m09l07-03","type":"single","competencies":["K5","K6"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l07","prompt":"What does the lesson want recorded for a first article on a wave line?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Flux type and density, preheat, wave and fog height, speed and pot temperature"},{"id":"b","text":"The operator name, the shift number and the panel serial only"},{"id":"c","text":"The pot analysis for copper and the date of the last filter change"},{"id":"d","text":"The reflow profile, since the wave shares the same thermal window"}],"correct":"a","explanation":"A first article is evidence that the window was actually set, so the lesson records the flux type and deposited density, the preheat, both heights, the speed and the pot temperature, plus any deviation. Names and serials identify the run, pot chemistry is a maintenance figure, and reflow belongs to a different process.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l07-04","type":"calc","competencies":["K5","K6"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l07","prompt":"An 80 mm wave runs at 25 mm/s. What contact time does that give, against a 2-4 s practical range?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"0.32 s, well below the range"},{"id":"b","text":"3.2 s, inside the range"},{"id":"c","text":"32 s, far above the range"},{"id":"d","text":"3.2 s, just above the range"}],"correct":"b","explanation":"80 / 25 = 3.2 s, comfortably inside 2-4 s. The 0.32 s answer slips a decimal, 32 s divides the wrong way for the units, and the last option gets the number right but misreads the band.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l07-e1","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5","K9"],"bloom":"understand","lessonId":"m09l07","prompt":"Why does the lesson treat a contaminated solder bath as a process issue rather than a maintenance one?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Contamination raises the pot temperature, which the heater cannot hold"},{"id":"b","text":"It changes the alloy liquidus, so the profile reference moves with it"},{"id":"c","text":"Dissolved metals and residues change wetting and the defect rate at the wave"},{"id":"d","text":"It thickens the flux, so the spray head has to be recalibrated"}],"correct":"c","explanation":"Copper, nickel, iron and flux residues enter the bath from boards, fixtures and nozzles and show up as a change in wetting and defects. The pot heater, the alloy liquidus and the flux spray are separate systems with their own controls.","source":"src-019"},{"questionId":"q-m09l07-e2","type":"single","pool":"exam","competencies":["K5"],"bloom":"apply","lessonId":"m09l07","prompt":"A 150 cm² board is fluxed at 0.20 mg/cm² of solids from a 2 % solids flux. What mass of solution is that?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"About 30 mg"},{"id":"b","text":"About 150 mg"},{"id":"c","text":"About 3,000 mg"},{"id":"d","text":"About 1,500 mg"}],"correct":"d","explanation":"0.20 x 150 = 30 mg of solids, and at 2 % solids that is 1,500 mg of solution — the lesson's own figure for this board. The 30 mg answer stops at the solids, 150 mg applies 1 % instead of 2 %, and 3,000 mg doubles the result.","source":"src-019"}],"sources":[{"id":"src-001","type":"standard","title":"IPC-A-610 Acceptability of Electronic Assemblies","edition":"Rev H","section":"§10 through-hole solder fill — vertical barrel fill minimums by class (75 % for Class 2 and Class 3)","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Primary IPC-A-610D fragments read: \"Class 2, fill to 75% but with exception for inner layer connections\" and \"All other solder joints remain according to IPC-A-610 Class 2\". Settled the round-10 contradiction — 75 % is the minimum for both classes; the 50 % figure belongs to IPC-6012 Rev F (bare board).","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currency":"Superseded. IPC-A-610 Rev J was released in April 2024 and is the current revision; Rev J also removed the \"Target\" condition category (Acceptable / Process Indicator / Defect). This course was written against Rev H and the criteria quoted here were not re-read against Rev J.","currentEdition":"Rev J (March 2024)","editionStatus":"superseded","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Written against Rev H. Rev J was published in March 2024 and is listed by IPC as current; the criteria quoted here were not re-read against Rev J.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-002","type":"standard","title":"J-STD-001 Requirements for Soldered Electrical and Electronic Assemblies","edition":"Rev G","section":"§8 cleanliness — objective evidence in place of the single ROSE limit","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Amendment 1 to Rev G (2018) and Rev H (September 2020) confirmed from IPC's own site and three independent reproductions: the 1.56 µg/cm² ROSE pass/fail was removed and replaced by objective evidence — SIR per IPC-TM-650 2.6.3.7, ion chromatography per 2.3.28, temperature-humidity-bias, or documented field history.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currency":"Superseded. J-STD-001 Rev J was released in 2024 and is the current revision. This course was written against Rev G/H and was not re-read against Rev J.","currentEdition":"Rev J (April 2024)","editionStatus":"superseded","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Written against Rev G/H. IPC lists J-STD-001 Rev J, April 2024, as current. Some vendor pages assert a \"Rev J 2024\" and others a later \"Rev H 2026\" — the publisher table shows J, April 2024.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-003","type":"standard","title":"J-STD-033 Handling, Packing, Shipping and Use of Moisture, Heat and ESD Sensitive Surface Mount Devices","edition":"Rev D","section":"Table 4-1 (user bake) and Table 4-2 (supplier dry-pack); Table 5-1 floor life; §4.2.7.1 cumulative bake","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Primary J-STD-033B.1 and Rev D text read. The full Table 4-1 matrix was recovered — thickness × MSL level × whether floor life was exceeded by more or less than 72 h. Table 4-2 is the supplier dry-pack bake at a fixed 24 h maximum exposure. Cumulative bake above 90 °C and up to 125 °C limited to 96 h; at 90 °C or below there is no limit.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev D (May 2018) — no longer maintained","editionStatus":"no-longer-maintained","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"IPC still lists Rev D as the newest J-STD-033 and marks the document \"no longer maintained\"; an E revision is a project, not a published standard. The course therefore cites the current edition.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-004","type":"standard","title":"IPC-7525C Stencil Design Guidelines (stencil design guidance: area ratio, aspect ratio, aperture sizing)","edition":"Rev C","section":"§1.1.2 area ratio; §1.1.3 aspect ratio","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Primary IPC-7525B §1.1.2 and §1.1.3 read verbatim. Revision history confirmed independently: 7525 May 2000, A February 2007, B October 2011, C November 2021 (current). IPC-7525D does not exist. Caveat that still stands: the 0.66 and 1.5 FIGURES are carried only by convergent secondary sources and have not been confirmed against the standard body text.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev C (November 2021; English release February 2022)","editionStatus":"current","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Rev C is the newest IPC-7525 in the IPC table.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-005","type":"standard","title":"J-STD-020 Classification of Moisture Sensitivity for Non-Hermetic Sealing Solid State Surface Mount Devices","edition":"Rev D (read against Rev E text)","section":"Table 4-1 and Table 4-2 classification temperatures; Table 5-2 classification reflow profile","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Primary J-STD-020E text read. Table 4-2 (Pb-free) is thickness × volume: below 1.6 mm every volume 260 °C; 1.6-2.5 mm 260/250/245; above 2.5 mm 250/245/245. Table 4-1 (SnPb) 235 °C for thickness below 2.5 mm and volume below 350 mm³, 220 °C otherwise. Profile parameters: tL 60-150 s, ts 60-120 s, ramp-up 3 °C/s max, ramp-down 6 °C/s max, tp 20 s SnPb / 30 s Pb-free.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currency":"Edition mismatch. The label says Rev D, the text actually read was Rev E. Rev E is the later revision; quote the Rev E values.","currentEdition":"Rev F (November 2022)","editionStatus":"edition-mismatch","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"The source label says Rev D while the text read was Rev E; IPC lists Rev F, November 2022, as current. Two revision gaps.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-006","type":"standard","title":"ISO 2859-1 Sampling procedures for inspection by attributes","edition":"ISO 2859-1:1999 (as amended 2011)","section":"Table I sample size code letters; Table II-A single sampling plans","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. A hosted copy of ISO 2859-1 itself was read, and the tables confirmed against three independent reproductions. Code letters A 2 through R 2000 (S 3150 omitted by the course); level II gives 501-1200 → J(80), 1201-3200 → K(125), 3201-10000 → L(200); at AQL 1.0 % K → Ac 3 / Re 4 and L → Ac 5 / Re 6.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"ISO 2859-1:1999 with 2011 amendments","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Not an IPC document; the edition is checked against ISO, not the IPC table.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-007","type":"standard","title":"IPC-7095 Design and Assembly Process Implementation for BGAs","edition":"Rev E (September 2024)","section":"BGA design and assembly process implementation — scope only","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from published references; the clause text was not read. Any clause-level claim resting on this source is unverified.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev E (September 2024)","editionStatus":"current","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Rev E is the newest IPC-7095 in the IPC table.","scopeOnly":true},{"id":"src-008","type":"standard","title":"IPC-7530 Guidelines for Temperature Profiling for Mass Soldering Processes","edition":"Rev B (January 2025)","section":"Reflow and wave profile guidance — ramp, soak, time above liquidus, cooling","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ via the AIM Solder profile supplement, which reproduces the IPC/JEDEC classification profile table verbatim: time above liquidus 30-90 s, soak zone 150-200 °C, ramp up rate below 3 °C/sec with 1-3 °C/sec as the recommended starting point, cooling below 4 °C/sec. The IPC-7530A industry draft note that component suppliers often recommend a more conservative 2-4 °C/s was also read.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev B (January 2025)","editionStatus":"current","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Rev B is the newest IPC-7530 in the IPC table.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-009","type":"standard","title":"IPC-7711/7721 Rework, Modification and Repair of Electronic Assemblies","edition":"IPC-7711C/7721C (January 2017)","section":"Rework and repair procedures; acceptable rework and repair methods","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ from two independent renderings of the primary text. IPC-7711 is Rework, IPC-7721 is Repair and Modification, published together as IPC-7711/7721. IPC-7721 judges laminate repair on the restored condition — insulation and clearance maintained, no epoxy in the hole or on internal pad walls, no reduction in hole diameter, no exposed adjacent circuitry — and sets no damage depth limit. The only 0.25 mm in IPC-7721 is the width of a milled channel for a foil jumper.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev D (January 2024)","editionStatus":"superseded","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Written against Rev C (January 2017). IPC lists IPC-7711/7721 Rev D, January 2024, as current.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-010","type":"standard","title":"IPC-CC-830 Qualification and Performance of Electrical Insulating Compound for Conformal Coating","edition":"Rev C","section":"§1.3.1 coating type designations; qualification and performance requirements","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ from two independent renderings. Type AR acrylic, Type ER EPOXY, Type SR silicone, Type UR POLYURETHANE, Type XY PARAXYLYLENE, Type UT ultra-thin, Type SC styrene block co-polymer. There is no Type DR. The course had ER as polyurethane and invented DR for parylene.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev C (January 2019)","editionStatus":"current","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Rev C is the newest IPC-CC-830 in the IPC table.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-011","type":"standard","title":"IPC-7351 Generic Requirements for Surface Mount Design and Land Pattern Standard","edition":"Rev B (June 2010) — no longer maintained","section":"Surface mount design and land pattern requirements — scope only","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from published references; the clause text was not read. Any clause-level claim resting on this source is unverified.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev B (June 2010) — no longer maintained","editionStatus":"no-longer-maintained","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"IPC lists IPC-7351 Rev B as the newest revision and marks the document \"no longer maintained\". IPC-7352, a land-pattern guideline issued in 2023, is a separate document and is not a replacement edition.","scopeOnly":true},{"id":"src-012","type":"standard","title":"ANSI/ESD S20.20 Development of an Electrostatic Discharge Control Program","edition":"ANSI/ESD S20.20-2021","section":"ESD control programme — grounded personnel, protective packaging, verified work surfaces, controlled handling","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ from the ESD Association's own IEC-equivalency chart and endorsed-standards page. ANSI/ESD S20.20-2021 is the programme standard, technically equivalent to IEC 61340-5-1.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"ANSI/ESD S20.20-2021","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","editionNote":"Outside the IPC revision table; the edition above is the one cited and was not re-checked in Phase 2.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-013","type":"standard","title":"IPC-TM-650 method 2.3.25 (ROSE) and 2.3.28 (ion chromatography)","edition":"rolling document; methods 2.3.25 and 2.3.28","section":"Method 2.3.25 (ROSE, soluble ionic contamination) and method 2.3.28 (ion chromatography)","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Method numbers and titles confirmed. 2.3.25 measures soluble ionic contamination as sodium chloride equivalent; 2.3.28 is ion chromatography, used to confirm the specific ions. Unit conversion cross-checked: 1.56 µg/cm² = 10.06 µg/in².","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"rolling document","editionStatus":"rolling","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"IPC-TM-650 is revised per test method, so it has no single edition.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-014","type":"vendor-doc","title":"AIM Solder — Profile Supplement for Wave Soldering Process (flux deposition 500-1500 µg/in²)","edition":"Rev 4","section":"Reflow and wave profile supplement — flux deposition, ramp, soak, time above liquidus, cooling","licenseNote":"public vendor document; confirm current revision before publication","verifiedBy":"READ. The AIM Solder supplement reproduces the IPC/JEDEC profile table and states flux deposition of 500-1500 µg/in² of flux solids, which converts to 0.775-2.325 g/m² — the course's 0.78-2.33 g/m² is exact. Also the source for the 30-90 s time-above-liquidus window.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","url":"https://www.aimsolder.com/wp-content/uploads/wave_solder_profile_supplement.pdf","currentEdition":"vendor document Rev 4","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Consumable supplier document, not an IPC standard; the revision is the supplier's.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-015","type":"standard","title":"J-STD-005 Requirements for Soldering Pastes (powder size distribution, Type 3 = 25-45 µm, 80% minimum in range)","edition":"Rev A","section":"Table 3-2 solder powder size distribution","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. The AIM Solder test report reproduces the standard's own Table 3-2 header and Type 3 row verbatim, confirmed independently by two further sources: none larger than 50 µm, less than 1 % larger than 45 µm, 80 % minimum between 25-45 µm, 10 % maximum smaller than 20 µm. Some older renderings of the table show a different layout, which is where the course's wrong tails came from.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev B (April 2024)","editionStatus":"superseded","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Written against J-STD-005 Rev A. IPC lists Rev B, April 2024, as current.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-016","type":"standard","title":"IPC-A-610H section 10 — through-hole solder fill (75% minimum vertical barrel fill, Class 2 and Class 3)","edition":"Rev H §10","section":"§10 through-hole solder fill — 75 % minimum vertical barrel fill","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Same primary IPC-A-610D fragments as src-001. Note for the record: IPC-A-610J removed the \"Target\" condition entirely, so in the current revision 100 % fill cannot be expressed as a target — it can only be a contractual requirement.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currency":"Superseded. The through-hole fill criteria quoted here were read against Rev H (and, for some figures, against Rev D fragments); IPC-A-610 Rev J is the current revision.","currentEdition":"Rev J (March 2024)","editionStatus":"superseded","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"The through-hole fill criteria quoted here were read against Rev H. Rev J is current and was not re-read.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-017","type":"standard","title":"J-STD-020 classification temperatures (260/250/245 °C by package size, tp 30-40 s, ramp-down ≤ 6 °C/s)","edition":"Rev D (read against Rev E text)","section":"Classification temperatures by package thickness and volume; tp, tL, ts, ramp limits","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Primary J-STD-020E text read, cross-checked against the same table reproduced in the Texas Instruments, Microchip and Abracon handling notes. The course's original \"260 small / 250 large / 245 very large\" was the 1.6-2.5 mm row only.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currency":"Edition mismatch. The label says Rev D, the text actually read was Rev E. Rev E is the later revision; quote the Rev E values.","currentEdition":"Rev F (November 2022)","editionStatus":"edition-mismatch","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"The source label says Rev D while the text read was Rev E; IPC lists Rev F, November 2022, as current.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-018","type":"standard","title":"IPC-6012 Qualification and Performance Specification for Rigid Printed Boards (qualification and performance requirements for bare boards)","edition":"Rev F (September 2023); the course's cleanliness figure is cited from Rev E §3.9.1","section":"§3.9.1 ionic contamination for bare boards; bow and twist limits","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"PARTLY READ. The 1.56 µg/cm² NaCl-equivalent limit is cited directly as IPC-6012E §3.9.1 and confirmed. Bow and twist confirmed through a quotation of IPC-6012 itself — 0.75 % for boards using surface mount components, 1.5 % for all others, measured per IPC-TM-650 2.4.22, with the same split in IPC-A-600 §2.11. The rest of the document was not read.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currency":"Partly superseded. IPC-6012 Rev F (October 2023) is the current revision; the cleanliness figure used in the course is cited from Rev E and was not re-verified in Rev F.","currentEdition":"Rev F (September 2023) plus addenda 6012FA (December 2025), 6012FS (February 2024), 6012EM (August 2020)","editionStatus":"current","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Rev F is current; the cleanliness figure in the course comes from Rev E §3.9.1 and is noted as such in the source entry.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-019","type":"standard","title":"J-STD-004 Requirements for Soldering Fluxes (paste CoA specification reference)","edition":"Rev B","section":"Flux classification by residue type and activity; residue requirements","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Classification by residue type and activity confirmed; low activity for clean fresh finishes, higher activity for oxidised or high-thermal-mass joints. Clause numbers not read — the classification scheme itself is what the course relies on.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev D (December 2023)","editionStatus":"superseded","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Written against J-STD-004 Rev B. IPC lists Rev D, December 2023, as current (Rev C was January 2022).","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-020","type":"standard","title":"J-STD-006 Soft Solder Alloys and Fluxed or Non-Fluxed Solid Solders for Electronic Soldering Applications (powder solder, other than paste)","edition":"Rev C","section":"Soft solder alloys — scope only","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from published references; the clause text was not read. Any clause-level claim resting on this source is unverified.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev C with Amendment 1 (November 2017)","editionStatus":"current","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Rev C with Amendment 1 is the newest J-STD-006 in the IPC table.","scopeOnly":true},{"id":"src-021","type":"standard","title":"J-STD-002 Solderability Tests for Component Leads, Terminations, Lugs, Terminals and Wires","edition":"Rev C","section":"Solderability tests for component leads and terminations — scope only","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from published references; the clause text was not read. Any clause-level claim resting on this source is unverified.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev E (November 2017)","editionStatus":"superseded","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Written against J-STD-002 Rev C. IPC lists Rev E, November 2017, as current.","scopeOnly":true},{"id":"src-022","type":"standard","title":"IPC-A-600 Acceptability of Printed Boards (visual and dimensional criteria)","edition":"Rev M (May 2025); §2.10.3 and §3.5.3.1 read","section":"§2.10.3 annular ring; §3.5.3.1 conductor width reduction","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. IPC-A-600 §2.10.3: \"Acceptable — Class 3: holes are not centered in the lands, but the annular ring measures 0.050 mm [0.0020 in] or more\", with up to 20 % local reduction for pits, dents, nicks, pinholes or splay. §3.5.3.1: allowable reduction of the minimum conductor width due to misregistration or isolated defects shall not exceed 20 % of the minimum conductor width (10 % at Class 3). These are bare-board criteria — the course had been attaching them to IPC-A-610 and IPC-7711/7721.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev M (May 2025)","editionStatus":"current","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Rev M is the newest IPC-A-600 in the IPC table.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-023","type":"standard","title":"IPC-4101 Specification for Base Materials for Rigid and Multilayer Printed Boards","edition":"Rev E with Amendment 1 (April 2022)","section":"Base materials for rigid and multilayer printed boards — scope only","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from published references; the clause text was not read. Any clause-level claim resting on this source is unverified.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev E with Amendment 1 (April 2022)","editionStatus":"current","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Rev E with Amendment 1 is the newest IPC-4101 in the IPC table.","scopeOnly":true},{"id":"src-024","type":"standard","title":"IPC-2221 Generic Standard on Printed Board Design","edition":"Rev C (December 2023)","section":"Generic standard on printed board design — scope only","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from published references; the clause text was not read. Any clause-level claim resting on this source is unverified.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev C (December 2023)","editionStatus":"current","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Rev C is the newest IPC-2221 in the IPC table.","scopeOnly":true},{"id":"src-025","type":"standard","title":"IPC-2222 Sectional Design Standard for Rigid Organic Printed Boards","edition":"Rev B (October 2020)","section":"Sectional design standard for rigid organic printed boards — scope only","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from published references; the clause text was not read. Any clause-level claim resting on this source is unverified.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev B (October 2020)","editionStatus":"current","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Rev B is the newest IPC-2222 in the IPC table.","scopeOnly":true},{"id":"src-026","type":"standard","title":"IPC-7526 Stencil and Misprinted Board Cleaning Handbook","edition":"Rev A (March 2022)","section":"Stencil and misprinted board cleaning — scope only","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from published references; the clause text was not read. Any clause-level claim resting on this source is unverified.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"Rev A (March 2022)","editionStatus":"current","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"Rev A is the newest IPC-7526 in the IPC table.","scopeOnly":true},{"id":"src-027","type":"standard","title":"ANSI/ESD S1.1 Wrist Straps","edition":"ANSI/ESD S1.1-2021","section":"Wrist straps — scope only","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from the ESD Association's published standards list; the method text was not read. Any clause-level claim resting on this source is unverified.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"ANSI/ESD S1.1-2021","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","editionNote":"Outside the IPC revision table; the edition above is the one cited and was not re-checked in Phase 2.","scopeOnly":true},{"id":"src-028","type":"standard","title":"ANSI/ESD STM4.1 Worksurfaces — Resistance Measurements (supersedes ANSI/ESD S4.1)","edition":"ANSI/ESD STM4.1-2017 (supersedes S4.1)","section":"Worksurfaces — resistance measurements — scope only","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from the ESD Association's published standards list; the method text was not read. Any clause-level claim resting on this source is unverified.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"ANSI/ESD STM4.1-2017 (supersedes S4.1)","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","editionNote":"Outside the IPC revision table; the edition above is the one cited and was not re-checked in Phase 2.","scopeOnly":true},{"id":"src-029","type":"standard","title":"ANSI/ESD S6.1 Grounding (equipotential bonding of racks, carts, shelving and tables)","edition":"ANSI/ESD S6.1-2019","section":"Grounding — equipotential bonding of racks, carts, shelving, tables and floor systems","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from the ESD Association's published standards list; the method text was not read. The course's claim is a scope statement, not a clause citation.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"ANSI/ESD S6.1-2019","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","editionNote":"Outside the IPC revision table; the edition above is the one cited and was not re-checked in Phase 2.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-030","type":"standard","title":"ANSI/ESD S541 Packaging Materials for ESD Sensitive Items","edition":"ANSI/ESD S541-2019","section":"Packaging materials for ESD sensitive items — shielding bags, conductive containers, closure methods","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from the ESD Association's published standards list; the method text was not read. The course's claim is a scope statement, not a clause citation.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"ANSI/ESD S541-2019","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","editionNote":"Outside the IPC revision table; the edition above is the one cited and was not re-checked in Phase 2.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-031","type":"standard","title":"ANSI/ESD STM3.1 Ionization (offset voltage and discharge time)","edition":"ANSI/ESD STM3.1-2024","section":"Ionization — offset voltage (balance) and discharge time","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Confirmed from the ESD Association's own IEC-equivalency chart: \"ANSI/ESD STM3.1 | IEC 61340-4-7 | Ionization\". This also settled the earlier draft's error — no STM97.7 or 97.8 covers ionization.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"ANSI/ESD STM3.1-2024","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","editionNote":"Outside the IPC revision table; the edition above is the one cited and was not re-checked in Phase 2.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-032","type":"standard","title":"ANSI/ESD STM11.11 Surface Resistance Measurements of Static Dissipative Planar Materials","edition":"ANSI/ESD STM11.11-2022","section":"Surface resistance measurements of static dissipative planar materials — scope only","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from the ESD Association's published standards list; the method text was not read. Any clause-level claim resting on this source is unverified.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"ANSI/ESD STM11.11-2022","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","editionNote":"Outside the IPC revision table; the edition above is the one cited and was not re-checked in Phase 2.","scopeOnly":true},{"id":"src-033","type":"standard","title":"ANSI/ESD STM97.1 Floor Materials and Footwear — Resistance Measurement in Combination with a Person","edition":"ANSI/ESD STM97.1-2015","section":"Floor materials and footwear — resistance measurement","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Confirmed from the ESD Association's own IEC-equivalency chart: \"ANSI/ESD STM97.1 & ANSI/ESD STM97.2 | IEC 61340-4-5 | Footwear and Flooring Systems\". Both STM97.1-2015 and STM97.2-2016 are technically equivalent to IEC 61340-4-5.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"ANSI/ESD STM97.1-2015","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","editionNote":"Outside the IPC revision table; the edition above is the one cited and was not re-checked in Phase 2.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-034","type":"standard","title":"ANSI/ESD STM97.2 Footwear/Flooring System — Voltage Measurement in Combination with a Person","edition":"ANSI/ESD STM97.2-2016","section":"Footwear/flooring system — voltage measurement in combination with a person","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Confirmed from the ESD Association's own IEC-equivalency chart, paired with STM97.1 as the IEC 61340-4-5 equivalent.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"ANSI/ESD STM97.2-2016","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","editionNote":"Outside the IPC revision table; the edition above is the one cited and was not re-checked in Phase 2.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-035","type":"standard","title":"IEC 61340-5-1 Protection of Electronic Devices from Electrostatic Phenomena — General Requirements","edition":"IEC 61340-5-1:2024 (edition 3.0)","section":"Protection of electronic devices from electrostatic phenomena — requirements","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Confirmed from the ESD Association's equivalency material that ANSI/ESD S20.20 is technically equivalent to IEC 61340-5-1. The equivalence is what the course claims, not the clause text.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"IEC 61340-5-1:2024 (edition 3.0)","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","editionNote":"Outside the IPC revision table; the edition above is the one cited and was not re-checked in Phase 2.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-036","type":"standard","title":"IEC 61340-4-7 Standard Test Methods for Specific Applications — Ionization","edition":"IEC 61340-4-7 (ESDA counterpart of ANSI/ESD STM3.1-2024)","section":"Standard test methods for specific applications — ionization tests","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Confirmed from the ESD Association's own IEC-equivalency chart as the IEC counterpart of ANSI/ESD STM3.1.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"IEC 61340-4-7 (ESDA counterpart of ANSI/ESD STM3.1-2024)","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","editionNote":"Outside the IPC revision table; the edition above is the one cited and was not re-checked in Phase 2.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-037","type":"standard","title":"ISO 9001 Quality Management Systems — Requirements (control of externally provided product)","edition":"ISO 9001:2015","section":"Clause 8.4 control of externally provided processes, products and services; nonconforming output","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Clause 8.4 (control of externally provided processes, products and services) confirmed. The course uses it to justify recording the evidence behind an accept, quarantine or reject decision at goods-in.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"ISO 9001:2015","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","editionNote":"Outside the IPC revision table; the edition above is the one cited and was not re-checked in Phase 2.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-038","type":"standard","title":"ANSI/AS9100 Quality Management Systems — Requirements for Aviation, Space and Defense Organizations","edition":"ANSI/AS9100 Rev D","section":"Quality management systems requirements for aviation, space and defence — nonconforming output","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"READ. Scope confirmed: control and documentation of nonconforming output. The course cites it alongside ISO 9001 as an example QMS under which a disposition must be documented.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"ANSI/AS9100 Rev D","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","editionNote":"Outside the IPC revision table; the edition above is the one cited and was not re-checked in Phase 2.","scopeOnly":false},{"id":"src-039","type":"standard","title":"UL 94 Tests for Flammability of Plastic Materials for Parts in Devices and Appliances","edition":"rolling document","section":"Tests for flammability of plastic materials for parts in devices and appliances","licenseNote":"paraphrase only","verifiedBy":"SCOPE. Designation and scope confirmed from published references; the clause text was not read. The course references it only as the flammability basis a material declaration may cite.","verifiedOn":"2026-11-01","currentEdition":"rolling document","editionStatus":"not-ipc","currentCheckedOn":"2026-10-31","currentSource":"https://www.electronics.org/ipc-document-revision-table","editionNote":"UL standard; edition checked against UL, not the IPC table.","scopeOnly":false}],"caseDoc":{"schemaVersion":1,"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py","generatedOn":"2026-10-31","thresholds":{"areaRatioMin":[0.66,"src-004","m03l02"],"aspectRatioMin":[1.5,"src-004","m03l05"],"tapeBakeMaxC":[40,"src-003","m02l04"],"dryCabinetMaxRhPct":[5,"src-003","m02l04"],"jstd020LargePackageC":[250,"src-005","m06l02"],"talWindowS":[[60,90],"src-008","m06l07"],"typicalPeakBandC":[[235,245],null,"m06l01"],"pastePeakWindowC":[[235,245],null,"m06l07"],"talWindowIpcs":[[30,90],"src-008","m06l05"],"peakClassificationLargeC":[250,"src-005","m06l05"],"holeFillMinPct":[75,"src-016","m08l05"],"holeFillExceptionPct":[50,"src-016","m08l05"],"waveContactWindowS":[[2,4],null,"m09l07"],"bgaVoidMaxPct":[25,"src-007","m07l04"],"conductorReductionClass2Pct":[20,"src-022","m11l04"],"conductorReductionClass3Pct":[10,"src-022","m11l04"],"annularRingMinMm":[0.05,"src-022","m11l04"],"handSetpointWindowC":[[330,360],null,"m11l02"],"bowTwistSmtPct":[0.75,"src-018","m01l06"],"spiVolumeWindowPct":[[75,125],null,"m04l05"],"selNozzleRangeMm":[[0.8,2],null,"m10l02"],"selDwellRangeS":[[0.8,2.5],null,"m10l04"],"roseLimitUgCm2":[1.56,"src-013","m12l03"]},"thread":[{"step":1,"moduleId":"m01","label":"Incoming release","asset":"Incoming release record","caseId":"cx-m01-01"},{"step":2,"moduleId":"m02","label":"MSL log","asset":"MSL exposure log","caseId":"cx-m02-01"},{"step":3,"moduleId":"m03","label":"Stencil","asset":"Stencil data sheet","caseId":"cx-m03-01"},{"step":4,"moduleId":"m04","label":"First print","asset":"SPI review sheet","caseId":"cx-m04-01"},{"step":5,"moduleId":"m05","label":"Placement","asset":"Placement offset record","caseId":"cx-m05-01"},{"step":6,"moduleId":"m06","label":"Reflow profile","asset":"Reflow profile log","caseId":"cx-m06-01"},{"step":7,"moduleId":"m08","label":"Through-hole","asset":"Hole and lead measurement sheet","caseId":"cx-m08-01"},{"step":8,"moduleId":"m09","label":"Wave","asset":"Wave solder process record","caseId":"cx-m09-01"},{"step":9,"moduleId":"m10","label":"Selective","asset":"Selective soldering program sheet","caseId":"cx-m10-01"},{"step":10,"moduleId":"m07","label":"AOI audit","asset":"AOI and X-ray audit","caseId":"cx-m07-01"},{"step":11,"moduleId":"m11","label":"Rework","asset":"Rework and repair record","caseId":"cx-m11-01"},{"step":12,"moduleId":"m13","label":"Test","asset":"Coverage model sheet","caseId":"cx-m13-01"},{"step":13,"moduleId":"m12","label":"Coating","asset":"Cleanliness and coating record","caseId":"cx-m12-01"},{"step":14,"moduleId":"m15","label":"Feedback","asset":"Design feedback pack","caseId":"cx-m15-01"},{"step":14,"moduleId":"m14","label":"8D report","asset":"8D problem report","caseId":"cx-m14-01"}],"board":{"id":"board-x","name":"Board X","note":"Fictional product used by every case in this course. The numbers are consistent across modules; nothing here describes a real customer board.","description":"6-layer 1.6 mm FR-4 controller board: QFP-64 at 0.5 mm pitch, QFP-48 at 0.4 mm pitch, one 256-ball BGA at 0.8 mm, 0402 passives, and one 20-pin THT connector.","facts":{"boardThicknessMm":1.6,"layers":6,"surfaceFinish":"ENIG","baseMaterial":"FR-4, Tg 170","stencilThicknessMm":0.125,"finePitchStencilThicknessMm":0.1,"paste":"SAC305, Type 4, no-clean","qfpPitchMm":0.5,"qfpPins":64,"finePitchPpm":0.4,"bgaBallPitchMm":0.8,"bgaBalls":256,"passive":"0402","thtConnector":"20-pin, 2.54 mm pitch, 1.00 mm finished hole, 0.60 mm lead","lotSize":1240,"opportunitiesPerBoard":620,"reflowAlloy":"SAC305","liquidusC":219,"solidusC":217,"talWindowS":[60,90],"pastePeakWindowC":[235,245],"typicalPeakBandC":[235,245],"peakClassificationLargeC":250,"holeFillMinPct":75,"holeFillExceptionPct":50,"holeFillExceptionLeads":14,"thtHoleNominalMm":1,"thtHoleToleranceMm":0.05,"waveContactWindowS":[2,4],"bgaVoidMaxPct":25,"conductorReductionPct":{"1":30,"2":20,"3":10},"annularRingMinMm":0.05,"handSetpointWindowC":[330,360],"roseLimitUgCm2":1.56,"boardLengthMm":160,"boardWidthMm":120,"boardAreaCm2":192,"bowTwistSmtPct":0.75,"bowTwistOtherPct":1.5,"spiVolumeWindowPct":[75,125],"selNozzleRangeMm":[0.8,2],"selDwellRangeS":[0.8,2.5]},"labels":{"boardLengthMm":"Board outline, length (mm)","boardWidthMm":"Board outline, width (mm)","boardAreaCm2":"Board surface area (cm²)","boardThicknessMm":"Board thickness (mm)","layers":"Layers","surfaceFinish":"Surface finish","baseMaterial":"Base material","stencilThicknessMm":"Stencil (mm)","finePitchStencilThicknessMm":"Stencil, fine-pitch region (mm)","paste":"Solder paste","qfpPitchMm":"QFP-64 pitch (mm)","qfpPins":"QFP-64 pins","finePitchPpm":"QFP-48 pitch (mm)","bgaBallPitchMm":"BGA ball pitch (mm)","bgaBalls":"BGA balls","passive":"Smallest passive","thtConnector":"THT connector","lotSize":"Lot size (boards)","opportunitiesPerBoard":"Solder joints per board","reflowAlloy":"Alloy","liquidusC":"Liquidus (°C)","solidusC":"Solidus (°C)","talWindowS":"Time above liquidus (s)","pastePeakWindowC":"Peak window, paste (°C)","typicalPeakBandC":"Typical production peak (°C)","peakClassificationLargeC":"Peak ceiling, large package (°C)","holeFillMinPct":"Minimum barrel fill, IPC-A-610 (%)","holeFillExceptionPct":"Thermal-plane exception fill (%)","holeFillExceptionLeads":"Leads before the exception applies","thtHoleNominalMm":"THT hole, finished (mm)","thtHoleToleranceMm":"THT hole tolerance (± mm)","waveContactWindowS":"Wave contact time (s)","bgaVoidMaxPct":"Void limit per BGA ball (%)","conductorReductionPct":"Conductor width reduction by class (%)","annularRingMinMm":"Annular ring, Class 3 external (mm)","handSetpointWindowC":"Hand-soldering setpoint (°C)","bowTwistSmtPct":"Bow and twist, SMT board, IPC-6012 (%)","bowTwistOtherPct":"Bow and twist, other boards, IPC-6012 (%)","spiVolumeWindowPct":"SPI volume window (% of nominal)","selNozzleRangeMm":"Selective nozzle ID (mm)","selDwellRangeS":"Selective dwell, low mass (s)"}},"cases":[{"caseId":"cx-m03-01","moduleId":"m03","title":"Stencil release audit: the QFP-48 apertures will not release","estMinutes":25,"level":"core","scenario":["Board X goes to a new stencil supplier. The first article prints clean on the 0402 passives and on the 0.5 mm QFP-64, but the 0.4 mm QFP-48 pads come up short in SPI: low volume on the inner rows, no offset, no bridging.","The stencil drawing issued to the supplier carries a single foil thickness of 0.125 mm and apertures of 0.20 mm x 0.80 mm on the fine-pitch part. Before you blame the printer, check whether the aperture geometry can release paste at all, and what the cheapest geometry change is that fixes it without touching the 0.5 mm part or the passives.","Work through the release indicators in the order the course uses them: area ratio, aspect ratio, then the aperture change. Use the aperture table in the data file."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"aperture-table.csv","kind":"csv","content":"aperture_id,component,shape,width_mm,length_mm,diameter_mm,foil_mm,pitch_mm\nA-01,0402 passive,rect,0.55,0.60,,0.125,0.50\nA-02,QFP-64 0.5 mm,rect,0.22,0.90,,0.125,0.50\nA-03,QFP-48 0.4 mm (current),rect,0.20,0.80,,0.125,0.40\nA-04,QFP-48 0.4 mm (thin region),rect,0.20,0.80,,0.100,0.40\nA-05,BGA 0.8 mm,round,,,0.50,0.125,0.80\nA-06,THT connector,rect,0.18,0.80,,0.125,2.54\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"Aperture A-03 (QFP-48, 0.20 mm x 0.80 mm, 0.125 mm foil). Area ratio = (L x W) / (2 x (L + W) x T). Give the value to two decimals — the next step compares it with the release indicator the course uses.","answer":{"value":0.64,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"dimensionless"},"hints":["The wall area is the perimeter times the foil thickness, and the perimeter of a rectangle is 2 x (L + W).","Numerator 0.20 x 0.80 = 0.160 mm². Denominator 2 x (0.20 + 0.80) x 0.125 = 0.250 mm².","0.160 / 0.250 = 0.64. Below the 0.66 release indicator the course uses."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.64 — below the 0.66 release indicator, so the aperture is the first suspect.","feedbackWrong":"Check the order of operations: area on top, perimeter times thickness underneath.","explanation":"Area ratio is (L x W) / (2 x (L + W) x T). For 0.20 x 0.80 at T = 0.125 the numerator is 0.160 mm² and the denominator 0.250 mm², giving 0.64. That is below the 0.66 guidance the course takes from IPC-7525, which is why release fails before any printer setting is touched."},{"id":"s2","type":"choice","prompt":"Against the release indicator the course uses, what does 0.64 mean for A-03?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Acceptable, because area ratio only matters below 0.5."},{"id":"b","text":"Irrelevant, because aspect ratio is the only release indicator."},{"id":"c","text":"Acceptable: it is above 0.60, the practical floor for a laser-cut foil."},{"id":"d","text":"Marginal to inadequate: it is below the 0.66 guidance, so release depends on paste rheology and SPI evidence, not on geometry alone."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["The lesson treats 0.66 as guidance rather than a wall, and asks what supports a marginal aperture.","0.64 is 0.02 under the indicator — close enough that some pastes will run it, which is exactly why it is called marginal."],"feedbackCorrect":"Marginal — 0.64 sits just under the 0.66 guidance, so release is not guaranteed by geometry.","feedbackWrong":"Re-read the lesson's wording: it gives a guidance value and then says what evidence can support an aperture below it.","explanation":"The course calls apertures below 0.66 marginal to inadequate: they may run with a favourable paste and a well-cut wall, but the release is then supported by SPI data and paste evidence rather than by geometry. 0.60 is not a floor the course states, and area ratio is not the only indicator — aspect ratio is its partner."},{"id":"s3","type":"numeric","prompt":"Same aperture printed through the 0.100 mm stepped region (A-04). What is the area ratio now?","answer":{"value":0.8,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"dimensionless"},"hints":["The perimeter does not change when the foil gets thinner; only the denominator's second factor changes.","Denominator becomes 2 x (0.20 + 0.80) x 0.100 = 0.200 mm².","0.160 / 0.200 = 0.80."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.80 — comfortably clear of the 0.66 indicator.","feedbackWrong":"Only the foil thickness changed: keep the numerator at 0.160 mm² and redo the denominator.","explanation":"At 0.100 mm the wall area drops to 0.200 mm², so the ratio rises to 0.80. That is the step-stencil remedy: thin the foil only over the fine-pitch region, because the reduction in wall contact is what buys the release."},{"id":"s4","type":"numeric","prompt":"For that same 0.20 mm aperture in 0.100 mm foil, what is the aspect ratio?","answer":{"value":2,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"dimensionless"},"hints":["Aspect ratio uses the smallest aperture dimension, not the length.","Aspect ratio = aperture width / foil thickness.","0.20 / 0.100 = 2.0."],"feedbackCorrect":"2.0 — above the 1.5 indicator as well.","feedbackWrong":"Use the narrow dimension (0.20 mm), divided by the foil thickness.","explanation":"Aspect ratio = smallest dimension / foil thickness = 0.20 / 0.100 = 2.0, above the 1.5 guidance. Both indicators now pass, so the geometry argument for the step stencil is complete."},{"id":"s5","type":"numeric","prompt":"The THT connector aperture A-06 is 0.18 mm x 0.80 mm in 0.125 mm foil. What is its area ratio?","answer":{"value":0.5878,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"dimensionless"},"hints":["Same formula as before; the narrow dimension drives the result.","Numerator 0.18 x 0.80 = 0.144 mm², denominator 2 x (0.18 + 0.80) x 0.125 = 0.245 mm².","0.144 / 0.245 = 0.588, which rounds to 0.59."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.59 — the worst aperture on the board.","feedbackWrong":"Recompute both the area and the perimeter for the narrower aperture.","explanation":"0.144 / 0.245 = 0.588, so 0.59. It is the lowest ratio on Board X and would be the second candidate for a step-down region if the connector pads show short deposits."},{"id":"s6","type":"numeric","prompt":"The BGA aperture A-05 is a round 0.50 mm opening in 0.125 mm foil. For a circle the area ratio is D / (4 x T). What is it?","answer":{"value":1,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"dimensionless"},"hints":["For a circle the perimeter is pi x D and the area is pi x D² / 4, so pi cancels.","What is left is D / (4 x T).","0.50 / (4 x 0.125) = 0.50 / 0.50 = 1.00."],"feedbackCorrect":"1.00 — a comfortable margin, as the round aperture should give.","feedbackWrong":"Use D / (4 x T) directly; do not square the diameter.","explanation":"Area ratio for a round aperture is D / (4 x T) = 0.50 / 0.500 = 1.00. The pi terms cancel between the circular area and the circular wall, which is why round BGA openings tolerate a thicker foil than a narrow rectangle at the same dimension."},{"id":"s7","type":"numeric","prompt":"How much paste does the 0.20 x 0.80 mm aperture hold in 0.100 mm foil, in nanolithres? (1 mm³ = 1000 nL)","answer":{"value":16,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"nL"},"hints":["Nominal volume is aperture area times foil thickness.","0.20 x 0.80 = 0.160 mm²; times 0.100 mm = 0.016 mm³.","0.016 mm³ x 1000 nL/mm³ = 16 nL."],"feedbackCorrect":"16 nL of nominal volume per aperture.","feedbackWrong":"Watch the unit: the volume in mm³ is smaller than the number in nL by a factor of 1000.","explanation":"Aperture area 0.160 mm² times foil 0.100 mm gives 0.016 mm³, which is 16 nL. Compare that with the joint requirement before choosing foil: thinner foil fixes release but reduces volume, which is why the two checks are a loop."},{"id":"s8","type":"choice","prompt":"Which change fixes A-03 without disturbing the 0.5 mm QFP-64 and the passives?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Step the foil down to 0.100 mm over the QFP-48 region only."},{"id":"b","text":"Raise squeegee pressure until the apertures fill."},{"id":"c","text":"Reduce the aperture width to 0.18 mm to lower the wall contact."},{"id":"d","text":"Reduce the whole stencil to 0.100 mm."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["Which parts of the board are already printing inside the indicators?","A-01 and A-02 sit comfortably above 0.66 at 0.125 mm, so a global change would move them for no reason.","The course's step-stencil lesson: put the transition away from fine-pitch features and keep each region at its own thickness."],"feedbackCorrect":"A stepped region at 0.100 mm over the fine-pitch part only.","feedbackWrong":"Check the ratios of the other apertures at 0.125 mm before choosing a global change.","explanation":"A-01 (0402) and A-02 (QFP-64 at 0.22 x 0.90) already pass at 0.125 mm, so thinning the whole foil would cut their paste volume for nothing and can cause its own defects. A stepped foil gives the QFP-48 the 0.80 it needs while leaving the rest of the board where it printed well. Pressure cannot repair a wall-dominated release, and a narrower aperture lowers the ratio further."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Area ratio of the current fine-pitch aperture computed correctly","points":15,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"Result judged against the release indicator, not against intuition","points":10,"steps":["s2"]},{"criterion":"Thinned-foil area ratio recomputed from geometry","points":15,"steps":["s3"]},{"criterion":"Aspect ratio computed with the narrow dimension","points":10,"steps":["s4"]},{"criterion":"Second-worst aperture identified and quantified","points":10,"steps":["s5"]},{"criterion":"Round-aperture rule applied, not the rectangle formula","points":10,"steps":["s6"]},{"criterion":"Nominal volume converted to nanolithres without a factor error","points":10,"steps":["s7"]},{"criterion":"Remedy chosen that does not move the apertures already in spec","points":20,"steps":["s8"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"stencil-sheet","name":"Stencil data sheet","status":"available","use":"Record every aperture, its foil thickness and its area ratio before the stencil is ordered.","lesson":"m03l05","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"stencil-sheet-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"aperture_id,component,shape,width_mm,length_mm,diameter_mm,foil_mm,pitch_mm\r\n,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"stencil-sheet-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"aperture_id,component,shape,width_mm,length_mm,diameter_mm,foil_mm,pitch_mm\nA-01,0402 passive,rect,0.55,0.60,,0.125,0.50\nA-02,QFP-64 0.5 mm,rect,0.22,0.90,,0.125,0.50\nA-03,QFP-48 0.4 mm (current),rect,0.20,0.80,,0.125,0.40\nA-04,QFP-48 0.4 mm (thin region),rect,0.20,0.80,,0.100,0.40\nA-05,BGA 0.8 mm,round,,,0.50,0.125,0.80\nA-06,THT connector,rect,0.18,0.80,,0.125,2.54\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"stencil-sheet-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# Stencil data sheet\n\n## What this template is for\nRecord every aperture, its foil thickness and its area ratio before the stencil is ordered.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per aperture. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `aperture_id`\n- `component`\n- `shape`\n- `width_mm`\n- `length_mm`\n- `diameter_mm`\n- `foil_mm`\n- `pitch_mm`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m03-01 (Stencil release audit: the QFP-48 apertures will not release), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m03l05 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"Aperture calculator (lesson m03l05)","href":"#/l/m03l05"},{"label":"Area ratio and aspect ratio (lesson m03l02)","href":"#/l/m03l02"}],"sources":["src-004","src-001"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py"},{"caseId":"cx-m01-01","moduleId":"m01","title":"Bare-board release: a twist figure that is out by a factor of two","estMinutes":30,"level":"core","scenario":["The bare-board lot for the next Board X build is on the dock: 2,800 pieces, 160 x 120 mm, ENIG finish, one supplier lot number on the box. The receiving inspector has already done part of the job and left two numbers that do not agree with each other.","The flatness check reports 2.60 mm under one raised corner by the production method, on a 160 x 120 mm board — a 200 mm diagonal. She wrote it up as 1.3 % and put the carton on hold. The certificate of conformance is present for the whole lot, the humidity indicator card is blue and the packaging is intact, except that the cartons carry two different date codes and the counts on the labels do not match what is in the boxes.","Decide what this lot is: accepted, quarantined or rejected. Then decide how many boards should have been pulled, because the answer changes whether the twist reading is even a defect."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"board-lot.csv","kind":"csv","content":"carton,labelled_date_code,boards_on_label,boards_in_box,twist_gap_mm,humi_card\n1,D2418,700,700,1.10,blue\n2,D2418,700,700,1.35,blue\n3,D2511,700,660,2.60,blue\n4,D2511,700,700,1.05,blue\n","note":"One row per carton. The twist gap is the measured height under the raised corner, production method, on the boards sampled from that carton."},{"name":"receipt-plan.csv","kind":"csv","content":"item,value\nlot_size_declared,2800\nlot_size_counted,2760\nboard_length_mm,160\nboard_width_mm,120\nsample_taken,20\ninspection_level,II\naql,1.0\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"The raised corner measures 2.60 mm and the board diagonal is 200 mm. What is the twist by the IPC-TM-650 2.4.22 production method, in percent?","answer":{"value":0.65,"tolerance":0.03,"unit":"%"},"hints":["The method divides the gap by twice the diagonal, not by the diagonal.","2.60 / (2 x 200) = 0.0065, and that is a fraction.","2.60 / 400 x 100 = 0.65 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.65 % — inside 0.75 %. The 1.3 % on the receiving sheet is the same gap divided by the diagonal once.","feedbackWrong":"Check the divisor: the production method uses twice the diagonal.","explanation":"2.60 / (2 x 200.0) = 0.0065 = 0.65 %. On a board that carries surface-mount parts the IPC-6012 default ceiling is 0.75 %, so this carton passes — while the 1.3 % on the sheet is over the limit and would have scrapped it. Dropping the factor of two is not a rounding error here, it is a disposition: the same 2.60 mm decides whether the lot ships. That is why IPC-TM-650 2.4.22 names the divisor explicitly rather than leaving it to a percentage formula."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"Turned around: at the same 0.75 % ceiling, what is the largest gap, in mm, that this board's diagonal may show?","answer":{"value":3,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["The gap is twice the diagonal times the percentage.","2 x 200 x 0.0075.","3.00 mm."],"feedbackCorrect":"3.00 mm allowed on the diagonal; the worst carton shows 2.60 mm.","feedbackWrong":"Solve gap/(2 x diagonal) = 0.0075 for the gap.","explanation":"2 x 200 x 0.75 % = 3.00 mm, and the worst carton measures 2.60 mm, which is the 0.65 % of the previous step seen from the other end. Doing it this way is what lets an inspector carry a ruler instead of a calculator: the allowed gap is a number on the drawing, and the percentage is a number in the record. Note that this is a limit for the gap, not for the board — a bow measured along the 160 mm edge is judged against 1.20 mm instead, because the percentage applies to the direction being measured."},{"id":"s3","type":"choice","prompt":"The lot is 2,800 boards and the quality plan requires ISO 2859-1 general inspection level II at AQL 1.0. What sampling plan applies?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Code K, n = 125, accept on 3 or fewer defectives, reject on 4."},{"id":"b","text":"Code K, n = 200, accept on 5 or fewer defectives."},{"id":"c","text":"Inspect every board, because 2,800 is a large lot."},{"id":"d","text":"Code J, n = 80, accept on 2 or fewer defectives."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["2,800 falls in the 1,201-3,200 band of Table I.","That band at level II gives code K.","Code K at AQL 1.0 gives n = 125, Ac = 3, Re = 4."],"feedbackCorrect":"Code K: 125 boards, accept at 3 defectives, reject at 4.","feedbackWrong":"Find the lot-size band first, then the code letter, then the AQL column.","explanation":"A lot of 1,201 to 3,200 at general level II is code K, and code K at AQL 1.0 is 125 pieces with Ac = 3, Re = 4. The inspector pulled 20. Twenty is not a plan, it is a look: at 20 pieces you cannot see enough of even a 2 % defect rate to tell it from a 5 % one, and the acceptance number that would go with it comes from a band this lot is not in. Re = Ac + 1 in single sampling, which is why the plan with n = 200 cannot exist: 200 pieces is code L, and that plan's numbers belong to a different lot size."},{"id":"s4","type":"numeric","prompt":"The count on the box does not match the labels: 2,760 boards instead of 2,800. The whole shipment is one supplier lot. What sample size, in pieces, was required for each lot size?","answer":{"value":125,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"pieces"},"hints":["Both 2,760 and 2,800 sit in the same Table I band.","The 1,201-3,200 band at level II is code K.","Code K is 125 pieces, and the shortfall does not change the band."],"feedbackCorrect":"125 in both cases — 40 boards do not move the lot to another band.","feedbackWrong":"Compare the two counts with the band edges before re-deriving anything.","explanation":"2,760 and 2,800 both fall in 1,201-3,200, so both are code K and both call for 125 pieces. That is the useful part of a table over an intuition: a 40-piece shortfall is a shortage to report, not a sampling change. The number that should bother the inspector is different — that the box and the labels disagree about what was shipped."},{"id":"s5","type":"choice","prompt":"The two date codes split this shipment into lots of 1,400 and 1,400. Both would still call for the same 125 pieces. Why does that matter here?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It means the AQL should be halved to keep the same protection."},{"id":"b","text":"It does not; 1,400 is a different lot size and needs its own plan."},{"id":"c","text":"The two date codes may be two lots, and a lot is the population the plan applies to. Splitting leaves two lots of 1,400, and both sit in the 1,201-3,200 band, so the plan holds either way."},{"id":"d","text":"It means the sample can be pulled from whichever carton is nearest."}],"answer":{"value":"c"},"hints":["A lot is one part number, revision, supplier lot and date code — not one shipment.","Split the boxes by date code and look at the two lot sizes.","Both fall in 1,201-3,200, so both keep code K and 125 pieces."],"feedbackCorrect":"Split into two lots by date code; the plan is unchanged, and the split is what the word 'lot' was always doing.","feedbackWrong":"Which quantity does the sampling plan actually apply to?","explanation":"The plan protects a population, and the population is the lot as defined: one part, one revision, one date code. A shipment carrying two date codes is two lots unless the quality plan says the difference does not affect the inspected characteristic. Here it does not change the numbers — two lots of 1,400 sit comfortably inside the K band — but the band is coarse, and a split near an edge does change it: a lot of 1,200 is code K only because the band starts at 1,201, and 1,150 would fall to code J and a sample of 80. The split also decides what a reject gets attributed to, which supplier explanation gets tested, and whether the 40 missing boards came from one lot or two."},{"id":"s6","type":"numeric","prompt":"The shipping paperwork and the box disagree by how many percent, counting the 2,760 boards in the boxes against the 2,800 on the labels?","answer":{"value":1.4286,"tolerance":0.1,"unit":"%"},"hints":["The gap is 40 boards.","Divide the gap by the declared quantity, not by the counted one.","40 / 2,800 x 100 = 1.4 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"1.4 % of the declared quantity is missing.","feedbackWrong":"Part over whole: the whole here is what the labels promised.","explanation":"40 / 2,800 = 1.4 %. Small enough to wave through, and not a cosmetic matter: the counts feed the build plan, the lot traceability and any later return of affected boards, and a carton whose label and contents disagree is a carton nobody can trace. This is the kind of finding that belongs in the record even when the product itself measures fine."},{"id":"s7","type":"choice","prompt":"On the evidence in the two files, what is the disposition of this shipment?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept the good cartons and reject carton 3."},{"id":"b","text":"Accept it: the twist is inside the limit and the humidity card is blue."},{"id":"c","text":"Reject it: the boards are out of flatness by a factor of eight."},{"id":"d","text":"Quarantine it: the product measurements pass, but the date codes and the counts do not let the lots be defined or traced until the supplier explains them."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["Separate what was measured from what cannot be established.","Nothing measured is a defect; what is missing is identity and quantity.","Quarantine holds material whose status is unclear, and this is that."],"feedbackCorrect":"Quarantine: nothing measured fails, and nothing about the lot can be established.","feedbackWrong":"Which of the findings could be dispositioned today, and which one needs the supplier?","explanation":"The twist is 0.20 % against a 0.75 % ceiling and the humidity indicator is blue, so the product side of this lot is fine — a rejection on flatness would be wrong, and it is what the 6 % reading would have produced. But two date codes in one shipment and 40 boards missing from the boxes leave the lot undefined: you cannot say which boards came from which manufacturing lot, so you cannot contain, return or trace them if one lot turns out bad. Quarantine is the disposition for 'evidence incomplete', and it is reversible once the supplier answers. Rejecting the lot would be a claim about the product that the data does not support."},{"id":"s8","type":"choice","prompt":"The supplier replies that both date codes come from the same lamination batch and the count difference is a labelling error. What does that reply change?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Everything: with one batch the shipment is one lot and can be released."},{"id":"b","text":"It answers the lot question, so the 125-piece sample can be pulled across the whole shipment and dispositioned as one lot; the 40 missing boards remain an open quantity issue with the supplier."},{"id":"c","text":"Nothing, because a supplier statement is never used at the dock."},{"id":"d","text":"It means the sample can be reduced to 20 pieces as originally taken."}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["Which of the two findings does the statement address?","It addresses identity (one lamination batch), not quantity.","Release follows the sample, and the quantity gap stays open."],"feedbackCorrect":"The lot question is closed and the sample can cover the shipment; the 40 boards stay open.","feedbackWrong":"Take the statement one finding at a time: which does it resolve?","explanation":"A supplier statement about one lamination batch can define the lot, provided it is recorded and traceable to the paperwork — that is what supplier documentation is for, and refusing to ever use it would quarantine every lot that arrives with a letter. It does not create 40 boards. So the sample proceeds at 125 pieces against the whole shipment, the plan still administers the product, and the shortage is closed separately with a debit or a replacement. Conflating the two is how a lot gets released while the traceability hole moves into the build."},{"id":"s9","type":"choice","prompt":"What makes this release record usable six months from now?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The two lot sizes and the date codes as received, the plan and AQL with the sample size and the accept number, the measured twist with its method and the limit it was compared with, the humidity card reading, the 40-board quantity gap with its closure, the quarantine and its release, and the supplier's statement."},{"id":"b","text":"A photograph of the carton labels."},{"id":"c","text":"A statement that the lot was inspected and released."},{"id":"d","text":"The inspector's name and the date."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["A record is judged by whether someone else could re-take the same decision.","The method matters as much as the measurement: 0.20 % and 6 % describe the same board.","Identity, plan, measurement with method, quantity, disposition, closure."],"feedbackCorrect":"Identity, plan and AQL, the twist with its method and limit, the humidity reading, the shortage and its closure, the hold and its release.","feedbackWrong":"Which of these would let another engineer reach the same disposition without you?","explanation":"Which of these would fail if the lot were questioned a year later, or if the product class changed? A record that says 'released' cannot answer either. The twist figure without its method is actively misleading, because 0.20 % and 6 % are the same 0.80 mm; the plan without its AQL cannot show whether 3 defectives was a pass; and the quarantine without its release leaves a permanent question mark over boards that are already assembled. A photo of a carton label is evidence for one of these facts, not a record of the decision."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Twist computed by the production method, with the factor of two","points":20,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"Bow limit derived for the measured direction","points":10,"steps":["s2"]},{"criterion":"ISO 2859-1 plan read off the table for the actual lot size","points":15,"steps":["s3","s4"]},{"criterion":"Lot defined before sampling, and the split shown not to change the plan","points":15,"steps":["s5"]},{"criterion":"Quantity gap quantified against the declared quantity","points":10,"steps":["s6"]},{"criterion":"Disposition separates a passing measurement from an undefined lot","points":20,"steps":["s7","s8"]},{"criterion":"Release record carries the method, the limit and the closure","points":10,"steps":["s9"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"incoming-release","name":"Incoming release record","status":"available","use":"Record the lot as received, the plan it was judged against, the measurement with its method, and what closed the hold — so a release can be defended when the product class or the standard moves.","lesson":"m01l07","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"incoming-release-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"carton,labelled_date_code,boards_on_label,boards_in_box,twist_gap_mm,humi_card\r\n,,,,,\r\n,,,,,\r\n,,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"incoming-release-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"carton,labelled_date_code,boards_on_label,boards_in_box,twist_gap_mm,humi_card\n1,D2418,700,700,1.10,blue\n2,D2418,700,700,1.35,blue\n3,D2511,700,660,2.60,blue\n4,D2511,700,700,1.05,blue\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"incoming-release-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# Incoming release record\n\n## What this template is for\nRecord the lot as received, the plan it was judged against, the measurement with its method, and what closed the hold — so a release can be defended when the product class or the standard moves.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per lot or carton. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `carton`\n- `labelled_date_code`\n- `boards_on_label`\n- `boards_in_box`\n- `twist_gap_mm`\n- `humi_card`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m01-01 (Bare-board release: a twist figure that is out by a factor of two), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m01l07 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"Sampling a lot (lesson m01l03)","href":"#/l/m01l03"},{"label":"Bare PCB receipt (lesson m01l06)","href":"#/l/m01l06"},{"label":"Nonconforming material at dock (lesson m01l07)","href":"#/l/m01l07"}],"sources":["src-018","src-006","src-037"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py"},{"caseId":"cx-m04-01","moduleId":"m04","title":"SPI: a passing mean over a two-humped print","estMinutes":35,"level":"advanced","scenario":["Board X has been running the same stencil for six weeks. The daily SPI summary is green: fine-pitch volume averages in the high eighties against nominal, a few pads flagged, nothing that stopped the line. The print operator says the board looks the same as it always did.","Then the SPI map is printed instead of the summary, and the six deposits printed on the first squeegee pass sit visibly above the six on the return pass. Nothing else about the stencil changed. The quality engineer wants to keep running because the mean is inside the window, and the operator wants to raise squeegee pressure because the low group is low.","A third number is waiting as well: the SPI program carries the 0.125 mm figure from the general stencil, while the fine-pitch region on the press is 0.100 mm. Decide what is actually wrong, and in what order to fix it."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"spi-deposits.csv","kind":"csv","content":"board,deposit,squeegee_pass,volume_nL,area_mm2\n1,1,first,18.32,0.198\n1,2,first,18.72,0.198\n1,3,first,17.68,0.198\n1,4,second,16.24,0.198\n1,5,second,15.52,0.198\n1,6,second,16.08,0.198\n2,1,first,18.40,0.198\n2,2,first,17.92,0.198\n2,3,first,18.24,0.198\n2,4,second,15.84,0.198\n2,5,second,16.48,0.198\n2,6,second,15.60,0.198\n","note":"Fine-pitch deposits, QFP-48 family. Volume in nanolitres as the SPI reports it; area is the measured paste footprint, close to the 0.198 mm2 aperture on every deposit."},{"name":"spi-setup.csv","kind":"csv","content":"item,value\naperture_width_mm,0.22\naperture_length_mm,0.90\npress_foil_mm,0.100\nprogram_foil_mm,0.125\nvolume_window_low_pct,75\nvolume_window_high_pct,125\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"The fine-pitch aperture is 0.22 mm x 0.90 mm and the region prints through the 0.100 mm foil. What is the nominal deposit volume, in nanolitres?","answer":{"value":19.8,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"nL"},"hints":["Volume is aperture area times foil thickness.","0.22 x 0.90 = 0.198 mm²; 0.198 x 0.100 = 0.0198 mm³.","A cubic millimetre is a microlitre, so 0.0198 mm³ = 19.8 nL."],"feedbackCorrect":"19.8 nL — every SPI percentage in this case is a share of it.","feedbackWrong":"Area times thickness, then mm³ to nL: multiply by 1000.","explanation":"0.198 mm² x 0.100 mm = 0.0198 mm³, and a cubic millimetre is a microlitre, so the deposit is 19.8 nL. This is the number the SPI machine computes when it prints 'volume %', and it is the number the whole case hangs on: 16 nL is not 'low' on its own, it is 81 % of this aperture and would be 65 % of a larger one. The foil figure matters as much as the area: the same aperture printed through 0.125 mm would hold 24.75 nL — which is exactly the nominal the SPI program was dividing by, and the trap waiting in step 9."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"Across the twelve deposits, what is the mean volume as a percentage of that nominal? One decimal.","answer":{"value":86.3,"tolerance":0.3,"unit":"%"},"hints":["Add the twelve volumes and divide by twelve.","The sum is 205.04 nL, so the mean is 17.09 nL.","17.09 / 19.8 x 100."],"feedbackCorrect":"86.3 % — comfortably inside the 75-125 % window.","feedbackWrong":"Mean first, as a share of nominal second.","explanation":"The volumes sum to 205.04 nL over twelve deposits, a mean of 17.09 nL, which is 86.3 % of the 19.8 nL nominal. Against a 75-125 % window that is a passing number, and it is the number on the daily summary — which is why this case exists. A mean cannot tell a tight process centred at 86 % from a process that prints 92 % on one pass and 81 % on the next."},{"id":"s3","type":"numeric","prompt":"The window is 75-125 % of nominal. What is the lower limit, in nanolitres?","answer":{"value":14.85,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"nL"},"hints":["Take 75 % of the same 19.8 nL nominal.","19.8 x 0.75.","14.85 nL."],"feedbackCorrect":"14.85 nL is the floor; 24.75 nL is the ceiling.","feedbackWrong":"Multiply the nominal by the window percentage.","explanation":"19.8 x 0.75 = 14.85 nL, and 19.8 x 1.25 = 24.75 nL at the top. Both clusters sit inside this pair, which is the awkward part: nothing here is out of specification, and a process can be incapable without producing a single out-of-limit print. That is the difference between spec and control that m04l02 draws, and the one the daily summary hides."},{"id":"s4","type":"numeric","prompt":"Compute Cp for the twelve deposits against that window. Two decimals.","answer":{"value":1.34,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"ratio"},"hints":["Cp is the window width over six standard deviations.","The window is 24.75 - 14.85 = 9.90 nL wide; the sample sd is 1.229 nL.","9.90 / (6 x 1.229)."],"feedbackCorrect":"Cp 1.34 — the spread alone would fit inside the window.","feedbackWrong":"Sample sd uses n-1, and Cp does not care where the mean sits.","explanation":"The window is 9.90 nL wide and the sample standard deviation of the twelve readings is 1.229 nL, so Cp = 9.90 / (6 x 1.229) = 1.34. On its own that reads like a capable process, and this is the classic trap: Cp assumes the process is centred and asks only whether the spread could fit. It could. The next number asks whether it does."},{"id":"s5","type":"numeric","prompt":"Compute Cpk for the same twelve deposits. Two decimals.","answer":{"value":0.61,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"ratio"},"hints":["Cpk is the distance from the mean to the nearer limit over three sd.","The mean is 17.09 nL; the nearer limit is the lower one at 14.85 nL.","(17.09 - 14.85) / (3 x 1.229)."],"feedbackCorrect":"Cpk 0.61 — the process is not centred, and the spread is not the whole story.","feedbackWrong":"Distance from the mean to the *nearer* limit, over 3 sd.","explanation":"(17.09 - 14.85) / (3 x 1.229) = 2.24 / 3.687 = 0.61. A Cpk under 1 means the process is producing prints outside the window at a rate the product cannot absorb, and 0.61 is not marginal. The gap between Cp 1.34 and Cpk 0.61 is entirely centring: the process prints a little low on average, and the low side is where the window runs out. A summary printing both numbers would have shown this six weeks ago."},{"id":"s6","type":"numeric","prompt":"Split the twelve deposits by squeegee pass and compute Cpk for the second pass alone. Two decimals.","answer":{"value":0.99,"tolerance":0.06,"unit":"ratio"},"hints":["Six values: 16.24, 15.52, 16.08, 15.84, 16.48, 15.60.","Their mean is 15.96 nL and their sd is 0.374 nL.","(15.96 - 14.85) / (3 x 0.374)."],"feedbackCorrect":"Cpk 0.99 on the second pass — and 3.05 on the first.","feedbackWrong":"Compute the mean and sd of the six second-pass readings only.","explanation":"The second pass averages 15.96 nL with an sd of 0.374 nL, giving Cpk = (15.96 - 14.85) / (3 x 0.374) = 0.99. The same arithmetic on the first pass gives 3.05 off a mean of 18.21 nL. Two populations, each tight, one sitting on the limit and one comfortable. That is what the pooled 0.61 was made of, and it is the difference between 'the process varies' and 'the process has two settings'."},{"id":"s7","type":"choice","prompt":"The mean is inside the window and the quality engineer wants to keep running. What does the split say?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Keep running: every deposit is inside the window, so there is nothing to act on."},{"id":"b","text":"The histogram is two humps, not one: a pooled mean and a pooled sd describe a process that does not exist. Find what separates the passes before tuning anything."},{"id":"c","text":"Raise squeegee pressure until the low group reaches the high group."},{"id":"d","text":"Lower the SPI window so the low group passes."}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["Ask what a single mean assumes about a set of readings.","Two tight clusters about 2.3 nL apart are not one wide cluster.","Identify the split before adjusting: something differs between the passes."],"feedbackCorrect":"Two populations, not one: the split is the finding, and its cause sits upstream of any setpoint.","feedbackWrong":"What would a histogram of these twelve values look like?","explanation":"The pooled sd of 1.229 nL is more than three times the sd inside either pass, and that inflation is not variation — it is the gap between two settings. A mean and a Cpk computed on the pool describe a process nobody runs. The first thing to establish is what differs between the forward and return stroke: squeegee pressure or angle on the reverse pass, a two-speed profile, board support that shifts, or a wipe cycle that lands between them. Pressure tuned to lift the low group would overfill the high group and turn one problem into two."},{"id":"s8","type":"numeric","prompt":"The mean deposit volume is 17.09 nL and the measured area is 0.198 mm². What average height does that imply, in mm?","answer":{"value":0.0863,"tolerance":0.002,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["Volume over area gives height, once the units agree.","17.09 nL = 0.01709 mm³.","0.01709 / 0.198."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.086 mm of paste on a 0.100 mm stencil — about 86 % of the foil.","feedbackWrong":"Convert nL to mm³ first: 1 nL = 0.001 mm³.","explanation":"17.09 nL is 0.01709 mm³, and over 0.198 mm² that is 0.086 mm. Height tracks volume almost exactly here because the area is nominal on every deposit, and that is itself the diagnostic: area normal with volume low means the paste did not fill the aperture to full depth, not that it spread. Had area been low as well, the story would be a clogged or partly blocked aperture instead."},{"id":"s9","type":"choice","prompt":"The SPI program was built against the 0.125 mm general stencil, and the fine-pitch region on the press is 0.100 mm — the foil the values above were measured against. On that program the screen reads 69 % of nominal. What is really there?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"55 %: the thinner foil means less paste, so the reading overstates the deposit."},{"id":"b","text":"69 % is right, but the window should be widened to match the foil."},{"id":"c","text":"69 % — the machine computes nominal from the aperture, so the foil in its program does not matter."},{"id":"d","text":"86 %: the program's nominal is 25 % too large, so every percentage it prints is 20 % too small, and the truth is 69 % x 0.125 / 0.100."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["Nominal = area x foil, and the machine uses the foil in its program.","A program foil thicker than reality makes the nominal too large, so the percentage it divides out is too small.","69 x 0.125 / 0.100 = 86.25 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"86 %, not 69 %: the program's nominal is 25 % too large, so every reading on that screen sits 20 % low.","feedbackWrong":"Which foil value is inside the nominal the machine divides by?","explanation":"The machine computes nominal as area x the foil in its program. Its nominal is 24.75 nL, while the press cuts apertures for 19.80 nL, so the 17.09 nL it is looking at prints on the screen as 69.0 % and is 86.3 % on the board. The direction is worth keeping straight: a program foil that is too thick inflates the denominator, so the screen reads LOW, by the ratio of the two foils — 0.100 / 0.125, or 20 %. Correcting it is one multiplication: 69.0 x 0.125 / 0.100 = 86.3 %. This is a measurement-system finding, not a print finding, and it belongs first. It is also the reason the absolute nanolitres matter: a volume does not care what the program believed, which is why the volumes above were used to compute the mean rather than the percentages on the screen."},{"id":"s10","type":"choice","prompt":"In what order do the three findings get handled?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Nothing: the mean is inside the window and the boards pass."},{"id":"b","text":"Print pressure first, then the split, then the SPI program."},{"id":"c","text":"Correct the SPI program's foil figure and re-read the data, then establish what differs between the two squeegee passes, then tune the print if it is still needed."},{"id":"d","text":"All three at once, since they are all part of the same print step."}],"answer":{"value":"c"},"hints":["A measurement that is wrong by a constant factor cannot rank anything.","The split is a process question and needs trustworthy data to be sized.","Measurement, then structure, then settings."],"feedbackCorrect":"Measurement first, then the two-population question, then the print settings — and one variable at a time.","feedbackWrong":"Which finding would make you doubt the numbers the other two rest on?","explanation":"The foil error is a constant factor — a 25 % oversize on the nominal, which shows up as every reading 20 % low — so it distorts the window, the Cpk and the decision to raise pressure. Correcting it costs nothing and gives the split a trustworthy scale. The split comes next because it is a structural difference between the passes, and until it is named, any pressure change is a guess applied to both halves of a two-humped distribution. Only then does the print get tuned, one variable at a time, with SPI re-read after each change — the m04l07 rule this case exists to exercise."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Nominal volume computed from aperture area and the press foil","points":15,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"Mean as a share of nominal, and the limits in the same unit","points":15,"steps":["s2","s3"]},{"criterion":"Cp and Cpk computed and the gap between them explained","points":20,"steps":["s4","s5"]},{"criterion":"Squeegee passes separated and capability computed per pass","points":20,"steps":["s6","s7"]},{"criterion":"Height derived from volume and area, and its meaning read","points":10,"steps":["s8"]},{"criterion":"SPI program's foil figure checked against the press before any print change","points":15,"steps":["s9"]},{"criterion":"Findings ordered: measurement, then structure, then settings","points":5,"steps":["s10"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"spi-review","name":"SPI review sheet","status":"available","use":"Keep each deposit's volume and area next to the pass it came from, so a pooled mean cannot hide two populations, and recompute capability when the SPI program or the stencil changes.","lesson":"m04l05","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"spi-review-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"board,deposit,squeegee_pass,volume_nL,area_mm2\r\n,,,,\r\n,,,,\r\n,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"spi-review-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"board,deposit,squeegee_pass,volume_nL,area_mm2\n1,1,first,18.32,0.198\n1,2,first,18.72,0.198\n1,3,first,17.68,0.198\n1,4,second,16.24,0.198\n1,5,second,15.52,0.198\n1,6,second,16.08,0.198\n2,1,first,18.40,0.198\n2,2,first,17.92,0.198\n2,3,first,18.24,0.198\n2,4,second,15.84,0.198\n2,5,second,16.48,0.198\n2,6,second,15.60,0.198\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"spi-review-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# SPI review sheet\n\n## What this template is for\nKeep each deposit's volume and area next to the pass it came from, so a pooled mean cannot hide two populations, and recompute capability when the SPI program or the stencil changes.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per deposit. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `board`\n- `deposit`\n- `squeegee_pass`\n- `volume_nL`\n- `area_mm2`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m04-01 (SPI: a passing mean over a two-humped print), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m04l05 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"Reading SPI data (lesson m04l02)","href":"#/l/m04l02"},{"label":"SPI as process evidence (lesson m04l05)","href":"#/l/m04l05"},{"label":"Printer setup (lesson m04l03)","href":"#/l/m04l03"}],"sources":["src-004","src-001"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py"},{"caseId":"cx-m05-01","moduleId":"m05","title":"Placement: an offset that passes and a rotation that does not","estMinutes":35,"level":"advanced","scenario":["Board X comes back from a placement-machine calibration. The first article of ten boards goes through the offset check and the report says 0.11 mm, against a 0.15 mm acceptance — a pass, and the line wants to release.","Two things on the board disagree with that report. The corner leads of the QFP-64 on the right-hand side show a toe overhang that the left-hand side does not, and a 0402 near the panel edge looks rotated in the AOI image while the parts near the middle look square.","The two global fiducials are 10 mm apart and their residuals are in the audit file, per board. Work out what the 0.11 mm is made of before anyone signs the release."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"placement-audit.csv","kind":"csv","content":"board,fiducial,dx_mm,dy_mm\n1,A,0.060,0.020\n1,B,0.070,0.160\n2,A,0.062,0.024\n2,B,0.072,0.158\n3,A,0.058,0.018\n3,B,0.068,0.162\n","note":"Residual per fiducial per board, in machine coordinates: measured position minus programmed position. Fiducial A sits at x = -5 mm, B at x = +5 mm on the panel."},{"name":"placement-setup.csv","kind":"csv","content":"item,value\nfiducial_separation_mm,10\nqfp64_pad_width_mm,0.30\nqfp64_lead_width_mm,0.20\nqfp64_corner_lead_radius_mm,7\nedge_part_radius_mm,40\nacceptance_offset_mm,0.15\nboards_in_audit,10\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"On board 1 the fiducials read A (0.060, 0.020) and B (0.070, 0.160), 10 mm apart. What rotation does that difference represent, in degrees?","answer":{"value":0.8021,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"deg"},"hints":["The rotation is the difference in the Y residual over the fiducial separation.","0.160 - 0.020 = 0.140 mm across 10 mm.","0.014 rad, and one radian is 57.3 degrees."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.80°: a small angle, and the reason the report's single offset hides it.","feedbackWrong":"Difference in Y over the separation, then radians to degrees.","explanation":"The Y residuals differ by 0.140 mm across a 10 mm baseline, which is 0.014 rad, or 0.80°. In X the two fiducials differ by only 0.010 mm over the same 10 mm, so almost all of the rotation is about Z. That figure is the whole case: 0.8° reads like nothing, and the next two steps price it."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"A component sits 40 mm from the fiducial midpoint — near the panel edge. What lateral displacement does that 0.80° cause there, in mm?","answer":{"value":0.56,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["Displacement is the lever arm times the angle in radians.","Use 0.014 rad, not 0.8.","40 x 0.014 = 0.56 mm."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.56 mm — nearly four times the acceptance the report passed.","feedbackWrong":"Convert degrees to radians first: multiply by pi/180.","explanation":"40 x 0.014 = 0.56 mm. The angle never changed; the lever arm did. This is why rotation is not a cosmetic finding: an 0.8° error is 0.11 mm at the fiducial midpoint, 0.28 mm at 20 mm and 0.56 mm at the panel edge. A placement check that samples only near the middle of the board reports a process that does not exist at its edges, which is exactly where a 0402 sits and what the AOI image shows."},{"id":"s3","type":"numeric","prompt":"The QFP-64 lead is 0.20 mm wide on a 0.30 mm pad. How far can it move sideways, in mm, before it stops sitting on the pad?","answer":{"value":0.05,"tolerance":0.003,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["The lead may move until its edge reaches the pad edge.","The pad is 0.10 mm wider than the lead, 0.05 mm on each side.","(0.30 - 0.20) / 2."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.05 mm each way — and the corner lead moves twice that.","feedbackWrong":"Halve the difference: the pad gives clearance on both sides.","explanation":"0.30 - 0.20 = 0.10 mm of extra pad, so the lead has 0.05 mm of play on either side before it overhangs. This is the threshold the next step compares against, and it is deliberately small: at 0.4 mm pitch classes the play is even less, which is why fine-pitch parts convert a tolerable board-level rotation into a rejected lead."},{"id":"s4","type":"numeric","prompt":"The corner lead of the QFP-64 is 7 mm from the package centre. What displacement does the same 0.80° cause there, in mm?","answer":{"value":0.098,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["Same lever-arm formula, shorter arm.","7 x 0.014.","0.098 mm."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.098 mm — twice the 0.05 mm of play, so the corner lead is already overhanging.","feedbackWrong":"Lever arm times angle in radians.","explanation":"7 x 0.014 = 0.098 mm against 0.05 mm of play: the corner lead overhangs by about a lead width's worth of pad. That is the toe overhang visible on the right-hand side and absent on the left, because the rotation is about the board centre and the two sides of the package swing in opposite directions. The offset number in the report could not show this; a per-lead or per-corner check could."},{"id":"s5","type":"numeric","prompt":"Across the ten boards the X residual of fiducial A reads 0.060, 0.066, 0.058, 0.062, 0.064, 0.056, 0.061, 0.063, 0.059, 0.061. What is three standard deviations, in mm?","answer":{"value":0.0088,"tolerance":0.003,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["Take the mean first, then the deviations from it.","The mean is 0.061 mm and the sample sd is about 0.0029 mm.","3 x 0.0029."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.009 mm — the machine repeats well; the error is in where it repeats to.","feedbackWrong":"Sample sd uses n-1; three sigma is three times it.","explanation":"The ten readings average 0.061 mm with a sample sd of 0.0029 mm, so three sigma is 0.009 mm. The machine is repeatable to about a hundredth of a millimetre and simultaneously wrong by 0.06 mm in X and 0.02 mm in Y, plus 0.8° of rotation. That combination is the textbook definition of repeatable and inaccurate, and it is why the two words are measured differently: repeatability is the spread about the mean, accuracy is the mean's distance from zero."},{"id":"s6","type":"choice","prompt":"The report's 0.11 mm is the magnitude of the mean residual of the two fiducials. What is wrong with accepting the setup on that number?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The error is in the acceptance limit, not in the measurement."},{"id":"b","text":"Nothing: 0.11 mm is below the 0.15 mm acceptance, and the process is repeatable."},{"id":"c","text":"The mean of two residuals cancels the rotation: it reports where the board is centred, not how it is turned, so a passing offset and an 0.80° rotation coexist in one number."},{"id":"d","text":"The acceptance should be 0.05 mm, so the setup fails on the number too."}],"answer":{"value":"c"},"hints":["Ask what averaging two points on a 10 mm baseline does to a rotation.","A rotation moves one fiducial up and the other down, so their mean barely moves.","Offset and rotation are two findings and need two numbers."],"feedbackCorrect":"The mean cancels the rotation: offset and rotation have to be reported separately, with the lever arm at which each was judged.","feedbackWrong":"Which component of the error survives averaging two opposite fiducials?","explanation":"A translation moves both fiducials the same way, so it survives the mean; a rotation moves them in opposite directions and almost disappears in it. The report therefore measured the one component that was acceptable and averaged away the one that was not. That is not a rounding problem — it is a report that cannot fail, and the same report would have passed a 2° rotation. The fix is a second number: the angle, with the radius at which it was judged."},{"id":"s7","type":"choice","prompt":"The offset is constant across boards and its direction does not change with board position. What does that pattern point at?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A worn nozzle on the QFP-64 head, since the leads are the visible symptom."},{"id":"b","text":"Paste slump after placement, since the parts look rotated later."},{"id":"c","text":"Board support or conveyor seating, since the error varies per board."},{"id":"d","text":"The board coordinate system itself: fiducial coordinates in the program, or the vision calibration that turns pixels into millimetres."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["The pattern is constant, not varying: something in the coordinate chain, not in the handling.","A per-board change would suggest support; a constant error points upstream.","The calibration event is the recent change to that chain."],"feedbackCorrect":"The coordinate system: program fiducial coordinates or vision calibration, both of which the recent calibration touched.","feedbackWrong":"Which of these would produce the same error on every board, in both axes, with a fixed rotation?","explanation":"An error that is constant in magnitude and direction is in the coordinate chain: the fiducial coordinates the program carries, or the camera calibration that converts pixels to millimetres. Board support and conveyor seating produce errors that change board to board, which is what the 0.009 mm repeatability rules out. A worn nozzle changes the part relative to the nozzle, not the board relative to the program, and it would not give a rotation that grows with distance from the centre. Since the machine has just been calibrated, the calibration record and the fiducial coordinate table are the two things to re-check, in that order."},{"id":"s8","type":"choice","prompt":"Which parts on this board does the 0.80° hurt first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The largest parts, because they have the most mass to swing."},{"id":"b","text":"The parts with the least pad play and the greatest distance from the rotation centre: fine-pitch corner leads and parts near the panel edge."},{"id":"c","text":"The parts nearest the fiducials, because the fiducials define the error."},{"id":"d","text":"All parts equally, since rotation is a board-level property."}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["Displacement is lever arm times angle.","Overhang is displacement minus the pad play.","Both factors matter, and they are in different places on the board."],"feedbackCorrect":"Fine-pitch corner leads and edge parts: the largest lever arms meeting the smallest play.","feedbackWrong":"Two properties decide which part fails first: how far it is from the centre, and how much room its pad gives it.","explanation":"The misplaced distance is the lever arm times the angle, so parts far from the rotation centre move most — the panel edge at 40 mm moves 0.56 mm while the QFP corner at 7 mm moves 0.098 mm. But movement only becomes a defect where the pad cannot absorb it, and the QFP's 0.05 mm of play is the tightest on the board. The two effects pick out different parts, which is why both the corner leads and the edge 0402 show symptoms while the parts in the middle look fine. Judging placement at the board's centre alone tests neither."},{"id":"s9","type":"choice","prompt":"What has to be in the placement record for this setup to be releasable?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The mean offset in X and Y with its sign, the rotation in degrees with its sign, the fiducial residuals, the lever arm at which each was judged, the repeatability over the sample, the calibration status and its date, and the program and library versions."},{"id":"b","text":"A photograph of the overhanging corner lead."},{"id":"c","text":"The machine's own report, exported as a PDF."},{"id":"d","text":"The acceptance limit and a pass/fail mark."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["A second engineer must be able to re-judge the setup without you.","Offset, rotation, lever arm, spread, calibration, versions.","One number cannot be re-judged, because it cannot fail."],"feedbackCorrect":"Offset with signs, rotation with its sign, the lever arm, the residuals, the spread, the calibration status and the program version.","feedbackWrong":"Which of these would let someone a year later decide whether the same setup is still acceptable?","explanation":"The record has to carry the two numbers the report collapsed into one, each with its sign, because a +0.8° and a -0.8° rotate opposite corners over the pad. The lever arm matters as much as the angle: 0.8° is harmless at 2 mm and fatal at 40 mm, so an angle without the radius at which it was judged cannot be re-judged. The calibration date and the program and library versions are what turn 'it passed' into 'it passed against this configuration', which is the difference between a record and a claim."},{"id":"s10","type":"choice","prompt":"What is the disposition of the setup?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Release: the offset is 0.11 mm, inside the 0.15 mm acceptance, and the machine repeats to 0.009 mm."},{"id":"b","text":"Hold the setup, re-check the fiducial coordinates and the vision calibration, and re-run the first article with offset and rotation reported separately."},{"id":"c","text":"Release, and instruct AOI to tighten its rotation limit so bad boards are caught downstream."},{"id":"d","text":"Adjust the QFP-64 program offset to compensate for the overhang."}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["The rejection is real: a corner lead is over its pad by twice the play.","The cause is upstream of the placement — in the coordinate chain.","Compensating a board-level rotation with a part-level offset fixes one corner and breaks the other."],"feedbackCorrect":"Hold, re-check the fiducial coordinates and vision calibration, re-run the first article with offset and rotation reported separately.","feedbackWrong":"Which action removes the cause, and which ones move it around the board?","explanation":"The corner lead is overhanging by twice its play, so releasing on the offset number ships a defect; the number is wrong, not the board. Tightening AOI accepts the same bad placement and pays for it downstream. Compensating the QFP offset with a constant shift fixes the right-hand corner and pushes the left-hand one off the pad, because a rotation is not a translation. The cause lives in the coordinate chain — fiducial coordinates or the calibration that just changed — so that is what gets re-checked, and the first article is re-run with the two numbers kept apart."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Rotation computed from the fiducial residuals, in degrees","points":15,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"Displacement computed from the lever arm, not from the angle alone","points":20,"steps":["s2","s4"]},{"criterion":"Pad play computed and used as the threshold for the corner lead","points":15,"steps":["s3"]},{"criterion":"Repeatability separated from accuracy, with the spread computed","points":15,"steps":["s5"]},{"criterion":"The mean shown to cancel a rotation, and the report judged on that","points":15,"steps":["s6"]},{"criterion":"Cause placed in the coordinate chain, not in handling or the nozzle","points":10,"steps":["s7"]},{"criterion":"Parts ranked by lever arm and pad play, not by size","points":5,"steps":["s8"]},{"criterion":"Disposition holds the setup and keeps the two numbers apart","points":5,"steps":["s9","s10"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"placement-audit","name":"Placement offset record","status":"available","use":"Keep the fiducial residuals, the resulting offset and the resulting rotation on one sheet, because an offset averaged over two fiducials cannot show a rotation.","lesson":"m05l05","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"placement-audit-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"board,fiducial,dx_mm,dy_mm\r\n,,,\r\n,,,\r\n,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"placement-audit-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"board,fiducial,dx_mm,dy_mm\n1,A,0.060,0.020\n1,B,0.070,0.160\n2,A,0.062,0.024\n2,B,0.072,0.158\n3,A,0.058,0.018\n3,B,0.068,0.162\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"placement-audit-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# Placement offset record\n\n## What this template is for\nKeep the fiducial residuals, the resulting offset and the resulting rotation on one sheet, because an offset averaged over two fiducials cannot show a rotation.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per fiducial reading. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `board`\n- `fiducial`\n- `dx_mm`\n- `dy_mm`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m05-01 (Placement: an offset that passes and a rotation that does not), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m05l05 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"Fiducials and board recognition (lesson m05l03)","href":"#/l/m05l03"},{"label":"Accuracy and repeatability (lesson m05l01)","href":"#/l/m05l01"},{"label":"First-article check (lesson m05l05)","href":"#/l/m05l05"}],"sources":["src-011","src-001"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py"},{"caseId":"cx-m10-01","moduleId":"m10","title":"Selective soldering: the 20-pin connector, a nozzle that fits and a cycle that does not","estMinutes":35,"level":"core","scenario":["Board X carries one 20-pin THT connector at 2.54 mm pitch: 1.00 mm finished holes, 0.60 mm leads, 1.6 mm board. The line has moved this connector from the wave to a selective machine and the first program is in front of you.","The proposed nozzle is 1.20 mm inside diameter and the path steps from pin to pin with 1.8 s of dwell. The planner has already costed the run: 25 s preheat, 12 s flux and air knife, and the joint time on top. Production wants the cycle shortened, and the first idea on the table is to drop the air-knife step.","The first article shows solder sitting on two adjacent pins. Nothing about the dwell or the temperature has been questioned yet. Work through the joint geometry, the flux and the cycle before the program is signed."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"selective-program.csv","kind":"csv","content":"section,value\njoints,20\ndwell_s,1.8\nmove_s,0.4\npreheat_s,25\nflux_knife_s,12\nnozzle_id_mm,1.20\nnozzle_min_for_joint_mm,1.0\n"},{"name":"joint-geometry.csv","kind":"csv","content":"joint,hole_nominal_mm,hole_tol_mm,lead_mm,lead_max_mm,pitch_mm\nP-01,1.00,0.05,0.60,0.63,2.54\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"Nominal 1.00 mm hole and 0.60 mm lead: what annular gap is there on each side, in mm?","answer":{"value":0.2,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["The lead sits in the middle of the hole.","The difference is 0.40 mm, and there are two sides.","(1.00 - 0.60) / 2."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.20 mm each side at nominal.","feedbackWrong":"Halve the difference — the gap exists on both sides of the lead.","explanation":"1.00 - 0.60 = 0.40 mm of total clearance, so 0.20 mm on each side. That is the space the solder has to climb and the space the nozzle has to respect. It is also the number that decides whether the connector is a wave job or a selective job: at 0.20 mm an annular wave fills easily, and a selective nozzle has to put solder into a gap only four tenths of a millimetre wide in total."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"At the worst combination in the drawing — 0.95 mm hole and 0.63 mm lead — what is the gap on each side, in mm?","answer":{"value":0.16,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["Use the small hole and the large lead, not the nominal pair.","0.95 - 0.63 = 0.32 mm.","0.32 / 2."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.16 mm — the tolerance eats a fifth of the nominal gap.","feedbackWrong":"Worst case is the smallest hole with the largest lead.","explanation":"0.95 - 0.63 = 0.32 mm, so 0.16 mm per side. The part-to-part tolerances take 0.04 mm off each side, which is a fifth of the nominal gap. Any nozzle or dwell chosen on nominal dimensions is therefore chosen on a joint that may not exist: the tightest joints on the board are 20 % harder to fill than the drawing suggests, and those are the ones a first article should look at."},{"id":"s3","type":"choice","prompt":"The course gives 0.8-2.0 mm as a practical nozzle inside diameter for 0.5-1.2 mm leads. The proposed nozzle is 1.20 mm. What is the objection to it?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"None: 1.20 mm is inside the 0.8-2.0 mm range, so it is a valid choice."},{"id":"b","text":"1.20 mm is inside the range but too large for this joint: it leaves only 0.10 mm to the pad on each side, so the meniscus sits on the pad edge and the nozzle can put solder where it does not belong."},{"id":"c","text":"It is too small to heat a 1.00 mm hole."},{"id":"d","text":"Nozzle diameter does not matter as long as the dwell is long enough."}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["The range in the lesson is a starting range for leads, not a qualification.","Compare the nozzle with the hole, not with the range.","(1.20 - 1.00) / 2 = 0.10 mm."],"feedbackCorrect":"Inside the range and still wrong for this joint: 0.10 mm of clearance each side puts the meniscus on the pad edge.","feedbackWrong":"What is the nozzle compared against — the course range, or this joint?","explanation":"(1.20 - 1.00) / 2 = 0.10 mm of clearance on each side, against a 2.54 mm pitch. The nozzle spans the hole plus 0.10 mm of the annular ring on each side, so the solder column it forms touches the pad edge where the neighbouring net begins. A range in a lesson is a place to start looking, not a qualification: the lesson says to verify against the adjacent pad spacing, and this joint's spacing is what rules the nozzle out. The symptom on the first article — solder on two adjacent pins — is what that looks like downstream."},{"id":"s4","type":"numeric","prompt":"Twenty joints at 1.8 s dwell and 0.4 s of travel between them: what is the total joint time, in seconds?","answer":{"value":44,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"s"},"hints":["Each joint costs dwell plus the move to the next one.","1.8 + 0.4 = 2.2 s per joint.","20 x 2.2."],"feedbackCorrect":"44 s of joint time.","feedbackWrong":"Dwell plus travel, twenty times.","explanation":"1.8 + 0.4 = 2.2 s per joint, so 44 s for the connector. Travel is not free: at 0.4 s per step it is 8 s of the 44, which is 18 % of the joint time spent moving. That is the number a path optimisation actually attacks, and it is worth knowing before anything is removed from the process to buy cycle time."},{"id":"s5","type":"numeric","prompt":"Add 25 s of preheat and 12 s of flux and air-knife time. What is the total cycle, in seconds?","answer":{"value":81,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"s"},"hints":["Sum the four blocks: joints, preheat, flux and knife.","44 + 25 + 12.","81 s."],"feedbackCorrect":"81 s per board.","feedbackWrong":"Add every block, including the ones that are not soldering.","explanation":"44 s of joints, 25 s of preheat and 12 s of flux and knife make 81 s. Only 54 % of that cycle is a nozzle over a joint; the rest is the board being prepared or the nozzle travelling. Any throughput plan that starts by cutting the soldering part is attacking the smaller half and the one that decides the joint."},{"id":"s6","type":"numeric","prompt":"At 81 s per board and one board in the machine at a time, what is the throughput in boards per hour? One decimal.","answer":{"value":44.4,"tolerance":0.3,"unit":"boards/h"},"hints":["An hour is 3,600 s.","3,600 / 81.","44.4 boards per hour."],"feedbackCorrect":"44.4 boards an hour at 100 % availability.","feedbackWrong":"Seconds in an hour divided by the cycle.","explanation":"3,600 / 81 = 44.4 boards an hour, before any downtime, fixture exchange or operator break. That is the ceiling the planner should be quoting, and it is worth stating as a ceiling: availability of 85 % turns it into 37.8, and a program that is 2 s faster but produces one reworked board an hour has not gained anything."},{"id":"s7","type":"choice","prompt":"Production proposes dropping the 8 s air-knife step, which takes the cycle to 73 s and the rate to 49.3 boards an hour. What is wrong with that trade?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The cycle cannot be recomputed without the machine."},{"id":"b","text":"Nothing: 49.3 against 44.4 is an 11 % gain for a step that only moves air."},{"id":"c","text":"The air knife controls how much flux reaches the joint; removing it leaves excess flux that boils at the nozzle, which is how blowback, voids and bridging appear — the gain is real and smaller than the cost."},{"id":"d","text":"It changes the dwell, which is the number that matters."}],"answer":{"value":"c"},"hints":["Ask what the air knife does to the joint, not to the board.","It meters flux; flux that boils at the nozzle disturbs the meniscus.","The 11 % gain is arithmetic; the defect rate is not in that arithmetic."],"feedbackCorrect":"The air knife meters flux; without it the excess boils at the nozzle and the gain of 11 % buys blowback and bridging.","feedbackWrong":"What does the air-knife step actually control?","explanation":"The air knife is the flux metering step: too little action leaves excess flux that boils violently when the nozzle arrives, which produces blowback, voids and the kind of solder placement the first article already shows. The arithmetic is honest — 73 s is 49.3 boards an hour, 11 % more — but it prices only the cycle. One additional reworked board an hour costs more than 4.9 boards of capacity, and the defect it creates is the one already visible on the sample. When a step is removed to buy time, the acceptance criteria have to be re-run, not assumed."},{"id":"s8","type":"choice","prompt":"Given the first article shows solder on two adjacent pins, which single variable changes first?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Preheat: raise it so the joint wets faster."},{"id":"b","text":"Flux volume: increase it so the joint wets more completely."},{"id":"c","text":"Dwell: shorten it from 1.8 s so less solder is delivered."},{"id":"d","text":"Nozzle inside diameter: bring it down toward the joint so the meniscus sits on the annular ring and no further."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["The defect is solder where it should not be, not solder missing where it should.","Dwell and flux change how much and how well; the nozzle changes where.","One variable, the one that matches the mechanism."],"feedbackCorrect":"The nozzle: 1.20 mm spans past the annular ring, and the defect is positional.","feedbackWrong":"Which variable controls where the solder sits, as opposed to how much or how well?","explanation":"The defect is topological: solder reached a pin it should not touch. Dwell and flux change how much solder and how well it wets, and shortening the dwell to starve a bridge is the classic way to trade a bridge for an incomplete fillet. The nozzle sets the footprint of the meniscus, and at 1.20 mm that footprint reaches past the pad edge on a 1.00 mm hole. Change the nozzle alone, re-run the first article, and leave the other three settings where they are — that is the one-variable rule from m10l02, and it is what makes the result attributable."},{"id":"s9","type":"choice","prompt":"The joint is a connector pin tied to an internal ground plane, so it is a high-mass joint. The program gives it the same 1.8 s as the signal pins. What does the course's range say?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"High-mass joints start at 1.5 s and reach 4.0 s; 1.8 s is at the bottom of that band, and the course prefers two shorter passes to one long dwell when the mass is large."},{"id":"b","text":"High-mass joints need less dwell because the plane conducts heat."},{"id":"c","text":"Dwell should be the same for every joint so the cycle is predictable."},{"id":"d","text":"1.8 s is inside the 0.8-2.5 s low-mass range, so it is fine everywhere."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["A ground-plane pin is a heat sink: the plane takes heat away from the joint.","The high-mass band starts above the low-mass one.","Two passes spread heat without overheating the neighbours."],"feedbackCorrect":"High-mass joints run 1.5-4.0 s, and two shorter passes beat one long dwell.","feedbackWrong":"Which direction does a thermal plane move the dwell?","explanation":"A pin tied into an internal plane conducts heat away from the joint as fast as the nozzle delivers it, so the same 1.8 s that fills a signal pin leaves a ground-plane pin below wetting temperature. The course's high-mass band starts at 1.5 s and runs to 4.0 s, and it recommends two shorter passes rather than one long one, because two passes let heat spread into the plane between contacts without holding the neighbouring components at temperature. A single dwell table for every joint is a table that is wrong for at least one group."},{"id":"s10","type":"choice","prompt":"What has to be in the selective program record before this run is released?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A photograph of the first article."},{"id":"b","text":"The nozzle size, the bath temperature and the board part number."},{"id":"c","text":"The nozzle size and shape per joint group, the dwell per group, the pass strategy, the flux volume and the air-knife settings, the preheat, the measured cycle, the joint temperatures from the thermocouples, and the first-article result against IPC-A-610 for the class."},{"id":"d","text":"The machine's exported program file."}],"answer":{"value":"c"},"hints":["A record is judged by whether the program can be rebuilt and defended.","Geometry, dwell, passes, flux, knife, preheat, cycle, temperatures, acceptance.","The program file holds the settings; it does not hold the evidence they work."],"feedbackCorrect":"Nozzle and shape per group, dwell per group, passes, flux, knife, preheat, measured cycle, joint temperatures and the first-article result.","feedbackWrong":"Which of these would let someone else re-run this program on another machine and know whether it worked?","explanation":"The exported program file is a set of coordinates and numbers with no evidence attached: it cannot say whether 1.8 s heats this joint to wetting temperature on this machine, and it will not transfer to a second machine with different thermal behaviour. The thermocouple readings are what turn a dwell into a claim, the air-knife and flux settings are what make the wetting repeatable, and the acceptance line against IPC-A-610 is what makes the result a judgement rather than an impression. Geometry per joint group matters because the groups have different thermal mass and one dwell table cannot serve both."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Annular gap computed at nominal and at the worst tolerance case","points":20,"steps":["s1","s2"]},{"criterion":"Nozzle judged against this joint, not against the course range","points":20,"steps":["s3"]},{"criterion":"Cycle time built from every block and converted to throughput","points":20,"steps":["s4","s5","s6"]},{"criterion":"Throughput gain priced against the air knife's function","points":15,"steps":["s7"]},{"criterion":"One variable changed, the one that matches the defect mechanism","points":10,"steps":["s8"]},{"criterion":"High-mass joints given their own dwell or pass strategy","points":10,"steps":["s9"]},{"criterion":"Program record carries the evidence, not just the settings","points":5,"steps":["s10"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"selective-program","name":"Selective soldering program sheet","status":"available","use":"Keep the nozzle, dwell, passes, flux, air knife and preheat beside the measured cycle, so a program can be re-run on another machine and still be judged against the joint temperature it produced.","lesson":"m10l04","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"selective-program-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"section,value\r\n,\r\n,\r\n,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"selective-program-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"section,value\njoints,20\ndwell_s,1.8\nmove_s,0.4\npreheat_s,25\nflux_knife_s,12\nnozzle_id_mm,1.20\nnozzle_min_for_joint_mm,1.0\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"selective-program-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# Selective soldering program sheet\n\n## What this template is for\nKeep the nozzle, dwell, passes, flux, air knife and preheat beside the measured cycle, so a program can be re-run on another machine and still be judged against the joint temperature it produced.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per program section. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `section`\n- `value`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m10-01 (Selective soldering: the 20-pin connector, a nozzle that fits and a cycle that does not), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m10l04 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"Nozzle strategy and dwell (lesson m10l02)","href":"#/l/m10l02"},{"label":"Programming a run (lesson m10l04)","href":"#/l/m10l04"},{"label":"Defects and validation (lesson m10l05)","href":"#/l/m10l05"}],"sources":["src-016","src-001"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py"},{"caseId":"cx-m13-01","moduleId":"m13","title":"Coverage: 92 % of the nodes and 70 % of the defects","estMinutes":40,"level":"advanced","scenario":["Board X leaves the line with three screens in series: AOI, in-circuit test and functional test. The test list shows 570 of the board's 620 joints on a probe or a scan cell, which the program reports as 92 % coverage, and the line quotes that number to customers.","A field return comes back with flux residue under a connector and no electrical symptom in the factory. Management asks for a fourth screen; engineering says the four screens together cannot catch that class and the money is better spent elsewhere.","The defect model is in the audit file: eight classes, their occurrence in ppm, and the detection probability of each screen against each class. Work out what the current strategy actually catches, what a fourth screen would add, and what has to be controlled some other way."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"coverage-model.csv","kind":"csv","content":"defect_class,occurrence_ppm,aoi_prob,xray_prob,ict_prob,fct_prob\nvisible open,420,0.95,0.00,0.99,0.85\nhidden open,260,0.05,0.85,0.60,0.70\nvisible bridge,310,0.97,0.00,0.99,0.90\nhidden short,90,0.10,0.80,0.55,0.60\nwrong value,60,0.15,0.00,0.95,0.50\npolarity,110,0.90,0.00,0.60,0.80\ninsufficient solder,380,0.92,0.30,0.35,0.40\ncontamination,70,0.10,0.00,0.02,0.05\n","note":"Detection probability is the chance that a screen flags a defect of that class, from this plant's test logs. Classes are assumed independent, which is the model's main weakness and is stated on the case page."},{"name":"test-strategy.csv","kind":"csv","content":"item,value\nnodes_total,620\nnodes_covered,570\nscreens_in_series,aoi ict fct\nboards_per_year,20000\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"The board has 620 joints and 570 are on a probe or a scan cell. What node coverage is that, in percent? One decimal.","answer":{"value":91.9,"tolerance":0.2,"unit":"%"},"hints":["Node coverage counts joints that a method can reach, not defects it can find.","570 / 620.","0.9194, so 91.9 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"91.9 % of the joints — the number on the test list.","feedbackWrong":"Divide the covered joints by the total, not by the defect classes.","explanation":"570 / 620 = 91.9 %. That is a real and useful number: it says 50 joints, mostly under the BGA and on the connector, have no direct electrical access. What it does not say is anything about defects. Node coverage counts reachable joints; defect coverage counts likely failures a method can detect, and the two differ by a factor that this case is about to compute."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"Sum the occurrence column of the defect model. What is the total defect rate, in ppm?","answer":{"value":1700,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"ppm"},"hints":["Add all eight classes.","420 + 260 + 310 + 90 + 60 + 110 + 380 + 70.","1,700 ppm."],"feedbackCorrect":"1,700 ppm across eight classes — that is the population every number below removes from.","feedbackWrong":"Sum the whole column; every later step divides by it.","explanation":"The eight classes add to 1,700 ppm, so about 1.7 defects per thousand boards before anything is screened. Each class has its own occurrence and its own detection probability, which is why a single coverage number is misleading: the classes that occur most are not the classes that are caught best, and the strategy's real performance is a weighted average over this column."},{"id":"s3","type":"numeric","prompt":"AOI runs alone. What defect coverage does it give, in percent of the 1,700 ppm?","answer":{"value":69.7824,"tolerance":0.3,"unit":"%"},"hints":["For each class, coverage is occurrence x AOI's probability for that class.","AOI sees the visible classes well and the hidden ones almost not at all.","Sum the caught occurrence and divide by 1,700."],"feedbackCorrect":"69.8 % — and the classes it misses are the expensive ones.","feedbackWrong":"Weight each class by its occurrence before adding the coverage up.","explanation":"AOI catches visible opens and bridges at over 95 % and hidden opens at 5 %, so the weighted coverage comes to 69.8 % of 1,700 ppm, or 514 ppm of escape. The number is not low because AOI is a bad machine; it is low because AOI is an optical method and most of the defect mass it misses is under a package. A strategy quoted at 70 % coverage is quoting which defects it catches, not how many boards it saves."},{"id":"s4","type":"numeric","prompt":"X-ray is added in series after AOI. What is the combined coverage, in percent?","answer":{"value":86.4806,"tolerance":0.3,"unit":"%"},"hints":["A defect escapes only if both screens miss it.","Multiply the two miss probabilities per class, then weight by occurrence.","The hidden classes are where X-ray earns its place."],"feedbackCorrect":"86.5 %: X-ray buys 17 points, almost all of them hidden opens and shorts.","feedbackWrong":"Combine by multiplying the misses, not by adding the hits.","explanation":"With AOI and X-ray in series the coverage rises from 69.8 % to 86.5 %, and the escape falls from 514 to 230 ppm. Almost the entire gain comes from two classes — hidden open and hidden short — where X-ray lifts detection from 5 % and 10 % to above 85 %. X-ray does nothing for visible opens, bridging, polarity or wrong value, because AOI already had those. Two screens in series are worth their cost only when they see different things; the arithmetic says exactly how much of that is true here."},{"id":"s5","type":"numeric","prompt":"Out of curiosity the plant looks at AOI followed by ICT — no X-ray, which is the arrangement it can afford today. What coverage would that give, in percent?","answer":{"value":86.8234,"tolerance":0.3,"unit":"%"},"hints":["Same series combination, different second screen.","ICT is strong on structure and weak on visible solder volume.","Compare the result with the X-ray arrangement of the previous step."],"feedbackCorrect":"86.8 %, marginally better than X-ray, and it reaches different classes.","feedbackWrong":"Multiply the misses per class, then weight.","explanation":"AOI plus ICT gives 86.8 % against AOI plus X-ray at 86.5 % — a difference of a third of one percent, which is exactly the kind of coincidence that misleads a purchasing decision. The two arrangements reach the same total by covering different classes: ICT also catches wrong values at 95 % and hidden opens at 60 %, while X-ray takes hidden opens to 85 % and hidden shorts to 80 %. When two strategies score the same, the classes they leave uncovered decide between them, not the score."},{"id":"s6","type":"numeric","prompt":"The current line runs AOI, ICT and functional test in series. What is its defect coverage, in percent?","answer":{"value":93.1221,"tolerance":0.3,"unit":"%"},"hints":["Three screens, so three miss factors per class.","Functional test earns its place on polarity and on the classes that break behaviour.","The residual on the contamination class will not move at all."],"feedbackCorrect":"93.1 % — the last 7 points are the ones nothing catches.","feedbackWrong":"Three factors per class; do not forget to weight by occurrence.","explanation":"AOI, ICT and functional test together reach 93.1 %, leaving 117 ppm. The remaining escape is not spread evenly: contamination contributes 59 of those 117 ppm, hidden opens 30 and hidden shorts 15, while visible opens and bridges are effectively gone at 0.03 and 0.01 ppm. A coverage figure near the top of its range feels like the end of the story and is really a list of the three classes still exposed."},{"id":"s7","type":"numeric","prompt":"Adding X-ray as a fourth screen raises coverage from 93.1 % to 95.5 %. What reduction in the escape does that buy, in percent? One decimal.","answer":{"value":34.6,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"%"},"hints":["Convert both coverages to escapes before comparing them.","93.1 % leaves 117 ppm; 95.5 % leaves 76.5 ppm.","(117 - 76.5) / 117."],"feedbackCorrect":"34.6 % fewer escapes — 2.4 coverage points is a third of what was getting through.","feedbackWrong":"Compare escapes with escapes: the coverage points are the input, not the answer.","explanation":"2.4 coverage points sound like a rounding error and are a third of the escape: 117 ppm becomes 76.5 ppm, a 34.6 % reduction. Coverage is a share of the whole defect population and escape is its complement, so near the top of the range small moves in coverage are large in relative terms. The same arithmetic runs the other way: to a line with no screens at all, the first screen is worth far more than the fourth, which is why the order of investment is not a matter of taste."},{"id":"s8","type":"choice","prompt":"The field return was flux residue under a connector with no electrical symptom. What does the model say?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Contamination is detected at 2-10 % by every screen in the list, so no fourth electrical or optical screen fixes it: it is controlled by cleaning, ionic testing and process control, not by adding test."},{"id":"b","text":"The return was a one-off and the model does not apply to it."},{"id":"c","text":"Functional test would have caught it if the test coverage were raised."},{"id":"d","text":"X-ray would have caught it, so the fourth screen should be X-ray."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["Look up the contamination row before arguing about screens.","The highest probability in that row is AOI at 10 %.","Some defect classes have no electrical or optical signature worth screening for."],"feedbackCorrect":"Contamination sits at 2-10 % detection across all four screens; it is controlled by cleaning and ionic testing, not by another screen.","feedbackWrong":"Which row of the model does the returned defect belong to?","explanation":"The contamination row gives 10 % to AOI, 0 % to X-ray, 2 % to ICT and 5 % to functional test. Four screens in series still leave 79 % of that class in place, and it contributes 59 ppm of the 76.5 the whole strategy leaves — three quarters of the remaining escape. Adding a fifth electrical screen changes almost nothing. The control for this class is the one m12 teaches: cleaning process, ionic testing and residue limits, with the test strategy telling you that the class exists rather than catching it."},{"id":"s9","type":"numeric","prompt":"At 20,000 boards a year, how many boards ship with a defect the current three-screen strategy did not catch?","answer":{"value":2.3385,"tolerance":1,"unit":"boards"},"hints":["The escape is in ppm; boards are the count.","The three-screen escape is 117 ppm.","20,000 x 117 / 1,000,000."],"feedbackCorrect":"About 2.3 boards a year — and about 1.2 of them are contamination.","feedbackWrong":"ppm means per million boards, so scale by the annual volume.","explanation":"20,000 x 117 / 1,000,000 = 2.3 boards a year. That is the number a field-return conversation should start from: not the coverage percentage and not the ppm, but how many units a year the strategy knowingly ships. Of those 2.3, about 1.2 carry the contamination class that no screen catches, which is the case for spending the next dollar on cleaning control rather than on a fifth inspection station."},{"id":"s10","type":"choice","prompt":"What has to accompany the strategy when it is documented?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The list of test machines and their purchase dates."},{"id":"b","text":"The first-pass yield target."},{"id":"c","text":"The coverage percentage, so the customer can compare suppliers."},{"id":"d","text":"The defect model with its sources and date, the classes the strategy leaves uncovered with their escape in ppm, the compensating controls for those classes, and the assumption behind the arithmetic — independent classes and probabilities from this plant's own logs."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["A coverage number without its model cannot be re-judged or compared.","The uncovered classes are the finding, not the shortfall.","The independence assumption is a real limit on the answer."],"feedbackCorrect":"The model, its sources and date, the uncovered classes with their escape and their compensating controls, and the assumption the arithmetic rests on.","feedbackWrong":"Which of these would let a second engineer challenge the conclusion, or reuse it in two years?","explanation":"A single coverage percentage is unfalsifiable: it cannot be recomputed, compared with another plant's, or revisited when the product changes. The model is what can be argued with, and the uncovered classes are the finding — a strategy is defined by what it does not catch. The independence assumption matters because in reality AOI and X-ray on the same void are correlated, so the combined coverage here is optimistic in some classes and pessimistic in others; printing the assumption is what lets the next engineer correct it instead of trusting the number."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Node coverage computed and distinguished from defect coverage","points":10,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"Defect model totalled and used as the denominator","points":10,"steps":["s2"]},{"criterion":"Series combination done by multiplying misses, per class","points":20,"steps":["s3","s4"]},{"criterion":"Two strategies that score the same separated by their uncovered classes","points":15,"steps":["s5"]},{"criterion":"Current strategy's residual escape attributed by class","points":15,"steps":["s6"]},{"criterion":"Marginal screen priced in escape reduction, not in coverage points","points":10,"steps":["s7"]},{"criterion":"Uncatchable class identified and handed to a non-test control","points":10,"steps":["s8"]},{"criterion":"Escape converted to boards a year, and the record states the model","points":10,"steps":["s9","s10"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"coverage-model","name":"Coverage model sheet","status":"available","use":"Keep the defect classes, their occurrence and each screen's detection probability on one sheet, so a coverage claim can be recomputed instead of quoted, and the uncovered classes carry a named control.","lesson":"m13l05","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"coverage-model-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"defect_class,occurrence_ppm,aoi_prob,xray_prob,ict_prob,fct_prob\r\n,,,,,\r\n,,,,,\r\n,,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"coverage-model-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"defect_class,occurrence_ppm,aoi_prob,xray_prob,ict_prob,fct_prob\nvisible open,420,0.95,0.00,0.99,0.85\nhidden open,260,0.05,0.85,0.60,0.70\nvisible bridge,310,0.97,0.00,0.99,0.90\nhidden short,90,0.10,0.80,0.55,0.60\nwrong value,60,0.15,0.00,0.95,0.50\npolarity,110,0.90,0.00,0.60,0.80\ninsufficient solder,380,0.92,0.30,0.35,0.40\ncontamination,70,0.10,0.00,0.02,0.05\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"coverage-model-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# Coverage model sheet\n\n## What this template is for\nKeep the defect classes, their occurrence and each screen's detection probability on one sheet, so a coverage claim can be recomputed instead of quoted, and the uncovered classes carry a named control.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per defect class. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `defect_class`\n- `occurrence_ppm`\n- `aoi_prob`\n- `xray_prob`\n- `ict_prob`\n- `fct_prob`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m13-01 (Coverage: 92 % of the nodes and 70 % of the defects), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m13l05 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"What each test level catches (lesson m13l01)","href":"#/l/m13l01"},{"label":"Coverage math (lesson m13l05)","href":"#/l/m13l05"},{"label":"Choosing a strategy (lesson m13l06)","href":"#/l/m13l06"}],"sources":["src-001","src-002"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py"},{"caseId":"cx-m12-01","moduleId":"m12","title":"Cleanliness: the board average passes and the site under the BGA fails","estMinutes":40,"level":"advanced","scenario":["Board X is cleaned and then conformally coated. This build is for a medical customer whose specification carries its own ionic limit rather than the reference value. The cleanliness gate is ROSE per IPC-TM-650 2.3.25, reported in micrograms of sodium chloride equivalent per square centimetre.","The line reports one number per board: total ionic contamination divided by the board area. Six boards from the run are in the extraction log. The process study says the line runs at 1.00 ug/cm2 with a standard deviation of 0.11, which sits comfortably inside the 1.56 reference limit — so the number has been treated as healthy for a year.","A field return from this build came back corroded under the BGA, where the cleaning solution never reached. This case is about the three things one number per board hides: the area it was divided by, the blank it was not subtracted from, and the local site it averaged away."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"cleanliness.csv","kind":"csv","content":"board,extracted_ug,blank_ug,area_cm2,method,limit_ug_cm2,limit_source,site_ug,site_area_cm2\nC-114,412,6,192,ROSE 2.3.25,0.5,medical customer spec,12.0,4.0\nC-118,268,6,192,ROSE 2.3.25,0.5,medical customer spec,,\nC-121,171.1,6,192,ROSE 2.3.25,0.5,medical customer spec,,\nC-124,583,6,192,ROSE 2.3.25,0.5,medical customer spec,,\nC-127,96,6,192,ROSE 2.3.25,0.5,medical customer spec,,\nC-131,149,6,192,ROSE 2.3.25,0.5,medical customer spec,,\n","note":"One row per board, whole-board extraction. The site columns are the separate local test on C-114: a 20 x 20 mm area under the BGA, extracted on its own."},{"name":"cleanliness-study.csv","kind":"csv","content":"item,value\nprocess_mean_ug_cm2,1.00\nprocess_sd_ug_cm2,0.11\nreference_limit_ug_cm2,1.56\ncustomer_limit_ug_cm2,0.50\nmask_connector_cm2,16.0\nmask_test_points_cm2,3.0\nmask_bga_cm2,0.377\ncoating_solids_fraction,0.38\ntarget_dry_film_um,35\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"Board C-114 extracted 412 ug of sodium chloride equivalent into the test solvent. Subtract the 6 ug method blank and divide by the 192 cm2 board area. What is the result, in ug/cm2? Two decimals.","answer":{"value":2.11,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"ug/cm2"},"hints":["The blank is the solvent, the container and the handling, not the board.","(412 - 6) / 192.","406 / 192 = 2.11 ug/cm2."],"feedbackCorrect":"2.11 ug/cm2 — four times the customer's limit, and the blank was worth 3 % of it.","feedbackWrong":"Subtract the blank first, then divide by the board area.","explanation":"406 ug over 192 cm2 is 2.11 ug/cm2 of sodium chloride equivalent. The blank looks like bookkeeping and is not: at a 0.5 ug/cm2 limit the 6 ug blank is worth 0.03 ug/cm2, and on a small board it can be worth more than the margin. Done the other way round — 412 / 192 — the answer is 2.15, a 2 % error here and a much larger one on a 30 cm2 assembly. A ROSE number without its area and its blank is not a measurement, it is a reading."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"This customer's specification sets 0.5 ug/cm2, not the 1.56 reference value. What is one board allowed to carry in total, in micrograms, on its 192 cm2?","answer":{"value":96,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"ug"},"hints":["The limit is per square centimetre, so multiply it by the area.","0.5 x 192.","96 ug for the whole board."],"feedbackCorrect":"96 ug for the whole board — C-114 brought 406 ug net into the cell.","feedbackWrong":"Multiply the per-area limit by the area; do not compare a total with a per-area limit.","explanation":"0.5 ug/cm2 on 192 cm2 allows 96 ug in the extraction cell; C-114 came in with 406 ug net. The same liquid that fails this board would pass it against the reference 1.56, which allows 299.5 ug, so the customer specification is not a formality — it is the limit that decides. A per-area limit and a total are different numbers, and the line compares them with each other more often than anyone would like to believe."},{"id":"s3","type":"choice","prompt":"The customer's spec says 0.5 ug/cm2, the reference value is 1.56, and the twelve study boards sit at 1.00. Which statement is right?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The boards are acceptable because the average is below the reference limit."},{"id":"b","text":"The 1.56 value is a legal maximum, so a customer cannot ask for less."},{"id":"c","text":"The boards pass, because 1.00 is below the 1.56 reference value and that is the limit that applies."},{"id":"d","text":"The boards fail the customer's 0.5 and would pass the reference 1.56; the customer limit governs, so the process is not in control for this build."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["The lesson treats 1.56 as the default that applies when nobody specified anything.","1.00 against 0.5 is a fail; 1.00 against 1.56 is a pass.","Decide which limit governs before deciding pass or fail."],"feedbackCorrect":"1.00 fails the 0.5 customer limit and passes the 1.56 reference — the customer limit governs, so this process is not capable for this build.","feedbackWrong":"One measurement cannot be compared with two limits and accepted if it clears either of them.","explanation":"1.56 ug/cm2 is what applies when nothing tighter has been specified; the lesson lists 1.0, 0.75 and 0.5 as the tiers customers write for telecom, aerospace and medical work. Here the customer wrote 0.5, so 0.5 governs. A process mean of 1.00 sits at twice the allowed level, and the whole distribution is above it — this is not a marginal call but a process that was never qualified against the limit it is measured against. That is the expensive form of the mistake: a line that has been passing itself against the wrong number, on paper, for a year."},{"id":"s4","type":"choice","prompt":"The line manager calls C-114 an outlier: one board at 2.11 against a mean of 1.00. What does the C-114 data actually show?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Not an outlier but what a whole-board average does to a local pocket: the 20 x 20 mm site under the BGA extracted 12 ug on its own, which is 3.00 ug/cm2 on the site while the rest of the board sits near 0.5."},{"id":"b","text":"An invalid site test, because ROSE is only defined per whole board."},{"id":"c","text":"A board to reclean and retest, with no further action."},{"id":"d","text":"An outlier, to be reported as an excursion, with the process left alone."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["Work the site out: 12 ug on 4 cm2.","Divide the whole-board result by 192 and it cannot see a 4 cm2 pocket.","A residue pocket is hidden by the area it is averaged over."],"feedbackCorrect":"3.00 ug/cm2 on the site against 2.11 for the board — the residue is a pocket under the BGA, and the average is the wrong instrument.","feedbackWrong":"Divide the site result by the site area, then compare it with the board average.","explanation":"12 ug on 4 cm2 is 3.00 ug/cm2 at the site. If the rest of the board sits near 0.5, then 4 cm2 out of 192 is producing almost the entire whole-board figure of 2.11 — which is exactly how a wash that cannot reach under a 256-ball BGA looks in a number that passes. The lesson is direct about it: averaging hides a local residue pocket, so high-risk areas are tested separately. The dangerous version of this is not the board that fails, it is the board whose average passes while a site fails."},{"id":"s5","type":"numeric","prompt":"C-121 extracted 171.1 ug with the same 6 ug blank and the same 192 cm2. What is its result, in ug/cm2? Two decimals.","answer":{"value":0.86,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"ug/cm2"},"hints":["Same blank, same area; only the extracted mass changes.","(171.1 - 6) / 192.","165.1 / 192 = 0.86 ug/cm2."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.86 ug/cm2 — 72 % over the customer limit and comfortably inside the reference.","feedbackWrong":"Subtract the blank, then divide by 192.","explanation":"0.86 ug/cm2 is the most instructive number in the case. Against the reference 1.56 this board passes with 45 % of the allowance unused; against the customer's 0.5 it is 72 % over. Same measurement, same instrument, opposite decisions, and the only difference is which limit the line writes on the report. A cleanliness result quoted without its limit is not a result."},{"id":"s6","type":"numeric","prompt":"The process runs at 1.00 ug/cm2 with a standard deviation of 0.11. To have no more than 0.1 % of boards above the customer's 0.50 limit, what mean would the process have to hold, in ug/cm2? Two decimals.","answer":{"value":0.16,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"ug/cm2"},"hints":["Work back from the limit: 0.1 % in one tail is 3.09 standard deviations.","needed mean = 0.50 - 3.09 x 0.11.","0.50 - 0.34 = 0.16 ug/cm2."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.16 ug/cm2 — the process is at 1.00, six times the mean it would have to hold.","feedbackWrong":"Subtract the allowance from the limit; a wide spread does not become acceptable because the mean is high.","explanation":"A one-sided 0.1 % tail sits 3.09 standard deviations below the limit, so the mean would have to hold 0.50 - 3.09 x 0.11 = 0.16 ug/cm2. The process is at 1.00, six times that. This is the number that turns a complaint into a project: the gap is not closed by better sampling or a stricter test, it is closed by cleaning chemistry, rinse quality, bath life and the fixtures that stop the wash reaching under the BGA. Reporting the study mean against the reference limit, for a customer whose limit is a third of it, is how a process spends a year looking healthy while every board it ships is out of specification."},{"id":"s7","type":"choice","prompt":"A board extracts 198 ug and passes ROSE, and a 20 x 20 mm site under a connector extracts nothing. Which result should block the coating?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Visual inspection, because neither test measures what blocks coating."},{"id":"b","text":"The whole-board result: 198 ug less the 6 ug blank is 192 ug over 192 cm2, which is 1.00 ug/cm2 against this customer's 0.5."},{"id":"c","text":"Neither: ROSE is a gate and both results are under their limits."},{"id":"d","text":"The whole-board result is an artefact of the blank and should be corrected upwards to 1.03, a pass against the reference."}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["Do the arithmetic on 198 ug before comparing anything.","(198 - 6) / 192 = 1.00, exactly twice the customer's limit.","A clean site does not rescue the rest of the board."],"feedbackCorrect":"The whole-board result is 1.00 after the blank, twice the customer limit, and it blocks the coating whatever the site shows.","feedbackWrong":"Compare 1.00 with 0.5, not with 1.56, and remember that a clean site is evidence about 4 cm2 only.","explanation":"(198 - 6) / 192 = 1.00 ug/cm2 exactly, twice this customer's limit, so the board fails and coating it would seal the residue in. The site is clean because the residue is somewhere else; a clean site is evidence about 4 cm2 and nothing more. This is the mirror image of C-114 — there the average passed and the site failed, here the site passes and the average fails — and in both cases one number taken alone gives the wrong disposition. The rule that covers both is to test the high-risk sites separately and require every result to be under the governing limit before coating."},{"id":"s8","type":"choice","prompt":"A 90 um wet film is applied and the coating is 38 % solids. What is the dry film, and is it inside a 25-130 um qualification window?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"237 um, because the solids are added rather than multiplied."},{"id":"b","text":"90 um, because the film thickness is what was applied, and that is inside the window."},{"id":"c","text":"34.2 um, which is inside the window but near its floor."},{"id":"d","text":"55.8 um, which is 90 um less the 38 % solids, inside the window."}],"answer":{"value":"c"},"hints":["The solvent leaves during cure; what stays is the solids fraction.","Multiply the wet film by 0.38.","90 x 0.38 = 34.2 um."],"feedbackCorrect":"34.2 um dry from a 90 um wet film — inside the window and close to the floor.","feedbackWrong":"The solids fraction multiplies the wet film; it does not divide it.","explanation":"90 um wet at 38 % solids leaves 34.2 um dry, inside the 25-130 um window and close enough to the floor that a 60 um wet film would fall to 22.8 um and out of it. The window is a qualification range, not a target, and the criterion that matters for protection is a minimum. Wet-film measurement before cure is the only cheap chance to catch this: after cure the thickness is measured destructively on coupons, so the trend across coupons matters more than any single reading."},{"id":"s9","type":"numeric","prompt":"Coating is applied at 35 um dry over the area that is not masked: 192 cm2 less 16.0 for the connector, 3.0 for the test points and 0.377 under the BGA. At 38 % solids, what wet volume has to be dispensed, in millilitres? Two decimals.","answer":{"value":1.59,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"mL"},"hints":["Coated area = 192 - 19.377 cm2.","Dry volume = area x thickness, with 35 um written as 0.0035 cm.","Wet volume = dry volume / 0.38, and 1 cm3 = 1 mL."],"feedbackCorrect":"1.59 mL of wet coating — the number the dispenser program needs.","feedbackWrong":"Divide the dry volume by the solids fraction; multiplying gives the solids, not the liquid.","explanation":"The coated area is 172.623 cm2, the dry film volume 0.604 cm3, and at 38 % solids the wet volume is 1.59 mL. A program that dispenses 0.60 mL paints a third of the board. The masked area is the part that is easy to get wrong, because it is not just the connector: the BGA needs masking of its own, and the test points are the area nobody remembers until the fixture cannot probe the board. That is what coating work really turns on — the areas that must stay bare decide the area that gets covered, and the volume follows."},{"id":"s10","type":"choice","prompt":"The customer asks for the evidence that this build is clean and coated. What is the package?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The ROSE result per board, because the pass limit is the whole criterion."},{"id":"b","text":"Per board: the extracted mass, the blank, the area, the method, the limit with its source, and the site results for the high-risk areas; plus coating thickness on coupons with the wet-to-dry arithmetic, the masked areas, and the disposition of the boards that failed."},{"id":"c","text":"A certificate stating that the process is qualified to IPC-CC-830."},{"id":"d","text":"The ion chromatography results, because they identify the ions and are therefore stricter than ROSE."}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["Ask what a second engineer would need in order to recompute a decision.","The limit, its source, the area and the blank are all part of the number.","The disposition of the failures is evidence too, not an admission."],"feedbackCorrect":"The record that carries its area, blank, limit and source, the local sites, the coating arithmetic and the failure dispositions is the one that can be audited.","feedbackWrong":"A certificate and a single number both describe the process; the record has to let somebody check it.","explanation":"The record has to answer the questions the numbers raise: which area the result applies to, which blank was subtracted, which limit governs and where it came from, what the high-risk sites measured on their own, how the coating thickness was derived from the wet film, which areas were masked, and what happened to the boards that failed. IPC-CC-830 qualifies a coating material and 2.3.25 defines the test; neither tells this customer whether this build is acceptable, and that judgement is what the package has to carry."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"ROSE result computed with the blank and the area, not from the raw extract","points":15,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"A per-area limit converted to a total for the board in question","points":10,"steps":["s2"]},{"criterion":"The governing limit identified among reference, customer and process mean","points":15,"steps":["s3"]},{"criterion":"Local site computed and the board average shown to hide it","points":10,"steps":["s4"]},{"criterion":"One measurement against both limits, and the decision shown to depend on which is written","points":15,"steps":["s5"]},{"criterion":"Process capability worked from the distribution, not the mean","points":10,"steps":["s6"]},{"criterion":"A clean site and a failing average both resolved to a disposition","points":10,"steps":["s7"]},{"criterion":"Wet-to-dry film arithmetic and the coating volume for the unmasked area","points":10,"steps":["s8","s9"]},{"criterion":"Evidence package carries area, blank, limit source, sites, coating arithmetic and dispositions","points":5,"steps":["s10"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"cleanliness-record","name":"Cleanliness and coating record","status":"available","use":"Report a cleanliness result with its area and its blank, the local site measured separately from the board average, the limit it was compared with and where that limit came from, so a pass cannot be quoted without the arithmetic behind it.","lesson":"m12l03","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"cleanliness-record-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"board,extracted_ug,blank_ug,area_cm2,method,limit_ug_cm2,limit_source,site_ug,site_area_cm2\r\n,,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"cleanliness-record-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"board,extracted_ug,blank_ug,area_cm2,method,limit_ug_cm2,limit_source,site_ug,site_area_cm2\nC-114,412,6,192,ROSE 2.3.25,0.5,medical customer spec,12.0,4.0\nC-118,268,6,192,ROSE 2.3.25,0.5,medical customer spec,,\nC-121,171.1,6,192,ROSE 2.3.25,0.5,medical customer spec,,\nC-124,583,6,192,ROSE 2.3.25,0.5,medical customer spec,,\nC-127,96,6,192,ROSE 2.3.25,0.5,medical customer spec,,\nC-131,149,6,192,ROSE 2.3.25,0.5,medical customer spec,,\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"cleanliness-record-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# Cleanliness and coating record\n\n## What this template is for\nReport a cleanliness result with its area and its blank, the local site measured separately from the board average, the limit it was compared with and where that limit came from, so a pass cannot be quoted without the arithmetic behind it.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per board. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `board`\n- `extracted_ug`\n- `blank_ug`\n- `area_cm2`\n- `method`\n- `limit_ug_cm2`\n- `limit_source`\n- `site_ug`\n- `site_area_cm2`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m12-01 (Cleanliness: the board average passes and the site under the BGA fails), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m12l03 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"What residue does to a board (lesson m12l01)","href":"#/l/m12l01"},{"label":"ROSE, ion chromatography, particles (lesson m12l03)","href":"#/l/m12l03"},{"label":"Coating application and masking (lesson m12l05)","href":"#/l/m12l05"}],"sources":["src-002","src-013","src-010"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py"},{"caseId":"cx-m15-01","moduleId":"m15","title":"Design feedback: a rule with a number, a pack with a cost","estMinutes":45,"level":"advanced","scenario":["Board X has run a production order of 1,240 boards for a year with the same placement problem the m05 case found: a rotation between the two fiducials that shows up as toe overhang on one side of the panel and as a rotated 0402 near the panel edge. The line compensates every shift and calls it a process problem.","The quality manager wants a complaint sent to the design team. The design team has three open questions and no data. Nothing this plant has written so far names a dimension, a tolerance, an owner or a cost, so nothing has been acted on — and the pack that would carry those things has never been assembled.","This case is the closing one. It produces two artefacts: a design rule with a number in it, and an evidence pack whose cost arithmetic an auditor can redo. Both come from measurements the other thirteen cases already made."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"production-pack.csv","kind":"csv","content":"item,value\nboards_started,1240\ndefects_confirmed,38\nboards_reworked,26\nboards_scrapped,12\ndpm_opportunities_per_board,620\nrework_min_per_defect,9\nlabour_rate_per_h,45\nboard_cost_usd,180\ndowntime_h,6.5\ndowntime_rate_per_h,40\ninspection_min_per_board,0.5\nstencil_and_fiducial_change_usd,1200\nexpected_defect_reduction,0.60\n","note":"One production order, one line, one product. The defect count is confirmed defects, not AOI calls: the m07 case is the reason that distinction is written down here."},{"name":"placement-facts.csv","kind":"csv","content":"item,value\nworst_fiducial_residual_mm,0.14\nfiducial_separation_mm,10.0\npanel_edge_part_radius_mm,40.0\nplacement_acceptance_mm,0.15\nboard_layers,6\nboard_diagonal_mm,200\n","note":"The placement numbers are the m05 audit's; the board ones are the same facts every other case reads."},{"name":"image-log.csv","kind":"csv","content":"image,view,field_of_view_mm,image_width_px,scale_bar_mm,what_it_shows\nIMG-0412,wide,40.0,1600,5.0,QFP-64 right-hand leads with toe overhang\nIMG-0413,close,8.0,1200,1.0,QFP-64 corner lead, 0.30 mm pad visible\nIMG-0414,close,8.0,1200,1.0,0402 near the panel edge, rotated\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"The worst fiducial residual is 0.14 mm across the two fiducials, and the part at the panel edge sits 40 mm from their midpoint. To keep that part inside the 0.15 mm acceptance, how far apart must the fiducials be, in millimetres? One decimal.","answer":{"value":37.3,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["Residual across two fiducials becomes an angle: residual / separation.","The displacement at radius r is r x that angle.","separation = residual / (acceptance / radius) = 0.14 / (0.15 / 40)."],"feedbackCorrect":"37.3 mm — the board's fiducials are 10 mm apart, which is why the rotation is 0.80 degrees and the edge part moves 0.56 mm.","feedbackWrong":"Solve for the separation, not for the displacement: the acceptance and the radius are both known.","explanation":"0.14 / (0.15 / 40) = 37.3 mm. The rule is that separation and the radius it has to protect are the only two numbers a fabricator controls — the residual is the machine's, the acceptance is the product's. Board X puts its two fiducials 10 mm apart near the middle, so a 0.14 mm residual becomes 0.80 degrees and a part 40 mm out moves 0.56 mm, almost four times the acceptance. Written as a rule it is one sentence with a dimension, a tolerance and a reason: 'place global fiducials at least 37 mm apart, diagonal at the panel corners, because a 0.15 mm residual across the pair must stay under 0.15 mm at the furthest part'. Written as a complaint it is 'placement is unstable', which is what the design team has been receiving."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"The order ran 1,240 boards with 38 confirmed defects. What is the first-pass yield, in percent? Two decimals.","answer":{"value":96.94,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"%"},"hints":["First-pass yield counts boards that passed without rework or scrap.","Boards that did not pass = 38 defects, one per board.","(1240 - 38) / 1240."],"feedbackCorrect":"96.94 % — and the number is a board rate, which is the first thing to fix.","feedbackWrong":"Subtract the affected boards from the started boards, then divide by the started boards.","explanation":"1,202 of 1,240 boards passed first time: 96.94 %. Stated this way the number looks healthy and means very little, because it counts boards, not defects, and Board X has 620 opportunities per board. The same 38 defects are 49.4 DPMO when divided by the opportunity count, and 30.6 defects per thousand boards when divided by the board count. Three numbers, one reality — which is why the pack has to state its denominator before it states a rate."},{"id":"s3","type":"numeric","prompt":"The design team asks how bad it is in the units the line uses. Express the same 38 defects per 1,000 boards. Two decimals.","answer":{"value":30.65,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"per 1000 boards"},"hints":["The denominator is the board count this time, not the opportunity count.","38 / 1240 x 1000.","30.65 per thousand boards."],"feedbackCorrect":"30.6 defects per thousand boards — the same data as 96.94 % yield and 49.4 DPMO.","feedbackWrong":"Multiply by 1,000 after dividing by the boards; do not divide by 620.","explanation":"38 / 1240 x 1000 = 30.65. The four numbers in this case — 96.94 % first-pass yield, 30.65 per thousand boards, 49.4 DPMO and 3.1 % of boards affected — are all the same 38 defects, and each one flatters or damns the line depending on who is reading. The m15 lesson's requirement is that the pack chooses a denominator and states it; the failure mode it prevents is a supplier and a customer arguing about a problem for an hour because each is quoting a different unit for the same fact."},{"id":"s4","type":"numeric","prompt":"Add the four quantified cost drivers of poor quality: rework, scrap, inspection and downtime. What is the total for this order, in USD? Two decimals.","answer":{"value":3141.5,"tolerance":1,"unit":"USD"},"hints":["Rework: 38 defects x 9 min at 45 USD/h.","Scrap: 12 boards at 180 USD. Inspection: 1240 boards x 0.5 min. Downtime: 6.5 h at 40 USD/h.","256.50 + 2160.00 + 465.00 + 260.00."],"feedbackCorrect":"3,141.50 USD for the order — and scrap is 69 % of it.","feedbackWrong":"Add only the four drivers that have a quantity and a unit price; the rest are estimates and belong in a separate line.","explanation":"Rework is 38 x 9 min x 45/60 = 256.50 USD; scrap is 12 x 180 = 2,160; inspection is 1,240 x 0.5 min = 465; downtime is 6.5 h at 40 = 260. The total is 3,141.50 USD, or 2.53 USD per board. Two things matter about that arithmetic and neither is the total. First, scrap is 69 % of the cost and rework is 8 %, so a programme aimed at reducing rework attacks the small number. Second, every driver has a quantity and a price in the pack, so each can be argued with separately — the lesson's rule is to label estimates as estimates and keep them off this line, which is why field failure exposure is mentioned in the narrative and excluded from the sum."},{"id":"s5","type":"numeric","prompt":"The change costs 1,200 USD and is expected to remove 60 % of the rework and scrap. In how many boards does it pay for itself? One decimal.","answer":{"value":1026.3,"tolerance":1,"unit":"boards"},"hints":["Removed per board = 0.60 x (rework + scrap) / boards.","0.60 x (256.50 + 2160.00) = 1,449.90 USD over 1,240 boards.","1,200 / 1.169 = 1,026 boards."],"feedbackCorrect":"1,026 boards — under one production order, which is why the ask is worth sending.","feedbackWrong":"Divide the change cost by the saving per board, not by the saving for the whole order.","explanation":"The change removes 0.60 x (256.50 + 2,160) = 1,449.90 USD across 1,240 boards, or 1.169 USD per board, so 1,200 USD is repaid in 1,026 boards — a single production order at Board X's volume. This is the sentence that decides whether a design request is read at all: not 'placement is unstable' but 'a 1,200 USD change pays back in one order and removes a class of defect that our test catches late'. The same arithmetic can kill an ask, and should — a change costing 40,000 USD to remove 300 USD per order is not a design problem, it is a preference."},{"id":"s6","type":"choice","prompt":"Three asks are drafted. Which one is written as a rule the design team can act on?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Place global fiducials at least 37 mm apart, on the panel diagonal at opposite corners, because a 0.14 mm residual across a 37 mm pair is 0.22 degrees and moves a part at 40 mm by 0.15 mm; verify on the first article with the fiducial audit sheet, pass when every part sits inside 0.15 mm."},{"id":"b","text":"The 0402 near the edge would not rotate if the panel had more breakaway rail."},{"id":"c","text":"IPC-7351 requires fiducials to be placed correctly; the current layout does not comply."},{"id":"d","text":"Placement is unstable at the panel edge; please review the fiducial scheme."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["A rule has a dimension, a tolerance and a pass condition.","It also names the evidence that verifies it.","The others are a complaint, a guess and an appeal to a standard the ask does not actually rest on."],"feedbackCorrect":"Only the second names the dimension, the tolerance, the mechanism, the verification and the pass condition — everything a designer needs to change a file.","feedbackWrong":"Look for a number, a tolerance and a pass condition in the same sentence.","explanation":"The second draft is the only one an engineer can act on without asking a question: 37 mm is a dimension, the diagonal placement is where it goes, the 0.15 mm is the criterion, the mechanism explains why, the audit sheet is the verification and the pass condition is stated. The first is a complaint with no number. The third is a guess — the rotation comes from the fiducial scheme, not from the rail. The fourth is the most common failure of all: IPC-7351 covers land patterns and does not specify fiducial separation, so citing it would invite exactly the reply it deserves, and the whole pack loses credibility with it."},{"id":"s7","type":"choice","prompt":"The images in the pack: what makes them usable as evidence a year later?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A photograph of the whole panel, because context is what matters."},{"id":"b","text":"High resolution, because a good camera settles arguments."},{"id":"c","text":"A scale reference and a stated field of view or magnification, in the file name or caption, with board, station, defect type, date and shift — plus a wide view next to the close-up."},{"id":"d","text":"A colour image, because colour shows flux and corrosion."}],"answer":{"value":"c"},"hints":["An image without a scale cannot be measured by the next person.","The lesson asks for the close-up and the wide view on the same defect.","Metadata in the caption is what makes the file findable."],"feedbackCorrect":"A scale reference, a stated field of view, full identification and both views — that is what turns a photograph into evidence.","feedbackWrong":"Resolution and colour are properties of the camera; evidence needs a scale and an identity.","explanation":"An image is evidence when somebody who was not there can measure it and interpret it: a scale bar or a known dimension, the field of view, and a caption carrying board, panel, station, defect type, date and shift. The pair matters too — the wide view shows which component and which neighbours, the close-up shows the joint — because a close-up alone cannot tell a designer that the defect is on the side of the panel where the rotation is largest. IMG-0413 in this pack is a 8.0 mm field across 1,200 pixels, so 6.67 um per pixel, and the 0.30 mm QFP pad is visible in it as a scale reference of its own."},{"id":"s8","type":"choice","prompt":"One week and 300 boards produced the 38 defects. How should the pack describe that sample?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"As a full population, because a week is a representative sample of a stable process."},{"id":"b","text":"As invalid, so the release should be refused."},{"id":"c","text":"As a stable production sample, because 38 defects is enough to see the mode."},{"id":"d","text":"As preliminary: the lesson asks for 30 consecutive production days or 1,000 boards, and 300 boards over one week meets neither, so the comparison is stated as preliminary and the action is to extend the window."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["Check both conditions in the lesson: 30 days or 1,000 boards.","300 boards is a third of the board count and a quarter of the days.","Preliminary is a description, not a rejection."],"feedbackCorrect":"Preliminary: 300 boards over one week meets neither threshold, so the pack says so and asks for a longer window.","feedbackWrong":"Count the boards and the days against the lesson's two conditions before deciding how strongly to state the rate.","explanation":"The m15 lesson asks for 30 consecutive production days or 1,000 boards, whichever gives a stable sample. Three hundred boards over one week is neither, so the rate is a preliminary estimate and the pack says so — that is a strength, not a weakness, because the alternative is a number presented as stable that a customer will later find was one good week. The rate on 38 defects is also about what the sample can support: at 30 per thousand boards, 300 boards gives roughly nine defects, which can see the mode and cannot rank causes."},{"id":"s9","type":"choice","prompt":"The three candidate asks are: the fiducial separation above; a paste aperture reduction on the QFP-48 (the m04 case's Cpk 0.61); and a request that the customer relax the 75-125 % SPI window. Which belongs in this pack to the design team?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"All three: the pack should carry every improvement the plant has identified."},{"id":"b","text":"The fiducial ask only — it is the one the plant cannot fix itself and that removes a defect class at its source; the aperture is a stencil the plant owns, and the SPI window is a customer specification, not a design parameter."},{"id":"c","text":"The aperture, because Cpk 0.61 is the worst number in the plant."},{"id":"d","text":"The SPI window, because the customer is the only party who can change it."}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["Ask who owns each change and who benefits.","A design feedback pack carries what only the designer can change.","The other two are real problems with different owners."],"feedbackCorrect":"Only the fiducial ask belongs here: the plant owns the stencil and the customer owns the specification, and neither is a design decision.","feedbackWrong":"Sort the three by owner before sorting them by severity.","explanation":"Three real problems, three different owners, and a pack that mixes them gets returned with 'not our scope' stamped on the top item. The fiducial separation is the design team's: it is a layout decision, it removes the rotation class at source, and it costs 1,200 USD to repay in one order. The QFP-48 aperture is the plant's own stencil — it goes to the stencil release, not to the design team, and it is arguably the more urgent of the two. The SPI window is the customer's specification and belongs in a deviation request. Separating them is the work; a pack with everything in it is a pack nobody owns."},{"id":"s10","type":"choice","prompt":"The pack goes out. What has to be in it for the change to be closed later?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The defect photographs and the complaint, because that is what convinces management."},{"id":"b","text":"Problem with its rate and denominator, the affected parts and revisions, the measured evidence with scales and windows, the proposed rule with its dimension and pass condition, the cost arithmetic with its drivers, the verification plan with a sample size, and the post-change data that closes it — plus whether the current activity is containment with an expiry."},{"id":"c","text":"A reference to IPC-A-610 Class 2, because acceptance settles the argument."},{"id":"d","text":"The cost estimate alone, because a design team acts on money."}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["Think about what closes a change rather than what opens it.","Closure needs post-change data against the same metric.","Containment has to be labelled, or it becomes the process."],"feedbackCorrect":"Problem, evidence, rule, cost, verification and the post-change data that closes it — with containment labelled and expiring.","feedbackWrong":"A pack that only argues for a change cannot close it.","explanation":"The lesson's closing requirement is the one most packs omit: the change is closed with post-change data over a stated window — at least 30 production days or 1,000 boards — against the same metric and the same denominator as the problem. Without it, a change is implemented and never verified, and the next engineer inherits a drawing revision nobody can justify. Two smaller items belong in the same list: the revision of every controlled document the change touches, and an explicit label on any temporary containment with its expiry condition, because a containment that is not given an expiry quietly becomes the process."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Design rule solved for the dimension the designer controls","points":15,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"Defect rate stated with its denominator, and the denominator shown to change the story","points":15,"steps":["s2","s3"]},{"criterion":"Cost of poor quality assembled from quantified drivers with unit prices","points":15,"steps":["s4"]},{"criterion":"Payback computed per board, so the ask carries a business case","points":10,"steps":["s5"]},{"criterion":"The ask written as a rule with dimension, tolerance, mechanism, verification and pass condition","points":15,"steps":["s6"]},{"criterion":"Image evidence carries scale, field of view, identity and both views","points":10,"steps":["s7"]},{"criterion":"Sample window judged against the 30-day / 1,000-board requirement","points":5,"steps":["s8"]},{"criterion":"Asks sorted by owner before severity","points":10,"steps":["s9"]},{"criterion":"Closure: post-change data, revisions, containment with expiry","points":5,"steps":["s10"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"design-feedback","name":"Design feedback pack","status":"available","use":"Carry one ask from problem to closure: the rate with its denominator, the rule with its dimension and pass condition, the cost drivers with their prices, the verification sample and the post-change result.","lesson":"m15l03","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"design-feedback-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"item,value\r\n,\r\n,\r\n,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"design-feedback-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"item,value\nboards_started,1240\ndefects_confirmed,38\nboards_reworked,26\nboards_scrapped,12\ndpm_opportunities_per_board,620\nrework_min_per_defect,9\nlabour_rate_per_h,45\nboard_cost_usd,180\ndowntime_h,6.5\ndowntime_rate_per_h,40\ninspection_min_per_board,0.5\nstencil_and_fiducial_change_usd,1200\nexpected_defect_reduction,0.60\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"design-feedback-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# Design feedback pack\n\n## What this template is for\nCarry one ask from problem to closure: the rate with its denominator, the rule with its dimension and pass condition, the cost drivers with their prices, the verification sample and the post-change result.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per ask. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `item`\n- `value`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m15-01 (Design feedback: a rule with a number, a pack with a cost), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m15l03 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"How designers read feedback (lesson m15l01)","href":"#/l/m15l01"},{"label":"The evidence pack (lesson m15l02)","href":"#/l/m15l02"},{"label":"Writing the change request (lesson m15l03)","href":"#/l/m15l03"},{"label":"Closure with post-change data (lesson m15l05)","href":"#/l/m15l05"}],"sources":["src-001","src-011","src-024"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py"},{"caseId":"cx-m02-01","moduleId":"m02","title":"Moisture breach: the BGA reel sat in a 7 % RH cabinet","estMinutes":20,"level":"core","scenario":["Board X's 256-ball BGA comes in on MSL 4 tape and reel. The reel was opened 58 h ago and went straight into the storage dry cabinet. The cabinet log shows 7 % RH for the whole period; the setpoint in the procedure is 5 %.","During the second shift a feeder was loaded and the reel sat on the machine for 6 h before the line went down. Reflow of that build is planned 10 h from now.","Decide the disposition. The lesson is explicit that a cabinet above the dry-storage requirement does not stop the clock, and that a tape-and-reel bake has a hard temperature ceiling."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"msl-log.csv","kind":"csv","content":"reel,part,package,msl,bag_open_h_ago,cabinet_rh_pct,line_exposure_h,reflow_in_h\nR-4412,BGA-256 14x14,FBGA,4,58,7,6,10\nR-4413,QFP-64 0.5 mm,QFP,3,58,7,0,10\nR-4414,0402 100 nF,chip,1,58,7,0,10\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"What is the J-STD-033 floor life for MSL 4, in hours at the reference condition?","answer":{"value":72,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"h"},"hints":["The values in the course run 168 h, 72 h, 48 h, 24 h down the levels.","MSL 4 is the third value in that sequence.","MSL 4 = 72 h."],"feedbackCorrect":"72 h — the number the whole disposition hangs on.","feedbackWrong":"This is a table lookup, not an arithmetic result: MSL 4 is 72 h.","explanation":"MSL 4 has 72 h of floor life at 30 °C / 60 % RH. MSL 3 is 168 h and MSL 5 is 48 h, which is why the levels are always quoted with their value."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"How many hours count against that floor life? Add the bag-open period and the time on the open feeder.","answer":{"value":64,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"h"},"hints":["A 7 % RH cabinet is above the 5 % requirement, so the cabinet time is not suspended.","The 6 h on the feeder is outside the cabinet entirely.","58 + 6 = 64 h."],"feedbackCorrect":"64 h counted — the cabinet gave no credit at 7 % RH.","feedbackWrong":"Every hour counts here: the cabinet was not compliant, so nothing is discounted.","explanation":"A cabinet at 7 % RH is above the 5 % dry-storage requirement, so exposure keeps accumulating; the 6 h on the open feeder adds on top. 58 + 6 = 64 h of a 72 h budget."},{"id":"s3","type":"numeric","prompt":"How much floor life is left for the BGA reel, in hours?","answer":{"value":8,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"h"},"hints":["Subtract the counted exposure from the floor life.","72 h minus 64 h.","8 h remain, against a reflow planned in 10 h."],"feedbackCorrect":"8 h left, and the reflow is 10 h away — the reel cannot simply proceed.","feedbackWrong":"Floor life minus counted exposure, in hours.","explanation":"72 - 64 = 8 h remain, but the planned reflow is 10 h away. The batch is 2 h short, so the decision is bake or reschedule, not release."},{"id":"s4","type":"choice","prompt":"The cabinet logged 7 % RH against a 5 % requirement. What does the course say that does to the clock?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It has no effect either way; only the bag seal matters."},{"id":"b","text":"It suspends the clock, because any dry cabinet below 10 % RH is equivalent to sealed dry pack."},{"id":"c","text":"It slows the clock, but 5 % RH or less is the condition that suspends it, so this storage gave no credit."},{"id":"d","text":"It resets the clock, because the parts were dry when they went in."}],"answer":{"value":"c"},"hints":["The lesson was corrected on exactly this point: 10 % RH is not the requirement.","5 % RH or less is what stops moisture uptake; above it the budget keeps shrinking, just more slowly."],"feedbackCorrect":"7 % RH slows the clock but does not suspend it, so no credit was given.","feedbackWrong":"Look at which relative humidity the lesson calls the requirement, not which is common on a cheap cabinet.","explanation":"Below 5 % RH the device stops gaining moisture, so the exposure clock is suspended. A 10 % cabinet is common and slows the clock rather than stopping it; at 7 % the parts were outside the requirement, so the full 64 h counts. The 10 % form is a mitigation for devices with budget to spare, never a substitute for the requirement."},{"id":"s5","type":"numeric","prompt":"The reel needs a bake. What is the maximum temperature the tape and reel may see, in °C?","answer":{"value":40,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"°C"},"hints":["Carrier tape and cover tape soften and deform when they get hot.","The lesson gives a fixed ceiling for tape and reel, not a range.","40 °C."],"feedbackCorrect":"40 °C — and at 40 °C the bake takes days, not hours.","feedbackWrong":"The value is a fixed ceiling in the lesson, well below the 125 °C tray bake.","explanation":"Tape and reel must not be baked above 40 °C because the carrier and cover tape deform. At that temperature the table runs to days for a thin body, which is why the 40 °C route does not fit a 10 h window."},{"id":"s6","type":"choice","prompt":"The reflow is 10 h away and a 40 °C bake will not finish in time. Which route does the lesson support?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Skip the bake and raise the reflow peak by 10 °C to drive the moisture out."},{"id":"b","text":"Run the reel as-is and inspect the BGA after reflow."},{"id":"c","text":"Bake the reel at 125 °C now and accept some tape deformation."},{"id":"d","text":"De-reel into trays, bake the loose parts at the permitted higher temperature, then re-reel; or move the build inside the remaining floor life."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["Which constraint is physical and which is scheduling?","The tape ceiling is a material limit; the schedule is not. The lesson offers de-reeling and rescheduling.","De-reel to a carrier that tolerates 125 °C, or move the reflow inside the 8 h."],"feedbackCorrect":"De-reel and bake at the higher temperature, or move the build inside the remaining floor life.","feedbackWrong":"The lesson never trades a material limit for a schedule.","explanation":"Baking a reel at 125 °C destroys the carrier tape, and raising the peak adds thermal stress without removing moisture. The two supported routes are de-reeling to a carrier rated for the 125 °C bake, or rescheduling the build inside the 8 h that remain. Reflowing an unbaked MSL 4 batch and inspecting afterwards turns a known risk into a detection gamble."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Floor life for MSL 4 taken from the table, not guessed","points":15,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"Exposure counted with no credit for a non-compliant cabinet","points":20,"steps":["s2"]},{"criterion":"Remaining life computed and compared with the reflow schedule","points":20,"steps":["s3"]},{"criterion":"7 % RH judged against the 5 % requirement","points":15,"steps":["s4"]},{"criterion":"Tape-and-reel bake ceiling known","points":10,"steps":["s5"]},{"criterion":"Route chosen that respects the material limit","points":20,"steps":["s6"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"msl-log","name":"MSL exposure log","status":"available","use":"Track bag-open time, cabinet humidity and line exposure against each package's floor life.","lesson":"m02l04","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"msl-log-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"reel,part,package,msl,bag_open_h_ago,cabinet_rh_pct,line_exposure_h,reflow_in_h\r\n,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"msl-log-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"reel,part,package,msl,bag_open_h_ago,cabinet_rh_pct,line_exposure_h,reflow_in_h\nR-4412,BGA-256 14x14,FBGA,4,58,7,6,10\nR-4413,QFP-64 0.5 mm,QFP,3,58,7,0,10\nR-4414,0402 100 nF,chip,1,58,7,0,10\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"msl-log-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# MSL exposure log\n\n## What this template is for\nTrack bag-open time, cabinet humidity and line exposure against each package's floor life.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per reel. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `reel`\n- `part`\n- `package`\n- `msl`\n- `bag_open_h_ago`\n- `cabinet_rh_pct`\n- `line_exposure_h`\n- `reflow_in_h`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m02-01 (Moisture breach: the BGA reel sat in a 7 % RH cabinet), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m02l04 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"Baking and dry cabinets (lesson m02l04)","href":"#/l/m02l04"},{"label":"Floor-life accounting (lesson m02l03)","href":"#/l/m02l03"}],"sources":["src-003","src-005"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py"},{"caseId":"cx-m06-01","moduleId":"m06","title":"Profile validation: Board X runs a 248 °C peak","estMinutes":25,"level":"core","scenario":["Board X is a 1.6 mm six-layer board with heavy ground planes and a large BGA. The thermal survey from the worst-case site gives the profile in the data file: 25 to 150 °C in 60 s, soak 150 to 190 °C for 90 s, 65 s above 219 °C, peak 248 °C, and cooling from 248 to 100 °C in 40 s.","Paste is SAC305. The paste window for this board is 60-90 s above liquidus (IPC-7530B guidance is 30-90 s; the datasheet narrows it). The peak is judged against a ceiling, not a target: the J-STD-020 classification temperature for a large package at 1.6-2.5 mm is 250 °C, while the course's typical production band is 235-245 °C.","Validate the profile, compute the margins, and decide whether it can be released with a documentation note or must be changed."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"profile.csv","kind":"csv","content":"interval,from_c,to_c,seconds,rate_c_per_s\nramp,25,150,60,\nsoak,150,190,90,\napproach,190,219,30,\nabove_liquidus,219,248,65,\npeak_hold,248,248,4,\ncooling,248,100,40,\n"},{"name":"thermocouples.csv","kind":"csv","note":"One profile, three sites. The middle column is the peak at that thermocouple; the offsets are why a single-point profile flatters the board.","content":"site,location,peak_c,tal_s,offset_from_reference_c,within_paste_band\nTC1,BGA centre joint,242,60,-6,yes\nTC2,QFP lead board edge,249,65,+1,no (over the 245 C top)\nTC3,0402 chip near edge,254,70,+6,no (over the 245 C top)\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"What is the ramp rate from 25 °C to 150 °C, in °C/s?","answer":{"value":2.0833,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"°C/s"},"hints":["Rate is a temperature difference over a time.","(150 - 25) = 125 °C over 60 s.","125 / 60 = 2.08 °C/s."],"feedbackCorrect":"2.08 °C/s — inside the 1-3 °C/s guidance.","feedbackWrong":"Divide the rise by the seconds, not the other way round.","explanation":"125 °C in 60 s gives 2.08 °C/s, which sits inside the 1-3 °C/s guidance the course takes from IPC-7530. This is the figure the m06 lesson works, and it matters because a ramp that is too fast drives tombstoning on the 0402s."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"The profile gives 65 s above 219 °C. Against the paste datasheet window of 60-90 s, how many seconds of margin are there at the lower end?","answer":{"value":5,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"s"},"hints":["The window opens at 60 s.","65 s minus 60 s.","5 s of margin."],"feedbackCorrect":"5 s above the lower bound — inside the window, with little margin.","feedbackWrong":"Subtract the window minimum from the measured time above liquidus.","explanation":"65 s clears the 60 s floor of the paste window by 5 s. The course treats that as passing but thin: a slower line or a colder zone could drop the board below the window without anyone changing the recipe."},{"id":"s2b","type":"numeric","prompt":"The survey ran three thermocouples. Using thermocouples.csv, what is the peak-to-peak spread across the board, in °C?","answer":{"value":12,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"°C"},"hints":["Spread is the hottest site minus the coldest, not a distance to a limit.","Hottest is the 0402 near the edge, coldest is the BGA centre joint.","254 - 242."],"feedbackCorrect":"12 °C across the board — and the mean of the three is the 248 °C the single-point profile reported.","feedbackWrong":"Subtract the coldest peak from the hottest; ignore the middle site.","explanation":"254 - 242 = 12 °C. The mean of the three sites is 248 °C, exactly the peak the single-point profile reported, which is why one thermocouple makes a graded board look uniform. The spread is the finding: 242 °C at the BGA centre is 3 °C under the paste band top, and 254 °C at the 0402 near the edge is 9 °C over it and 4 °C over the 250 °C classification ceiling for a large package. The single average of 248 °C sits inside the band and hides both facts, which is why the record has to carry the spread and not the average."},{"id":"s3","type":"numeric","prompt":"Peak is 248 °C and the large-package classification temperature is 250 °C. What margin is left, in °C?","answer":{"value":2,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"°C"},"hints":["Margin is limit minus measured, not measured minus limit.","250 - 248.","2 °C."],"feedbackCorrect":"2 °C of margin to the classification limit.","feedbackWrong":"The limit is the upper number; subtract the measurement from it.","explanation":"250 - 248 leaves 2 °C. The classification temperature is a ceiling for the package, so a 2 °C margin means any zone drift or heavier board can push the peak over it — worth a note in the profile record even though the profile passes."},{"id":"s4","type":"numeric","prompt":"Cooling runs from 248 °C to 100 °C in 40 s. What is the cooling rate in °C/s?","answer":{"value":3.7,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"°C/s"},"hints":["Same rate formula, with the drop as the temperature difference.","248 - 100 = 148 °C.","148 / 40 = 3.7 °C/s."],"feedbackCorrect":"3.7 °C/s — fast cooling, good for grain structure but hard on large components.","feedbackWrong":"Use the drop (148 °C) over the 40 s cooling window.","explanation":"148 °C in 40 s is 3.7 °C/s. The course notes that cooling that fast is good for fine grain but raises the thermal gradient across a large BGA, so it is a parameter to record and watch rather than a defect."},{"id":"s5","type":"choice","prompt":"Which disposition does the three-site survey earn?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Reject: the 0402 near the edge peaks over the paste band and over the package ceiling, so the profile cannot be released as measured."},{"id":"b","text":"Accept and document: the average peak and the time above liquidus are inside their windows, so the spread is recorded as a note only."},{"id":"c","text":"Accept without comment: a profile only fails if the joints look wrong."},{"id":"d","text":"Reject: time above liquidus must always exceed 90 s on a heavy board."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["The average is not the finding: look at the hottest and the coldest site.","254 °C at the 0402 near the edge is above both the paste band top (245 °C) and the large-package ceiling (250 °C).","A profile is judged on the worst measured point, not on the mean, so it cannot be released as measured."],"feedbackCorrect":"Reject and change: the hot corner exceeds the paste band and the package ceiling.","feedbackWrong":"Averaging the three sites hides the one that fails. Judge the worst point.","explanation":"254 °C at the 0402 near the edge is 9 °C over the paste band top and 4 °C over the J-STD-020 ceiling for a large package, so the profile fails at that point however good the 248 °C average looks. Time above liquidus is inside its window at every site, and the BGA centre at 242 °C is fine, which is exactly why the failing corner has to be found from the survey rather than from a single thermocouple. This is the lesson the case is built on: a mean is not a measurement of the worst case."},{"id":"s6","type":"numeric","prompt":"A first-principles check: the oven delivers about 100 W and the board's measured thermal mass is 90 J/K. What ramp rate does that predict, in °C/s?","answer":{"value":1.1111,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"°C/s"},"hints":["Ramp rate from heat input is power divided by thermal mass.","100 W / 90 J/K.","1.1 °C/s."],"feedbackCorrect":"1.1 °C/s predicted, against 2.08 °C/s measured.","feedbackWrong":"Power over thermal mass; the units work out to °C/s directly.","explanation":"100 / 90 = 1.1 °C/s, roughly half the measured ramp. The model is a sanity check, not an oracle: the real oven adds zone power and the board receives radiated heat, so a measured rate above the model is expected — but a factor of two is worth checking before the model is used to set a recipe."},{"id":"s7","type":"choice","prompt":"What should be done with the factor-of-two gap between the model and the measurement?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Trust the model: the measured thermocouple must be wrong."},{"id":"b","text":"Trust the measurement and record the model's assumptions as unvalidated for this board; re-measure a second worst-case site before using the model to predict zone settings."},{"id":"c","text":"Average the two values and use that as the ramp rate."},{"id":"d","text":"Ignore both and use the paste datasheet ramp figure."}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["A thermocouple on the worst-case site is direct evidence; a lumped model is an estimate.","The lesson's own wording is to treat the model as an estimate and verify against measurement.","Keep the measurement, flag the model, and take a second site."],"feedbackCorrect":"Keep the measurement, mark the model unvalidated for Board X, and check a second worst-case site.","feedbackWrong":"The question is which number is evidence and which is an assumption.","explanation":"The thermocouple is direct evidence; the 100 W / 90 J/K model is a lumped estimate that ignores radiation, zone coupling and board mass distribution. Averaging an estimate with a measurement produces a number with no physical meaning, and the datasheet ramp is a paste guideline, not this board's behaviour. Record the gap and re-measure a second site before the model is trusted."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Ramp rate computed from the recorded interval","points":15,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"Time above liquidus compared with the paste window","points":15,"steps":["s2"]},{"criterion":"Peak margin taken against the correct package class","points":15,"steps":["s3"]},{"criterion":"Spread read from the three-site survey, not from the average peak","points":8,"steps":["s2b"]},{"criterion":"Cooling rate computed and interpreted","points":10,"steps":["s4"]},{"criterion":"Disposition judged on the worst site, not on the average","points":14,"steps":["s5"]},{"criterion":"Model-versus-measurement check performed","points":10,"steps":["s6"]},{"criterion":"Evidence ranked above the estimate","points":13,"steps":["s7"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"reflow-log","name":"Reflow profile log","status":"available","use":"Record the measured interval, its rate and where each thermocouple sat before a profile is released.","lesson":"m06l07","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"reflow-log-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"interval,from_c,to_c,seconds,rate_c_per_s\r\n,,,,\r\n,,,,\r\n,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"reflow-log-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"interval,from_c,to_c,seconds,rate_c_per_s\nramp,25,150,60,\nsoak,150,190,90,\napproach,190,219,30,\nabove_liquidus,219,248,65,\npeak_hold,248,248,4,\ncooling,248,100,40,\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"reflow-log-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# Reflow profile log\n\n## What this template is for\nRecord the measured interval, its rate and where each thermocouple sat before a profile is released.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per interval or thermocouple. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `interval`\n- `from_c`\n- `to_c`\n- `seconds`\n- `rate_c_per_s`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m06-01 (Profile validation: Board X runs a 248 °C peak), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m06l07 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"Profile anatomy (lesson m06l02)","href":"#/l/m06l02"},{"label":"Alloy systems and profile families (lesson m06l05)","href":"#/l/m06l05"}],"sources":["src-008","src-005"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py","spec":[{"label":"Liquidus","value":219,"unit":"°C"},{"label":"Time above liquidus","value":60,"to":90,"unit":"s","note":"Board X paste window (m06l07); IPC-7530B guidance is 30-90 s (m06l05)"},{"label":"Peak window, paste","value":235,"to":245,"unit":"°C","note":"Typical SAC305 band, m06l01 and the paste datasheet"},{"label":"Peak ceiling, large package","value":250,"unit":"°C","note":"J-STD-020 Table 4-2, 1.6-2.5 mm, large package (m06l05)"}]},{"caseId":"cx-m08-01","moduleId":"m08","title":"Through-hole fill: a connector joint measures 1.10 mm of 1.60 mm","estMinutes":25,"level":"core","scenario":["Board X carries one 20-pin THT connector on a 1.6 mm board. The land pattern calls for a 1.00 mm finished hole with a tolerance of 0.05 mm; the connector datasheet gives a 0.60 mm lead with a tolerance of 0.03 mm.","First article after the wave shows the joint in the measurement sheet at 1.10 mm of vertical fill. The operator reports that most joints look full and asks for the build to be released.","Decide the disposition against the applicable IPC-A-610 class, and say what the measurement sheet has to record so that the answer stops being a matter of who looked at the board."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"tht-joints.csv","kind":"csv","note":"Hole and lead diameters are the measured extremes on this build, not the drawing nominals. Fill is the vertical barrel fill from cross-section, as a share of the 1.6 mm board.","content":"joint,part,hole_nominal_mm,hole_min_mm,hole_max_mm,lead_nominal_mm,lead_max_mm,board_mm,fill_mm,fill_pct,leads_on_thermal_plane\nJ-01,THT-20 pin connector,1.00,0.95,1.05,0.60,0.63,1.60,1.10,68.75,0\nJ-02,THT-20 pin connector,1.00,0.95,1.05,0.60,0.63,1.60,1.30,81.25,0\nJ-03,THT-20 pin connector,1.00,0.95,1.05,0.60,0.63,1.60,1.25,78.13,0\n"},{"name":"wave-run.csv","kind":"csv","note":"The settings the first article ran at. Contact time is the wave width divided by conveyor speed.","content":"run,flux,preheat_top_c,pot_c,wave_width_mm,conveyor_m_per_min,contact_s,board_dir\nFA-1,rosin mildly activated,105,255,50,1.5,2.0,connector upstream\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"Using the measurement sheet, what is the nominal diametral clearance on J-01, in mm?","answer":{"value":0.4,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["Diametral clearance is the hole diameter minus the lead diameter.","Nominal hole 1.00 mm, nominal lead 0.60 mm.","1.00 - 0.60 = 0.40 mm."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.40 mm nominal, and the worst case is not that number.","feedbackWrong":"Take the finished hole diameter, not the drill diameter, then subtract the lead.","explanation":"1.00 - 0.60 = 0.40 mm of diametral clearance. The radial clearance is half of that, 0.20 mm, which is the gap the solder has to climb through. The nominal case is the one the drawing shows; the joint that fails is rarely the nominal one."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"What is the worst-case tight diametral clearance, in mm: smallest finished hole against largest lead?","answer":{"value":0.32,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["Tight means the smallest hole against the largest lead.","Hole 1.00 - 0.05 = 0.95 mm; lead 0.60 + 0.03 = 0.63 mm.","0.95 - 0.63 = 0.32 mm."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.32 mm in the worst case, against 0.40 mm nominal.","feedbackWrong":"Both tolerances have to be used in their worst direction at the same time.","explanation":"A 0.95 mm hole with a 0.63 mm lead leaves 0.32 mm diametral, or 0.16 mm radial. The 0.08 mm of clearance lost to tolerance is a fifth of the nominal gap, and it is what separates a full barrel from a stalled one on a cold joint."},{"id":"s3","type":"numeric","prompt":"What is that worst-case radial clearance, in mm?","answer":{"value":0.16,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["Radial clearance is half the diametral clearance.","0.32 / 2.","0.16 mm."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.16 mm radially: the gap the solder draws through.","feedbackWrong":"Halve the diametral figure rather than repeating the subtraction.","explanation":"0.32 / 2 = 0.16 mm radially. That is the capillary gap: wide enough to fill when hole and lead are clean and hot, narrow enough that a low top-side temperature stops the flow before the barrel fills."},{"id":"s4","type":"numeric","prompt":"At nominal diameters, what is the annular volume the solder has to fill through the 1.6 mm board, in mm3? Use pi = 3.14159 and give two decimals.","answer":{"value":0.8,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"mm3"},"hints":["The annulus is the hole cylinder minus the lead cylinder.","pi * (0.5^2 - 0.3^2) * 1.6.","pi * 0.16 * 1.6 = 0.80 mm3."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.80 mm3 of annulus; the 75 % minimum asks for 0.60 mm3 of solder in it.","feedbackWrong":"Subtract the lead cross-section from the hole cross-section before multiplying by thickness.","explanation":"The hole holds pi * 0.5^2 * 1.6 = 1.26 mm3 and the lead takes pi * 0.3^2 * 1.6 = 0.45 mm3, leaving 0.80 mm3 of annulus. At the 75 % acceptance minimum the joint has to show 0.60 mm3 of solder to be acceptable at all."},{"id":"s5","type":"numeric","prompt":"Cross-section shows 1.10 mm of fill on a 1.60 mm board. What is the vertical fill as a percentage? One decimal place.","answer":{"value":68.8,"tolerance":0.2,"unit":"%"},"hints":["Fill percentage is filled depth divided by board thickness.","1.10 / 1.60.","68.75 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"68.75 %, under the 75 % minimum.","feedbackWrong":"Divide the filled depth by the full board thickness and scale to percent.","explanation":"1.10 / 1.60 = 0.6875, so 68.75 %. The number matters because IPC-A-610 sets the minimum vertical barrel fill at 75 % for Class 2 and Class 3 alike; 68.75 % is a defect, not a cosmetic shortfall."},{"id":"s8","type":"numeric","prompt":"The minimum is 75 % of the 1.60 mm board. How many millimetres of fill is J-01 short, in mm?","answer":{"value":0.1,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["Turn the percentage minimum into a depth first: 0.75 x 1.60 mm.","1.20 mm required, 1.10 mm measured.","0.10 mm short."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.10 mm short: a tenth of a millimetre, on a joint the operator called full.","feedbackWrong":"Convert the percentage to a depth before subtracting the measurement.","explanation":"The minimum depth is 0.75 * 1.60 = 1.20 mm and the joint has 1.10 mm, so it is 0.10 mm short. Ten hundredths of a millimetre decide between a released build and a rework, which is why fill is measured on a cross-section rather than judged from the solder-side fillet."},{"id":"s6","type":"choice","prompt":"What is the correct disposition of J-01?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept it under the thermal-plane exception for 14 or more leads."},{"id":"b","text":"Accept it and raise the pot temperature by 10 °C so the next board fills further."},{"id":"c","text":"Accept it: 68.75 % is close to 75 % and the joint is electrically connected."},{"id":"d","text":"Reject it against the 75 % minimum and rework it; every other joint in the sheet is judged on its own."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["The minimum for Class 2 and Class 3 is the same number; which is it?","The exception is for parts with 14 or more leads tied into internal thermal planes. The sheet records zero leads on a plane for this connector.","68.75 % is below 75 %, so the joint fails."],"feedbackCorrect":"Reject J-01 and rework it: the 75 % minimum has no near-miss band.","feedbackWrong":"Which column in the sheet says whether this joint is acceptable?","explanation":"IPC-A-610 sets a 75 % minimum vertical fill for Class 2 and Class 3, and 68.75 % is below it. The 50 % / 1.2 mm figure is a documented exception for parts with fourteen or more leads tied into internal thermal planes; the sheet records zero leads on a plane, so the exception does not apply. Raising the pot temperature to rescue one joint changes every other joint on the board and leaves the mechanism in place."},{"id":"s7","type":"choice","prompt":"The other joints read 81.25 % and 78.13 %. What is the defensible reading of the sheet?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The build fails on J-01, and the sheet has to record each joint so the failing one cannot be averaged away."},{"id":"b","text":"The build passes because two joints out of three are above the minimum."},{"id":"c","text":"The build passes once J-01 is re-inspected by a second operator."},{"id":"d","text":"The build passes: the mean of the three joints is 76.04 %, above 75 %."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["Averaging is exactly the mistake this sheet exists to prevent.","One joint below the minimum is a defect on that joint, whatever the mean is.","Judge every joint: the average of acceptable and unacceptable is unacceptable."],"feedbackCorrect":"Judge joint by joint. J-01 fails, and a mean cannot repair it.","feedbackWrong":"Ask what the average would hide if the sheet recorded only a mean.","explanation":"The mean of 68.75, 81.25 and 78.13 is 76.04 %, which clears 75 % and hides the defect. Acceptance in IPC-A-610 is per joint, so the sheet must carry every joint; a summary that reports only an average cannot show which barrel is short. It is the same mistake as judging a reflow profile by its mean peak."},{"id":"s9","type":"choice","prompt":"What control actually addresses the short fill, given the wave record?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept the fill and add 100 % X-ray inspection."},{"id":"b","text":"Raise the conveyor speed so the board spends less time in the wave."},{"id":"c","text":"Raise preheat so the top side of the hole reaches a temperature that lets the solder climb, and verify dwell and contact time against the equipment limits."},{"id":"d","text":"Add flux by hand at the connector before the wave."}],"answer":{"value":"c"},"hints":["Look at the wave record: contact time is 2.0 s, the bottom of the 2-4 s window the course teaches.","A short fill on a clean, wetted joint points at the thermal side of the process, not at solder volume.","Preheat and dwell decide whether the barrel fills; inspection only finds it later."],"feedbackCorrect":"Raise preheat and check dwell against the equipment limits. Inspection after the fact does not fill a barrel.","feedbackWrong":"Which setting in wave-run.csv sits at the edge of its window?","explanation":"Contact time is 2.0 s, the very bottom of the 2-4 s window, and top-side preheat is 105 °C in a 90-120 °C band: both at the weak end for a connector that acts as a heat sink. Raising preheat and lengthening contact inside equipment limits addresses the mechanism. Speeding the conveyor shortens contact and makes it worse, and adding X-ray only proves the defect exists on every board after the cost is sunk."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Nominal clearance computed from finished diameters, not from the drill","points":10,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"Worst-case clearance built from both tolerances at once","points":15,"steps":["s2"]},{"criterion":"Radial clearance derived from the diametral figure","points":10,"steps":["s3"]},{"criterion":"Annular volume computed as hole minus lead, in the right unit","points":15,"steps":["s4"]},{"criterion":"Fill percentage computed against the board thickness","points":10,"steps":["s5"]},{"criterion":"Shortfall in millimetres derived from the percentage minimum","points":10,"steps":["s8"]},{"criterion":"Joint judged against 75 %, with the exception ruled out on evidence","points":15,"steps":["s6"]},{"criterion":"Each joint judged on its own, not on the average","points":10,"steps":["s7"]},{"criterion":"Control chosen that matches the mechanism, not the inspection","points":5,"steps":["s9"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"tht-measure","name":"Hole and lead measurement sheet","status":"available","use":"Record finished hole, measured lead diameter and fill percentage for every through-hole part before the first article is signed off.","lesson":"m08l05","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"tht-measure-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"joint,part,hole_nominal_mm,hole_min_mm,hole_max_mm,lead_nominal_mm,lead_max_mm,board_mm,fill_mm,fill_pct,leads_on_thermal_plane\r\n,,,,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"tht-measure-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"joint,part,hole_nominal_mm,hole_min_mm,hole_max_mm,lead_nominal_mm,lead_max_mm,board_mm,fill_mm,fill_pct,leads_on_thermal_plane\nJ-01,THT-20 pin connector,1.00,0.95,1.05,0.60,0.63,1.60,1.10,68.75,0\nJ-02,THT-20 pin connector,1.00,0.95,1.05,0.60,0.63,1.60,1.30,81.25,0\nJ-03,THT-20 pin connector,1.00,0.95,1.05,0.60,0.63,1.60,1.25,78.13,0\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"tht-measure-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# Hole and lead measurement sheet\n\n## What this template is for\nRecord finished hole, measured lead diameter and fill percentage for every through-hole part before the first article is signed off.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per through-hole joint. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `joint`\n- `part`\n- `hole_nominal_mm`\n- `hole_min_mm`\n- `hole_max_mm`\n- `lead_nominal_mm`\n- `lead_max_mm`\n- `board_mm`\n- `fill_mm`\n- `fill_pct`\n- `leads_on_thermal_plane`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m08-01 (Through-hole fill: a connector joint measures 1.10 mm of 1.60 mm), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m08l05 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]},{"templateId":"wave-record","name":"Wave solder process record","status":"available","use":"Log flux, preheat, wave height, conveyor speed and pot temperature for the run, so a later defect can be traced to a setting instead of a memory.","lesson":"m09l07","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"wave-record-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"run,flux,preheat_top_c,pot_c,wave_width_mm,conveyor_m_per_min,contact_s,board_dir\r\n,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"wave-record-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"run,flux,preheat_top_c,pot_c,wave_width_mm,conveyor_m_per_min,contact_s,board_dir\nFA-1,rosin mildly activated,105,255,50,1.5,2.0,connector upstream\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"wave-record-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# Wave solder process record\n\n## What this template is for\nLog flux, preheat, wave height, conveyor speed and pot temperature for the run, so a later defect can be traced to a setting instead of a memory.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per run or setting. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `run`\n- `flux`\n- `preheat_top_c`\n- `pot_c`\n- `wave_width_mm`\n- `conveyor_m_per_min`\n- `contact_s`\n- `board_dir`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m08-01 (Through-hole fill: a connector joint measures 1.10 mm of 1.60 mm), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m09l07 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"Hole, lead and percent fill (lesson m08l02)","href":"#/l/m08l02"},{"label":"Calculation drill: clearance and fill (lesson m08l05)","href":"#/l/m08l05"},{"label":"Insufficient-fill diagnosis (lesson m09l06)","href":"#/l/m09l06"}],"sources":["src-016","src-001"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py","spec":[{"label":"Minimum barrel fill","value":75,"unit":"%","note":"IPC-A-610, Class 2 and Class 3 alike (m08l05)"},{"label":"Thermal-plane exception","value":50,"unit":"%","note":"Documented exception for 14 or more leads in internal planes (m08l05)"},{"label":"Contact time","value":2,"to":4,"unit":"s","note":"Course window for wave contact (m09l07)"}]},{"caseId":"cx-m09-01","moduleId":"m09","title":"Wave run FA-1: a short barrel on one joint and a bridge on another","estMinutes":25,"level":"core","scenario":["The wave run that produced the m08 first article is recorded in wave-run.csv: rosin mildly activated flux, 105 °C top-side preheat, a 255 °C pot, a 50 mm wave and 1.5 m/min conveyor on a single-wave machine. The connector ran upstream.","Two findings came off the same board. J-01 is short of fill at 1.10 mm of a 1.60 mm barrel. On the neighbouring pin a 3.2 mm solder bridge spans a 2.54 mm pitch. The line wants one explanation for both, and the fastest one is 'the solder pot is too cold'.","Check the record against the course's wave windows, then decide what to change and in what order."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"wave-run.csv","kind":"csv","note":"One board, one pass. contact_s is the wave width divided by the conveyor speed; the course window for contact time is 2-4 s.","content":"run,flux,preheat_top_c,pot_c,wave_width_mm,conveyor_m_per_min,contact_s,board_dir\nFA-1,rosin mildly activated,105,255,50,1.5,2.0,connector upstream\n"},{"name":"joint-findings.csv","kind":"csv","note":"The whole connector, one row per pin: all 20 joints of the first article, not only the two that failed. Fill is vertical barrel fill from cross-section; span and pitch are filled in only where a bridge was measured.","content":"joint,defect,fill_pct,span_mm,pitch_mm,side\nJ-01,insufficient fill,68.75,,,solder side\nJ-02,bridge,,3.20,2.54,solder side\nJ-03,,78.13,,,solder side\nJ-04,,80.00,,,solder side\nJ-05,,81.25,,,solder side\nJ-06,,79.38,,,solder side\nJ-07,,76.88,,,solder side\nJ-08,,82.50,,,solder side\nJ-09,,78.75,,,solder side\nJ-10,,80.63,,,solder side\nJ-11,,77.50,,,solder side\nJ-12,,79.38,,,solder side\nJ-13,,81.88,,,solder side\nJ-14,,78.13,,,solder side\nJ-15,,80.00,,,solder side\nJ-16,,76.25,,,solder side\nJ-17,,79.38,,,solder side\nJ-18,,81.25,,,solder side\nJ-19,,77.50,,,solder side\nJ-20,,80.63,,,solder side\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"The wave is 50 mm wide and the conveyor runs at 1.5 m/min. What is the contact time, in seconds?","answer":{"value":2,"tolerance":0.1,"unit":"s"},"hints":["Convert both lengths to the same unit before dividing.","50 mm = 0.050 m; 1.5 m/min = 0.025 m/s.","0.050 / 0.025 = 2.0 s."],"feedbackCorrect":"2.0 s: inside the 2-4 s window, but at the bottom of it.","feedbackWrong":"Divide the wave width in metres by the conveyor speed in metres per second.","explanation":"0.050 m of wave over 0.025 m/s of board travel gives 2.0 s of contact. The course window is 2-4 s, so the run is legal, and the board passes through in the shortest time the window allows. For a connector that acts as a heat sink that is the first thing to look at, not the pot temperature."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"The pot runs at 255 °C. What is that above the SAC305 liquidus of 219 °C, in °C?","answer":{"value":36,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"°C"},"hints":["Liquidus is the temperature the alloy must exceed, not a target.","255 - 219.","36 °C above liquidus."],"feedbackCorrect":"36 °C above liquidus: the pot is hot, not cold.","feedbackWrong":"Subtract the liquidus from the pot temperature, not the other way round.","explanation":"255 - 219 = 36 °C. The course's own window for SAC305 pots is 250-260 °C, so the pot sits in the middle of it. The hypothesis that a cold pot explains the short fill is contradicted by the record before anyone touches a knob."},{"id":"s3","type":"choice","prompt":"The record is inside every window: pot 255 °C, contact 2.0 s, flux correct. Which pair of settings is actually weak for a heat-sinking connector?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The pot temperature and the flux type."},{"id":"b","text":"The top-side preheat, at 105 °C, and the contact time, at the floor of its window."},{"id":"c","text":"The conveyor speed, which is far too slow."},{"id":"d","text":"Nothing: the settings are fine and the defect must be incoming material."}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["The course's preheat range is 90-120 °C top side; where in that band is 105 °C?","A connector pulls heat out of the joint, so the thermal side needs margin, not the bare minimum.","Preheat and contact time carry the fill, and both are at the weak end."],"feedbackCorrect":"Top-side preheat (105 °C) and contact time (2.0 s) are the weak settings.","feedbackWrong":"Which two numbers in the record sit at the bottom of their windows?","explanation":"Pot temperature is mid-window and flux is the right family for this board, so neither explains anything. Preheat at 105 °C is in the lower half of the 90-120 °C band and contact time is at the 2.0 s floor, on a part that conducts heat away from the joint. A theory that needs a defect in the material ignores two settings that are already at the weak end of the only windows the course gives."},{"id":"s4","type":"numeric","prompt":"The bridge on J-02 spans 3.20 mm across a 2.54 mm pitch. How many pitches does the bridge cover? Two decimals.","answer":{"value":1.26,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"dimensionless"},"hints":["One pitch is the centre-to-centre distance between adjacent pins.","3.20 / 2.54.","1.26 pitches: it reaches past the neighbour."],"feedbackCorrect":"1.26 pitches: the bridge is not a whisker, it is a pool.","feedbackWrong":"Divide the span by the pitch; the units cancel.","explanation":"3.20 / 2.54 = 1.26. A bridge that covers more than one pitch is a volume and drainage problem, not a fine-pitch whisker: solder sat on the board long enough to pool between two pads. On the same board, one joint is short and the neighbour is flooded, which is what uneven wetting and drainage looks like."},{"id":"s5","type":"choice","prompt":"One joint is short and its neighbour is flooded on the same pass. What does that pair of findings point to?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"A bad batch of connectors, since both findings are on the same part."},{"id":"b","text":"A single cause: the pot is too cold, so some joints do not fill and some flood."},{"id":"c","text":"A wetting and drainage problem around one component: the joint that did not reach temperature stayed short, and solder pooled where drainage was poor."},{"id":"d","text":"Two unrelated random defects; no action is possible."}],"answer":{"value":"c"},"hints":["A cold pot would make every joint worse, and the record says the pot is not cold.","Short fill and a pool on the same connector come from an uneven thermal and flow field, not from the solder temperature.","The mechanism has to explain both findings at once, or it is not the mechanism."],"feedbackCorrect":"An uneven thermal and drainage field around one component: one pin too cold to fill, its neighbour with nowhere to drain.","feedbackWrong":"Which explanation accounts for a short barrel and a flooded neighbour at the same time?","explanation":"A cold pot affects the whole board, and the other joints on this board fill to 76.3-82.5 %. Two findings two pins apart on the same part are a local thermal and flow problem: the connector shields the wave and pulls heat from its own joints, so one barrel stalls while solder pools where it cannot drain. Naming one mechanism that explains both findings is the difference between a fix and an experiment."},{"id":"s6","type":"choice","prompt":"Which single change should be made first, so that its effect can be measured?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Raise top-side preheat within the 90-120 °C band and lengthen contact toward the middle of the 2-4 s window, then re-run one board and re-measure."},{"id":"b","text":"Change the flux to water-soluble and keep every other setting."},{"id":"c","text":"Add a pallet and keep every setting, so the board is supported better."},{"id":"d","text":"Raise the pot temperature from 255 to 270 °C and shorten the contact time."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["One change at a time is what makes the next measurement mean something.","The two weak settings are thermal: preheat and contact time.","Raise preheat, lengthen contact inside the window, re-run one board."],"feedbackCorrect":"Raise preheat and lengthen contact inside their windows, one board, then re-measure.","feedbackWrong":"Which change touches the settings the record already flags as weak?","explanation":"Preheat and contact time are the two settings at the weak end and they act on the same mechanism: heat in the barrel and time for solder to climb. Changing flux, pallet and pot at once makes the result unattributable, and 270 °C is above the course's 250-260 °C SAC305 window with no evidence that heat is the binding constraint. One change, one board, one measurement is how a wave process is qualified."},{"id":"s7","type":"numeric","prompt":"The first-article sheet covers every pin of the connector. What percentage of its joints is nonconforming against the 75 % minimum? One decimal.","answer":{"value":5,"tolerance":0.1,"unit":"%"},"hints":["The denominator is every joint on the sheet, not only the ones that failed.","Count the rows first: the connector has twenty joints.","1 / 20 = 5.0 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"5.0 % of the connector's joints: one pin in twenty.","feedbackWrong":"Divide the failing joints by the joints inspected.","explanation":"1 / 20 = 5.0 %. On a sample this small a single joint is five percent, which is why first-article acceptance is per joint and why a run of three boards is not a process capability study. The number is small and the joint is still a defect."},{"id":"s8","type":"choice","prompt":"The supplier asks whether the whole lot should be rejected. What does the record support?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Release the lot: 5 % of one connector is trivial."},{"id":"b","text":"Release the lot and inspect 100 % by eye, since the defect is visible."},{"id":"c","text":"Reject the lot: one nonconforming joint condemns every board."},{"id":"d","text":"Contain the boards that have run, fix the process settings, re-qualify with a first article, and judge the lot against the re-qualified process rather than against one joint."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["A single joint is evidence about the process, not a measurement of the lot.","Containment first, then a process fix, then re-qualification: the same order as an 8D.","The lot is judged against the corrected process, not against the finding that started the investigation."],"feedbackCorrect":"Contain what has run, fix the settings, re-qualify the process, then judge the lot.","feedbackWrong":"Which decision does one joint actually support: a lot verdict, or a process verdict?","explanation":"One joint on a three-board first article is a process signal. Containment holds the boards that ran, the settings get corrected and proven on a first article, and only then does the lot get judged. Rejecting everything on one joint is as unjustified as releasing everything on 5 %, and a 100 % visual check cannot measure barrel fill: the short barrel is on the underside of the joint."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Contact time computed from wave width and conveyor speed","points":15,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"Pot margin over liquidus computed and judged against the SAC305 window","points":10,"steps":["s2"]},{"criterion":"Weak settings identified from the record, not assumed","points":15,"steps":["s3"]},{"criterion":"Bridge span expressed in pitches","points":10,"steps":["s4"]},{"criterion":"One mechanism named that explains both findings","points":15,"steps":["s5"]},{"criterion":"One change at a time, inside the published windows","points":15,"steps":["s6"]},{"criterion":"Nonconforming share computed on joints, not boards","points":10,"steps":["s7"]},{"criterion":"Lot decision separated from the process decision","points":10,"steps":["s8"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"wave-record","name":"Wave solder process record","status":"available","use":"Log flux, preheat, wave height, conveyor speed and pot temperature for the run, so a later defect can be traced to a setting instead of a memory.","lesson":"m09l07","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"wave-record-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"run,flux,preheat_top_c,pot_c,wave_width_mm,conveyor_m_per_min,contact_s,board_dir\r\n,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"wave-record-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"run,flux,preheat_top_c,pot_c,wave_width_mm,conveyor_m_per_min,contact_s,board_dir\nFA-1,rosin mildly activated,105,255,50,1.5,2.0,connector upstream\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"wave-record-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# Wave solder process record\n\n## What this template is for\nLog flux, preheat, wave height, conveyor speed and pot temperature for the run, so a later defect can be traced to a setting instead of a memory.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per run or setting. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `run`\n- `flux`\n- `preheat_top_c`\n- `pot_c`\n- `wave_width_mm`\n- `conveyor_m_per_min`\n- `contact_s`\n- `board_dir`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m09-01 (Wave run FA-1: a short barrel on one joint and a bridge on another), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m09l07 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"Wave process control (lesson m09l07)","href":"#/l/m09l07"},{"label":"Wave defect atlas (lesson m09l06)","href":"#/l/m09l06"},{"label":"Preparing the board for the wave (lesson m08l04)","href":"#/l/m08l04"}],"sources":["src-001","src-016"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py","spec":[{"label":"Pot temperature, SAC305","value":250,"to":260,"unit":"°C","note":"Course window for wave pots (m09l07)"},{"label":"Contact time","value":2,"to":4,"unit":"s","note":"Course window (m09l07)"},{"label":"Top-side preheat","value":90,"to":120,"unit":"°C","note":"Course band (m09l07)"}]},{"caseId":"cx-m07-01","moduleId":"m07","title":"AOI audit: a 70 % true-call rate that still let 18 defects through","estMinutes":30,"level":"advanced","scenario":["Board X ran twelve thousand boards through AOI. The audit log for four windows is in aoi-audit.csv: boards inspected, calls raised, calls verified, true defects, false calls and the defects found later by test, X-ray and visual audit.","The line reads the numbers optimistically: most of the calls were real, so the program is good. Quality has the opposite worry: eighteen defects left the line after AOI, and one of the X-ray follow-ups on a BGA ball looks bad.","Compute the three rates that matter, say what they do and do not prove, and judge the ball in xray-bga.csv against the applicable limit."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"aoi-audit.csv","kind":"csv","note":"One row per review window. true_calls + false_calls = verified_calls, and verified calls are a subset of the calls raised; escapes are defects AOI did not call that were found later.","content":"window,boards,calls,verified_calls,true_calls,false_calls,escapes\nW-36,3000,90,75,53,22,5\nW-37,3000,90,75,53,22,4\nW-38,3000,90,75,52,23,5\nW-39,3000,90,75,52,23,4\n"},{"name":"xray-bga.csv","kind":"csv","note":"Measured diameters from the X-ray image, one row per ball, as projected areas on the image. A ball may carry more than one void: the criterion counts their cumulative area, not the largest one. Diameters are read as equivalent-circle diameters.","content":"ball,ball_d_mm,void1_d_mm,void2_d_mm,location\nA1,0.45,0.28,0.14,edge\nA2,0.45,0.10,,centre\nA3,0.45,0.12,0.06,centre\nA4,0.45,0.32,,edge\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"What was the verification coverage, in percent: verified calls over the calls AOI raised? One decimal.","answer":{"value":83.3,"tolerance":0.2,"unit":"%"},"hints":["Coverage is about the calls that were reviewed, not about the boards.","Add the verified_calls column and divide by the calls column.","300 / 360 = 83.3 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"83.3 % of the calls were verified; the remaining 60 were never looked at.","feedbackWrong":"Verified calls over total calls, from the columns in the audit log.","explanation":"300 of 360 calls were verified, so coverage is 83.3 %. That number matters more than it looks: every rate computed from the verified subset is an estimate for the whole population, and the 60 unverified calls are an unknown. A false-call rate quoted without its coverage is a number with no error bar."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"What is the false-call rate among verified calls, in percent? One decimal.","answer":{"value":30,"tolerance":0.2,"unit":"%"},"hints":["The denominator is the calls that were verified, not the calls raised.","90 / 300.","30.0 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"30.0 % of verified calls were good boards.","feedbackWrong":"False calls over verified calls; the raised calls belong in coverage, not here.","explanation":"90 of 300 verified calls were false, so 30.0 %. Not 25 % (that would divide by the 360 raised) and not 0.75 % (that would divide by the boards). Three different denominators give three different-looking programs out of the same week, which is why the definition has to travel with the number."},{"id":"s3","type":"numeric","prompt":"What is the escape rate at board level, in percent? Give two decimals.","answer":{"value":0.15,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"%"},"hints":["Escape rate is defects found after AOI over the units inspected.","18 / 12000.","0.15 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.15 % of boards carried a defect AOI did not call.","feedbackWrong":"Defects found later, over boards inspected: not over the calls.","explanation":"18 / 12,000 = 0.15 %. That is the number a customer will ask for, and it is a board rate: it says nothing about which joint failed. The same 18 defects could sit in 18 boards at one joint each, or in one board at 18 joints, and a joint-level escape rate would answer a different question."},{"id":"s4","type":"numeric","prompt":"Quality wants the escape rate against the defects AOI actually caught. Using verified true calls and escapes, what is that rate, in percent? One decimal.","answer":{"value":7.9,"tolerance":0.2,"unit":"%"},"hints":["The denominator is the opportunities AOI judged: true calls plus escapes.","210 true calls, 18 escapes.","18 / 228 = 7.9 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"7.9 % of the judged defects escaped AOI.","feedbackWrong":"True calls are the verified calls minus the false calls; add the escapes to them.","explanation":"210 true calls plus 18 escapes is 228 found defects, so 18 / 228 = 7.9 %. This is the share of defects that AOI missed, and it is two orders of magnitude larger than the board-level rate. Both are true; they answer different questions, and reporting only the flattering one is how an escape problem stays invisible."},{"id":"s5","type":"choice","prompt":"Using the audit rows, 210 of the 300 verified calls were real defects and 90 were not. What is the correct reading of that result?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept the program: a 70 % true-call rate is a good result on its own."},{"id":"b","text":"The program still has a 30 % false-call rate that costs review capacity and a 7.9 % escape rate on judged defects; the true-call rate alone proves neither capacity nor coverage."},{"id":"c","text":"Reject the program: any escape means AOI is not fit for purpose."},{"id":"d","text":"Tighten every threshold until the escape rate is zero."}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["A true-call rate is one ratio; the cost of review and the cost of escapes are two others.","False calls consume operator attention, and attention is what makes a 30 % rate dangerous.","Judge the program on false calls, escapes and coverage together, not on the flattering ratio."],"feedbackCorrect":"Only 70 % of verified calls were real defects, 90 false calls burn reviewer attention, and 7.9 % of judged defects escaped.","feedbackWrong":"Which two costs does the true-call rate leave out?","explanation":"A 70 % true-call rate is arithmetic, not a verdict. The same week produced 90 false calls that had to be reviewed by hand and 18 defects that left the line. False calls are not free: they train operators to clear calls quickly, which is exactly how the next escape happens. Tightening every threshold to chase zero escapes moves the cost to review capacity and, at some point, to people dismissing calls without looking. The program is judged on all three rates and on what the customer needs."},{"id":"s6","type":"numeric","prompt":"Ball A1 measures 0.45 mm and carries two voids, 0.28 mm and 0.14 mm. What is the cumulative void area as a percentage of the ball area? One decimal.","answer":{"value":48.4,"tolerance":0.2,"unit":"%"},"hints":["Area scales with the square of the diameter, so two voids do not add as diameters.","Sum the void areas first, then divide by the ball area.","(0.28^2 + 0.14^2) / 0.45^2 = 48.4 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"48.4 % of the ball area — nearly double the limit.","feedbackWrong":"Sum the void areas, not the diameters, and divide by the full ball area.","explanation":"The voids have radii 0.14 mm and 0.07 mm, so their areas are pi*0.0196 = 0.0616 mm2 and pi*0.0049 = 0.0154 mm2, 0.0770 mm2 together. The ball has radius 0.225 mm and an area of pi*0.0506 = 0.1590 mm2. 0.0770 / 0.1590 = 48.4 %, which is nearly twice the 25 % limit. The arithmetic shortcut is to divide squares of diameters, since pi cancels: (0.28^2 + 0.14^2) / 0.45^2 gives the same 48.4 %. What the step is really testing is that two voids add as areas: adding the diameters would give a plausible and wrong 42 %."},{"id":"s7","type":"choice","prompt":"Against the 25 % per-ball criterion, what is the disposition of ball A1?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept: voids at the ball edge drain better than central voids."},{"id":"b","text":"Accept and average the four balls: the array as a whole is under 25 %."},{"id":"c","text":"Accept: the largest single void is 0.28 mm, which is under half the ball."},{"id":"d","text":"Reject the ball: the cumulative void area is far above 25 % of the ball area, and the criterion counts cumulative area per ball, not the largest void."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["The criterion is cumulative projected area per ball, in an X-ray image.","Two voids both count; the diameter of the larger one is not the measure.","A ball over the limit fails on its own, whatever the rest of the array reads."],"feedbackCorrect":"Reject the ball: cumulative void area is well over 25 %, and the limit is per ball.","feedbackWrong":"Which of the four numbers in the sentence is the criterion written against?","explanation":"The limit is cumulative projected void area against ball area, per ball, and A1 is far over it. Comparing one void's diameter with the ball's diameter is a different calculation that happens to look reassuring. Edge location is a reliability concern on top of the area, not a credit against it, and averaging across the array would let a failing ball hide behind three good ones — the same averaging error as in m06 and m08."},{"id":"s8","type":"numeric","prompt":"Ball A2 carries one 0.10 mm void in a 0.45 mm ball. What is its void percentage? One decimal.","answer":{"value":4.9,"tolerance":0.1,"unit":"%"},"hints":["With circular voids in one ball, the area ratio is the square of the diameter ratio.","(0.10 / 0.45)^2 x 100.","4.9 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"4.9 %: a void this size is inside the limit, not a defect.","feedbackWrong":"Square the diameter ratio; the pi cancels.","explanation":"(0.10 / 0.45)^2 = 0.0494, so 4.9 %. Small central voids are common and the criterion is not a zero-void rule: chasing a void-free joint costs rework and heat cycles and buys nothing the standard asks for. The judgement is A1 fails and A2 passes, on the same board, which is what per-ball criteria are for."},{"id":"s9","type":"choice","prompt":"What does the X-ray finding change about the AOI program?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It confirms the escape question: AOI cannot see under the package, so the hidden-joint sample has to be defined in the program rather than assumed to be covered by the AOI call rate."},{"id":"b","text":"It means AOI should be replaced by X-ray on every board."},{"id":"c","text":"It means the AOI thresholds were too loose and must be tightened."},{"id":"d","text":"Nothing: voids under a BGA are invisible to AOI, so it is a separate issue."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["Ask what AOI can physically see: component presence, fillet shape, exposed joints.","A BGA ball is behind the package, so no AOI threshold reaches it.","The program's coverage claim has to name the method that covers each defect class."],"feedbackCorrect":"It defines a gap in coverage: hidden joints need their own sample, not a tighter AOI threshold.","feedbackWrong":"Which defect classes in this case could AOI never have called?","explanation":"AOI sees what the camera sees, and a BGA ball is under the package. The 18 escapes and the failed ball are the same lesson from two directions: the program's coverage claim has to be written per defect class, with X-ray or another method named for the hidden ones. Tightening AOI thresholds cannot find a void under an opaque package, and X-raying every board is a cost decision, not a threshold decision."},{"id":"s10","type":"choice","prompt":"The failing ball is on the same Board X assembly that already carries the 8D from the AOI case. What belongs in that 8D, on this evidence?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"An action to reject the whole lot on one ball."},{"id":"b","text":"An action to tighten the AOI void threshold, since AOI is the inspection step."},{"id":"c","text":"A containment of the affected boards plus a process action on the reflow and paste side, with the X-ray sample defined so the escape is measured rather than assumed."},{"id":"d","text":"A note that voids are normal and no action is needed."}],"answer":{"value":"c"},"hints":["An AOI threshold cannot see a void under the package, so it cannot be the action.","The 8D needs containment, a cause on the process, and a measurement that will show whether the action worked.","Contain, act on the process, define the X-ray sample that measures the escape."],"feedbackCorrect":"Containment, a process action on reflow and paste, and an X-ray sample that measures the escape.","feedbackWrong":"Which of these actions could actually change the void percentage?","explanation":"A void forms inside the joint during reflow: paste chemistry, profile and the escape path for volatiles decide its size, so the process action lives on that side. AOI cannot see it at all, so a tighter AOI threshold changes nothing. Containment holds the boards already built, and the X-ray sample is what turns 'we re-inspected' into a number the 8D can be closed against — the same rule m14 applies when it refuses to close an 8D on an improvement instead of the agreed metric."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Coverage computed on calls, with its consequence stated","points":10,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"False-call rate computed on verified calls, not on raised calls","points":10,"steps":["s2"]},{"criterion":"Board-level escape rate computed on boards","points":10,"steps":["s3"]},{"criterion":"Escape rate against judged defects computed on a matching denominator","points":15,"steps":["s4"]},{"criterion":"Program judged on false calls, escapes and coverage together","points":15,"steps":["s5"]},{"criterion":"Cumulative void area computed per ball, not from the largest void","points":15,"steps":["s6","s8"]},{"criterion":"Ball rejected on the per-ball criterion, not averaged over the array","points":10,"steps":["s7"]},{"criterion":"Hidden-joint gap named as a coverage problem, not a threshold problem","points":5,"steps":["s9"]},{"criterion":"8D action placed on the process, with a sample that measures the escape","points":10,"steps":["s10"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"aoi-audit","name":"AOI and X-ray audit log","status":"available","use":"Record calls, verified calls, true defects, false calls and escapes for one window, so the three rates are computed from one consistent denominator.","lesson":"m07l03","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"aoi-audit-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"window,boards,calls,verified_calls,true_calls,false_calls,escapes\r\n,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"aoi-audit-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"window,boards,calls,verified_calls,true_calls,false_calls,escapes\nW-36,3000,90,75,53,22,5\nW-37,3000,90,75,53,22,4\nW-38,3000,90,75,52,23,5\nW-39,3000,90,75,52,23,4\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"aoi-audit-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# AOI and X-ray audit log\n\n## What this template is for\nRecord calls, verified calls, true defects, false calls and escapes for one window, so the three rates are computed from one consistent denominator.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per audit window. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `window`\n- `boards`\n- `calls`\n- `verified_calls`\n- `true_calls`\n- `false_calls`\n- `escapes`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m07-01 (AOI audit: a 70 % true-call rate that still let 18 defects through), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m07l03 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]},{"templateId":"xray-void","name":"X-ray void record","status":"available","use":"Record measured ball and void diameters per ball so the void percentage is a calculation, not an impression from a greyscale image.","lesson":"m07l04","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"xray-void-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"ball,ball_d_mm,void1_d_mm,void2_d_mm,location\r\n,,,,\r\n,,,,\r\n,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"xray-void-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"ball,ball_d_mm,void1_d_mm,void2_d_mm,location\nA1,0.45,0.28,0.14,edge\nA2,0.45,0.10,,centre\nA3,0.45,0.12,0.06,centre\nA4,0.45,0.32,,edge\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"xray-void-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# X-ray void record\n\n## What this template is for\nRecord measured ball and void diameters per ball so the void percentage is a calculation, not an impression from a greyscale image.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per ball. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `ball`\n- `ball_d_mm`\n- `void1_d_mm`\n- `void2_d_mm`\n- `location`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m07-01 (AOI audit: a 70 % true-call rate that still let 18 defects through), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m07l04 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"AOI metrics and verification (lesson m07l03)","href":"#/l/m07l03"},{"label":"X-ray interpretation (lesson m07l04)","href":"#/l/m07l04"},{"label":"False calls and escapes are linked (lesson m07l02)","href":"#/l/m07l02"}],"sources":["src-001","src-007"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py","spec":[{"label":"Void limit per BGA ball","value":25,"unit":"%","note":"Cumulative projected area, per ball, X-ray image (m07l04)"},{"label":"Verification coverage","value":83.3,"unit":"%","note":"Verified calls over calls raised, this window"},{"label":"Escape rate","value":0.15,"unit":"%","note":"Defects found later over boards inspected"}]},{"caseId":"cx-m11-01","moduleId":"m11","title":"Rework record: a connector joint, a nicked trace and a hand-held iron","estMinutes":30,"level":"advanced","scenario":["The short-filled J-01 from the wave case is reworked by hand on a Class 2 Board X assembly. The record in rework-record.csv carries the setpoint, the preheat, the dwell of each contact and what the operator measured afterwards: one trace nicked while the connector came off, and one joint that needed three contacts.","Two decisions are waiting. The first is the trace: the drawing sets a minimum width of 0.280 mm, and the trace measures 0.248 mm after the nick. The second is the thermal budget: three contacts on one joint, and a row where the first contact produced no solder flow at all.","Judge the repair against the class, then say what the record has to contain so the next engineer can reproduce the result."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"rework-record.csv","kind":"csv","note":"One row per assembly. There is one dwell column per contact, so the total iron time is a sum over the row; dry_flag marks a contact that produced no solder flow.","content":"assembly,operation,tip,setpoint_c,preheat_c,contacts,dwell1_s,dwell2_s,dwell3_s,trace_drawn_mm,trace_min_mm,trace_after_mm,dry_flag\nB-07,J-01 rework,chisel 1.2 mm,345,95,3,2.6,0.4,0.2,0.300,0.280,0.248,0\nB-08,J-01 rework,chisel 1.2 mm,345,95,2,2.2,0.1,0.0,0.300,0.280,0.280,1\nB-09,J-01 rework,chisel 1.2 mm,345,95,1,2.4,0.0,0.0,0.300,0.280,0.280,0\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"choice","prompt":"B-07 ran at a 345 °C setpoint. Against the course's hand-soldering band of 330-360 °C, how does that setpoint read?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Too cold for lead-free and a likely cause of the cold joint."},{"id":"b","text":"Inside the band, near its middle: the setpoint is defensible, so the cause of the short fill is elsewhere in the budget."},{"id":"c","text":"Too hot, and above every published limit for SAC305."},{"id":"d","text":"Irrelevant, because only the measured joint temperature matters."}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["The course band for hand soldering lead-free is 330-360 °C, and J-STD-001 does not fix one number for every joint.","345 sits between 330 and 360.","The setpoint is inside the band, so it is not the defect."],"feedbackCorrect":"345 °C is inside the 330-360 °C band: the setpoint is not what went wrong.","feedbackWrong":"Compare the setpoint with the band the lesson gives, not with a number from memory.","explanation":"345 °C sits in the middle of the course's 330-360 °C band, and published sources are looser still: IPC-7711 procedures start at about 315 °C and say to change it as necessary, vendor tables quote up to 400 °C for lead-free. So the setpoint is not the defect, and 'raise the temperature' is not a diagnosis. The setpoint is also only half the budget: contact time, preheat and how many times the joint is heated decide the outcome."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"B-07 was heated for dwells of 2.6, 0.4 and 0.2 s. What is the total iron time on the board, in seconds? One decimal.","answer":{"value":3.2,"tolerance":0.1,"unit":"s"},"hints":["The budget counts every contact, not only the successful one.","2.6 + 0.4 + 0.2.","3.2 s."],"feedbackCorrect":"3.2 s in total: the successful contact plus two more the record kept.","feedbackWrong":"Add every dwell in the row, including the ones that produced nothing.","explanation":"2.6 + 0.4 + 0.2 = 3.2 s. A single 2.6 s contact is unremarkable; 3.2 s across three contacts means the joint was brought to temperature, lost it, and was brought up again. Repeated heating grows the intermetallic layer and stresses the pad, which is why the count of contacts belongs in the record next to the dwell."},{"id":"s3","type":"numeric","prompt":"The iron delivers about 45 W during contact. How much energy went into the board on B-07, in joules?","answer":{"value":144,"tolerance":2,"unit":"J"},"hints":["Energy is power times time.","45 W x 3.2 s.","144 J."],"feedbackCorrect":"144 J of iron time on one joint.","feedbackWrong":"Multiply the total seconds by the power; J = W x s.","explanation":"45 W for 3.2 s is 144 J. This is the number to put in the record beside the setpoint: a joint that took 144 J is a joint with a thermal problem — a ground plane, a cold preheat, or a tip too small for the pad — and not a joint that needed a hotter setpoint. It becomes the baseline for the next board, which is the whole point of recording a thermal budget."},{"id":"s4","type":"numeric","prompt":"The drawing sets a minimum conductor width of 0.280 mm. The nicked trace measures 0.248 mm. What is the width reduction against that minimum, in percent? One decimal.","answer":{"value":11.4,"tolerance":0.2,"unit":"%"},"hints":["The limit is written against the minimum width on the drawing, not the as-drawn width.","(0.280 - 0.248) / 0.280.","11.4 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"11.4 %: inside Class 2, outside Class 3.","feedbackWrong":"The loss over the minimum width: 0.032 mm on a 0.280 mm trace.","explanation":"0.032 / 0.280 = 11.4 %. The denominator matters more than the numerator. The same 0.248 mm trace passes a Class 2 limit of 20 % and fails a Class 3 limit of 10 %, because IPC-A-600 caps the reduction of the minimum conductor width the drawing already fixes. Dividing by the as-drawn 0.300 mm would give a flattering 17.3 % and still pass Class 2, which is exactly why the standard names the minimum and not the nominal."},{"id":"s5","type":"numeric","prompt":"What is the narrowest that trace may become and still meet the Class 2 limit, in mm?","answer":{"value":0.224,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["Turn the percentage into a width: the limit is 20 % of the 0.280 mm minimum.","0.280 x 0.80.","0.224 mm."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.224 mm is the floor for Class 2 on this drawing.","feedbackWrong":"Subtract the allowed loss from the minimum width, or multiply by (1 - 20 %).","explanation":"0.280 mm less 20 % is 0.224 mm. The board measures 0.248 mm, so it passes with 0.024 mm to spare, a real margin rather than a rounding one. That margin is what the record protects: a repair judged by eye as slightly thinner has no way of saying whether it sits 0.024 mm inside the limit or 0.024 mm outside it."},{"id":"s6","type":"numeric","prompt":"The same repair on a Class 3 board would be judged against 10 %. What is the narrowest acceptable width there, in mm?","answer":{"value":0.252,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"mm"},"hints":["Class 3 allows a tenth of the minimum width, not a fifth.","0.280 x 0.90.","0.252 mm."],"feedbackCorrect":"0.252 mm on Class 3: the same 0.248 mm trace would fail there.","feedbackWrong":"Multiply the minimum width by (1 - 10 %).","explanation":"0.280 x 0.90 = 0.252 mm. At 0.248 mm the trace is 0.004 mm under the Class 3 floor, so the identical physical repair passes Class 2 and fails Class 3 by four microns. That is why the class has to be on the record before the repair: it is set by the product, not by how bad the nick looks, and at this margin nothing but the number separates the two answers."},{"id":"s7","type":"choice","prompt":"What is the disposition of the trace on B-07, a Class 2 board?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept: 20 % is at the limit, not over it, and the record shows the measurement."},{"id":"b","text":"Reject the board: any nick during rework is a repair and repairs are not allowed."},{"id":"c","text":"Accept and do not record it, since the board still works."},{"id":"d","text":"Accept on Class 3 limits, which are looser."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["The limit is a maximum reduction, so anything under it passes.","Class 2 allows 20 %; the board measured 11.4 %.","Accept, and record the measurement, the denominator and the class it was judged against."],"feedbackCorrect":"Accept: 11.4 % is inside the 20 % limit, and both numbers go in the record.","feedbackWrong":"Is the measured reduction over the limit for the class this board actually is?","explanation":"11.4 % against a 20 % limit passes with margin. Recording it is what makes the acceptance durable: a year later the board either has a line that says '0.248 mm against a 0.280 mm minimum, 11.4 %, Class 2 limit 20 %' or it has nothing, and 'it still works' is not a record. Class 3 would have made the same measurement a defect at 0.252 mm, so the class is part of the finding and not a detail of the paperwork."},{"id":"s8","type":"choice","prompt":"B-08 shows dry_flag 1 on its first contact and two contacts in total. What does that row say about the process, rather than about the operator?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The solder wire was the wrong diameter."},{"id":"b","text":"Nothing: a dry contact is an operator error and is not a process signal."},{"id":"c","text":"The joint needed more heat than one contact could deliver, so the preheat, tip size or board support is loading the joint beyond the budget — the same thermal question the wave case raised."},{"id":"d","text":"The setpoint was too low and should be raised for every joint."}],"answer":{"value":"c"},"hints":["A contact that produced no flow means the joint was not at temperature.","Two contacts on the same joint is a repeat of the m08 finding: the thermal path is the constraint.","Fix the heat path, not the setpoint for every joint on the line."],"feedbackCorrect":"The joint is thermally loaded: preheat, tip size or support, not a global setpoint change.","feedbackWrong":"What is the difference between 'this joint is hard to heat' and 'the iron is too cold'?","explanation":"A dry contact is the record's way of saying the joint was not at wetting temperature, and the second contact is the recovery. Raising the setpoint for the whole line would overheat every easy joint to rescue one hard one; the m08 case reached the same conclusion from the wave side. The fix is in the heat path — preheat the board, use a tip that matches the pad, support the board — and the record is what shows the fix worked on the next one."},{"id":"s9","type":"choice","prompt":"What has to be in the rework record for the next engineer to reproduce this result?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The serial number and the date, because the process is standard."},{"id":"b","text":"A photograph of the joint, since numbers cannot describe solder."},{"id":"c","text":"The pass/fail verdict and the operator's initials."},{"id":"d","text":"Required class, setpoint, tip, preheat, dwells and number of contacts, measured trace width with the limit it was judged against, and the inspection result."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["Reproducing a result needs the settings, the measurements and the standard they were judged against.","A verdict without the numbers cannot be re-judged when the class or the standard changes.","Class, settings, measurements, limits, result."],"feedbackCorrect":"Class, settings, measurements, the limit each was judged against, and the result.","feedbackWrong":"Which of these could another engineer use to reach the same decision without asking you?","explanation":"A record that says 'passed' cannot be re-judged when the product class changes or the standard moves from Rev C to Rev D, and that is exactly what happened to this course's own source. Class, setpoint, tip, preheat, dwells, contact count, the measured width against the minimum it was compared with, and the inspection result make the decision reproducible. A photo is useful evidence and not a substitute for the numbers."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Setpoint judged against the band, not against a remembered number","points":10,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"Thermal budget counted over every contact","points":15,"steps":["s2"]},{"criterion":"Energy in joules derived from power and time","points":10,"steps":["s3"]},{"criterion":"Conductor reduction measured against the drawing width","points":15,"steps":["s4"]},{"criterion":"Class 2 width floor computed from the percentage limit","points":10,"steps":["s5"]},{"criterion":"Class 3 floor computed and the same repair shown to fail there","points":10,"steps":["s6"]},{"criterion":"Disposition at the limit accepted with the measurement recorded","points":15,"steps":["s7"]},{"criterion":"Dry contact read as a thermal-load signal, not as a setpoint problem","points":10,"steps":["s8"]},{"criterion":"Record contents named so the decision can be re-judged","points":5,"steps":["s9"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"rework-record","name":"Rework and repair record","status":"available","use":"Log setpoint, tip, preheat, dwell, number of contacts and the measured result against the class limit, so a repair can be defended a year later.","lesson":"m11l05","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"rework-record-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"assembly,operation,tip,setpoint_c,preheat_c,contacts,dwell1_s,dwell2_s,dwell3_s,trace_drawn_mm,trace_min_mm,trace_after_mm,dry_flag\r\n,,,,,,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,,,,,,\r\n,,,,,,,,,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"rework-record-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"assembly,operation,tip,setpoint_c,preheat_c,contacts,dwell1_s,dwell2_s,dwell3_s,trace_drawn_mm,trace_min_mm,trace_after_mm,dry_flag\nB-07,J-01 rework,chisel 1.2 mm,345,95,3,2.6,0.4,0.2,0.300,0.280,0.248,0\nB-08,J-01 rework,chisel 1.2 mm,345,95,2,2.2,0.1,0.0,0.300,0.280,0.280,1\nB-09,J-01 rework,chisel 1.2 mm,345,95,1,2.4,0.0,0.0,0.300,0.280,0.280,0\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"rework-record-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# Rework and repair record\n\n## What this template is for\nLog setpoint, tip, preheat, dwell, number of contacts and the measured result against the class limit, so a repair can be defended a year later.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per rework operation. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `assembly`\n- `operation`\n- `tip`\n- `setpoint_c`\n- `preheat_c`\n- `contacts`\n- `dwell1_s`\n- `dwell2_s`\n- `dwell3_s`\n- `trace_drawn_mm`\n- `trace_min_mm`\n- `trace_after_mm`\n- `dry_flag`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m11-01 (Rework record: a connector joint, a nicked trace and a hand-held iron), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m11l05 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"Thermal budget (lesson m11l02)","href":"#/l/m11l02"},{"label":"Repair limits and classes (lesson m11l04)","href":"#/l/m11l04"},{"label":"Acceptance of rework (lesson m11l05)","href":"#/l/m11l05"}],"sources":["src-009","src-022","src-001"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py","spec":[{"label":"Hand-soldering setpoint","value":330,"to":360,"unit":"°C","note":"Course band (m11l02)"},{"label":"Conductor reduction, Class 2","value":20,"unit":"%","note":"IPC-A-600 (m11l04)"},{"label":"Conductor reduction, Class 3","value":10,"unit":"%","note":"IPC-A-600 (m11l04)"},{"label":"Annular ring, Class 3 external","value":0.05,"unit":"mm","note":"IPC-A-600 (m11l04)"}]},{"caseId":"cx-m14-01","moduleId":"m14","title":"AOI over-rejection on Board X: DPMO, sampling scope and an 8D that is not closed","estMinutes":35,"level":"advanced","scenario":["Board X runs 1,240 boards a day with 620 solder opportunities per board. Last week AOI raised 47 calls against the BGA and the fine-pitch QFP; the defect review confirmed 38 of them and threw 9 out as false calls.","The 8D team contained the suspect lot and needs a sampling plan for the retroactive search: one lot is 3,000 boards, another is 5,000. The control plan requires ISO 2859-1 general inspection level II at AQL 1.0.","A corrective action has been running for six weeks on a different defect family: 96 defects in 6,000 boards before, 21 defects in 7,500 boards after. The agreed effectiveness target is 1,000 ppm.","Compute the metrics the 8D needs, and be honest about what the numbers do and do not prove."],"dataFiles":[{"name":"defect-log.csv","kind":"csv","content":"family,calls,confirmed,false_calls,boards_in_window\nBGA void / non-wet,47,38,9,1240\nQFP bridging,0,0,0,1240\n"},{"name":"capa-window.csv","kind":"csv","content":"phase,defects,boards,ppm\nbefore,96,6000,\nafter,21,7500,\ntarget,,,1000\n"}],"steps":[{"id":"s1","type":"numeric","prompt":"Compute DPMO for Board X from the confirmed defects. DPMO = defects / (boards x opportunities per board) x 1,000,000. One decimal.","answer":{"value":49.4,"tolerance":0.3,"unit":"DPMO"},"hints":["Use confirmed defects, not AOI calls.","38 / (1,240 x 620) = 38 / 768,800.","38 / 768,800 x 1e6 = 49.4."],"feedbackCorrect":"49.4 DPMO — computed from confirmed defects only.","feedbackWrong":"Multiply boards by opportunities first, then divide, then scale to a million.","explanation":"38 / (1,240 x 620) = 38 / 768,800 = 4.94 x 10^-5, which is 49.4 defects per million opportunities. Using the 47 AOI calls instead would inflate the number to about 61 DPMO and turn an inspection problem into a process problem."},{"id":"s2","type":"numeric","prompt":"AOI raised 47 calls of which 38 were confirmed. What is the false-call rate, in percent? One decimal.","answer":{"value":19.1,"tolerance":0.3,"unit":"%"},"hints":["The false calls are the calls that did not survive review.","47 - 38 = 9 false calls out of 47 calls.","9 / 47 = 19.1 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"19.1 % false calls — an inspection capability issue, not a solder issue.","feedbackWrong":"Divide the false calls by the total calls, not by the confirmed defects.","explanation":"9 / 47 = 19.1 %. The course treats false calls as a separate metric from escape rate: a high false-call rate costs review time and erodes trust in the station, but it does not put a bad board in the field the way an escape does."},{"id":"s3","type":"choice","prompt":"For a lot of 3,000 boards, what is the ISO 2859-1 code letter at general inspection level II?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"J"},{"id":"b","text":"K"},{"id":"c","text":"L"},{"id":"d","text":"M"}],"answer":{"value":"b"},"hints":["The lot-size ranges for level II step at 1,200 and at 3,200.","3,000 falls in the 1,201-3,200 band.","That band is code K: n = 125 at AQL 1.0."],"feedbackCorrect":"Code K for 3,000 boards: sample 125, Ac 3, Re 4.","feedbackWrong":"Find the lot-size band first; the letter follows from the band, not from the count.","explanation":"At general inspection level II a lot of 3,000 sits in the 1,201-3,200 band, which is code K: 125 pieces sampled, accept at 3, reject at 4 for AQL 1.0. Code L belongs to the next band (3,201-10,000) and code J to the one below (501-1,200)."},{"id":"s4","type":"numeric","prompt":"For that 3,000-board lot at code K, AQL 1.0, how many pieces are sampled?","answer":{"value":125,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"pieces"},"hints":["Code K fixes the sample size.","It is the n column for K, not a percentage of the lot.","n = 125."],"feedbackCorrect":"125 pieces sampled.","feedbackWrong":"This is a table lookup for code K at AQL 1.0.","explanation":"Code K samples 125 pieces. The accept number is 3 and the reject number 4, so finding 3 or fewer nonconforming units accepts the lot and 4 rejects it — the plan is not a zero-defect rule."},{"id":"s5","type":"choice","prompt":"The retroactive search on the 3,000-board lot finds 3 nonconforming boards. What does the plan say?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Accept the lot against this plan (Ac = 3) — acceptance sampling qualifies the lot, not the process, so the 8D stays open until the ppm target is met."},{"id":"b","text":"Accept the lot and close the 8D."},{"id":"c","text":"Reject and double the sample until zero defects are found."},{"id":"d","text":"Reject the lot: any nonconformity in a containment sample fails it."}],"answer":{"value":"a"},"hints":["Ac is the acceptance number for the plan, and it is not zero.","Ac = 3 means 3 or fewer accepts; Re = 4 is the reject threshold.","Accepting the lot on the sampling plan does not close the 8D."],"feedbackCorrect":"Accept against Ac = 3, and keep the 8D open — sampling says nothing about the cause.","feedbackWrong":"Separate the sampling decision from the corrective-action decision.","explanation":"At AQL 1.0 code K the acceptance number is 3, so three nonconforming boards accept the lot. That is a statement about the lot against an agreed quality level, not evidence that the cause is fixed: acceptance sampling with Ac > 0 always passes some nonconforming units, which is why the 8D is closed on the effectiveness metric, not on the sample."},{"id":"s6","type":"numeric","prompt":"The second lot is 5,000 boards at the same level and AQL. How many pieces does the plan sample?","answer":{"value":200,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"pieces"},"hints":["5,000 falls in the 3,201-10,000 band.","That band is code L.","Code L samples 200 pieces: Ac 5, Re 6."],"feedbackCorrect":"200 pieces at code L (Ac 5, Re 6).","feedbackWrong":"Find the band for 5,000 first, then read n for that code.","explanation":"5,000 boards is code L, which samples 200 pieces with Ac 5 and Re 6 at AQL 1.0. Note that the sample grows far more slowly than the lot: doubling the lot from 3,000 to 5,000 raises the sample from 125 to 200, which is why sampling alone cannot bound a large escape."},{"id":"s7","type":"numeric","prompt":"Corrective-action window: 96 defects in 6,000 boards. What is that rate in ppm?","answer":{"value":16000,"tolerance":30,"unit":"ppm"},"hints":["ppm is defects per million boards.","96 / 6,000 = 0.016.","0.016 x 1e6 = 16,000 ppm."],"feedbackCorrect":"16,000 ppm before the action.","feedbackWrong":"Divide defects by boards, then multiply by a million.","explanation":"96 / 6,000 = 0.016, which is 16,000 defects per million boards. Stating it in ppm rather than percent keeps the comparison with the 1,000 ppm target in one scale."},{"id":"s8","type":"numeric","prompt":"After the action: 21 defects in 7,500 boards. What is the rate in ppm?","answer":{"value":2800,"tolerance":30,"unit":"ppm"},"hints":["Same conversion as the before figure.","21 / 7,500 = 0.0028.","0.0028 x 1e6 = 2,800 ppm."],"feedbackCorrect":"2,800 ppm after the action.","feedbackWrong":"Defects over boards, then scale to a million.","explanation":"21 / 7,500 = 0.0028, so 2,800 ppm. The two windows are different lengths, which is exactly why the comparison is made on a rate and not on a defect count."},{"id":"s9","type":"numeric","prompt":"What is the reduction between the two rates, in percent? One decimal.","answer":{"value":82.5,"tolerance":0.5,"unit":"%"},"hints":["Reduction is measured against the before rate.","(16,000 - 2,800) / 16,000.","13,200 / 16,000 = 82.5 %."],"feedbackCorrect":"82.5 % reduction — a real improvement, and still not the target.","feedbackWrong":"Divide the improvement by the before rate, then multiply by 100.","explanation":"(16,000 - 2,800) / 16,000 = 82.5 %. An 82.5 % reduction sounds like success until it is put against the target: 2,800 ppm is still nearly three times the agreed 1,000 ppm, so by the metric the course uses the action is not yet effective."},{"id":"s10","type":"choice","prompt":"The effectiveness target is 1,000 ppm and the after rate is 2,800 ppm. What is the correct 8D status?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Close the 8D and open a new one for the residual 2,800 ppm."},{"id":"b","text":"Change the target to 3,000 ppm so the action passes."},{"id":"c","text":"Close the 8D: an 82.5 % reduction is a passing result."},{"id":"d","text":"Keep the 8D open: the action improved the rate but did not reach the agreed metric, so effectiveness is not demonstrated."}],"answer":{"value":"d"},"hints":["Effectiveness is judged against the metric agreed before the action.","2,800 is above 1,000.","The course closes an 8D on the metric, not on the improvement."],"feedbackCorrect":"Keep it open — the improvement is real, the target is not met.","feedbackWrong":"Which number was agreed before the action started?","explanation":"An 82.5 % improvement with a residual rate nearly three times the target is a partial result. The course's rule is that the 8D stays open until the effectiveness metric is met with evidence; renegotiating the target after the fact destroys the point of having one, and opening a second 8D for the same cause just moves the paperwork."}],"rubric":[{"criterion":"DPMO computed from confirmed defects with the right denominator","points":10,"steps":["s1"]},{"criterion":"False-call rate computed separately from defect rate","points":10,"steps":["s2"]},{"criterion":"Correct ISO 2859-1 code letter for the lot size","points":10,"steps":["s3"]},{"criterion":"Sample size read for the code, not guessed","points":10,"steps":["s4"]},{"criterion":"Accept/reject decision separated from cause and closure","points":15,"steps":["s5"]},{"criterion":"Second lot handled with its own code and sample","points":10,"steps":["s6"]},{"criterion":"Before and after rates expressed in the same unit","points":10,"steps":["s7"]},{"criterion":"After rate computed on its own window","points":10,"steps":["s8"]},{"criterion":"Reduction computed against the before rate","points":5,"steps":["s9"]},{"criterion":"8D status judged on the agreed metric, not on the improvement","points":10,"steps":["s10"]}],"templates":[{"templateId":"8d","name":"8D problem report","status":"available","use":"Carry a defect from containment through root cause to a verified action and a closed-out escape check.","lesson":"m14l05","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"8d-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"d,title,content,evidence,owner,status\r\n,,,,,\r\n,,,,,\r\n,,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"8d-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"d,title,content,evidence,owner,status\r\nD2,Describe the problem,49.4 DPMO,\"AOI/BGA void family, Board X lot\",process engineer,open\r\nD4,Root cause verified,49.4 DPMO,false-call audit,process engineer,open\r\nD6,Action implemented and measured,16000 ppm,before-window count,process engineer,open\r\nD7,Action verified in a second window,2800 ppm,after-window count,process engineer,open\r\nD8,Effectiveness against the target,\"Keep the 8D open: the action improved the rate but did not reach the agreed metric, so effectiveness is not demonstrated.\",agreed metric,process engineer,open\r\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"8d-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# 8D problem report\n\n## What this template is for\nCarry a defect from containment through root cause to a verified action and a closed-out escape check.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per 8D report. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `d`\n- `title`\n- `content`\n- `evidence`\n- `owner`\n- `status`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m14-01 (AOI over-rejection on Board X: DPMO, sampling scope and an 8D that is not closed), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m14l05 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]},{"templateId":"pareto","name":"Defect Pareto","status":"available","use":"Rank defect families by count so the largest loss gets the first action, and keep the ranking next to the 8D it justified.","lesson":"m14l01","variants":[{"variant":"blank","file":"pareto-blank.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Blank","content":"family,calls,confirmed,false_calls,boards_in_window\r\n,,,,\r\n,,,,\r\n,,,,\r\n"},{"variant":"example","file":"pareto-example.csv","mime":"text/csv","label":"Example","content":"family,calls,confirmed,false_calls,boards_in_window\nBGA void / non-wet,47,38,9,1240\nQFP bridging,0,0,0,1240\n"},{"variant":"guide","file":"pareto-guide.md","mime":"text/markdown","label":"Guide","content":"# Defect Pareto\n\n## What this template is for\nRank defect families by count so the largest loss gets the first action, and keep the ranking next to the 8D it justified.\n\n## How to fill it in\n1. Copy one row per item. Do not merge rows: one aperture, one reel, one interval or one 8D discipline per line.\n2. Keep the column order. The columns below are the ones the course's own worked examples use.\n3. Leave a cell blank rather than writing a guess. A blank cell is a question for the engineer; a guessed number is a defect in the record.\n\n## Columns\n- `family`\n- `calls`\n- `confirmed`\n- `false_calls`\n- `boards_in_window`\n\n## Worked example\nThe example variant of this file is the data file from case cx-m14-01 (AOI over-rejection on Board X: DPMO, sampling scope and an 8D that is not closed), filled in with Board X numbers.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\nEvery value in the example is produced by scripts/gen_cases.py and checked by scripts/check-cases.js. If you change a number in the example, the checker fails: that is deliberate.\n\n## Course link\nSee lesson m14l01 for the reasoning behind each column.\n"}]}],"toolLinks":[{"label":"Defect data before opinions (lesson m14l01)","href":"#/l/m14l01"},{"label":"Corrective vs preventive action (lesson m14l05)","href":"#/l/m14l05"},{"label":"8D workshop (lesson m14l07)","href":"#/l/m14l07"}],"sources":["src-006","src-001"],"generatedBy":"scripts/gen_cases.py"}]},"templates":{"version":"v1","date":"2026-10-31","notice":"Training template. Adapt to your quality system.","templates":[{"slug":"incoming-checklist","order":1,"title":"Incoming inspection checklist","kind":"docx","modules":["m01"],"cases":["cx-m01-01"],"lesson":"m01l04","standard":"ISO 2859-1 (sampling), IPC-6012 (bare-board acceptability)","use":"Record what was checked on the dock, what was sampled, which lot the sample came from, and what the disposition is — so a quarantine can be explained a year later.","files":{"blank":"incoming-checklist-blank-v1-2026-10-31.docx","example":"incoming-checklist-example-v1-2026-10-31.docx","guide":"incoming-checklist-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":37622,"example":37857,"guide":1884}},{"slug":"msl-log","order":2,"title":"MSL exposure log","kind":"xlsx","modules":["m02"],"cases":["cx-m02-01"],"lesson":"m02l04","standard":"J-STD-033 (floor life, dry storage, bake)","use":"Track the clock on every moisture-sensitive reel: floor life, what has been counted against it, what is left, and what the bake decision is.","files":{"blank":"msl-log-blank-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","example":"msl-log-example-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","guide":"msl-log-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":8091,"example":8320,"guide":2106}},{"slug":"stencil-check","order":3,"title":"Stencil inspection sheet","kind":"xlsx","modules":["m03","m04"],"cases":["cx-m03-01","cx-m04-01"],"lesson":"m03l05","standard":"IPC-7525 (area and aspect ratio), IPC-7526 (stencil cleaning)","use":"Compute the release indicators for every aperture on the board and decide which need a stepped foil, before the first print.","files":{"blank":"stencil-check-blank-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","example":"stencil-check-example-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","guide":"stencil-check-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":7870,"example":8021,"guide":2172}},{"slug":"reflow-profile","order":4,"title":"Reflow profile log","kind":"xlsx","modules":["m06"],"cases":["cx-m06-01"],"lesson":"m06l07","standard":"IPC-7530 (profiling), J-STD-020 (classification temperatures)","use":"Record the thermocouple readings, compute the ramp rates and the time above liquidus, and decide whether the profile is inside the window.","files":{"blank":"reflow-profile-blank-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","example":"reflow-profile-example-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","guide":"reflow-profile-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":8281,"example":8460,"guide":2160}},{"slug":"design-feedback-pack","order":15,"title":"Design feedback pack","kind":"xlsx","modules":["m15"],"cases":["cx-m15-01"],"lesson":"m15l04","standard":"The plant's own production data; the placement acceptance and the fiducial geometry are Board X's","use":"Turn a production result into a design ask with a number in it and a price on it, so the design team can act on the next revision.","files":{"blank":"design-feedback-pack-blank-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","example":"design-feedback-pack-example-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","guide":"design-feedback-pack-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":8483,"example":8624,"guide":1492}},{"slug":"coverage-model","order":14,"title":"Test coverage model","kind":"xlsx","modules":["m13"],"cases":["cx-m13-01"],"lesson":"m13l05","standard":"The plant's own defect model and screen probabilities; node coverage is a test-program measure, not a standard's","use":"Work out what a series of screens actually catches, class by class, instead of quoting the share of joints that carry a probe.","files":{"blank":"coverage-model-blank-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","example":"coverage-model-example-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","guide":"coverage-model-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":8489,"example":8889,"guide":1549}},{"slug":"cleanliness-record","order":13,"title":"Cleanliness and coating record","kind":"xlsx","modules":["m12"],"cases":["cx-m12-01"],"lesson":"m12l03","standard":"IPC-TM-650 2.3.25 (ROSE); the customer's limit is a customer value, not the reference one","use":"Report an ionic result with the area it was divided by, the blank it was subtracted from and the local site it hides, then work out the coating volume from the film the drawing asks for.","files":{"blank":"cleanliness-record-blank-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","example":"cleanliness-record-example-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","guide":"cleanliness-record-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":8610,"example":8837,"guide":1546}},{"slug":"rework-record","order":12,"title":"Rework and repair record","kind":"xlsx","modules":["m11"],"cases":["cx-m11-01"],"lesson":"m11l06","standard":"IPC-7711/7721 (rework and repair method), with the width limit from IPC-A-600 (20 % Class 2, 10 % Class 3)","use":"Record what the repair did to the board — heat, contacts, the trace it touched — and judge the damage against the class the customer requires.","files":{"blank":"rework-record-blank-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","example":"rework-record-example-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","guide":"rework-record-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":9233,"example":9567,"guide":1540}},{"slug":"selective-program-record","order":11,"title":"Selective solder program record","kind":"xlsx","modules":["m10"],"cases":["cx-m10-01"],"lesson":"m10l05","standard":"IPC-A-610 (joint acceptance), IPC-7711/7721 (rework of what the program gets wrong)","use":"Check the nozzle against the joint it has to fill, and put the cycle time on the sheet before anyone promises a rate.","files":{"blank":"selective-program-record-blank-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","example":"selective-program-record-example-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","guide":"selective-program-record-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":8216,"example":8424,"guide":1515}},{"slug":"wave-run-log","order":10,"title":"Wave solder run log","kind":"xlsx","modules":["m09"],"cases":["cx-m09-01"],"lesson":"m09l07","standard":"IPC-A-610 (joint acceptance); the wave windows are the machine's, taken from the course and the equipment datasheet","use":"Record the settings a wave run actually ran at, compute the contact time and the pot margin each one gives, and keep the findings on the same page as the record that explains them.","files":{"blank":"wave-run-log-blank-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","example":"wave-run-log-example-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","guide":"wave-run-log-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":8362,"example":8538,"guide":1601}},{"slug":"tht-fill-record","order":9,"title":"Through-hole fill record","kind":"xlsx","modules":["m08"],"cases":["cx-m08-01"],"lesson":"m08l05","standard":"IPC-A-610 (vertical barrel fill, and the inner-layer exception)","use":"Record the barrel fill on every through-hole joint, compute it against the board thickness, and disposition it against the class that applies — without letting the thermal-plane exception become the rule.","files":{"blank":"tht-fill-record-blank-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","example":"tht-fill-record-example-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","guide":"tht-fill-record-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":8299,"example":8567,"guide":1548}},{"slug":"placement-record","order":8,"title":"Placement audit record","kind":"xlsx","modules":["m05"],"cases":["cx-m05-01"],"lesson":"m05l06","standard":"IPC-A-610 (placement and soldering criteria); machine capability targets are the plant's, not a standard's","use":"Turn two fiducial readings into the translation and the rotation the placement report leaves out, and carry each one out to the parts that sit furthest from the rotation centre.","files":{"blank":"placement-record-blank-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","example":"placement-record-example-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","guide":"placement-record-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":8403,"example":8613,"guide":1583}},{"slug":"spi-record","order":7,"title":"SPI print record","kind":"xlsx","modules":["m04"],"cases":["cx-m04-01"],"lesson":"m04l05","standard":"IPC-7527 (solder paste printing), IPC-7525 (stencil design)","use":"Record every deposit's volume against the nominal the stencil actually cuts, and keep the mean from hiding a print that has two humps.","files":{"blank":"spi-record-blank-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","example":"spi-record-example-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","guide":"spi-record-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":9046,"example":9400,"guide":1509}},{"slug":"8d-report","order":5,"title":"8D problem report","kind":"docx","modules":["m14"],"cases":["cx-m14-01"],"lesson":"m14l05","standard":"8D discipline; IATF 16949 / AS9100 corrective-action expectations","use":"Take a defect from containment to closure in the standard eight disciplines, with the numbers that prove each one.","files":{"blank":"8d-report-blank-v1-2026-10-31.docx","example":"8d-report-example-v1-2026-10-31.docx","guide":"8d-report-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":37931,"example":38681,"guide":1859}},{"slug":"pareto-defects","order":6,"title":"Defect Pareto sheet","kind":"xlsx","modules":["m07","m14"],"cases":["cx-m07-01","cx-m14-01"],"lesson":"m14l04","standard":"Pareto analysis; defect classification per IPC-A-610 class","use":"Rank defect types by count, carry the cumulative share, and show which few types account for most of the loss.","files":{"blank":"pareto-defects-blank-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","example":"pareto-defects-example-v1-2026-10-31.xlsx","guide":"pareto-defects-guide-v1-2026-10-31.md"},"bytes":{"blank":7451,"example":7727,"guide":2024}}]},"reference":{"schemaVersion":1,"generatedBy":"scripts/build-reference.js","generatedFor":"phase 5: glossary and formula reference","counts":{"lessons":92,"terms":309,"formulas":11},"terms":[{"term":"8D","def":"An eight-discipline problem-solving report structure used for escapes and recurring defects.","definedIn":"m14l03","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Accept number","def":"The largest number of defective units in the sample that still allows the lot to be accepted.","definedIn":"m01l03","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Acceptable quality level","def":"The quality level at which a sampling plan accepts a lot with high probability when the supplier's process runs at that average.","definedIn":"m01l03","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Accuracy","def":"How close the commanded placement position is to the true board location.","definedIn":"m05l01","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Action limit","def":"The measured value at which the process is stopped or investigated, set from capability and geometry.","definedIn":"m05l04","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Activity","def":"The ability of a flux to remove oxides from the metal surface during soldering.","definedIn":"m09l02","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Air knife","def":"A timed air or nitrogen curtain that removes excess flux from the board surface.","definedIn":"m10l04","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Annular clearance","def":"The radial gap between the finished hole wall and the lead.","definedIn":"m08l01","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Annular ring","def":"The width of copper remaining around a drilled hole after plating.","definedIn":"m11l04","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Aperture reduction","def":"Deliberately making the stencil opening smaller than the land to control paste volume and bridging.","definedIn":"m03l04","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Area ratio","def":"Aperture opening area divided by the stencil sidewall area, the usual indicator of paste release.","definedIn":"m03l01","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":["m03l02","m03l03","m04l01"]},{"term":"Area reduction","def":"The percentage by which the aperture area is made smaller than the pad area.","definedIn":"m04l01","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Aspect ratio","def":"Aperture width divided by stencil thickness, taken on the narrowest dimension of a rectangular aperture.","definedIn":"m03l01","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":["m03l02","m03l03","m04l01"]},{"term":"Aspect ratio (plated through-hole)","def":"Hole depth divided by hole diameter, which governs how easily solder fills the barrel.","definedIn":"m08l02","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":["m09l06"]},{"term":"Attenuation length","def":"The distance over which the temperature rise from a source falls by a factor of e.","definedIn":"m10l03","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Bake","def":"Controlled heating that drives absorbed moisture out of a package so the floor-life clock can restart.","definedIn":"m02l02","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Bed-of-nails","def":"A fixture with spring-loaded probes that contact all test points at once.","definedIn":"m13l01","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Bimodal histogram","def":"A distribution with two peaks, showing that two different populations are being measured together.","definedIn":"m04l05","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Blank correction","def":"Subtracting contamination contributed by solvent, container and handling from the measured result.","definedIn":"m12l03","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Blowhole","def":"A void or crater in the joint caused by expanding trapped volatiles.","definedIn":"m09l06","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Board clearance","def":"The gap between the wave crest and the board underside at wave entry.","definedIn":"m09l04","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Boundary sample","def":"A physical specimen showing the limit of acceptability, used to align inspectors.","definedIn":"m14l07","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Boundary scan","def":"An electrical test method that uses scan cells inside devices to drive and observe package pins.","definedIn":"m07l01","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":["m13l01"]},{"term":"Bounded change","def":"A single, limited change to the process that can be verified against a defined outcome.","definedIn":"m06l06","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Bounded change control","def":"Changing one variable at a time and verifying the outcome before the next change.","definedIn":"m06l07","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"BSDL","def":"The boundary-scan description language file that tells the tester how a device's scan cells are arranged.","definedIn":"m13l04","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Bypass","def":"A joint that the wave failed to wet at all.","definedIn":"m09l06","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Calibration interval","def":"The period within which an instrument or axis must be recalibrated.","definedIn":"m05l05","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"CAPA","def":"Corrective and preventive action, the quality-system record behind the 8D.","definedIn":"m14l05","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Capture velocity","def":"The air speed at the extraction inlet needed to pull fume away from the joint.","definedIn":"m11l01","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Carrier","def":"A fixture that holds the board flat through the wave.","definedIn":"m08l04","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Cavitation","def":"The formation and collapse of vapour bubbles in a liquid that dislodges contamination.","definedIn":"m12l02","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Centre offset","def":"The translation of a component centre from its intended position, reported as X and Y.","definedIn":"m05l06","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Certificate of analysis","def":"A supplier report of measured or tested values for named characteristics of a specific lot.","definedIn":"m01l02","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":["m01l05"]},{"term":"Certificate of analysis (CoA)","def":"Supplier report of measured or tested values for named characteristics of a specific lot — alloy, metal content, flux chemistry, plating thickness and similar.","definedIn":"m01l01","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Certificate of conformance","def":"A supplier statement that the delivered lot conforms to the purchase order and the acceptance criteria it references.","definedIn":"m01l02","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Certificate of conformance (CoC)","def":"Supplier declaration that a lot meets the purchase order and the referenced acceptance criteria; it is evidence of declared conformity, not of inspection by the buyer.","definedIn":"m01l01","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Change control","def":"The approval route for a documented change to a process or specification.","definedIn":"m14l05","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Change point","def":"A point in time at which a process measurement shifts, often following a deliberate change.","definedIn":"m14l02","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Classification drill","def":"A structured exercise in naming defect type and disposition from images.","definedIn":"m07l06","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Classification temperature","def":"The peak temperature J-STD-020 allows for a package, set by thickness and volume.","definedIn":"m06l01","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":["m06l05"]},{"term":"Clinch","def":"Bending the protruding lead to hold the component during handling and soldering.","definedIn":"m08l03","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Cold chain","def":"The controlled temperature path a material follows from manufacture through storage and transport to use.","definedIn":"m01l05","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Cold joint","def":"A dull, grainy joint that never wet properly because of insufficient heat or poor flux activity.","definedIn":"m07l06","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Collapse","def":"The reduction in BGA ball height during reflow as the ball settles onto the land.","definedIn":"m07l04","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":["m11l03"]},{"term":"Common-cause spread","def":"The stable, ordinary variation of a process that is running as designed.","definedIn":"m04l02","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Component library","def":"The stored geometry, orientation and handling data the machine uses for each part number.","definedIn":"m05l05","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Concession","def":"A written approval to use material that does not meet the stated requirement.","definedIn":"m01l07","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Condition","def":"An observable state that may or may not be a defect depending on the class and criteria applied.","definedIn":"m07l05","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Conductive anodic filament","def":"A copper filament that grows through laminate along glass fibres under bias and moisture.","definedIn":"m12l01","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Contact resistance","def":"The resistance added by the probe-to-pad interface, which must stay low and stable.","definedIn":"m13l02","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Contact time","def":"The interval a board spends in contact with the solder wave.","definedIn":"m09l01","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Containment","def":"Immediate action to stop nonconforming material from moving further into the process or to the customer.","definedIn":"m01l07","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":["m02l06","m14l03"]},{"term":"Control plan","def":"The document stating what is measured, how often, against what limit, and the reaction when it fails.","definedIn":"m14l06","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Coplanarity","def":"How well the leads or terminations of a component lie in one plane.","definedIn":"m05l06","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Cost of poor quality","def":"The cost of scrap, rework, inspection and lost capacity caused by defects.","definedIn":"m15l02","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Counterfeit part","def":"A part whose identity, grade or origin is misrepresented, including re-marked, refurbished or unauthorised copies.","definedIn":"m01l04","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Cp","def":"The specification width divided by six standard deviations, a measure of spread alone.","definedIn":"m04l05","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Cpk","def":"The distance from the mean to the nearer specification limit in standard deviations, a measure of spread and centring.","definedIn":"m04l05","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Crest height","def":"The height of the solder wave above the nozzle opening.","definedIn":"m09l07","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Cumulative bake time","def":"The total time a package has spent above 90 °C, limited because solderability degrades with it.","definedIn":"m02l04","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Cumulative exposure","def":"The total time a device has spent outside a compliant moisture barrier bag since its last valid reset.","definedIn":"m02l03","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Date code","def":"The manufacturer's code for the production date of a lot, read against that manufacturer's convention.","definedIn":"m01l04","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"DCR","def":"Design change request, the engineering request that initiates a change before formal approval.","definedIn":"m15l03","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Defect coverage","def":"The fraction of expected defect modes a method can detect, weighted by occurrence.","definedIn":"m13l05","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Defect density","def":"Defects normalised to board area or joint count, allowing comparison across sizes.","definedIn":"m15l02","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Defects per million opportunities","def":"A defect rate normalised by the number of countable opportunities in the product.","definedIn":"m15l02","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Delamination (board laminate)","def":"Separation between layers of laminate within the board.","definedIn":"m11l04","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Delamination (package)","def":"Separation between layers inside a package, such as mold compound and die or substrate.","definedIn":"m02l01","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Design rule","def":"A measurable constraint applied during layout or stencil design.","definedIn":"m15l01","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Design variable","def":"A drawing, layer, dimension or library attribute the designer controls.","definedIn":"m15l01","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Detection escape","def":"A defect that passed the inspection or test intended to catch it.","definedIn":"m14l02","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":["m14l04","m14l07"]},{"term":"Detection limit","def":"The smallest value an analytical method can report as distinguishable from background.","definedIn":"m12l03","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Detection probability","def":"The likelihood that a method reports a present defect of a given class.","definedIn":"m13l05","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Deviation","def":"A limited, time-bound and approved acceptance of a lot outside a requirement, with the exposure and the additional checks recorded.","definedIn":"m01l01","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":["m11l06"]},{"term":"Dewetting","def":"Solder that spreads and then retracts, leaving thin irregular areas on the pad.","definedIn":"m06l02","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":["m06l06"]},{"term":"Dewetting (conformal coating)","def":"Coating pulling back from an area and leaving it thinly covered or uncovered.","definedIn":"m12l06","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Diametral clearance","def":"Hole diameter minus lead diameter at the joint.","definedIn":"m08l05","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Die shadow","def":"The area under the die where the package mass changes heat flow and paste behaviour.","definedIn":"m07l04","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Disagreement audit","def":"A second review of a sample of dispositions to test consistency.","definedIn":"m07l03","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Disposition","def":"The recorded decision on a nonconforming item: accept, reject, rework, repair, scrap or use-as-is.","definedIn":"m07l05","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":["m11l04","m11l06"]},{"term":"Dissipative","def":"A surface resistance range that bleeds charge away slowly enough to protect sensitive devices.","definedIn":"m12l06","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Disturbed joint","def":"A joint whose component moved while the solder was solidifying, leaving a rough surface.","definedIn":"m11l02","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Document control","def":"The system that holds current revisions and marks superseded ones obsolete.","definedIn":"m15l05","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Documented exception","def":"A stated allowance in the standard that permits a lower value under defined conditions.","definedIn":"m08l05","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Drainage (flux and gas venting)","def":"The path that lets flux and air escape from under a board or mask.","definedIn":"m08l04","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Drainage (wave soldering)","def":"How readily solder leaves the joint after the wave passes.","definedIn":"m09l05","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Dross","def":"Oxide and intermetallic debris that forms on molten solder.","definedIn":"m09l01","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":["m09l07"]},{"term":"Dry joint","def":"A joint that received too little solder to wet and form properly.","definedIn":"m08l03","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Dry pack","def":"The moisture barrier bag, desiccant and humidity indicator card that protect a moisture-sensitive device before use.","definedIn":"m02l04","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Dry-film thickness","def":"The coating thickness remaining after solvents have evaporated and cure is complete.","definedIn":"m12l04","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Dwell","def":"The time the board underside spends in contact with the solder wave.","definedIn":"m09l04","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Dye-and-pry","def":"A destructive test that reveals cracks by forcing dye into them and separating the part.","definedIn":"m05l02","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"ECN","def":"Engineering change notice, the formal record that authorises a design change.","definedIn":"m15l01","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Effectiveness metric","def":"The measurable rate used to prove that a corrective action worked.","definedIn":"m14l05","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Engineering disposition","def":"The formal decision on whether a nonconforming assembly may be used, repaired or scrapped.","definedIn":"m11l05","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"EPA","def":"An ESD protected area, the controlled zone where sensitive assemblies are handled.","definedIn":"m12l02","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Equipotential bonding","def":"Connecting conductive items so they sit at the same potential and cannot discharge into each other.","definedIn":"m02l05","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Escape","def":"A defect the inspection did not call and a later stage or the field found.","definedIn":"m07l02","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":["m13l05","m13l06"]},{"term":"ESD protected area","def":"A defined space in which all conductive items and personnel are bonded and sensitive items are handled under documented controls.","definedIn":"m02l05","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Excursion","def":"A period in which a material left its specified storage or transport temperature range.","definedIn":"m01l05","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Expected deposit volume","def":"Aperture volume corrected for taper and multiplied by transfer efficiency.","definedIn":"m03l05","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"EXTEST","def":"The scan instruction that drives and observes package pins for interconnect testing.","definedIn":"m13l04","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"False call","def":"An inspection call that verification shows was not a defect.","definedIn":"m07l02","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":["m13l03","m14l01"]},{"term":"Fault tree","def":"A top-down logic diagram using AND and OR gates to decompose a failure event.","definedIn":"m14l02","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Feeder map","def":"The table that assigns each part number to a physical feeder slot.","definedIn":"m05l05","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Fiducial","def":"A high-contrast mark used by the placement machine to establish coordinates.","definedIn":"m15l04","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Fill factor","def":"Measured deposit volume divided by the theoretical aperture volume.","definedIn":"m04l07","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Fill height","def":"The height the solder reaches inside the plated hole, measured from the exit side.","definedIn":"m08l01","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Fine pitch","def":"Component lead spacing small enough that aperture dimensions approach the limits of paste release.","definedIn":"m03l02","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"First article","def":"The first unit built to a changed design, inspected in full.","definedIn":"m15l03","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"First-article inspection","def":"A full check of the first unit produced after a change.","definedIn":"m14l06","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"First-in, first-out","def":"Consuming the oldest acceptable lot first so material does not expire in stores.","definedIn":"m01l05","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"First-pass yield","def":"Units completed without rework divided by units started.","definedIn":"m07l03","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Fish-eye","def":"A circular area where coating fails to wet the surface, leaving bare substrate.","definedIn":"m12l06","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Floor life","def":"The time a moisture-sensitive component may spend out of its dry pack at a stated ambient condition before reflow, assigned by moisture sensitivity level.","definedIn":"m01l02","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":["m01l06","m02l01"]},{"term":"Floor-life deviation","def":"A batch whose recorded exposure exceeds the allowed floor life before reflow.","definedIn":"m02l06","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Floor-life suspension","def":"The stop of floor-life accumulation that J-STD-033 grants at 5 % RH or less in qualified dry storage.","definedIn":"m02l04","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Flux activation range","def":"The temperature band in which the flux removes oxides effectively.","definedIn":"m09l07","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Flux migration","def":"Flux travelling away from the intended joint onto neighbouring pads or components.","definedIn":"m10l01","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Flux-cored wire","def":"Solder wire with a flux core that cleans the interface as it melts.","definedIn":"m11l02","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Flux-on-the-fly","def":"Flux applied immediately before solder contact so it stays active at wetting temperature.","definedIn":"m10l02","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Fluxometer","def":"An instrument that measures the flux solids deposited per unit area.","definedIn":"m09l02","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":["m09l04"]},{"term":"Fog height","def":"The height of the flux fog applied to the board underside before the wave.","definedIn":"m09l02","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":["m09l03"]},{"term":"FTIR conversion","def":"A spectroscopic check of how completely the coating has cured.","definedIn":"m12l05","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Global fiducial","def":"A board or panel reference mark used to establish origin, scale and rotation for the whole board.","definedIn":"m05l03","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Golden board","def":"A known-good reference board used to build and check the inspection program.","definedIn":"m07l02","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":["m13l02"]},{"term":"Guard","def":"A driven node that isolates a component or net so it can be measured in circuit.","definedIn":"m13l02","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Guard circuit","def":"A driven node that isolates a component so its value can be measured in circuit.","definedIn":"m13l01","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Heat sink","def":"A large copper area that conducts heat away from a joint.","definedIn":"m06l04","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"High mix","def":"A production profile with many different assemblies in small quantities.","definedIn":"m13l06","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Hoof tip","def":"A curved tip shape that carries a solder bead to a joint, useful on through-hole and large pads.","definedIn":"m11l01","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Horizontal deployment","def":"Applying a corrective action to other products, lines or sites with the same risk.","definedIn":"m14l04","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Humidity indicator card","def":"A card in a dry pack that changes colour to show whether the pack exceeded a stated humidity threshold.","definedIn":"m01l02","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":["m02l02","m02l04"]},{"term":"Icicle","def":"A pointed solder projection formed on a through-hole joint or lead during solidification.","definedIn":"m07l06","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"IDCODE","def":"A device identification register readable through the scan port.","definedIn":"m13l04","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Insertion force","def":"The force needed to push a lead into a plated hole, an indicator of clearance and burrs.","definedIn":"m08l02","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Interface temperature","def":"The temperature at the boundary between solder and the surfaces being joined.","definedIn":"m11l02","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Intermetallic compound","def":"The brittle layer formed between solder and the pad metal during melting.","definedIn":"m06l02","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Ion chromatography","def":"A test that measures ionic contamination left by flux residues.","definedIn":"m09l01","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":["m12l03"]},{"term":"Ionizer","def":"Equipment that neutralises static charge by delivering balanced positive and negative ions to the air.","definedIn":"m02l05","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"IPC-CC-830","def":"The qualification and performance standard for conformal coating materials.","definedIn":"m12l04","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Joint group","def":"A set of joints processed with one nozzle path, flux application and thermal profile.","definedIn":"m10l01","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Keep-out","def":"An area that must stay uncoated, defined in the coating program or mask layout.","definedIn":"m12l05","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Keepout","def":"An area kept clear of components or copper for a process reason.","definedIn":"m15l04","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Laminar X-ray","def":"Tomographic X-ray that reconstructs a slice through the assembly.","definedIn":"m07l01","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":["m07l04"]},{"term":"Land pattern","def":"The copper geometry on the board that the aperture and the component termination must match.","definedIn":"m03l04","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Latent defect","def":"A fault that is present but does not show under the test conditions applied.","definedIn":"m13l01","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Latent ESD failure","def":"Damage that leaves a part working at test and failing later in service.","definedIn":"m02l06","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Lead-in and lead-out","def":"The non-soldering travel into and away from a joint.","definedIn":"m10l04","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Liquidus","def":"The temperature above which an alloy is fully molten.","definedIn":"m06l02","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Local fiducial","def":"A reference mark near a fine-pitch area that compensates local distortion or warpage.","definedIn":"m05l03","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Low-energy zone","def":"The region behind an obstacle where the wave arrives weakened and cooled.","definedIn":"m09l05","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Manufacturer lot traceability","def":"Evidence linking the packaged parts to a specific manufacturing lot at the original manufacturer.","definedIn":"m01l04","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Matched conditions","def":"A comparison where product, line, materials and process settings are held comparable.","definedIn":"m15l02","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Material review board","def":"The cross-functional group that dispositions nonconforming material and records the basis for its decision.","definedIn":"m01l07","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Measurement confidence","def":"The SPI system's own rating of how reliable a given reading is.","definedIn":"m04l02","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Measurement integrity","def":"The ability of an inspection system to detect defects without inventing them.","definedIn":"m14l01","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Measurement report","def":"The supplier's record of measured stencil characteristics against the issued drawing.","definedIn":"m03l05","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Meniscus","def":"The curved solder surface at the top of a filled hole or at the joint edge.","definedIn":"m09l06","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":["m10l02"]},{"term":"Metal load","def":"The percentage of metal powder by weight in the solder paste.","definedIn":"m04l04","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Mini-wave","def":"A small localised solder wave formed by a nozzle, used for selective joints.","definedIn":"m10l01","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Misprint","def":"A paste deposit that is displaced, incomplete or absent relative to the aperture.","definedIn":"m07l06","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Moisture barrier bag","def":"A foil-laminate bag that slows moisture ingress and carries the MSL and exposure data.","definedIn":"m02l02","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":["m02l03"]},{"term":"Moisture sensitivity level","def":"The J-STD-020 classification of how much ambient exposure a non-hermetic package tolerates before reflow.","definedIn":"m02l01","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":["m10l03"]},{"term":"Mouse bite","def":"A breakaway tab with drilled perforations used to separate a board from its panel.","definedIn":"m15l04","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Near-eutectic","def":"An alloy close to the composition with the lowest melting point in its system.","definedIn":"m06l05","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Nitrogen reflow","def":"Reflow in a nitrogen atmosphere that limits oxide formation during melting.","definedIn":"m06l05","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Node coverage","def":"The fraction of electrical nodes a test method exercises.","definedIn":"m13l05","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Nozzle map","def":"The machine table that pairs each component with its nozzle and vacuum setting.","definedIn":"m05l01","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Occurrence root cause","def":"The process condition that created the defect.","definedIn":"m14l04","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Organic solderability preservative","def":"An organic coating on copper that protects solderability and is displaced during soldering.","definedIn":"m01l06","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Overtravel","def":"The extra downward travel after first contact, which establishes vacuum without compressing the part.","definedIn":"m05l02","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Pad-level profile","def":"A temperature record taken at the joint rather than at the oven air.","definedIn":"m06l04","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Pallet opening","def":"The cut-out that exposes a joint to the wave through the fixture.","definedIn":"m09l05","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Panelisation","def":"The arrangement of boards on a production panel, which a design change can affect.","definedIn":"m15l03","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Parallel efficiency","def":"The fraction of the ideal multi-probe throughput a tester actually achieves.","definedIn":"m13l03","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Pareto","def":"A ranked bar chart of defect counts or rates by category, used to choose where to work first.","definedIn":"m14l01","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Particle count","def":"The number of particles above a stated size in a defined area or volume.","definedIn":"m12l03","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Paste relief","def":"A reduction or splitting of paste aperture area to control volume and voiding.","definedIn":"m15l04","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Paste roll","def":"The bead of paste that rolls ahead of the squeegee and feeds the apertures.","definedIn":"m04l03","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Paste-in-pad","def":"A repair method that prints or dispenses paste onto the land pattern before placing the component.","definedIn":"m11l03","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Patch test","def":"A rapid extraction test that estimates ionic contamination over a defined area.","definedIn":"m12l06","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"PFMEA","def":"Process failure mode and effects analysis, the risk document the 8D updates.","definedIn":"m14l05","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":["m14l06"]},{"term":"Pick height","def":"The nozzle Z position at which it contacts the component in the feeder or tray.","definedIn":"m05l01","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":["m05l02"]},{"term":"Pilot build","def":"A limited production run used to confirm a change before release.","definedIn":"m15l05","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Pilot panel","def":"A limited build used to prove a change before full release.","definedIn":"m15l01","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Pitch class","def":"The component lead spacing band that governs aperture and foil choices.","definedIn":"m04l01","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Plating thickness","def":"The copper thickness on the hole wall, which sets current capacity and wall strength.","definedIn":"m08l01","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Pogo pin","def":"A spring-loaded contact used in bed-of-nails fixtures to press onto a test point.","definedIn":"m13l03","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":["m13l06"]},{"term":"Point load","def":"Force concentrated on a small area, which is what causes cracks in brittle components.","definedIn":"m05l02","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Poka-yoke","def":"A design or process feature that prevents an error from being made.","definedIn":"m14l04","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Polarity defect","def":"A polarised component placed in the reverse orientation.","definedIn":"m05l04","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Popcorn effect","def":"Package cracking or interface separation caused by moisture vaporising during reflow.","definedIn":"m02l01","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Pot temperature","def":"The temperature of the molten solder in the wave reservoir.","definedIn":"m09l07","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Pre-production sample","def":"A controlled first run inspected before the line is released.","definedIn":"m05l05","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Probe mark","def":"The indentation or witness mark a probe leaves on a test pad.","definedIn":"m13l03","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Process capability","def":"How well a process holds its specification, stated as a measurable index.","definedIn":"m14l07","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Process qualification","def":"Formal evidence that a process meets requirements under defined conditions.","definedIn":"m06l07","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Process window","def":"The range of print metrics that produces acceptable joints for a product.","definedIn":"m04l02","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":["m08l02"]},{"term":"Program revision","def":"The versioned record of path, dwell, pass and process settings.","definedIn":"m10l05","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Projected height","def":"The obstacle dimension that faces the wave travel direction.","definedIn":"m09l05","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Projection image","def":"A 2D X-ray image in which every layer contributes to the same picture.","definedIn":"m07l01","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Pull test","def":"A mechanical test that measures the force needed to separate a joint.","definedIn":"m10l05","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Qualified deviation","def":"A documented engineering approval to use a value outside the issued specification.","definedIn":"m03l05","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Qualifying bake","def":"A bake performed to the J-STD-033 condition for the package and MSL, recorded and followed by proper repacking.","definedIn":"m02l03","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Quarantine","def":"Controlled hold on a lot whose conformity is uncertain, preserved until evidence, retest, supplier review or an approved disposition closes it.","definedIn":"m01l01","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":["m01l04","m01l07"]},{"term":"Radial clearance","def":"Half the diametral clearance, the gap on one side of the lead.","definedIn":"m08l05","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Re-inspection","def":"The inspection of a repaired area and its surroundings against the required class.","definedIn":"m11l05","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Reaction plan","def":"The defined response when a measured value falls outside its limit.","definedIn":"m14l06","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Recipe adjustment","def":"A small, bounded change to oven settings verified by profile and inspection.","definedIn":"m06l07","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Reference condition","def":"The 30 °C and 60 % RH ambient at which J-STD-033 floor lives are defined.","definedIn":"m02l02","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Registration","def":"How accurately the aperture pattern aligns with the board pads during printing.","definedIn":"m03l06","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Repair","def":"Making a nonconforming assembly acceptable without necessarily restoring the design.","definedIn":"m11l05","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Repeatability","def":"How tightly repeated placements cluster around their own mean.","definedIn":"m05l01","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Reset point","def":"The bake completion or dry-event moment from which floor life is counted again.","definedIn":"m02l03","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Residual risk","def":"The remaining weakness a repair leaves behind, accepted or rejected by the product owner.","definedIn":"m11l04","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":["m15l03"]},{"term":"Retroactive search","def":"Identifying other units that share a suspect cause with the detected defect.","definedIn":"m14l03","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Rework","def":"Processing that restores a nonconforming item to the original acceptance criteria.","definedIn":"m07l05","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":["m11l05"]},{"term":"Rework loop","def":"The time and cost from detecting a defect to producing a corrected unit.","definedIn":"m13l06","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Rollback plan","def":"The documented route back to the previous revision if a change fails.","definedIn":"m15l05","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Root cause","def":"The condition whose correction prevents recurrence under the same assumptions.","definedIn":"m14l02","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"ROSE","def":"Resistivity of solvent extract, a test of soluble ionic contamination expressed as sodium chloride equivalent.","definedIn":"m12l01","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Rotation error","def":"Angular displacement of the component about its centre after placement.","definedIn":"m05l06","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Sacrificial board","def":"A production-equivalent board used for thermal or destructive testing.","definedIn":"m10l05","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Sample size code letter","def":"The ISO 2859-1 letter derived from lot size and inspection level that fixes the sample size for the plan.","definedIn":"m01l03","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Saponifier","def":"An alkaline additive that converts flux residue into a water-soluble soap for rinsing.","definedIn":"m12l01","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":["m12l02"]},{"term":"Semi-aqueous cleaning","def":"A process using solvent chemistry followed by an aqueous rinse.","definedIn":"m12l02","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Separation","def":"The stencil-to-board gap set at the start of the print stroke.","definedIn":"m04l03","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Shading","def":"Incomplete wetting on a through-hole or pad caused by oxidation or insufficient flux activity.","definedIn":"m09l01","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Shadowing","def":"A dry or starved joint caused by geometry blocking the solder wave.","definedIn":"m08l03","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Shadowing (conformal coating cure)","def":"An area that line-of-sight cure cannot reach under a component or behind a tall part.","definedIn":"m12l04","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Shelf life","def":"The time a sealed material remains usable under stated storage conditions before requalification.","definedIn":"m01l06","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Shielding bag","def":"Packaging that limits electrostatic field exposure to the parts inside it.","definedIn":"m02l05","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Silicone migration","def":"The transfer of low-molecular silicone to nearby surfaces, raising contact resistance.","definedIn":"m12l04","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Single-variable experiment","def":"A trial that changes one process setting and holds every other condition constant.","definedIn":"m04l07","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Skew","def":"Angular misalignment of a placed component against the board coordinate system.","definedIn":"m05l03","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":["m05l04"]},{"term":"Skew (print offset)","def":"A consistent directional displacement of deposits across pads or panel locations.","definedIn":"m04l06","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Slot nozzle","def":"A nozzle with a narrow linear opening, used for rows and long joints.","definedIn":"m10l02","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Slump","def":"Lateral flow of the deposit after release, showing as larger area and lower height.","definedIn":"m04l04","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Slumping","def":"Lateral flow of the deposit after release, producing a wider, flatter print.","definedIn":"m04l06","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Snap-off","def":"The controlled separation of the board from the stencil after the print stroke.","definedIn":"m04l03","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Snap-off distance","def":"The gap the board and stencil reach during separation after the print stroke.","definedIn":"m04l07","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Soak","def":"The near-isothermal segment where the assembly equalises and the flux works before melting.","definedIn":"m06l02","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Solids content","def":"The non-volatile fraction of a flux that remains after the solvent evaporates.","definedIn":"m09l02","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Solids deposition","def":"The mass of flux solids left per unit of board area.","definedIn":"m09l03","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Solidus","def":"The temperature below which an alloy is fully solid.","definedIn":"m06l05","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Special cause","def":"A variation that comes from an identifiable change rather than from ordinary process noise.","definedIn":"m04l02","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"SPI artefact","def":"A reading caused by the inspection setup rather than by the printed deposit.","definedIn":"m04l07","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Stage Pareto","def":"Defect counts sorted by the process stage where they were detected or attributed.","definedIn":"m14l07","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Stand-off","def":"The gap between the component body and the board surface.","definedIn":"m08l03","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Standoff","def":"The gap between the component body and the board surface.","definedIn":"m10l01","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Stencil serial number","def":"The unique identifier that ties a stencil to its measurement report and lifetime history.","definedIn":"m03l06","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Step change","def":"An abrupt shift in a trend chart caused by a single event in the process.","definedIn":"m04l05","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Step depth","def":"The local thickness difference built into a step stencil.","definedIn":"m03l06","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Step stencil","def":"A stencil with locally reduced thickness that lowers paste volume in selected areas.","definedIn":"m03l01","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":["m03l03"]},{"term":"Stratification","def":"Splitting a metric by product, line, shift or defect type so the population is comparable.","definedIn":"m07l03","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":["m14l01"]},{"term":"Surface insulation resistance","def":"The resistance measured across a surface between conductors under humidity and applied bias.","definedIn":"m12l01","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Switching rules","def":"The standard's rules for moving between normal, tightened and reduced inspection according to recent inspection results.","definedIn":"m01l03","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"TAP controller","def":"The state machine inside a scan-capable device that sequences test access port operations.","definedIn":"m13l04","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Tension","def":"The stretching force on the stencil mesh or foil, expressed in N/cm, that keeps the print area flat.","definedIn":"m03l06","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Test access point","def":"A pad or via reserved for probing, sized for the probe tip and clearance.","definedIn":"m13l02","moduleId":"m13","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Test coupon","def":"A sample board that reproduces the production laminate, copper and component mass.","definedIn":"m06l03","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Test coverage map","def":"The record of which defects each inspection and test step can detect.","definedIn":"m15l05","moduleId":"m15","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Thermal budget (component limit)","def":"The temperature and time a part may tolerate without damage.","definedIn":"m10l05","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Thermal budget (rework)","def":"The combination of tip temperature, contact time and heat cycles a joint receives.","definedIn":"m11l01","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":["m11l02"]},{"term":"Thermal cycle","def":"One complete heating and cooling exposure counted against the allowed rework budget.","definedIn":"m11l03","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":["m11l06"]},{"term":"Thermal lag","def":"The delay between oven air temperature and the temperature reached at the joint.","definedIn":"m06l03","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Thermal mass","def":"The heat capacity of the board and its parts, which sets how fast they heat and cool.","definedIn":"m06l04","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":["m10l02"]},{"term":"Thermal pad","def":"The large central land under a QFN or LGA that carries heat and needs a controlled paste volume.","definedIn":"m03l04","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Thermal relief","def":"Spokes in a plane connection that reduce heat flow into or out of a pad.","definedIn":"m06l04","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":["m08l01"]},{"term":"Thermal resistance","def":"How strongly a path resists heat flow; the lowest-resistance path carries the most heat.","definedIn":"m10l03","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Thermal shield","def":"A physical barrier that reduces radiant or convective heat reaching a part.","definedIn":"m10l03","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Thermal shock","def":"Rapid temperature change that can crack components or delaminate the board.","definedIn":"m09l03","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Thermal survey","def":"A recorded temperature history from thermocouples on a representative board.","definedIn":"m06l03","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Thermocouple","def":"A temperature sensor attached to the board to record the actual thermal history.","definedIn":"m06l01","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Thermocouple map","def":"The record of where each thermocouple was attached and what it measured.","definedIn":"m06l07","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Thixotropy","def":"The recovery of paste viscosity after shear, which lets a printed deposit hold its shape.","definedIn":"m04l04","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Time above liquidus","def":"The interval during which the solder alloy is fully molten.","definedIn":"m06l01","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Tolerance stack-up","def":"The combined worst-case effect of several independent tolerances on one gap.","definedIn":"m08l02","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Tombstone","def":"A chip component standing on one end after reflow because one joint wetted before the other.","definedIn":"m05l04","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":["m06l06"]},{"term":"Tombstoning","def":"One end of a chip component lifting during reflow because the two joints wet at different rates.","definedIn":"m03l04","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Top-surface temperature","def":"The board temperature on the component side just before wave contact.","definedIn":"m09l03","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Touch-up","def":"A short iron pass used to correct a joint after the main rework heating.","definedIn":"m11l06","moduleId":"m11","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Traceability","def":"The record linking an assembly to its materials, equipment, programs and time window.","definedIn":"m14l03","moduleId":"m14","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Traceability range","def":"The set of units, lots and handling records that the evidence shows to be affected by an exposure.","definedIn":"m02l06","moduleId":"m02","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Transfer efficiency","def":"The share of nominal aperture volume that reaches the pad as a printed deposit.","definedIn":"m03l01","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":["m03l02"]},{"term":"Under-fill","def":"A deposit that is short in volume, height and area because the aperture did not fill or release.","definedIn":"m04l06","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Use-as-is","def":"A documented concession to accept an item that does not meet the stated requirement.","definedIn":"m07l05","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Vacuum hold-down","def":"A fixture method that pulls the board flat against a support surface.","definedIn":"m10l04","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Verification coverage","def":"The share of inspection calls that were actually reviewed.","definedIn":"m07l02","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":["m07l03"]},{"term":"Version control","def":"Keeping each program revision tied to the board, fixture and material set it was qualified with.","definedIn":"m10l04","moduleId":"m10","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Vertical barrel fill","def":"The percentage of the plated hole depth filled with solder.","definedIn":"m08l05","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Vision calibration","def":"The established relationship between camera pixel measurements and machine coordinates.","definedIn":"m05l03","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Void","def":"A cavity of trapped gas or flux residue inside a solder joint.","definedIn":"m06l06","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":["m07l04"]},{"term":"Volume percent","def":"Measured deposit volume divided by nominal aperture volume, expressed as a percentage.","definedIn":"m04l04","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Volume percentage","def":"Measured deposit volume as a percentage of the nominal aperture volume.","definedIn":"m04l06","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Volume solids","def":"The fraction of the wet coating that remains as dry film after cure.","definedIn":"m12l05","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Wake","def":"The region downstream of an obstacle where the solder wave is disturbed or absent.","definedIn":"m08l04","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Wall taper","def":"The change in aperture size between the top and bottom of the laser-cut wall.","definedIn":"m03l03","moduleId":"m03","alsoDefinedIn":["m03l05"]},{"term":"Warpage","def":"Departure of a board from flatness, measured as bow or twist against a reference surface.","definedIn":"m01l06","moduleId":"m01","alsoDefinedIn":["m11l03"]},{"term":"Wave-side lead protrusion","def":"The length of lead standing proud of the solder side after clinching.","definedIn":"m08l04","moduleId":"m08","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Wet film thickness","def":"The coating thickness measured immediately after application, before solvent loss.","definedIn":"m12l05","moduleId":"m12","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Wettable flank","def":"A plated side feature on a QFN that lets optical inspection judge the fillet.","definedIn":"m07l01","moduleId":"m07","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Wetting","def":"The spreading of molten solder over a metal surface to form a metallurgical bond.","definedIn":"m06l01","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":["m11l01"]},{"term":"Wetting imbalance","def":"Uneven solder wetting that pulls a component out of position during reflow.","definedIn":"m05l06","moduleId":"m05","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Wipe cycle","def":"The interval and method used to clean the stencil underside between prints.","definedIn":"m04l03","moduleId":"m04","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Witness board","def":"A test board used to measure the effective contact length and wave footprint.","definedIn":"m09l04","moduleId":"m09","alsoDefinedIn":[]},{"term":"Worst-case site","def":"The location on the board that reaches the lowest or the highest temperature.","definedIn":"m06l03","moduleId":"m06","alsoDefinedIn":[]}],"formulas":[{"name":"Area ratio (stencil aperture)","expr":"AR = (L × W) / (2 × (L + W) × T)","where":"aperture opening area over sidewall area; L and W are the aperture sides, T the foil thickness. Release is usually acceptable at AR ≥ 0.66","lessonId":"m03l01","lessonTitle":"The paste volume budget: what the joint needs before the aperture is drawn","moduleId":"m03","alsoUsedIn":["m03l02","m03l03","m03l04","m03l05","m04l01","m06l06"],"quote":"Area ratio is the aperture area divided by the sidewall area of the stencil wall.","heading":"Area ratio compares contact area with wall adhesion"},{"name":"Aspect ratio (stencil aperture)","expr":"W / T","where":"narrowest aperture dimension W over foil thickness T; not the plated-hole aspect ratio (depth over diameter) of modules 8 and 9","lessonId":"m03l01","lessonTitle":"The paste volume budget: what the joint needs before the aperture is drawn","moduleId":"m03","alsoUsedIn":["m03l02","m03l03","m03l05","m04l01"],"quote":"Aspect ratio is aperture width divided by stencil thickness, and for rectangular apertures you take the smaller dimension, because that dimension controls the narrowest release path.","heading":"Aspect ratio describes depth against width"},{"name":"Squeegee pressure per blade length","expr":"p = F / L","where":"N/mm; F is the force on the blade in N, L the blade length in mm. The course band is 0.2 to 0.6 N/mm (0.5 to 1.5 kg per 25 mm)","lessonId":"m04l03","lessonTitle":"Printer setup: squeegee pressure and speed, snap-off, separation, wipe cycle","moduleId":"m04","alsoUsedIn":["m04l07"],"quote":"Industry guidance clusters around 0.5 to 1.5 kg per 25 mm of blade, which is 0.2 to 0.6 N/mm; work in N/mm and state the blade length with every setting, because 0.2 N/mm on a 250 mm blade is 50 N and 0.6 N/mm is 150 N.","heading":"Squeegee pressure"},{"name":"Process capability, Cp and Cpk","expr":"Cp = (USL − LSL) / (6 × sd);  Cpk = min[(USL − mean), (mean − LSL)] / (3 × sd)","where":"capability of a stable process against its specification limits; Cp ignores centring, Cpk includes it","lessonId":"m04l05","lessonTitle":"SPI as process evidence: mean, spread, capability, stop/adjust decisions","moduleId":"m04","alsoUsedIn":["m15l01"],"quote":"Cpk adds centring — whether it actually is centred enough to meet them.","heading":"Capability for volume and height"},{"name":"Wave contact time from contact length","expr":"t = L / v","where":"dwell over the wave in s; L is the effective contact length in mm, v the conveyor speed in mm/s","lessonId":"m09l04","lessonTitle":"Contact time and fog height: dwell calculation, wave height vs board clearance","moduleId":"m09","alsoUsedIn":[],"quote":"Calculate it as t = L / v, where L is the effective contact length in mm and v the conveyor speed in mm/s.","heading":"Contact time and why it matters"},{"name":"Shadow length behind a tall part at the wave","expr":"shadow = h × tan(θ)","where":"a line-of-sight screening estimate; h is the part height, θ the wave contact angle measured FROM VERTICAL, plus a 0.5 to 1.0 mm margin for turbulence and flux depletion. Lesson m08l03 shows why the conveyor incline must not be used as that angle (it gives a 137 mm shadow that no line observes)","lessonId":"m09l05","lessonTitle":"Shadowing and orientation: shading, pallets, board direction, lead geometry","moduleId":"m09","alsoUsedIn":["m08l03"],"quote":"Estimate shadow length using a line-of-sight model: shadow length = height * tan(angle).","heading":"Worked calculation"},{"name":"Thermal budget index for hand rework","expr":"index = (tip setpoint − board preheat) × contact time","where":"a comparable number for one rework contact, in °C·s","lessonId":"m11l02","lessonTitle":"Technique: contact order, dwell, feed, lead-free discipline, technique defects","moduleId":"m11","alsoUsedIn":[],"quote":"State thermal budget: tip setpoint 330-350 °C, board preheat 100 °C, contact time 1.5-2.5 s, initial contact; thermal budget index = (tip setpoint - board preheat) × contact time = 230-250 °C × 1.5-2.5 s = 345-625 °C·s per contact.","heading":"Worked calculation"},{"name":"Dry-film thickness from wet film","expr":"DFT = WFT × (volume solids fraction)","where":"coating thickness after cure; WFT is the measured wet film, volume solids as a fraction (45 % → 0.45)","lessonId":"m12l05","lessonTitle":"Application and masking: spray, dip, selective, thickness, cure, repairability","moduleId":"m12","alsoUsedIn":[],"quote":"Step 1: Use the relationship between wet film thickness, dry film thickness, and volume solids: DFT = WFT × volume solids fraction.","heading":"Worked calculation"},{"name":"Residual escape rate of an inspection and test strategy","expr":"escape ppm = Σ occurrence_i × (1 − detection_i)","where":"per defect class i: occurrence rate in ppm times the fraction the strategy does not detect; weighted coverage is 1 − escape / Σ occurrence","lessonId":"m13l05","lessonTitle":"Coverage math: node vs defect coverage, escape risk, where inspection ends and test starts","moduleId":"m13","alsoUsedIn":[],"quote":"Where a defect class occurs at a known rate and a method detects a fraction of it, the residual escape rate is the occurrence rate multiplied by the undetected fraction.","heading":"Escape risk is coverage plus occurrence"},{"name":"Suspect quantity for containment","expr":"suspect boards = production rate × suspect window","where":"the window runs from the earliest credible suspect time (last verified good, or the last material or setting change if earlier) to the line stop","lessonId":"m14l03","lessonTitle":"Containment and traceability: quarantine scope, lot trace, retroactive search","moduleId":"m14","alsoUsedIn":[],"quote":"Calculate the suspect window, suspect board count, and quarantine quantity.","heading":"Worked calculation"},{"name":"Sample size for a no-defect demonstration","expr":"n = ln(1 − confidence) / ln(1 − p)","where":"units that must all pass to show a defect rate no worse than p at the stated confidence (binomial upper bound)","lessonId":"m15l01","lessonTitle":"How designers read process feedback: language, format, what gets acted on","moduleId":"m15","alsoUsedIn":["m14l06"],"quote":"With confidence = 0.95 and target = 0.0010, n = ln(0.05) / ln(0.999) = 2995.","heading":"Worked calculation"}],"termConflicts":[{"term":"Quarantine","first":"m01l01","again":"m01l04"},{"term":"Certificate of analysis","first":"m01l02","again":"m01l05"},{"term":"Floor life","first":"m01l02","again":"m01l06"},{"term":"Quarantine","first":"m01l01","again":"m01l07"},{"term":"Floor life","first":"m01l02","again":"m02l01"},{"term":"Humidity indicator card","first":"m01l02","again":"m02l02"},{"term":"Moisture barrier bag","first":"m02l02","again":"m02l03"},{"term":"Humidity indicator card","first":"m01l02","again":"m02l04"},{"term":"Containment","first":"m01l07","again":"m02l06"},{"term":"Area ratio","first":"m03l01","again":"m03l02"},{"term":"Aspect ratio","first":"m03l01","again":"m03l02"},{"term":"Transfer efficiency","first":"m03l01","again":"m03l02"},{"term":"Step stencil","first":"m03l01","again":"m03l03"},{"term":"Area ratio","first":"m03l01","again":"m03l03"},{"term":"Aspect ratio","first":"m03l01","again":"m03l03"},{"term":"Wall taper","first":"m03l03","again":"m03l05"},{"term":"Area ratio","first":"m03l01","again":"m04l01"},{"term":"Aspect ratio","first":"m03l01","again":"m04l01"},{"term":"Pick height","first":"m05l01","again":"m05l02"},{"term":"Skew","first":"m05l03","again":"m05l04"},{"term":"Classification temperature","first":"m06l01","again":"m06l05"},{"term":"Tombstone","first":"m05l04","again":"m06l06"},{"term":"Dewetting","first":"m06l02","again":"m06l06"},{"term":"Verification coverage","first":"m07l02","again":"m07l03"},{"term":"Void","first":"m06l06","again":"m07l04"},{"term":"Laminar X-ray","first":"m07l01","again":"m07l04"},{"term":"Thermal relief","first":"m06l04","again":"m08l01"},{"term":"Process window","first":"m04l02","again":"m08l02"},{"term":"Fog height","first":"m09l02","again":"m09l03"},{"term":"Fluxometer","first":"m09l02","again":"m09l04"},{"term":"Aspect ratio (plated through-hole)","first":"m08l02","again":"m09l06"},{"term":"Dross","first":"m09l01","again":"m09l07"},{"term":"Meniscus","first":"m09l06","again":"m10l02"},{"term":"Thermal mass","first":"m06l04","again":"m10l02"},{"term":"Moisture sensitivity level","first":"m02l01","again":"m10l03"},{"term":"Wetting","first":"m06l01","again":"m11l01"},{"term":"Thermal budget (rework)","first":"m11l01","again":"m11l02"},{"term":"Collapse","first":"m07l04","again":"m11l03"},{"term":"Warpage","first":"m01l06","again":"m11l03"},{"term":"Disposition","first":"m07l05","again":"m11l04"},{"term":"Rework","first":"m07l05","again":"m11l05"},{"term":"Deviation","first":"m01l01","again":"m11l06"},{"term":"Disposition","first":"m07l05","again":"m11l06"},{"term":"Thermal cycle","first":"m11l03","again":"m11l06"},{"term":"Saponifier","first":"m12l01","again":"m12l02"},{"term":"Ion chromatography","first":"m09l01","again":"m12l03"},{"term":"Boundary scan","first":"m07l01","again":"m13l01"},{"term":"Golden board","first":"m07l02","again":"m13l02"},{"term":"False call","first":"m07l02","again":"m13l03"},{"term":"Escape","first":"m07l02","again":"m13l05"},{"term":"Pogo pin","first":"m13l03","again":"m13l06"},{"term":"Escape","first":"m07l02","again":"m13l06"},{"term":"False call","first":"m07l02","again":"m14l01"},{"term":"Stratification","first":"m07l03","again":"m14l01"},{"term":"Containment","first":"m01l07","again":"m14l03"},{"term":"Detection escape","first":"m14l02","again":"m14l04"},{"term":"PFMEA","first":"m14l05","again":"m14l06"},{"term":"Detection escape","first":"m14l02","again":"m14l07"},{"term":"Residual risk","first":"m11l04","again":"m15l03"}],"formulasNotFound":[]}};
